1 OCCU 1er chirurgien de Charles VII en 1422, Chatelain de Tréchy-le-Bas et de Crécy, Seigneur de Bouy, Seigneur de Courpoy - anobli en 1425
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} from many trees on Geneanet
2 CONT
2 CONT https://gw.geneanet.org/cisa37?n=thiery+du+corpoy&oc=&p=regnault&type=fiche
2 CONT
2 CONT Regnaud (Regnault, Renaud, or Reginald) Thierry was the first Surgeon to the King, in this case Charles VII.
2 CONT
2 CONT Some genealogical studies suggest that Renaud Thierry was the grandfather of Robert Thiery de Saint-Thomas, himself the grandfather of the famous Jean Thiery, whose legacy remains an anecdotal tale known to notaries and other gene
2 CONC alogists (see the article dated 10/1/2001 "A Notarial Act, 1781" by Gérard Goglin). The best-known study documenting this lineage is that of Monsieur Thierry-Miegt, published in 1894 (with a print run of 500 copies) and based on a
2 CONC ncient manuscripts.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cited as early as 1422 as Surgeon to the King, it can be assumed, contrary to what is sometimes read, that he was not born in 1410 but rather around 1400: if he had been born around 1410, he would have been a surgeon in short trou
2 CONC sers, only 12 years old in 1422.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is also known to have been Dean of the Church in 1429, and therefore a religious figure like many physicians of the time.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the history of the Church, the deanship refers to the dignity of dean. The office of dean was not only administrative; it also had corporate foundations. The rural chapter (all the priests of the deanery) could elect the dean o
2 CONC r propose a candidate to the bishop. The centralization of episcopal power in the 13th century led to a weakening of the dean's powers, which were redefined more precisely by the diocesan or rural chapters. The dean exercised, wit
2 CONC hout legal authority, oversight of the clergy, controlled the distribution of prebends, convened and presided over rural chapters, informed the clergy of synodal decisions, and led the clerical brotherhood of his parish. During th
2 CONC e Catholic Reformation, deans were tasked with pastoral visitations, which were more numerous than before, to more closely monitor the parish clergy on behalf of the bishop.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1430, a priest (or dean) could be married, despite the fact that in 1123, under the pontificate of Callistus II, the First Lateran Council declared priestly marriages null and void, and in 1139, the Second Lateran Council, unde
2 CONC r the pontificate of Innocent II, confirmed the decree of the previous council. It should be noted that in the 15th century, 50% of religious orders were still married and accepted by the people as such. This was a time of transit
2 CONC ion in the status of Catholic religious orders.
2 CONT
2 CONT It therefore seems possible that Regnaud Thierry had descendants and that he was the great-great-grandfather of Jean Thiery.
2 CONT
2 CONT Regnaud Thierry is also said to be the castellan and lord of "Tréchy-le-bas," where he is said to have died in 1455. It is possible that this hamlet disappeared and was replaced by a current village called Etréchy in the parish of
2 CONC  Mehun-sur-Yèvre, or the current hamlet of Tréchy in Seine-et-Marne, near Montereau-Fault-Yonne.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Hôtel Charles VII Mehun-sur-Yvre On December 29, 1429, he was present when Joan of Arc came to Mehun to collect her letters of ennoblement, and it was he who lodged her in the residence he owned in this royal city. Legend has 
2 CONC it that the house still exists and is believed to be the current Hôtel Charles VII in Mehun.
2 CONT
2 CONT Finally, Regnaud Thierry died after 1452, since he testified at the retrial of the Maid, having known her during the investigation held in Orléans in 1452. He testified in her favor.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the archives, the clerk records the following deposition from him: "Audivit ab ea illud quod dicebat, videlicet quod erat missa a Deo ad nobilem Dalphinum pro levando obsidionem Aurelianensem, et pro ducendo regem Remis ad sacr
2 CONC andum et coronandum" (Deposition of Regnault Thierry, Trial, vol. III, p. 25).
2 CONT
2 CONT In French: A witness reports that in Chinon, he heard the Maid say that she was sent from God to the noble Dauphin to lift the siege of Orléans and to lead the king to Reims for his coronation.
2 CONT
2 CONT Another excerpt from Regnaud Thierry's deposition:
2 CONT
2 CONT "...Here is something I witnessed. Joan was at the siege of Saint-Pierre-le-Moustier. When the town was stormed, the men-at-arms prepared to plunder the church and remove the vases and other precious objects; but Joan opposed them
2 CONC  with manly energy, and by her defenses she succeeded in preventing anything from being touched."
2 CONT
2 CONT "Deposition of Master Regnaud Thierry, surgeon to the king, at the trial of Joan of Arc, Abbey of Saint-Benoît."
2 CONT
2 CONT While Mr. Thierry-Miegt's study remains questionable on certain points (he attributes to Regnaud a surprising ancestry dating back to Clovis...), it is nevertheless possible that this surgeon was indeed the grandfather of Robert T
2 CONC hierry de Saint-Thomas, Royal Constable and Archer of the Gratien d'Aguerre Company, ennobled in 1510 in Bar by Duke Antoine, it is said...
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.histoire-genealogie.com/spip.php?article741
2 CONT
2 CONT egnaud;Renault,Renaut,Renaud? Very rich bourgeois, living in the most beautiful hotel in the city and owning many fiefs. -1439: cited: Lord of the mills of Crécy -Chatelain of Tréchy-le-bas in 1447 (hamlet disappeared = Tréchy le 
2 CONC bas/Mehun.sur.Yèvres.18). -Archer of the Company of Gratien d'Aguerre -Office of Surgeon of the Dauphin 1418, who became King, and was first Surgeon of CHARLES.VII. until around 1453 -December 14, 1422: allocation of a pension of 
2 CONC 600 pounds -He was ennobled in 1425 - He was close to Joan of Arc, hosted her in Mehun where she received her letters of Nobility in 1429, house called "Hôtel de la Pucelle, rue de l'horloge". He testified at the rehabilitation tr
2 CONC ial of Joan of Arc in 1452 and testified vigorously in her favor. He owned a substantial property fortune: land, farms, several houses in Mehun (fief of the "Grande Maison" - former Barbarin and Sauthenet hotels). Widowed, he ente
2 CONC red the orders (between 1452 and 1455) and became Dean of the chapter of the Notre-Dame collegiate church in Mehun. A multifaceted, somewhat mysterious figure. A chapel in the choir of the church of Mehun still bears his coat of a
2 CONC rms on the keystone: "azure with a golden chevron, accompanied by three silver partridges, membered and besqueted gules, 2 and 1." - On February 3, 1466, he made a donation in front of Jehan de la Rivière, notary in Bourges, for t
2 CONC he benefit of his grandson Renaud Bonin, dean of the church of Mehun.
2 CONT
