2 CONT Monday, October 1, 2001, by Gérard Goglin Document intended to replace the baptismal and burial certificates and to establish the rights to the fabulous inheritance of the Merchant of Venice (d. in 1676)
2 CONT
2 CONT (The spelling of the original document is preserved) November 14, 1781, Investigation Extract from the minutes of the registry of the Royal Provost Court of Andelot Investigation conducted by us, Pierre Nicolas Baudot, Counselor t
2 CONC o the King, President Provost, Investigating Examiner Commissioner, and Lieutenant General of Police in the Royal Provost Court of Andelot, assisted by Jean Baptiste Huin, ordinary clerk. At the request of Guillaume Foissy, a bour
2 CONC geois currently residing in Paris, entitled to claim and bearer the heir of the late Jean Thièry, who died in Venice in 1667 and was the grandson of Robert Thièry, Constable of the King of France. Following and in execution of our
2 CONC  order of today duly sealed and at the request of the petition presented to us the same day by the said Guillaume Foissy; in which investigation we separately heard all the witnesses summoned by virtue of our said order and whose 
2 CONC depositions were received as follows. On the fourteenth of November one thousand seven hundred and eighty-one, at two o'clock in the morning at our hotel, François Hebert, laborer residing in Signeville, aged eighty-five, summoned
2 CONC  by writ of Routier of this day, a copy of which he presented to us after swearing to tell the truth and that the above-dated petition was read to him, which we gave him to understand that he said he was not a relative, servant, o
2 CONC r domestic of the petitioning party. Deposes that he remembers very precisely having heard in his youth from a number of people who had witnessed the fact that the church of the village of Signeville, the place where the deponent 
2 CONC was born, was burned down by the enemies of the state, that this fact, supported by the most constant tradition, leaves no doubt in the country that all the registers and papers of the said church perished in this fire, which is w
2 CONC hy there are no longer any from that time; also adds the deponent that he often heard in his youth about Antoine Thièry who died in the said place of Signeville on October 3, 1693, that the said Antoine Thièry, as long as he lived
2 CONC , was always considered and was recognized as the grandson of Robert Thièry, gendarme of the King of France, and that this fact, still very recent at the time of the deponent's early age, was considered constant, which is all he s
2 CONC aid he knew of the said facts. Having read to him his present deposition, he said that it contains the truth, that he persists in it, and paid the tax we paid him of fifteen sols and declared that he could not sign because of his 
2 CONC infirmities and his great age. As for us, we signed with our clerk, signed Baudot and Huin with initials. Then come the testimonies of Henry Larulet, a ploughman living in Signeville, aged fifty-three, Gervais Rémy, a farrier, liv
2 CONC ing in Signeville, aged fifty-three, and finally Claude Hebert, a ploughman, living in Signeville, aged fifty-two. They are all identical to that of François Hebert. Made under our seal, that of our clerk, on the days, month, and 
2 CONC year mentioned above, signed Baudot and Huin with initials. Delivered for dispatch by the undersigned chief clerk, the applicant, on the said Foissy, by Mr. Claude Couhez, his attorney in this seat. Signed HUIN Checked in Andelot 
2 CONC on 15/9/1781. Received twelve sols ten deniers. Signatures illegible.
