2 CONT
2 CONT Thiébaud "confirmed in 1182 the donation of the church of Faletans to the Abbey of St. Paul. He called himself a knight and named Dame Laure, his wife, in the donation he made to the monastery of Lieu-Croissant, of a meix and a ma
2 CONC n in Faletans."
2 CONT
2 CONT This village was part of the provostship of Rochefort and was divided into several fiefs. Different meix were legally dependent on the lordship of Rochefort; the others were divided in unequal proportions between the fiefs known a
2 CONC s La Tour, Vaudrey, Montrond, Bernaud, and Amange. The most important was La Tour; the village provostship was attached to it. A noble family, very distinguished in the province, owned it. The obituary of Saint-Paul of Besançon re
2 CONC ads: Nono kal. In May 1088, Guillelmus de Phaletans died, subdiaconus noster redditus, apud nos sepultus, à quo habemus ecclesiam de Phaletans.
2 CONT The land of Falletans, near Rochefort, had as its first lord Thiébaud de Falletans, knight, who confirmed in 1182 the donation of the church of Falletans to the Abbey of Saint-Paul de Besançon. He, and Laure his wife, gave to the 
2 CONC monastery of Lieu-Croissant, a meix and a man in Falletans, a donation recalled in 1259 in the donation that Estevenin de Falletans made to Renaud his son of his property in Falletans.
2 CONT
2 CONT In Girefontaine (70), Thiébaud de Falletans is considered the benefactor of the Templar House; He is said to have given the Templars his domain of l'Etoile, near Lons-le-Saunier, known as Puy de Saint Corneille, this estate alread
2 CONC y coveted for its renowned vineyards. Soon, the Templars built a house and a chapel dedicated to Saint George there.
2 CONT
2 CONT The House of Faletans was established in Salins from the beginning of the 14th century. It took its name from the land of Faletans located within the jurisdiction of the bailiwick of Dole and owned until the 15th century by the el
2 CONC der branch of this house.
2 CONT The oldest known lord was Renaud de Faletans, knight and lord of the said place, of l'Etoile and Saule, who paid homage on the feast day of Saint Vincent in 1269 to Laure de Commercy, widow of Jean, Count of Burgundy, of the Bois 
2 CONC de la Sale and all that he owned from the Château de Pymont to Saule. In 1291, he sold to Perrenet de Rochefort, the estate of one of his subjects in Faletans. He had from Sibille, his wife, Étienne and Amey, author of the branch 
2 CONC established in Salins.
2 CONT
2 CONT
