1 NOTE {geni:about_me} According to Medlands the information below is incorrect. Lesceline was daughter of Geoffrey de Clinton and Lesceline and not Agnes de Beaumont.
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ENGLISHNOBILITYMEDIEVAL3.htm#LescelineClintonMNormanVerdun '''From Medlands:''']
2 CONT
2 CONT GEOFFREY de Clinton (-[29 Sep 1130/1 Aug 1133]).  Chamberlain and Sheriff under King Henry I from [1108].  Orderic Vitalis names "Goisfredus de Clintona, Radulfus Basset et Hugo de Bocalanda…" among those who were "de ignobili sti
2 CONC rpe" and whom King Henry I raised "de pulvere" (from the dust)[2695].  "…Gaufrido de Clint…" witnessed the charter dated to [1107/16] under which Henry I King of England granted property to "uxori Willelmi vicecomitis de Oxeneford
2 CONC  et filiis suis"[2696].  "…Gaufr de Glint…" subscribed the charter dated to [10 Apr/29 May] 1121 which records the arrangements for the marriage of "Miloni de Gloec" and "Sibilia filia Beorndi de Novo Mercato"[2697].  “Gaufridus d
2 CONC e Clintona, Henrici regis camerarius” founded Kenilworth priory, Warwickshire by undated charter[2698].  "G. de Clinton" donated "manerium de Hichendon…ecclesiam de Stanes" [Stone priory] to Kenilworth priory, with the consent of 
2 CONC "Nicholai de Stafford", by charter dated to [1122/25][2699].  The 1130 Pipe Roll records "Elye Giff" in Northamptonshire[2700].  Orderic Vitalis records disputes between "Goisfredia de Clintonia" and King Henry I, dated to [1130][
2 CONC 2701].  Henry II King of England confirmed the property of Savigny abbey, including the donation by "Gaufridi camerarii de Clinton et Gaufridi filii eius" of "molendinum de Breneia", by charter dated to [1156/58][2702].  Military 
2 CONC fee certifications in the Red Book of the Exchequer, in 1166, record "Gal[fridus] camerarius de Clintone" holding knights’ fees held "Robertus de Scrupa" in Gloucestershire, and "Galfridus de Clintone" holding 3 knights’ fees in W
2 CONC allingford, Berkshire[2703].  
2 CONT
2 CONT m '''LESCELINE''', daughter of ---.  The primary source which confirms her marriage has not yet been identified.   
2 CONT
2 CONT Geoffrey & his wife had children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT a)         GEOFFREY de Clinton (-after 1166).  “Gaufridus de Clintona" confirmed donations to Kenilworth priory, Warwickshire, for the soul of "patris mei Gaufridi camerarii…ipsius ecclesie fundator", by undated charter[2704].  "G
2 CONC aufridus de Clintone" confirmed the donation of land "in…Chersintona" made to Eynsham abbey by "Willelmus de Clintone patruus meus" by charter dated to [before 1152] witnessed by "Agnes uxor mea, Willelmus de Plan, Osbertus de Cli
2 CONC nt[one]…"[2705].  Henry II King of England confirmed the property of Savigny abbey, including the donation by "Gaufridi camerarii de Clinton et Gaufridi filii eius" of "molendinum de Breneia", by charter dated to [1156/58][2706]. 
2 CONC  Henry II King of England confirmed the property of Savigny abbey, including the donation by "Gaufridi camerarii de Clinton et Gaufridi filii eius" of "molendinum de Breneia", by charter dated to [1156/58][2707].  “Gaufridus camer
2 CONC arius de Clintona” founded Bretford nunnery in Warwickshire by undated charter, witnessed by “Agnete uxore mea cum filio meo Henrico...Osbertus de Clintona...”[2708].  Military fee certifications in the Red Book of the Exchequer, 
2 CONC in 1166, record "Gal[fridus] camerarius de Clintone" holding knights’ fees held "Robertus de Scrupa" in Gloucestershire, and "Galfridus de Clintone" holding 3 knights’ fees in Wallingford, Berkshire[2709].  m AGNES of Warwick, dau
2 CONC ghter of ROGER Earl of Warwick & his wife Gundred de Warenne of Surrey.  The marriage settlement of "Rogerus comes Warr…Agnetem filiam meam" and "Gaufrido camerario" is undated, witnessed by "…Roberto fratre meo et Gaufrido et Hen
2 CONC rico…Hastecill de Haruc…Willelmus de Glint[ona]…Hug[one] de Glint[ona] et Maur[icio] fratre eius…"[2710].  "Gaufridus de Clintone" confirmed the donation of land "in…Chersintona" made to Eynsham abbey by "Willelmus de Clintone pat
2 CONC ruus meus" by charter dated to [before 1152] witnessed by "Agnes uxor mea, Willelmus de Plan, Osbertus de Clint[one]…"[2711].  “Gaufridus camerarius de Clintona” founded Bretford nunnery in Warwickshire by undated charter, witness
2 CONC ed by “Agnete uxore mea cum filio meo Henrico...Osbertus de Clintona...”[2712].  Geoffrey & his wife had two children:  i)          HENRY de Clinton (-[1214/16]).  “Gaufridus camerarius de Clintona” founded Bretford nunnery in War
2 CONC wickshire by undated charter, witnessed by “Agnete uxore mea cum filio meo Henrico...Osbertus de Clintona...”[2713].  “Henricus de Clintona" confirmed donations to Kenilworth priory, Warwickshire, founded by "G. de Clinton avus me
2 CONC us", including the donation by "Lecelinæ et concessione Bertrami de Verduno filio eius" of "ecclesiam de Etha", by undated charter[2714].  The Red Book of the Exchequer records "Henricus de Clinton" holding one knight’s fee "de ho
2 CONC nore Gloucestriæ" in Buckinghamshire in [1210/12][2715].  m AMICE de Bidun, daughter of JOHN de Bidun & his wife Alice Mauduit.  The Rotuli de Dominabus of 1185 records that the daughters of “Alizia de Bidune, soror Willelmi Maudu
2 CONC it” married “primogenitam…Hugo de Clinton…”[2716].  Bracton records a claim, dated 1231, by "Amicabilis de Wuttona" against "abbatem de Lauendena" concerning "ecclesiam de Wuttona…advocacionem", claimed by "Ricardus de Bello Campo
2 CONC " and which, according to "ipsi Amicabilis et Ricardus", was granted by "Johannes de Bidun pater predicte Amicabilis et Sarre uxoris predicti Ricardi" to "Walterum de Bidun", and that "Halnacus pater ipsius Johannis" had presented
2 CONC  the church to "Walterus de Bidun qui fuit cancellarius Regis Scocie" and "post mortem Walteri de Bidun" to "predictus Walterum", while the abbot summoned "Amiciam de Clingtona filiam predicti Johannis de Bidun…Ermiceda de Bidun e
2 CONC t Robertus filius Galfridi"[2717].  A writ dated 11 Nov "39 Hen III", after the death of "Maud de Rocheford, sometime the wife of John de Bydun", states that "John Bidun died without heirs of his body and had five sisters…Amicia w
2 CONC ho had three daughters, Amabel, Isabel and Agnes…"[2718].  Henry & his wife had four children:  (a)       HENRY de Clinton (-1233 or before).  Farrer records that Henry’s son Henry obtained seisin of his father’s lands in Kenilwor
2 CONC th on 17 Mar 1218[2719].  (b)       AMABEL de Clinton .  A writ dated 11 Nov "39 Hen III", after the death of "Maud de Rocheford, sometime the wife of John de Bydun", states that "John Bidun died without heirs of his body and had 
2 CONC five sisters…Amicia who had three daughters, Amabel, Isabel and Agnes" and adding that "Amabel died without heir"[2720].  m LUKE de Columbiers, son of ---.  (c)       ISABEL de Clinton .  A writ dated 11 Nov "39 Hen III", after th
2 CONC e death of "Maud de Rocheford, sometime the wife of John de Bydun", states that "John Bidun died without heirs of his body and had five sisters…Amicia who had three daughters, Amabel, Isabel and Agnes" and adding that "Isabel had 
2 CONC Henry son of Ralph, who is her heir"[2721].  m RALPH, son of JOHN [de Merston] & his wife ---.  (d)       AGNES de Clinton (-after 14 Dec 1255).  A writ dated 11 Nov "39 Hen III", after the death of "Maud de Rocheford, sometime th
2 CONC e wife of John de Bydun", states that "John Bidun died without heirs of his body and had five sisters…Amicia who had three daughters, Amabel, Isabel and Agnes" and adding that "Agnes, wife of Warin de Brageham, still lives"[2722].
2 CONC   m WARIN de Brageham, son of ---.  ii)         WILLIAM de Clinton (-after 1210).  The Testa de Nevill lists knights who held land in Buckinghamshire, Bedfordshire, dated to [1208/10]: "Willelmus de Clinton" held "Eston"[2723].  T
2 CONC he Red Book of the Exchequer records "Willelmus de Clintone" holding "Estone per serjanteriam lardenariæ, vel i militis" in Buckinghamshire in [1210/12][2724]. 
