2 CONT
2 CONT Roger de Beaumont, 2nd Earl of Warwick (1102 – 12 June 1153) was the elder son of Henry de Beaumont, 1st Earl of Warwick and Marguerite, daughter of Geoffrey II of Perche and Beatrix of Montdidier. He was also known as Roger de Ne
2 CONC wburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was generally considered to have been a devout and pious man; a chronicle of the period, the Gesta Regis Stephani, speaks of him as a "man of gentle disposition". The borough of Warwick remembers him as the founder of the Hospi
2 CONC tal of S. Michael for lepers which he endowed with the tithes of Wedgnock, and other property; he also endowed the House of the Templars beyond the bridge. In the reign of Stephen he founded a priory dedicated to S. Cenydd at Llan
2 CONC gennith, Co. Glamorgan and he attached it as a cell to the Abbey of S. Taurinus at Evreux in Normandy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family and children[edit]
2 CONT
2 CONT He married 1130 Gundred de Warenne, daughter of William de Warenne, 2nd Earl of Surrey and Elizabeth de Vermandois and had children: 1.William de Beaumont, 3rd Earl of Warwick. 2.Waleran de Beaumont, 4th Earl of Warwick (1153 – 12
2 CONC  December 1204). 3.Henry de Beaumont, was Dean of Salisbury in 1205. 4.Agnes de Beaumont, married Geoffrey de Clinton, Chamberlain to the King and son of Geoffrey de Clinton, the founder of Kenilworth Castle and Priory. 5.Margaret
2 CONC  de Beaumont. 6.Gundred de Beaumont (c.1135–1200), married: 1.Hugh Bigod, 1st Earl of Norfolk; 2.Roger de Glanville.www.findagrave.com
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert De Newburgh
2 CONT BIRTH 1100
2 CONT Haute-Normandie, France
2 CONT DEATH 30 Aug 1159 (aged 58–59)
2 CONT Le Bec-Hellouin, Departement de l'Eure, Haute-Normandie, France
2 CONT BURIAL
2 CONT Bec Abbey
2 CONT Le Bec-Hellouin, Departement de l'Eure, Haute-Normandie, France
2 CONT MEMORIAL ID 120058228 
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert de Newburgh was born about 1100 and died on 30 Aug 1159 and is buried at Bec Abbey. His wife was Godelbreda and they had at least five sons.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the son of Henry de Newburgh, 1st of the Norman earls of Warwick and his wife, Margaret de Perche. He was the brother to Roger de Newburgh, the 2nd Earl of Warwick.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert succeeded to his father's estate in Ponteaudemer, Normany, where he resided and served as Seneschal of that Dukedom.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert received from Henry I of England (1068-1135) the grant of the manor of Winfrith in Dorsetshire, England.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a great benefactor of the Abbey of Bec in Normandy, in late life he became a monk there.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert de Newburgh's ancestor were those that settled in Dorsetshire, England and later America where they use the different surnames including but not limited to: NEWBURGH, NEWBOROUGH, NEWBURY and NEWBERRY.
2 CONT
