1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Geoffrey de Clinton (d. c. 1134) was an Anglo-Norman noble, chamberlain and treasurer to King Henry I of England. He was foremost amongst the men king Henry "raised from the dust".[1]. He married Lescelina
2 CONT
2 CONT Clinton's family origins are a little obscure. The surname probably derives from the village of Glympton in Oxfordshire.[2], though the family ultimately derived from Saint-Pierre-de-Semilly (Manche, arr. St. Lô, canton St.-Clair)
2 CONC  in western Normandy.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT It appears that Clinton spent some years as a minor official of the king's, until the 1118 fall of the treasurer Herbert camerarius, who was accused of plotting against the king. By 1120 Clinton had taken his place.
2 CONT
2 CONT Not too long afterwards Clinton was appointed sheriff of Warwickshire (by 1121), to act as counterweight to the Earl of Warwick, Roger de Beaumont, who Henry I did not trust. The 1122 rebellion of Roger's cousin Waleran de Beaumon
2 CONC t, 1st Earl of Worcester increased the king's suspicions still further, and he compelled Roger to grant Clinton a substantial part of his Warwickshire domains.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Clinton further secured his position by starting work on the great castle of Kenilworth, only two miles from the earl's central fortress at Warwick.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Clinton received other land grants from Henry I, and he used his position of political influence to enrich himself in other ways[6]. In sum his total wealth was just below the level of the greatest magnates of the kingdom.[7] He h
2 CONC ad enough to spend 2,000 pounds to insure his nephew Roger de Clinton's election as Bishop of Coventry.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT Around Easter 1130 Clinton was accused of treason, but was acquitted by a panel including David I of Scotland, who was sitting in his capacity as Earl of Huntingdon. The substance of the accusations against Clinton are not known, 
2 CONC although since he was Henry I's treasurer, financial malfeasance of some sort is possible.[9] The Beaumonts were back in favor, and it may be that they were behind the proceedings.[10]
2 CONT
2 CONT Clinton remained in the royal service, though he was never as influential as he had been. He died sometime between 1133 and 1135.[11]
2 CONT
2 CONT It appears that Clinton's land-holding must have been primarily life tenencies since his descendants property was not nearly so imposing.[12] His son and successor, also named Geoffrey, became engaged in a violent quarrel with the
2 CONC  Earl of Warwick early in the reign of King Stephen of England. The Clintons nearly lost everything, but in the end a settlement was reached (probably in the summer of 1138) by which the younger Geoffrey de Clinton married Warwick
2 CONC 's daughter Agnes.[13]
2 CONT
2 CONT The direct Clinton male line seems to have died out during the reign of Henry III. The later Baron Clintons, Earls of Lincoln, and Dukes of Newcastle-Under-Lyne descend from the elder Geoffrey de Clinton's nephew Osbert.[14]
2 CONT
2 CONT A fortification has existed on the site from Saxon times, but the current ruin is of Norman origin; a great square stone tower built by Geoffrey de Clinton, Treasurer and Chief Justice of England to Henry I, in about 1125. Henry I
2 CONC I took control of the castle during the Revolt of 1173-1174, giving the Clintons another castle in Buckinghamshire by way of compensation.
2 CONT
2 CONT Work then began to improve the defensive qualities of the castle, continuing during the reign of Henry III and transforming the castle into one of the strongest in the Midlands. The strategic advantages of water defences had long 
2 CONC been known, and at Kenilworth a great man made lake was created to defend three sides of the castle. Covering over 100 acres (0.4 km²) it was an expensive endeavour, but the value in keeping siege engines at a distance and as a ba
2 CONC rrier to assault or mining was immense.
2 CONT
