1 OCCU Comte de Vermandois, Valois, et Crepy; Head of the French Contingent in the First Crusade; Crusader Knight in the "Crusade of the Faint-hearted", Herre till Chamont-en-Vexin, 1087 greve av Vermandois, greve av Vermandois
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''''Note regarding his name:''' Sources show that Hugues Magnus was a compound name. See below for details.''
2 CONT
2 CONT == Wikipedia ==
2 CONT
2 CONT From the English Wikipedia page of Hugh I, Count of Vermandois:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hugh_I,_Count_of_Vermandois
2 CONT
2 CONT French Wikipedia: (1057-1102)
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hugues_Ier_de_Vermandois
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh I (1053 – October 18, 1101), called Magnus or the Great, was a younger son of Henry I of France and Anne of Kiev and younger brother of Philip I. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was in his own right Count of Vermandois, but an ineffectual leader and soldier, great only in his boasting. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Indeed, Steven Runciman is certain that his nickname Magnus (greater or elder), applied to him by William of Tyre, is a copyist's error, and should be Minus (younger), referring to Hugh as younger brother of the King of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT In early 1096 Hugh and Philip began discussing the First Crusade after news of the Council of Clermont reached them in Paris. Although Philip could not participate, as he had been excommunicated, Hugh was said to have been influen
2 CONC ced to join the Crusade after an eclipse of the moon on February 11, 1096.
2 CONT
2 CONT That summer Hugh's army left France for Italy, where they would cross the Adriatic Sea into territory of the Byzantine Empire, unlike the other Crusader armies who were travelling by land. On the way, many of the soldiers led by f
2 CONC ellow Crusader Emicho of Flonheim joined Hugh's army after Emicho was defeated by the Hungarians (under King Coloman I "The Booklover" at Moson fortress), whose land he had been pillaging. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh crossed the Adriatic from Bari in Southern Italy, but many of his ships were destroyed in a storm off the Byzantine port of Dyrrhachium.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh and most of his army was rescued and escorted to Constantinople, where they arrived in November 1096. Prior to his arrival (he would be the first to arrive in Constantinople), Hugh sent an arrogant, insulting letter to Easter
2 CONC n Roman Emperor Alexius I Comnenus, according to the Emperor's biography by his daughter (the Alexiad), demanding that Alexius meet with him:
2 CONT
2 CONT "Know, O King, that I am King of Kings, and superior to all, who are under the sky. You are now permitted to greet me, on my arrival, and to receive me with magnificence, as befits my nobility."[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexius was already wary of the armies about to arrive, after the unruly mob led by Peter the Hermit had passed through earlier in the year ("The People's Crusade"). Alexius kept Hugh in custody in a monastery until Hugh swore an 
2 CONC oath of vassalage to him.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the Crusaders had successfully made their way across Seljuk territory and, in 1098, captured Antioch, Hugh (and Baldwin of Hainault were) sent back to Constantinople to appeal for reinforcements from Alexius (Baldwin mysteri
2 CONC ously vanishes in an ambush along the way). Alexius was uninterested in sending an expedition to claim the city so late in summer. (This triggers off a series of arguments in Antioch, where Bohemund asserts that Alexius had violat
2 CONC ed his oath to assist the crusades, and therefore, the city by rights was his. This argument, and an outbreak of typhus, ties up the Crusaders for the remainder of the year.) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh, instead of returning to Antioch to help plan the siege of Jerusalem, went back to France. There he was scorned for not having fulfilled his vow as a Crusader to complete a pilgrimage to Jerusalem, and Pope Paschal II threate
2 CONC ned to excommunicate him. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh joined the minor Crusade of 1101 ("The Crusade of the Faint-Hearted," alongside William IX of Aquitaine and Welf I, Duke of Bavaria, and accompanied by Ida of Austria, mother of Leopold III of Austria). Half of this army was 
2 CONC allowed to set sail from Constantinople for Palestine, while the other half marched overland, reaching Heraclea by September. Hugh was wounded in battle with the Turks (ambushed by Kilij Arslan) in September, and died of his wound
2 CONC s on October 18 in Tarsus. (Their group continued eastward under William of Nevers and Raymond of Toulouse, arriving at Jerusalem in Easter 1102. Kilij Arslan later establishes his capital at Konya after his victories over the "Cr
2 CONC usade of the Faint-Hearted.")
2 CONT
2 CONT == Family and children ==
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Adele of Vermandois, the daughter of Herbert IV, Count of Vermandois and Alice, Countess of Valois. They had nine children:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Matilda(1080-1130), married Ralph I of Beaugency
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Beatrice (1082-after1144), married Hugh III of Gournay
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Ralph I (1085-1152)
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Elizabeth of Vermandois, Countess of Leicester (1085-1131)
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. Constance (1086-??), married Godfrey de la Ferté-Gaucher
2 CONT
2 CONT 6. Agnes (1090-1125), married Boniface of Savone
2 CONT
2 CONT 7. Henry (1091-1130), Lord of Chaumont en Vexin
2 CONT
2 CONT 8. Simon (1093-1148)
2 CONT
2 CONT 9. William (c. 1094-c.1096)
2 CONT
2 CONT [In order originally presented on Wikipedia page:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Count Raoul I of Vermandois
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Henry, senior of Chaumont-en-Vexin, (d. 1130).
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Simon, Bishop of Noyon
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Elizabeth de Vermandois, married Robert de Beaumont, 1st Earl of Leicester; William de Warenne, 2nd Earl of Surrey
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. Matilde de Vermandois, married Raoul I of Beaugency
2 CONT
2 CONT 6. Constance de Vermandois, married Godefroy de la Ferte-Gaucher
2 CONT
2 CONT 7. Agnes de Vermandois, married Margrave Boniface del Vasto. Mother of Adelaide del Vasto.
2 CONT
2 CONT 8. Beatrix de Vermandois, married Hugh III of Gournay-en-Bray
2 CONT
2 CONT 9. Emma de Vermandois)
2 CONT
