2 CONT From Darryl Lundy's Peerage page on Hugh de Crepi:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.thepeerage.com/p10318.htm#i103173
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh de Crépi, Comte de Vermandois et de Valois1
2 CONT
2 CONT M, #103173, b. 1057, d. 18 October 1102
2 CONT
2 CONT Last Edited=7 Feb 2005
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh de Crépi, Comte de Vermandois et de Valois was born in 1057. He was the son of Henri I, Roi de France and Anne of Kiev.2,3
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Aelis de Vermandois, Comtesse de Vermandois, daughter of Heribert V, Comte de Vermandois et de Valois and Adele de Crépi, circa 1080.2
2 CONT
2 CONT He died on 18 October 1102 at Tarsus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh de Crépi, Comte de Vermandois et de Valois was a member of the House of Capet. Hugh de Crépi, Comte de Vermandois et de Valois also went by the nick-name of Hugh 'le Grand'.3
2 CONT
2 CONT He gained the title of Comte de Vermandois. He gained the title of Comte de Valois.
2 CONT
2 CONT Child of Hugh de Crépi, Comte de Vermandois et de Valois and Aelis de Vermandois, Comtesse de Vermandois
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Elizabeth de Vermandois+1 d. 17 Feb 1131
2 CONT
2 CONT Citations
2 CONT
2 CONT [S6] G.E. Cokayne; with Vicary Gibbs, H.A. Doubleday, Geoffrey H. White, Duncan Warrand and Lord Howard de Walden, editors, The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain and the United Kingdom, Extant, Extinct 
2 CONC or Dormant, new ed., 13 volumes in 14 (1910-1959; reprint in 6 volumes, Gloucester, U.K.: Alan Sutton Publishing, 2000), volume VII, page 526. Hereinafter cited as The Complete Peerage.
2 CONT
2 CONT [S16] Jirí Louda and Michael MacLagan, Lines of Succession: Heraldry of the Royal Families of Europe, 2nd edition (London, U.K.: Little, Brown and Company, 1999), table 64. Hereinafter cited as Lines of Succession.
2 CONT
2 CONT [S6] Cokayne, and others, The Complete Peerage, volume XII/2, page 829.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Foundation for Medieval Genealogy page on Northern France:
2 CONT
2 CONT HUGUES "le Maisné" de France, son of HENRI I King of France & his second wife Anna Iaroslavna of Kiev (1057-Tarsus 18 Oct 1102, bur Tarsus, Church of St Paul).
2 CONT
2 CONT The Liber Modernorum Regum Francorum names (in order) "Philippum, Hugonem atque Rotbertum" as the three sons of King Henri and his wife Anna[1325].
2 CONT
2 CONT William of Tyre records "dominus Hugo Magnus" as brother of Philippe I King of France[1326]. He succeeded as Comte de Vermandois et de Valois, by right of his wife. He left France in Aug 1096 as head of the contingent of his broth
2 CONC er Philippe I King of France which left on the First Crusade[1327].
2 CONT
2 CONT The Alexeiad names "a certain Hugh, brother of the king of France" when recording that he "sent an absurd message to the emperor proposing that he should be given a magnificent reception" after arriving in Constantinople[1328]. He
2 CONC  was shipwrecked off Durazzo, but accompanied to Constantinople by the Byzantine admiral Manuel Butumites[1329].
2 CONT
2 CONT Albert of Aix records that "Hugonem Magnum fratrem regis Franciæ, Drogonem et Clareboldum" were held in chains in prison by the emperor at Constantinople but were released after the intervention of "Baldewinus Hainaucorum comes et
2 CONC  Heinricus de Ascha" who were sent as envoys by Godefroi de Bouillon[1330].
2 CONT
2 CONT Albert of Aix records that "Hugo, Drogo, Willelmus Carpentarius et Clareboldus" joined the army of Godefroi de Bouillon after their release from captivity in Constantinople, dated to end 1096[1331]. He took part in the siege of An
2 CONC tioch in 1098. He was sent on a mission to Emperor Alexios I, but was surprised in a Turkish ambush near Nikaia but escaped with his life[1332].
2 CONT
2 CONT He returned to France after the capture of Antioch in 1098 to raise another army which he led as part of the second wave of the First Crusade, leaving France in Mar 1101.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert of Torigny records the death in 1102 of "Hugo Magnus apud Tarsum"[1333]. He died from wounds received fighting the Turks near Tarsus in Asia Minor[1334].
2 CONT
2 CONT m (after 1067) as her first husband, ADELAIS Ctss de Vermandois, de Valois et de Crépy, daughter and heiress of HERIBERT [IV] Comte de Vermandois & his wife Adelais de Valois ([1065]-28 Sep [1120/24]).
2 CONT
2 CONT The Genealogiæ Scriptoris Fusniacensis names "Adelaide comitissa Veromandensium" as wife of "Hugonem Magnum"[1335]. Her husband left her as regent in Vermandois when he left on crusade.
2 CONT
2 CONT She married secondly (1103) as his first wife, Renaud de Clermont. "Adela…Viromandorum comitissa, filius…meus Radulphus" renounced their claim to certain serfs in favour of the abbey of Compiègne Saint-Corneille, with the consent 
2 CONC of "filiorum meorum Radulphi, Henrici, Symonis", by charter dated 1114[1336].
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1117, Louis VI "le Gros" King of France restored to her the county of Amiens which had been usurped by Thomas de Marle[1337].
2 CONT
2 CONT Comte Hugues & his wife had nine children:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Mathilde de Vermandois (married as second wife Raoul, Seigneur de Baugency)
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Agnes de Vermandois (b. c.1085, d. after 1127, m. as second wife Bonifacio di Saluzzo, Marchese del Vasto)
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Constance de Vermandois (d. after 1118, m. Godefroi de la Ferte-Gaucher, Vicomte de Meaux)
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Isabelle/Elisabeth de Vermandois (b. before 1088, d. 17 February 1131, buried Lewes, OUR ANCESTOR, m. firstly Robert de Beaumont, Comte de Meulan, secondly William de Warenne, Earl of Surrey, OUR ANCESTOR)
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. Raoul I "le Vaillant" de Vermandois (b. c.1094, d. 13 October 1152, buried St-Arnould in Crepy, succeeded as Comte de Vermandois)
2 CONT
2 CONT 6. Henri de Vermandois (d. 1130, Seigneur de Chaumont-en-Vexin)
2 CONT
2 CONT 7. Simon de Vermandois (d. 10 February 1148 in Seleukia returning from second crusade, buried Cistercian Abbey of Notre-Dame-de-l'Assomption, Ourscamp)
2 CONT
2 CONT 8. Guillaume de Vermandois (d. after 1096, parentage uncertain)
2 CONT
2 CONT 9. Beatrix de Vermandois (d. after 1144, married Hugues IV, Seigneur de Gourney-en-Bray)
2 CONT
