1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Sandal Castle (Wakefield) was built in the early 12th century by William, second Earl Warenne, after receiving the manor of Wakefield from Henry I for his support at the battle of Tinchebrai in 1106. 
2 CONT Source: https://www.wakefieldhistoricalsociety.org.uk/projects/pontefract-to-fotheringhay/history/sandal-castle/
2 CONT
2 CONT http://genealogics.org/getperson.php?personID=I00015374&tree=LEO
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Warenne-25
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.familysearch.org/tree/person/details/L8MB-C1W
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Foundation for Medieval Genealogy page on English Earls:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ENGLISH%20NOBILITY%20MEDIEVAL.htm#_Toc186716618
2 CONT
2 CONT WILLIAM de Warenne, son of WILLIAM de Warenne Earl of Surrey & his first wife Gundred --- (-[11 May] 1138, bur Lewes Priory).  
2 CONT
2 CONT His parentage is specified by Orderic Vitalis[979].  "Willelmo et Reynaldo filiis et heredibus meis" are named in the charter of "Willelmus de Warenna…Surreie comes" dated 1080[980].  He succeeded his father in 1088 as Earl of Sur
2 CONC rey, though usually styled Earl de Warenne.  
2 CONT
2 CONT He supported Robert Duke of Normandy in 1101 against his brother Henry I King of England, who confiscated his lands in England in consequence although they were restored in 1103[981].  
2 CONT
2 CONT "…Guillaume comte de Varennes…" witnessed the undated charter under which Robert III Duke of Normandy donated property to Saint-Etienne de Caen[982].  “W comes de Warenna et Isabella comitissa uxor mea necnon filii nostri Willelmu
2 CONC s…et Radulfus” donated property to Castle Acre Priory by undated charter[983].  
2 CONT
2 CONT m (1118) as her second husband, ISABELLE [Elisabeth] de Vermandois, widow of ROBERT de Beaumont Comte de Meulan, Earl of Leicester, daughter of HUGUES I "le Maisné" Comte de Vermandois et de Valois [Capet] & his wife Adelais Ctss 
2 CONC de Vermandois [Carolingian] ([before 1088][984]-17 Feb 1131, bur Lewes Priory).  
2 CONT
2 CONT Guillaume de Jumièges names "Elisabeth fille de Hugues-le-Grand comte de Vermandois" as wife of "Guillaume II de Warenne comte de Surrey", specifying that her previous husband had been "Robert comte de Meulan" by whom she had thre
2 CONC e sons and three daughters[985].  
2 CONT
2 CONT William de Garenne donated property to St Faith, Longueville by charter dated to [1130], witnessed by "Ysabel comitissa uxor comitis et Willelmo et Radulfo filii eorum"[986].  “W comes de Warenna et Isabella comitissa uxor mea nec
2 CONC non filii nostril Willelmus…et Radulfus” donated property to Castle Acre Priory by undated charter[987].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Earl William & his wife had five children:  
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. William de Warenne (1119-19 January 1148, Killed in the Battle of Laodicea in the Second Crusade).
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Gundred (b. 1120 or after, d. after 1166, OUR ANCESTOR)
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Ralph (d. after 1130)
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Ada de Warenne (d. 1178, married Henry King of Scotland, Earl of Huntingdon, son of David King of Scotland, 1115-12 June 1152, buried Kelso Abbey, Roxburghshire)
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. Rainald de Warenne (d. 1179, married Alice de Wormegay)
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Wikipedia page on William de Warenne, 2nd Earl of Surrey:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_de_Warenne,_2nd_Earl_of_Surrey
2 CONT
2 CONT William de Warenne, 2nd Earl of Surrey (died 1138), was the son of William de Warenne, 1st Earl of Surrey and his first wife Gundred. He is more often referred to as Earl Warenne or Earl of Warenne than as Earl of Surrey.
2 CONT
2 CONT In January 1091, William assisted Hugh of Grantmesnil (d.1094) in his defense of Courcy against the forces of Robert de Belleme and Duke Robert [1].
2 CONT
2 CONT Sometime around 1093 he tried to marry Matilda (or Edith), daughter of king Malcolm III of Scotland. She instead married Henry I of England, and this may be the cause of William's great dislike of Henry I, which was to be his appa
2 CONC rent motivator in the following years.
2 CONT
2 CONT He accompanied Robert Curthose (Duke Robert) in his 1101 invasion of England, and afterwards lost his English lands and titles and was exiled to Normandy[2]. There he complained to Curthose that he expended great effort on the duk
2 CONC e's behalf and had in return lost most of his possessions. Curthose's return to England in 1103 was apparently made to convince his brother to restore William's earldom. This was successful, though Curthose had to give up all he h
2 CONC ad received after the 1101 invasion, and subsequently William was loyal to Henry.
2 CONT
2 CONT To further insure William's loyalty Henry considered marrying him to one of his many illegitimate daughters. He was however dissuaded by Archbishop Anselm of Canterbury, for any of the daughters would have been within the prohibit
2 CONC ed degrees of consanguinity. The precise nature of the consanguineous relationship Anselm had in mind has been much debated, but it is most likely he was referring to common descent from the father of duchess Gunnor.
2 CONT
2 CONT William was one of the commanders on Henry's side (against Robert Curthose) at the Battle of Tinchebray in 1106. Afterwards, with his loyalty thus proven, he became more prominent in Henry's court.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1110, Curthose's son William Clito escaped along with Helias of Saint-Saens, and afterwards Warenne received the forfeited Saint-Saens lands, which were very near his own in upper Normandy. By this maneuver king Henry further a
2 CONC ssured his loyalty, for the successful return of Clito would mean at the very least Warenne's loss of this new territory.
2 CONT
2 CONT He fought at the Battle of Bremule in 1119 [3], and was at Henry's deathbed in 1135.
2 CONT
2 CONT William's death is recorded as 11-May-1138 in the register of Lewes priory and he was buried with his father at the chapter-house there.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1118 William acquired the royal-blooded bride he desired when married Elizabeth de Vermandois. She was a daughter of count Hugh of Vermandois, a son of Henry I of France, and was the widow of Robert de Beaumont, 1st Earl of Lei
2 CONC cester.
2 CONT
2 CONT By Elizabeth he had three sons and two daughters:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. William de Warenne, 3rd Earl of Surrey;
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Reginald de Warenne, who inherited his father's property in upper Normandy, including the castles of Bellencombre and Mortemer[4] He married Adeline, daughter of William, lord of Wormgay in Norfolk, by whom he had a son William
2 CONC  (founder of the priory of Wormegay), whose daughter and sole heir Beatrice married first Dodo, lord Bardolf, and secondly Hubert de Burgh; Reginald was one of the persecutors of Archbishop Thomas in 1170.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Ralph de Warenne
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Gundrada de Warenne, who married first Roger de Beaumont, 2nd Earl of Warwick, and second William, lord of Kendal, and is most remembered for expelling king Stephen's garrison from Warwick Castle 
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. Ada de Warenne, who married Henry of Scotland, 3rd Earl of Huntingdon who made many grants to the priory of Lewes.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Footnotes
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.^ Orderic Vitalis, p. 692
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.^ Orderic Vitalis p.785
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.^ Orderic Vitalis p.853-4
2 CONT
2 CONT 4.^ Faedera, i.18.
2 CONT
2 CONT 5.^ Manuscript Register of Lewes
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT C. Warren Hollister, "The Taming of a Turbulent Earl: Henry I and William of Warenne", Historical Reflections 3 (1976) 83-91
2 CONT
2 CONT C. Warren Hollister, Henry I (2001)
2 CONT
2 CONT Warren Family History Project
2 CONT
2 CONT The Ecclesiastical History of Orderic Vitalis, ed. M. Chibnall, vol. 2, p. 264 (Oxford, 1990).
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT WILLIAM II DE WARREN, 2ND EARL OF SURREY  of Sussex, England, son of  William, Earl and Gundred, Countess of Surrey, was born circa 1081, died on 11 May 1138 in England and was buried in Priory Of Lewes, Lewes, Sussex, England. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He married in France, between 1108 and 1118, COUNTESS ISABEL (ELIZABETH) DE VERMANDOIS of Valois, Bretagne, France, daughter of Duke Hugh Crepi and Countess Adelaide (de VERMANDOIS), who was born circa 1081, was christened in 1131
2 CONC , died on 13 Feb. 1131 in England, and was buried in Lewes, Sussex, England.
2 CONT
2 CONT "The Royal Line" chart erroneously(?) has him as the son of Gundred and grandson of William the Conqueror. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT WILLIAM III4 DE WARENNE, 3RD EARL OF SURREY, b. in June 1118, d. on 19 Jan. 1148 in Laodicea, Syria; m. (AAH-14) ELA TALVAS. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ADA of Huntingdon, Huntingdonshire, England, b. circa 1104, d. in 1178; m. (E-17) HENRY DE HUNTINGDON, EARL in 1139. 
2 CONT
2 CONT GUNDRED DE WARENNE of Warwick, Warwickshire, England, b. between 1107 and 1127, d. after 1167 in Warwickshire; m. (1) ROGER DE NEWBURGH, 2ND EARL OF WARWICK before 1130; m. (2) WILLIAM DE LANCASTER I, 5TH BARON KENDAL circa 1154. 
2 CONT
2 CONT REGINALD DE WARENNE (WARREN) of Vermandois, Normandy, France, b. circa 1113. 
2 CONT
2 CONT RALPH DE WARENNE (WARREN) of Vermandois, Normandy, France, b. circa 1115. 
2 CONT
