2 CONT Fought at Tenchefrai, 1106 for King Henry I
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT He accompanied Robert Curthose in his 1101 invasion of England, and afterwards lost his English lands and titles and was exiled to Normandy. There he complained to Curthose that he expended great effort in the duke's behalf and ha
2 CONC d in return lost most of his possessions. Curthose's return to England in 1103 was apparently made to convince his brother to restore William's earldom. This was successful, though Curthose had to give up he had received after the
2 CONC  1101 invasion, and subsequently William was loyal to king Henry.
2 CONT
2 CONT To further insure William's loyalty Henry considered marrying him to one of his many illegitimate daughters. He was however dissuaded by Archbishop Anselm of Canterbury, for any of the daughters would have been within the prohibit
2 CONC ed degrees of consanguinity. The precise nature of the consanguinous relationship Anselm had in mind has been much debated, but it is most likely he was referring to common descent from the father of duchess Gunnor.
2 CONT
2 CONT William was one of the commanders on Henry's side (against Robert Curthose) at the Battle of Tinchebray in 1106. Afterwards, with his loyalty thus proven, he became more prominent in Henry's court.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://thepeerage.com/p17627.htm#i176268
2 CONT
2 CONT William II de Warenne, 2nd Earl of Surrey1
2 CONT
2 CONT M, #176268, d. circa 11 May 1138
2 CONT
2 CONT Last Edited=28 Dec 2009
2 CONT
2 CONT William II de Warenne, 2nd Earl of Surrey was the son of William I de Warenne, 1st Earl of Surrey and Gundreda (?).1 He married Elizabeth de Vermandois, daughter of Hugh de Crépi, Comte de Vermandois et de Valois and Aelis de Verm
2 CONC andois, Comtesse de Vermandois, after 5 June 1118.2 He died circa 11 May 1138.1
2 CONT
2 CONT William II de Warenne, 2nd Earl of Surrey succeeded to the title of 2nd Earl of Surrey [E., 1088] in 1088.1
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of William II de Warenne, 2nd Earl of Surrey and Elizabeth de Vermandois
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Ada de Warenne+3 d. c 1178
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Reginald de Warenne+4
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. William III de Warenne, 3rd Earl of Surrey+1 b. c 1119, d. 19 Jan 1147/48
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Gundred de Warenne+4 b. c 1120, d. 1166
2 CONT
2 CONT Citations
2 CONT
2 CONT [S6] G.E. Cokayne; with Vicary Gibbs, H.A. Doubleday, Geoffrey H. White, Duncan Warrand and Lord Howard de Walden, editors, The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain and the United Kingdom, Extant, Extinct 
2 CONC or Dormant, new ed., 13 volumes in 14 (1910-1959; reprint in 6 volumes, Gloucester, U.K.: Alan Sutton Publishing, 2000), volume XII/1, page 496. Hereinafter cited as The Complete Peerage.
2 CONT
2 CONT [S6] Cokayne, and others, The Complete Peerage, volume XII/1, page 495.
2 CONT
2 CONT [S11] Alison Weir, Britain's Royal Family: A Complete Genealogy (London, U.K.: The Bodley Head, 1999), page 192. Hereinafter cited as Britain's Royal Family.
2 CONT
2 CONT [S22] Sir Bernard Burke, C.B. LL.D., A Genealogical History of the Dormant, Abeyant, Forfeited, and Extinct Peerages of the British Empire, new edition (1883; reprint, Baltimore, Maryland: Genealogical Publishing Company, 1978), p
2 CONC age 569. Hereinafter cited as Burkes Extinct Peerage.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT William II de Warenne (Guillaume de Varennes), 2nd Earl of Surrey, assisted Hugh of Grantmesnil (d.1094) in his defense of Courcy against the forces of Robert de Belleme and Duke Robert. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sometime around 1093 he tried to marry Matilda (or Edith), daughter of king Malcolm III of Scotland. She instead married King Henry I of England, and this may be the cause of William's great dislike of the King, which was to be hi
2 CONC s apparent motivator in the following years.
2 CONT
2 CONT He accompanied Robert Curthose (Duke Robert) in his 1101 invasion of England, and afterward lost his English lands and titles and was exiled to Normandy. There he complained to Curthose that he expended great effort on the Duke's 
2 CONC behalf and had in return lost most of his possessions. Curthose's return to England in 1103 was apparently made to convince his brother to restore William's earldom. This was successful (though Curthose had to give up all he had r
2 CONC eceived after the 1101 invasion). Subsequently William was loyal to Henry.
2 CONT
2 CONT To further insure William's loyalty Henry considered marrying him to one of his many illegitimate daughters. He was however dissuaded by Archbishop Anselm of Canterbury, for any of the daughters would have been within the prohibit
2 CONC ed degrees of consanguinity. The precise nature of the consanguineous relationship Anselm had in mind has been much debated, but it is most likely he was referring to common descent from the father of duchess Gunnor.
2 CONT
2 CONT William was one of the commanders on Henry's side (against Robert Curthose) at the Battle of Tinchebray in 1106. Afterward, with his loyalty thus proven, he became more prominent in Henry's court.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1110, Curthose's son William Clito escaped along with Helias of Saint-Saens, and afterward Warenne received the forfeited Saint-Saens lands, which were very near his own in upper Normandy. By this maneuver king Henry further as
2 CONC sured his loyalty, for the successful return of Clito would mean at the very least Warenne's loss of this new territory.
2 CONT
2 CONT He fought at the Battle of Bremule in 1119, and he was at Henry's deathbed in 1135.
2 CONT
2 CONT William was our ancestor through three distinct descent lines--through his daughter Ada, his daughter Gundred, and his son William, each of whom was independently our ancestor.
2 CONT
2 CONT See http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_de_Warenne,_2nd_Earl_of_Surrey for more information.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT William de Warenne, 2nd Earl of Surrey (died 1138), was the son of William de Warenne, 1st Earl of Surrey and his first wife Gundred. He is more often referred to as Earl Warenne or Earl of Warenne than as Earl of Surrey.
2 CONT
2 CONT In January 1091, William assisted Hugh of Grantmesnil (d.1094) in his defense of Courcy against the forces of Robert de Belleme and Duke Robert [1].
2 CONT
2 CONT Sometime around 1093 he tried to marry Matilda (or Edith), daughter of king Malcolm III of Scotland. She instead married Henry I of England, and this may be the cause of William's great dislike of Henry I, which was to be his appa
2 CONC rent motivator in the following years.
2 CONT
2 CONT He accompanied Robert Curthose (Duke Robert) in his 1101 invasion of England, and afterwards lost his English lands and titles and was exiled to Normandy[2]. There he complained to Curthose that he expended great effort on the duk
2 CONC e's behalf and had in return lost most of his possessions. Curthose's return to England in 1103 was apparently made to convince his brother to restore William's earldom. This was successful, though Curthose had to give up all he h
2 CONC ad received after the 1101 invasion, and subsequently William was loyal to Henry.
2 CONT
2 CONT To further insure William's loyalty Henry considered marrying him to one of his many illegitimate daughters. He was however dissuaded by Archbishop Anselm of Canterbury, for any of the daughters would have been within the prohibit
2 CONC ed degrees of consanguinity. The precise nature of the consanguineous relationship Anselm had in mind has been much debated, but it is most likely he was referring to common descent from the father of duchess Gunnor.
2 CONT
2 CONT William was one of the commanders on Henry's side (against Robert Curthose) at the Battle of Tinchebray in 1106. Afterwards, with his loyalty thus proven, he became more prominent in Henry's court.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1110, Curthose's son William Clito escaped along with Helias of Saint-Saens, and afterwards Warenne received the forfeited Saint-Saens lands, which were very near his own in upper Normandy. By this maneuver king Henry further a
2 CONC ssured his loyalty, for the successful return of Clito would mean at the very least Warenne's loss of this new territory.
2 CONT
2 CONT He fought at the Battle of Bremule in 1119 [3], and was at Henry's deathbed in 1135.
2 CONT
2 CONT William's death is recorded as 11-May-1138 in the register of Lewes priory and he was buried with his father at the chapter-house there.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1118 William acquired the royal-blooded bride he desired when married Elizabeth de Vermandois. She was a daughter of count Hugh of Vermandois, a son of Henry I of France, and was the widow of Robert de Beaumont, 1st Earl of Lei
2 CONC cester.
2 CONT
2 CONT By Elizabeth he had three sons and two daughters:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * William de Warenne, 3rd Earl of Surrey;
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Reginald de Warenne, who inherited his father's property in upper Normandy, including the castles of Bellencombre and Mortemer[4] He married Adeline, daughter of William, lord of Wormgay in Norfolk, by whom he had a son Will
2 CONC iam (founder of the priory of Wormegay), whose daughter and sole heir Beatrice married first Dodo, lord Bardolf, and secondly Hubert de Burgh; Reginald was one of the persecutors of Archbishop Thomas in 1170.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Ralph de Warenne
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Gundrada de Warenne, who married first Roger de Beaumont, 2nd Earl of Warwick, and second William, lord of Kendal, and is most remembered for expelling king Stephen's garrison from Warwick Castle;
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Ada de Warenne, who married Henry of Scotland, 3rd Earl of Huntingdon who made many grants to the priory of Lewes.
2 CONT
