2 CONT Page 117 Memoirs of the Ancient Earl os Warren & Surrey
2 CONT
2 CONT William de Warren married Isabel with the consent of the king his father. Her blood indeed was so noble, that the match was hardly unequal, for her father was related to the Normand kings of England, and her mother was a daughter 
2 CONC of Hugh the great, earl of Vermandois, and second brother to Philip I. king of France. By the said Henry, this Ada had three sons, and as many daughters, viz. Malcolme, and William,* both kings of Scotland, and David earl of Hunti
2 CONC ngton, &c. Ada, who was married to Floris earl of Holland; Margaret to Conan le Petit earl of Britany; and Maud, who died young. Ada the mother died in 1178.
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabel, countess of Warren, died Feb. 13, 1131. The earl died May 11, 1138, having enjoyed the title near fifty years, and was buried at his father's feet in the chapter house of Lewes.
2 CONT
2 CONT This William, when earl of Northumberland, had so high an opinion of his mother's family, that he called himself William de Warren, as may be seen in a charter of his Brinkeburne priory in Dugdale's Monast. vol. II. p. 203. Rouen 
2 CONC William Rufus gives his father the Title of First Earl
2 CONT
2 CONT The widow is Countess of Meuilent
2 CONT
2 CONT 2nd Earl of Surrey. He commanded the 3rd Division of the Kings Army in the Battles of Tinchbray and Brenneville and was named the Governor of Rouen. He married Isabel de Vermandois, daughter of Hugh the Great, king of France
2 CONT
2 CONT It is through Isabel de Vermandois that the lineage goes back to CHARLEMAGNE
2 CONT
2 CONT c 1088 William de Warenne, 2nd Earl, son of the 1st Earl. To the Priory at Lewes, founded by his parents, he gave the 'living' and income from the Church at Conisbrough together with the Churches at Braithwell, Dinnington, Harthil
2 CONC l, Fishlake and Hatfield and the Chapels of Thorne and Armthorpe. He gave the tithe of eels from his fisheries at Hatfield to the Abbey of Roche.
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: According to Ancestral Roots, Isabel preceeded William in death in 13 Feb 1130/31--not July 1147.
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: (l) He had encouraged Henry to fight when William (de Tancarville) the Chamberlain urged him to retreat. His alleged speech to the King before the battle is given in "Chron. Men de Hida", pp. 316-7. Note: (c) According to He
2 CONC nry of Huntingdon [their daughter Ada's husband], the death of Isabel's 1st husband was hastened by an (unnamed) Earl carrying her off, by force or fraud. The truth of this is open to question. Note: (g) He left 3 sons: William - 
2 CONC 3rd Earl, Ralph, and Rainald, ancestor of the Warennes of Wormegay; and 2 daughters: (1) Gundred, who m. 1st Roger de Beaumont, 2nd Earl of Warwick; 2ndly, (as his 2nd wife), William de Lancaster; (2) Ada, who m. Henry, Earl of Hu
2 CONC ntingdon, s. of David I, King of Scotland, by whom she was mother of Malcolm IV and William the Lion, Kings of Scotland. Note: (h) Before her son William, 3rd Earl, went on crusade in June 1147. Note: Note: <nowiki>---------------
2 CONC -----------------------------------------------------------</nowiki> This nobleman, William de Warrenne (Earl of Warrenne), 2nd Earl of Surrey, joined Robert de Belesmé, Earl of Arundel and Shrewsbury, in favour of Robert Curthose
2 CONC  against Henry I, and in consequence forfeited his English earldom and estates, but those were subsequently restored to him and he was ever afterwards a good and faithful subject to King Henry. His lordship m. Isabel, dau. of Hugh
2 CONC  the Great, Earl of Vermandois, and widow of Robert, Earl of Mellent, by whom he had issue, William, Reginald, Ralph, Gundred, and Adeline. The earl d. 11 May, 1138, and was s. by his eldest son, William de Warrenne, 3rd Earl of S
2 CONC urrey. [Sir Bernard Burke, Dormant, Abeyant, Forfeited, and Extinct Peerages, Burke's Peerage, Ltd., London, 1883, p. 569, Warren, Earls of Surrey] Title: Magna Charta Sureties 1215, Frederick Lewis Weis, additions by Walter Lee S
2 CONC heppard Jr, 5th Edition, 1999
2 CONT
2 CONT Page: 155-1
2 CONT
2 CONT Title: Burke's Peerage & Baronetage, 106th Edition, Charles Mosley Editor-in-Chief, 1999
2 CONT
2 CONT Page: 2944
2 CONT
2 CONT Title: Complete Peerage of England Scotland Ireland Great Britain and the United Kingdom, by G. E Cokayne, Sutton Publishing Ltd, 2000
2 CONT
2 CONT Page: XII/1:495-6
2 CONT
2 CONT Title: The Plantagenet Ancestry, by William Henry Turton, 1968
2 CONT
2 CONT Page: 112
2 CONT
2 CONT ____________
2 CONT
2 CONT Another source,
2 CONT
2 CONT 2nd Earl of Warren and 2nd Earl of Surrey, joined Robert de Belesme, Earl of Arundel and Shrewsbury, in favor of Robert Curthose, against King Henry I., and in consequence forfeited his English earldom and estates; but those were 
2 CONC subsequently restored to him, and he was ever afterwards a good and faithful subject to King Henry. He married Isabel Vermandois, Countess of Leicester, daughter of Hugh the Great, Earl of Vermandois, and Alice, his wife, daughter
2 CONC  of Hubert, 4th Count de Vermandois, son of Henry, 3rd Count de Vermandois, by his wife, Edgina, daughter of Edward the Elder, King of England, son of Alfred the Great, King of England. Isabel was also the widow of Robert, Earl of
2 CONC  Mellent, and granddaughter of King Henry I of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT ________
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Wikipedia
2 CONT
2 CONT He is more often referred to as Earl Warenne or Earl of Warenne than as Earl of Surrey.
2 CONT
2 CONT In January 1091, William assisted Hugh of Grantmesnil (d.1094) in his defense of Courcy against the forces of Robert de Belleme and Duke Robert [1].
2 CONT
2 CONT Sometime around 1093 he tried to marry Matilda (or Edith), daughter of king Malcolm III of Scotland. She instead married Henry I of England, and this may be the cause of William's great dislike of Henry I, which was to be his appa
2 CONC rent motivator in the following years.
2 CONT
2 CONT He accompanied Robert Curthose (Duke Robert) in his 1101 invasion of England, and afterwards lost his English lands and titles and was exiled to Normandy[2]. There he complained to Curthose that he expended great effort on the duk
2 CONC e's behalf and had in return lost most of his possessions. Curthose's return to England in 1103 was apparently made to convince his brother to restore William's earldom. This was successful, though Curthose had to give up all he h
2 CONC ad received after the 1101 invasion, and subsequently William was loyal to Henry.
2 CONT
2 CONT To further insure William's loyalty Henry considered marrying him to one of his many illegitimate daughters. He was however dissuaded by Archbishop Anselm of Canterbury, for any of the daughters would have been within the prohibit
2 CONC ed degrees of consanguinity. The precise nature of the consanguineous relationship Anselm had in mind has been much debated, but it is most likely he was referring to common descent from the father of duchess Gunnor.
2 CONT
2 CONT William was one of the commanders on Henry's side (against Robert Curthose) at the Battle of Tinchebray in 1106. Afterwards, with his loyalty thus proven, he became more prominent in Henry's court.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1110, Curthose's son William Clito escaped along with Helias of Saint-Saens, and afterwards Warenne received the forfeited Saint-Saens lands, which were very near his own in upper Normandy. By this maneuver king Henry further a
2 CONC ssured his loyalty, for the successful return of Clito would mean at the very least Warenne's loss of this new territory.
2 CONT
2 CONT He fought at the Battle of Bremule in 1119 [3], and was at Henry's deathbed in 1135.
2 CONT
2 CONT William's death is recorded as 11-May-1138 in the register of Lewes priory and he was buried with his father at the chapter-house there.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1118 William acquired the royal-blooded bride he desired when married Elizabeth de Vermandois. She was a daughter of count Hugh of Vermandois, a son of Henry I of France, and was the widow of Robert de Beaumont, 1st Earl of Lei
2 CONC cester.
2 CONT
2 CONT By Elizabeth he had three sons and two daughters:
2 CONT
2 CONT William de Warenne, 3rd Earl of Surrey;
2 CONT
2 CONT Reginald de Warenne, who inherited his father's property in upper Normandy, including the castles of Bellencombre and Mortemer[4] He married Adeline, daughter of William, lord of Wormgay in Norfolk, by whom he had a son William (f
2 CONC ounder of the priory of Wormegay), whose daughter and sole heir Beatrice married first Dodo, lord Bardolf, and secondly Hubert de Burgh; Reginald was one of the persecutors of Archbishop Thomas in 1170.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ralph de Warenne
2 CONT
2 CONT Gundrada de Warenne, who married first Roger de Beaumont, 2nd Earl of Warwick, and second William, lord of Kendal, and is most remembered for expelling king Stephen's garrison from Warwick Castle;
2 CONT
2 CONT Ada de Warenne, who married Henry of Scotland, 3rd Earl of Huntingdon who made many grants to the priory of Lewes
2 CONT
