2 CONT '''fr.Wikipédia''' (Auto-translated French): 
2 CONT
2 CONT "...In 1033, Onfroy de Vieilles, lord of Beaumont and Pont-Audemer, decided to rebuild the monastery plundered by the Normans. From 1035, monastic life resumed with the arrival of six monks from Fontenelle under the direction of A
2 CONC bbot Eimard. In 1040, Aubrée, wife of Onfroy, established a monastery of nuns at Saint-Michel-de-Préaux dedicated to Saint Léger.... At the end of his life, Onfroy de Vieilles took the monastic habit and ended his days at the abbe
2 CONC y, as did his son Roger de Beaumont in 1094. They were buried there." — [https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abbaye_Saint-Pierre_de_Préaux fr.Wikipédia "Abbaye Saint-Pierre de Préaux"].
2 CONT
2 CONT ====Wikipedia Vitals====
2 CONT
2 CONT * fr.Wikipédia, [https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Onfroi_de_Vieilles '''Onfroi de Vielles'''], dernière modification 28 juillet 2022.
2 CONT * en.Wikipedia [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Humphrey_de_Vieilles '''Humphrey de Vielles'''], updated 15 July 2022.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Humphrey de Vieilles[1] (died c. 1050) was the first holder of the "grand honneur" of Beaumont-le-Roger, one of the most important groups of domains in eastern Normandy[2].... He was married to Albreda or Alberée de la Haye Auber
2 CONC ie.
2 CONT
2 CONT "His early life and origins are the subject of much discussion. As reported by later Norman chronicler Robert of Torigni, he was the son of Thorold de Pont-Audemer and grandson of a Torf, from whose name derived that of the villag
2 CONC e of Tourville-sur-Pont-Audemer.[3] Humphrey's mother, according to Robert of Torigni, was Duvelina, sister of Gunnor, concubine of Richard I, Duke of Normandy....."
2 CONT
2 CONT [''For more biographical information, please click one of the above links.'']
2 CONT
2 CONT ====Family and Descendants====
2 CONT
2 CONT >"His known children by his wife Albreda or Alberée de la Haye were:
2 CONT >>'''1.''' Robert, the elder, assassinated by Roger de Clères[6] after 1066 and buried at the abbey of Saint-Pierre de Préaux;[7]
2 CONT >>'''2.''' Roger de Beaumont, known as le Barbe († 1094), who succeeded his father.
2 CONT >>'''3.''' Henri de Beaumont fights Roger de Toesny with his brother Roger, sent by his father in 1040.[8]
2 CONT >>'''4.''' Dunelma (perhaps a corrupted form of Duvelina, the name of her grandmother) sister of Roger of Beaumont and mother of a daughter who was a nun at Saint-Léger de Préaux
2 CONT >One other possible child:  Guillaume de Beaumont, Monk at the abbey of Saint-Pierre de Préaux"
2 CONT
2 CONT ====Notes and references====
2 CONT
2 CONT # Vieilles is the name of a former village, now merged with Beaumont-le-Roger
2 CONT # Pierre Bauduin, La première Normandie (Xe-XIe siècles), Presses Universitaires de Caen, 2004, p.216-217. Among the other grands honneurs of the Pays d'Ouche, were those of Breteuil and of Conches
2 CONT # William of Jumièges reports that this Turolf was the brother of Turquetil, the first lord of Harcourt, and the uncle of Ansketil de Harcourt. The archaeologist Jacques Le Maho supports Torf's identification with Turstin
2 CONT # Veronica Gazeau, Monachisme et aristocratie au XIe siècle : l'exemple de la famille de Beaumont,, PhD thesis, University of Caen, 1986-1987 (dactyl.), p.67-73. The abbot of Bernay, Raoul, parent of Humphrey, would have entrusted
2 CONC  to him between 1027 and 1040, part of the heritage of his monastery. Like other lords of the beginning of the 11th century, like the family of Bellême, he increased the family's power by recovering or winning of ecclesiastical la
2 CONC nds
2 CONT # Orderic Vitalis, History of Normandy, Éd. Guizot, 1826, vol. III, livre VIII, p. 373. Charpillon et Caresme, Dictionnaire historique des communes de l'Eure, vol I, 1879, art. Beaumont-le-Roger
2 CONT
2 CONT >'''Sources'''
2 CONT >i)  (French) Pierre Bauduin, La première Normandie (Xe-XIe siècles), Presses Universitaires de Caen, 2004
2 CONT >ii)  (French) Véronique Gazeau, Monachisme et aristocratie au XIe siècle : l'exemple de la famille de Beaumont, doctoral thesis, Université de Caen, 1986-1987 (dactyl.)
2 CONT >iii) Seigneurs de Beaumont-le-Roger on Medieval Lands
