2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From the English Wikipedia page on Anne of Kiev:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anne_of_Kiev
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne of Kiev or Anna Yaroslavna (between 1024 and 1032 – 1075), daughter of Yaroslav I of Kiev and his wife Princess Ingegerd of Sweden, was the queen consort of France as the wife of Henry I, and regent for her son Philip I.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of his first wife, Matilda, King Henry searched the courts of Europe for a suitable bride, but could not locate a princess who was not related to him within illegal degrees of kinship. At last he sent an embassy to
2 CONC  distant Kiev, which returned with Anne (also called Agnes or Anna).
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne and Henry were married at the cathedral of Reims on 19 May 1051.
2 CONT
2 CONT As a curious fact, several daughters of Yaroslav got married with catholic occidental monarchs; Anne's older sister Anastasia of Kiev, got married around 1039 with the king Andrew I of Hungary.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT
2 CONT With Henry I of France:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Philip (23 May 1052 – 30 July 1108) - Anne is credited with bringing the name Philip to Western Europe. She imported this Greek name (Philippos, from philos (love) and hippos (horse), meaning "the one that love horses") from he
2 CONC r Eastern Orthodox culture.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Hugh (1057 – 18 October 1102) - called the Great or Magnus, later Count of Crépi, who married the heiress of Vermandois and died on crusade in Tarsus, Cilicia.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Robert (c. 1055–c. 1060)
2 CONT
2 CONT Regency
2 CONT
2 CONT For six years after Henry's death in 1060, she served as regent for Philip, who was only seven at the time. She was the first queen of France to serve as regent. Her co-regent was Count Baldwin V of Flanders. Anne was a literate w
2 CONC oman, rare for the time, but there was some opposition to her as regent on the grounds that her mastery of French was less than fluent.
2 CONT
2 CONT A year after the king's death, Anne, acting as regent, took a passionate fancy for Count Ralph III of Valois, a man whose political ambition encouraged him to repudiate his wife to marry Anne in 1062. Accused of adultery, Ralph's 
2 CONC wife appealed to Pope Alexander II, who excommunicated the couple.
2 CONT
2 CONT The young king Philip forgave his mother, which was just as well, since he was to find himself in a very similar predicament in the 1090s.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ralph died in September 1074, at which time Anne returned to the French court. She died in 1075, was buried at Villiers Abbey, La-Ferte-Alais, Essonne and her obits were celebrated on 5 September.
2 CONT
2 CONT Note
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1717, Tsar Peter the Great stopped in the cathedral in Reims where the French monarchs were crowned. He was shown the missal on which all French kings since the 11th century swore their coronation oaths. To everyone's surprise,
2 CONC  he began reading from the missal which was written in Old Church Slavonic, the ancestor of all literary Slavonic languages, and used in Christian Orthodox Churches during the services; its function was the same as Latin in Wester
2 CONC n Europe.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna had brought the missal with her from Kiev to the Church where she and Henry had taken their vows. All French monarchs, save the Bonapartes, were crowned after swearing their oaths on it.
2 CONT
2 CONT Autograph of Anne: АNА РЪНNА which is understood either as Ane reine in Old French or Anna regina in Latin; among the oldest samples of the Cyrillic script.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT Bauthier, Robert-Henri. Anne de Kiev reine de France et la politique royale au Xe siècle, revue des Etudes Slaves, Vol. 57, 1985
