2 CONT
2 CONT From Epistolae: Medieval Women's Latin Letters, biography on Anne of Kiev:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://epistolae.ccnmtl.columbia.edu/woman/116.html
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne of Kiev (Anna Yaroslavna), sometimes called Agnes, daughter of Yaroslav I of Kiev and Princess Ingegerd of Sweden; Yaroslav was grand-duke of Kiev and described in medieval histories as king of the Russians “rex Russorum” (Hu
2 CONC gh of Fleury, Aubry des Trois Fontaines, and Raoul Tortaire).
2 CONT
2 CONT Jaroslav’s sister was married to Casimir, king of Poland. Jaroslav and Ingegard had nine children, several of whom were married to royalty: Anastasia to Andrew I of Hungary, Elisabeth to Harold of Norway and later to Sven of Denma
2 CONC rk, Isiaslav to the sister of Casimir of Poland, Vsevolod to a daughter of the Byzantine emperor, Vladimir to a niece of a former queen of France, Matilda of Stade.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne was married in 1051 to Henry I [1], king of France, whose first two wives [2] had died; she was consecrated queen at Reims when Liebert, bishop of Cambray was consecrated. Anne brought no land to the marriage, but did bring c
2 CONC onnections and wealth, probably including a jacinth which Suger later mounted in the reliquary of St. Denis (Bauthier, 550; Hallu,168, citing Comptes de Suger).
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne and Henry had three sons, Philip I [3], born 1052, Robert, born 1054, died young, and Hugh, born 1057, who became count of Vermandois by marriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne is associated in four of Henry’s acts, towards the end of his reign:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. In 1058, a charter of concession to the monastery St. Maur-les-Fosses [4], “annuente mea conjuge Anna et prole Philippo, Roberto ac Hugone” (HGF, 11.600, XXXII); 2. A donation of Villas to the monastery of Hasnon, with the sign
2 CONC atures of Henrici regis, Philippi Regis pueri, A. Reginae (HGF 11.599, XXXI);
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. In 1059, assent to giving restored church to abbey of Coulombs, signed by Henry king, Anna queen his wife (Reginae uxoris ejus), and Philip, son of the king (HGF, 11.604, XXXV); and
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. A concession to St. Martin des Champs, which the king, queen, and son Philip with his brothers confirm with their signatures, “Ego ipse Rex Henricus, et Regina pariter, et Philippus filius meus cum fratribus suis manufirmatam c
2 CONC orroboravimus” (HGF, 11.605-6, XXXVI).
2 CONT
2 CONT But when Henry died in 1160, Anne participated in the government of her son, although Baldwin V of Flanders, married to Henry’s sister, Adele, was technically regent. Baldwin described himself as “procurator et bajulus regni” but 
2 CONC the bishop of Chartres speaks of Philip and Anne as his sovreigns (Prou, document VI, 1060).
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip declares that as a child he took up the kingdom together with his mother (“ego, Philippus, filius ejus [Henrici] admodum parvulus, regnum unacum matre suscepissem,” Prou, XIII, 1061).
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne signed various royal acts during Philip’s reign:
2 CONT
2 CONT I, see Prou,
2 CONT
2 CONT II, reconfirmation of a document of Henry’s confirming a donation to a monastery St. Pere de Chartres,“Philipus rex cum matre regina, hanc cartam firmavit”;
2 CONT
2 CONT III, confirming consent to the donation of a church to the abbey of St Pere, “Concessit autem hoc domnus meus rex Philipus, cum regina matre sua”;
2 CONT
2 CONT IV, gift to the abbey of St. Denis at the request of his paternal aunt, Adele, and the intervention of his mother, “per interventum matris mee A,” who signed it;
2 CONT
2 CONT V, renunciation of certain royal rights over a monastery of St. Lucien de Beauvais, “per interventum matris nostrae,” with her signature;
2 CONT
2 CONT VI, confirmation of a charter to abbot of Marmoutier to build a church, with the advice and will also of our most pious royal lords, “dominorum nostrorum piisimorum regum, Philippi scilicet et matris ejus, Agnetis”;
2 CONT
2 CONT X, donation to St. Nicaise of Reims, with the counsel of our beloved mother “consilioque dilectissime matris nostre Anne”;
2 CONT
2 CONT XI, grants exemption to basilica of St. Adrieu in the presence of the queen “Interfuerunt autem etiam huic adstipulationi Agnes regina, …”;
2 CONT
2 CONT XVI, concession to abbey of St. Crepin, which is signed apparently by Anne’s hand in Cyrillic, Anna queen; XVIII, confirming a gift to the abbey of St. Benoit-sur-Loire, signed Anna regina;
2 CONT
2 CONT XIX, confirmation of his father’s gift to St. Martin-des-Champs, with the good-will of his mother and count Baldwin, “favente matre atque comite Balduino”;
2 CONT
2 CONT XXXII, confirmation of renunciation of certain customs, signed Anna regina; according certain freedoms extended to other royal churches to a church restored by his mother, at her request, “mater mea, nomine Anna, divina inspirante
2 CONC ”;
2 CONT
2 CONT LXXV, confirms the charter founding the monastery of Pontlevoy, signed Anne, matris Philippi regis.
2 CONT
2 CONT Documents II-VI are dated 1060,
2 CONT
2 CONT X, XI, 1061,
2 CONT
2 CONT XVI, 1063;
2 CONT
2 CONT XVIII, XIX,1065,
2 CONT
2 CONT XXXII, 1067,
2 CONT
2 CONT XLIII, 1069,
2 CONT
2 CONT LXXV, 1075.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Anne signs, it is either the first signature listed, or the first following the king’s (in XXXII, several bishops precede the signatures of the king and the queen).
2 CONT
2 CONT There is also a confirmation by Henry of a donation to St. Peter of Chartres, dated 1059, which Philip and his mother signed in 1062, after Henry’s death, “Post mortem autem Henrici Regis, secundo anno regni sui Philippus Rex cum 
2 CONC Regina matre sua … manu propria firmavit” (HGF, 11.602-4, XXXIV). Hallu lists 23 acts of Philip's which mention Anne or carry her signature, four of them probably after her death, 185ff.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1061, Anne remarried to a powerful ally of the king, Raoul, count of Crepy and Valois, the Vexin, Amiens, Bar-sur-Aube, Vitry, Peronne, and Montdidier, who repudiated his wife, accusing her of adultery, to marry Anne. But the w
2 CONC ife, Eleanor of Champagne, appealed to the pope; Alexander II responded to her complaint, asking the archbishop of Reims to investigate, try to save the marriage or take appropriate steps. Raoul refused to take her back and was ex
2 CONC communicated.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nonetheless, he remained an important royal ally, signing some of Philip’s acts. Raoul died in 1074 and Anne returned to Philip’s court.
2 CONT
2 CONT There is one document of Raoul’s which Anne signed along with Raoul’s son, mentioned in HGF 11.433, dated 1069, a concession to a castle.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT Biographical notes:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1 Henry was a son of Robert the Pious and Constance of Arles who preferred her younger son Robert to Henry to succeed his father, but primogeniture prevailed. See Jean Dunbabin, “What’s in a Name? Philip, King of France,” Speculum
2 CONC  68 (1993), 949-68 and Chronica Regum Francorum, HGF 11.393.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT 2 Robert-Henri Bauthier notes that Henry was betrothed first to a Matilda, daughter of Conrad II of Burgundy and Gisela, who died before the marriage was consumated, then to another Matilda, daughter of Liudolf of Stade, granddaug
2 CONC hter of the same Gisela, and niece of Henry III (Anne de Kiev reine de France et la politique royale au Xe siècle, revue des Etudes Slaves, Vol. 57, 1985, 543-45).
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT 3 Anne is said to have introduced the Greek name Philip into the French royal family. Though, as Dunbabin notes, there is no recorded Philip in the Jaroslav family, the choice was probably meant to please her, as the name of an ap
2 CONC ostle important in the Greek tradition.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT 4 According to Hallu, p.72, this document included a prohibition to the king's cooks to make abusive requisitions of cattle for the royal table.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT 5 This biography draws on the following studies of Anne:
2 CONT
2 CONT Bauthier, Robert-Henri. Anne de Kiev reine de France et la politique royale au Xe siècle, revue des Etudes Slaves, Vol. 57, 1985, 539-64.
2 CONT
2 CONT Caix de Saint-Amour, Anne de Russie, reine de France et comtesse de Valois au Xie siecle, 2nd ed. 1896.
2 CONT
2 CONT Fliche, Augustin, Le Regne de Philippe Ier, Roi de France 1060-1108 (1912, repr. Geneva: Slaktkine, 1975).
2 CONT
2 CONT Hallu, Roger, Anne de Kiev, reine de France (Rome: Universita cattolica ucraina, 1973)
2 CONT
2 CONT Prou, Maurice, Recueil des Actes de Philippe I, Roi de France (Paris: Klincksieck, 1908).
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
