2 CONT from &quot;Our Folk&quot; by Albert D Hart, Jr.
1 NOTE Earl of Warren
1 NOTE He fought with William the Conqueror in the Battle of Hastings 1066.  He became the first Earl of Warren and Surrey in England.
1 NOTE He came to England with William the Conqueror.
1 NOTE He came to England with William the Conqueror.
1 NOTE BIOGRAPHY
2 CONT William was the son of Rodulf I de Warenne and Béatrice, and a grand nephew of Gunnor, wife of Richard I, duke of Normandy. As a young man he helped secure Duke William's hold on Normandy, most notably in the campaigns of 1052 thr
2 CONC ough 1054 which culminated in the Battle of Mortemer. After this battle Roger de Mortemer forfeited most of his lands, and the duke gave them to William. The de Warenne surname derives from the castle of that name on the River Var
2 CONC enne, which flows through the territory William acquired in Upper Normandy.
2 CONT
2 CONT William was one of the nobles who advised Duke William when the decision to invade England was being considered. He fought at Hastings, and afterwards received the Rape of Lewes in Sussex, and subsequently lands in twelve other sh
2 CONC ires. In addition to the cluster around Lewes, there were clusters around the castles he built at Castle Acre in Norfolk and Conisborough in Yorkshire. He also built castles at Lewes (Sussex) and Reigate (Surrey). By the time of t
2 CONC he Domesday survey he was one of the wealthiest landholders in England with holdings in 12 counties.
2 CONT
2 CONT About 1070 he married Gundred, said to be the sister of Gerbod the Fleming, earl of Chester. They had two sons and a daughter, of whom William II would have progeny. There is a story that William and Gundred visited the abbey of C
2 CONC luny in France. Because they found there 'such great holiness and religion and charity' they felt compelled to take some of it home with them. 'And so we sent and asked the Abbot Hugh and all the holy body to give us two, or three
2 CONC , or four holy monks from their holy flock to whom we might give the church, which we had converted from wood to stone, below our castle at Lewes.'
2 CONT
2 CONT William fought against rebels at the Isle of Ely in 1071 where he showed a special desire to hunt down the Saxon rebel Hereward 'the Wake' who had murdered his brother the year before.
2 CONT
2 CONT William was loyal to William II Rufus, and it was probably in early 1088 that he was created earl of Surrey. He died shortly afterwards, on 24 June that year, of wounds he received while helping suppress the rebellion of 1088.
