2 CONT Original individual @P2203432574@ (@MS_NHFETTERLYFAMIL0@) merged with @P2203942468@ (@MS_NHFETTERLYFAMIL0@)
1 NOTE _P_CCINFO 1-20792
2 CONT Sources: RC 103; Norr, p72; Onslow; Kraentzler 1163, 1214, 1332, 1424,1486, 1598; A. Roots 184; AF; Moriarty; Antiquities of Shropshire, Vol. 4.
2 CONT Moriarty and RC say this woman was Wevia. Moriarty says Englishgenealogists nearly always mistakenly say Osbern married her sister, Aveline. But in NEHGR 75/59 he says she was Avelina, third sister of Gunnora.
2 CONT Norr says early sources say "Aveline" married Osbern de Bolebec, butlater sources say it was Weva/Wevie/Wevia who married him. Take your choice of spelling. He says she probably was the youngest sister (born about956) of Gunora/Gu
2 CONC nnor.
2 CONT Onslow says one of Wevia's sisters was called Adelina.
2 CONT Norr gives birth date as about 956 and RC 103 about 942.
2 CONT K-1214: Senfrie de Crepon, Wevia's sister.
2 CONT K-1424: Aveline de Crepin. (Kraentzler can't decide which is correct).
2 CONT Roots: Duvelina, sister of the Duchess Gunnora.
2 CONT Antiquities: Wevia. Cites one marriage, to Thurold de Pontaudemer.
2 CONT Ayers, p409, calls her Aveline, sister of Gunnora.
2 CONT
2 CONT SOURCES:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.  Wagner, Anthony, _Pedigree and Progress_, Phillimore, 1975, Charts 45& 46, pp. 204-205.  Wevie married Osbern of Bolbec, Lord of Longueville.  Theywere the parents of Walter of Bolbec.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.  Ancestral File (8XJV-44).  Gives her birthdate as about 974, whichmakes her a generation younger than her sisters.
1 NOTE _P_CCINFO 1-20792
2 CONT Name Suffix:<NSFX> Princess of Denmark Ancestral File Number:<AFN> V9V0-8K
2 CONT !"Royal Ancestors" by Michel Call, 1989, chart # 11527. Ancesrry and Progentry of Captain James Blount - Immigrant, by Robert F. Pfafman, p E-33.
2 CONT Original individual @I10447@ (@MS_NHFETTERLYFAMIL0@) merged with @I1053@ (@MS_NHFETTERLYFAMIL0@)
1 NOTE _P_CCINFO 1-20792
2 CONT Sources: Kraentzler 1164, 1486; Dukes of Normandy by Onslow; Pfafman;Norr.
2 CONT Dukes calls her Sainfrida and says she was married to thesuperintendent of forests at Secheville near Arques. Richard I visited Secheville and metthe beautiful Sainfrida, about whom he had heard, and "had the impudence tosuggest t
2 CONC o her husband that he should be allowed to make her better acquaintance.
2 CONT "The poor man was much perturbed and went in trouble to his wife. Thelady had no mind to become the mistress of the Duke, but her morals were notso rigid as to prevent her from making capital our of the situation for her family. S
2 CONC ainfrida had three sisters, Gunnor, Adelina and Veva (Wevia),and a brother named Herfast. Gunnor was a very pretty girl and by no means particular. Sainfrida told her husband to pretend to fall in with theDuke's proposals and give
2 CONC  Richard facilities to pay her a visit. But whenRichard arrived, Gunnor took Sainfrida's place. The Duke did not discover thetrick until the following day; but so delighted was he with Gunnor that notonly did he forgive Sainfrida,
2 CONC  but thanked her for having saved him from mortalsin! The result was that Gunnor became his wife by Danish custom and eventuallythe mother of a numerous family, the eldest of which was Richard II ofNormandy."
2 CONT K-1486 says Senfrie's husband was "a forester of Schecville."
2 CONT Norr says Senfrie had a daughter Josceline who married Roger deMontgomery. He says others say she was the daughter of Wevia and Osbern  de Bolebecand married Hugh de Montgomery. RC does not agree with Norr and cites manysources su
2 CONC pporting the RC lines.
2 CONT
2 CONT SOURCES:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.  Wagner, Anthony, _Pedigree and Progress_, Phillimore, 1975, Charts 45& 46, pp. 204-205.  Senfrie married N. N., the Forester of the Duke of Normandyat Sauqueville, near Arques.  They were the parents of Josceline, wife ofRoger
2 CONC  I de Montgomery and mother of Roger II de Montgomery.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.  Montgomery, Bo Gabriel de, _Origin and History of the Montgomerys_,1948, p. 44:  There has been much confusion about whether Joseline was marriedto the elder Roger or his son Hugo, and which of the sisters of the DuchessGunnor
2 CONC  she was the daughter of.  In the third Charter of Troarn, Roger II in his ownwords calls himself "Ego Rogerius, ex Normannis Normannus, magni autem Rogerii filius" (_Cartulary of Troarn_, fol. 1).  In a letter to Henry I, Ives,Bi
2 CONC shop of Chartres says "Gonnora et Senfria sorores fuerunt...ex Senfria excivit Joscelina, ex Joscelina, Rogerius de Monte Gummeri, ex Rogerio, Mabiliasoror Roberti Bellimensis" (Migne, _Patrologia latina, CLXII, 261).  Thus,Joscel
2 CONC ine was the daughter of Senfrie, not Wevia as stated by William of Jumieges,and was the mother of Roger II.
