2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Yaroslav I the Wise (c. 978 in Kiev - February 20, 1054 in Kiev) (East Slavic: Ярослав Мудрый; Christian name: George; Old Norse: Jarizleifr) was thrice Grand Prince of Novgorod and Kiev, uniting the two principalities for a time 
2 CONC under his rule. During his lengthy reign, Kievan Rus' reached a zenith of its cultural flowering and military power.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Born in 978
2 CONT
2 CONT Acceded in 1019
2 CONT
2 CONT Died on Ferburary 20, 1054 at Kiev
2 CONT
2 CONT Kievan Rus achieved its greatest power and splendor under Yaroslav the Wise in the 11th century. Yaroslav made Kiev a great city and built magnificent buildings, including the notable Cathedral of Saint Sophia or Hagia Sophia of K
2 CONC iev. Yaroslav did much to develop Kievan Rus education and culture. He also revised the first Russian law code, the so-called Russkaya Pravda or Russian Justice.  After his death in 1054, Kievan Rus declined.  Yaroslav's grandson,
2 CONC  Vladimir II Monomachus, made the final attempt to unite Kievan Rus, but after his death in 1125 the fragmentation continued as other Kievan Rus principalities challenged Kiev's supremacy.
2 CONT
2 CONT     By the 13th century, the East Slavic lands became a loose federation of city-states, held together by common language, religion, traditions, and customs. Although ruled by members of the house of Rurik, these city-states were 
2 CONC often at war with one another.  The area became an easy target for bands of invading Asiatic Mongols.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Yaroslav married 1019 to Ingeborg (Ingerid), a daughter of Olaf Skötkonung, King of Sweden.
2 CONT
2 CONT Yaroslav and Ingeborg had the following children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne of Kiev
2 CONT
2 CONT Isiaslav I, (1025 - 1078) Prince of Kiev, married a daughter of King Mieszko of Poland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wsevolod I, (1030 - 1093) Prince of Kiev.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anastasia of Kiev, married circa 1046 to Andrew I, King of Hungary.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT b. 980
2 CONT
2 CONT d. Feb. 2, 1054
2 CONT
2 CONT byname YAROSLAV THE WISE, Russian YAROSLAV MUDRY, grand prince of Kiev from 1019 to 1054.
2 CONT
2 CONT A son of the grand prince Vladimir, he was vice-regent of Novgorod at the time of his father's death in 1015. Then his eldest surviving brother, Svyatopolk the Accursed, killed three of his other brothers and seized power in Kiev.
2 CONC  Yaroslav, with the active support of the Novgorodians and the help of Varangian (Viking) mercenaries, defeated Svyatopolk and became the grand prince of Kiev in 1019.
2 CONT
2 CONT Yaroslav began consolidating the Kievan state through both cultural and administrative improvements and through military campaigns. He promoted the spread of Christianity in the Kievan state, gathered a large collection of books, 
2 CONC and employed many scribes to translate Greek religious texts into the Slavic language. He founded churches and monasteries and issued statutes regulating the legal position of the Christian Church and the rights of the clergy. Wit
2 CONC h the help of Byzantine architects and craftsmen, Yaroslav fortified and beautified Kiev along Byzantine lines. He built the majestic Cathedral of St. Sophia and the famous Golden Gate of the Kievan fortress. Under Yaroslav the co
2 CONC dification of legal customs and princely enactments was begun, and this work served as the basis for a law code called the Russkaya Pravda ("Russian Justice").
2 CONT
2 CONT Yaroslav pursued an active foreign policy, and his forces won several notable military victories. He regained Galicia from the Poles, decisively defeated the nomadic Pechenegs on the Kievan state's southern frontier, and expanded 
2 CONC Kievan possessions in the Baltic region, suppressing the Lithuanians, Estonians, and Finnish tribes. His military campaign against Constantinople in 1043 was a failure, however.
2 CONT
2 CONT Trade with the East and West played an important role in Kievan Rus in the 11th century, and Yaroslav maintained diplomatic relations with the European states. His daughters Elizabeth, Anna, and Anastasia were married respectively
2 CONC  to Harald III of Norway, Henry I of France, and Andrew I of Hungary.
2 CONT
2 CONT In his testament, Yaroslav sought to prevent a power struggle among his five sons by dividing his empire among them and enjoining the younger four sons to obey the eldest, Izyaslav, who was to succeed his father as grand prince of
2 CONC  Kiev. This advice had no lasting effect, and civil war ensued after Yaroslav's death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Copyright © 1994-2001 Encyclopædia Britannica, Inc.
2 CONT
