2 CONT Yaroslav I, byname YAROSLAV THE WISE, Russian YAROSLAV MUDRY (b. 980--d.Feb. 2, 1054), grand prince of Kiev from 1019 to 1054.
2 CONT A son of the grand prince Vladimir, he was vice-regent of Novgorod at thetime of his father's death in 1015. Then his eldest surviving brother,Svyatopolk the Accursed, killed three of his other brothers and seizedpower in Kiev. Ya
2 CONC roslav, with the active support of the Novgorodians andthe help of Varangian (Viking) mercenaries, defeated Svyatopolk andbecame the grand prince of Kiev in 1019.
2 CONT Yaroslav began consolidating the Kievan state through both cultural andadministrative improvements and through military campaigns. He promotedthe spread of Christianity in the Kievan state, gathered a largecollection of books, and
2 CONC  employed many scribes to translate Greekreligious texts into the Slavic language. He founded churches andmonasteries and issued statutes regulating the legal position of theChristian Church and the rights of the clergy. With the 
2 CONC help of Byzantinearchitects and craftsmen, Yaroslav fortified and beautified Kiev alongByzantine lines. He built the majestic Cathedral of St. Sophia and thefamous Golden Gate of the Kievan fortress. Under Yaroslav, thecodificatio
2 CONC n of legal customs and princely enactments was begun, and thiswork served as the basis for a law code called the Russkaya Pravda("Russian Justice").
2 CONT Yaroslav pursued an active foreign policy, and his forces won severalnotable military victories. He regained Galicia from the Poles,decisively defeated the nomadic Pechenegs on the Kievan state's southernfrontier, and expanded Kie
2 CONC van possessions in the Baltic region,suppressing the Lithuanians, Estonians, and Finnish tribes. His militarycampaign against Constantinople in 1043 was a failure, however.
2 CONT Trade with the East and West played an important role in Kievan Rus inthe 11th century, and Yaroslav maintained diplomatic relations with theEuropean states. His daughters Elizabeth, Anna, and Anastasia weremarried respectively to
2 CONC  Harald III of Norway, Henry I of France, andAndrew I of Hungary.
2 CONT In his testament, Yaroslav sought to prevent a power struggle among hisfive sons by dividing his empire among them and enjoining the youngerfour sons to obey the eldest, Izyaslav, who was to succeed his father asgrand prince of Ki
2 CONC ev. This advice had no lasting effect, and civil warensued after Yaroslav's death. {Britannica CD, 1997, YAROSLAV I]AncestralFile Number:<AFN> 952M-GVYaroslav I the Wise, Grand Prince of Kiev
2 CONT Born in 978
2 CONT Acceded in 1019
2 CONT Died on Ferburary 20, 1054 at Kiev
2 CONT
2 CONT   Kievan Rus achieved its greatest power and splendor under Yaroslav theWise in the 11th century. Yaroslav made Kiev a great city and builtmagnificent buildings, including the notable Cathedral of Saint Sophia orHagia Sophia of Ki
2 CONC ev. Yaroslav did much to develop Kievan Rus educationand culture. He also revised the first Russian law code, the so-calledRusskaya Pravda or Russian Justice. After his death in 1054, Kievan Rusdeclined. Yaroslav's grandson, Vladi
2 CONC mir II Monomachus, made the finalattempt to unite Kievan Rus, but after his death in 1125 thefragmentation continued as other Kievan Rus principalities challengedKiev's supremacy.
2 CONT   By the 13th century, the East Slavic lands became a loose federation ofcity-states, held together by common language, religion, traditions, andcustoms. Although ruled by members of the house of Rurik, thesecity-states were often
2 CONC  at war with one another. The area became an easytarget for bands of invading Asiatic Mongols.
2 CONT
2 CONT .....Microsoft® Encarta® Encyclopedia 99. © 1993-1998 MicrosoftCorporation.
2 CONT Yaroslav had a son, but the name of the mother is unknown:
2 CONT Elias, Prince of Novgorod
2 CONT Yaroslav married 1019 to Ingeborg Olafsdottir, a daughter of OlafSkötkonung, King of Sweden.
2 CONT Yaroslav and Ingeborg had the following children:
2 CONT Anne of Kiev
2 CONT Izyaslav I, Prince of Kiev, 1054 - 1078, deposed 1068 - 1069 and 1073 -1076.
2 CONT Svyatoslav II, Prince of Kiev, 1073 - 1076
2 CONT Vsevolod I, Prince of Kiev, 1076 - 1093, deposed 1077 - 1078.
2 CONT Ellisif ( or Elizabeth) of Kiev, married first in 1045 to Harald IIIHardrada, King of Norway, who was slain on September 25, 1066 at theBattle of Stamford Bridge. Ellisif married second in 1067 to Svend II,King of Denmark.
2 CONT Valdimar ( or Holti) the Nimble, Prince of Novgorod, 1036 - 1052.
2 CONT Anastasia Agmunda of Kiev, married circa 1046 to Andrew I, King ofHungary.
2 CONT Viacheslav, Prince of Smolensk 1054 - 1056
2 CONT Igor of Vladimir, Prince of Vladimir 1054 - 1060
2 CONT Dobronega (or Maria) of Kiev, married 1038 to Casimir I, King of Poland.
