2 CONT Mother: Adelaide of Aquitaine
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage with 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.	Rozala (who changed her name to Susannah after becoming queen) 
2 CONT
2 CONT She was the daughter of Berengar II of Italy and the widow of Arnulf II of Flanders, with whom she had children, the oldest of whom was of age to assume the offices of count of Flanders. Robert divorced her within a year of his fa
2 CONC ther's death. No children together.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.	Bertha (but the marriage got annulled)
2 CONT
2 CONT He tried instead to marry Bertha, daughter of Conrad of Burgundy, around the time of his father's death. She was a widow of Odo I of Blois, but was also Robert's cousin. For reasons of consanguinity, Pope Gregory V refused to sanc
2 CONC tion the marriage, and Robert was excommunicated. After long negotiations with Gregory's successor, Sylvester II, the marriage was annulled.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.	Constance of Arles
2 CONT
2 CONT Finally, in 1001, Robert entered into his final and longest-lasting marriage to Constance of Arles, the daughter of William I of Provence. She was an ambitious and scheming woman, who made life miserable for her husband by encoura
2 CONC ging her sons to revolt against their father.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children with Constance of Arles;
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.	Constance, married Manasses de Dammartin
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.	Hedwig (or Advisa) of France, married Renauld I, Count of Nevers on 25 January 1016 and had issue.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.	Hugh Magnus, co-king (1017–1025)
2 CONT
2 CONT 4.	Henry I, successor
2 CONT
2 CONT 5.	Robert, became Duke of Burgundy
2 CONT
2 CONT 6.	Odo (1013–c.1056), who may have been mentally retarded and died after his brother's failed invasion of Normandy
2 CONT
2 CONT 7.	Adela (d. 1079), married firstly Richard III of Normandy and secondly Baldwin V of Flanders.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert had no children from his short-lived marriage to Susanna. His illegal marriage to Bertha gave him one stillborn son in 999, but only Constance gave him surviving children. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert also left an illegitimate son: Rudolph, Bishop of Bourges.
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT ROBERT (II) de France, son of HUGUES Capet King of France & his wife Adelais [de Poitou] (Orléans ([27 Mar] 972-Château de Melun 20 Jul 1031, bur église de l'Abbaye royale de Saint-Denis).  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Genealogiæ Scriptoris Fusniacensis names "Robertum regem et filiam Hadevidem…comitissam Hainonensium" as the children of King Hugues[148].  
2 CONT
2 CONT He was invested as associate-king with his father 25 Dec 987, consecrated 1 Apr 988 at the cathedral of Sainte-Croix in Orléans[149].  He succeeded his father in 996 as ROBERT II "le Pieux"[150] King of France.  
2 CONT
2 CONT He claimed the duchy of Burgundy on the death of his paternal uncle Duke Henri in 1002, but took 12 years to complete its conquest in the face of opposition from Otto-Guillaume Comte de Mâcon[151].  
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of Emperor Heinrich II King of Germany in 1024, King Robert supported the rebels (led by Frédéric II Duke of Upper Lotharingia) opposed to King Konrad II but he refused the crown of Italy which they offered it to h
2 CONC im.  Robert nevertheless sent troops to attack Metz, but was repulsed[152].  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Chronicle of Alberic de Trois-Fontaines records the death in 1031 of "rex Francorum Robertus"[153].  Rodolfus Glaber records the death of King Robert at Melun in July and his place of burial[154].  The necrology of Chartres ca
2 CONC thedral records the death "XII Kal Aug" of "Rotbertus rex"[155].  The necrology of the abbey of Saint-Denis records the death "XIII Kal Aug" of "Rotbertus…Francorum rex"[156]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT m firstly (988, before 1 Apr, repudiated [991/92]) as her second husband, ROZALA [Suzanne] di Ivrea, widow of ARNOUL II “le Jeune” Count of Flanders, daughter of BERENGARIO II ex-King of Italy [Ivrea] & his wife Willa of Tuscany-A
2 CONC rles ([950/960]-13 Dec 1003 or 7 Feb 1004, bur Gent, church of the Abbey de Saint-Pierre du Mont-Blandin).  
2 CONT
2 CONT Richer records that King Robert repudiated his wife "Susannam…genere Italicam eo quod anus esset" but refused to allow her to retake her castle at Montreuil, whereupon she constructed another nearby[165].  She returned to Flanders
2 CONC  after she was repudiated by her second husband, and became one of the principal advisers of her son Count Baldwin IV.  France retained Montreuil-sur-Mer.  
2 CONT
2 CONT m secondly ([late 996/early 997], divorced Sep 1001) BERTHE of Burgundy, widow of EUDES I Comte de Blois et de Chartres, daughter of CONRAD I “le Pacifique” King of Burgundy [Welf] & his wife Mathilde de France [Carolingian] ([964
2 CONC /965]-16 Jan after 1010).  
2 CONT
2 CONT Pope Gregory V called on King Robert to repudiate his wife in 998 on grounds of consanguinity.  The request was repeated in 1001 by the court of Rome.  Robert at first refused and the kingdom of France was excommunicated[173].  Th
2 CONC e king, in reaction to the 1108 assassination of his favourite Hugues de Beauvais who had served Queen Berthe, visited Rome in 1008 in an unsuccessful attempt to divorce his third wife in order to take back Berthe[175].  
2 CONT
2 CONT m thirdly (after Sep 1001 before 25 Aug 1003) CONSTANCE d'Arles, daughter of GUILLAUME II “le Libérateur” Comte d’Arles [Provence] et Marquis & his wife Adelais [Blanche] d’Anjou ([987/89]-Château de Melun 22 or 25 Jul 1032, bur é
2 CONC glise de l'Abbaye royale de Saint-Denis).  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Historia Francorum names "Constantiam, filiam Guillelmi comitis Arelatensis, natam de Blanca sorore Gaufridi comitis Andegavensis" as wife of King Robert[178].  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Chronicle of Alberic de Trois-Fontaines also names "Constantia filia fuit Blanche comitisse Arelatensis" as wife of "Robertus rex"[179].  The Chronicon Hugonis names "Constantiam" as wife of "Robertus", specifying that she was
2 CONC  "cognatam Hugonis Autisiodorensis episcopi comitis Cabilonensis"[180].  This is presumably based on Rodulfus Glaber who states incorrectly that "Constantiam…filiam…prioris Willemi Aquitanie ducis" was wife of King Robert II, spec
2 CONC ifying that she was "cognatam" of Hugues Comte de Chalon Bishop of Auxerre[181].  The only relationship so far identified between the two is that Constance's maternal uncle, Geoffroy I Comte d'Anjou, was the second husband of the 
2 CONC mother of Comte Hugues.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Rodulfus Glauber dates her marriage to "about the year 1000"[182].  
2 CONT
2 CONT The king attempted to separate from Constance in 1008 in order to take back his second wife, according to Rodulfus Glaber through the influence of "Hugo dictus Beluacensis"[183], but he restored Constance's royal prerogatives end-
2 CONC 1009[184].  
2 CONT
2 CONT She opposed her husband's proposal to crown their second son Henri as associate king in 1026, supporting the candidature of her third son Robert[185].  She organised two revolts against King Robert, and another against her son Kin
2 CONC g Henri I after his accession in 1031[186].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Rodolfus Glaber records the death of Queen Constance in the same city as her husband [Melun] and in the same month [July] in the following year, as well as her place of burial[187].  The necrology of the abbey of Saint-Germain-des
2 CONC -Prés records the death "XI Kal Aug" of "regina Constancia"[188].  The necrology of Argenteuil Priory records the death "VIII Kal Aug" of "Constancia regina"[189]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT King Robert & his third wife had [seven] children:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. [CONSTANCE de France.  
2 CONT
2 CONT There is no proof that Constance, wife of Manassès de Dammartin, was the daughter of King Robert II, the affiliation being proposed for onomastic reasons only[190]. It is, however, supported by the presence of the king and queen a
2 CONC t a donation by Comte Manassès in 1031[191].  Rodolfus Glaber records that King Robert had two daughters by his wife Constance[192], presumably referring to Hedwige and Adela.  According to Europäische Stammtafeln[193], the wife o
2 CONC f Manassès was "Constance [de Dammartin]", presumably on the theory that she brought her husband the county of Dammartin.  
2 CONT
2 CONT m ([1023 or before]) MANASSES Comte de Dammartin-en-Goële, son of HILDUIN II de Montdidier Seigneur de Ramérupt & his wife --- (-killed in battle Ornel, near Etain, Bar-le-Duc 15 Nov or 15 Dec 1037).]
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. HEDWIGE [Avoie] de France ([1003]-5 Jun after 1063).  
2 CONT
2 CONT Rodolfus Glaber records that "Rainaldus…Landrici comitis filius" married a daughter of King Robert[194].  The Chronici Hugonis Floriacensis names "Adelaidem…Rainaldi comitis Nivernensis uxorem" as the daughter of King Robert and h
2 CONC is wife Constance[195].  The Historia Nivernensium Comitum records that the wife of "Renaldum" was "sorori Regis Roberti, filii Hugonis Capitonis"[196].  The Annales Vizeliacenses also specifies that Renaud's wife was the sister n
2 CONC ot daughter of King Robert II[197].  However, this is chronologically unlikely given that King Robert and his known sisters were born in the 970s, more than twenty years before the earliest possible date of birth of Comte Renaud. 
2 CONC  Her marriage was agreed by her father as part of his alliance with Landry Comte de Nevers after capturing Auxerre, which the king gave to his daughter as dowry[198].  "Rainaldus comes Nivernensis" donated property "Belmontis" to 
2 CONC Cluny, for the souls of "…uxoris mee Advise…" by charter dated to [1028/40][199].  She founded the abbeys of Crisenon and Issenon.  
2 CONT
2 CONT m (1006, soon after 25 Jan 1016) RENAUD de Nevers, son of LANDRY Comte de Nevers & his wife Mathilde de Bourgogne-Comté (-killed in battle Sainte-Vertu, Yonne 29 May 1040, bur Auxerre, Saint-Germain).  He succeeded his father in 1
2 CONC 028 as RENAUD I Comte de Nevers.  He was killed in battle against Robert I Duke of Burgundy, his brother-in-law. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. HUGUES de France (1007-28 Aug 1025, bur Compiègne, church of the Abbaye de Saint-Corneille).  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Historia Francorum names (in order) "Hugonem qui cognominatus est Magnus, Henricum, Robertum, Odonem" as the four sons of King Robert and Constance[200].  
2 CONT
2 CONT He was consecrated associate-king 9 Jun 1017, at Compiègne, church of the Abbaye de Saint-Corneille, when he was "barely 10 years old" according to Rodolfus Glaber[201].  He rebelled against his father claiming the full authority 
2 CONC of his position as associate-king, but later submitted[202].  
2 CONT
2 CONT The necrology of the abbey of Saint-Germain-des-Prés records the death "V Kal Sep" of "Hugo iuvenis rex Francorum"[203].  The necrology of Argenteuil Priory records the death "V Kal Sep" of "Hugo iuvenis rex"[204].  Rodolfus Glabe
2 CONC r records his place of burial[205]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 4.  HENRI de France ([end 1009/May 1010]-Palais de Vitry-aux-Loges, forêt d’Orléans, Loiret 4 Aug 1060, bur église de l'Abbaye royale de Saint-Denis).  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Historia Francorum names (in order) "Hugonem qui cognominatus est Magnus, Henricum, Robertum, Odonem" as the four sons of King Robert and Constance[206].  
2 CONT
2 CONT He succeeded his father in 1031 as HENRI I King of France.   
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT 5.  ROBERT de France ([1011/12]-church of Fleury-sur-Ouche, Côte d’Or 18 Mar 1076, bur Saint-Seine-l'Abbaye, Côte d’Or).  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Historia Francorum names (in order) "Hugonem qui cognominatus est Magnus, Henricum, Robertum, Odonem" as the four sons of King Robert and Constance[207].  Rodulfus Glauber names "Heinricus rex…germanium suum Rotbertum" when re
2 CONC cording the latter's installation as duke of Burgundy by his brother[208].  
2 CONT
2 CONT His mother supported him as candidate to be consecrated associate king in 1027, in place of his older brother Henri who was supported by their father.  His father named him heir to the duchy of Burgundy in 1030.  He was installed 
2 CONC as ROBERT I "le Vieux" Duke of Burgundy in 1032 by his brother King Henri I.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT 6.  EUDES de France ([1013]-Germigny-des-Prés, near Sully, Loiret 15 May [1057/59]).  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Historia Francorum names (in order) "Hugonem qui cognominatus est Magnus, Henricum, Robertum, Odonem" as the four sons of King Robert and Constance[209].  
2 CONT
2 CONT He allied himself with Eudes II Comte de Blois in the war against his brother Henri I King of France 1034-1041.  He was defeated and imprisoned at Orléans.  After his release, he fought for the king in Normandy, but was defeated i
2 CONC n 1054 at Mortemer.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Orderic Vitalis records the war between the Normans and "Henricum regem" in 1054 when "Odonem fratrem suum" was defeated by "Roberti Aucensis comiitis et Rogerii de Mortuomari" who led the Norman forces "apud Mortuum-mare"[210].  
2 CONC He owned land near Bellême[211].  
2 CONT
2 CONT The necrology of the abbey of Saint-Denis records the death "Id Mai" of "Odo Roberti regis filius"[212]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 7. ADELA de France (-Messines 8 Jan 1079, bur Messines, Benedictine monastery).  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Chronicle of Alberic de Trois-Fontaines names "soror…regis Henrici Adela" as wife of "Balduino Insulano"[213].  The Genealogiæ Scriptoris Fusniacensis names "Alam comitissam Flandrensem" as the daughter of King Robert[214].  S
2 CONC he is named as daughter of King Robert in a manuscript whose attribution to Orderic Vitalis is disputed, which also refers to her marriage[215].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Kerrebrouck mentions her betrothal to Duke Richard "très jeune" but does not cite the primary source on which this is based[216].  "Richardus Nortmannorum dux" agreed grants of property to "Adela" on the occasion of their marriage
2 CONC  by charter dated Jan 1026, which does not specify her parentage[217].  Her father gave her the seigneurie of Corbie as her dowry.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Ctss de Contenance. She founded the Benedictine monastery at Messines near Ypres.  
2 CONT
2 CONT The necrology of the abbey of Saint-Denis records the death "VI Id Jan" of "Adelaidis comitissa"[218].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Betrothed (Jan 1027) to RICHARD III Duke of Normandy, son of RICHARD I Duke of Normandy & his first wife Judith de Bretagne ([1001]-6 Aug 1027).  
2 CONT
2 CONT m (Amiens 1028) BAUDOUIN de Flandre, son of BAUDOUIN IV "le Barbu/Pulchrae Barbae" Count of Flanders & his first wife Ogive de Luxembourg ([1012/13]-Lille 1 Sep 1067, bur Lille, Saint-Pierre).  He succeeded his father in 1035 as B
2 CONC AUDOUIN V “le Pieux/Insulanus” Count of Flanders.  He was regent of France for his nephew Philippe I King of France 1060-1066/67. 
2 CONT
