2 CONT Military career:
2 CONT
2 CONT The kingdom Robert inherited was not large, and in an effort to increase his power, he vigorously pursued his claim to any feudal lands which became vacant, which action usually resulted in war with a counter-claimant. In 1003, hi
2 CONC s invasion of the Duchy of Burgundy was thwarted and it would not be until 1016 that he was finally able to get the support of the Church and be recognized as Duke of Burgundy.
2 CONT
2 CONT The pious Robert made few friends and many enemies, including his own sons: Hugh Magnus, Henry, and Robert. They turned against their father in a civil war over power and property. Hugh died in revolt in 1025. In a conflict with H
2 CONC enry and the younger Robert, King Robert's army was beaten and he retreated to Beaugency outside Paris, his capital. He died in the middle of the war with his sons on 20 July 1031 at Melun. He was interred with Constance in Saint 
2 CONC Denis Basilica. He was succeeded by son Henry in both France and Burgundy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert had no children from his short-lived marriage to Susanna. His illegal marriage to Bertha gave him one stillborn son in 999, but only Constance gave him surviving children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Constance, married Manasses de Dammartin 
2 CONT
2 CONT Hedwig (or known as Advisa of Auxerre), married Renauld I, Count of Nevers on 25 January 1016 and had issues. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh Magnus, co-king (1017–1025) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry I, successor 
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert, became Duke of Burgundy 
2 CONT
2 CONT Odo (1013–c.1056), who may have been mentally retarded and died after his brother's failed invasion of Normandy 
2 CONT
2 CONT Adela (d. 1079), married firstly Richard III of Normandy and secondly Baldwin V of Flanders. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert also left an illegitimate son: Rudolph, Bishop of Bourges.
2 CONT
