2 CONT https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hugues_de_Beauvais
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugues de Beauvais (* before 970; † before May 1008) was a French aristocrat of the Middle Ages, who was Count of the Kingdom after 991 and until his death. … His wife is not known, but studies by historian and archaeologist Adolp
2 CONC he de Dion[14] connect her with the region around '''Nogent-le-Roi and Épernon'''.
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT http://racineshistoire.free.fr/LGN/PDF/Broyes.pdf
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugues de Blois ''dit «de Beauvais»''
2 CONT before 970 +X 1008 (assassinated on the orders of Count Fulk "Nerra" of Anjou), Count Palatine, Count of Nogent (-l'Erambert, now -Le Roi) then of Dreux (~1006/08), loyal to King Robert II "the Pious," Forester of the Yveline Fore
2 CONC st, Advocate of the church of Orléans, served his relative Odo II of Blois in the county of Dreux (ceded Dreux to the King and recovered his rights over Beauvais) married ? (perhaps from the Epernon lineage, said to be '''Lady of 
2 CONC Nogent''' (-Le-Roi); married 2) '''William of Hainaut,''' Forester of Yveline, castellan of Montfort, Beynes and Epernon, invested by King Robert II)
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amaury_Ier_de_Montfort
2 CONT
2 CONT Amaury I, who died in 1053, was the second seignor of Montfort l'Amaury. Amaury I was the son of Guillaume de Montfort. The latter had built a wooden castle on a natural clod that took the name of Montfort.  Mother: '''NN of Nogen
2 CONC t, Dame de Montfort and Epernon'''. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amalrich_I._von_Montfort
2 CONT
2 CONT Amalrich I of Montfort (French: Amaury; † after 1052/53) was in the early 11th century. Century the first known lord of Montfort-l'Amaury and thus the ancestor of the house of Montfort-l'Amaury.  '''His origin is unclear'''. Order
2 CONC icus Vitalis later reported that he was the son of a "Wilhelm of Haingau", which, however, is not proven by any contemporary source. According to an entry in the chronicle of Saint-Denis, his mother was a '''lady of Nogent'''. Bet
2 CONC ween the years 1022 and 1031, Amalrich appears as a witness to several documents, King Robert II of France. In the year 1052/53 he founded the Priory Saint-Thomas in Épernon, after which nothing more is reported on him. He had at 
2 CONC least two sons, his successor Simon I and Mainer.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guillaume_de_Montfort
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/Lord-Guillaume-de-Hainaut-Seigneur-de-Montfort-l-Amaury-Count-de-Haynault/6000000000123850731?through=6000000031928221957 '''Guillaume le Hennuyuer'''] [4] became the first castellain of Montfort and, 
2 CONC having convowed with the [https://www.geni.com/people/dame-de-Nogent/6000000031928221957?through=6000000000123850731 '''dowager of Épernon'''], widow of [https://www.geni.com/people/Hugues-de-Beauvais-seigneur-de-Nogent/6000000017
2 CONC 043598398 '''Hugues de Beauvais'''], administrator of the chartraine part of the Yvelines forest, he succeeded him in this last charge[3] when the latter was assassinated in 1008. He succeeded in making the new seigneury hereditar
2 CONC y, and passed it on to his son [https://www.geni.com/people/Amaury-I-seigneur-de-Montfort/6000000000124012386?through=6000000031928221957 '''Amaury I'''].
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guillaume_de_Montfort
2 CONT
2 CONT Divenuto scudiero di Montfort e, dopo essersi sposato con la vedova di Hugues de Beauvais, amministratore del paese di Yvelines, gli succederà quando quest'ultimo verrà assassinato nel 1008, riuscendo a rendere ereditario il nuovo
2 CONC  titolo e a trasmetterlo al suo figlio Amaury I de Montfort.
2 CONT
2 CONT “He became Montfort's squire and, after marrying the '''widow of Hugues de Beauvais''', administrator of the region of Yvelines, he succeeded him when the latter was murdered in 1008, managing to make the new title hereditary and 
2 CONC pass it on to his son Amaury I de Montfort.”
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Comments
2 CONT
2 CONT From [https://fasg.org/projects/henryproject/data/amaur000.htm '''The Henry Project: The Ancestors of King Henry II of England'''] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Amaury I de Montfort l'Amaury
2 CONT Lord of Montfort l'Amaury, fl. 1022-1060.
2 CONT
2 CONT Possible mother: '''NN de Nogent'''.
2 CONT The continuator of Aimoin de Fleury has a passage which indicates that a man (name unclear) who fortified Montfort and Épernon married a lady of Nogent and had a son Amaury, father of two sons Simon and Amaury, said Simon being th
2 CONC e father Amaury de Montfort and Bertrade, countess of Anjou ["... in tempore Regis Roberti benia fuit de dominio San Germani. <erasure> Ipse firmavit Montifortem et Sparnomum: quandam quoque dominam de Novigento habuit uxorem; de 
2 CONC qua unum filium habuit, nuncupatum Almaricum. Idem Almaricus duos filios habuit, scilicet Simonem et Almaricum. Symon procreavit Almaricum de Monteforti et Bertrandam Comitissam Andegavensam. ..." Ex Continuatione Historiæ Aimoini
2 CONC  Monachi Floriac., RHF 11: 275]. At the point where the note "<erasure>" appears, the last part of one line and the beginning of the next have been carefully erased, amounting to nearly a (short) line, or several words. This erasu
2 CONC re is not indicated by the editors of RHF, but is discussed by Rhein, working from the same manuscript [Rhein (1910), 27-8]. In a note to the passage in the continuation of Aimoin de Fleury, the editors of RHF suggested suggest th
2 CONC at the passage should be read: "Tempore Roberti Regis Almaricus firmavit etc." [RHF 11: 275, note (e)] Such an emendation, which seems to have no clear basis, would appear to make Amaury the son of an otherwise unattested Amaury. 
2 CONC If the erasure is ignored altogether, then king Robert appears to be the antecedent of the word ipse which appears immediately after the erasure. This is the approach of the Chronicle of Saint-Denis, which makes king Robert [II] o
2 CONC f France the person who fortified Montfort and Êpernon, married a lady of Nogent, and had son Amaury [de Montfort] ["Au tens de ce Roi fu faite banie de la segnurie de l'Abeie de S. Denise. Cit Rois Robert ferma le chastel de Mont
2 CONC fort et d'Espernon; une Dame de Nojent ot epousée; de cele ot un fil qui ot non Amauris. Cil Amauris ot deulx fuix, Symon et Amauri, etc." Chron. S. Denis, RHF 10: 311; see also RHF 11: 275, note (e)]. There is no good reason to a
2 CONC ccept this version.
2 CONT
2 CONT This evident reference to the mother of Amaury does not necessarily conflict with Orderic's information about his father. It could plausibly be conjectured that the erased words referred to a Guillaume who was the antecedent of th
2 CONC e word ipse appearing immediately after the erasure, but there is no good evidence that this was the case. Nevertheless, it is common to combine the accounts of Orderic and Aimoin's continuator by giving Amaury as the son of Guill
2 CONC aume de Hainaut by a lady of Nogent [e.g., Anselme 6: 71]. While it is possible that this scenario correctly gives Amaury's father and mother, the documentation in both cases is less than ideal.
2 CONT
2 CONT
