2 CONT # https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/House_of_Montfort The House of Montfort was a medieval French noble house that eventually found its way to the Kingdom of England and originated the famous Simon de Montfort, 6th Earl of Leicester. 
2 CONC However, his father, Simon de Monfort the Elder, who led the French Crusaders during the Albigensian Crusade, is far more notorious in France and among military medievalists. The family began when Hugh Capet granted a petty lordsh
2 CONC ip to [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guillaume_de_Montfort_of_Hainaut Guillaume de Montfort] in the Île-de-France. His successors were to be the vassals of the counts of Beaumont. Guillaume's son, [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Am
2 CONC aury_I_de_Montfort Amaury] began building a castle that would eventually become the eponymous Montfort-l'Amaury. The project, however, was incomplete when he died c. 1053, but his son, [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Simon_I_de_Mon
2 CONC tfort Simon],  finished it in 1067. His great-grandson, Simon IV would eventually marry the heiress of Leicester, and their son, Simon V would become the first Montfort earl of Leicester.[1] During the 13th century the family lost
2 CONC  its ancestral seat of Montfort-l'Amaury to the House of Dreux.[1]
