1 NOTE SOURCE NOTES:
2 CONT http://mariah.stonemarche.org/famfiles/fam01448.htm
1 NOTE _P_CCINFO 2-2438
1 NOTE OR "RAGNHEIDR""OF POLOTSK"
1 NOTE _P_CCINFO 1-20792
2 CONT Original individual @P2442110466@ (@MS_NHFETTERLYFAMIL0@) merged with @P2447683497@ (@MS_NHFETTERLYFAMIL0@)
2 CONT Original individual @P2442110466@ (@MS_NHFETTERLYFAMIL0@) merged with @P2442109208@ (@MS_NHFETTERLYFAMIL0@)
2 CONT Original individual @P2442110466@ (@MS_NHFETTERLYFAMIL0@) merged with @P2308141582@ (@MS_NHFETTERLYFAMIL0@)
1 NOTE BIOGRAPHY
2 CONT Ragneda (Ragnhild/Rogneda) was born about 962, the daughter of Ragnvald Olavson (Rogwolod/Rognwald), who came from Scandinavia and established himself at Polatsk in the mid-10th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT It has been speculated that Ragnvald belonged to the Ynglings royal family of Norway. About 978 she gave birth to a son Iziaslav whose paternity has not been established. In or about 980, Vladimir of Novgorod, on learning that Rag
2 CONC neda was betrothed to his brother Jaropolk I Swjatowslawitsch, grand duke of Kiev, took Polatsk and forced Ragneda to marry him. Having raped Ragneda in the presence of her parents, he ordered them to be killed, along with two of 
2 CONC Ragneda's brothers.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ragneda gave him several children. Her sons by Vladimir were Jaroslav I Vladimirovitch, Vsevolod and Mistislav. She also bore two daughters, one of whom is named by Nestor the Chronicler as Predslava (taken as a concubine by Boles
2 CONC law I Chrobry, king of Poland, according to Gallus). A later chronicle tells a story, most likely taken from a Norse saga, of Ragneda plotting against Vladimir and asking her eldest son Iziaslav, to kill him. As was the Norse roya
2 CONC l custom, she was sent with her eldest son to govern Polatsk, the land of her parents. Iziaslav's line continued to rule Polatsk, and the town of Izyaslavl named after him, until the Mongol invasion.
2 CONT
2 CONT After Vladimir converted to Christianity and took Anna Porphyrogenita of Byzantium as his wife, he had to divorce all his previous wives, including Ragneda. After that she joined a convent and took the name Anastasia. She died abo
2 CONC ut 1002.
1 NOTE Rogneda of Polotsk (962-1002) is the Slavic name for Ragnhild, whose father Ragnvald (Slavic: Rogvolod) came from overseas (i.e., from Scandinavia) and established himself at Polatsk in the mid-10th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT It has been speculated that Ragnvald belonged to the Ynglings royal family of Norway. In or about 980, Vladimir of Novgorod, on learning that she was betrothed to his half-brother Yaropolk I of Kiev, took Polotsk and forced Rogned
2 CONC a to marry him. Having raped Rogneda in the presence of her parents, he ordered them to be killed, along with two of Rogneda's brothers.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rogneda gave him several children. The four sons were Yaroslav the Wise, Vsevolod, Mstislav of Chernigov, and Izyaslav of Polotsk. She also bore two daughters, one of whom is named by Nestor the Chronicler as Predslava (taken as a
2 CONC  concubine of Boleslaus I of Poland, according to Gallus). A later chronicle tells a story, most likely taken from a Norse saga, of Rogneda plotting against Vladimir and asking her elder son, Izyaslav, to kill him. As was the Nors
2 CONC e royal custom, she was sent with her elder son to govern the land of her parents, i.e. Polotsk. Izyaslav's line continued to rule Polotsk and the newly-found town of Izyaslavl until the Mongol invasion.
2 CONT
2 CONT After Vladimir converted to Christianity and took Anna Porphyrogeneta as his wife, he had to divorce all his previous wives, including Rogneda. After that, she entered the convent and took the name Anastasia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Around 1825 Kondraty Ryleev wrote a narrative poem entitled Rogneda. This poem became a literary source for her portrayal in the nationalist Russian opera Rogneda by Alexander Serov, which premiered in 1865.
1 NOTE 494px-Anton_Losenko
