2 CONT
2 CONT Noteringar
2 CONT
2 CONT Estrid (Astrid) var slaviska och kom från västslaviskt område längs södra Östersjökusten. Födelseåret är osäkert; en källa anger ca 985. Enligt en källa lämnade hon sin make 1019 och gifte sig med den norske kungen Olav Haraldsson
2 CONC , men andra uppgifter gör gällande att hon stannade hos Olof, som avled år 1022.
2 CONT
2 CONT (Källa: bl. a. Maja Hagerman - Spåren av kungens män)
2 CONT
2 CONT In English:
2 CONT
2 CONT Estrd
2 CONT
2 CONT Born 979
2 CONT
2 CONT Family with Olof Skotkonung (Eriksson) (980-1022)
2 CONT
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Ingegerd "Holy Anna of Novgorod" Olofsdotter (1000-1050)
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Anund James Olofsson (1007-1050)
2 CONT
2 CONT Entries:
2 CONT
2 CONT Estrid (Astrid) was a Slav that came from the western Slavic regions along the southern Baltic Sea coast. Her year of birth is uncertain, as another source indicates approximately 985. According to one source, she left her husband
2 CONC  in 1019 and married the Norwegian King Olav Haraldsson, but other data argues she stayed with Olof, who died in 1022.
2 CONT
2 CONT (Source: Maja Haberman - Traces of the King's Men)
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Wikipedia page on Estrid of the Obotrites:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Estrid_of_the_Obotrites
2 CONT
2 CONT Estrid (or Astrid) of the Obotrites (ca. 979 – 1035), was a Medieval and Viking age Swedish Queen consort and West Slavic Princess, married to Olof Skötkonung, the King of Sweden, ca. 1000–1022, mother of king Anund Jacob of Swede
2 CONC n and the Russian Saint and Queen (Grand Princess) Ingegerd Olofsdotter.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Legend says that Estrid was taken back to Sweden from a war in the West Slavic area of Mecklenburg as a war-prize. She was most likely given by her father, a tribal chief of the Polabian Obotrites, as a peace offering in a marriag
2 CONC e to seal the peace, and she is thought to have brought with her a great dowry, as a great Slavic influence is represented in Sweden from her time, mainly among craftsmen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her husband also had a mistress, Edla, who came from the same area in Europe as herself, and who was possibly taken to Sweden at the same time. The king treated Edla and Estrid the same way and gave his son and his two daughters w
2 CONC ith Edla the same privilegies as the children he had with Estrid, though it was Estrid he married and made Queen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Queen Estrid was baptised with her husband, their children and large numbers of the Swedish royal court in 1008, when the Swedish royal family converted to Christianity, although the king promised to respect the freedom of religio
2 CONC n - Sweden was not to be Christian until the last religious war between Inge the Elder and Blot-Sweyn of 1084-1088.
2 CONT
2 CONT Snorre Sturlasson wrote about her, that Estrid was unkind to the children (Emund, Astrid and Holmfrid) of her husband's mistress Edla; "Queen Estrid was arrogant and not kind towards her stepchildren, and therefore the king sent h
2 CONC is son Emund to Vendland, where he was brought up by his maternal relatives".
2 CONT
2 CONT Not much is known of Estrid as a person. Snorre Sturlasson mentions her as a lover of pomp and luxury, and as hard and strict towards her servants.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Ingegerd Olofsdotter (circa 1001-1054), Grand Princess of Kiev, in Russia called Anna, married Yaroslav I the Wise, prince of Novgorod and Kiev.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Anund Jacob (circa 1010-1050), king of Sweden, succeeded Olaf as king in c. 1022.
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
2 CONT Herman Lindqvist: "Historien om Sveriges drottningar" (History of the queens of Sweden) (2006) (In Swedish)
2 CONT
2 CONT Åke Ohlmarks: "Alla Sveriges drottningar" (All the queens of Sweden) (In Swedish)
2 CONT
2 CONT Snorri Sturluson
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Wikipedia page on the Obotrites:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Obotrites
2 CONT
2 CONT The Obotrites (German: Abodriten), also commonly known as the Obodrites, Abotrites, or Abodrites, were a confederation of medieval West Slavic tribes within the territory of modern Mecklenburg and Holstein in northern Germany (see
2 CONC  Polabian Slavs). For decades they were allies of Charlemagne in his wars against Germanic Saxons and Slavic Veleti.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 798 the Obodrites, ruled by prince Drożko, or Draško (Thrasco, orig.), defeated Saxons in the battle on Swentana river. The still heathen Saxons (Nordalbingians) were dispersed by the emperor, and the part of their former land 
2 CONC in Holstein north of Elbe was awarded to the Obotrites in 804, as a reward for their victory. This however was soon reverted by invasion of Danes.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Bavarian Geographer, an anonymous medieval document compiled in Regensburg in 830, contains a list of the tribes in Central Eastern Europe to the east of the Elbe. The list includes the Nortabtrezi (Obotrites) - with 53 civita
2 CONC tes. Adam of Bremen referred to them as the Reregi because of their lucrative trade emporium Reric. In common with other Slavic groups, they were often described by Germanic sources as Wends.
2 CONT
2 CONT The main tribes[1] of the Obotritic confederation were:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. the Obotrites proper (Wismar Bay to Lake Schwerin);
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. the Wagrians (the eastern Holstein as part of Saxony);
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. the Warnower (the upper Warnow and Mildenitz);
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. the Polabians proper (between the Trave and the Elbe, SUPPOSEDLY OUR ANCESTOR COMES FROM HERE).
2 CONT
2 CONT Other tribes associated with the confederation include:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. the Linonen near Lenzen,
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. the Travnjane near the Trave,
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. the Drevani in the Hanoverian Wendland and the northern Altmark.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT The Limes Saxoniae forming the border between the Saxons to the west and the Obotrites to the eastAs allies of the Carolingian kings and the empire of their Ottonian successors, the Obotrites fought from 808 to 1200 against the ki
2 CONC ngs of Denmark, who wished to rule the Baltic region independently of the empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT When opportunities arose, for instance upon the death of an emperor, they would seek to seize power; and in 983 Hamburg was destroyed by the Obotrites under their king, Mstivoj. At times they levied tribute from the Danes and Saxo
2 CONC ns.
2 CONT
2 CONT Under the leadership of Niklot, they resisted a Christian assault during the Wendish Crusade.
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Wikipedia page on the Polabians:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polabians_(tribe)
2 CONT
2 CONT The Polabians (German: Polaben; Latin: Polabi) were a constituent West Slavic tribe of the Obotrites who lived between the Trave and the Elbe. The main settlement of the Polabians was Racisburg (modern Ratzeburg), named after thei
2 CONC r Prince Ratibor. The Polabians were similar to the Drevani, also known as the Draväno-polaben or Drevanen, in Lüchow-Dannenberg.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ben notes: The picture used to depict this person is not actually Estrid, but rather is a Swedish chess piece (a Queen) from Estrid's time period, near the turn of the first millenium AD.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Estrid of the Obotrites
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Estrid (or Astrid) of the Obotrites (ca. 979 – 1035), was a Swedish queen consort, married to Olof Skötkonung, the King of Sweden, ca. 1000, and mother of king Anund Jacob of Sweden.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Legend say Estrid was taken back to Sweden as a war-price; she was most likely given by her father, a chieftain of the Polabian Obotrites, as a peace offering in a marriage to seal the peace, and the guess is that she brought a gr
2 CONC eat dowry, as a great Slavic influence is represented in Sweden from her time, mainly among craftsmen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her husband also had a mistress, Edla, who came from the same area in Europe as herself and who was perhaps taken to Sweden at the same time, and the king treated Edla and Estrid the same way and gave his son and two daughters wit
2 CONC h Edla the same privilegies as the children he had with Estrid, though it was Estrid he married and made queen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Queen Estrid was baptised with her husband, their children and large parts of the Swedish court in 1008, when the Swedish royal family converted to Christianity, though the king promised to respect the freedom of religion - Sweden
2 CONC  was not to be Christian until the last religion war of 1084-1088.
2 CONT
2 CONT Snorre Sturlasson wrote, that Estrid was unkind to the children (Emund, Astrid and Holmfrid) of her husband's mistress Edla;
2 CONT
2 CONT " Queen Estrid was arrogant and not kind towards her stepchildren and therefore the king sent his son Emund to Vendland, were he was brought up by his maternal relatives".
2 CONT
2 CONT Not much is known of Estrid as a person. Snorre Sturlasson mentions her as the lover of pomp and luxury and as hard and strict towards her servants.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Children
2 CONT
2 CONT Their son Anund Jacob succeeded Olaf as king. A daughter, Ingegerd Olofsdotter, in Russia called Anna, married Yaroslav I the Wise, prince of Novgorod and Kiev.
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
2 CONT Herman Lindqvist, "Historien om Sverige" (In Swedish)
2 CONT
2 CONT Åke Ohlmarks "Alla Sveriges drottningar" (In Swedish)
