1 OCCU Kung i Sverige ca 995-1022, King of Sweden, King, Konge av Sverige, Kung av Sverige, Skotakonungur, Kung i Sverige, Kung, Ruotsin kuningas 993-1022, Ruotsin kuningas, otti kristinuskon kasteen I. Ruotsissa., Kung i Sverige ca 995 
2 CONC - ca 1022
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Olof Skötkonung + Estrid (wife)<br/>
2 CONT 1. Anund Jacob<br/>
2 CONT 2. Ingegerd (married to Yaroslav the Wise)<br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT Olof Skötkonung + Edla (partner)<br/>
2 CONT 3. Astrid<br/>
2 CONT 4. Emund (Emund den Gamla / Emund the Old)<br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT A fifth child, Homfrid (married to Sven Ladejarl), is sometimes mentioned as daughter of Edla and sometimes as the sister to Olof. She can of course be both….
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Foundation for Medieval Genealogy page on Sweden:
2 CONT
2 CONT '''content to clean up'''
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/SWEDEN.htm#OlofIdied1022A
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.friesian.com/germania.htm#norse
2 CONT
2 CONT OLOF I 995-1022, ANUND JAKOB 1022-1050, EMUND 1050-1060
2 CONT
2 CONT OLOF, son of ERIK "Segersäll/the Victorious" King of Sweden & his wife Sigrid "Storråda/the Haughty" ([before 985]-[1022]).
2 CONT
2 CONT Adam of Bremen names Olav as son of King Erik & his (unnamed) wife who later married Svend King of Denmark[38].  Snorre names "Olaf the Swede" as the son of "the Swedish king, Eirik the Victorious" and his wife Sigrid[39].
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Saxo Grammaticus, after his father's death, he returned to Sweden [from Denmark] with "his mother Syritha, and stayed there exercising his sovereignty under his mother's tutelage"[40].
2 CONT
2 CONT He succeeded as OLOF I "Skotkonung/under-King" King of Sweden.
2 CONT
2 CONT Adam of Bremen records that King Olof invaded Denmark and expelled King Svend, but allowed him to return to his kingdom because "matrem suam habuerit"[41].
2 CONT
2 CONT He converted to Christianity and was baptised as "JACOBUS"[42].
2 CONT
2 CONT m ESTRED of the Obotrites, daughter of ---.  Adam of Bremen names "filiamque Sclavorum Estred nomine de Obodritis" as wife of "Olaph rex Sueonum"[43].
2 CONT
2 CONT Mistress (1): EDLA, daughter of ---, from Vinland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Snorre names "Edla, a daughter of an earl of Vindland" as the concubine of King Olof[44].
2 CONT
2 CONT King Olof & his wife had two children:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. INGIGERD Olafsdottir ([1000/03]-10 Feb 1050).
2 CONT
2 CONT Snorre names "the king's daughter Ingegerd" when recording that she was used as intermediary to effect a reconciliation between her father and Olav Haraldson King of Norway, and that her marriage to the Norwegian king was proposed
2 CONC [45].  Adam of Bremen names "filius Iacobus et filia Ingrad" as the children of "Olaph rex Sueonum" and his wife Estred, specifying that Ingrad married "rex sanctus Gerzlef de Ruzzia"[46].
2 CONT
2 CONT Morkinskinna names “Queen Ingigerdr the daughter of King Óláfr the Swede” as wife of “King Yaroslav [of] Russia”[47].
2 CONT
2 CONT Her birth date range is estimated based from the birth of her oldest child in 1020, and her youngest known child in [1036].
2 CONT
2 CONT Snorre records the betrothal of "Ingegerd the king's daughter" and "King Jarisleif…from Russia"[48].  The Historia Norwegie records the marriage of "sororem Olaui Sueonensis…Margaretam" and "rex Iarezlafus de Ruscia" at her brothe
2 CONC r's instigation, after her betrothal to Olav of Norway was terminated[49].
2 CONT
2 CONT It is more probable that she was the daughter rather than sister of King Olof if it is correct that she had 10 children by her husband.  She is referred to as IRINA in Russian sources[50].
2 CONT
2 CONT The Primary Chronicle records the death of "the Princess wife of Yaroslav" 10 Feb [1048/50][51].
2 CONT
2 CONT m (1019) as his second wife, IAROSLAV I Vladimirovich Grand Prince of Kiev, son of VLADIMIR Grand Prince of Kiev & his first wife Rognoda of Polotsk ([978]-20 Feb 1054).
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. ANUND JAKOB (-[1052]).
2 CONT
2 CONT Adam of Bremen names "filius Iacobus et filia Ingrad" as the children of "Olaph rex Sueonum" and his wife Estred, in a later passage clarifying that the son was "Anund…dictus est Iacobus"[52].
2 CONT
2 CONT He succeeded his father in [1022] as ANUND JAKOB King of Sweden.
2 CONT
2 CONT m as her first husband, GUNHILD Svensdatter, daughter of Jarl SVEN Haakonsson & his wife Holmfrid of Sweden (-1060 or after).
2 CONT
2 CONT The primary source which confirms her first marriage has not yet been identified.  Adam of Bremen refers to the marriage of "rex iuvenis Suein" and "consanguineam a Suedia", the king being threatened with excommunication by the Ar
2 CONC chbishop of Bremen and papal letters, his wife being named "Gunhild [vel Giuthe] reginam" in a later passage which records that after her separation she devoted herself to charitable activities on her estates[53].
2 CONT
2 CONT Her parentage is referred to in Knytlinga Saga[54].  Snorre records that "Gunhild, Earl Svein's other daughter" was married to "the Danish king Svein Ulfson"[55].
2 CONT
2 CONT She married secondly (1052) as his first wife, Svend II King of Denmark.
2 CONT
2 CONT King Olof had three illegitimate children by Mistress (1):
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. ASTRID.
2 CONT
2 CONT Snorre names "Emund, Astrid, Holmfrid" as the children of King Olof by his concubine Edla, specifying that Astrid was brought up in West Gautland in the house of Egil[56].
2 CONT
2 CONT Adam of Bremen records that the wife of Olav King of Norway was "rege Sueonum…filiam"[57].  The Historia Norwegie records that Olav married "soror Margarete" after his betrothal to the latter was terminated by her marriage to "rex
2 CONC  Iarezlafus de Ruscia"[58].
2 CONT
2 CONT Snorre records the marriage of King Olav and "Astrid, daughter of the Swedish king Olaf"[59].  Her marriage was arranged to appease Swedish opposition to King Olav II's recently assumed rule.  She remained in Sweden with her daugh
2 CONC ter when her husband left for Russia[60].
2 CONT
2 CONT m (Feb 1019) OLAV II King of Norway, son of HARALD "Grenske" King of Vingulmark, Vestfold and Agder & his wife Asta Gudbransdatter (maybe posthumously 995-killed in battle Stiklestad 29 Jul 1030, bur in a sandbank in the river at 
2 CONC Trondheim, transferred to St Clement's church which later became Trondheim Cathedral).
2 CONT
2 CONT 4.  HOLMFRID .
2 CONT
2 CONT Snorre names "Emund, Astrid, Holmfrid" as the children of King Olof by his concubine Edla[61].
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. EMUND (-1060).
2 CONT
2 CONT Snorre names "Emund, Astrid, Holmfrid" as the children of King Olof by his concubine Edla, specifying that Emund was sent to Vindland to be fostered by his mother's relations where "he for a long time neglected his Christianity"[6
2 CONC 2].  Adam of Bremen names "Emund" as son of "rex Olaph…a concubina"[63].
2 CONT
2 CONT He succeeded in [1052] as EMUND Slemme "den Gamle/the Old" King of Sweden.
2 CONT
2 CONT m [firstly] ---.  The name of Emund's first wife is not known.  [m secondly as her second husband, ASTRID Njalsdotter, widow of RAGNVALD Ulfsson Jarl of Västergötland, daughter of NJAL --- & his wife ---.
2 CONT
2 CONT The primary source which confirms her parentage and two marriages has not yet been identified.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her supposed second marriage to Ragnvald may be nothing more than a guess based on Adam of Bremen recording that "nepos eius [=rex Sueonum Emund] Stinkel" succeeded on the death of Emund[64].]
2 CONT
2 CONT King Emund & his [first] wife had [two] children:
2 CONT
2 CONT a)  ANUND (-before 1056).
2 CONT
2 CONT The primary source which confirms his parentage has not yet been identified.
2 CONT
2 CONT b)  [daughter .
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Europäische Stammtafeln[65], Stenkil married the unnamed daughter of King Emund.  The primary source on which this is based has not yet been identified.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is possible that the marriage is uncorroborated in contemporary documentation but was assumed by later genealogists to explain Stenkil's succession as king.  However, the accession could have been justified solely on the basis 
2 CONC of his being the king's stepson, although it is not impossible that it was also confirmed by subsequent marriage to his predecessor's daughter, if indeed he had one[66].
2 CONT
2 CONT m STENKIL Ragnvaldson, son of RAGNVALD Jarl of Västergötland & his wife Ingeborg of Norway (-1066).
2 CONT
2 CONT He succeeded his father-in-law in 1060 as STENKIL King of Sweden.]
2 CONT
2 CONT * http://www.thepeerage.com/p4289.htm#i42889
2 CONT * http://books.google.com/books?id=6B4CAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA135&dq=skotkonung&lr=& ei=pmPYSNiILJbQzATd9_DrDg#PPA148,M1
2 CONT
2 CONT "There is some debate about the meaning of the name, Skotkonung, but the most reasonable explanation is that it stems from skattekonung, meaning 'treasure king', since he was the first king to mint money of Sweden."  by Martina Sp
2 CONC rague, Sweden, pg. 62.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT association (1): Edla
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage (2): Estrid of the Obotrites
2 CONT
2 CONT Another name for Olof was Olaf the Tax King.
2 CONT
2 CONT General Notes:
2 CONT
2 CONT Olof lot seg døpe i Husaby i Västergötland som nå ble en støtte for de kristne konger i deres strid mot Upsveerne. Det store offerstedet i Upsala forble enda i et århundre hedenskapens faste borg.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sverige hadde fred under Oluf, som sies å ha holdt mest av å sitte hjemme, hvor han førte et prektig hoff. Han var neppe spesielt likt av sitt folk, spesielt ikke av upsveerne, som ikke kunne tilgi at han hadde gått over til krist
2 CONC endommen. De tvang ham til å slutte forlik med Olav den Hellige i Norge som han hadde stilt seg fientlig til. Da han ikke holdt seg til avtalen, ble han avsatt av upsveerne som valge hans sønn, Anund Jakob, til konge. Olov beholdt
2 CONC  imidlertid kongeverdigheten til sin død i 1021. Kort tid tidligere ble det holdt et møte mellom ham og Olav den Hellige hvor han måtte gå med på fred og forbund med Norge.
2 CONT
2 CONT Samtidig kildemateriale om Olof foreligger dels i form av noen hundre mynt med innskrifter, som nevner ham og flere myntmestere samt pregningsbyen Sigtuna, dels i form av et dobbeltsidig blyavtrykk fra denne myntpregning funnet ve
2 CONC d utgravinger der. Myntene er etterligninger av anglosaksiske typer fra slutten av 900-tallet. Den Olav som i henhold til en anglosaksisk krønika angrep London i 994 sammen med Svend Tveskjegg, kan derfor ha vært Olof Skötkonung o
2 CONC g ikke Olav Trygvesson som man har antatt tidligere. Olof er den første av de svenske konger som det kan bevises har latt prege mynt. Slike foreligger også fra hans sønn og etterfølger, Anund Jakob, men den svenske myntningen syne
2 CONC s deretter å ha stanset opp og har først blitt igjenopptatt mot slutten av det følgende århundre.
2 CONT
2 CONT I "Gesta Hammaburgensis ecclesiae pontificum" skrevet på 1070-tallet av Adams av Bremen, oppgis at Olofs far, Erik Segersäll, beseiret Svend Tjugeskjegg og besatte Danmark, at Svend tok sitt rike i besittelse etter Eriks død, samt
2 CONC  at Olof fordrev ham og erobret Danmark. Da Svend hadde giftet seg med Eriks enke, ble han gjeninnsatt av Olof. Hvorvidt dette er riktig har blitt trukket i tvil, man har antatt at det kun vitnet om Adams kritiske syn på Svend. Se
2 CONC nere har det imidlertid blitt påpekt at informasjonen skulle kunne stamme fra Svends dattersønn kong Svend Estridsson.
2 CONT
2 CONT Adam av Bremen oppgir også at Olof var aliert med Svends sønn Knut den mektige da denne erobret England i 1015. Videre skal Olof i følge Adam ha ønsket å rive ned det hedniske tempelet i (Gamle) Uppsala. Han grundet biskopsetet i 
2 CONC Skara og sendte store gaver til erkebiskop Unwan i Bremen gjennom formidling av Thurgot, den første biskopen i Skara.
2 CONT
2 CONT King of Sweden whose efforts to impose Christianity were frustrated by the leading non-Christian Swedish chieftains.
2 CONT
2 CONT The son of King Erik the Victorious, Olaf opposed the development of a strong Norwegian state and joined Sweyn I Forkbeard, king of Denmark, and his allies in a victorious war against Norway in 1000.
2 CONT
2 CONT Olaf subsequently married his illegitimate daughter Holmfrith to the earl Sweyn, one of the Danish viceroys in Norway. Initially opposed to Olaf II Haraldsson, king of Norway, he later made peace with him and married his other ill
2 CONC egitimate daughter, Astrid, to the Norwegian ruler. His legitimate daughter, Ingigerth, married Yaroslav I the Wise, grand prince of Kiev.
2 CONT
2 CONT A committed Christian, Olaf was prevented by advocates of the native Norse religion, based at the temple at Uppsala, from personally enforcing conversion. Missionaries from many European countries, however, carried out the work of
2 CONC  conversion. Olaf's life is described extensively in Icelandic sagas of the 13th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT Noted events in his life were:
2 CONT
2 CONT • Acceded: King of Sweden, Cir 995.
2 CONT
2 CONT Olof had children with Edla.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Died in winter, Husaby in Västergötland
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Swedish-language Historiska Personer i Sverige och Norden page on Olof Skotkonung - Eriksson (English below): http://historiska-personer.nu/min-s/pea1bc5fa.html
2 CONT
2 CONT In English:
2 CONT
2 CONT Olof Skotkonung (Eriksson)
2 CONT * Occupation: King of Sweden c.995 - c.1022
2 CONT * Father: Eric The Victorious (945-995)
2 CONT * Mother: Sigrid (d. 1000)
2 CONT * Born: 980
2 CONT * Died: 1022
2 CONT * Buried: Husaby, Husaby Cathedral
2 CONT
2 CONT Family with Edla:
2 CONT
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Edmund gamle "Slemme" (The Elder) Olofsson (995-1061)
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Astrid Olofsdotter (d. 1035)
2 CONT
2 CONT Family with Estrid (b. 979)
2 CONT
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Ingegerd "Holy Anna of Novgorod" Olofsdotter (1000-1050)
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Anund James Olofsson (1007-1050)
2 CONT
2 CONT Entries:
2 CONT
2 CONT Olof was born about 980, died in 1022.
2 CONT
2 CONT Olof is reported to have been the first king to rule over both the Swedes and the Goths (Geats), which is believed to have been achieved in the 990s. He is an obscure figure about whom we know little other than he actually existed
2 CONC , that he was a Christian (by tradition, baptized by St. Brigida at Husaby, Vastergotland, in 1008), that he was the son of Eric the Victorious, but that his mother's name is uncertain.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the first to adopt coins in Sweden, minting them at Sigtuna, which he seems to have founded. Coins were introduced by the English.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is unclear how much real power he may have held. In the uplands, he had little, for he had great unease over Uppsala. He is also later removed by the Swedes. However, Sweden's first diocese was established at Skara during his t
2 CONC ime.
2 CONT
2 CONT Olof must have participated in the Battle of Svolder as allies of King Sweyn of Denmark and Earl Eirik. That battle, which took place on 9 September 1000, defeated the King Olav Haraldsson of Norway.
2 CONT
2 CONT The name Skotkonung means "tributary king," but he never seemed to have received tributes. This name possibly refers to the coins he introduced. According to one theory, they were never supposed to have been circulated as legal te
2 CONC nder, but were instead considered to be gifts ("tribute") to reward allies.
2 CONT
2 CONT Olof was the half brother of Canute of Denmark, and they formed a major political alliance. In Sigtuna, coins have been found with Canute's name, the so-called Knut coins with the text CNUT REX EN or "Knut, King of Swedes," and in
2 CONC  a letter from 1027, Canute calls himself the "King of All England and Denmark and the Vikings, and some of the Swedes."
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From the English Wikipedia page on Olof Skotkonung:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olof_Sk%C3%B6tkonung
2 CONT
2 CONT Olof Skötkonung (English exonym: Olaf Scotking ; Old Icelandic: Óláfr sœnski, Old Swedish: Olawær skotkonongær) was a Swedish king, son of Eric the Victorious and Sigrid the Haughty. He was born around 980 and he succeeded his fat
2 CONC her in 995.
2 CONT
2 CONT Etymology
2 CONT
2 CONT One of many explanations to his Swedish name Skötkonung is that it means "tributary king" and one English scholar speculates about a tributary relationship to the Danish king Sweyn Forkbeard, who was his stepfather.[1] This explan
2 CONC ation is however highly speculative as it is not supported by any evidence or historical sources.
2 CONT
2 CONT Another possible explanation[citation needed] of the name "Skötkonung" is that it means "treasure king" and refers to the fact that he was the first Swedish king to stamp coins. An ancient land ownership ceremony called scotting (
2 CONC from which the Swedish word for a tax or treasure also derives) may also have been involved.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Life
2 CONT
2 CONT Our knowledge of Olof is mostly based on Snorri Sturluson's and Adam of Bremen's accounts, which have been subject to criticism from source-critical scholars. But according to Adam of Bremen, Sweyn Forkbeard was forced to defend h
2 CONC is Danish kingdom from attacks by Olof who claimed the Danish throne.
2 CONT
2 CONT The conflict was resolved by Sweyn's marriage with Olaf's mother and the two kings were thereafter allies. Also Snorri Sturluson describes Sweyn and Olof as equal allies when they defeated the Norwegian king Olav Tryggvason in the
2 CONC  battle of Svolder 1000, and thereafter divided Norway between themselves.
2 CONT
2 CONT Viking expedition to Wendland
2 CONT
2 CONT In a Viking expedition to Wendland, he had captured Edla, the daughter of a Wendish chieftain, and she gave him the son Emund (who was to become king of Sweden), and the daughter Astrid -later wife of Olaf II of Norway. He later m
2 CONC arried Estrid of the Obotrites, and she bore him the son Anund Jacob and the daughter Ingegerd Olofsdotter.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alliance with Sweyn Forkbeard
2 CONT
2 CONT Olof is said to have preferred royal sports to war and therefore Sweyn Forkbeard retook Denmark, which Olof's father Eric had conquered.[3] Olof also lost the right to tribute which his predecessors had preserved in what is now Es
2 CONC tonia and Latvia.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1000, he allied with Sweyn Forkbeard, who was married to Olof's mother, and with the Norwegian Jarls Eric and Sven, against the Norwegian King Olaf Tryggvason. Olaf Tryggvason died in the Battle of Svolder and Olof gained a par
2 CONC t of Trøndelag as well as modern Bohuslän.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Norwegian-Swedish War
2 CONT
2 CONT When the Norwegian kingdom was reestablished by Olaf II of Norway, a new war erupted between Norway and Sweden. Many men in both Sweden and Norway tried to reconcile the kings.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1018, Olof's cousin, the earl of Västergötland, Ragnvald Ulfsson and the Norwegian king's emissaries Björn Stallare and Hjalti Skeggiason had arrived at the thing of Uppsala in an attempt to sway the Swedish king to accept peac
2 CONC e and as a warrant marry his daughter Ingegerd Olofsdotter (OUR ANCESTOR) to the king of Norway. The Swedish king was greatly angered and threatened to banish Ragnvald from his kingdom, but Ragnvald was supported by his foster-fat
2 CONC her Thorgny Lawspeaker.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thorgny delivered a powerful speech in which he reminded the king of the great Viking expeditions in the East that predecessors such as Erik Eymundsson and Björn had undertaken, without having the hubris not to listen to their men
2 CONC 's advice. Thorgny, himself, had taken part in many successful pillaging expeditions with Olof's father Eric the Victorious and even Eric had listened to his men.
2 CONT
2 CONT The present king wanted nothing but Norway, which no Swedish king before him had desired. This displeased the Swedish people, who were eager to follow the king on new ventures in the East to win back the kingdoms that paid tribute
2 CONC  to his ancestors, but it was the wish of the people that the king make peace with the king of Norway and give him his daughter Ingegerd as queen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thorgny finished his speech by saying: if you do not desire to do so, we shall assault you and kill you and not brook anymore of your warmongering and obstinacy. Our ancestors have done so, who at Mula thing threw five kings in a 
2 CONC well, kings who were too arrogant as you are against us.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, Olof married his daughter Ingegerd-Irene to Yaroslav I the Wise instead. An impending war was settled when Olof agreed to share his power with his son Anund Jacob. Olof was also forced to accept a settlement with Olaf II 
2 CONC of Norway at Kungahälla, who already had been married (unbeknownst to Olof) with Olof's daughter, Astrid, through the Geatish jarl Ragnvald Ulfsson.
2 CONT
2 CONT Christian King
2 CONT
2 CONT Olof was baptised, probably by the missionary Sigfrid, c.1008, and he was the first Swedish king to remain Christian until his death. However, according to Adam of Bremen, the fact that the vast majority of the Swedes were still p
2 CONC agan forced him to limit Christian activities to the already Christian border province of Västergötland.
2 CONT
2 CONT When he stamped coins in Sigtuna in the province of Uppland Olof used the word rex for king. OLUF REX as in the coin displayed above or OLAF REX. The use of Latin seems to suggest that he was already baptised at this time but on t
2 CONC he other hand the coins were imitating English pennies in type and style.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sigtuna is written SITUN, ZINT (in the coin above), ZTNETEI, or SIDEI. The two last has been deciphered as Si(gtuna) Dei meaning God's Sigtuna.[5][6]
2 CONT
2 CONT Óláfsdrápa sœnska
2 CONT
2 CONT The Icelandic skald Óttarr svarti spent some time at Olof's court and composed the poem Óláfsdrápa sœnska describing Olof's war expeditions in the east. Other skalds who served Olof were Gunnlaugr ormstunga, Hrafn Önundarson and G
2 CONC izurr svarti.
2 CONT
2 CONT Death
2 CONT
2 CONT His death is said to have taken place in the winter of 1021–1022. According to a legend he was martyred at Stockholm after refusing to sacrifice to pagan gods. He's venerated as a saint in the Catholic Church.
2 CONT
2 CONT Since the 1740s, it has been claimed that he was buried in Husaby in the Christian part of his kingdom, but such identifications are controversial. The remains in the alleged grave are also too young to be his.
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.^ Sawyer, Peter (1997). The Oxford Illustrated History of the Vikings. Oxford University Press, 1997. ISBN 0192854348, p.169.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.^ Throne of a Thousand Years by Jacob Truedson Demitz
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.^ Adam of Bremen (book 2, chapter 30)
2 CONT
2 CONT 4.^ Snorri Sturluson, "Heimskringla" (Olav Tryggvason's saga, chapter 113)
2 CONT
2 CONT 5.^ Thunmark-Nylén, Lena + (1981). Vikingatidens ABC, Statens historiska museum, 1981. ISBN 91-7192-490-6, p.232.
2 CONT
2 CONT 6.^ Maiander, Harry + (1947). Sveriges historia genom tiderna. Första delen. Stockholm, 1947. p.159.
2 CONT
