1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guillaume_Ier_de_Provence
2 CONT
2 CONT *https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_I_of_Provence
2 CONT
2 CONT *http://genealogics.org/getperson.php?personID=I00094928&tree=LEO
2 CONT
2 CONT Guillaume II 'le Liberateur' Comte d'Arles
2 CONT
2 CONT Parents: Boson Comte d'Arles & Constantia Spouses: 1. Arsinde (no children) 2. Adélaïs d'Anjou Children:
2 CONT * Guillaume III Comte de Provence ([986/87]-1018 before 30 May) married Gerberge de Mâcon
2 CONT * Constance ([987/89]-Château de Melun 22 or 25 Jul 1032) married Robert II King of France
2 CONT -------------
2 CONT   From the Foundation for Medieval Genealogy page for Provence: 
2 CONT
2 CONT *http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/PROVENCE.htm#GuillaumeIIArlesProvencedied993
2 CONT
2 CONT GUILLAUME [II] “le Libérateur”, son of ([955]-Avignon 993 after 29 Aug, bur Sarrians, église de Sainte-Croix).  
2 CONT
2 CONT "Boso comes et uxor sua Constantia…illorum filii…Willelmus comes, Rotbaldus comes, Pontius juvenis" signed the charter dated May [963] under which "Gencius et uxor mea Aiburga" donated property "in pago Aquense superiore ad castru
2 CONC m…Ansoyse" to Monmajour[250].  
2 CONT
2 CONT The order of birth of the two sons of Boson is unclear.  The May [963] charter suggests that Guillaume was his older son.  However, Guillaume is omitted from the charter dated Mar 965 under which "eius filio Rothboldo et fratre ei
2 CONC us Wilelmo comite" consented to the charter of "Bosoni comitis, filii Rothboldi quondam"[251], which suggests that Rotbald was older.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Comte de Provence, charters showing that both he and his older brother Rotbald were recorded as counts during the same period, although it is not known whether this was a joint countship or whether there was a geographical split b
2 CONC etween their jurisdictions.   
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Marquis de Provence. ''' "Vuilelmus marchius Arelatense Provintie" donated property "in comitatu Avinionense, in agro Rupiano, in loco…la Lona" to Saint-Victor, Marseille by charter dated 17 Apr 979, signed by "Arsinda comitiss
2 CONC a"[252].  "Willelmus comes" donated property to Cluny by charter dated 28 Aug [990] signed by "Rodbaldus comes, Adalaix comitissa, Wilelmus comes et filius eius Wilelmus"[253].  "Dominus princeps et marchio istius provinciæ…Willel
2 CONC mus cum coniuge sua…Adelaix et filio suo…Willelmo" restored property to the abbey of Saint-Césaire d´Arles by charter dated 992, subscribed by "Domnus Rotbaldus comes…Willelmus comes filius Rotbaldi et uxor sua Lucia, Wilelmus com
2 CONC es Tolosanus et uxor sua Ema…"[254].  He became a monk.  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' firstly (before Apr 970) ARSINDE, daughter of --- (-after 17 Apr 979).  "Wilelmus comes Provincie et coniunx mea Arsinna" donated property to Saint-Victor de Marseille by charter dated Apr 970[255].  
2 CONT
2 CONT "Vuilelmus marchius Arelatense Provintie" donated property "in comitatu Avinionense, in agro Rupiano, in loco…la Lona" to Saint-Victor, Marseille by charter dated 17 Apr 979, signed by "Arsinda comitissa"[256].  Szabolcs de Vajay 
2 CONC suggests that the first wife of Marquis Guillaume was the sister of Adelais, whose first testament dated 4 Oct 978 names her, basing the hypothesis on onomastics and favorable chronology[257].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Under this testament of "Adelais", she donated her foundation "Narbonam…sanctique Salvatoris" to "sororibus meis et domnæ Arsindæ comitissæ", bequeathed "mea hereditas de Vidiliano" to "Arsindi sorori meæ", "alodes de Tolomiano" t
2 CONC o "Ermesindi", and "mea hereditas de Artimiciano" to "Garsindi"[258].  The wording suggests that "Arsindi…Ermesindi…Garsindi" were all sisters of the testator, although the relationship is not specified in the case of Arsinde.  
2 CONT
2 CONT It is probable that "domnæ Arsindæ comitissæ" in this document was the wife of Guillaume II Comte de Provence as no other Ctss Arsende has been  identified at the time.  However, the wording of the passage in which she is named su
2 CONC ggests that she was a different person from "Arsindi sorori meæ".  Szabolcs de Vajay suggests (as reported by Settipani: the Szabolcs article has not yet been consulted) that the testator was the possible daughter of Arnaud [I] Co
2 CONC mte de Comminges.  
2 CONT
2 CONT She can be identified as Adelais, widow of Matfried Vicomte de Narbonne, as the document names the couple´s two sons whose affiliation is confirmed by other primary sources.  As explained more fully in the document TOULOUSE, KINGS
2 CONC , DUKES & COUNTS, other primary source documentation suggests that the wife of Vicomte Matfried may have been the daughter of Raymond Pons Comte de Toulouse.  If this is correct, the chronology suggests that her sister would have 
2 CONC been too old to have married Guillaume II Comte de Provence.  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' secondly ([984/86]) as her fourth husband, ADELAIS [Blanche] d'Anjou, widow firstly of ETIENNE de Brioude, secondly of RAYMOND IV Comte de Toulouse, divorced wife (thirdly) of LOUIS V King of the West Franks, daughter of F
2 CONC OULQUES II "le Bon" Comte d’Anjou & his first wife Gerberge --- ([945/50]-1026, bur Montmajour, near Arles).    
2 CONT
2 CONT Comte Guillaume [II] & his second wife had two children:  1. Guillaume III (b. 986/987, d. 1018, before 30 May, Comte de Provence) 2. Constance (b. 987/989, d. 22 or 25 July 1032 at the Chateau de Melun, buried eglise de l'Abbaye 
2 CONC royale de St-Denis, Queen of France)
2 CONT
2 CONT References:
2 CONT
2 CONT [250] Manteyer (1908), p. 225, quoting Chantelou Histoire de Monmajour, Revue Historique de Provence, 1ère année, p. 37. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [251] Gallia Christiana Novissima, Marseille, Col. 47-48, no. 66, and Marseille Saint-Victor Tome I,  29, p. 40. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [252] Marseille Saint-Victor Tome II, Appendix, 1042, p. 509. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [253] Cluny Tome III, 1837, p. 80.   
2 CONT
2 CONT [254] Histoire Générale de Languedoc 3rd Edn. Tome V, Preuves, Chartes et Diplômes, 153, col. 325. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [255] Marseille Saint-Victor Tome I,  598, p. 590. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [256] Marseille Saint-Victor Tome II, Appendix, 1042, p. 509. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [257] Vajay, S. de 'Comtesses d'origine occitane dans la Marche d'Espagne aux 10e et 11e siècles', Hidalguia 28 (1980), p. 756, cited in Settipani (2004), p. 63 footnote 1.
2 CONT
2 CONT [258] Histoire Générale de Languedoc 3rd Edn. Tome V, Preuves, Chartes et Diplômes, 130, col. 284. 
2 CONT ---------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT William I (c. 950 – 993, after 29 August), called the Liberator, was Count of Avignon in 962, Count of Provence from 968 to his abdication. In 975 or 979, he took the title of marchio or margrave (Marquis de Provence Arles). He is
2 CONC  often considered the founder of the county of Provence. (French Wikipedia says that he was Prince of All Provence in 991. He is called William II because of an uncle who carried the same name.)
2 CONT
2 CONT He and his elder brother Rotbold II, sons of Boso II of Arles and Constance of Viennois, daughter of Charles-Constantine, both carried the title of comes or count concurrently, but it is unknown if they were joint-counts of the wh
2 CONC ole of Provence or if the region was divided. His brother never bore any other title than count so long as William lived, so the latter seems to have attained a certain supremacy.
2 CONT
2 CONT (According to French Wikipedia, the brothers obtained the shared title after their father Boson and uncle William relinquished the title in their favor between 962 and 966.)
2 CONT
2 CONT In 980, he was installed as Count of Arles. 
2 CONT
2 CONT His sobriquet comes from his victories against the Saracens by which he liberated Provence from their threat, which had been constant since the establishment of a base at Fraxinet. (French Wikipedia states: Following the removal o
2 CONC f the Abbot of Mayeul in July 972 by Saracens who established themselves at Maures in the late 9th century, Count William and his brother Rotbold led the Provencal army, reinforced by troops from Count Ardouin of Turin, against th
2 CONC e Moors.) At the Battle of Tourtour in 973, with the assistance of the counts of the High Alps and the viscounts of Marseille and Fos, he definitively routed the Saracens, chasing them forever from Provence. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He reorganised the region east of the Rhône, which he conquered from the Saracens and which had been given him as a gift from King Conrad of Burgundy. Also by royal consent, he and his descendants controlled the fisc (treasury) in
2 CONC  Provence. 
2 CONT
2 CONT With Isarn, Bishop of Grenoble, he repopulated Dauphiné and settled an Italian count named Ugo Blavia near Fréjus in the early 970s in order to bring that land back to cultivation. For all this, he figures prominently in Ralph Gla
2 CONC ber's chronicle with the title of dux and he appears in a charter of 992 as pater patriae.
2 CONT
2 CONT (French Wikipedia states: Like his father Boson, William is advised by a viscount. From 977, he accompanied all his movements. A group of judges rendered justice under William. He moved to Arles in the early 980s. In 991, the Bish
2 CONC op of Frejus, Riculf, begged for a restitution of some of the old estates of the bishopric. William granted half of Frejus and the village of Puget. In 992, he donates important areas of Camargue to the Monastery of St-Jean d'Arle
2 CONC s.)
2 CONT
2 CONT He donated land to Cluny and retired to become a monk, dying at Avignon, where he was buried in the church of Saint-Croix at Sarrians. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was succeeded as margrave by his brother. His great principality began to diminish soon after his death as the castles of his vassals, which he had kept carefully under ducal control, soon became allods of their possessors.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and issue
2 CONT
2 CONT He married first between 968 and April 970 Arsenda, daughter of Arnaud, Comte of Comminges and Arsinde de Carcassonne, and their son was:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Odile de Province (or Odilie de Nice, c. 976 to c. 1032) 2. William II of Provence (c. 981 to before May 30, 1018)
2 CONT
2 CONT He married 2nd (against papal advice as she had just separated from her husband, the future King of France Louis V) in 984 (after Arsinde died in 983 at roughly age 33), Adelaide of Anjou, daughter of Fulk II of Anjou and Gerberga
2 CONC , and their children were:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Constance of Arles (973 - 1034, OUR ANCESTOR), married Robert II of France 2. Another daughter named Ermengarde of Arles whose parentage is disputed; she later marries Robert I d'Auvergne.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT Lewis, Archibald R. The Development of Southern French and Catalan Society, 718–1050. University of Texas Press: Austin, 1965.
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph Hyacinthe Albans - Gallia Christiana Novissima, Volume 3 - Edition 1895 book available on Gallica
2 CONT
2 CONT Martin Aurell, Jean - Paul Boyer and Noel Coulet - Provence in the Middle Ages
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT William I (c. 950 – 993, after 29 August), called the Liberator, was Count of Provence from 968 to his abdication. In 975 or 979, he took the title of marchio or margrave. He is often considered the founder of the county of Proven
2 CONC ce. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He and his elder brother Rotbold II, sons of Boso II of Arles and Constance of Viennois, daughter of Charles-Constantine, both carried the title of comes or count concurrently, but it is unknown if they were joint-counts of the wh
2 CONC ole of Provence or if the region was divided. His brother never bore any other title than count so long as William lived, so the latter seems to have attained a certain supremacy.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 980, he was installed as Count of Arles. His sobriquet comes from his victories against the Saracens by which he liberated Provence from their threat, which had been constant since the establishment of a base at Fraxinet. 
2 CONT
2 CONT At the Battle of Tourtour in 973, with the assistance of the counts of the High Alps and the viscounts of Marseille and Fos, he definitively routed the Saracens, chasing them forever from Provence. He reorganised the region east o
2 CONC f the Rhône, which he conquered from the Saracens and which had been given him as a gift from King Conrad of Burgundy. Also by royal consent, he and his descendants controlled the fisc in Provence. With Isarn, Bishop of Grenoble, 
2 CONC he repopulated Dauphiné and settled an Italian count named Ugo Blavia near Fréjus in 970 in order to bring that land back to cultivation. For all this, he figures prominently in Ralph Glaber's chronicle with the title of dux and h
2 CONC e appears in a charter of 992 as pater patriae.
2 CONT
2 CONT He donated land to Cluny and retired to become a monk, dying at Avignon, where he was buried in the church of Saint-Croix at Sarrians. He was succeeded as margrave by his brother. His great principality began to diminish soon afte
2 CONC r his death as the castles of his vassals, which he had kept carefully under ducal control, soon became allods of their possessors.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and issue
2 CONT
2 CONT He married 1st Arsenda, daughter of Arnold of Comminges and their son was:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. William II of Provence
2 CONT
2 CONT He married 2nd (against papal advice) in 984, Adelaide of Anjou, daughter of Fulk II of Anjou and Gerberga, and their daughter was:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Constance of Arles (973 - 1034), married Robert II of France
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Guillaume II 'le Libérateur' Comte de Provence, 955-993 also Marquis de Provence, Comte d'Arles
2 CONT
2 CONT [NOTE: Not to be confused with Guillaume II of Toulouse]
2 CONT
2 CONT -son of Boson II Comte d'Arles and Constance de Provence -brother of Rotbald
2 CONT
2 CONT 1st married Arsinde (possibly of Comminges)
2 CONT
2 CONT 2nd married Adelais d'Anjou, by whom he had 2 children:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.         Guillaume III,  (c.986/87-1018) Comte de Provence 
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.         Constance d'Arles (c 987/89]-1032,  m Robert II King of France
2 CONT
2 CONT Some sources also name a third child, Ermengarde d'Arles, but this is contested.
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/PROVENCE.htm 
2 CONT
2 CONT GUILLAUME [II] “le Libérateur” ([955]-Avignon 993 after 29 Aug, bur Sarrians, église de Sainte-Croix).  "Boso comes et uxor sua Constantia…illorum filii…Willelmus comes, Rotbaldus comes, Pontius juvenis" signed the charter dated M
2 CONC ay [963] under which "Gencius et uxor mea Aiburga" donated property "in pago Aquense superiore ad castrum…Ansoyse" to Monmajour[250].  The order of birth of the two sons of Boson is unclear.  The May [963] charter suggests that Gu
2 CONC illaume was his older son.  However, Guillaume is omitted from the charter dated Mar 965 under which "eius filio Rothboldo et fratre eius Wilelmo comite" consented to the charter of "Bosoni comitis, filii Rothboldi quondam"[251], 
2 CONC which suggests that Rotbald was older.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Comte de Provence, charters showing that both he and his older brother Rotbald were recorded as counts during the same period, although it is not known whether this was a joint countship or whether there was a geographical split b
2 CONC etween their jurisdictions.   
2 CONT
2 CONT Marquis de Provence.  "Vuilelmus marchius Arelatense Provintie" donated property "in comitatu Avinionense, in agro Rupiano, in loco…la Lona" to Saint-Victor, Marseille by charter dated 17 Apr 979, signed by "Arsinda comitissa"[252
2 CONC ].  "Willelmus comes" donated property to Cluny by charter dated 28 Aug [990] signed by "Rodbaldus comes, Adalaix comitissa, Wilelmus comes et filius eius Wilelmus"[253].  "Dominus princeps et marchio istius provinciæ…Willelmus cu
2 CONC m coniuge sua…Adelaix et filio suo…Willelmo" restored property to the abbey of Saint-Césaire d´Arles by charter dated 992, subscribed by "Domnus Rotbaldus comes…Willelmus comes filius Rotbaldi et uxor sua Lucia, Wilelmus comes Tol
2 CONC osanus et uxor sua Ema…"[254].  He became a monk. 
2 CONT
2 CONT m firstly (before Apr 970) ARSINDE, daughter of --- (-after 17 Apr 979).  "Wilelmus comes Provincie et coniunx mea Arsinna" donated property to Saint-Victor de Marseille by charter dated Apr 970[255].  "Vuilelmus marchius Arelaten
2 CONC se Provintie" donated property "in comitatu Avinionense, in agro Rupiano, in loco…la Lona" to Saint-Victor, Marseille by charter dated 17 Apr 979, signed by "Arsinda comitissa"[256].  Szabolcs de Vajay suggests that the first wife
2 CONC  of Marquis Guillaume was the sister of Adelais, whose first testament dated 4 Oct 978 names her, basing the hypothesis on onomastics and favorable chronology[257].  Under this testament of "Adelais", she donated her foundation "N
2 CONC arbonam…sanctique Salvatoris" to "sororibus meis et domnæ Arsindæ comitissæ", bequeathed "mea hereditas de Vidiliano" to "Arsindi sorori meæ", "alodes de Tolomiano" to "Ermesindi", and "mea hereditas de Artimiciano" to "Garsindi"[
2 CONC 258].  The wording suggests that "Arsindi…Ermesindi…Garsindi" were all sisters of the testator, although the relationship is not specified in the case of Arsinde.  It is probable that "domnæ Arsindæ comitissæ" in this document was
2 CONC  the wife of Guillaume II Comte de Provence as no other Ctss Arsende has been  identified at the time.  However, the wording of the passage in which she is named suggests that she was a different person from "Arsindi sorori meæ". 
2 CONC  Szabolcs de Vajay suggests (as reported by Settipani: the Szabolcs article has not yet been consulted) that the testator was the possible daughter of Arnaud [I] Comte de Comminges.  She can be identified as Adelais, widow of Matf
2 CONC ried Vicomte de Narbonne, as the document names the couple´s two sons whose affiliation is confirmed by other primary sources.  As explained more fully in the document TOULOUSE, KINGS, DUKES & COUNTS, other primary source document
2 CONC ation suggests that the wife of Vicomte Matfried may have been the daughter of Raymond Pons Comte de Toulouse.  If this is correct, the chronology suggests that her sister would have been too old to have married Guillaume II Comte
2 CONC  de Provence. 
2 CONT
2 CONT m secondly ([984/86]) as her fourth husband, ADELAIS [Blanche] d'Anjou, widow firstly of ETIENNE de Brioude, secondly of RAYMOND IV Comte de Toulouse, divorced wife (thirdly) of LOUIS V King of the West Franks, daughter of FOULQUE
2 CONC S II "le Bon" Comte d’Anjou & his first wife Gerberge --- ([945/50]-1026, bur Montmajour, near Arles).  A manuscript written by Arnoux, monk at Saint-André-lès-Avignon, records the death in 1026 of "Adalax comitissa"[277].  The ne
2 CONC crology of Saint-Pierre de Mâcon records the death "IV Kal Jun" of "Adalasia comitissa vocata regali progenie orta"[278].  An enquiry dated 2 Jan 1215 records that "comitissa Blanca" was buried "apud Montem Majorem"[279]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Comte Guillaume [II] & his second wife had two children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.         GUILLAUME [III] ([986/87]-1018 before 30 May).    Comte de Provence 992, minor until 994.   
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.         CONSTANCE ([987/89]-Château de Melun 22 or 25 Jul 1032, bur église de l'Abbaye royale de Saint-Denis).    m ([Sep 1001/25 Aug 1003]) as his third wife, ROBERT II King of France, son of HUGUES Capet King of France & his 
2 CONC wife Adelais d’Aquitaine (Orléans ([27 Mar] 972-Château de Melun 20 Jul 1031, bur église de l'Abbaye royale de Saint-Denis).    
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Guillaume Ier de Provence Un article de Wikipédia, l'encyclopédie libre.
2 CONT
2 CONT Guillaume Ier de Provence dit le Libérateur, (né v. 955 - mort en 993, après le 29 août), fils de Boson II, comte d'Arles et de Constance de Provence, il fut successivement comte d'Avignon (962), comte de Provence (972), marquis d
2 CONC e la Provence arlésienne (979) et prince de toute la Provence (991)[1]. En raison d'un oncle appelé lui aussi Guillaume[2], il est parfois dénommé Guillaume II de Provence.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ses premières années 
2 CONT
2 CONT Guillaume et son frère ainé Roubaud, succèdent à leur père Boson et oncle appelé lui aussi Guillaume entre 962 et 966. Le comté de Provence leur appartient en indivision, Guillaume devenant comte d'Avignon et Roubaud comte d'Arles
2 CONC  suivant la division opérée à la génération précédente entre leur père et oncle. Il épouse entre 968 et avril 970[3], Arsinde de Comminges[4], fille d'Arnaud, comte de Comminges et d'Arsinde de Carcassonne. Si Arsinde, sa première
2 CONC  femme, a été parfois été confondue avec Adelaïde, sa seconde, pour ne lui faire qu'une seule et unique épouse, la controverse est aujourd'hui terminée[5]. De cette première union seraient nés :
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Odile de Provence dite Odile de Nice (c.976-c.1032)
2 CONT     * Guillaume II de Provence (c.981-av. 30 mai 1018)[6].
2 CONT
2 CONT La libération de la Provence et ses conséquences 
2 CONT
2 CONT A la suite à l'enlèvement de l'abbé Mayeul en juillet 972 par les bandes de Sarrasins installées dans le massif des Maures depuis la fin du IXe siècle, le comte Guillaume et son frère Roubaud prennent la tête de l'ost provençal re
2 CONC nforcé par les troupes d'Ardouin, comte de Turin. Ils traquent les Maures (quelques centaines d'hommes au mieux) qu'ils écrasent à la bataille de Tourtour en 973, puis les chassent de Provence[7]. Cette campagne militaire contre l
2 CONC es Sarrasins conduite sans les troupes de Conrad, masque en fait une mise au pas de la Provence, de l'aristocratie locale et des communautés urbaines et paysannes qui avaient jusque là toujours refusé la mutation féodale et le pou
2 CONC voir comtal. Elle permet à Guillaume d'obtenir la suzeraineté de fait de la Provence et avec le consentement royal, de contrôler le fisc de la Provence. Il distribue les terres reconquises à ses vassaux, arbitre les différents et 
2 CONC crée ainsi la féodalité provençale[8]. Avec Isarn, évêque de Grenoble, il entreprend de repeupler le Dauphiné et autorise un comte italien nommé Ugo Blavia à se fixer près de Fréjus au début des années 970 pour remettre les terres
2 CONC  en cultures.
2 CONT
2 CONT Son gouvernement et sa renommée
2 CONT
2 CONT Comme son père Boson, Guillaume se fait conseiller par un vicomte qui dès 977 l'accompagne dans tous ces déplacements et il s'appuie sur un groupe important de juges pour rendre la justice. Devenu marquis de Provence en 979, il s'
2 CONC installe à Arles au début des années 980. Sa première femme Arsinde de Comminges (c.950-983) venant à décéder, il épouse en 984 dans cette cité, contre l'avis du pape, Adélaïde d'Anjou qui vient de se séparer de son époux, le futu
2 CONC r roi de France, Louis V. Le couple aurait eu deux enfants :
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Constance d'Arles (986-1032) reine de France par son mariage avec Robert II vers l'an 1000,
2 CONT     * et une autre fille Ermengarde d'Arles, dont la filiation est plus contestée[9]; Ermengarde d'Arles épouse par la suite Robert Ier d'Auvergne.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pour tout ceci, il est un personnage important des chroniques de Raoul Glaber qui le traite de duc et il apparaît dans une charte de 992 avec le nom de pater patriae.
2 CONT
2 CONT La fin de sa vie 
2 CONT
2 CONT À la fin de sa vie Guillaume devient très pieux et restitue de nombreux biens au temporel de l'Eglise. Déjà en 991[10], à la demande de l'évêque de Fréjus, Riculf[11], qui implore à Arles auprès du prince la restitution des ancien
2 CONC s domaines de l'évêché, Guillaume accède à cette pétition et lui accorde de surplus la moitié de Fréjus et le village de Puget[12]. En 992, il rend également d’importants domaines en Camargue au monastère Saint-Jean d'Arles. En 99
2 CONC 3, se sentant mourir dans la ville d'Avignon dont il a été le comte, il prend l'habit de moine et fait appel à l'abbé Mayeul pour soulager son âme. Il fait des restitutions et des offrandes à l'abbaye de Cluny[13], et c'est entour
2 CONC é par la multitude de ses sujets, que Guillaume de Provence passe de vie à trépas dans cette ville, peu après le 29 août 993. Avant de mourir, il avait émis le vœu d'être inhumé à Sarrians, près de Carpentras, dans le prieuré en c
2 CONC ours de construction sur la villa offerte à l'abbaye bourguignonne[14].
2 CONT
