2 CONT Guillaume II 'le Libérateur' Comte de Provence, 955-993 also Marquis de Provence, Comte d'Arles
2 CONT
2 CONT [NOTE: Not to be confused with Guillaume II of Toulouse]
2 CONT
2 CONT -son of Boson II Comte d'Arles and Constance de Provence -brother of Rotbald
2 CONT
2 CONT 1st married Arsinde (possibly of Comminges)
2 CONT
2 CONT 2nd married Adelais d'Anjou, by whom he had 2 children:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.         Guillaume III,  (c.986/87-1018) Comte de Provence 
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.         Constance d'Arles (c 987/89]-1032,  m Robert II King of France
2 CONT
2 CONT Some sources also name a third child, Ermengarde d'Arles, but this is contested.
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/PROVENCE.htm 
2 CONT
2 CONT GUILLAUME [II] “le Libérateur” ([955]-Avignon 993 after 29 Aug, bur Sarrians, église de Sainte-Croix).  "Boso comes et uxor sua Constantia…illorum filii…Willelmus comes, Rotbaldus comes, Pontius juvenis" signed the charter dated M
2 CONC ay [963] under which "Gencius et uxor mea Aiburga" donated property "in pago Aquense superiore ad castrum…Ansoyse" to Monmajour[250].  The order of birth of the two sons of Boson is unclear.  The May [963] charter suggests that Gu
2 CONC illaume was his older son.  However, Guillaume is omitted from the charter dated Mar 965 under which "eius filio Rothboldo et fratre eius Wilelmo comite" consented to the charter of "Bosoni comitis, filii Rothboldi quondam"[251], 
2 CONC which suggests that Rotbald was older.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Comte de Provence, charters showing that both he and his older brother Rotbald were recorded as counts during the same period, although it is not known whether this was a joint countship or whether there was a geographical split b
2 CONC etween their jurisdictions.   
2 CONT
2 CONT Marquis de Provence.  "Vuilelmus marchius Arelatense Provintie" donated property "in comitatu Avinionense, in agro Rupiano, in loco…la Lona" to Saint-Victor, Marseille by charter dated 17 Apr 979, signed by "Arsinda comitissa"[252
2 CONC ].  "Willelmus comes" donated property to Cluny by charter dated 28 Aug [990] signed by "Rodbaldus comes, Adalaix comitissa, Wilelmus comes et filius eius Wilelmus"[253].  "Dominus princeps et marchio istius provinciæ…Willelmus cu
2 CONC m coniuge sua…Adelaix et filio suo…Willelmo" restored property to the abbey of Saint-Césaire d´Arles by charter dated 992, subscribed by "Domnus Rotbaldus comes…Willelmus comes filius Rotbaldi et uxor sua Lucia, Wilelmus comes Tol
2 CONC osanus et uxor sua Ema…"[254].  He became a monk. 
2 CONT
2 CONT m firstly (before Apr 970) ARSINDE, daughter of --- (-after 17 Apr 979).  "Wilelmus comes Provincie et coniunx mea Arsinna" donated property to Saint-Victor de Marseille by charter dated Apr 970[255].  "Vuilelmus marchius Arelaten
2 CONC se Provintie" donated property "in comitatu Avinionense, in agro Rupiano, in loco…la Lona" to Saint-Victor, Marseille by charter dated 17 Apr 979, signed by "Arsinda comitissa"[256].  Szabolcs de Vajay suggests that the first wife
2 CONC  of Marquis Guillaume was the sister of Adelais, whose first testament dated 4 Oct 978 names her, basing the hypothesis on onomastics and favorable chronology[257].  Under this testament of "Adelais", she donated her foundation "N
2 CONC arbonam…sanctique Salvatoris" to "sororibus meis et domnæ Arsindæ comitissæ", bequeathed "mea hereditas de Vidiliano" to "Arsindi sorori meæ", "alodes de Tolomiano" to "Ermesindi", and "mea hereditas de Artimiciano" to "Garsindi"[
2 CONC 258].  The wording suggests that "Arsindi…Ermesindi…Garsindi" were all sisters of the testator, although the relationship is not specified in the case of Arsinde.  It is probable that "domnæ Arsindæ comitissæ" in this document was
2 CONC  the wife of Guillaume II Comte de Provence as no other Ctss Arsende has been  identified at the time.  However, the wording of the passage in which she is named suggests that she was a different person from "Arsindi sorori meæ". 
2 CONC  Szabolcs de Vajay suggests (as reported by Settipani: the Szabolcs article has not yet been consulted) that the testator was the possible daughter of Arnaud [I] Comte de Comminges.  She can be identified as Adelais, widow of Matf
2 CONC ried Vicomte de Narbonne, as the document names the couple´s two sons whose affiliation is confirmed by other primary sources.  As explained more fully in the document TOULOUSE, KINGS, DUKES & COUNTS, other primary source document
2 CONC ation suggests that the wife of Vicomte Matfried may have been the daughter of Raymond Pons Comte de Toulouse.  If this is correct, the chronology suggests that her sister would have been too old to have married Guillaume II Comte
2 CONC  de Provence. 
2 CONT
2 CONT m secondly ([984/86]) as her fourth husband, ADELAIS [Blanche] d'Anjou, widow firstly of ETIENNE de Brioude, secondly of RAYMOND IV Comte de Toulouse, divorced wife (thirdly) of LOUIS V King of the West Franks, daughter of FOULQUE
2 CONC S II "le Bon" Comte d’Anjou & his first wife Gerberge --- ([945/50]-1026, bur Montmajour, near Arles).  A manuscript written by Arnoux, monk at Saint-André-lès-Avignon, records the death in 1026 of "Adalax comitissa"[277].  The ne
2 CONC crology of Saint-Pierre de Mâcon records the death "IV Kal Jun" of "Adalasia comitissa vocata regali progenie orta"[278].  An enquiry dated 2 Jan 1215 records that "comitissa Blanca" was buried "apud Montem Majorem"[279]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Comte Guillaume [II] & his second wife had two children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.         GUILLAUME [III] ([986/87]-1018 before 30 May).    Comte de Provence 992, minor until 994.   
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.         CONSTANCE ([987/89]-Château de Melun 22 or 25 Jul 1032, bur église de l'Abbaye royale de Saint-Denis).    m ([Sep 1001/25 Aug 1003]) as his third wife, ROBERT II King of France, son of HUGUES Capet King of France & his 
2 CONC wife Adelais d’Aquitaine (Orléans ([27 Mar] 972-Château de Melun 20 Jul 1031, bur église de l'Abbaye royale de Saint-Denis).    
2 CONT
