2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_I_of_Provence
2 CONT
2 CONT William I (c. 950 – 993, after 29 August), called the Liberator, was Count of Provence from 968 to his abdication. In 975 or 979, he took the title of marchio or margrave. He is often considered the founder of the county of Proven
2 CONC ce. He and his elder brother Rotbold II, sons of Boso II of Arles and Constance of Viennois, daughter of Charles-Constantine, both carried the title of comes or count concurrently, but it is unknown if they were joint-counts of th
2 CONC e whole of Provence or if the region was divided. His brother never bore any other title than count so long as William lived, so the latter seems to have attained a certain supremacy.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 980, he was installed as Count of Arles. His sobriquet comes from his victories against the Saracens by which he liberated Provence from their threat, which had been constant since the establishment of a base at Fraxinet. At th
2 CONC e Battle of Tourtour in 973, with the assistance of the counts of the High Alps and the viscounts of Marseille and Fos, he definitively routed the Saracens, chasing them forever from Provence. He reorganised the region east of the
2 CONC  Rhône, which he conquered from the Saracens and which had been given him as a gift from King Conrad of Burgundy. Also by royal consent, he and his descendants controlled the fisc in Provence. With Isarn, Bishop of Grenoble, he re
2 CONC populated Dauphiné and settled an Italian count named Ugo Blavia near Fréjus in 970 in order to bring that land back to cultivation. For all this, he figures prominently in Ralph Glaber's chronicle with the title of dux and he app
2 CONC ears in a charter of 992 as pater patriae.
2 CONT
2 CONT He donated land to Cluny and retired to become a monk, dying at Avignon, where he was buried in the church of Saint-Croix at Sarrians. He was succeeded as margrave by his brother. His great principality began to diminish soon afte
2 CONC r his death as the castles of his vassals, which he had kept carefully under ducal control, soon became allods of their possessors.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and issue
2 CONT
2 CONT He married 1st Arsenda, daughter of Arnold of Comminges and their son was: William II of Provence
2 CONT
2 CONT He married 2nd (against papal advice) in 984, Adelaide of Anjou, daughter of Fulk II of Anjou and Gerberga of Maine, and their daughter was: Constance of Arles (973 - 1034), married Robert II of France
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_I_of_Provence
