2 CONT He donated land to Cluny and retired to become a monk, dying at Avignon, where he was buried in the church of Saint-Croix at Sarrians. He was succeeded as margrave by his brother. His great principality began to diminish soon afte
2 CONC r his death as the castles of his vassals, which he had kept carefully under ducal control, soon became allods of their possessors.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT William I (or II) (c. 950 – after 29 August 993), called the Liberator, was Count of Provence from 968 to his abdication. In 975 or 979, he took the title of marchio or margrave. He is often considered the founder of the county of
2 CONC  Provence. He and his elder brother Rotbold II were sons of Boso II of Arles and Constance of Viennois, daughter of Charles-Constantine. They both carried the title of comes or count concurrently, but it is unknown if they were jo
2 CONC int-counts of the whole of Provence or if the region was divided. His brother never bore any other title than count so long as William lived, so the latter seems to have attained a certain supremacy.  In 980, he was installed as C
2 CONC ount of Arles. His sobriquet comes from his victories against the Saracens by which he liberated Provence from their threat, which had been constant since the establishment of a base at Fraxinet. At the Battle of Tourtour in 973, 
2 CONC with the assistance of the counts of the High Alps and the viscounts of Marseille and Fos, he definitively routed the Saracens, chasing them forever from Provence. He reorganised the region east of the Rhône, which he conquered fr
2 CONC om the Saracens and which had been given him as a gift from King Conrad of Burgundy. Also by royal consent, he and his descendants controlled the fisc in Provence. With Isarn, Bishop of Grenoble, he repopulated Dauphiné and settle
2 CONC d an Italian count named Ugo Blavia near Fréjus in 970 in order to bring that land back to cultivation. For all this, he figures prominently in Ralph Glaber's chronicle with the title of dux and he appears in a charter of 992 as p
2 CONC ater patriae.  He donated land to Cluny and retired to become a monk, dying at Avignon, where he was buried in the church of Saint-Croix at Sarrians. He was succeeded as margrave by his brother. His great principality began to dim
2 CONC inish soon after his death as the castles of his vassals, which he had kept carefully under ducal control, soon became allods of their possessors.  Marriage and issue:  He married 1st Arsenda, daughter of Arnold of Comminges[1] an
2 CONC d their son was:
2 CONT  William II (or III) of Provence.  He married 2nd (against papal advice) in 984, Adelaide-Blanche of Anjou, daughter of Fulk II of Anjou and Gerberga, and their daughter was:
2 CONT  Constance of Arles (986–1034), married Robert II of France
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Guillaume l. le Libe´rateur war graf und später Markgraf von Provence sowie als Wilhelm ll. Graf von Aries. Er war der jüngere Sohn der Grafen Boso ll.und der Constance von Provence aus dem Haus der Buviniden.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT William I Taillefer 
2 CONT  * Birth:  unknown 
2 CONT * Provence-Alpes-Côte d'Azur, France  
2 CONT * Death:  Aug., 993, France 
2 CONT
2 CONT William I of Provence, Marquis of and founder of Provence, Count of Arles. Nicknamed the Liberator.
2 CONT
2 CONT William was the son of Boso II, Count of Avignon and Arles, and Constance of Viennois, the grandson of Rotbald I, Charles Constantine, Count of Vienne and Teutberge of Troye. Brother of Rotbold II. He was born circa 950 in Provenc
2 CONC e, and died in 993 in Avignon.
2 CONT
2 CONT William married Arsenda, daughter of Arnold of Comminges, and had one son,
2 CONT *  William II, Count of Provence, husband of Gerberge of Macon.
2 CONT
2 CONT Secondly, he married Blanche of Alix of Anjou, the daughter of Foulques II d' Anjou m Gerberge du Maine. They had three children:
2 CONT * Constance of Provence, wife of Robert II, King of France
2 CONT * Ermengarde, wife of Robert I, Count of Auvergne
2 CONT * Toda, wife of Bernard I, Count de Besalu....  
2 CONT
2 CONT  [http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=61543673  See more at]
2 CONT
2 CONT ==========================
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia 
2 CONT
2 CONT William I (c. 950 – after 29 August 993), called the Liberator, was Count of Provence from 968 to his abdication. In 975 or 979, he took the title of marchio or margrave. He is often considered the founder of the county of Provenc
2 CONC e. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He and his elder brother Rotbold II were sons of Boso II of Arles and Constance of Viennois, daughter of Charles-Constantine. They both carried the title of comes or count concurrently, but it is unknown if they were joint-counts 
2 CONC of the whole of Provence or if the region was divided. His brother never bore any other title than count so long as William lived, so the latter seems to have attained a certain supremacy.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 980, he was installed as Count of Arles. His sobriquet comes from his victories against the Saracens by which he liberated Provence from their threat, which had been constant since the establishment of a base at Fraxinet. At th
2 CONC e Battle of Tourtour in 973, with the assistance of the counts of the High Alps and the viscounts of Marseille and Fos, he definitively routed the Saracens, chasing them forever from Provence. He reorganised the region east of the
2 CONC  Rhône, which he conquered from the Saracens and which had been given him as a gift from King Conrad of Burgundy. Also by royal consent, he and his descendants controlled the fisc in Provence. With Isarn, Bishop of Grenoble, he re
2 CONC populated Dauphiné and settled an Italian count named Ugo Blavia near Fréjus in 970 in order to bring that land back to cultivation. For all this, he figures prominently in Ralph Glaber's chronicle with the title of dux and he app
2 CONC ears in a charter of 992 as pater patriae.
2 CONT
2 CONT He donated land to Cluny and retired to become a monk, dying at Avignon, where he was buried in the church of Saint-Croix at Sarrians. He was succeeded as margrave by his brother. His great principality began to diminish soon afte
2 CONC r his death as the castles of his vassals, which he had kept carefully under ducal control, soon became allods of their possessors.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Marriage and issue==
2 CONT
2 CONT He married 1st Arsenda, daughter of Arnold of Comminges[1] and their son was:
2 CONT * William II (or III) of Provence.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT He married 2nd (against papal advice) in 984, Adelaide-Blanche of Anjou, daughter of Fulk II of Anjou and Gerberga, and their daughter was:
2 CONT * Constance of Arles (986–1034), married Robert II of France.[1]
2 CONT
