1 NOTE He ruled in 979.
1 NOTE William III Taillefer (also spelled Tallefer or Tallifer; 975 - September 1037) was the Count of Toulouse, Albi, and Quercy from 972 or 978 to his death. He was the first of the Toulousain branch of his family to bear the title ma
2 CONC rchio, which he inherited (c.975) from Raymond II of Rouergue.
2 CONT
2 CONT His parentage has been subject to reevaluation. He has traditionally been called son of Raymond III Pons and Garsinda.[1] However, recent research has revealed that William was instead son of Adelais of Anjou, known to have marrie
2 CONC d a Raymond, "Prince of Gothia". This discovery has required a complete reevaluation of the succession to the County of Toulouse during this period, and no scholarly consensus has developed.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT He and his vassals were notorious usurpers of church property. He stole from the abbey of Lézat, but gave it back between 1015 and 1025. Pope John XIX ordered him to stop his vassals from taking the lands of Moissac, a problem la
2 CONC ter remedied by his successor, Pons, who gave Moissac to Cluny.
2 CONT
2 CONT William became the most powerful prince in western Languedoc and he saw the rise of the House of Capet in France and a corresponding decrease in royal authority recognised in the south. He bore the title of marchio prefatus in pag
2 CONC o Tholosano: "prefect margrave in the Toulousain country." His influence extended into the Narbonensis and even Provence, on behalf of his wife. His power did not remain undiminished in his own city of Toulouse, where he was force
2 CONC d by a council of local noblemen and clerics to give up dues imposed on the market there.
2 CONT
2 CONT Before 992, William married Emma, daughter of Rotbold III of Provence. From her he gained titles and lands to Provence. From a prior marriage, he had two sons, Raymond and Hugh, who died young. His eldest son by Emma, Pons, inheri
2 CONC ted Toulouse and the title of Margrave of Provence. His second son Bertrand became Count of Forcalquier, a Provençal fief. He had two daughters: Rangarda, wife of Peter Raymond of Carcassonne by Emma, and Ildegarda Elisa, wife of
2 CONC  Fulk Bertrand of Provence by Emma. He had an illegitimate daughter that married Otto Raymond of L'Isle-Jourdain.
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes
2 CONT ^ e.g. Lewis, p 341. 
2 CONT ^ Some historians have suggested a single additional generation (referred to as Raymond III of Toulouse, his father Raymond Pons being stripped of an ordinal), while others follow the Codice de Roda in giving Raymond Pons a son Ra
2 CONC ymond who in turn had sons Hugh and Raymond. Identifying the last with the husband of Adelais inserts two generations, making William the great-grandson of Raymond Pons. Yet another reconstruction suggests that Raymond and Garsind
2 CONC a died childless, and that Toulouse passed to kinsman Raymond II of Rouergue, from whom the husband of Adelais would descend. See Martin de Framond, "La succession des comtes de Toulouse autour de l'an mil(940--1030): reconsidéra
2 CONC tions", Annales du Midi 204 (1993), pp 461--488; Thierry Stasser, "Adélaïde d'Anjou. Sa famille, ses mariages, sa descendance", Le Moyen Age 103,1 (1997): 9-52; FMG for different reconstructions. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT Lewis, Archibald R. The Development of Southern French and Catalan Society, 718-1050. University of Texas Press: Austin, 1965. 
2 CONT Foundation for Medieval Genealogy: Toulouse. 
2 CONT Thierry Stasser, "Adélaïde d'Anjou. Sa famille, ses mariages, sa descendance", Le Moyen Age 103,1 (1997): 9-52 
1 NOTE [De La Pole.FTW]
2 CONT Sources: RC 298, 333, 347; Kraentzler 1161, 1171, 1181, 1202, 1217, 1218, 1258; A. Roots 53, 141A; AF.   RC: Count of Provence and Arles, Marquis of Provence. Died 994, a monk at Avignon. K: Guilaume I d'Arles, Count d'Arles and P
2 CONC rovence.   K: Guillaume I (Taillefer), Count de Toulouse, Count d'Arles and Provence. Duke de Provence.   Roots: William (or Guillaume) II, Count of Arles and Provence. Married (1) Arsenda de Comminges.
