2 CONT Fourth Husband, from the FMG Page on Provence: http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/PROVENCE.htm#GuillaumeIIArlesProvencedied993
2 CONT
2 CONT GUILLAUME [II] “le Libérateur”, son of ([955]-Avignon 993 after 29 Aug, bur Sarrians, église de Sainte-Croix).  
2 CONT
2 CONT "Boso comes et uxor sua Constantia…illorum filii…Willelmus comes, Rotbaldus comes, Pontius juvenis" signed the charter dated May [963] under which "Gencius et uxor mea Aiburga" donated property "in pago Aquense superiore ad castru
2 CONC m…Ansoyse" to Monmajour[250].  
2 CONT
2 CONT The order of birth of the two sons of Boson is unclear.  The May [963] charter suggests that Guillaume was his older son.  However, Guillaume is omitted from the charter dated Mar 965 under which "eius filio Rothboldo et fratre ei
2 CONC us Wilelmo comite" consented to the charter of "Bosoni comitis, filii Rothboldi quondam"[251], which suggests that Rotbald was older.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Comte de Provence, charters showing that both he and his older brother Rotbald were recorded as counts during the same period, although it is not known whether this was a joint countship or whether there was a geographical split b
2 CONC etween their jurisdictions.   
2 CONT
2 CONT Marquis de Provence.  "Vuilelmus marchius Arelatense Provintie" donated property "in comitatu Avinionense, in agro Rupiano, in loco…la Lona" to Saint-Victor, Marseille by charter dated 17 Apr 979, signed by "Arsinda comitissa"[252
2 CONC ].  "Willelmus comes" donated property to Cluny by charter dated 28 Aug [990] signed by "Rodbaldus comes, Adalaix comitissa, Wilelmus comes et filius eius Wilelmus"[253].  "Dominus princeps et marchio istius provinciæ…Willelmus cu
2 CONC m coniuge sua…Adelaix et filio suo…Willelmo" restored property to the abbey of Saint-Césaire d´Arles by charter dated 992, subscribed by "Domnus Rotbaldus comes…Willelmus comes filius Rotbaldi et uxor sua Lucia, Wilelmus comes Tol
2 CONC osanus et uxor sua Ema…"[254].  
2 CONT
2 CONT He became a monk. 
2 CONT
2 CONT m firstly (before Apr 970) ARSINDE, daughter of --- (-after 17 Apr 979).   "Wilelmus comes Provincie et coniunx mea Arsinna" donated property to Saint-Victor de Marseille by charter dated Apr 970[255].  "Vuilelmus marchius Arelate
2 CONC nse Provintie" donated property "in comitatu Avinionense, in agro Rupiano, in loco…la Lona" to Saint-Victor, Marseille by charter dated 17 Apr 979, signed by "Arsinda comitissa"[256].   Szabolcs de Vajay suggests that the first wi
2 CONC fe of Marquis Guillaume was the sister of Adelais, whose first testament dated 4 Oct 978 names her, basing the hypothesis on onomastics and favorable chronology[257].  Under this testament of "Adelais", she donated her foundation 
2 CONC "Narbonam…sanctique Salvatoris" to "sororibus meis et domnæ Arsindæ comitissæ", bequeathed "mea hereditas de Vidiliano" to "Arsindi sorori meæ", "alodes de Tolomiano" to "Ermesindi", and "mea hereditas de Artimiciano" to "Garsindi
2 CONC "[258].  The wording suggests that "Arsindi…Ermesindi…Garsindi" were all sisters of the testator, although the relationship is not specified in the case of Arsinde.  It is probable that "domnæ Arsindæ comitissæ" in this document w
2 CONC as the wife of Guillaume II Comte de Provence as no other Ctss Arsende has been  identified at the time.  However, the wording of the passage in which she is named suggests that she was a different person from "Arsindi sorori meæ"
2 CONC .  Szabolcs de Vajay suggests (as reported by Settipani: the Szabolcs article has not yet been consulted) that the testator was the possible daughter of Arnaud [I] Comte de Comminges.  She can be identified as Adelais, widow of Ma
2 CONC tfried Vicomte de Narbonne, as the document names the couple´s two sons whose affiliation is confirmed by other primary sources.   As explained more fully in the document TOULOUSE, KINGS, DUKES & COUNTS, other primary source docum
2 CONC entation suggests that the wife of Vicomte Matfried may have been the daughter of Raymond Pons Comte de Toulouse.  If this is correct, the chronology suggests that her sister would have been too old to have married Guillaume II Co
2 CONC mte de Provence. 
2 CONT
2 CONT m secondly ([984/86]) as her fourth husband, ADELAIS [Blanche] d'Anjou, widow firstly of ETIENNE de Brioude, secondly of RAYMOND IV Comte de Toulouse, divorced wife (thirdly) of LOUIS V King of the West Franks.
2 CONT
2 CONT Comte Guillaume [II] & his second wife had two children:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. GUILLAUME [III] ([986/87]-1018 before 30 May).   "Willelmus comes" donated property to Cluny by charter dated 28 Aug [990] signed by "Rodbaldus comes, Adalaix comitissa, Wilelmus comes et filius eius Wilelmus"[280].  "Pontius…M
2 CONC assiliensis ecclesie pontifex" issued a charter dated 1005 with the consent of "domni Rodhbaldi comitis et domne Adalaizis comitisse, domnique Guillelmi comitis filii eius"[281].   According to Europäische Stammtafeln[282], he was
2 CONC  the son of Comte Guillaume by his first wife but the primary source on which this is based has not been identified.  It is probably speculative, based on his marriage date, which suggests that he was born earlier than the date of
2 CONC  his father's second marriage.   In any case, Guillaume's parentage appears to be proved by the charter dated 1005.   Comte de Provence 992, minor until 994.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. CONSTANCE ([987/89]-Château de Melun 22 or 25 Jul 1032, bur église de l'Abbaye royale de Saint-Denis).   The Historia Francorum names "Constantiam, filiam Guillelmi comitis Arelatensis, natam de Blanca sorore Gaufridi comitis A
2 CONC ndegavensis" as wife of King Robert[283].  The Chronicle of Alberic de Trois-Fontaines also names "Constantia filia fuit Blanche comitisse Arelatensis" as wife of "Robertus rex"[284].  The Chronicon Hugonis names "Constantiam" as 
2 CONC wife of "Robertus", specifying that she was "cognatam Hugonis Autisiodorensis episcopi comitis Cabilonensis"[285].  This is presumably based on Rodulfus Glaber who states incorrectly that "Constantiam…filiam…prioris Willemi Aquita
2 CONC nie ducis" was wife of King Robert II, specifying that she was "cognatam" of Hugues Comte de Chalon Bishop of Auxerre[286].  The only relationship so far identified between the two is that Constance's maternal uncle, Geoffroy I Co
2 CONC mte d'Anjou, was the second husband of the mother of Comte Hugues.   Rodulfus Glauber dates her marriage to "about the year 1000"[287].  The king attempted to separate from Constance in 1008 in order to take back his second wife, 
2 CONC according to Rodulfus Glaber through the influence of "Hugo dictus Beluacensis"[288], but he restored Constance's royal prerogatives end 1009[289].   She opposed her husband's proposal to crown their second son Henri as associate 
2 CONC king in 1026, supporting the candidature of her third son Robert[290].  She organised two revolts against King Robert and another against her son King Henri I after his accession[291].   Rodolfus Glaber records the death of Queen 
2 CONC Constance in the same city as her husband [Melun] and in the same month [Jul] in the following year, and her place of burial[292].  The necrology of the abbey of Saint-Germain-des-Prés records the death "XI Kal Aug" of "regina Con
2 CONC stancia"[293].  The necrology of Argenteuil Priory records the death "VIII Kal Aug" of "Constancia regina"[294].   m ([Sep 1001/25 Aug 1003]) as his third wife, ROBERT II King of France, son of HUGUES Capet King of France & his wi
2 CONC fe Adelais d’Aquitaine (Orléans ([27 Mar] 972-Château de Melun 20 Jul 1031, bur église de l'Abbaye royale de Saint-Denis).   
2 CONT
