2 CONT Adbelahide or Adele or Adelaide of Aquitaine (or Adelaide of Poitiers) (c. 945 or 952 – 1004)[1] was the daughter of William III, Duke of Aquitaine and Adele of Normandy, daughter of Rollo of Normandy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her father used her as security for a truce with Hugh Capet, whom she married in 969.[2] In 987, after the death of Louis V, the last Carolingian king of France, Hugh was elected the new king with Adelaide as queen. They were proc
2 CONC laimed at Senlis and blessed at Noyon. They were the founders of the Capetian dynasty of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Adeleide and Hugh's children were: Hedwig, Countess of Mons (or Hadevide, or Avoise) (c. 969–after 1013), wife of Reginar IV, Count of Mons Robert II (972–1031), the future king of France. Crowned co-king 987 in order to consolida
2 CONC te the new dynasty Gisèle, Countess of Ponthieu (c. 970–1002), wife of Hugh I, Count of Ponthieu
2 CONT
2 CONT A number of other daughters are less reliably attested
2 CONT
