2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Adbelahide or Adele or Adelaide of Aquitaine (or Adelaide of Poitiers) (c. 945 or 952 – 1004),[1] was queen consort of France by marriage to Hugh Capet.
2 CONT
2 CONT Life[edit] Adelaide was the daughter of William III, Duke of Aquitaine and Adele of Normandy, daughter of Rollo of Normandy. Her father used her as security for a truce with Hugh Capet, whom she married in 969.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 987, after the death of Louis V, the last Carolingian king of France, Hugh was elected the new king with Adelaide as queen. They were proclaimed at Senlis and blessed at Noyon. They were the founders of the Capetian dynasty of 
2 CONC France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh apparently trusted in her judgement and allowed her to take part in government: he proposed her to negotiate for him with the regent of the German Empire, empress Theophanu, committing himself beforehand to their agreement.[1
2 CONC ]
2 CONT
2 CONT Children[edit] Adeleide and Hugh's children were:
2 CONT
2 CONT Hedwig, Countess of Mons (or Hadevide, or Avoise) (c. 969 – after 1013), wife of Reginar IV, Count of Mons Robert II (972–1031), the future king of France. Crowned co-king 987 in order to consolidate the new dynasty Gisèle, Counte
2 CONC ss of Ponthieu (c. 970–1002), wife of Hugh I, Count of Ponthieu A number of other daughters are less reliably attested.
2 CONT
