2 CONT Legacy
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh Capet died on 24 October 996 in Paris and was interred in the Saint Denis Basilica. His son Robert continued to reign.
2 CONT
2 CONT Most historians regard the beginnings of modern France with the coronation of Hugh Capet. This is because, as Count of Paris, he made the city his power centre. The monarch began a long process of exerting control of the rest of t
2 CONC he country from there.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is regarded as the founder of the Capetian dynasty. The direct Capetians, or the House of Capet, ruled France from 987 to 1328; thereafter, the Kingdom was ruled by cadet branches of the dynasty. All French kings through Louis 
2 CONC Philippe, and all royals since then, have belonged to the dynasty. Furthermore, cadet branches of the House continue to reign in Spain and Luxembourg.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Le 3 juillet 987, dans la vieille cathédrale de Noyon, Hugues Capet devient roi de France sous le nom d'Hugues 1er.
2 CONT
2 CONT Il est sacré par l'évêque de Reims, Adalbéron, selon un rituel germanique inauguré par Pépin le Bref.
2 CONT
2 CONT Deux jours plus tôt, à Senlis (ou peut-être à Noyon ou Soissons), les principaux seigneurs du royaume ont offert la couronne au comte de Paris, au détriment de l'ultime héritier de Charlemagne et des rois carolingiens.
2 CONT
2 CONT Le royaume sur lequel va régner Hugues Capet s'étend sur la partie ouest des régions occupées par les Francs, de la Manche à la Meuse, et correspond en gros au bassin parisien.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cette région fortement romanisée s'appelait Neustrie sous Clovis et ses successeurs mérovingiens. Elle est appelée Francie occidentale au temps de Charlemagne et des carolingiens. Les Capétiens vont en faire le noyau de la France 
2 CONC actuelle.
2 CONT
2 CONT L'avènement d'Hugues Capet, c'est la vraie naissance de la France, au moment où naît l'Europe que nous connaissons.
2 CONT
2 CONT En 1119, dans une lettre au pape Calixte II, un lointain descendant de Hugues Capet, le roi Louis VI, se proclame «roi de la France, non plus des Francs, et fils particulier de l'Église romaine». C'est le premier texte où il est f
2 CONC ait référence au mot France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Emergence de la France
2 CONT
2 CONT Au cours du Xe siècle, deux aïeux d'Hugues Capet, dont le duc Eudes, avaient déjà été élus roi de Francie occidentale mais ils n'avaient pu conserver la couronne dans leur famille.
2 CONT
2 CONT Au moment de son sacre, Hugues 1er est un homme mûr de 47 ans. C'est un seigneur puissant et respecté qui possède en propre de vastes domaines autour de Paris et Orléans qui en font l'un des principaux seigneurs de Francie occiden
2 CONC tale. Il s'agit de seigneuries laïques et d'abbayes.
2 CONT
2 CONT Le surnom de Capet que porte le roi fait précisément allusion à ses nombreuses chapes d'abbés, dont celle, prestigieuse entre toutes, de Saint-Martin-de-Tours.
2 CONT
2 CONT Malgré le sacre de Reims, Hugues doit défendre sa légitimité les armes à la main. Selon le chroniqueur Adhémar de Chabannes, l'un de ses vassaux, Adalbert de Périgord, refusant de lever le siège de Tours, Hugues lui demande:
2 CONT
2 CONT - Qui t'a fait comte?
2 CONT
2 CONT Et l'autre de répliquer:
2 CONT
2 CONT - Qui t'a fait roi?
2 CONT
2 CONT Jusqu'en 991, Hugues doit aussi combattre le parti carolingien, qui garde de solides partisans, dont Charles de Lorraine.
2 CONT
2 CONT Le fidèle Adalbéron étant mort en janvier 989, Hugues Capet tente de se concilier Charles de Lorraine en nommant son neveu Arnou à la tête de l'archevêché de Reims. Mais Arnoul trahit son bienfaiteur et fait entrer à Reims les tro
2 CONC upes carolingiennes. Avec le concours du savant Gerbert, Hugues réunit un concile près de Reims. Il obtient la déposition de l'archevêque Arnoul et fait nommer Gerbert à sa place.
2 CONT
2 CONT A la faveur d'une trahison, le roi arrive enfin à capturer Charles de Lorraine à Laon. Il se retourne ensuite contre le comte de Blois Eudes 1er avec le concours de son voisin, le comte d'Anjou.
2 CONT
2 CONT L'époque du roi Hugues Capet, à l'approche de l'An 1000, voit l'apparition des châteaux forts en pierre. Jusque-là, les seigneurs se contentaient de fortifications en bois entourées de palissades, juchées sur une colline ou, à déf
2 CONC aut, sur une «motte» (une colline artificielle).
2 CONT
2 CONT Le premier château en pierre est dû au comte d'Anjou lui-même, le fameux Foulques Nerra. Il a été érigé à Langeais, au bord de la Loire.
2 CONT
2 CONT Le pré carré
2 CONT
2 CONT Arrondissant le domaine royal, ou «pré carré», à la manière modeste et tenace des paysans d'autrefois, Hugues 1er et ses descendants accroissent peu à peu leur richesse, consolident leur autorité et font émerger une nation nouvell
2 CONC e du désordre carolingien.
2 CONT
2 CONT Les premières générations de Capétiens respectent la règle féodale de l'élection. Mais Hugues et ses successeurs ont soin de faire élire de leur vivant leur fils aîné pour leur succéder et de le faire sacrer roi à Reims.
2 CONT
2 CONT Les Grands du royaume se prêtent de bon gré à la manoeuvre. Le fils aîné du roi régnant a l'avantage d'avoir été préparé à la succession par son père et son élection coupe court à toute querelle entre d'éventuels prétendants.
2 CONT
2 CONT Les féodaux s'habituent peu à peu à une succession héréditaire. Ils l'acceptent d'autant mieux qu'Hugues Capet et ses premiers descendants font preuve d'une sage réserve face à des seigneurs parfois plus puissants et plus riches q
2 CONC u'eux-mêmes.
2 CONT
2 CONT De génération en génération, les descendants d'Hugues Capet auront la double chance de vivre assez longtemps pour se faire accepter par leurs pairs et d'avoir un fils apte à leur succéder.
2 CONT
2 CONT C'est seulement avec Philippe II Auguste, deux siècle plus tard, que la royauté sera devenue assez forte pour ignorer le rite de l'élection. Philippe Auguste dédaignera de faire désigner son fils de son vivant. Louis VIII dit Le L
2 CONC ion lui succèdera automatiquement et sans difficulté le 14 juillet 1223.
2 CONT
2 CONT La succession héréditaire sera dès lors la règle en France. Mais ce principe restera relativement exceptionnel en Europe jusqu'à la fin du Moyen Âge, beaucoup de dynasties royales perpétuant le principe de l'élection à vie (Allema
2 CONC gne, Pologne, Russie,...). De nombreuses communautés conserveront par ailleurs un gouvernement de type républicain, en Suisse ou encore en Italie
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Duque de França, suserano da Borgonha. Abade leigo de S. Martinho de Tours, S. Dinis de Paris, St. Germain de Auxerre, etc. Investido como rei dos Francos em Noyon 1.6.987 e sagrado a 3.7.987 pelo arcebispo Alberto de Reims. Fundo
2 CONC u a dinastia dos Capetos.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Leo: Genealogisches Handbuch des Adels, Fürstliche Häuser , Reference: 1961.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT The son of Hugh the Great, Duke of France, and Hedwige of Saxony, daughter of the German king Henry the Fowler, Hugh was born in 941.[2] His paternal family, the Robertians, were powerful landowners in the Île-de-France.[3] His gr
2 CONC andfather had been King Robert I.[3] His grandmother Beatrice was a Carolingian, a daughter of Herbert I of Vermandois.[2] This makes him the fifth great-grandson of Charlemagne through Pepin of Italy.[4] King Odo was his grand-un
2 CONC cle and King Rudolph the son-in-law of his grandfather, King Robert I.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh Capet was born into a well-connected and powerful family with many ties to the reigning nobility of Europe.[b] But Hugh's father was never king. When Rudolph died in 936, Hugh the Great organised the return of Louis d'Outreme
2 CONC r, son of Charles the Simple, from his exile at the court of Athelstan of England.[6] Hugh the Great's motives are unknown, but it is presumed that he acted to forestall Rudolph's brother, Hugh the Black, Duke of Burgundy, from at
2 CONC tempting to take the French throne, or to prevent it from falling into the hands of Herbert II of Vermandois or Richard the Fearless, Duke of Normandy.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 956, Hugh inherited his father's estates and became one of the most powerful nobles in the much-reduced kingdom of West Francia.[8] As he was not yet an adult, his mother acted as his guardian,[9]and young Hugh's neighbours too
2 CONC k advantage. Theobald I of Blois, a former vassal of Hugh's father, took the counties of Chartres and Châteaudun. Further south, on the border of the kingdom, Fulk II of Anjou, another former client of Hugh the Great, carved out a
2 CONC  principality at Hugh's expense and that of the Bretons.[10]
2 CONT
2 CONT  A denier of Hugh Capet when he was Duke of France, calling him "duke by the grace of God" (Dux Dei Gratia). Minted at Paris (Parisi Civita) The realm in which Hugh grew up, and of which he would one day be king, bore no resemblan
2 CONC ce to modern France. Hugh's predecessors did not call themselves kings of France, and that title was not used by his successors until the time of his descendant, Philip II. Kings ruled as rex Francorum ("King of the Franks") and t
2 CONC he lands they ruled comprised only a small part of the former Carolingian Empire. The eastern Frankish lands, the Holy Roman Empire, were ruled by the Ottonian dynasty, represented by Hugh's first cousin Otto II and then by Otto's
2 CONC  son, Otto III. The lands south of the river Loire had largely ceased to be part of the West Francia kingdom in the years after Charles the Simple was deposed in 922. Both the Duchy of Normandy and the Duchy of Burgundy were large
2 CONC ly independent, and Brittany entirely so—although from 956 Burgundy was ruled by Hugh's brothers Otto and Henry.[11]
2 CONT
2 CONT Election and extent of power[edit]
2 CONT
2 CONT From 977 to 986, Hugh Capet allied himself with the Holy Roman Emperors Otto II and Otto III and with Archbishop Adalberon of Reims to dominate the Carolingian king, Lothair. By 986, he was king in all but name. After Lothair's so
2 CONC n Louis died in May 987, Adalberon and Gerbert of Aurillac convened an assembly of nobles to elect Hugh Capet as their king. In front of an electoral assembly at Senlis, Adalberon gave a stirring oration and pleaded to the nobles:
2 CONT
2 CONT Crown the Duke. He is most illustrious by his exploits, his nobility, his forces. The throne is not acquired by hereditary right; no one should be raised to it unless distinguished not only for nobility of birth, but for the goodn
2 CONC ess of his soul.[12]
2 CONT
2 CONT He was elected and crowned rex Francorum at Noyon in Picardy on 3 July 987, by the prelate of Reims, the first of the Capetian house. Immediately after his coronation, Hugh began to push for the coronation of his son Robert. Hugh'
2 CONC s own claimed reason was that he was planning an expedition against the Moorish armies harassing Borrel II of Barcelona, an invasion which never occurred, and that the stability of the country necessitated two kings should he die 
2 CONC while on expedition.[13] Ralph Glaber, however, attributes Hugh's request to his old age and inability to control the nobility.[14] Modern scholarship has largely imputed to Hugh the motive of establishing a dynasty against the pr
2 CONC etension of electoral power on the part of the aristocracy, but this is not the typical view of contemporaries and even some modern scholars have been less sceptical of Hugh's "plan" to campaign in Spain.[14] Robert was eventually
2 CONC  crowned on 25 December that same year.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Denier of Hugh Capet for Beauvais Hugh Capet possessed minor properties near Chartres and Angers. Between Paris and Orléans he possessed towns and estates amounting to approximately 400 square miles (1,000 km2). His authority end
2 CONC ed there, and if he dared travel outside his small area, he risked being captured and held for ransom, though his life would be largely safe.[citation needed] Indeed, there was a plot in 993, masterminded by Adalberon, Bishop of L
2 CONC aon and Odo I of Blois, to deliver Hugh Capet into the custody of Otto III. The plot failed, but the fact that no one was punished illustrates how tenuous his hold on power was. Beyond his power base, in the rest of France, there 
2 CONC were still as many codes of law as there were fiefdoms. The "country" operated with 150 different forms of currency and at least a dozen languages.[citation needed] Uniting all this into one cohesive unit was a formidable task and
2 CONC  a constant struggle between those who wore the crown of France and its feudal lords. Therefore, Hugh Capet's reign was marked by numerous power struggles with the vassals on the borders of the Seine and the Loire.
2 CONT
2 CONT While Hugh Capet's military power was limited and he had to seek military aid from Richard I of Normandy, his unanimous election as king gave him great moral authority and influence. Adémar de Chabannes records, probably apocrypha
2 CONC lly, that during an argument with the Count of Auvergne, Hugh demanded of him: "Who made you count?" The count riposted: "Who made you king?".[15]
2 CONT
