1 NOTE From THE RUFUS PARKS PEDIGREE by Brian J.L. Berry, chart pg 55.
2 CONT
2 CONT Page 58:
2 CONT
2 CONT In the hierarchy of Christian saints, Martin of Tours ranks high.  This 4C soldier saint was serving in the Roman Army when the incident occurred which led to his conversion and ultimate sainthood.  One winter day in gallic Amiens
2 CONC  he was so appalled at the plight of a half-naked begger at the city gate, that he drew his sword, slashed his own cloak in two, gave one piece to the freezing beggar and wrapped in the rag.  It was preserved for centuries in the 
2 CONC oratory at Tours, and when Hugh, son of Hugh Magnus, became Duke of Franks and lay Abbout of St. Martin's, the nickname Capet (Cape=cape) was attributed to him.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 11.    HUGH CAPET, 939+-d. Paris 24 Oct. 996, lay Abbot of St. Martin of Tours and first king of the Capetian Dynasty.  His successors ruled France in direct line of descent for more than 300 years.  On his father's death he becam
2 CONC e Duke of Franks, inherited vast estates in the region of Paris and Orleans and became one of the most powerful vassels of the kingdom of France and a serious threat to its last two Carolingian rulers.  He supported King Lothair i
2 CONC n his effort to to wrest Lorraine away from Emperor Otto II in 978, but opposed Lothair later and his successor Louis V.  On the latter's death in May 987, the Archbishop of Reims declared the crown elective rather than hereditary
2 CONC , persuading the nolbes to elect HUGH king.  He was so proclamed in June and was crowned by the archbishop 3 July 987.  Despite possession of the royal domain his position was not strong.  He had to cope with belligerent vassals, 
2 CONC buying their allegiance by enfeoffing them with territory, even when still involved in conflict with them.  Soon after his election he arranges the election and coronation of his son Robert, who succeeded him without undue difficu
2 CONC lty.  Our descent is by way of a dau. EDITH.  HUGH mar. ADELAIDE of Poitous, 945-c.1004, dau of WILLIAM I and III, Count of Poitou and Duke of Aquitaine and his wife GERLOC (ADELE).
2 CONT
