2 CONT   NAME Hugh "Capet" King Of /FRANCE/
1 NOTE Släktnamnet "Chapet" kom av att Hugo, i egenskap av Abbot för klostret Saint-Martine de 
2 CONT Tours och Saint Germain des Prés, bar en kort rock, som kallades för Cappa.
1 NOTE Hogo föddes i en mäktig ,adlig familj varav två var medlemmar och redan innehaft den Franska 
2 CONT tronen under 800-900 talet. Han ville egentligen bli lekman-Abbot och hade år 980 förberett 
2 CONT flytten av helgonrt Saint Valérys relik till katedralen i Amiens. Han ärvde då sin faders land och 
2 CONT blev sin tids mäktigaste adelsman i Frankrike.
1 NOTE He ruled from 987 to 996.
1 NOTE House of Capet in France starts with Hugh Capet
2 CONT http://trees.ancestry.com/rd?f=document&guid=90c14b9b-f934-4953-a902-856ff027e2da&tid=7412207&pid=-1098979566
1 NOTE BIOGRAPHY
2 CONT King of France and founder of the Capetingian dynasty, Hugues was the eldest son of Hugues 'the Great', duke of The Franks, comte de Paris, and his third wife Hadewich von Sachsen. When his father died in 956, he succeeded to his 
2 CONC numerous fiefs around Paris and Orléans. His cousin, the Frankish king Lothair, recognised him somewhat reluctantly as duke of the Franks.
2 CONT
2 CONT Many of the counts in northern France did homage to Hugues as their overlord, and Richard I, duke of Normandy, was both his vassal and his brother-in-law. The authority of Hugues extended over certain districts south of the Loire,
2 CONC  and owing to his intervention Lothair was obliged to recognise Hugues' brother Henri as duke of Burgundy. Hugues Capet supported his royal suzerain when Lothair and Emperor Otto II fought for the possession of Lorraine. However H
2 CONC ugues was chagrined at the king's conduct in making peace in 980, and went to Rome to conclude an alliance with Otto.
2 CONT
2 CONT Laying more stress upon independence than upon loyalty, Hugues appears to have acted haughtily toward Lothair, and also towards Lothar's son and successor, Louis V. However, neither king was strong enough to punish this powerful v
2 CONC assal. When Louis V died without children in May 987, both Hugues and the late king's uncle, Charles, duke of Lorraine, were candidates for the vacant throne. In this contest Hugues had as his champions Adelberon, archbishop of Re
2 CONC ims, and Gerbert, afterwards Pope Sylvester II. Their energy prevailed; declaring the Frankish crown to be an elective and not a hereditary dignity, Adelberon secured the election of his friend and crowned him, probably at Noyon, 
2 CONC in July 987. Charles of Lorraine was not prepared to bow before his successful rival; before Hugues had secured the coronation of his son Robert as his colleague and successor in December 987, Charles found allies and attacked the
2 CONC  king. Hugues had the worst of the earlier part of the struggle and was in serious straits until he was saved by his partisan Adelberon, who in 991 treacherously seized Charles and handed him over to the king.
2 CONT
2 CONT This capture virtually ended the wars, but one of its side issues was a quarrel between Hugues and Pope John XV, who was supported by the Holy Roman Empire, then under the rule of the empress Theophano Skleraina as regent for her 
2 CONC son, the young Emperor Otto III.
2 CONT
2 CONT The origin of Hugues' name of Capet, which was also applied to his father, has been the subject of some discussion. It is derived from the Latin 'Capa', or 'Cappa', a cape; but whether Hugues derived it from the cape, which he wor
2 CONC e as abbot of St. Martin's, or from his youthful habit of seizing capes, or from some other cause, is unknown.
2 CONT
2 CONT The identification of his wife as 'of Aquitaine' or 'of Poitou' is erroneous; her parentage is unknown. She was identified as 'of Aquitaine' solely on the basis of a charter, which survives only in a 14th-century _vidimus_ (litera
2 CONC lly, 'we have seen'), in which a woman of Aquitaine named Adela appears with a husband whose name is actually given as Ebles. Louis Halphen and Ferdinand Lot, the editors of the 'vidimus', assumed that this Adela must be Hugues Ca
2 CONC pet's wife and arbitrarily changed 'Ebles' to 'Hugues'.
2 CONT
2 CONT (See Constance Bouchard, _Eleanor's Divorce from Louis VII. The Uses of Consanguinity_, in _Eleanor of Aquitaine, Lord and Lady_, editors Bonnie Wheeler and John Parsons (New York, Palgrave, 2002) pages 223-235.)
