2 CONT
2 CONT Gunnora or Gunnor (c. 936–1031) was the wife and consort of Richard I of Normandy. Her parentage is unknown, earliest sources reporting solely that she was of Danish ancestry and naming siblings including brother Herfast de Crepon
2 CONC  who is sometimes erroneously given as her father.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was living with her sister Seinfreda, the wife of a local forester, when Richard, hunting nearby, heard of the beauty of the forester's wife. He is said to have ordered Seinfreda to come to his bed, but the lady substituted he
2 CONC r unmarried sister, Gunnora. Richard, it is said, was pleased that by this subterfuge he had been saved from committing adultery, and the two became lovers. Gunnora long acted as Richard's mistress or wife by more danico, but when
2 CONC  Richard was prevented from nominating their son Robert to be Archbishop of Rouen, the two were married, making their children legitimate in the eyes of the church.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gunnora, both as mistress and duchess, was able to use her influence to see her kin favored, and several of the most prominent Conquest-era Norman magnates, including the Montgomery, Warenne, Mortimer, Vernon/Redvers, and Fitz Osb
2 CONC ern families, were descendants of her brother and sisters.
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard and Gunnora were parents to six children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard II "the Good", Duke of Normandy (966)
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert, Archbishop of Rouen, Count of Evreux, died 1037.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mauger, Earl of Corbeil, died after 1033.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Danus, died between 985 and 989
2 CONT
2 CONT Emma of Normandy (c. 985-1052) wife of two kings of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Maud of Normandy, wife of Odo II of Blois, Count of Blois, Champagne and Chartres
2 CONT
2 CONT Preceded by
2 CONT
2 CONT Emma of Paris Duchess consort of Normandy
2 CONT
2 CONT 989 - 996 Succeeded by
2 CONT
2 CONT Judith of Brittany
2 CONT
2 CONT Retrieved from "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gunnora,_Duchess_of_Normandy"
2 CONT
