1 NOTE _P_CCINFO 1-20792
2 CONT Sources: RC 24, 89, 166, 168, 222; Kraentzler 1153, 1156, 1174, 1180,1194, 1211, 1432, 1443; A. Roots 121E; Coe; Norr; Onslow; Pfafman; Davis.
2 CONT Roots: (Danish wife) Gunnor, died 1027 or 1031, daughter of forester of Arques.
2 CONT K. calls her Gonnor de Crepon. Norr: Gunnor (Gunora), (952)-1031.
2 CONT Onslow in "The Dukes of Normandy and Their Orgin" says:
2 CONT Richard I heard about a charming woman married to the superintendentof forests at Secheville near Arques. Richard visited Secheville, met the forester's beautiful wife, Sainfrida, and "had the impudence to suggest to her husband t
2 CONC hat he should be allowed to make her better acquaintance.
2 CONT "The poor man was much perturbed and went in trouble to his wife. Thelady had no mind to become the mistress of the Duke, but her morals were notso rigid as to prevent her from making capital our of the situation for her family. S
2 CONC ainfrida had three sisters, Gunnor, Adelina and Veva (Wevia),and a brother named Herfast. Gunnor was a very pretty girl and by no means particular. Sainfrida told her husband to pretend to fall in with theDuke's proposals and give
2 CONC  Richard facilities to pay her a visit. But whenRichard arrived, Gunnor took Sainfrida's place. The Duke did not discover thetrick until the following day; but so delighted was he with Gunnor that notonly did he forgive Sainfrida,
2 CONC  but thanked her for having saved him from mortalsin! The result was that Gunnor became his wife by Danish custom and eventuallythe mother of a numerous family, the eldest of which was Richard II ofNormandy."
2 CONT Gunnor apparently had children by Richard before they were married andbefore his marriage to Emma. Evidently the Richard-Gunnor union was legitimizedlater.
2 CONT RC 166 says Gunnor's father was Herbastus de Crepon, Forester ofArques (in Denmark). Were there two foresters, the father of Gunnor and Sainfridaand also the husband of Sainfrida&#00063; Or did the story become tangled over the ye
2 CONC ars&#00063;
2 CONT Coe spells name Gunore.
2 CONT Davis: Gonnor, daughter of a forester of Sauqueville in Britanny.Second wife of Richard I. She married (2) Eperleng, "farmer" of the mills ofPitres.
2 CONT
2 CONT *****
2 CONT
2 CONT SOURCES:
2 CONT 1.  Wingate, John.  _William the Conqueror_, genealogical tables andnotes.
2 CONT Gunnor was the sister of Herfast of Denmark.  She was the mistress,or
2 CONT wife "by the Danish custom" of Richard I, Duke of Normandy, andmarried
2 CONT him after the death of his wife, Emma.  She had both legitimate and
2 CONT illegitimate children by Richard.
2 CONT 2.  Douglas, David C.  _William the Conqueror_: The Norman Impact Upon
2 CONT England_.  Berkeley and Los Angeles: University of CaliforniaPress,
2 CONT 1964; page 145.  He calls Gunnor the sister of Herfast and the auntof      Osbern, the steward of William the Conqueror's father, DukeRobert.
2 CONT 3.  Stuart, Roderick W.  _Royalty for Commoners_.  2nd Edition.Baltimore,
2 CONT MD: Genealogical Publishing Company, Inc., 1992; lines 89-1 and166-33.
2 CONT 4.  Norr, Vernon M.  _Some Early English Pedigrees_, page 60,generation 38,
2 CONT and page 23a.  States that Richard I had issue by and later md (2)(after
2 CONT Agnes died) Gunora, b. 952, d. 1031; sister of a forester's wifewhom
2 CONT Richard desired but who tricked him with her younger sister.
2 CONT 5.  Ancestral File (AFN:9HMD-WL).  One line gives her birth date as"abt 935"
2 CONT and only refers to her as "Concubine 1".  She is also shown withthe
2 CONT following other parents in the Ancestral File (AFN:FLGR-TL):
2 CONT Herbastus /DE CREPON/ (AFN:FLGR-SF) and Herbastus De C /MRS./
2 CONT _UID9342BC284EA3B847BC4AF52C91E516C5211A
2 CONT Sources: A. Roots 121E; RC 166; Onslow; Davis; AF.
2 CONT Davis: Emma, first wife; daughter of Hugh, Great Count of France.
2 CONT Source: Ancestral Roots 39.
2 CONT Roots: Mother of illegitimate daughter by Richard I.
2 CONT Name Suffix:<NSFX> Princess France Ancestral File Number:<AFN> 9G85-N7
2 CONT !"France in the Middle Ages,987-1460" by George Duby,1987, chart 6.
