2 CONT '''RICHARD, son of GUILLAUME Comte [ de Normandie] & his first [wife] Sprota --- (Fécamp [932]-Fécamp 20 Nov 996, bur Fécamp[76]).'''  Guillaume of Jumièges records that, after the rebel “Riulfus” was defeated at the battle of "Pr
2 CONC atum-belli", a messenger arrived “a...Fiscannensis castri” and reported the birth of his son to “nobilissima puella Danico more sibi iuncta...Sprota” to Guillaume who ordered him to be sent immediately to “Baiocas...episcopo Henri
2 CONC co” for baptism as “Richardum”[77].  Flodoard records "filio ipsius Willelmi, nato de concubina Brittana" being granted the land of the Normans by King Louis after his father's death[78].  Richard is described as "a boy" on the de
2 CONC ath of his father by Dudo of Saint-Quentin[79]. Guillaume of Jumièges records that, after his father was killed, “puerum Richardum” was recalled from Bayeux and placed "sub tutela Bernardi Dani"[80]. ''' He succeeded his father as
2 CONC  RICHARD I "Sans Peur" Comte [de Normandie].'''  Orderic Vitalis records that "Willelmus dux" was killed in 942 “fraude Arnulphi Flandrensis satrapæ” and that “Ricardus filius eius...tunc decem annorum” was made “dux Normannorum” 
2 CONC a position which he held for 54 years[81].  He used the title Comte de Rouen/comes Rothomagensium, and from 966 Marquis des Normands/marchio Normannorum[82].  Guillaume of Jumièges records that Louis IV King of the West Franks, af
2 CONC ter the death of Richard´s father, marched on Rouen, was received by “Rodulphus et Bernardus atque Anslech totius Normannici ducatus tutores”, and captured Richard, who was taken to Laon but was freed by “Osmundus...consilio cum Y
2 CONC vone patre Willelmi de Belismo” and taken to “Silvanectis” where “Bernardus...comes” protected “nepotem suum Richardum”[83].  His forces defeated the army of Otto I King of Germany after it attempted to capture Rouen in revenge fo
2 CONC r the escape of comte Richard from captivity[84].  Comte Richard defeated French forces after King Lothaire of France captured Evreux.  Dudo de Saint-Quentin records that, soon after succeeding, Richard suppressed the rebellion of
2 CONC  Rodulf "Torta", who was banished and fled to Paris[85].  These events are not dated, but are recounted with the betrothal of Richard to the daughter of Hugues Duc des Francs, which is dated to 956.  Hugues "le Grand" Duc des Fran
2 CONC cs nominated comte Richard as guardian of his son, the future Hugues "Capet" King of France, in 956, the arrangement being confirmed by Richard's betrothal to Hugues's sister[86].  He invited William of Volpiano, Italian abbot of 
2 CONC Saint-Bénigne at Dijon, to reform the Norman abbeys, and installed monks at Mont-Saint-Michel and Fécamp[87].  He agreed a non-aggression pact with Æthelred II King of England 1 Mar 991, designed no doubt to prevent either side fr
2 CONC om sheltering Viking marauders[88].  "Ricardus filius Willelmi, dux Normannie" founded Louviers "in Ebroicensi pago" by undated charter[89].  Guillaume of Jumièges records the death “apud Fiscannum” in 996 of “Richardus dux primus
2 CONC ”[90].  Orderic Vitalis records the death in 996 of "Ricardo seniore"[91].  The Brevis Relatio de Origine Willelmi Conquestoris records that "Ricardus…filius Willelmi et alius Ricardus" were buried "Fiscanni"[92]. '''married  firs
2 CONC tly (betrothed 956, Rouen 960) EMMA, daughter of HUGUES "le Grand" Duc des Francs, Comte de Paris & his third wife Hedwig of Germany ([943]-after 19 Mar 968).'''  The Liber Modernorum Regum Francorum records the marriage in 956 of
2 CONC  "Richardus filius Guillelmi principis Normannorum" with "filiam Hugonis ducis", although she is not named[93].  Guillaume of Jumièges records the betrothal of “Hugo dux...filiam suam...Emmam” and “puerum Richardum”, with the cons
2 CONC ent of “Bernardi Silvanectensis”, and in a later passage their marriage[94].  No direct proof has yet been identified that Emma was the daughter of her father's third marriage.  However, this is likely given that betrothals at the
2 CONC  time normally took place when the female partner was still a child or in early adolescence.  Guillaume of Jumièges records the death without children of “Emma uxor eius filia Hugonis Magni”[95]. '''married  secondly ([before 989]
2 CONC ) GUNNORA, daughter of --- ([950]-5 Jan 1031).'''  Guillaume de Jumièges records that "in domo forestarii...hospiti suo...uxorem suam Sainfriam" rejected the advances of Richard I Comte [de Normandie] and sent “Gunnorem sororem su
2 CONC am” to his bed in her place[96].  Guillaume of Jumièges records that Richard married “Gunnor ex nobilissima Danorum prosapia ortam”, in the sentence which follows the record of the death of Richard´s first wife[97].  According to 
2 CONC Robert de Torigny, the marriage took place to legitimise Richard and Gunnora's son Robert to permit his appointment as Archbishop of Rouen[98].  The Chronicle of Alberic de Trois-Fontaines names "Gunnor" as the wife of "dux Norman
2 CONC nie primus Richardus"[99].  "Duke Richard [II]" donated property to the abbey of Mont Saint-Michel by charter dated to [1024/26], subscribed by "…Gonnor matris comitis…"[100].  Robert of Torigny records the death in 1030 of "Gunno
2 CONC r comitissa uxor primi Ricardi"[101].  The necrology of Saint-Père-en-Vallée records the death "Non Jan" of "Gonnoridis…comitissa Normannie"[102]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Richard & his second wife GUNNORA had eight children (legitimated [before 989] by the subsequent marriage of their parents):''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1.         '''RICHARD''' (-23 Aug 1026, bur Fécamp).  Guillaume of Jumièges names “Richardum...et Robertum atque Malgerium aliosque duos” as the sons of Richard and his wife “Gunnor ex nobilissima Danorum prosapia ortam”[103].  
2 CONC '''He succeeded his father in 996 as RICHARD II "le Bon/l'Irascible" Comte de Normandie.  Duke of Normandy [1015].'''   
2 CONT * 2.         '''ROBERT''' (-1037).  Guillaume of Jumièges names “Richardum...et Robertum atque Malgerium aliosque duos” as the sons of Richard and his wife “Gunnor ex nobilissima Danorum prosapia ortam”[104].  The Chronicle of Alb
2 CONC eric de Trois-Fontaines names "Robertus archiepiscopus Rothomagensis" as brother of "dux Normannie Richardus II"[105].  He is named as brother of Duke Richard II by Orderic Vitalis[106].  Comte d'Evreux.  Archbishop of Rouen 989, 
2 CONC after his parents married to legitimise him in order to regularise his appointment according to Robert de Torigny[107].  An agreement between the abbots of Jumièges and Bougeuil concerning an exchange of land in Poitou, by charter
2 CONC  dated [13 Apr/4 Apr] 1012, is subscribed by "Richardus…filius Ricardi principi magni…Robertus archiepiscopus…ecclesie Rotomagensis et Vuillelmus et Malgerus fratres Richardi comitis…"[108].  Guillaume of Jumièges records the succ
2 CONC ession of Robert after the death of his brother, his siege of “Ebroicum...urbem” due to his enmity for “Robertum archipræsulum” who fled to “Robertum regem Francorum” before being reconciled with Duke Robert[109].  Robert of Torig
2 CONC ny records the death in 1037 of "Robertus…archiepiscopus Rothomagensis"[110]. 
2 CONT * 3.         '''ROBERT''' ["Danus"] (-12 Aug [985/89]).  Guillaume of Jumièges names “Richardum...et Robertum atque Malgerium aliosque duos” as the sons of Richard and his wife “Gunnor ex nobilissima Danorum prosapia ortam”[111]. 
2 CONC  Robert of Torigny names "Ricardum…qui ei successit et Robertum postea archiepiscopum Rothomagensium et Malgerium comitem Curbuliensem, aliosque duos" as the sons of "Ricardi primi ducis Normanniæ" and Gunnora[112].  Houts names R
2 CONC obert “Danus” as one of these unnamed sons of Richard but does not cite the corresponding source[113].  The necrology of Saint-Père-en-Vallée records the death "II Id Aug" of "Robertus puer filius comitis Richardi"[114]. 
2 CONT * 4.         '''MAUGER''' (-[1033/40]).  Guillaume of Jumièges names “Richardum...et Robertum atque Malgerium aliosque duos” as the sons of Richard and his wife “Gunnor ex nobilissima Danorum prosapia ortam”[115].  Robert of Torig
2 CONC ny names "Ricardum…qui ei successit et Robertum postea archiepiscopum Rothomagensium et Malgerium comitem Curbuliensem, aliosque duos" as the sons of "Ricardi primi ducis Normanniæ" and Gunnora[116].  An agreement between the abbo
2 CONC ts of Jumièges and Bougeuil concerning an exchange of land in Poitou, by charter dated [13 Apr/4 Apr] 1012, is subscribed by "Richardus…filius Ricardi principi magni…Robertus archiepiscopus…ecclesie Rotomagensis et Vuillelmus et M
2 CONC algerus fratres Richardi comitis…"[117].  Comte de Corbeil, by right of his wife. 
2 CONT * 5.         '''Son''' .  Guillaume of Jumièges names “Richardum...et Robertum atque Malgerium aliosque duos” as the sons of Richard and his wife “Gunnor ex nobilissima Danorum prosapia ortam”[118].  Robert of Torigny names "Ricar
2 CONC dum…qui ei successit et Robertum postea archiepiscopum Rothomagensium et Malgerium comitem Curbuliensem, aliosque duos" as the sons of "Ricardi primi ducis Normanniæ" and Gunnora[119].  No reference has been found to the name of t
2 CONC his son. 
2 CONT * 6.         '''EMMA''' ([985]-Winchester 14 Mar 1052, bur Winchester Cathedral).  Guillaume de Poitou names “genitrix Emma filia Ricardi primi, genitor Ædelredus rex Anglorum” as parents of “Edwardus ac Alveradus”[120].  Guillaum
2 CONC e of Jumièges names “Emma...secunda Hadvis...tertia Mathildis” as the three daughters of Richard and his wife “Gunnor ex nobilissima Danorum prosapia ortam”, adding that Emma '''married “Edelredo regi Anglorum” by whom she was mot
2 CONC her of “rex Edwardum et Alvredum”[121].'''  The Chronicle of Alberic de Trois-Fontaines names "Emma Anglorum regina" as sister of "dux Normannie Richardus II"[122].  Emma was described by Henry of Huntingdon as "Emma Normanorum ge
2 CONC mma"[123], although it is not known whether this was a particular indication of her beauty or mere hyperbole.  She was known as ÆLFGIFU in England[124].  Her first husband sent her to her brother's court in Normandy in 1013 after 
2 CONC the invasion of Svend King of Denmark[125].  She was living in Normandy in 1017 when King Æthelred's successor King Canute proposed marriage to her.  Guillaume of Jumièges records that, after the death of “Edelredus rex”, “Emmam r
2 CONC eginam” married “rex...Chunutus...Christiano more”, and names their children “Hardechunutum postmodum regem Danorum et filiam...Gunnildem quæ nupsit Henrico Romanorum Imperatori”[126].  Roger of Wendover records the marriage in Ju
2 CONC l 1018 of "Cnuto" and "ducem Ricardum…Emmam sororem suam et regis Ethelredi relictam"[127].  After the death of her second husband, she continued to live at Winchester.  After the election of her step-son as regent in early 1036, 
2 CONC it was recognised that she would continue to live there to look after the interests of her son Harthacnut who had nominally succeeded his father as King of England and Denmark but was still absent in Denmark.  It is likely that sh
2 CONC e encouraged her sons by her first husband, Edward and Alfred, to join her, Alfred being captured and murdered during the visit.  After Harold was recognised as king of England in 1037, Queen Emma was expelled and took refuge at B
2 CONC ruges[128].  She commissioned the Encomium Emmæ Reginæ from a Flemish convent at Saint-Omer, maybe St Bertin's, designed to promote her son Harthacnut's claim to the English throne.  Harthacnut joined her in Bruges in early 1040, 
2 CONC and after the death of King Harold, they returned together to England.  After the accession of Edward "the Confessor", her son by her first husband, Emma appears to have supported the rival claim of Magnus King of Norway[129].  Wh
2 CONC atever the truth of this, King Edward did confiscate her property in 1043 according to the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle[130].  She seems to have spent the last years of her life in retirement in Winchester[131].  The Anglo-Saxon Chronicl
2 CONC e records the death of "Ælfgifu Emma, the mother of king Edward and of king Harthacnut" in 1052[132].  '''married firstly (betrothed 1000, 1002[133]) as his [second/third] wife, ÆTHELRED II King of England, son of EDGAR "the Peaca
2 CONC ble" King of England & his second wife Ælfthryth''' ([966]-London 23 Apr 1016, bur Old St Paul's Cathedral).  '''married secondly (2 or 31 Jul 1017) CANUTE King of England, son of SVEND I "Tveskæg/Forkbeard" King of Denmark & his 
2 CONC [second wife Sigrid of Poland] ([995]-'''Shaftesbury, Dorset 12 Nov 1035, bur Winchester Cathedral).  King of Denmark 1018, King of Norway 1028. 
2 CONT * 7.         '''HAVISE''' (-21 Feb 1034).  Guillaume of Jumièges names “Emma...secunda Hadvis...tertia Mathildis” as the three daughters of Richard and his wife “Gunnor ex nobilissima Danorum prosapia ortam”, adding that Havise ma
2 CONC rried “Goiffredo Britannorum comiti” by whom she had “Alanum et Eudonem duces”[134].  A charter dated 1008 records that, after the death of "Gaufrido comite Britanniæ", "filii eius Alanus et Eudo cum matre eorum Hadeguisia" restor
2 CONC ed the abbey of Saint-Méen[135].  "…Aduise matre eorum comitum…" signed the charter dated to [1013/22] under which "Alanus et Egio Britannorum monarchi" founded the priory of Livré "in pago Redonensi"[136].  The Chronico Kemperleg
2 CONC iensi records the death "1034 IX Kal Mar" of "Haduisa comitissa Britanniæ, vidua Gauffridi"[137].  '''married  (996) GEOFFROY I Duke of Brittany, son of CONAN I "le Tort" Duke of Brittany & his wife Ermengarde d'Anjou ([980]-20 No
2 CONC v 1008).''' 
2 CONT * 8.         '''MATHILDE''' (-[1005]).  Guillaume of Jumièges names “Emma...secunda Hadvis...tertia Mathildis” as the three daughters of Richard and his wife “Gunnor ex nobilissima Danorum prosapia ortam”, adding that Mathilde mar
2 CONC ried “Odoni comiti” by whom she was childless, a later passage stating that her dowry was “medietatem Dorcasini castri”[138].  The same source recounts that this provided the basis for the dispute between Comte Eudes and Duke Rich
2 CONC ard which escalated into the construction of the château de Tillières (“castrum Tegulense”) {Verneuil, Eure} and Eudes's defeat while attempting to capture it.  '''married  ([1003/04]) as his first wife, EUDES [II] Comte de Blois,
2 CONC  son of EUDES [I] Comte de Blois & his wife Berthe de Bourgogne [Welf] ([982/83]-15 Nov 1037).''' 
