2 CONT
2 CONT Richard épouse Emma, fille d'Hugues le Grand et sœur d'Hugues Capet, en 960. Ils n'ont pas d'enfants. C'est de Gunnor, épousée more danico que Richard a une descendance :
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Richard II, son successeur ;
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Robert, comte d'Évreux et archevêque de Rouen ;
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Mauger, comte de Corbeil ;
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Emma, future reine des Anglo-Saxons (par son premier mariage avec Ethelred II), puis des Danois (par ses secondes noces avec Knut II) ;
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Havoise, épouse de Geoffroy, comte de Rennes ;
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Mathilde († 1004), épouse d'Eudes II de Blois, comte de Blois.
2 CONT
2 CONT Il est probable que le duc Richard eut d'autres concubines, des « frilla » (concubine à la manière danoise), et de nombreux bâtards dont :
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Godefroi de Brionne († 1015), comte d'Eu et de Brionne (cf. famille De Clare) ;
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Guillaume de Brionne († 1058), comte d'Hiémois, qui succèdera à son neveu Gilbert de Brionne († 1040), à Eu.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Robert, comte de l'Avranchin (ou comte de Mortain)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Papie
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Béatrice
2 CONT
2 CONT Les nombreux descendants du duc Richard et de son fils aîné sont appelés les Richardides.
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard was born to William I of Normandy, ruler of Normandy, and his wife, Sprota. He was still a boy when his father died in 942. His mother was a Breton concubine captured in war and bound to William by a Danish marriage. After
2 CONC  William died, Sprota became the wife of Esperleng, a wealthy miller; Rodulf of Ivry was their son and Richard's half-brother.
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard was still a boy when his father died, and so he was powerless to stop Louis IV of France when he seized Normandy. Louis kept him in confinement in his youth at Lâon, but he escaped with the assistance of Osmond de Centvill
2 CONC e, Bernard de Senlis (who had been a companion of Rollo of Normandy), Ivo de Bellèsme, and Bernard the Dane (ancestor of families of Harcourt and Beaumont). In 968, Richard agreed to "commend" himself to Hugh, Count of Paris. He t
2 CONC hen allied himself with the Norman and Viking leaders, drove Louis out of Rouen, and took back Normandy by 947. He later quarrelled with Ethelred II of England regarding Viking invasions of England because Normandy had been buying
2 CONC  up much of the stolen booty.
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard was bilingual, having been well educated at Bayeux. He was more partial to his Danish subjects than to the French. During his reign, Normandy became completely Gallicized and Christianized. He introduced the feudal system 
2 CONC and Normandy became one of the most thoroughly feudalized states on the continent. He carried out a major reorganization of the Norman military system, based on heavy cavalry. He also became guardian of the young Hugh, Count of Pa
2 CONC ris, on the elder Hugh's death in 956.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married 1st (960) Emma (not to be confused with Emma of France), daughter of Hugh "The Great" of France, and Hedwiga de Sachsen. They were betrothed when both were very young. She died 19 Mar 968, with no issue.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Robert of Torigni, not long after Emma's death, Duke Richard went out hunting and stopped at the house of a local forester. He became enamoured of the forester's wife, Seinfreda, but she being a virtuous woman, sugges
2 CONC ted he court her unmarried sister, Gunnor, instead. Gunnor became his mistress, and her family rose to prominence. Her brother, Herefast de Crepon, may have been involved in a controversial heresy trial. Gunnor was, like Richard, 
2 CONC of Norse descent, being a Dane by blood. Richard finally married her to legitimize their children:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Richard II "the Good", Duke of Normandy (966)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Robert, Archbishop of Rouen, Count of Evreux, le Danois (Danus) died 1037.*
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Mauger, Earl of Corbeil, died after 1033; his alleged grandson (or perhaps great-grandson) was Robert Fitzhamon, an important Anglo-Norman baron.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Emma of Normandy (c.985-1052) wife of two kings of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Maud of Normandy, wife of Odo II of Blois, Count of Blois, Champagne and Chartres
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Hawise of Normandy (b. ca. 978), d. 21 February 1034. m. Geoffrey I, Duke of Brittany
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Beatrice of Normandy, Abbess of Montvilliers d.1034 m. Ebles of Turenne (d.1030 (divorced)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Papia m. Gilbert de St Valery.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Fressenda (ca. 995-ca. 1057), m. Tancred of Hauteville.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Muriella m. Tancred of Hauteville.
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard was known to have had several other mistresses and produced children with many of them. Known children are:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Geoffrey, Count of Brionne, (b. ca. 970)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * William, Count of Eu (ca. 972-26 January 1057/58) m. Leseline de Turqueville (d. 26 January 1057/58).
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Hawise of Normandy (b. ca. 978), d. 21 February 1034. m. Geoffrey I, Duke of Brittany
2 CONT
2 CONT He died in Fecamp, France on November 20, 996 of natural causes.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Danus = Robert Archbishop of Rouen:
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert le Danois fut archevêque de Rouen (989-1037) et comte d'Évreux (996-1037) au temps des premiers ducs de Normandie.
2 CONT
2 CONT Fils du duc Richard Ier de Normandie et de Gunnor, il fait donc partie de ce puissant groupe aristocratique appelé les Richardides. Son père lui confie très jeune l'archevêché de Rouen, principal siège ecclésiastique du duché de N
2 CONC ormandie. L'historiographe Guillaume de Jumièges rapporte que les clercs se sont opposés à cette décision tant que le duc refusait de se marier chrétiennement avec sa frilla Gunnor. Selon le voeu du clergé, Richard Ier de Normandi
2 CONC e épousa finalement Gunnor et leur fils Robert put devenir archevêque.
2 CONT
2 CONT Quelques années plus tard, peut-être juste après la mort de son père en 996, Robert reçoit la charge du comté d'Évreux. Cette double fonction – archevêque de Rouen et comte – fait de lui le plus puissant personnage de Normandie ap
2 CONC rès le duc. De surcroît, le nouveau duc, Richard II (996-1026), est son frère.
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------------------
