2 CONT Marriage and Descendants
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Beatrice of Hainaut, the daughter of Reginar IV, Count of Mons and Hedwig of France (daughter of Hugh Capet). From this union he had:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Alix, who succeeded him, and was married to Hilduin IV of Montdidier, Count of Ramerupt and Roucy, by whom she was the mother of Ebles II of Roucy. Her daughter, Margaret de Roucy, married Hugh, Count of Clermont, and they were
2 CONC  the parents of Adeliza, who married Gilbert Fitz Richard;
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Hedwig, who inherited the lands of Rumigny after the death of her uncle, Eudes, and that married Geoffrey IV, Lord of Florennes.
2 CONT
2 CONT Around 1020, Ebles separated from his wife[8][5] and took holy orders, being therefore elected archbishop of Reims.[1] He also became count of Reims in 1023 and afterwards bound the county to the archdiocese. Beatrice then went on
2 CONC  to marry Manasses de Ramerupt.
2 CONT
2 CONT ************
2 CONT From fmgMedlands 
2 CONT https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/nfralaoncou.htm#_Toc494629909
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.         [EBLES (-11 May 1033).  The Genealogiciæ Scriptoris Fusniacensis names "Lebaldus de Malla et…Iveta comitissa de Retest" as brother and sister of "Ebalus de Roceio"[389].  Comte de Roucy 997.  The Chronicle of Alberic de
2 CONC  Trois-Fontaines records the succession in 1023 as archbishop of Reims of "Ebalus qui fuit comes de Roseio"[390].  Archbishop of Reims 1021.  Moranvillé casts doubt on the co-identity of Ebles Comte de Roucy with Ebles Archbishop 
2 CONC of Reims[391].  He deems significant that Alberic uses “Roseio” in the passage cited above, whereas in all other passages in which he refers to the Roucy family he uses “Roceio”.  He also cites two sources which report the appoint
2 CONC ment of the new archbishop but make no reference to his reported Roucy origin.  Firstly, a letter of Fulbert Bishop of Chartres addressed to Guy Bishop of Soissons dated 1021[392], and secondly Hariulf’s Chronicle of Saint-Riquier
2 CONC [393].  Both these sources refer only briefly to the archbishop, without any description of his background, so the absence of any reference in them to his antecedents would not be surprising.  As for the difference in spelling Ros
2 CONC eio/Roceio, this could be explained by a simple mistake or transcription error.  In any case, no reference has been found to any “comte de Roussy/Rousy”, as opposed to Roucy, at that time.  It is correct that there are numerous ex
2 CONC amples of information in Alberic which is disproved by other primary sources.  However, they are far outnumbered by cases in which his information is corroborated, and are insufficient to conclude that Alberic should be assumed to
2 CONC  be unreliable.  The Annales Mosomagenses record the death in 1033 of “Oebalus Remorum archiepiscopus”[394].  The necrology of Reims records the death “V Id Mai” of “dominus Ebalus archiepiscopus” and his donation of “ecclesiam de
2 CONC  Betignivilla”[395].  
2 CONT
2 CONT m (divorced before 1021) as her first husband, BEATRIX de Hainaut, daughter of REGINAR IV Comte de Hainaut & his wife Hedwige de France (after [996]-).  The Chronicle of Alberic de Trois-Fontaines names "Beatricem" as daughter of 
2 CONC "Rainero comiti de Hainaco" & his wife Hedwige, naming her husband "Ebalus de Roceio"[396].  The Genealogiæ Scriptoris Fusniacensis gives more details, naming "Beatricem" daughter of "Hadevidem…comitissam Hainonensium" and specify
2 CONC ing that she married "Ebalus de Roceio, cuius frater fuit Lebaldus de Malla et soror Iveta comitissa de Roitest" and later "Manasses cui agnomen Calva-asina"[397].  She married secondly ([1021]) Manassès de Ramerupt  "Calva-asina"
2 CONC  [Montdidier].  Comte Ebles & his wife had two children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ************
2 CONT
2 CONT His parentage has been the subject of much discussion and dispute, with many conflicting interpretations. Ebles and his apparent siblings (Eudes/Odo "the Strong", Lietard de Marle, and Jutta Countess of Rethel) have long been posi
2 CONC ted as children of Gislebert de Roucy because of inheritance of the Count of Roucy title. However, recent scholarship by Jean-Noel Mathieu (20000; see Todd Farmerie's summary, below) problematizes this model and presents an altern
2 CONC ative which places these four instead as children of Eudes of Poitou and an unnamed daughter of Aubri II de Macon and Ermentrude.
2 CONT
2 CONT From: "Todd A. Farmerie" <farmerie@interfold.com> 
2 CONT Subject: Mathieu: Parentage of Ebles, Count of Roucy 
2 CONT Date: Wed, 21 Nov 2001 16:28:09 -0700
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean-Noel Mathieu, La succession au comte de Roucy aux environs
2 CONT de l'an mil. Les origines de l'archeveque de Reims Ebles
2 CONT (1021-1033). in Onomastique et Parente dans l'Occident medieval,
2 CONT K.S.B. Keats-Rohan and C. Settipani, eds., (2000) pp. 75-84.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 967, Renaud, Count of Roucy died, leaving four children: 
2 CONT Bruno, Gislebert, Ermentrude, and another daughter, name unknown,
2 CONT who married Fromond II of Sens. Bruno (a name which by this time
2 CONT seemed to mark those destined for the church) became Bishop of
2 CONT Langres, while Gislebert succeeded his father as Count of Roucy,
2 CONT dying about 1000. At this time (in fact, for a period before)
2 CONT Roucy passes into a period of obscurity, which only resolves with
2 CONT the appearance of Ebles, Count of Roucy, in the 1020s, who was
2 CONT likewise made Archbishop of Reims. Ebles has traditionally been
2 CONT placed in a direct line of succession, viewed as son of
2 CONT Gislebert, but Gislebert is not known to have married or had
2 CONT children, while several other factors raise red flags (i.e. the
2 CONT name Ebles has no prior exemplar in this family, the time from
2 CONT the death of Renaud to the appearance of Ebles seems long for
2 CONT only one intervening generation). This leads Mathieu to conclude
2 CONT that Ebles was not son of Gislebert, and came about his title
2 CONT through a less direct route.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ebles' family consisted of a brother, Eudes, known from
2 CONT contemporary documents, and two siblings, Letaud of Marle and
2 CONT Jutta, wife of Manasses of Rethel found in an ancient
2 CONT genealogical source from Foigny. (Jutta is not discussed
2 CONT further, but while Ebles apparently d. 1033, Manasses was not
2 CONT even active until 1066, and was still living in 1081 - the
2 CONT chronology doesn't work, so there must be some sort of error
2 CONT here, as others have pointed out). This gives us a group of
2 CONT names, Ebles, Eudes, and Letaud (leaving out Jutta because of the
2 CONT chronological difficulties) that can be useful in identifying
2 CONT their kinship. Also of use are the titles themselves. Rumigny,
2 CONT Coucy (in the hands of the maternal grandson of Letaud, and
2 CONT thought to have come through this line) and Reims itself have
2 CONT links to the Blois counts, and specifically the descendants of
2 CONT Thibaud with Liegarde of Vermandois. This is where the data
2 CONT ends, and the hypotheses begin.
2 CONT
2 CONT The name Ebles appears prominantly only in the family of Ebles
2 CONT Manzer, Count of Poitou. His grandson William II (IV), Count of
2 CONT Poitou, married Emma, sister of Eudes I, Count of Blois and son
2 CONT of Thibaud and Liegarde, so a descent from this marriage would
2 CONT seemingly explain not only the names Ebles and Eudes, but also
2 CONT the descent of some of the smaller holdings of the family. The
2 CONT chronology is a bit of a challenge because of the multiple
2 CONT marriages and children of William III (V), but it would seem that
2 CONT Ebles Count of Roucy would belong in the generation of his
2 CONT grandchildren, and a possible candidate for his father is an
2 CONT Ebles, brother of Count William III, who is known from only one
2 CONT document dating from the reign on Robert II of France. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Such a solution leaves unaddressed the succession to Roucy
2 CONT itself, as well as the name Letaud, and so provisionally
2 CONT accepting Eudes as father, we turn to the maternal lineage. The
2 CONT most obvious earlier occurance for Letaud is the father of Count
2 CONT Aubri of Macon. This man married a woman named Ermentrude, and
2 CONT from an early date, she has been identified with the daughter of
2 CONT Renaud de Roucy, and subsequent wife of Otto-William, Count of
2 CONT Burgundy. However, recently it has been argued that there are
2 CONT difficulties with this identification, mostly chronological. 
2 CONT However, a closer look shows that these problems can be resolved,
2 CONT and an early source specifically states that the widow of Aubri
2 CONT married William, and from this marriage came Otto, Count of
2 CONT Macon. Thus it would seem that the name Letaud and the Roucy
2 CONT inheritance would have been united in a child of the marriage of
2 CONT Aubri II with Ermentrude de Roucy.
2 CONT
2 CONT (As an aside, this resolution of the chronological difficulties
2 CONT with identifying Ermentrude as wife both of Aubri and
2 CONT Otto-William requires the supposition that Aubri married twice,
2 CONT and that Beatrice, wife of Geoffrey of Gatinais, ancestress of
2 CONT the Anjou Counts, must have been by the earlier wife. The author
2 CONT hypothesizes that she may have been a sister of Hugh Capet. 
2 CONT While not explained, the reasoning would seem to be based on the
2 CONT name Beatrice, a name found in that family since Robert (I)
2 CONT married Beatrice of Vermandois. Other writings in the same
2 CONT compilation add a generation, making Beatrice daughter of Aubri
2 CONT by a daughter of Geoffrey, Vicomte of Orleans, who in turn is
2 CONT made brother-in-law, of Hugh Capet.)
2 CONT
2 CONT By hypothesizing a marriage between Eudes of Poitou and a
2 CONT daughter of Aubri II and Ermentrude, Mathieu brings together the
2 CONT various names, Ebles, Eudes and Letaud, with the various titles,
2 CONT Rumigny, Coucy, Reims, and most importantly Roucy. (It would
2 CONT also recapitulate a common pattern, in which brothers William and
2 CONT Ebles of Poitou married half-sisters daughters of Ermentrude.) 
2 CONT The solution is not without problems - most importantly, Ebles
2 CONT and his wife Beatrice of Hainaut would both be
2 CONT great-grandchildren of William I (III) (assuming the correct
2 CONT identification of Adelaide, wife of Hugh Capet). Mathieu
2 CONT suggests that this marriage, which produced just two daughters,
2 CONT may have been ended in premature separation due to consanginity.
2 CONT
2 CONT That, then, is the Mathieu hypothesis.
2 CONT
2 CONT taf
2 CONT --------------------
