1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Ranulph I de Varennes''' was born circa 998. He was the son of Guillaume de Varennes and N. N. de Torta. Ranulph I de Varennes married Beatrice of Rouen, daughter of Tesselin of Rouen and N. N. de Crepon, before 1036. Ranulph I
2 CONC  de Varennes died after 1074.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT Beatrice of Rouen b. circa 980
2 CONT
2 CONT Children	
2 CONT # Roger de Mortemer+ b. c 1029?, d. b 1086
2 CONT # Guillaume I de Varennes, 1st Earl of Surrey+ b. c 1036, d. 24 Jun 1088
2 CONT # Ranulph II de Warenne+ b. a 1036, d. a 1074
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/144124159/ranulf_i_hugh-de_warenne
2 CONT
2 CONT (Probably) not the father of https://www.geni.com/people/William-de-Warenne-1st-Earl-of-Surrey/6000000005406630017
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT He is believed to have possibly been a brother or close kinsman to [http://www.geni.com/people/Roger-de-Mortimer/6000000000437137217 Roger de Mortimer], Seigneur de Mortemer-sur-Eaulne, near Neufchâtel-en-Brai, Normandy
2 CONT
2 CONT They were possibly sons of [http://www.geni.com/people/Gautier-de-St-Martin/6000000000232597777 Walter de Saint-Martin]. There is also a possibility that they were sons of [http://www.geni.com/people/Hugh-de-Coutances-Bishop-of-Co
2 CONC utances/6000000015019969348 Hugues de Coutances, Bishop of Coutances]. I have come across some sources that suggest yet another possible parentage--i.e.,  that he may have been the illegitimate son of [http://www.geni.com/people/H
2 CONC ugh-d-Ivry/6000000008177334543 Hugh d'Ivry, Bishop of Bayeux].
2 CONT
2 CONT Ranulph II de Warenne b abt 1012.married Béatrice de Vascoeuïl. [His son Radolf/Ranulf II married Emma]
2 CONT
2 CONT Child of Ranulph II de Warenne and Emma were:
2 CONT
2 CONT *     * Ranulph III de Warenne b abt 1032.
2 CONT *     * Sir William I de Warenne b abt 1034.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.medievalgenealogy.org.uk/cp/surrey.shtml:
2 CONT
2 CONT Some corrections and additions to the Complete Peerage: Volume 12, Part 1: Surrey
2 CONT
2 CONT Index SURREY
2 CONT
2 CONT See also "proposed" section
2 CONT
2 CONT Volume 12, Part 1, pages 491-3 (as modified by volume 14): RODULF DE WARENNE I ... is said to have held land outside the walls of Rouen under Robert I, Duke of Normandy (d. 1035) ... He also held land at Vascoeuil (dept. Eure), wh
2 CONC ich he gave about 1053 to the abbey of St. Pierre des Préaux,(c) and in the pays de Caux, north of Rouen, where he sold 4 churches with tithes to the Holy Trinity in 1059, and gave another church, also with tithes, in 1074. He m. 
2 CONC Beatrice, whose mother was almost certainly a sister of Gotmund Rufus DE VASCOEUIL, [and] da. of Tesselin, Vicomte of Rouen.(f) She was living about 1053. 
2 CONT
2 CONT RODULF DE WARENNE II, 1st s. and h., is known only from his subscriptions to two charters of his father for the Holy Trinity of Rouen. As his father's lands near Rouen and in the pays de Caux did not pass to his son William or Wil
2 CONC liam's descendants, it is likely that Rodulf suc. to them on his father's death; he m. Emma, whose parentage is unknown.(d) 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. WILLIAM DE WARENNE I was 1st s. of Rodulf II.
2 CONT
2 CONT Page 491, note g (continuation on p. 492): The entries for Rodulf I and Rodulf II need considerable revision, see "Aspects of Robert of Torigny's Genealogies revisited", K.S.B. Keats-Rohan, Nottingham Med. Studies, vol. 37, 1993, 
2 CONC pp. 21-4. Page 492, note c: Stapleton, Archaeological Journal, vol. iii, p. 11. His 1st wife Beatrice consented. Loyd points out that Vascoeuil had formed part of the ducal demesne (op. cit., p. 98); and it seems likely that the l
2 CONC and there was brought in by Beatrice. Note f: Keats-Rohan, op. cit. above, p. 24. Beatrice is there shown to have probably been a great-niece of Gunnor, 2nd wife of Richard I, Duke of Normandy. Page 493, note d: See Keats-Rohan, o
2 CONC p. cit., p. 22.
2 CONT
2 CONT Keats-Rohan's suggestion that Beatrice was a great niece of Gunnor must be viewed as somewhat conjectural. It is an attempt to explain Robert de Torigny's statement that William de Warenne I's (unnamed) mother was a niece of Gunno
2 CONC r, which is chronologically difficult because it seems to leave too few generations between Gunnor and William. Keats-Rohan's suggestion relies on the evidence that Beatrice was the daughter of Tesselin, vicomte of Rouen, in conju
2 CONC nction with Robert de Torigny's statement that a (different) niece of Gunnor married a vicomte of Rouen. But he actually specifies Richard, vicomte of Rouen, who was Tesselin's son. (An alternative conjecture might be that William
2 CONC  de Warenne's mother Emma was a great niece, rather than a niece, of Gunnor.)
2 CONT
2 CONT In Keats-Rohan's revised version, the dates originally given in relation to Rodulf I and his supposed 2nd wife, now apply to the marriage of Rodulf II and his wife Emma - they were married in or before 1059, and were both living i
2 CONC n 1074 [cf. vol. 12, part 1, p. 492, notes h and i, citing Cart. Ste Trinité, nos xxix and xxxv]. The charter evidence shows that Rodulf II and Emma had an elder son Rodulf III and a younger son William I. The statement about the 
2 CONC Warenne lands in Normandy relates to Rodulf III, and should read something like this (with the original conclusion restored): As Rodulf II's lands near Rouen and in the pays de Caux did not pass to his younger son William I or Wil
2 CONC liam's descendants, it is likely that his elder son Rodulf III suc. to them on Rodulf II's death; and as part of these lands had passed to the barons of Esneval by 1152, it is likely that Rodulf III married and left issue.
2 CONT
2 CONT [Stewart Baldwin pointed out Keats-Rohan's revision in June 1996; the problem was subsequently discussed by Alan Wilson, Todd Farmerie and others.]
2 CONT
2 CONT =<nowiki>----------------------</nowiki>=
2 CONT
2 CONT In a passage very relevant to this profile, Charles Cawley writes:
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ENGLISH%20NOBILITY%20MEDIEVAL2.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT There has been considerable debate about the ancestry of Roger [I] de Mortemer.  The first question relates to the possible relationship between Roger [I] de Mortemer and William de Warenne 1st Earl of Surrey.  This issue is discu
2 CONC ssed in the Complete Peerage which concludes that "its exact nature has not at present been discovered"[211].  The fact of the family relationship is indicated by Orderic Vitalis who, in a passage recounting an alleged death-bed s
2 CONC peech of William I King of England, records that the castle of Mortemer, confiscated from Roger [I] de Mortemer after the battle of Mortemer in 1054, was granted to "Guillelmo de Guarenna consanguineo eius"[212].  In addition to t
2 CONC his, Robert de Torigny, in his description of abbeys in Normandy, records that "Rogerius de Mortuo Mari, filius Walterii de Sancto Martino, frater vero primi Willermi de Warenna" founded "monasterium Sancti Victoris"[213].  A thir
2 CONC d source, Guillaume of Jumièges records that “nepotes...plures...Gunnor...una earum” married “patri primi Willelmi de Warenna” by whom she had “idem Willelmus postea comes Surreiæ et Rogerus de Mortuo-mari frater ipsius” [although
2 CONC  an undated charter quoted in the document NORMANDY NOBILITY, which records a sale of property by “Hugo de Flamenvilla”, indicates that Raoul´s second wife was the mother of his son Guillaume][214].  The second source is clearly i
2 CONC ncorrect as regards the parentage of William de Warenne, whose father is confirmed in other primary sources as Raoul de Warenne (see the document NORMANDY NOBILITY, WARENNE).  It is also clear that Roger [I] de Mortemer (already h
2 CONC older of a castle in 1054) must have been considerably older than William de Warenne, and so could hardly have been his brother.  Stapleton proposed in 1846 that Roger [I] de Mortimer and Raoul de Warenne, father of William de War
2 CONC enne 1st Earl of Surrey, were brothers, arguing that, because they are both mentioned in charters of Sainte-Trinité de Rouen in connection with the same property (see below), they had probably inherited it jointly[215]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Stapleton also proposed that Roger [I] de Mortemer was the same person as Roger, son of "Bishop Hugues".  The question of the identity of Bishop Hugues is discussed in the document NORMANDY NOBILITY.  Roger, son of the bishop, is 
2 CONC named in three charters, two of which name his father as Bishop Hugues.  Firstly, "…ejusdem Rodulfi de Guarethna., Beatricis uxori eius, Rogerii filii episcopi, Huberti filii Turoldi…" witnessed an undated charter which records an
2 CONC  agreement between Sainte-Trinité de Rouen and "Rodulfo Warethnæ" to buy land "in Blovilla…apud villam…Merdeplud…et terram prati Sottevillæ"[216].  Secondly, "Rogerius, Hugonis episcopi filius" sold serfs "sub suo dominio in Blovi
2 CONC lla et Einardi mansionali et Novillula et in Scurra vel Merdepluet villa…et suæ domus propriæ in urbe Rotomagi" to Sainte-Trinité de Rouen, with the consent of "sua uxore Odain…et eorum filiis Willelmo et Hugone", by undated chart
2 CONC er[217].  Thirdly, "Rodulfus de Warenna eiusque conjux…Emma cum filiis suis Rodulfo…atque Willelmo" sold "totius Osulfi Villæ eiusdem Caletensis pagi", sold by "Guillelmo filio Rogerii filii Hugonis episcopi", to Sainte-Trinité de
2 CONC  Rouen by charter dated 1074[218].  The Complete Peerage dismisses Stapleton´s hypothesis[219].  It argues firstly that the wife of Roger [I] de Mortemer is named Hawise in primary sources, compared with Oda as the wife of Roger, 
2 CONC son of the bishop, and also that the bishop´s son is recorded with children named Guillaume and Hugues, whereas Roger [I]´s heir was named Ralph, although it would not be beyond the stretch of imagination to combine the two famili
2 CONC es, with Roger having married twice.  The third difficulty proposed by the Complete Peerage is harder to dismiss.  This is that the 1074 charter quoted above implies that Roger, father of Guillaume, was already deceased at the tim
2 CONC e of the sale of their property to Raoul de Warenne, whereas sources demonstrate that Roger [I] de Mortemer was still alive in 1078.  A further difficulty with Stapleton´s hypothesis is that, if it was correct, the same person wou
2 CONC ld have been referred to in the sources sometimes as "filius episcopi" and sometimes as "de Mortuomari".  Such dual appellations are unusual.  Different primary sources at the time usually refer to the same individual by the same 
2 CONC name and epithet, presumably reflecting the style by which he was normally known among his contemporaries.  If a person was known by two names, the style "X qui et Y" was usually adopted in the sources.  One possible explanation f
2 CONC or this apparent exception to normal practice is that, after the confiscation of his castle, "Rogerius de Mortuomari" became known as "Rogerius filius episcopi", although this does not appear consistent with the survival of the na
2 CONC me Mortimer among Roger´s descendants long after the castle was lost. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT
