1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==concerns==
2 CONT '''Stirnet's "Viking01" page (link below) reports this Anslech ("Amflec of Briquebec (Brigenberg)") as a son of Hugues "Barbatus" ("Hugh Barbatus").  He is not a son of https://www.geni.com/people/Hrolf-Bigod/6000000009437210537 a
2 CONC nd https://www.geni.com/people/Gerlotte-de-Blois/6000000004533630264.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==brief biography==
2 CONT Lancelot De Briquebec (son of Hugh Barbatus)31, 31 was born 905 in Bricquibec, Manche, Normandy, France31, 31, and died 955 in Briquebec, La Manche, Normandy, France31, 31.He married Gerlotte De Blois.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==family==
2 CONT Children of Lancelot De Briquebec and Gerlotte De Blois are:
2 CONT
2 CONT * Anslec De Bertrand, b. 937, Tillieres, Normandy, France31, 31, d. 962, Seigneur, DE, USA31.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==notes==
2 CONT Date of birth might specifically be either 905 or 909.
2 CONT
2 CONT Date of death might specifically be 955.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==comments==
2 CONT Surname has also been reported to be:
2 CONT
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/surnames/de-bricquebec de Bricquebec]
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/surnames/de-briquebec de Briquebec]
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/surnames/de-briquibec de Briquibec]
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/surnames/de-brioquebec de Brioquebec]
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/surnames/de-brioquibec de Brioquibec]
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/surnames/de-barbatus de Barbatus] (which is a corruption of his father's epithet)
2 CONT
2 CONT Given name has also been reported to be:
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Lancelot
2 CONT * '''Amflec
2 CONT * '''Anselc
2 CONT * '''Alsac
2 CONT * '''Oslac
2 CONT
2 CONT Date and place of birth have also been (erroneously?) reported to be:
2 CONT
2 CONT * circa 898 in Normandy, France
2 CONT * circa 920 in Normandy, France
2 CONT * circa 937 at Tillières, Maine-et-Loire, Pays de la Loire, France
2 CONT
2 CONT Date and place of death have also been (erroneously?) reported to be 990 at an unspecified location.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==links==
2 CONT * https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anslech_de_Bricquebec
2 CONT * https://www.stirnet.com/genie/data/ancient/tz/viking01.php#lnklo (membership required to view without interruption)
2 CONT
2 CONT ==content to clean up==
2 CONT Anslec (Lancelot) de Bastembourg, ancestor of the barons of Briquebec, son of Hrolf Turstin, was a powerful lord in early Normandy. Tradition attributes the foundation of the château at Briquebec, in the northern Cotentin Peninsul
2 CONC a (la Manche) of Normandy, to the Norman, Anslech. The dukes of Normandy made Bricquebec one of their strongholds.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anslec played a powerful political role after Duke William Longsword was assassinated in December 942. Then, he became part of a triumvirate which directed the future of the Norman duchy during the minority of Richard, partnering 
2 CONC with Bernard de Pont-Audemer, Osmond de Conteville and Raoul Taisson.
2 CONT
2 CONT =='''Anslech de Bricquebec'''==
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anslech_de_Bricquebec
2 CONT
2 CONT Anslech or Anslec de Bricquebec (active in the 930s and 940s) played a major political role in the first days of the duchy of Normandy, though the sources on him are rather opaque.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===In the Anglo-Norman chronicles===
2 CONT
2 CONT Around 1000, Dudo of Saint-Quentin evoked Anslech as one of the three secretarii to the jarl of the Normans, William I (v.927-942). He thus makes him a part of the entourage close to the son of Rollo.[1] A later source, le Roman d
2 CONC e Rou, explains that Anslech supported Guillaume when Rioulf began an important rebellion against him.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT We see the same figure again in the writings of William of Jumièges. After the assassination of William I, Anslech, Raoul (called Taisson l'Ancien) and Bernard the Dane formed what William of Jumièges called "guardians of the whol
2 CONC e duchy of Normandy",[3] awaiting the majority of the new duke Richard. In 943, they welcomed the king of the Franks, Louis IV to Rouen, who came as overlord to receive the homage of the inhabitants of Rouen.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Rise and fall===
2 CONT
2 CONT The sagas from Norway and the islands make Anslech a Norman noble, Danish or Norwegian in origin but always from the old Viking nobility. '''Son of Rollo Turstain Brico (or Hrolf Turstan)''', a supposed nephew of Rollo, in these h
2 CONC e was the grandson of a certain Rollanger Rognvaldsson, a half-brother of Rollo, and the great-grandson of Rognvald Eysteinsson.[4] This genealogy is noted proven.
2 CONT
2 CONT All the same, his descendents continue to be discussed. A tradition - held since the 17th century, though with no evidence - considers him as the ancestor of the families of Montfort and Bertran via his son Tursten of Bastembourg.
2 CONC [5] Finally, he is traditionally presented as the founder of the castle of Bricquebec in Cotentin (perhaps at the beginning of the 10th century), from which comes his nickname Anslech of Bricquebec, though this too is an assumptio
2 CONC n.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Notes and references===
2 CONT
2 CONT # ^ Dudo of Saint-Quentin, De moribus et actis primorum Normanniae ducum, Ed. Jules Lair, Caen, F. Le Blanc-Hardel, 1865, p.220
2 CONT # ^ Wace and Benoît de Saint-Maure, Roman de Rou, ed. Le Prévost et Langlois, 1827, p.109
2 CONT # ^ William of Jumièges, History of the Normans, ed. Guizot, Brière, 1826, Livre IV, p.79 (French translation of Gesta Normannorum ducum written c.1172)
2 CONT # ^ (French) Léchaudé d'Anisy, Recherches sur le Domesday, Le Saulnier, tome 1, 1842, p.244-249
2 CONT # ^ (French) Christophe Maneuvrier, Paysages et sociétés rurales au Moyen Âge. Le Pays d’Auge jusqu’à la fin du XIIIe siècle, doctoral thesis, University of Caen, 2000, vol. 1, (dactyl.), p.98. L'asendance douteuse d'Anslech est n
2 CONC otamment reprise de Charles de Gerville, « Mémoires sur les anciens châteaux du département de la Manche », Mémoires de la Société des Antiquaires de Normandie, tome 1, 1825, p.247
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Sources===
2 CONT
2 CONT * Dudo of Saint-Quentin, De moribus et actis primorum Normanniae ducum, Ed. Jules Lair, Caen, F. Le Blanc-Hardel, 1865
2 CONT * William of Jumièges, History of the Normans, ed. Guizot, Brière, 1826, Livre IV, p. 79 (French translation of Gesta Normannorum ducum written c.1172)
2 CONT
