1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bernard_the_Dane
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Bernard the Dane''' (French - Bernard le Danois; c.880 - before 960), was a Viking jarl (earl) of Danish origins. He put himself in the service of another jarl, Rollo, to conquer and colonize what would become Normandy. After t
2 CONC he accords of Saint-Clair-sur-Epte that officially gave birth to the duchy of Normandy (911), Bernard converted to Christianity at Rouen the following year (912) and shortly afterwards received from Rollo the county of Pont-Audeme
2 CONC r  in Roumois (today in the Eure département) then, later, the city of Harcourt.
2 CONT
2 CONT Under Rollo's son and successor Duke William the Longsword, Bernard was charged at the beginning of the 930s with putting down the serious uprising led by a certain Riouf (a Norman from the west, who had besieged the Duke in Rouen
2 CONC ), then in around 935 he put down a revolt in the Bessin and Cotentin by Viking communities completely independent from the young and fragile power of the dukedom, unlike the east of the duchy of Normandy where its ducal power was
2 CONC  affirmed a little later.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bernard's powerful political role truly emerged after Duke William was assassinated in December 942. Then, he became part of a triumvirate which directed the future of the Norman duchy during the minority of Richard. Bernard becam
2 CONC e regent of the duchy of Normandy in December 942, beside Anslech de Bricquebec, Osmond de Conteville and Raoul Taisson.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 945-946, he appealed to Harald Bluetooth and his Danes to defend the duchy when it was attacked by the Carolingian king Louis of Outremer and Hugh the Great, duke of the Franks. Louis was attempting to retake the lands of the w
2 CONC est in Normandy that had been granted to the Viking bands thirty years earlier.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bernard died a few years late (before 960). He was the ancestor of two great Anglo-Norman baronial families, the Beaumonts and the Harcourts.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT When in 911 the Viking chief Rollo obtained the territories that would make up Normandy through the Treaty of Saint Clair-sur-Epte, he distributed domains to his main supporters among those who had accompanied him on his expeditio
2 CONC ns against the English and the Neustrians. After the conquest of Normandy, considerable lands (notably the seigneurie of Harcourt, near Brionne, were granted to Bernard the Dane as a reward for his exploits, and from him they desc
2 CONC ended upon the lords (seigneurs) of Harcourt.
2 CONT
2 CONT Named after its seigneurie of Harcourt in Normandy, the House of Harcourt was a powerful Norman family, descended from the Viking Bernard the Dane. Its mottos were "Gesta verbis praeveniant" (Olonde branch), "Gesta verbis praeveni
2 CONC ent" (Beuvron branch), and "Le bon temps viendra ... de France" (English branch).
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1280 they established the Collège d'Harcourt in Paris, now the Lycée Saint-Louis at 44 boulevard Saint-Michel.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bernard "The Dane" Prince of Denmark (904-950) [Pedigree]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Son of Duke of the Angrian Saxons BRUNO and Princess of the Holstein Saxons HASALA
2 CONT
2 CONT AKA Bernard "le Danois"
2 CONT
2 CONT Founding father of the House of Harcourt
2 CONT
2 CONT What follows in the Harcourt line comes mainly from the book called
2 CONT
2 CONT " A History of the Family Harcourt " written by William Harcourt-Bath in1930. I have a copy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Chief counsellor to Rollo in the conquest of Normandy, France. Rollo's wife and Bernard's wife are sisters.
2 CONT
2 CONT His descendents followed William the Conqueror into England.
2 CONT
2 CONT The family is a direct descendent of Charlemagne and the Trojan Senator Antenor
2 CONT
2 CONT The name seems to come from "Herr" ( A Teuteonic courtesy title), meaning lord or gentleman and court meaning hall or mansion. I.E."HerrCourt" .
2 CONT
2 CONT The name has evolved over the years, Herecourt , Harecourt , Harcourt.
2 CONT
2 CONT b. ABT 904, Normandy, France
2 CONT
2 CONT b. ABT. 860, Saxony or Maer,Norway
2 CONT
2 CONT d. ABT. 950, Normandy,France
2 CONT
2 CONT d. 955
2 CONT
2 CONT Married Sprota (Sprote) de Berengaria or BOUGOGNE (880-936)
2 CONT
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. TORF I,Seigneur de Tourville (901-970)
2 CONT
2 CONT References: [RFC]
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Bernard the Saxon1
2 CONT
2 CONT b. 904
2 CONT
2 CONT      Bernard the Saxon was a nobleman of the royal blood of Saxony.1 He acquired, when Rollo the Dane made himself master of Normandy, the lordships of Harcourt, Caileville, and Beaufidel in that principality in 876.1 He was born 
2 CONC in 904.2 He married Sprote de Bourgogne.2
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT 	Sprote de Bourgogne b. 908
2 CONT
2 CONT Child	
2 CONT
2 CONT     * seigneur de Pont-Audemer Torf de Torville+ b. 9281
2 CONT
2 CONT Citations
2 CONT
2 CONT    1. [S603] C.B., LL.D., Ulster King of Arms Sir Bernard Burke, B:xP, pg. 261.
2 CONT
2 CONT    2. [S467] GdRdF, online http://jeanjacques.villemag.free.fr/, Comte d'Harcourt.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT FMG does not cite primary sources for the parents of Torf.  Others (see below) claim that he is the son of Bernard the Dane.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://stanhopefamilyhistory.webs.com/
2 CONT
2 CONT Bernard the Dane - claimed to be the ancestor of the Harcourts by Gilles-André de La Roque [Histoire généalogique de la maison de Harcourt, 1663]. Little is known of him: His early career in the 930s was concerned with supressing 
2 CONC revolts by factions opposed to the ruling hierarchy in the emerging state of Normandy. After the assassination of William Longsword, Bernard became a regent of the 'duchy' of Normandy in December 942, charged with the protection o
2 CONC f the the young 'Duke' Richard, together with, among others, [1.1.1.2.2.1.] Anslech de Briquebec.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==========================================
