2 CONT Sviatoslav I Igorevich was a warrior prince of Kievan Rus'. The son of Igor of Kiev and Olga, Sviatoslav is famous for his incessant campaigns in the east and south, which precipitated the collapse of two great powers of Eastern E
2 CONC urope—Khazaria and the First Bulgarian Empire; he also subdued the Volga Bulgars, the Alans, and numerous East Slavic tribes, and at times was allied with the Pechenegs and Magyars. His decade-long reign over Rus' was marked by ra
2 CONC pid expansion into the Volga River valley, the Pontic steppe and the Balkans. By the end of his short life, Sviatoslav carved out for himself the largest state in Europe, eventually moving his capital from Kiev to Pereyaslavets on
2 CONC  the Danube in 969. In contrast with his mother's conversion to Christianity, Sviatoslav remained a staunch pagan all of his life. Due to his abrupt death in combat, Sviatoslav's conquests, for the most part, were not consolidated
2 CONC  into a functioning empire, while his failure to establish a stable succession led to civil war among his successors.
2 CONT
2 CONT == Personality ==
2 CONT
2 CONT Sviatoslav was the first true ruler of Kievan Rus' whose name is indisputably Slavic in origin (as opposed to his predecessors, whose names are ultimately derived from Old Norse). This name is not recorded in other medieval Slavic
2 CONC  countries. Even in Rus', it was attested only among the members of the house of Rurik, as were the names of Sviatoslav's immediate successors: Vladimir, Yaroslav, Mstislav). Some scholars speculate that the name of Sviatoslav, co
2 CONC mposed of the Slavic roots for "holy" and "glory", was an artificial derivation combining those of his predecessors Oleg and Rurik (they mean "holy" and "glorious" in Old Norse, respectively).
2 CONT Virtually nothing is known about his childhood and youth, which he spent reigning in Novgorod. Sviatoslav's father, Igor, was killed by the Drevlians around 945 and his mother, Olga, ruled as regent in Kiev until Sviatoslav's matu
2 CONC rity (ca. 963). His tutor was a Varangian named Asmud. "Quick as a leopard," Sviatoslav appears to have had little patience for administration. His life was spent with his druzhina (roughly, "troops") in permanent warfare against 
2 CONC neighboring states. According to the Primary Chronicle:
2 CONT
2 CONT “Upon his expeditions he carried with him neither wagons nor kettles, and boiled no meat, but cut off small strips of horseflesh, game or beef, and ate it after roasting it on the coals. Nor did he have a tent, but he spread out a
2 CONC  horse-blanket under him, and set his saddle under his head, and all his retinue did likewise.	” Sviatoslav was noted by Leo the Deacon to be of average height and build. He shaved his head and his beard (or possibly just had a wi
2 CONC spy beard) but wore a bushy mustache and a one or two sidelocks as a sign of his nobility. He preferred to dress in white, and it was noted that his garments were much cleaner than those of his men. He wore a single large gold ear
2 CONC ring bearing a ruby and two pearls. His mother converted to Christianity at the court of Byzantine Emperor Constantine Porphyrogenitus in 945 or 957. However, Sviatoslav continued to worship Perun, Veles, Svarog and the other gods
2 CONC  and goddesses of the Slavic pantheon. He remained a pagan for all of his life; according to the Primary Chronicle, he believed that his warriors would lose respect for him and mock him if he became a Christian. The allegiance of 
2 CONC his warriors was of paramount importance in his conquest of an empire that stretched from the Volga to the Danube.
2 CONT
2 CONT == Family ==
2 CONT
2 CONT Very little is known of Sviatoslav's family life. It is possible that Sviatoslav was not the only (and the eldest) son of his parents. The Russo-Byzantine treaty of 945 mentions a certain Predslava, Volodislav's wife, as the noble
2 CONC st of the Rus' women after Olga. George Vernadsky was among many historians to speculate that Volodislav was Igor's eldest son and heir who died at some point during Olga's regency. At the time of Igor's death, Sviatoslav was stil
2 CONC l a child and he was raised by his mother or at her instructions. Her influence, however, did not extend to his religious observance.
2 CONT Sviatoslav, had several children, but the origin of his wives is not specified in the chronicle. By his wives, he had Yaropolk and Oleg. By Malusha, a woman of indeterminate origins, Sviatoslav had Vladimir, who would ultimately b
2 CONC reak with his father's paganism and convert Rus to Christianity. John Skylitzes reported that Vladimir had a brother named Sfengus; whether this Sfengus was a son of Sviatoslav, a son of Malusha by a prior or subsequent husband, o
2 CONC r an unrelated Rus' nobleman is unclear. When Sviatoslav went on campaign he left his various relations as regents in the main cities of his realm: his mother Olga and later Yaropolk in Kiev, Vladimir in Novgorod, and Oleg over th
2 CONC e Drevlians.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Cousins ===
2 CONT
2 CONT The following people are mentioned as the Igor's nephews:
2 CONT Sludy Prasten Akun
2 CONT
2 CONT === Sons ===
2 CONT
2 CONT Oleg
2 CONT Yaropolk Vladimir, a son of Malusha (supposedly slavanized version of Malfried)
2 CONT
2 CONT == Eastern campaigns ==
2 CONT
2 CONT Shortly after his accession to the throne, Sviatoslav began campaigning to expand the Rus' control over the Volga valley and the Pontic steppe region. His greatest success was the conquest of Khazaria, which for centuries had been
2 CONC  one of the strongest states of Eastern Europe. The sources are not clear about the roots of the conflict between Khazaria and Rus', so several possibilities have been suggested. The Rus' had an interest in removing the Khazar hol
2 CONC d on the Volga trade route because the Khazars collected duties from the goods transported by the Volga. Historians have suggested that the Byzantine Empire may have incited the Rus' against the Khazars, who fell out with the Byza
2 CONC ntines after the persecutions of the Jews in the reign of Romanus I Lecapenus.
2 CONT Sviatoslav began by rallying the Khazars' East Slavic vassal tribes to his cause. Those who would not join him, such as the Vyatichs, were attacked and forced to pay tribute to the Kievan Rus' rather than the Khazars. According to
2 CONC  a legend recorded in the Primary Chronicle, Sviatoslav sent a message to the Vyatich rulers, consisting of a single phrase: "I want to come at you!" (Old East Slavic: "хощю на вы ити") This phrase is used in modern Russian (usual
2 CONC ly misquoted as "Иду на вы") to denote an unequivocal declaration of one's intentions. Proceeding by the Oka and Volga rivers, he invaded Volga Bulgaria and exacted tribute from the local population, thus bringing under Kievan con
2 CONC trol the upper Volga River. He employed Oghuz and Pecheneg mercenaries in this campaign, perhaps to counter the Khazars' and Bulgars' superior cavalry. Sviatoslav destroyed the Khazar city of Sarkel around 965, and possibly sacked
2 CONC  (but did not occupy) the Khazar city of Kerch on the Crimea. At Sarkel he established a Rus' settlement called Belaya Vyezha ("the white tower" or "the white fortress", the East Slavic translation for "Sarkel"). He subsequently (
2 CONC probably in 968 or 969) destroyed the Khazar capital of Atil. A visitor to Atil wrote soon after Sviatoslav's campaign: "The Rus attacked, and no grape or raisin remained, not a leaf on a branch." The exact chronology of his Khaza
2 CONC r campaign is uncertain and disputed; for example, Mikhail Artamonov and David Christian proposed that the sack of Sarkel came after the destruction of Atil. Although Ibn Haukal reports Sviatoslav's sack of Samandar in modern-day 
2 CONC Dagestan, the Rus' leader did not bother to occupy the Khazar heartlands north of the Caucasus Mountains permanently. On his way back to Kiev, Sviatoslav chose to strike against the Ossetians and force them into subservience. Ther
2 CONC efore, Khazar successor statelets continued their precarious existence in the region. The destruction of Khazar imperial power paved the way for Kievan Rus' to dominate north-south trade routes through the steppe and across the Bl
2 CONC ack Sea, routes that formerly had been a major source of revenue for the Khazars. Moreover, Sviatoslav's campaigns led to increased Slavic settlement in the region of the Saltovo-Mayaki culture, greatly changing the demographics a
2 CONC nd culture of the transitional area between the forest and the steppe.
2 CONT
2 CONT == Campaigns in the Balkans ==
2 CONT
2 CONT The annihilation of Khazaria was undertaken against the background of the Rus'-Byzantine alliance, concluded in the wake of Igor's Byzantine campaign in 944. Close military ties between the Rus' and Byzantium are illustrated by th
2 CONC e fact, reported by John Skylitzes, that a Rus' detachment accompanied Byzantine Emperor Nikephoros Phokas in his victorious naval expedition to Crete.
2 CONT In 967 or 968 Nikephoros sent to Sviatoslav his agent, Kalokyros, with the task of talking Sviatoslav into assisting him in a war against Bulgaria. Sviatoslav was paid 15,000 pounds of gold and set sail with an army of 60,000 men,
2 CONC  including thousands of Pecheneg mercenaries. Sviatoslav defeated the Bulgarian ruler Boris II and proceeded to occupy the whole of northern Bulgaria. Meanwhile, the Byzantines bribed the Pechenegs to attack and besiege Kiev, wher
2 CONC e Olga stayed with Sviatoslav's son Vladimir. The siege was relieved by the druzhina of Pretich, and immediately following the Pecheneg retreat, Olga sent a reproachful letter to Sviatoslav. He promptly returned and defeated the P
2 CONC echenegs, who continued to threaten Kiev. Sviatoslav refused to turn his Balkan conquests over to the Byzantines, and the parties fell out as a result. To the chagrin of his boyars and mother (who died within three days after lear
2 CONC ning about his decision), Sviatoslav decided to move his capital to Pereyaslavets in the mouth of the Danube due to the great potential of that location as a commercial hub. In the Primary Chronicle record for 969, Sviatoslav expl
2 CONC ains that it is to Pereyaslavets, the centre of his lands, "all the riches flow: gold, silks, wine, and various fruits from Greece, silver and horses from Hungary and Bohemia, and from Rus furs, wax, honey, and slaves". In summer 
2 CONC 969, Sviatoslav left Rus' again, dividing his dominion into three parts, each under a nominal rule of one of his sons. At the head of an army that included Pecheneg and Magyar auxiliary troops, he invaded Bulgaria again, devastati
2 CONC ng Thrace, capturing the city of Philippopolis, and massacring its inhabitants. Nikephoros responded by repairing the defenses of Constantinople and raising new squadrons of armored cavalry. In the midst of his preparations, Nikep
2 CONC horos was overthrown and killed by John Tzimiskes, who thus became the new Byzantine emperor. John Tzimiskes first attempted to persuade Sviatoslav into leaving Bulgaria, but was unsuccessful. Challenging the Byzantine authority, 
2 CONC Sviatoslav crossed the Danube and laid siege to Adrianople, causing panic on the streets of Constantinople in summer 970. Later that year, the Byzantines launched a counteroffensive. Being occupied with suppressing a revolt of Bar
2 CONC das Phokas in Asia Minor, John Tzimiskes sent his commander-in-chief, Bardas Skleros, who defeated the coalition of Rus', Pechenegs, Magyars, and Bulgarians in the Battle of Arcadiopolis. Meanwhile, John, having quelled the revolt
2 CONC  of Bardas Phokas, came to the Balkans with a large army and promoting himself as the liberator of Bulgaria from Sviatoslav, penetrated the impracticable mountain passes and shortly thereafter captured Marcianopolis, where the Rus
2 CONC  were holding a number of Bulgar princes hostage. Sviatoslav retreated to Dorostolon, which the Byzantine armies besieged for sixty-five days. Cut off and surrounded, Sviatoslav came to terms with John and agreed to abandon the Ba
2 CONC lkans, renounce his claims to the southern Crimea and return west of the Dnieper River. In return, the Byzantine emperor supplied the Rus' with food and safe passage home. Sviatoslav and his men set sail and landed on Berezan Isla
2 CONC nd at the mouth of the Dnieper, where they made camp for the winter. Several months later, their camp was devastated by famine, so that even a horse's head could not be bought for less than a half-grivna, reports the Kievan chroni
2 CONC cler of the Primary Chronicle. While Sviatoslav's campaign brought no tangible results for the Rus', it weakened the Bulgarian statehood and left it vulnerable to the attacks of Basil the Bulgar-Slayer four decades later.
2 CONT
2 CONT == Death and aftermath ==
2 CONT
2 CONT Fearing that the peace with Sviatoslav would not endure, the Byzantine emperor induced the Pecheneg khan Kurya to kill Sviatoslav before he reached Kiev. This was in line with the policy outlined by Constantine VII Porphyrogenitus
2 CONC  in De Administrando Imperio of fomenting strife between the Rus' and the Pechenegs. According to the Slavic chronicle, Sveneld attempted to warn Sviatoslav to avoid the Dnieper cataracts, but the prince slighted his wise advice a
2 CONC nd was ambushed and slain by the Pechenegs when he tried to cross the cataracts near Khortitsa early in 972. The Primary Chronicle reports that his skull was made into a chalice by the Pecheneg khan, Kurya.
2 CONT Following Sviatoslav's death, tensions between his sons grew. A war broke out between Sviatoslav's legitimate sons, Oleg and Yaropolk, in 976, at the conclusion of which Oleg was killed. In 977 Vladimir fled Novgorod to escape Ole
2 CONC g's fate and went to Scandinavia, where he raised an army of Varangians and returned in 980. Yaropolk was killed and Vladimir became the sole ruler of Kievan Rus'.
2 CONT
2 CONT == In art and literature ==
2 CONT
2 CONT Sviatoslav has long been a hero of Russian, Ukrainian, and Belarusian patriots due to his great military successes. His figure first attracted attention of Russian artists and poets during the Russo-Turkish War, 1768–1774, which p
2 CONC rovided obvious parallels with Sviatoslav's push towards Constaninople. Russia's southward expansion and Catherine II's imperialistic ventures in the Balkans seemed to have been legitimized by Sviatoslav's campaigns eight centurie
2 CONC s earlier.
2 CONT Among the works created during the war was Yakov Knyazhnin's tragedy Olga (1772). The Russian playwright chose to introduce Sviatoslav as his protagonist, although his active participation in the events following Igor's death is o
2 CONC ut of sync with the traditional chronology. Knyazhnin's rival Nikolai Nikolev (1758–1815) also wrote a play on the subject of Sviatoslav's life. Ivan Akimov's painting Sviatoslav's Return from the Danube to Kiev (1773) explores th
2 CONC e conflict between military honour and family attachment. It is a vivid example of Poussinesque rendering of early medieval subject matter. In the 19th century, interest in Sviatoslav's career waned. Klavdiy Lebedev depicted an ep
2 CONC isode of Sviatoslav's meeting with Emperor John in his well-known painting, while Eugene Lanceray sculpted an equestrian statue of Sviatoslav in the early 20th century. Sviatoslav appears in the Slavophile poems of Velimir Khlebni
2 CONC kov as an epitome of militant Slavdom:
2 CONT
2 CONT Знаменитый сок Дуная,	Pouring the famed juice of the Danube Наливая в глубь главы,	Into the depth of my head, Стану пить я, вспоминая	I shall drink and remember Светлых клич: "Иду на вы!".	The cry of the bright ones: "I come at yo
2 CONC u!"
2 CONT
2 CONT He is the villain of Samuel Gordon's novel The Lost Kingdom, or the Passing of the Khazars, a fictionalized account of the destruction of Khazaria by the Rus'. The Slavic warrior figures in a more positive context in the story "Ch
2 CONC ernye Strely Vyaticha" by Vadim Viktorovich Kargalov; the story is included in his book Istoricheskie povesti. In 2005, reports circulated that a village in the Belgorod region had erected a monument to Sviatoslav's victory over t
2 CONC he Khazars by the Russian sculptor Vyacheslav Klykov. The reports described the 13-meter tall statue as depicting a Rus' cavalryman trampling a supine Khazar bearing a Star of David. This created an outcry within the Jewish commun
2 CONC ity of Russia. The controversy was further exacerbated by Klykov's connections with Pamyat and other anti-Semitic organizations, as well as by his involvement in the "letter of 500", a controversial appeal to the Prosecutor Genera
2 CONC l to review all Jewish organizations in Russia for extremism. The Press Center of the Belgorod Regional Administration responded by stating that a planned monument to Sviatoslav had not yet been constructed, but would show "respec
2 CONC t towards representatives of all nationalities and religions." When the statue was unveiled, the shield bore a twelve-pointed star.
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes:
2 CONT
2 CONT The name of Svyatoslav's wives are not preserved. For those who listed Debrima and Elfira as names of the mother(s) of Oleg and Yaropolk, please provide the source (Esfir is named as a mistress by the FMG, not a wife). Svyatoslav'
2 CONC s relationship with "Predslava" has not been defined - she is either a wife (speculates FMG) or a daughter (speculates Russian Wikipedia) or an aunt (speculates English Wikipedia). If someone has discovered a primary source docume
2 CONC nt identifying her as Svyatoslav's wife, please provide it.
2 CONT
2 CONT Svyatoslav's birth location is not identified. Indeed, his birth year is approximated. The problem is the age of his mother, Olga, being too old to have gave birth in 942 (if she was born in 879 as the Primary Chronicle states). A
2 CONC nd if there is record of Svyatoslav being baptized, please provide it. Although Olga may have converted to Christianity in 945 (during the subjugation of the Drevlians?), Svyatoslav refused to convert for fear of losing the respec
2 CONC t of his soldiers.
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Russian Biographical Dictionary:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.rulex.ru/01180088.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Prins (knjaz) Svjatoslav I av Kiev (omkring 942 – 972) var en varjagisk eller östslavisk krigare som utvidgade Kievriket till det för den tiden största i Europa. Han flyttade rikets huvudstad till Perejaslavets i Bulgarien år 969.
2 CONC  Svjatoslav hade för avsikt att göra Bulgarien till ett centrum för hans imperium. Han tvingades att ge upp Balkanländerna 971 i kriget mot den byzantinske härskaren Johannes I Tzimiskes. Vid återresan från det misslyckade fälttåg
2 CONC et mot Bysans blev Svjatoslav år 972 dödad medelst armborstpil av petjeneger.
2 CONT
2 CONT Det finns inget nedtecknat om Svyatoslavs barn- och ungdomsperiod i Novgorod. Hans mor, S:t Olga, var härskarinna av Kiev fram till Svjatoslav blev myndig, omkring 963. Sviatoslav var en notorisk och hårdnackad hedning som förneka
2 CONC de kristendomen, till skillnad från hans mor som döptes omkring 957.
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From the English Wikipedia page on Sviatoslav I of Kiev:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sviatoslav_I_of_Kiev
2 CONT
2 CONT Sviatoslav I of Kiev (Old East Slavic: С~тославъ (Свąтославъ)[1] Игорєвичь (Sventoslavŭ Igorevichǐ), Russian: Святослав Игоревич, Ukrainian: Святослав Ігорович, Bulgarian: Светослав, Greek: Σφενδοσθλάβος (Sfendoslavos) ) (c. 942 –
2 CONC  March 972) was a warrior prince of Kievan Rus'.
2 CONT
2 CONT The son of Igor of Kiev and Olga, Sviatoslav is famous for his incessant campaigns in the east and south, which precipitated the collapse of two great powers of Eastern Europe—Khazaria and the First Bulgarian Empire; he also subdu
2 CONC ed the Volga Bulgars, the Alans, and numerous East Slavic tribes, and at times was allied with the Pechenegs and Magyars.
2 CONT
2 CONT His decade-long reign over Rus' was marked by rapid expansion into the Volga River valley, the Pontic steppe and the Balkans. By the end of his short life, Sviatoslav carved out for himself the largest state in Europe, eventually 
2 CONC moving his capital from Kiev to Pereyaslavets on the Danube in 969.
2 CONT
2 CONT In contrast with his mother's conversion to Christianity, Sviatoslav remained a staunch pagan all of his life. Due to his abrupt death in combat, Sviatoslav's conquests, for the most part, were not consolidated into a functioning 
2 CONC empire, while his failure to establish a stable succession led to civil war among his successors.
2 CONT
2 CONT Personality
2 CONT
2 CONT The Kievan Rus' at the beginning of Sviatoslav's reign (in red), showing his sphere of influence to 972 (in orange)
2 CONT
2 CONT Sviatoslav was the first true ruler of Kievan Rus' whose name is indisputably Slavic in origin (as opposed to his predecessors, whose names are ultimately derived from Old Norse). This name is not recorded in other medieval Slavic
2 CONC  countries. Even in Rus', it was attested only among the members of the house of Rurik, as were the names of Sviatoslav's immediate successors: Vladimir, Yaroslav, Mstislav).[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Some scholars speculate that the name of Sviatoslav, composed of the Slavic roots for "holy" and "glory", was an artificial derivation combining those of his predecessors Oleg and Rurik (they mean "holy" and "glorious" in Old Nors
2 CONC e, respectively).[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Virtually nothing is known about his childhood and youth, which he spent reigning in Novgorod. Sviatoslav's father, Igor, was killed by the Drevlians around 942 and his mother, Olga, ruled as regent in Kiev until Sviatoslav's matu
2 CONC rity (ca. 963).[4] His tutor was a Varangian named Asmud.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Quick as a leopard,"[5] Sviatoslav appears to have had little patience for administration. His life was spent with his druzhina (roughly, "troops") in permanent warfare against neighboring states.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the Primary Chronicle: "Upon his expeditions he carried with him neither wagons nor kettles, and boiled no meat, but cut off small strips of horseflesh, game or beef, and ate it after roasting it on the coals. Nor did
2 CONC  he have a tent, but he spread out a horse-blanket under him, and set his saddle under his head, and all his retinue did likewise.[6]”
2 CONT
2 CONT Sviatoslav was noted by Leo the Deacon to be of average height and build. He shaved his head and his beard (or possibly just had a wispy beard) but wore a bushy mustache and a one or two sidelocks as a sign of his nobility. He pre
2 CONC ferred to dress in white, and it was noted that his garments were much cleaner than those of his men. He wore a single large gold earring bearing a ruby and two pearls.[7][8]
2 CONT
2 CONT His mother converted to Christianity at the court of Byzantine Emperor Constantine Porphyrogenitus in 945 or 957. However,[9] Sviatoslav continued to worship Perun, Veles, Svarog and the other gods and goddesses of the Slavic pant
2 CONC heon. He remained a pagan for all of his life; according to the Primary Chronicle, he believed that his warriors would lose respect for him and mock him if he became a Christian.[10] The allegiance of his warriors was of paramount
2 CONC  importance in his conquest of an empire that stretched from the Volga to the Danube.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Very little is known of Sviatoslav's family life. It is possible that Sviatoslav was not the only (nor the eldest) son of his parents. The Russo-Byzantine treaty of 945 mentions a certain Predslava, Volodislav's wife, as the noble
2 CONC st of the Rus' women after Olga. George Vernadsky was among many historians to speculate that Volodislav was Igor's eldest son and heir who died at some point during Olga's regency.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the time of Igor's death, Sviatoslav was still a child and he was raised by his mother or at her instructions. Her influence, however, did not extend to his religious observance.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sviatoslav, had several children, but the origin of his wives is not specified in the chronicle. By his wives, he had Yaropolk and Oleg.[11]
2 CONT
2 CONT By Malusha, a woman of indeterminate origins,[12] Sviatoslav had Vladimir, who would ultimately break with his father's paganism and convert Rus to Christianity. John Skylitzes reported that Vladimir had a brother named Sfengus; w
2 CONC hether this Sfengus was a son of Sviatoslav, a son of Malusha by a prior or subsequent husband, or an unrelated Rus' nobleman is unclear.[13]
2 CONT
2 CONT When Sviatoslav went on campaign he left his various relations as regents in the main cities of his realm: his mother Olga and later Yaropolk in Kiev, Vladimir in Novgorod, and Oleg over the Drevlians.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eastern campaigns
2 CONT
2 CONT Shortly after his accession to the throne, Sviatoslav began campaigning to expand the Rus control over the Volga valley and the Pontic steppe region. His greatest success was the conquest of Khazaria, which for centuries had been 
2 CONC one of the strongest states of Eastern Europe.
2 CONT
2 CONT The sources are not clear about the roots of the conflict between Khazaria and Rus', so several possibilities have been suggested. The Rus' had an interest in removing the Khazar hold on the Volga trade route because the Khazars c
2 CONC ollected duties from the goods transported by the Volga. Historians have suggested that the Byzantine Empire may have incited the Rus' against the Khazars, who fell out with the Byzantines after the persecutions of the Jews in the
2 CONC  reign of Romanus I Lecapenus.[14]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sviatoslav began by rallying the Khazars' East Slavic vassal tribes to his cause. Those who would not join him, such as the Vyatichs, were attacked and forced to pay tribute to the Kievan Rus' rather than the Khazars.[15]
2 CONT
2 CONT According to a legend recorded in the Primary Chronicle, Sviatoslav sent a message to the Vyatich rulers, consisting of a single phrase: "I want to come at you!" (Old East Slavic: "хощю на вы ити")[16] This phrase is used in moder
2 CONC n Russian (usually misquoted as "Иду на вы" or "I'm coming to you!") to denote an unequivocal declaration of one's intentions.
2 CONT
2 CONT Proceeding by the Oka and Volga rivers, he invaded Volga Bulgaria and exacted tribute from the local population, thus bringing under Kievan control the upper Volga River. He employed Oghuz and Pecheneg mercenaries in this campaign
2 CONC , perhaps to counter the Khazars' and Bulgars' superior cavalry.[17]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sviatoslav destroyed the Khazar city of Sarkel around 965, and possibly sacked (but did not occupy) the Khazar city of Kerch on the Crimea.[18] At Sarkel he established a Rus' settlement called Belaya Vyezha ("the white tower" or 
2 CONC "the white fortress", the East Slavic translation for "Sarkel").[19]
2 CONT
2 CONT He subsequently (probably in 968 or 969) destroyed the Khazar capital of Atil.[20] A visitor to Atil wrote soon after Sviatoslav's campaign: "The Rus attacked, and no grape or raisin remained, not a leaf on a branch."[21]
2 CONT
2 CONT The exact chronology of his Khazar campaign is uncertain and disputed; for example, Mikhail Artamonov and David Christian proposed that the sack of Sarkel came after the destruction of Atil.[22]
2 CONT
2 CONT Although Ibn Haukal reports Sviatoslav's sack of Samandar in modern-day Dagestan, the Rus' leader did not bother to occupy the Khazar heartlands north of the Caucasus Mountains permanently. On his way back to Kiev, Sviatoslav chos
2 CONC e to strike against the Ossetians and force them into subservience.[23] Therefore, Khazar successor statelets continued their precarious existence in the region.[24]
2 CONT
2 CONT The destruction of Khazar imperial power paved the way for Kievan Rus' to dominate north-south trade routes through the steppe and across the Black Sea, routes that formerly had been a major source of revenue for the Khazars. More
2 CONC over, Sviatoslav's campaigns led to increased Slavic settlement in the region of the Saltovo-Mayaki culture, greatly changing the demographics and culture of the transitional area between the forest and the steppe.[25]
2 CONT
2 CONT Campaigns in the Balkans
2 CONT
2 CONT The annihilation of Khazaria was undertaken against the background of the Rus'-Byzantine alliance, concluded in the wake of Igor's Byzantine campaign in 944.[26] Close military ties between the Rus' and Byzantium are illustrated b
2 CONC y the fact, reported by John Skylitzes, that a Rus' detachment accompanied Byzantine Emperor Nicephorus Phocas in his victorious naval expedition to Crete.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 967 or 968[27] Nicephorus sent to Sviatoslav his agent, Kalokyros, with the task of talking Sviatoslav into assisting him in a war against Bulgaria.[28] Sviatoslav was paid 15,000 pounds of gold and set sail with an army of 60,
2 CONC 000 men, including thousands of Pecheneg mercenaries.[29][30]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sviatoslav defeated the Bulgarian ruler Boris II [31] and proceeded to occupy the whole of northern Bulgaria. Meanwhile, the Byzantines bribed the Pechenegs to attack and besiege Kiev, where Olga stayed with Sviatoslav's son Vladi
2 CONC mir.
2 CONT
2 CONT The siege was relieved by the druzhina of Pretich, and immediately following the Pecheneg retreat, Olga sent a reproachful letter to Sviatoslav. He promptly returned and defeated the Pechenegs, who continued to threaten Kiev.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sviatoslav refused to turn his Balkan conquests over to the Byzantines, and the parties fell out as a result. To the chagrin of his boyars and mother (who died within three days after learning about his decision), Sviatoslav decid
2 CONC ed to move his capital to Pereyaslavets in the mouth of the Danube due to the great potential of that location as a commercial hub.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the Primary Chronicle record for 969, Sviatoslav explains that it is to Pereyaslavets, the centre of his lands, "all the riches flow: gold, silks, wine, and various fruits from Greece, silver and horses from Hungary and Bohemia
2 CONC , and from Rus furs, wax, honey, and slaves".
2 CONT
2 CONT In summer 969, Sviatoslav left Rus' again, dividing his dominion into three parts, each under a nominal rule of one of his sons. At the head of an army that included Pecheneg and Magyar auxiliary troops, he invaded Bulgaria again,
2 CONC  devastating Thrace, capturing the city of Philippopolis, and massacring its inhabitants.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nicephorus responded by fortifying the defenses of Constantinople and raising new squadrons of armored cavalry. In the midst of his preparations, Nicephorus was overthrown and killed by John Tzimiskes, who thus became the new Byza
2 CONC ntine emperor.[32]
2 CONT
2 CONT John Tzimiskes first attempted to persuade Sviatoslav into leaving Bulgaria, but was unsuccessful. Challenging the Byzantine authority, Sviatoslav crossed the Danube and laid siege to Adrianople, causing panic on the streets of Co
2 CONC nstantinople in summer 970.[33]
2 CONT
2 CONT Later that year, the Byzantines launched a counteroffensive. Being occupied with suppressing a revolt of Bardas Phocas in Asia Minor, John Tzimiskes sent his commander-in-chief, Bardas Sklerus, who defeated the coalition of Rus', 
2 CONC Pechenegs, Magyars, and Bulgarians in the Battle of Arcadiopolis.[34]
2 CONT
2 CONT John, after having quelled the revolt of Bardas Phocas, came to the Balkans with a large army, and promoting himself as the liberator of Bulgaria from Sviatoslav, penetrated the impracticable mountain passes and shortly thereafter
2 CONC  captured Marcianopolis, where the Rus were holding a number of Bulgar princes hostage.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sviatoslav retreated to Dorostol, which the Byzantine armies besieged for 65 days. Cut off and surrounded, Sviatoslav came to terms with John and agreed to abandon the Balkans, renounce his claims to the southern Crimea and return
2 CONC  west of the Dnieper River. In return, the Byzantine emperor supplied the Rus' with food and safe passage home.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sviatoslav and his men set sail and landed on Berezan Island at the mouth of the Dnieper, where they made camp for the winter. Several months later, their camp was devastated by famine, so that even a horse's head could not be bou
2 CONC ght for less than a half-grivna, reports the Kievan chronicler of the Primary Chronicle.[35]
2 CONT
2 CONT While Sviatoslav's campaign brought no tangible results for the Rus', it weakened the Bulgarian statehood and left it vulnerable to the attacks of Basil the Bulgar-Slayer four decades later.
2 CONT
2 CONT Death and aftermath
2 CONT
2 CONT Fearing that the peace with Sviatoslav would not endure, the Byzantine emperor induced the Pecheneg khan Kurya to kill Sviatoslav before he reached Kiev. This was in line with the policy outlined by Constantine VII Porphyrogenitus
2 CONC  in De Administrando Imperio of fomenting strife between the Rus' and the Pechenegs.[36]
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the Slavic chronicle, Sveneld attempted to warn Sviatoslav to avoid the Dnieper cataracts, but the prince slighted his wise advice and was ambushed and slain by the Pechenegs when he tried to cross the cataracts near 
2 CONC Khortitsa early in 972. The Primary Chronicle reports that his skull was made into a chalice by the Pecheneg khan, Kurya.[37]
2 CONT
2 CONT Following Sviatoslav's death, tensions between his sons grew. A war broke out between Sviatoslav's legitimate sons, Oleg and Yaropolk, in 976, at the conclusion of which Oleg was killed.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 977 Vladimir fled Novgorod to escape Oleg's fate and went to Scandinavia, where he raised an army of Varangians and returned in 980. Yaropolk was killed and Vladimir became the sole ruler of Kievan Rus'.
2 CONT
2 CONT In art and literature
2 CONT
2 CONT Sviatoslav has long been a hero of Russian, Ukrainian, and Belarusian patriots due to his great military successes. His figure first attracted attention of Russian artists and poets during the Russo-Turkish War, 1768–1774, which p
2 CONC rovided obvious parallels with Sviatoslav's push towards Constaninople. Russia's southward expansion and Catherine II's imperialistic ventures in the Balkans seemed to have been legitimized by Sviatoslav's campaigns eight centurie
2 CONC s earlier.
2 CONT
2 CONT Among the works created during the war was Yakov Knyazhnin's tragedy Olga (1772). The Russian playwright chose to introduce Sviatoslav as his protagonist, although his active participation in the events following Igor's death is o
2 CONC ut of sync with the traditional chronology. Knyazhnin's rival Nikolai Nikolev (1758–1815) also wrote a play on the subject of Sviatoslav's life.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ivan Akimov's painting Sviatoslav's Return from the Danube to Kiev (1773) explores the conflict between military honour and family attachment. It is a vivid example of Poussinesque rendering of early medieval subject matter.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the 19th century, interest in Sviatoslav's career waned. Klavdiy Lebedev depicted an episode of Sviatoslav's meeting with Emperor John in his well-known painting, while Eugene Lanceray sculpted an equestrian statue of Sviatosla
2 CONC v in the early 20th century.[38]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sviatoslav appears in the Slavophile poems of Velimir Khlebnikov as an epitome of militant Slavdom:
2 CONT
2 CONT Pouring the famed juice of the Danube
2 CONT
2 CONT Into the depth of my head,
2 CONT
2 CONT I shall drink and remember
2 CONT
2 CONT The cry of the bright ones: "I come at you!" [39]
2 CONT
2 CONT He is the villain of Samuel Gordon's novel The Lost Kingdom, or the Passing of the Khazars,[40] a fictionalized account of the destruction of Khazaria by the Rus'. The Slavic warrior figures in a more positive context in the story
2 CONC  "Chernye Strely Vyaticha" by Vadim Viktorovich Kargalov; the story is included in his book Istoricheskie povesti.[41]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 2005, reports circulated that a village in the Belgorod region had erected a monument to Sviatoslav's victory over the Khazars by the Russian sculptor Vyacheslav Klykov. The reports described the 13-meter tall statue as depicti
2 CONC ng a Rus' cavalryman trampling a supine Khazar bearing a Star of David.
2 CONT
2 CONT This created an outcry within the Jewish community of Russia. The controversy was further exacerbated by Klykov's connections with Pamyat and other anti-Semitic organizations, as well as by his involvement in the "letter of 500", 
2 CONC a controversial appeal to the Prosecutor General to review all Jewish organizations in Russia for extremism.[42]
2 CONT
2 CONT The Press Center of the Belgorod Regional Administration responded by stating that a planned monument to Sviatoslav had not yet been constructed, but would show "respect towards representatives of all nationalities and religions."
2 CONC [43] When the statue was unveiled, the shield bore a twelve-pointed star.
2 CONT
