2 CONT
2 CONT From the English Wikipedia page on Malusha:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malusha
2 CONT
2 CONT Malusha (Ukrainian and Russian: Малуша) historically is known as a housekeeper (kholopka) for Olga of Kiev and a concubine of Sviatoslav I of Kiev. According to Slavonic chronicles, she was the mother of Vladimir the Great and sis
2 CONC ter of Dobrynya. The Norse sagas describe Vladimir's mother as a prophetess who lived to the age of 100 and was brought from her cave to the palace to predict the future.
2 CONT
2 CONT Royal origin
2 CONT
2 CONT As the chronicles are silent on the subject of Malusha's pedigree, 19th-century Russian historians devised various theories to explain her parentage and name. An archaeologist Dmitry Prozorovsky believed that Malusha was the daugh
2 CONC ter of Mal, a Drevlyan leader.
2 CONT
2 CONT A prominent chronicle researcher and linguist Alexei Shakhmatov considered Malusha to be the daughter of Mstisha Sveneldovich, son of a Kievan voyevoda Sveneld. He believed that the name Malusha was a slavinized version of a Scand
2 CONC inavian name Malfried. Another Russian historian Dmitry Ilovaisky came to an opposite conclusion that the Slavic name Malusha was turned into a Scandinavian Malfried. Ukrainian historian Mykhailo Hrushevsky criticized both of thes
2 CONC e versions.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Primary Chronicle records that a certain Malfried died in 1000. This record follows that of Rogneda's death. Since Rogneda was Vladimir's wife, historians assume that Malfried was another close relative of the ruling prince, p
2 CONC referably his wife or mother.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Unknown source:
2 CONT
2 CONT Malusha (Russian: Малуша) was a housekeeper and concubine of Sviatoslav I of Kiev. According to Slavonic chronicles, she was the mother of Vladimir the Great and sister of Dobrynya. The Norse sagas describe Vladimir's mother as a 
2 CONC prophetess who lived to the age of 100 and was brought from her cave to the palace to predict the future.
2 CONT
2 CONT As the chronicles are silent on the subject of Malusha's pedigree, 19th-century Russian historians devised various theories to explain her parentage and name. An archaeologist Dmitry Prozorovsky believed that Malusha was the daugh
2 CONC ter of Mal, a Drevlyan leader. A prominent chronicle researcher and linguist Alexei Shakhmatov considered Malusha to be the daughter of Mstisha Sveneldovich, son of a Kievan voyevoda Sveneld. He believed that the name Malusha was 
2 CONC a slavinized version of a Scandinavian name Malfried. Another Russian historian Dmitry Ilovaisky came to an opposite conclusion that the Slavic name Malusha was turned into a Scandinavian Malfried. Historian Mykhailo Hrushevsky cr
2 CONC iticized both of these versions.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Primary Chronicle records that a certain Malfried died in 1000. This record follows that of Rogneda's death. Since Rogneda was Vladimir's wife, historians assume that Malfried was another close relative of the ruling prince, p
2 CONC referably his wife or mother.
2 CONT
