1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ='''Foulques II 'le Bon' Comte d'Anjou'''=
2 CONT ''Last updated 8 August 2025''
2 CONT
2 CONT Fulk II, Count of Anjou (abt 905-abt 960), was a son of Fulk the Red and his wife Roscilla de Loches, daughter of Warnerius, Seigneur de Villentrois. He succeeded his father in 942 as the second count of Anjou, also called the cou
2 CONC nt of Angers, and remained in power until 960 [sic: 958].
2 CONT
2 CONT He was often at war with the Bretons. He seems to have been a man of culture, a poet and an artist. By reputation a saintly man, he dressed as a cleric. Of Louis IV, king of France, who had mocked his piety, Foulques commented, 'a
2 CONC n illiterate king is a crowned ass'.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Angevins, Fulk II included, had become particularly adept at establishing marriage alliances that furthered their goals. His father, Fulk the Red, had arranged his marriage to a Carolingian, Gerberga, the daughter of Ratburnus
2 CONC  I Viscount of Vienne. Among other things this alliance opened the doors for their daughter Adelaide-Blanche to marry a future king of France and their son Guy to become Bishop of le Puy.
2 CONT
2 CONT After Gerberga died about 952, Fulk made another astute political marriage. He married the widow of Alan II, Duke of Brittany. Alan II had also been Count of Nantes, and the marriage probably gave Fulk control of Nantes. She was a
2 CONC lso the sister of Theobald I, Count of Blois. This allowed Fulk II to form an alliance with the House of Blois.
2 CONT
2 CONT Comte Foulques II & his first wife had children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Children Confirmed by Stewart Baldwin’s [https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000220527154851&graph_node_id=profile-2272418&mode=tagged ''Henry Project''] 
2 CONT
2 CONT # '''Geoffroi d'Anjou''', son of Foulques and Gerberge. Geoffroy I "Grisegonelle", d. 987, count of Anjou; m. (1) Adèle, living 6 March 974, daughter of Robert I, count of Troyes. m. (2) Adélaïde, living 999, widow of Lambert, cou
2 CONC nt of Chalon. In a charter of 970, count Gaufridus mentions his father Fulco and mother Gerberga [1] Geoffrey I, Count of Anjou, married Adelaide of Vermandois.[2] [3] GEOFFROY d'Anjou ([938/40]-21 Jul 987, bur Châteauneuf, église
2 CONC  Saint-Martin). [4]
2 CONT # '''Guy d'Anjou''', living 13 April 993, d. 8 February 994-6. He appears as abbot Wido, brother of count Geoffroy in a charter of 966. The Chronicle of Saint-Pierre du Puy mentions him as a brother of Geoffroy Grisegonelle and of
2 CONC  countess Adélaïde, wife of Étienne. [1] Guy of Anjou, Bishop of le Puy.[2] [3] GUY d'Anjou (-before 995).[4]
2 CONT # '''Adelais alias Blanche''' d. 1026; m. (1) Étienne de Brioude, fl. 936-957, d. prob. 970×5; m. (2) Raymond de Toulouse; m. (3) ca. 980, Louis V, d. 21 May 987, king of France, 986-7; m. (4) Guillaume I (or II), d. aft. 29 Augus
2 CONC t 993, marquis of Provence. The Saint-Aubin genealogies give Fulco Bonus as the father of Blanca, mother of Constantia, wife of king Robert.[1] Adelaide-Blanche of Anjou, married five times.[2] [3] ADELAIS [Blanche] d'Anjou ([940/
2 CONC 50]-[29 May 1026, bur Montmajour, near Arles]). [4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Medlands shows an additional son: (Henry Project is uncertain)
2 CONT #  DROGON d'Anjou (-998)
2 CONT
2 CONT The Henry Project shows “Probable additional daughter:”
2 CONT
2 CONT #Adèle;  m. Gautier I, count of Valois, Vexin, and Amiens.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wikipedia shows “Falsely Attributed Sons”
2 CONT
2 CONT # Bouchard de Vendôme, born Anjou, say 943. Bouchard "le Vieux", count of Paris, Corbeil, and Vendôme. Falsely attributed as a son of Foulques. Bouchard, Count of Vendome.
2 CONT # Humbert le Veneur. Falsely attributed as a son of Foulques. Humbert d'Anjou, mentioned 957.
2 CONT
2 CONT No issue by his second wife. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Fulk II died at Tours. Fulk's date of death 11 November 958 is given by Christian Settipani in his work ''La Noblesse du Midi Carolingien''.
2 CONT
2 CONT >"Foulques was still living in September 958, when he and Thibaut were present at an assembly of Breton leaders at Angers...but was deceased by September 960, when his son Geoffroy appears as count."
2 CONT
2 CONT Wikipedia shows him buried at the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basilica_of_Saint_Martin,_Tours Basilica of Saint Martin] in Tours, France.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT '''MEDIEVAL LANDS''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT FOULQUES d'Anjou, son of FOULQUES I "le Roux" Comte d'Anjou & his wife Roscille de "Loches" ([920]-11 Nov 958).   The Gesta Consulum Andegavorum names "Fulco Rufus alium [filium] …tertium iuniorem…alter Fulco cognominatus Bonus" w
2 CONC hen recording that he succeeded his father[61].  He succeeded his father in 942 as FOULQUES II "le Bon" Comte d'Anjou.  He made peace with the Normans.  
2 CONT
2 CONT m firstly (937) GERBERGE, daughter of --- (-before 952).  "Gaufridus…Andecavorum comes" names "patris mei Fulconis, matris quoque meæ Gerbergæ" in his charter dated 19 Jun 966[62].  Maurice Chaume suggested that she was Gerberge, 
2 CONC daughter of Geoffroy Vicomte d'Orléans [Comte de Gâtinais], based on onomastic reasons only to explain the use of the name Geoffroy in the family of the Comtes d'Anjou[63].  
2 CONT
2 CONT m secondly (after 952) as her second husband, --- de Blois, widow of ALAIN II “Barbetorte” Duke of Brittany, daughter of THIBAUT [I] "l'Ancien" Comte de Blois & his [second wife Richilde ---].  The Chronicle of Nantes records the 
2 CONC marriage of "Theobaldus comes Blesensis…sororem suam relictam Alani Barbætortæ ducis" and "Fulconi comiti Andegavensi"[64].  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Comte Foulques II & his first wife had four children:'''  
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.         '''GEOFFROY d'Anjou''' ([938/40]-21 Jul 987, bur Châteauneuf, église Saint-Martin).  The Gesta Consulum Andegavorum names "primogenitus Gofridus…Guido…episcopus Podii…tertius minor Drogo" as the three sons of "Fulco Piu
2 CONC s"[65].  He succeeded his father in 958 as GEOFFROY I "Grisgonelle" Comte d'Anjou.  
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.         '''GUY d'Anjou''' (-before 995).  The Gesta Consulum Andegavorum names "primogenitus Gofridus…Guido…episcopus Podii…tertius minor Drogo" as the three sons of "Fulco Pius"[66].  The Chronicle of Saint-Pierre du Puy refer
2 CONC s to the bishop who was "frater germanus comes Gaufridus cognomento Grisogonella", clarifying in a later passage that he was "dominus Guido sanctæ Vallavensis ecclesiæ…episcopus"[67].  "Gaufridus…Andecavorum comes", with the conse
2 CONC nt of "fratre meo Widone abate", established the right of the comtes d'Anjou to appoint abbots of Saint-Aubin d'Angers, by charter dated 19 Jun 966[68].  He was appointed Bishop of Le Puy in 975 by Lothaire King of the Franks.  
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.         '''ADELAIS''' [Blanche] d'Anjou ([940/50]-[29 May 1026, bur Montmajour, near Arles]).  Her parentage and first marriage are confirmed by the Chronicle of Saint-Pierre du Puy which names "comes Gaufridus cognomento Griso
2 CONC gonella…Pontius et Bertrandus eius nepotes…matre eorum Adalaide sorore ipsius"[69], the brothers Pons and Bertrand being confirmed in other sources as the sons of Etienne de Brioude, for example the charter dated 1000 under which 
2 CONC "duo germani fratres…Pontius, alter Bertrandus" donated property to Saint-Chaffre for the souls of "patris sui Stephani matrisque nomine Alaicis"[70]. ….   '''m firstly''' ([950/60]) as his second wife,'''ETIENNE de Brioude''', so
2 CONC n of BERTRAND --- & his wife Emilgarde [Emilde] --- (-before [970/75]).  '''m secondly''' ([970/75]) '''RAYMOND IV Comte de Toulouse''', son of RAYMOND III Comte de Toulouse & his wife Gundinildis --- ([945/55]-killed "Carazo" [97
2 CONC 2/79]).  '''m thirdly''' (Vieux-Brioude, Haute-Loire 982, divorced 984) '''LOUIS associate King of the Franks''', son of LOTHAIRE King of the Franks & Emma d'Arles [Italy] ([966/67]-Compiègne 21 May 987, bur Compiègne, église coll
2 CONC égiale de Saint-Corneille).  Crowned King of Aquitaine the day of his marriage in 982.  He succeeded his father in 986 as LOUIS V King of the Franks.  '''m fourthly''' ([984/86]) as his second wife, '''GUILLAUME [II] "le Libérateu
2 CONC r" Comte d'Arles Marquis de Provence''', son of BOSON [II] Comte d'Arles & his wife Constantia [de Vienne] ([955]-Avignon 993 after 29 Aug, bur Sarrians, église de Sainte-Croix).  ['''m fifthly''' (before 1016) as his second wife,
2 CONC  '''OTHON GUILLAUME Comte de Mâcon et de Nevers''' [Bourgogne-Comté], son of ADALBERTO associate-King of Italy & his wife Gerberge de Chalon ([960/62]-Dijon 21 Sep 1026).]   
2 CONT
2 CONT 4.         '''DROGON d'Anjou''' (-998).  The Gesta Consulum Andegavorum names "primogenitus Gofridus…Guido…episcopus Podii…tertius minor Drogo" as the three sons of "Fulco Pius", specifying that Drogo succeeded his brother as Bish
2 CONC op of Le Puy[90].     
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT ===In English:===
2 CONT
2 CONT from https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foulques_II_d'Anjou
2 CONT
2 CONT Foulque II of Anjou (The Good) was Comte d'Anjou in the 10th century, and Comte de Nantes and Duke of Brittany between 958 and 960. He was from the Ingelgerien family and a son of Foulque I Rufus and Roscille Loches.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Biography'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''His Life'''
2 CONT
2 CONT He was cited for the first time in a charter of his father in 929, along with his mother and brother Guy. He was signatory to a charter of his father in August 941, and he probably succeeded him shortly after.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was allied with the Robertians, in contrast to the Comte de Blois, but the death of Alain II Barbetorte, Duke of Brittany in 952 caused a shift of alliances.
2 CONT
2 CONT Foulque II married the widow of Alain Barbetorte and took control of the County of Nantes in the name of Barbetorte's son Drogo. Drogo died in 958 of unknown conditions. Foulque II of Anjou became Comte de Nantes at that time unti
2 CONC l 960, when Hoel I, born out of the second marriage of Alain Barbetorte, became Comte de Nantes.
2 CONT
2 CONT Foulque ceded Saumur to Comte de Blois Thibaut the Cheat. It is noted that his successors retook Saumur almost a century later. Guillaume III, Comte de Poitiers, took Meron.
2 CONT
2 CONT Despite certain misdeeds attributed to him, he leaves a memory of an educated prince, a poet and an artist. He was citied for the last time in September 958 at a meeting attended by the Comte de Blois, Comte de Anjou, and the Lord
2 CONC s of Brittany. At this meeting, held in the County of Veron, Foulque II became Comte de Nantes and Duc de Bretagne (Brittany). In September 960, his son Geoffroy is cited as being the Comte d'Anjou, so Foulque's death would be bet
2 CONC ween these two dates.
2 CONT
2 CONT Foulque the Good is quoted in the Chronicle of the Exploits of the Comte de Anjou, written between 1100 and 1140 by an Angevin monnk at the request of Foulque le Rechin. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Weddings and Children'''
2 CONT
2 CONT In 937, Foulque was married for the first time to a certain Gerberga (OUR ANCESTOR), who died before 952. There is no record of her origin. One hypothesis says that she was a daughter of Ratburn I, Vicomte de Vienne, and his wife 
2 CONC Gerberga. Ratburn was himself the son of Berrillo, Vicomte de Vienne and Ermengarde, daughter of Boson, King of Provence. This hypothesis is based on the appearance of surnames Gerberga and Ermengarde among the Ingelgeriens, and g
2 CONC ood relations between Anjou and Burgundy at the time. The argument is in itself not a proof, only a clue, as the appearance of the name Ermengarde can be otherwise explained. The only remaining argument in favor of this hypothesis
2 CONC  is the name of Gerberga, which is too common to be usable. Another hypothesis has the advantage of explaining the appearance of the name Geoffroy among the counts of Anjou and that would be that she is from the Comte Gatinais.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Foulque and Gerberga gave birth to:'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1. Geoffroy I Gresegonel (d. 987), Comte d'Anjou
2 CONT * 2. Guy d'Anjou (d. 984), Bishop of Le Puy-en-Velay 
2 CONT *3. Adelaid d'Anjou (b. 940s, d. 1026), nicknamed "The White", married several times: 
2 CONT **3a. Up to the 950s, Stephen Broude, Vicomte de Gevaudan
2 CONT **3b. Up to 975 with Raimond (d. 978) Comte de Toulouse 
2 CONT **3c. In 982 to Louis V (d. 987), King of France, but separated soon after.
2 CONT **3d. Around 984/986 to William I (d. 983) Comte de Provence.
2 CONT *4. Probably Adele, who married Gautier I, Comte Vexon, Valois, et Amiens. Her parentage to Foulque II was suggested by the names of Foulque, Geoffroy, and Guy among the sons of Walter and Adele.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Other children who were assigned to Foulque II in error:'''
2 CONT
2 CONT *1. Dreux or Drogo, who was Bishop of Le Puy after his brother, but the list of Bishops of Le Puy does not mention this name in the 10th century, and his presence in genealogies is probably because of a confusion with Drogo of Bri
2 CONC ttany, son of Roscille de Blois and Alain Barbetorte. He was a stepson of Foulque II when the latter married Roscille de Blois, widow of Alain Barbetorte. *2. Humbert the Huntsman, cited by Mabille in 1871, but whose existence is 
2 CONC not in any document.
2 CONT * 3. Bouchard the Venerable (d. 1007) Comte de Vendome, also cited by Mabille in 1871, but his parentage has been established since.
2 CONT
2 CONT Widowed, Foulque remarried in 954 with Roscille de Blois, widow of Alain II Barbetorte, Duke of Brittany and Comte de Nantes, and sister of Thibaut I the Cheater, Comte de Blois.
2 CONT
2 CONT As Comte d'Anjou, preceded by Foulque I the Red, and succeeded by Geoffroy I Grisegonel.
2 CONT
2 CONT As Duke of Brittany, preceded by Drogo de Bretagne and Hoel I de Bretagne.
2 CONT
2 CONT
