1 NOTE Source: THE RUFUS PARKS PEDIGREE by Brian J.L. Berry
2 CONT
2 CONT Page 55 chart:  William III, C. of Poitou, of Aquiataine (c.925-63).= Gerloc (Adèla) (d.c. 962)
2 CONT
2 CONT !Page 58:  11. notes of HUGH CAPET.   . . . William I and III . . .
2 CONT
2 CONT !Availability: The libraries of Ken, Karen, Kristen, Kevin, Brian, Amy, Adam and FAL
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: "Ancestral Roots of Sixty Colonists" by Frederick Lewis Weis.
2 CONT
2 CONT Page 125 line (144A-19):
2 CONT
2 CONT 19.   William I of Poitou by (2), b. ca. 925, d. 3 Apr. 963, Count of Poitou; m. 925, Gerloc (Adèle), d. ca. 14 Oct. 962, dau of Rollo, Duke of Normandy.  (Brandenburg p.66).
1 NOTE SOURCES
2 CONT Selvågætta II
1 NOTE Aquitaine, the région encompassing the départements of Dordogne, Gironde,Landes, Lot-et-Garonne, and Pyrénées-Atlantiques in southwestern France.The name Aquitaine is probably a form of Auscetani, which in turn is alengthened form
2 CONC  of Ausces and is thus cognate with the words Basque andWasconia (Gascony). The capital is Bordeaux.
2 CONT
2 CONT In Julius Caesar's description of Gaul, "Aquitania" was an area extendingfrom the Pyrenees to the Garonne River. The Roman emperor Augustus(reigned 27 BC-AD 14) made it a Roman administrative district, and itsborders were extended
2 CONC  as far north as the Loire River and east to theMassif Central.
2 CONT
2 CONT A Visigothic province in the 5th century, Aquitaine came under Frankishrule in the 6th century, retaining a measure of provincial identityexploited by local rulers. Long resistant in the 8th century, it wasfinally subdued by Charl
2 CONC emagne, who bestowed it (less Gascony) as akingdom upon his son Louis (the future emperor Louis I). It remained akingdom under Louis's son Pepin I and the latter's son Pepin II, itschief towns being Toulouse, Limoges, and Poitiers
2 CONC . Devastation by theNormans in the 9th century resulted in political and social upheavalsduring the course of which various feudal domains were established. Alittle before 845, the title Duke d'Aquitaine, was revived, and in 893Ki
2 CONC ng Charles III ordered that Count Rainulf II, who then held Aquitaine,should be poisoned, after which the King bestowed the duchy upon Williamthe Pious, Count of Auvergne, founder of the Abbey of Cluny. He wassucceeded by his neph
2 CONC ew, Count William II, in 918, and there followed along line of dukes.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the first half of the 10th century the counts of Auvergne, ofToulouse, and of Poitiers each claimed this ducal title, but it waseventually secured by William I, count of Poitiers (William IIId'Aquitaine). The powerful house of 
2 CONC the counts of Poitiers retainedAquitaine during the 10th and 11th centuries. William IV fought againstHugh Capet, King of France; William VI added Cascony; and William IXbecame famous as a crusader and troubadour. Then, on the dea
2 CONC th withoutheirs of the last duke, William X (William VIII of Poitiers), in 1137,his daughter Eleanor united Aquitaine to the kingdom of France by hermarriage with Louis VII. When Louis divorced her, however, Eleanord'Aquitaine mar
2 CONC ried in 1152 the count of Anjou, Henry Plantagenet, whotwo years later became king of England as Henry II. The duchy thus passedto her new husband, who, having suppressed a revolt there, gave it to hisson, Richard the Lion-Heart (
2 CONC later Richard I of England), who spent mostof his life in Aquitaine, often subduing rebellious vassals. When Richarddied in 1199, the duchy reverted to Eleanor, and, on her death five yearslater, it was united to the English crown
2 CONC  and henceforward followed thefortunes of the English possessions in France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Aquitaine, as it came to the English kings, stretched as of old from theLoire to the Pyrenees, but its extent was curtailed on the southeast bythe wide lands of the counts of Toulouse. The name Guyenne (or Guienne),a corruption d'
2 CONC Aquitaine, seems to have come into use about the 10thcentury, and the subsequent history d'Aquitaine is merged in that ofGascony and Guyenne, which were completely reunited to France by the endof the Hundred Years' War. [Encyclopa
2 CONC edia Britannica CD '97]
2 CONT ----------------
