2 CONT From the [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hugh_the_Great English Wikipedia page] on Hugh the Great (with references to additional material on the [http://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hugues_le_Grand_%28Robertien%29 French Wikipedia page] :
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Hugh the Great''' (898 – 16 June 956) was duke of the Franks and count of Paris, son of King Robert I of France (with Beatrice de Vermandois) and nephew of King Odo. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was born in Paris, Ile-de-France, France. His eldest son was Hugh Capet who became King of France in 987. His family is known as the Robertians.
2 CONT
2 CONT (French Wikipedia notes that he was the Count of Paris, Marquis of Neustria 923-956, Duke of the Franks 936-956, and Comte d'Auxerre 954-956, and was a forefather of the Capets. He was sometimes called Hugh the White because of hi
2 CONC s pale complexion - he was a powerful figure in the Kingdom of West Francia.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh's first wife was Eadhild, daughter of Edward the Elder, king of England, and sister of King Athelstan. At the death of his step-brother King Rudolph (Raoul), duke of Burgundy, in January 936, Hugh was in possession of nearly 
2 CONC all the region between the Loire and the Seine, corresponding to the ancient Neustria, with the exception of the territory ceded to the Normans in 911. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He took a very active part in bringing Louis IV (d'Outremer, who had followed his mother in exile to England) from the Kingdom of England in 936, but in the same year Hugh married Hedwige of Saxony (sister of d'Ogive, Louis' mothe
2 CONC r), a daughter of Henry the Fowler of Germany and Matilda of Ringelheim, and soon quarrelled with Louis. 
2 CONT
2 CONT (He had sought an advantage over Herbert II de Vermandois in his struggle for hegemony in West Francia. Louis IV was crowned on Sunday, June 19, 936, by Artaud, Archbishop of Rheims, in the abbey church of St-Vincent de Laon, his 
2 CONC hometown and Carolingian stronghold. A month later, July 25, 936, Louis IV gave Hugh the title of Dux Francorum, or Duke of the Franks - a title that was explained in a document from December 26 that same year in which Louis IV st
2 CONC ated that Hugh was "in all our regina the second after us," equal to a viceroy position or a Merovingian Mayor of the Palace.)
2 CONT
2 CONT (In July 937, he became a lay abbot of St-Germain d'Auxerre and champion of Autun, two major powers in the principality of Burgundy. He also increased his power by challenging the legitimacy of Hugh the Black to proclaim himself P
2 CONC rince of Burgundy, a position he thought he inherited from his brother, King Raoul. However, the young King sought to free himself of the overbearing Hugh, and took up arms to fight, joining forces with Herbert de Vermandois and W
2 CONC illiam Longsword, Duke of Normandy. In 940, Louis IV was defeated near Rheims.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh even paid homage to the Emperor Otto the Great, and supported him in his struggle against Louis. When Louis fell into the hands of the Normans in 945, he was handed over to Hugh, who released him in 946 only on condition that
2 CONC  he should surrender the fortress of Laon (after consultation with Thibaud de Blois, and under considerable pressure from the Emperor). 
2 CONT
2 CONT (In 946, he settled the estate of Herbert de Vermandois, dividing it among his children - Hugh's nephews - and weakening the power of that dynasty.At the Synod of Ingelheim in 948, the third in a series after Verdun and Mouzon, Lo
2 CONC uis IV succeeded at getting the Archbishop of Rheims to excommunicate Hugh the Great.)
2 CONT
2 CONT At the council of Ingelheim (948) Hugh was condemned, under pain of excommunication, to make reparation to Louis. It was not, however, until 950 that the powerful vassal became reconciled with his suzerain and restored Laon. But n
2 CONC ew difficulties arose, and peace was not finally concluded until 953.
2 CONT
2 CONT On the death of Louis IV, Hugh was one of the first to recognize Lothair as his successor, and, at the intervention of Queen Gerberga, was instrumental in having him crowned. In recognition of this service Hugh was invested by the
2 CONC  new king with the duchies of Burgundy (his suzerainty over which had already been nominally recognized by Louis IV) and Aquitaine. But his expedition in 955 to take possession of Aquitaine was unsuccessful. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In the same year, however, Giselbert, duke of Burgundy, acknowledged himself his vassal and betrothed his daughter to Hugh's son Otto. At Giselbert's death (8 April 956) Hugh became effective master of the duchy, but died soon aft
2 CONC erwards, on the 16 or 17 June 956, in Dourdan.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the Divine Comedy Dante meets the soul of Duke Hugh in Purgatory, lamenting the avarice of his descendants.
2 CONT
2 CONT French Wikipedia:
2 CONT
2 CONT From Raingarde, his concubine, he had Herbert or Heribert (d. 996), who became Bishop of Auxerre. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He married a daughter of Roger du Maine in 914, and then to Eadhilda or Edith of Wessex, daughter of Edward the Elder, in 926, and finally to Hedwig of Saxony (922-965) daughter of Henry I l'Oiseleur in 937, the latter of which ga
2 CONC ve him:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Beatrice, who married Frederic I, Comte de Bar and Duc de Haute-Lotharingie
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Hugues Capet, King of the Franks in 987 (OUR ANCESTOR)
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Emma, who married Richard I, Duc de Normandie
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Otton and Eudes-Henri, ducs of Bourgogne.
2 CONT
