2 CONT Hugo 'den store', kallades även Hugo 'den vite'. Fransk storman med titeln 'frankernas hertig', son till Robert I. Genom stora egendomar vid Seine och Loire dominerade Hugo landets politik från 940-talet till sin död. Ludvig IV st
2 CONC red länge om makten med Hugo och hans efterträdare Lothar tvingades ge Hugo kontrollen även över Burgund och Akvitanien.
2 CONT
2 CONT Källa: Bra Böcker.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT      Hugues of Neustria, Comte de Paris was born circa 895. He was the son of Robert I, Roi de France.1 He married, firstly, Eadhilda (?), daughter of Eadweard I, King of Wessex and Ælflæd (?), between 926 and 927.2 He married, th
2 CONC irdly, Hedwig von Sachsen, daughter of Heinrich I von Sachsen, Holy Roman Emperor and Mathilda von Ringelheim, before 14 September 938.1 He died on 9 June 956.
2 CONT
2 CONT      Hugues of Neustria, Comte de Paris also went by the nick-name of Hugues 'the Great'.1 He gained the title of Comte de Paris. He gained the title of Duc de France.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.thepeerage.com/p10519.htm#i105190
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT '''Hugo, o Grande''' (895 — 19 de Junho de 956) foi duque dos francos e conde de Paris. Era filho do segundo casamento do rei Roberto I da França, com Beatriz de Vermandois, e sobrinho do rei Odo. Seu filho mais velho, Hugo Capeto
2 CONC , tornar-se-ia rei da França em 987. Sua família é conhecida como os Robertinos.
2 CONT
2 CONT Em 923, quando seu pai morreu, Hugo recusou o trono da França, sendo seu cunhado, Raul, duque da Borgonha, eleito rei. Quando este faleceu, em 936, Hugo era dono de quase toda a região entre o Loire e o Sena, a área da antiga Nêus
2 CONC tria, com exceção do território cedido aos normandos, em 911. Recusando-se a suceder ao trono novamente, empenhou-se em trazer Luís IV de volta da Inglaterra. Todavia, ele e o novo rei logo vieram a se desentender, e Hugo chegou a
2 CONC té a prestas homenagem a Oto I, imperador desde 936, e o apoiou na contenda com Luís. Quando este caiu nas mãos dos normandos, em 945, ele foi entregue a Hugo, que o libertou, em 946, porém, sob a condição de que lhe fosse dada a 
2 CONC fortaleza de Laon. No conselho de Ingelheim, dois anos depois, Hugo foi condenado sob pena de excomunhão como reparação para Luís. Apenas em 950 que o poderoso vassalo reconciliou-se com seu suserano, devolvendo-lhe Laon. Mas nova
2 CONC s dificuldades surgiram, e as negociações de paz só se concluíram em 953.
2 CONT
2 CONT À morte de Luís, em 954, Hugo foi o primeiro a reconhecer Lotário como o sucessor, e, por intermédio da rainha Gergerga, foi instrumental para sua coroação. Em reconhecimento por seu serviço, Hugo foi investido pelo novo rei com o
2 CONC s ducados da Borgonha (sua soberania sobre a qual já fora nominalmente reconhecida por Luís IV) e da Aquitânia. Todavia, sua expedição para tomar posse da Aquitânia, em 955, fracassou. No mesmo ano, Gilberto,duque da Borgonha, rec
2 CONC onheceu a suserania de Hugo, e deu a filha em casamento ao Oto, filho de Hugo. À morte de Gilberto, em abril de 956, Hugo se tornou o mestre efetivo do ducado, mas ele próprio morreu logo depois, em Dourdan.
2 CONT
2 CONT Casou por 3 vezes, a primeira em 922 com Judite do Maine, filha de Roger do Maine, de quem não teve filhos.
2 CONT
2 CONT O segundo casamento foi em 926 com Edhilda de Inglaterra, filha do rei Eduardo, o Velho, de quem igualmente não teve filhos.
2 CONT
2 CONT O terceiro casamento aconteceu em 937 com Hedwige da Saxônia (filha de Henrique I da Germânia "o Passarinheiro" e sua segunda esposa Matilde de Ringelheim "Santa Matilde"), de quem teve 5 filhos:
2 CONT
2 CONT #     Beatriz da França (939 —?), casada com Frederico I, duque da Alta-Lorena.
2 CONT #     Hugo I, rei da França (895 — 16 de Junho de 956), mais conhecido como "Hugo Capeto", casou com Adelaide da Aquitânia.
2 CONT #     Ema de Paris (943 — 966), casou-se em 960 com Ricardo I, Duque da Normandia, de quem foi a primeira esposa. Não tiveram filhos.
2 CONT #     Otão de França, duque da Borgonha (945 —?), casou com Luitegarda de Borgonha.
2 CONT #     Odo-Henrique, Duque da Borgonha, "o Grande", duque da Baixa Borgonha (946 — 1002), casou primeira vez com Gerberga de Chalon (ou Mâcon), casou segunda vez com Gersenda da Gasconha e casou terceira vez com Matilde de Chalon, 
2 CONC Senhora de Donzy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Fora do casamento, foi pai de:
2 CONT
2 CONT #    Heriberto, bispo de Auxerre
2 CONT
2 CONT ''in: Wikipédia, a enciclopédia livre <http://pt.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hugo,_o_Grande>''
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Leo: Genealogisches Handbuch des Adels, Fürstliche Häuser , Reference: 1961.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Hugh the Great (898 – 16 June 956) was the Duke of the Franks and Count of Paris.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the son of King Robert I of France and Béatrice of Vermandois, daughter of Herbert I, Count of Vermandois.[1] He was born in Paris, Île-de-France, France. His eldest son was Hugh Capet who became King of France in 987.[2] H
2 CONC is family is known as the Robertians.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 922 the barons of western Francia, after revolting against the Carolingian king Charles the Simple (who fled his kingdom under their onslaught), elected Robert I, Hugh's father, as King of Western Francia.[4] At the death of Ro
2 CONC bert I, in battle at Soissons in 923, Hugh refused the crown and it went to his brother-in-law, Rudolph of France.[4] Charles, however, sought help in regaining his crown from his cousin Herbert II, Count of Vermandois, who instea
2 CONC d of helping the king imprisoned him.[4] Herbert then used his prisoner as an advantage in pressing his own ambitions, using the threat of releasing the king up until Charles' death in 929.[5] From then on Herbert II of Vermandois
2 CONC  struggled with king Rudolph and his vassal Hugh the Great.[4] Finally Rudolph and Herbert II came to an agreement in 935.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT At the death of Rudolph, King of Western Francia, in 936, Hugh was in possession of nearly all of the region between the Loire and the Seine, corresponding to the ancient Neustria, with the exceptions of Anjou and of the territory
2 CONC  ceded to the Normans in 911.[6] He took a very active part in bringing Louis IV (d'Outremer) from the Kingdom of England in 936.[7] In 937 Hugh married Hedwige of Saxony, a daughter of Henry the Fowler of Germany and Matilda of R
2 CONC ingelheim, and soon quarrelled with Louis.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 938 King Louis IV began attacking fortresses and lands formerly held by members of his family, some held by Herbert II of Vermandois.[9] In 939 king Louis attacked Hugh the Great and William I, Duke of Normandy, after which a t
2 CONC ruce was concluded lasting until June.[10] That same year Hugh, along with Herbert II of Vermandois, Arnulf I, Count of Flanders and Duke William Longsword paid homage to the Emperor Otto the Great, and supported him in his strugg
2 CONC le against Louis.[11] When Louis fell into the hands of the Normans in 945, he was handed over to Hugh in exchange for their young duke Richard.[12] Hugh released Louis IV in 946 on condition that he should surrender the fortress 
2 CONC of Laon.[13] In 948 at a church council at Ingelheim the bishops, all but two being from Germany, condemned and excommunicated Hugh in absentia, and returned Archbishop Artauld to his see at Reims.[14] Hugh's response was to attac
2 CONC k Soissons and Reims while the excommunication was repeated by a council at Trier.[14] Hugh finally relented and made peace with Louis IV, the church and his brother-in-law Otto the Great.[14]
2 CONT
2 CONT On the death of Louis IV, Hugh was one of the first to recognize Lothair as his successor, and, at the intervention of Queen Gerberga, was instrumental in having him crowned.[14] In recognition of this service Hugh was invested by
2 CONC  the new king with the duchies of Burgundy and Aquitaine.[15] In the same year, however, Giselbert, duke of Burgundy, acknowledged himself his vassal and betrothed his daughter to Hugh's son Otto-Henry.[15] On 16 June 956 Hugh the
2 CONC  Great died in Dourdan.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Family[edit]
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh married first, in 922, Judith, daughter of Roger Comte du Maine & his wife Rothilde.[1] She died childless in 925.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh's second wife was Eadhild, daughter of Edward the Elder, king of the Anglo-Saxons, and sister of King Æthelstan.[1] They married in 926 and she died in 938, childless.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh's third wife was Hedwig of Saxony, daughter of Henry the Fowler and Matilda of Ringelheim She and Hugh had: Beatrice married Frederick I, Duke of Upper Lorraine.[a][1] Hugh Capet.[16] Emma.(c. 943-aft. 968).[16] Otto, Duke of
2 CONC  Burgundy, a minor in 956.[15] Odo-Henry I, Duke of Burgundy (d. 1002).[15]
2 CONT
