1 NOTE From THE RUFUS PARKS PEDIGREE by Brian J.L. Berry,  chart pg 55.
2 CONT
2 CONT Page 57:
2 CONT
2 CONT 10.     Hugh Magnus (the Great), d. 16/17 June 956; Count of Paris, Duke of Franks and Burgandy, lay Abbot of St. Martin of Tours, of St. Germain d'Auxerre and of Marienval.  Formidable rivalries prevented him from claiming the th
2 CONC rone upon his father's death.  He played power politics against King Louis d'Outremer, a Carolingian whom he had helped put on the throne.  HUGH was excommunicated for rebellion and finally submitted to the king 950.   He mar. (3)
2 CONC  937 HEDWIG of Saxony, dau. of HENRY I the Fowler, Emperor of Germany.
1 NOTE Comte de Paris , marquis de Neustrie de 923 à 956, puis duc des Francs, comte d'Auxerre de 954 à sa mort
1 NOTE duque de Francia y conde de París,
1 NOTE He was Duke of France.
1 NOTE He was Duke of France and Count of Paris.
1 NOTE BIOGRAPHY
2 CONT Hugues was born about 900, the son of Robert I, king of France and nephew of King Eudes. His eldest son Hugues Capet would become king of France in 987. His family is known as the Robertians.
2 CONT
2 CONT About 922 Hugues married Judith de Bourges. After her death without progeny in 925, he married Eadhild/Eadhylde of Wessex, daughter of Edward 'the Elder', king of England, and half-sister of Athelstan, Edmund I and Edred, all king
2 CONC s of England. Eadhild died in 937 without progeny.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the death in 936 of Raoul, king of France and duke of Burgundy, Hugues was in possession of nearly all the region between the Loire and the Seine, corresponding to the ancient Neustria, with the exception of the territory ceded
2 CONC  to the Normans in 911. He took a very active part in bringing Louis IV d'Outremer from the kingdom of England in 936. In 938 Hugues married Hadevich of Saxony, a daughter of emperor-elect Heinrich I 'the Fowler' and Mathilde von 
2 CONC Ringelheim. Hugues and Hadevich had at least five children, of whom three, including Hugues Capet, would have progeny.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugues fell out with Louis IV soon after Louis' accession, and he even paid homage to Louis' brother-in-law Otto I, emperor from 936, and supported him in his struggle against Louis. When Louis fell into the hands of the Normans i
2 CONC n 945, he was handed over to Hugues, who released him in 946 only on condition that he should surrender the fortress of Laon. At the council of Ingelheim in 948, Hugues was condemned under pain of excommunication, to make reparati
2 CONC on to Louis. It was not, however, until 950 that the powerful vassal became reconciled with his sovereign and returned Laon to Louis. But new difficulties arose, and peace was not finally concluded until 953.
2 CONT
2 CONT On the death of Louis IV in 954, Hugues was one of the first to recognise his son Lothar as his successor, and, at the intervention of Queen Gerberga, was instrumental in having him crowned. In recognition of this service Hugues w
2 CONC as invested by the new king with the duchies of Burgundy (his sovereignty over which he had already been nominally recognised by Louis IV) and Aquitaine. His expedition in 955 to take possession of Aquitaine was unsuccessful, but 
2 CONC in the same year, Giselbert, duc de Bourgogne and comte de Châlon sur Saône, acknowledged himself as Hugues' vassal and betrothed his daughter to Hugues' son Otto. At Giselbert's death on 8 April 956, Hugues became effective maste
2 CONC r of the duchy, but he died soon afterwards, on the 16 June 956 at Dourdan.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the _Divine Comedy_ Dante meets the soul of Hugues in Purgatory, lamenting the avarice of his descendants.
1 NOTE He was Duke of France.
1 NOTE He was Duke of France and Count of Paris.
