1 NOTE {geni:about_me} This is Sprota de Senlis or de Bretagne: do not confuse with Sprote de Bourgogne, possible partner of Bernard 'the Dane'. This Sprota was partner of William Longsword, Count of Rouen and ruler of Normandy; later sh
2 CONC e married Asperling de Vaudreuil.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sprota''' or '''Sprote'''. Her parentage is conjectural. She is traditionally said to have been a daughter of [https://www.geni.com/people/H%C3%A9ribert-I-comte-de-Vermandois/6000000001441509684 Herbert, Count of Senlis & Verma
2 CONC ndois]. She was said by Flodoard to have been from Brittany, which might mean that she was of Celtic, Scandinavian or Frankish origin. Her name suggests the latter. Also sometimes called Adela.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sprota 'Adela' de Bretagne de Senlis  (c.911 - 945) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Concubine, wife, or partner to William 'Longsword' (Langaspjót, called Guillaume Longe-Épée in French) son of Rollo and 2nd ruler of Normandy, Count of Rouen. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother to Richard 'Sans-Peur' and Raoul d'Ivry.
2 CONT
2 CONT Remarried Asperling or Esperleng, several children (see below)
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/NORMAN%20NOBILITY.htm#_Toc243182165
2 CONT
2 CONT Sprota was married to William I of Normandy, ruler of Normandy. Her son Richard the first was still a boy when his father died in 942. Sprota was a Breton concubine captured in war and bound to William by a Danish marriage. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After William died, Sprota became the wife of Esperleng, a wealthy miller; Rodulf of Ivry was their son and Richard's half-brother.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sprota was NOT married to Nigel St. Saveur. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Do NOT confuse with Sprote de Bourgogne who was married to Bernard "the Dane" Harcourt. 
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT [Several people have entered Sprota to Geni as "Adela", but this is probably wrong. Please enter sources if any.]
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Sprota married Esperleng after the relationship with Guillaume/William:
2 CONT
2 CONT (from Medieval Lands)
2 CONT
2 CONT SPROTA, daughter of ---.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Guillaume de Jumièges records that Guillaume married "une très-noble jeune fille Sprota…selon l'usage des Danois"[62].  From Brittany.  
2 CONT
2 CONT It is possible that Sprota was Count Guillaume's concubine rather than wife, particularly as no reference has been found to a dissolution of any marriage before she married Esperleng.  
2 CONT
2 CONT She married Esperleng de Pîtres, by whom she had Rodulf [Raoul] Comte d'Ivry.
2 CONT
2 CONT ESPERLENG de Pîtres, son of --- .  
2 CONT
2 CONT m SPROTA, daughter of --- .  From Brittany.  Sprota was previously the concubine or wife of Guillaume I Comte [de Normandie].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Guillaume de Jumièges records the marriage of Sprota and "Asperleng" who owned the mills in the valley of la Risle[730].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Esperling & his wife had [four or more] children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT a)         RAOUL d'Ivry (-after 1011).  Guillaume de Jumièges names Raoul as uterine brother of Richard Comte [de Normandie], specifying that the latter consulted him about arrangements for the succession in Normandy when dying[73
2 CONC 1].  Comte de Bayeux.  
2 CONT
2 CONT m AUBREE, daughter of --- (-murdered <nowiki>----</nowiki>).  Guillaume de Jumièges records the marriage of Raoul and "Eranberge…née dans une certaine terre du pays de Caux que l'on appelle Caville ou Cacheville"[732].  She is nam
2 CONC ed as wife of Raoul by Orderic Vitalis, who says that she built the castle of Ivry, executed the architect Lanfred to prevent him from completing a similar construction elsewhere, attempted to expel her husband from the castle, an
2 CONC d was killed by him[733].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Comte Raoul & his wife had five children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT i)          HUGUES d'Ivry (-Oct 1049).  
2 CONT
2 CONT Guillaume de Jumièges names Hugues bishop of Bayeux as son of comte Raoul, when recording that the castle of Ivry was confiscated from him by Robert II Duke of Normandy[734].  Seigneur d'Ivry.  Bishop of Bayeux 1015.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugues had [two] illegitimate children by an unknown mistress or mistresses: 
2 CONT
2 CONT (a)        ROGER .  
2 CONT
2 CONT "Rogerius Hugonis episcopi filius" sold land in Blovilla and Novillula to Sainte-Trinité in an undated charter[735].  
2 CONT
2 CONT m ODA, daughter of ---.  "Odain uxore sua" is named in the undated charter of "Rogerius Hugonis episcopi filius"[736].  Roger & his wife had two children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT (1)        GUILLAUME .  
2 CONT
2 CONT "Willelmo et Hugone eorum filiis" are named in the undated charter of "Rogerius Hugonis episcopi filius"[737].  "Guillelmo filio Rogerii filii Hugonis episcopi" purchased land from "Rodulfus de Warenna" dated 1074[738]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT (2)        HUGUES .  
2 CONT
2 CONT "Willelmo et Hugone eorum filiis" are named in the undated charter of "Rogerius Hugonis episcopi filius"[739]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT (b)        [AUBREE .  
2 CONT
2 CONT Chibnall speculates that the grandmother of Ascelin Goël may have been the daughter of Hugues Bishop of Bayeux, which may have provided her grandson with a claim to Ivry by inheritance[740], assuming that her illegitimacy presente
2 CONC d no obstacle.  Her two marriages are shown in Europäische Stammtafeln[741], but the primary sources which confirm them have not yet been identified.  
2 CONT
2 CONT m firstly ROBERT d'Ivry, son of ---.  [1060].  
2 CONT
2 CONT m secondly ALBERT de Cravent .] 
2 CONT
2 CONT ii)         EMMA d'Ivry .  Guillaume de Jumièges records that one of the daughters of Raoul & his wife married Osbern de Crepon[742].  After her husband died, she became abbess of St Amand at Rouen[743].  
2 CONT
2 CONT m OSBERN de Crepon, son of HERFAST & his wife --- (-murdered [1040]). 
2 CONT
2 CONT iii)        daughter .  
2 CONT
2 CONT Guillaume de Jumièges records that the other (unnamed) daughter of Raoul & his wife married Richard de Belfage, naming their son Robert and recording that one of their several daughters married Hugues de Montfort[744].  
2 CONT
2 CONT m RICHARD de Beaufour, son of ---.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard & his wife had [four or more] children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT (a)       ROBERT .  
2 CONT
2 CONT Guillaume de Jumièges records that the other (unnamed) daughter of Raoul & his wife married Richard de Belfage, naming their son Robert and recording that one of their several daughters married Hugues de Montfort[745]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT (b)       daughter .  
2 CONT
2 CONT Guillaume de Jumièges records that the wife of "Hugues le second…[fils de] Hugues de Montfort dit le Barbu" was "la fille de Richard de Belfage"[746].  m as his first wife, HUGUES [II] de Montfort, son of HUGUES [I] de Montfort-su
2 CONC r-Risle & his wife --- (-1088 or after). 
2 CONT
2 CONT (c)       daughters .  
2 CONT
2 CONT Guillaume de Jumièges records that the other (unnamed) daughter of Raoul & his wife married Richard de Belfage, naming their son Robert and recording that one of their several daughters married Hugues de Montfort[747]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT iv)       RAOUL d'Ivry (-after [1020/30]).  
2 CONT
2 CONT "Hugo Baiocassine urbis episcopus et Rodulfi quondam comitis filius" donated property to Jumièges by charter dated to [1020/30][748].  It is assumed that the donors were brothers although this is not certain. 
2 CONT
2 CONT v)        JEAN d'Ivry (-1079).  Brother of Hugues, according to Orderic Vitalis[749].  Bishop of Avranches 1061.  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Chronicon S. Stephani Cadomensis records that "Joannes filius Rodulfi comitis fratris Ricardi" succeeded as Archbishop of Rouen in 1069, having been bishop of Avranches for seven years and three months; the same source records
2 CONC  the death in 1079 of "Joannes Rothomag. Archiepiscopus"[750]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT b)         daughters .  
2 CONT
2 CONT Guillaume de Jumièges records that Asperleng & Sprota had several daughters "qui dans la suite furent mariées en Normandie avec des nobles"[751]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Sprota was a Breton concubine captured in war and bound to William by a Danish marriage. After William died, Sprota became the wife of Esperleng, a wealthy miller; Rodulf of Ivry was their son and Richard I's half-brother.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Regarding Sporta:
2 CONT
2 CONT William I Longsword (French: Guillaume Longue-Épée, Latin: Willermus Longa Spata, Scandinavian: Vilhjálmr Langaspjót; 893 – 17 December 942) was the second Duke of Normandy from his father's death until his own assassination. The 
2 CONC title dux (duke) was not in use at the time and has been applied to early Norman rulers retroactively; William actually used the title comes (count).
2 CONT
2 CONT Little is known about his early years. He was born in Bayeux or Rouen to Rollo and his wife Poppa. All that is known of Poppa is that she was a Christian, and the daughter to Berengar of Rennes, the previous lord of Brittania Nova
2 CONC , which eventually became western Normandy. According to the William's planctus, he was baptised a Christian.
2 CONT
2 CONT William succeeded Rollo sometime around 927. It appears that he faced a rebellion early in his reign, from Normans who felt he had become too Gallicised. Subsequent years are obscure. In 939 William became involved in a war with A
2 CONC rnulf I of Flanders, which soon became intertwined with the other conflicts troubling the reign of Louis IV. He was killed by followers of Arnulf while at a meeting to settle their conflict. His son Richard the Fearless, child of 
2 CONC his first wife, Sprota, succeeded him. William also left a widow, Liègard (Liutgard), who died in 985.
2 CONT
2 CONT The funerary monument of William Longsword in the cathedral of Rouen, France. The monument is from the XIVth century.[edit] Sources
