2 CONT ( http://homepages.rootsweb.ancestry.com/~cousin/html/index.htm ) Ref: http://www.robertsewell.ca/normandy.html Sprota was the name of a Breton captive who William I, Duke of Normandy took as a wife in the Viking fashion and by he
2 CONC r had a son, Richard I, Duke of Normandy. After the death of her husband William, she became the wife of Esperleng and mother of Rodulf of Ivry. Sprota was married to William I of Normandy, ruler of Normandy. Her son Richard the f
2 CONC irst was still a boy when his father died in 942. Sprota was a Breton concubine captured in war and bound to William by a Danish marriage. Sprota was married to William I of Normandy, ruler of Normandy. Her son Richard the first w
2 CONC as still a boy when his father died in 942. Sprota was a Breton concubine captured in war and bound to William by a Danish marriage. She eventually married William Longsword but it seems had her children by him before that. Died e
2 CONC ither in Normandel, Orne, Basse-Normandie, France or Kerlouan, Finistere, Bretagne, France Called a Breton concubine by Flodoard [s.a. 943: "Rex Ludowicus filio ipsius Willelmi, nato de concubina Britanna, terram Nortmannorum dedi
2 CONC t; ..." (clearly referring to Richard), MGH SS 3, 389, van Houts (2000), 47], she was first given a name by William of Jumièges [GND iii, 2 (vol. 1, 78-9)], who, writing in the second half of the eleventh century, stated that she 
2 CONC was bound to William by the Danish custom ("... Danico more iuncta, nomine Sprota ...").
2 CONT
2 CONT Although the name "Sprota" gives us a convenient name to refer to the mother of Richard I, the unusual form of the name and the lateness of the source (more than 100 years later) make it uncertain that her name has been correctly 
2 CONC reported, hence the above use of quotes
2 CONT
2 CONT Sporte Senlis De Bretagne (Sporte Senlis De De Normandy)
2 CONT
2 CONT Sporte Senlis De Bretagne was born on June 21, 911 in Bretagne, France, The Daughter of Heribert I De Vermandois and Senlis.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sporte Senlis De Bretagne married William Longsword De Normandy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sporte Senlis De Bretagne had 2 children. Their names are Godfrey Ginbe Beulac, and Richard I De Normandy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sporte Senlis De Normandy (nee De Bretagne) passed away on March 09, 940 in Normandy, France.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Sprota de Senlis: do NOT confuse with Sprote de Bourgogne who married Bernard 'the Dane'. This Sprota was partner of William Longsword (Vilhjalm Langaspjót/Guillaume Longue-Épée), later she married Esperleng de Vandreuil.*
2 CONT
2 CONT Sprota or Sprote. Her parentage is conjectural. She is traditionally said to have been a daughter of Bernard, Count of Senlis. She was said by Flodoard to have been from Brittany, which might mean that she was of Celtic, Scandinav
2 CONC ian or Frankish origin. Her name suggests the latter.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sprota 'Adela' de Bretagne de St. Liz (de Senlis) (c.911 - 945)
2 CONT
2 CONT Married or partner to William Langaspjót, called Guillaume Longe-Épée in French.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother to Richard 'Sans-Peur'
2 CONT
2 CONT Remarried Esperleng, several children (see below)
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/NORMAN%20NOBILITY.htm#_Toc243182165
2 CONT
2 CONT Sprota was married to William I of Normandy, ruler of Normandy. Her son Richard the first was still a boy when his father died in 942. Sprota was a Breton concubine captured in war and bound to William by a Danish marriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT After William died, Sprota became the wife of Esperleng, a wealthy miller; Rodulf of Ivry was their son and Richard's half-brother.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sprota was NOT married to Nigel St. Saveur.
2 CONT
2 CONT Do NOT confuse with Sprote de Bourgogne who was married to Bernard "the Dane" Harcourt.
2 CONT
2 CONT [Several people have entered Sprota to Geni as "Adela", but this is probably wrong. Please enter sources if any.]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sprota married Esperleng after the relationship with Guillaume/William:
2 CONT
2 CONT (from Medieval Lands)
2 CONT
2 CONT SPROTA, daughter of ---.
2 CONT
2 CONT Guillaume de Jumièges records that Guillaume married "une très-noble jeune fille Sprota…selon l'usage des Danois"[62]. From Brittany.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is possible that Sprota was Count Guillaume's concubine rather than wife, particularly as no reference has been found to a dissolution of any marriage before she married Esperleng.
2 CONT
2 CONT She married Esperleng de Pîtres, by whom she had Rodulf [Raoul] Comte d'Ivry.
2 CONT
2 CONT ESPERLENG de Pîtres, son of --- .
2 CONT
2 CONT m SPROTA, daughter of --- . From Brittany. Sprota was previously the concubine or wife of Guillaume I Comte [de Normandie].
2 CONT
2 CONT Guillaume de Jumièges records the marriage of Sprota and "Asperleng" who owned the mills in the valley of la Risle[730].
2 CONT
2 CONT Esperling & his wife had [four or more] children:
2 CONT
2 CONT a) RAOUL d'Ivry (-after 1011). Guillaume de Jumièges names Raoul as uterine brother of Richard Comte [de Normandie], specifying that the latter consulted him about arrangements for the succession in Normandy when dying[731]. Comte
2 CONC  de Bayeux.
2 CONT
2 CONT m AUBREE, daughter of --- (-murdered ). Guillaume de Jumièges records the marriage of Raoul and "Eranberge…née dans une certaine terre du pays de Caux que l'on appelle Caville ou Cacheville"[732]. She is named as wife of Raoul by 
2 CONC Orderic Vitalis, who says that she built the castle of Ivry, executed the architect Lanfred to prevent him from completing a similar construction elsewhere, attempted to expel her husband from the castle, and was killed by him[733
2 CONC ].
2 CONT
2 CONT Comte Raoul & his wife had five children:
2 CONT
2 CONT i) HUGUES d'Ivry (-Oct 1049).
2 CONT
2 CONT Guillaume de Jumièges names Hugues bishop of Bayeux as son of comte Raoul, when recording that the castle of Ivry was confiscated from him by Robert II Duke of Normandy[734]. Seigneur d'Ivry. Bishop of Bayeux 1015.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugues had [two] illegitimate children by an unknown mistress or mistresses:
2 CONT
2 CONT (a) ROGER .
2 CONT
2 CONT "Rogerius Hugonis episcopi filius" sold land in Blovilla and Novillula to Sainte-Trinité in an undated charter[735].
2 CONT
2 CONT m ODA, daughter of ---. "Odain uxore sua" is named in the undated charter of "Rogerius Hugonis episcopi filius"[736]. Roger & his wife had two children:
2 CONT
2 CONT (1) GUILLAUME .
2 CONT
2 CONT "Willelmo et Hugone eorum filiis" are named in the undated charter of "Rogerius Hugonis episcopi filius"[737]. "Guillelmo filio Rogerii filii Hugonis episcopi" purchased land from "Rodulfus de Warenna" dated 1074[738].
2 CONT
2 CONT (2) HUGUES .
2 CONT
2 CONT "Willelmo et Hugone eorum filiis" are named in the undated charter of "Rogerius Hugonis episcopi filius"[739].
2 CONT
2 CONT (b) [AUBREE .
2 CONT
2 CONT Chibnall speculates that the grandmother of Ascelin Goël may have been the daughter of Hugues Bishop of Bayeux, which may have provided her grandson with a claim to Ivry by inheritance[740], assuming that her illegitimacy presente
2 CONC d no obstacle. Her two marriages are shown in Europäische Stammtafeln[741], but the primary sources which confirm them have not yet been identified.
2 CONT
2 CONT m firstly ROBERT d'Ivry, son of ---. [1060].
2 CONT
2 CONT m secondly ALBERT de Cravent .]
2 CONT
2 CONT ii) EMMA d'Ivry . Guillaume de Jumièges records that one of the daughters of Raoul & his wife married Osbern de Crepon[742]. After her husband died, she became abbess of St Amand at Rouen[743].
2 CONT
2 CONT m OSBERN de Crepon, son of HERFAST & his wife --- (-murdered [1040]).
2 CONT
2 CONT iii) daughter .
2 CONT
2 CONT Guillaume de Jumièges records that the other (unnamed) daughter of Raoul & his wife married Richard de Belfage, naming their son Robert and recording that one of their several daughters married Hugues de Montfort[744].
2 CONT
2 CONT m RICHARD de Beaufour, son of ---.
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard & his wife had [four or more] children:
2 CONT
2 CONT (a) ROBERT .
2 CONT
2 CONT Guillaume de Jumièges records that the other (unnamed) daughter of Raoul & his wife married Richard de Belfage, naming their son Robert and recording that one of their several daughters married Hugues de Montfort[745].
2 CONT
2 CONT (b) daughter .
