2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT From [https://www.tourismeloiret.com/en/diffusio/illustres/dame-heloise-pithiviers_TFOILLCEN045V509S30 tourismeloiret.com] 
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of her husband during a pilgrimage to Rome, Héloise took control of the seigniorial castle in Pithiviers. Keen to fortify the estate, she had a keep and a master tower built. She also funded the reconstruction of t
2 CONC he church of St George which was elevated to the status of collegiate. She skilfully transformed the town into a renowned pilgrimage centre; however, it quickly became a simple stage along the Way of Saint James. Upon her death, t
2 CONC he seigniory passed on to her youngest son Oldaric, future Bishop of Orléans.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Héloïse_de_Pithiviers Héloïse of Pithiviers] at French Wikipedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Héloïse (or Helvide or Helvise) of Pithiviers (or Blois), lady of Pithiviers, was born between 965 and 970 and died before 1025.
2 CONT
2 CONT She is the sister of Roger de Blois, bishop of Beauvais in 999-1022 and probably of the Palatine Count Hugues de Beauvais. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Historians[1],[2] have demonstrated several times that she cannot be the daughter of [https://www.geni.com/people/Odo-I-count-of-Blois/6000000002043186469 Count Eudes I of Blois] and [https://www.geni.com/people/Berthe-de-Bourgogn
2 CONC e-reine-consort-de-France/6000000009756433107 Berthe of Burgundy] [3].
2 CONT
2 CONT His agnatic filiation is more easily explained by linking it to Hugues de Blois[4],[5]. The origin of Héloïse currently proposed for her cognatic filiation links her to the Hugonids (cf. the Counts of Laon and Bassigny). This expl
2 CONC ains the anthroponyms of Roger I of Blois, Hugues and Héloïse.
2 CONT
2 CONT She married Renart called de Broyes and gave him several children including Odalric or Oury[6]. If Renart and his children are the first to be qualified as lords of Pithiviers, the qualifier of lady[7] of Pithiviers and other elem
2 CONC ents demonstrate that this castellance formed the dowry of Héloïse[8].
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of her husband during a pilgrimage to Rome[9], she was in charge of the castle seigneury of Pithiviers. She has a dungeon or a master tower built there. Reinforced and raised to 33 meters at the end of the 11th cen
2 CONC tury, it dominated the city for nearly 840 years before its demolition in 1837[10]. Its architect is Lanfroi or Lanfroy, the same one who worked on the construction site of the Château d'Ivry.
2 CONT
2 CONT It also financed the reconstruction of the castle church that became the Collegiate Church of Saint-Georges. She endowed the chapter with 12 canons and a dignitary. She was buried there before 1025. Embarrassed by the new main tow
2 CONC er, this church will not have the initially planned nave. But she will benefit from a bedside with an outpatient and 4 radiant apses favoring the exposure of relics.
2 CONT
2 CONT Around 998, Héloïse de Pithiviers welcomed the Armenian hermit Saint Gregory of Nicopolis and installed him in the chapel of the monks of Vertou in Baudrevilliers (Bondaroy). When he died, around 1006, she had his remains brought 
2 CONC back to Pithiviers in the Collegiate Church of Saint-Georges[11].
2 CONT
2 CONT The relatively exceptional life of Héloïse and her achievements (donjon, collegiate church) marked her contemporaries. Apart from hagiographic narratives, she is a notable character in a Chanson de Geste, Garin le Loherain (Cycle 
2 CONC Lorrain). She indirectly influenced other storytellers or chroniclers[8], such as the Anglo-Norman monk Orderic Vital.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family and offspring 
2 CONT
2 CONT From her union with Renart are born[12]:
2 CONT
2 CONT # Odalric: lord of Pithiviers after his mother and bishop of Orleans from 1022 to 1033[13];
2 CONT # Héloïse (II) of Pithiviers or Châteaudun who married Geoffroy I du Perche in 1004.
2 CONT # [sic: stepson]  Isembart (or Erambert) (around 965/70 - around 1028): heir[14] of Broyes and his father's Champagne property; on the side of Héloïse, his uncles by marriage Hugues de Beauvais and Roger I de Blois, leave him (100
2 CONC 8) the seigneury of Nogent-le-Roi which will then bear the name of Nogent-l'Erambert.
2 CONT
2 CONT To learn more:
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacques CHARLES (Supplement to the Quarterly Bulletin of the Archaeological and Historical Society of the Orléanais), Pithiviers through the centuries, Courrier du Loiret, 1964, 107 p. (read online [archive]). https://gallica.bnf.
2 CONC fr/ark:/12148/bpt6k6570310r/f1.item.r=pithiviers.zoom
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT *https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/PARIS%20REGION%20NOBILITY.htm#HelvisPithiviersMRenart
2 CONT  Two siblings: 
2 CONT #         ROGER (-24 Jun 1022).  .. ... .... ..... 
2 CONT
2 CONT #        '''HELOISE''' (-Jan ---).  The Vita of St Gregory Archbishop of Nikopolis records "nobilis matrona Allvisa" in "Pithiueris" as mother of "Odolricum", specifying that Odolric was "unigenitum suum" in relation to his mother
2 CONC [1072].  Labande confirms that Odolric was the son of Rainard de Broyes seigneur de Pithiviers and his wife Héloise[1073].  Her family origin is confirmed by charter dated 1028 of Robert II King of France, confirming donations to 
2 CONC Notre-Dame de Colombes, which names her son "Aurelianensium præsul Odolricus" and "iamdicti Odolrici præsulis avunculus Rogerius Belvacensis episcopus"[1074].  The necrology of Pithiviers Saint-Georges records the death in Jan of 
2 CONC "dominæ Heloysiæ quondam ducissæ Aurelianensis", adding that she founded the church[1075].  The anachronistic "ducissa" indicates that the record was not contemporary, although it provides some indication of the relative importanc
2 CONC e accorded to her family in the region.  <br> '''m''' as his second wife, RAINARD, son of ---.  Seigneur de Pithiviers.   
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT --------
2 CONT
2 CONT ===[https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/chamchalons.htm#Isembartdiedafter1028A FMG "Champagne Nobility: Champagne (Marne)]===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''ISEMBART, son of RAINARD & his first wife ---''' (-after 1028).  "Odolricus Aurelianorum episcopus...et frater meus Isembardus" confirmed the possession of “æcclesiam in pago Dunensi...Ursi Villaris” to Saint-Père-en-Vallée by 
2 CONC charter dated to before 1028[47].  A charter dated 1028 of Robert II King of France, confirming donations to Notre-Dame de Colombes, names "Aurelianensium præsul Odolricus" and "iamdicti Odolrici præsulis avunculus Rogerius Belvac
2 CONC ensis episcopus", and is signed by "Isambardi fratris ipsius episcopi, Hugonis filii ipsius Isamberti"[48].  
2 CONT
2 CONT ===[http://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liste_des_seigneurs_de_Broyes Wikipédia Listes des seigneurs de Broyes]===
2 CONT
2 CONT ''''''Renart de Nogent'''[2], (ou Renard de Broyes, ou Renaud de Broyes), (vers 940 - Rome ?), seigneur de Broyes, de Beaufort et de Pithiviers''' vers 960. Guérin de Lorraine, qui vivait sous le règne de Louis VII de France parle
2 CONC  de l'épouse de Renart en ces termes1 :
2 CONT
2 CONT « Sept filles ot li (issues de) Loheranz Herviz (Hervé de Lorraine). Prues (prudes) et courtoises, et de moult riche pris (et bien dotées), Maria les au mieus de son païs (il les maria au mieux dans son pays). L'aisnée en ot (issu
2 CONC e) d'Orlenois Hernais (d'Hervé de Lorraine), Icelle ot nom la belle Hellois (avait pour nom la belle Héloise), Qui tint Peviers (qui tenait Pithiviers), et la riche tor sist (et son château). Ses fiuz si ot nom Dus Ernais (de ses 
2 CONC fils l'un se nommait Ernais, pour Isembart), Li pruez, li sages, li chevaliers gentis (croyant, sage et preux chevalier). Si ot un frère qui à lestres fu mis (qui avait un frère lettré). Huedes ot nom (qui se nommait Eudes, diminu
2 CONC tif d'Oldoric), moult su pruez et gentis, Esuesque fu d'Orliens et del païs (qui fut évêque d'Orléans dans son pays), »
2 CONT
2 CONT Il semble plus vraisemblable, pour André Duchesne, qu''''elle était la fille d'Eudes Ier de Blois''', car une charte du roi Robert II de France concernant l'abbaye de Colombs, près de Nogent, cite Roger Ier de Blois évêque de Beau
2 CONC vais et fils d'Eudes Ier comme oncle maternel de l'évêque Oldoric, fils d'Héloise. Rainard décèdera à Rome lors d'un pèlerinage. En son souvenir sa veuve fondait la Collégiale Saint-Georges à Pithiviers1.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Mariages et succession''' :
2 CONT Sa première épouse est inconnue, il épouse en secondes noces vers 970 '''Héloïse (ou Alluise, ou Ayluise, ou Heloyse, ou Helvise) de Pithiviers'''3. Il a4 :
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Du premier mariage''' :'''Isembart''' qui suit,
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Du second mariage''' :'''Oldoric'''5, (ou Odry, ou Orri, ou Oury) évêque d'Orléans de 1022 à 1033, seigneur de Pithiviers et de Nogent.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Harold Sandler wrote on 6/9/2013 Re: Heloise de Blois
2 CONT
2 CONT According to my calculations and the various sources I have found, my reconstruction of the genealogy is as follows:
2 CONT
2 CONT Heloise de Blois, dame de Pithiviers b. abt 0980  Father: Odo (Eudes) I, count of Blois b. abt 950 d. 996  Mother: Bertha of Burgundy (964-1015)  She Married Renard de Beaufort (born abt 970)
2 CONT
2 CONT She had a daughter Beline de Beaufort born about 995.  Beline married Isembert de Broyes (995-1028). They had a son Hugh Bardoul de Broyes b. unk, died 1058
2 CONT
2 CONT I hope this is helpful, but I must point out that various sources have various dates for all these people. It should be pointed out that Odo's entry on Wikipedia does not mention Heloise as a daughter, but Wikipedia is not a perfe
2 CONC ct reference. Other sources link her to to Odo (Eudes) I, count of Blois.  Some Sources I referenced:
2 CONT
2 CONT Heloise de Blois (b. c. 980) Wife of Renard de Beaufort (b. c. 970)  http://fabpedigree.com/s053/f289965.htm  http://www.geni.com/people/H%C3%A9lo%C3%AFse-de-Blois/6000000002005...
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.ourfamtree.org/browse.cfm/Heloise-de-Blois/p11168  http://www.celtic-casimir.com/webtree/4/29807.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT Beline de Beaufort b. Abt 995) - Daughter of Helois de Blois & Renard de Beaufort  http://gw.geneanet.org/loic15?lang=fr&amp;p=isembart&amp;n=de%20broyes  http://gw.geneanet.org/loic15?lang=fr;p=beline;n=de+beaufort  http://wc.roo
2 CONC tsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&amp;db=tmebl...
2 CONT
2 CONT Isembart de Broyes (995-1028) - Husband of Beline de Beaufort  http://gw.geneanet.org/loic15?lang=fr&amp;p=isembart&amp;n=de%20broyes (Isembart)  http://freepages.genealogy.rootsweb.com/~greenefamily/lape...
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh Bardoul de Broyes (Abt 1000-1058) - Son of Isembart and Beline de Beaufort  http://www.amjancestry.com/I03966.html  http://fabpedigree.com/s038/f072491.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT Eudes (Odo) I, Count of Blois (950-996) Husband of Bertha of Burgundy  http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Odo_I_of_Blois  http://www.celtic-casimir.com/webtree/2/64604.htm  http://www.geni.com/people/Eudes-I-Comte-de-Blois/6000000002043
2 CONC 186469
2 CONT
2 CONT Bertha of Burgundy (964-1015) - Wife of Eudes I and mother of Heloise de Blois  http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bertha_of_Burgundy
2 CONT
2 CONT Conrad I "the Peaceful" King of Burgundy (925-18 Oct 993) - Father of Bertha of Burgundy and husband of Matilda of France (943-981)  http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conrad_of_Burgundy
