2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Foulques I "le Reux" (Fulk the Red, Fulco Rufus) Viscount of Angers, before 898-931×940.
2 CONT
2 CONT Count of Anjou, 931×940 (or 929)-after 941.
2 CONT
2 CONT Count of Nantes, 907×9-914×9.
2 CONT
2 CONT Viscount of Tours, before 905-before 909.
2 CONT
2 CONT Abbot of Saint-Aubin d'Angers and Saint-Lézin, before 924-after 941.
2 CONT
2 CONT Foulques (Fulco) first appears as a witness to a charter of count (later king) Eudes, abbot of of Saint Martin de Tours, in April 886 [BEC 30 (1859): 431-3]. He first appears with the title of viscount in a charter of viscount Har
2 CONC drad of Tours on 29 September 898 [Signum Fulconis vicecomitis, Mabille (1871), xcii-xciii]. In a charter of a certain Archambaldus dated 5 July 905, he appears as viscount of both Angers and Tours [Signum Fulconis Turonorum et An
2 CONC decavorum vicecomitis, Mabille (1871): xcv], but does not appear to have the latter title for long, for a charter dated 30 October 909 has Tedbaldus signing as viscount of Tours, immediate after "count" Fulco [Signum domni Fulconi
2 CONC s Andecavorum comitis; signum Tedbaldi Turonorum vicecomitis, Mabille (1871), xcviii]. As shown by Werner, this use of the comital title by Foulques comes from a brief period when he was count of Nantes, confirmed by the appearanc
2 CONC e of Foulques as count of Nantes and viscount of Angers in a charter of count (later king) Robert, abbot of Saint-Martin de Tours, dated 31 March 914 [S. Fulconis Namnetens. comitis et Andegavensis vicecomitis, Werner (1958), 287]
2 CONC , and by the statement of the Chronicle of Nantes that Foulques ("Fulco Ruffus") had possessed Nantes [Chr. Nantes, 122]. Foulques had apparently obtained the countship of Nantes between 907 (the death of Alain Barbetorte) and 30 
2 CONC October 909, and lost it before 919 [see the detailed discussion in Werner (1958), 265-8, 284-8]. He appears as abbot and viscount in a charter of a certain Fulculf dated 13 August 924 [Signum Fulconi[s] abbatis atque vicecomitis,
2 CONC  Cart. S.-Aubin 1: 60], and calls himself count of the Angevins and abbot of Saint-Aubin d'Angers and Saint-Lézin in a charter of 929 [Cart. S.-Aubin 1: 203-4 (see below)]. However, as he was still not signing as count in two char
2 CONC ters of Hugues le Grand on 3 May 930 [S. Fulconis vicecomitis, Werner (1958), 284] and 26 March 931 [Signum domni Fulconis, Mabille (1871), ciii], he does not appear to have been recognized as count of Anjou by his Robertian lords
2 CONC  until sometime between 931 and 26 December 940, when he appears as count in a charter of Hugues le Grand [Signum Fulconis comitis, Mabille (1871), cv-cviii, erroneously dated 943; see Werner (1958), 286]. Foulques was still livin
2 CONC g in [13×31] August 941, when he signed a charter along with his son Foulques [Signum domni Fulconis. Signum Fulconis filii ipsius, Mabille (1871), cv]. See Werner (1958), 264-279, and the detailed list of charters mentioning Foul
2 CONC ques on pp. 283-6, many of which are printed in full in Mabille (1871).
2 CONT
2 CONT Date of Birth: Unknown.
2 CONT
2 CONT Although there is no direct evidence for his date of birth, an estimate of 870 or a little earlier should not be far off, given his long career.
2 CONT
2 CONT Place of Birth: Unknown.
2 CONT
2 CONT Date of Death: After 13 August 941.
2 CONT
2 CONT Both Foulques and his son Foulques witnessed a charter between 13 and 31 August 941, so Foulques was still living on the former date. A charter was witnessed by a count Foulques of Anjou in May 942 [RHF 9: 723], often identified a
2 CONC s Foulques II, but, as Werner points out, it is not certain which Foulques was the signer of that charter, and the date of death of Foulques I remains unknown. Nevertheless, given his long career, he probably did not live long aft
2 CONC er 941.
2 CONT
2 CONT Place of Death: Unknown.
2 CONT
2 CONT Father: Ingelger.
2 CONT
2 CONT The name of Foulques's father is confirmed by a charter of 929 [Cart. S.-Aubin 1: 203-4 (see below)], but nothing else is known about him. For Ingelger as a figure of legend, see the Commentary section.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother: see Commentary Section.
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse: Roscille, d. after 929, daughter of Garnier (Warnerius) and Tescende.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the seventh year of king Raoul, Fulco (Foulques), his wife Roscilla, and his sons Widdo (Gui) and Fulco, gave a donation to Saint-Aubin d'Angers for the benefit of his soul and the souls of his father (genitor) Ingelgerius, his
2 CONC  son Ingelgerius, his father-in-law Warnerius and the latter's wife Tescenda ["Ego Fulco, Andecavorum comes, abbas quoque Sancti Albini Sanctique Lizinii, necnon et uxor mea Roscilla et filii mei, Widdo ac Fulco, nullius cogentis 
2 CONC imperium, sed nostra plenissima voluntate, fatetur nos, pro Dei amore et pro remedium mee anime vel anime Ingelgario, genitor meo, atque Ingelgerio, filio meo, necnon pro anima Warnerio, socro meo et uxore sua, Tescenda, ..." Cart
2 CONC . S.-Aubin, 1: 203-4; see also Cart. Angers, 75].
2 CONT
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT
2 CONT [Cart. S.-Aubin, 1: 203-4 (see above)]
2 CONT
2 CONT MALE Ingelger, d. before 929 (probably 927).
2 CONT
2 CONT For the death date, see Werner (1958), 271.
2 CONT
2 CONT MALE Gui, d. 966×985, canon of Saint-Martin de Tours; bishop of Soissons, 937-966×985.
2 CONT
2 CONT He became bishop of Soissons in 937 in succession to Abbo ["Abbo Suessorum praesul defungitur; et Wido filius Fulco Andegavensis, sancti Martini Turonensis canonicus, eius episcopatu potitur", Flodoard's Annals, s.a. 937, MGH SS 3
2 CONC : 385]. Gui was still living in 966, as a charter of Geoffroy I (son of Foulques II) mentions Geoffroy's avunculus bishop Widdo [Cart. S.-Aubin, 1: 62-3].
2 CONT
2 CONT MALE Foulques II "le Bon", d. after 958, count of Anjou, after 941-after 958, m. Gerberge.
2 CONT
2 CONT See Commentary section for supposed additional child.
2 CONT
2 CONT Commentary
2 CONT
2 CONT Although the 929 charter mentioned above confirms that the father of Foulques was named Ingelger, contemporary records tell us nothing further about this Ingelger, or about other ancestors of Fulk. A history of the counts of Anjou
2 CONC  compiled in the twelfth century, Gesta Consulum Andegavorum, purports to give an account of the ancestors of Foulques [Spicilegium 3: 237-243].
2 CONT
