2 CONT ===Cawley’s Medlands 
2 CONT NORTHERN FRANCE
2 CONT AMIENS, MONTREUIL, PONTHIEU
2 CONT v5.0 Updated 28 April 2025
2 CONT Chapter 2.    COMTES de MONTREUIL
2 CONT https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/nfraamp.htm#_Toc127521366
2 CONT
2 CONT *1.         HELGAUD, son of --- (-[866]).  Abbé de Saint-Riquier.  The Chronique de Saint Riquier records that "le comte Helgaud" succeeded "Rodolphe…du sang imperial" as lay abbot of Saint-Riquier and that he was succeeded by "Gu
2 CONC elfon…du sang royal"[681].  "Rodolphe" is identified with Rudolf Comte de Sens, brother of Empress Judith, who died in 866, and "Guelfon" as Rudolf's son.  This casts doubt on the chronology of this passage as, if the relationship
2 CONC  between Rudolf and Welf is correctly identified, it is likely that the son succeeded the father as lay abbot as such a post was often hereditary.  same person as…?  '''HELGAUD (-after 879''').  Flodoard records "terra filiorum Ba
2 CONC lduini, Rodulfi quoque de Gangeio atque Hilgaudi" in 925[682].  Comte de Montreuil 877-879.  It is probable that Helgaud, abbé de Saint-Riquier, and his son Herluin are in fact the same people as '''Helgaud''' '''Comte de Montreui
2 CONC l 877-79''' and his son Herluin who died in 945, and that the Chronique de Saint-Riquier is inaccurate in its chronology relating to the supposedly earlier father and son of these names.    … m ---.  … The name of Helgaud's wife i
2 CONC s not known.  Helgaud & his wife had one child: [sic: three children]
2 CONT ** a)         HERLUIN (-killed in battle 13 Jul 945).  … Comte Herluin & his second wife had [two or more] children: …
2 CONT **b)         '''ERARD''' .  Flodoard names "Ebrardum fratrem Erluini" when recording that his castle of Ham was captured in 932 by "Heribertus comes"[693].  Seigneur du château de Ham (Picardie). 
2 CONT ** c)         LAMBERT (-killed in battle after 945).  Guillaume de Jumièges records that “Lambertus frater eius” was killed trying to avenge the death of “Herluini”[694]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/House_of_Ham
2 CONT
2 CONT The House of Ham was an ancient noble family from Picardy whose power was reinforced by a castle whose existence is attested in the 10th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT Origins
2 CONT Ham is located in Santerre, on the eastern edge of the Somme department with that of Aisne. Le château de Ham is mentioned from the 10th century, then held by a lord of the House of Ponthieu.
2 CONT
2 CONT First House of Ham
2 CONT The oldest lord of Ham is a certain Erard I, son of Count Helgaud de Ponthieu. He was still lord of Ham in 932 when Count Herbert II of Vermandois besieged and then took the castle of Ham.[1] Herbert II used the citadel as a base 
2 CONC to devastate the regions of Noyon and Soissons.[2] It is very likely that the castle of Ham remained in the possession of the counts of Vermandois for some generations.
2 CONT
2 CONT No period document gives it any posterity, but this has not prevented not very rigorous historians from asserting the existence of a House of Ham from Erard I who held Ham for two generations, before extinguishing in the person of
2 CONC  Pavie of Ham[note 1].
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===References 
2 CONT # https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ham,_Somme Mentioned for the first time in 932 as a possession of the seigneur Erard, junior member of the Counts of Ponthieu. The town was later conquered by the Counts of Vermandois in the 12th ce
2 CONC ntury
