1 NOTE Ancestral File Number:<AFN> B6DP-12
1 NOTE MORIARTY, PG 221; GRAND DUCHESS OF KIEV, RUSSIA.
1 NOTE MORIARTY, PG 221; GRAND DUCHESS OF KIEV, RUSSIA.
1 NOTE Grand Duchess & Regent of KIEV
2 CONT
2 CONT Première soit-disante "sainte" orthodoxe Russe !
2 CONT "She spent great effort to avenge her husband's death at the hands of the Drevlians, and succeeded in slaughtering many of them and interring some in a ship burial, while still alive."
2 CONT
2 CONT "Prekrasa" = (la très Belle)
1 NOTE MORIARTY, PG 221; GRAND DUCHESS OF KIEV, RUSSIA.
1 NOTE --Other Fields
2 CONT
2 CONT Ref Number: 1380
1 NOTE Line 5287 from GEDCOM File not recognizable or too long:
2 CONT   NAME Olga Grand Duchess Of /KIEV/
1 NOTE RESEARCH NOTES:
2 CONT Princes of Kiev, Varangian extraction; Regent of Kievan Rus 945-~963, sainted
2 CONT After her husband Igor was murdered in 945, she was Rus's regent for her
2 CONT underage son Svetoslav.  Thereafter she went to Byzantium
2 CONT and lot seg d*pe. I daapen fikk she navnet Helena. She was the first russian
2 CONT saint e became a Christian. Den greske, men ikke den romerske, church gjorde
2 CONT henne til helgen. Her saint's day is  11 July.
1 NOTE SOURCE NOTES:
2 CONT Bu149 http://www.brainyencyclopedia.com/encyclopedia/o/ol/olga_of_kiev.html
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olga_of_Kiev
1 NOTE Olga den hellige er identisk med St. Helena
1 NOTE Foreldre ukjent.
1 NOTE MORIARTY, PG 221; GRAND DUCHESS OF KIEV, RUSSIA.
1 NOTE MORIARTY, PG 221; GRAND DUCHESS OF KIEV, RUSSIA.
1 NOTE Från Pskov
1 NOTE _P_CCINFO 2-2438
1 NOTE OR "HELGA""OLGA"; GRAND PRINCESS OF KIEV 945-964
1 NOTE BIOGRAPHY
2 CONT Olga a Pskov woman of Varangian extraction, married Igor/Ingvar, the future grand duke of Kiev. The Primary Chronicle gives 879 as her date of birth, which is rather unlikely given the fact that her only son was born probably some
2 CONC  65 years after that date. After Igor's death, she ruled Kievan Rus as regent from 945 to about 963 for their son Svjatoslav.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the start of her reign, Olga went to great lengths to avenge her husband's death at the hands of the Drevlians, and succeeded in slaughtering many of them and interring some in a ship burial, while still alive. She is reputed t
2 CONC o have scalded captives to death and another, probably apocryphal, story tells of how she destroyed a town hostile to her. She asked that each household present her with a dove as a gift, then tied burning papers to the legs of ea
2 CONC ch dove which she then released to fly back to their homes. The avian incendiaries set fire to the thatched roofs of their respective homes and the town was destroyed. More importantly in the long term, Olga changed the system of 
2 CONC tribute gathering (poliudie) in what may be regarded as the first legal reform recorded in Eastern Europe.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was the first Rus ruler to convert to Christianity, either in 945 or in 957. The ceremonies of her formal reception in Constantinople were minutely described by Emperor Constantine VII Porphyrogennetos in his book _De Ceremoni
2 CONC is._ Following her baptism she took the Christian name Yelena, after the empress-consort Helena Lekapena. The Slavonic chronicles add apocryphal details to the account of her baptism, such as the story how she charmed and 'outwitt
2 CONC ed' Constantine and how she spurned his matrimonial proposals.
2 CONT
2 CONT Seven Latin sources document Olga's embassy to Emperor Otto I in 959. The continuation of Regino of Prüm mentions that the envoys requested the emperor to appoint a bishop and priests for their nation. The chronicler accuses the e
2 CONC nvoys of lies, commenting that their trick was not exposed until later. Thietmar of Merseburg says that the first archbishop of Magdeburg, before being promoted to this high rank, was sent by Emperor Otto to the country of Rus (Ru
2 CONC sciae) as a simple bishop but was expelled by pagans. The same data are duplicated in the annals of Quedlinburg and Hildesheim.
2 CONT
2 CONT Olga was one of the first people of Rus to be proclaimed saint, for her efforts to spread the Christian religion in the country. However, she failed to convert her son Svjatoslav, and it was left to her grandson and pupil Vladimir
2 CONC  I to make Christianity the lasting state religion. During her son's prolonged military campaigns she remained in charge of Kiev, residing in the castle of Vyshgorod together with her grandsons. She died in Kiev on 11 July 969, so
2 CONC on after the city's siege by the Pechenegs in 968.
1 NOTE Although her parentage is not known, she is regarded as of Norse extraction. She was baptized in Constantinople under the name "Helena". She is regarded  as a saint in the Greek Orthodox Church. After her husband died in 945, she 
2 CONC ruled in his place. In 964 she abdicated in favor of her son.
1 NOTE 443618161. Olga den Hellige N.NSDTR (16957) was born in 890. (16958) She was baptised in 957 in Konstantinopel. (16959) She was a Fyrstinne in Russland. (16960) Efterat hennes mann Igor var blitt myrdet i 945, førte hun i 10 år R
2 CONC usslands regjering for sin umyndige sønn Svetoslav I. derefter drog hun til Konstantinopel og lot si døpe i 957. I dåpen fikk hun navnet Helena. Hun var den første russiske fyrstinne som blev kristen. Den greske, men ikke den rom
2 CONC erske kirke gjorde henne til helgen. Hennes dag er 11.7.
1 NOTE !BIRTH: "Royal Ancestors" by Michel Call - Based on Call Family Pedigrees FHL
2 CONT film 844805 & 844806, Family History Library, Salt Lake City, UT. Copy of
2 CONT "Royal Ancestors" owned by Lynn Bernhard, Orem, UT.
2 CONT
2 CONT Data From Lynn Jeffrey Bernhard, 2445 W 450 South #4, Springville UT  84663-4950 
2 CONT email - bernhardengineer@netscape.net
1 NOTE Hun er den første kristne russ.fyrstinne. Den greske kirke kanomiserte
2 CONT henne. Hennes dag er 11 juni.
