1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Malk "Niskinya" Lyubechanin (Prince Mal) was a 10th-century historical figure and a Drevlian prince who ruled from the Drevlian capital of Iskorosten (modern-day Korosten).
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Key facts about him:==
2 CONT
2 CONT * Historical role: In 945, he led a Drevlian uprising against the Kievan Prince Igor Rurikovich, who had angered the people by attempting to collect tribute (*polyudye*) a second time. Igor was killed during the revolt.
2 CONT * Marriage proposal: Following Igor's death, the Drevlians sent envoys to Kyiv proposing that Princess Olga marry Mal. Olga exacted brutal revenge on the Drevlians for her husband's death and burned Iskorosten to the ground; howev
2 CONC er, Mal himself likely survived and was taken prisoner.
2 CONT * Origins of his names: He appears in chronicles under the names Mal or Malk Lyubechanin. The nickname "Niskinya" (found, for instance, in the writings of the Polish historian Jan Długosz) alludes to his short stature.
2 CONT * Offspring: According to a historiographical hypothesis (first proposed by historian D. I. Prozorovsky), the *voivode* (military commander) Dobrynya was Mal's son, while Malusha—Princess Olga's housekeeper and the mother of Princ
2 CONC e Vladimir Svyatoslavich, the baptizer of Rus'—was his daughter.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Note to English speakers: Mala is the Russian genitive form of Mal. Malk is generally his accepted name (although Mal is sometimes also used).
2 CONT
2 CONT Also, Liubech refers to a place in Ukraine (located at: 51°42'2.42"N, 30°39'49.79"E, or 140 km/86 miles north of Kiev and the present Ukrainian-Belarusian border), and not the Northern German city.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the Foundation for Medieval Genealogy page on Russia Rurikid:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/RUSSIA,%20Rurik.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT Malk of Lyubech died around 1002 and was the father of Malusha and Dobrinya.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Comments:=
2 CONT
2 CONT In his book "Lament for Jerusalem" (1999) N Kozlov writes: "The Great Prince Vladimir the Saint according to chronicles was the son of a slave named Malusha, who was a housekeeper of his grandmother Grand Duchess Olga. Malusha was
2 CONC  the daughter of a rabbi, "Rabbi" - from Liubech, also bore the Hebrew name Malk *, which is not surprising when you consider that one of the oldest Russian cities, Liubech to 882 years was a vassal state of the Jewish Khazar Khan
2 CONC ate, paid tribute to him that is confirmed, in particular, the fact of taking on the title of Grand Duke Vladimir Kagan, and chronicles recorded quite peculiar to the Slavs".
2 CONT
2 CONT ==From Wikipedia in English:==
2 CONT
2 CONT Mal (d. after 945) was a Prince of the Drevlians who led an uprising in 945, during which the prince killed Igor. Sources also suggest he may have been named Malden. The name Mal links to the Drevlian town of Malin.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==The Uprising of 945==
2 CONT
2 CONT "I sent to him saying, "Why are you going again? We've already taken all the tribute. Do not listen to them, Igor. And the Drevlians came out of the city as sparrows, and Igor and the vigilantes killed them because they were littl
2 CONC e."
2 CONT
2 CONT Mal attempted to woo the widow of Igor, the Princess Olga:
2 CONT
2 CONT "They said the same Drevlians: 'Here we killed a Russian prince, take his wife Olga for our Prince Svyatoslav Mal and take and make him into what we want.'"
2 CONT
2 CONT Olga twice dealt with the ambassadors of Mal and massacred the Drevlian nobility at the grave of Igor, and then went to war against the distinguished men and defeated them. The last pockets of resistance were in the city of Iskoro
2 CONC sten. The fate of Mal in the Tale of Bygone Years is left unsaid.
2 CONT
2 CONT "And how the city burned, its elders taken prisoner, and other people killed, and the others became enslaved men, and the rest were left to pay tribute."
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Possible Kinship==
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Basil Tatischev and the Ioakimovsky Chronicle, Mal was the son of Prince Niskinin of the Drevlian.
2 CONT
2 CONT Some historians believe Mal's children were Malusha, housekeeper for Princess Olga and mother of Prince Vladimir, and Dobrynya. They occupied positions in the court household with Olga and Svyatoslav. But they failed to reach cons
2 CONC iderable heights in public administration. Dobrynya became governor in Novgorod, where he, Malusha, and Vladimir were soon sent.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Chronicle calls Malusha's father Malk Lyubchanin. In epics, Dobrynya carries the patronym of Nikitich and is reputed to be a "country bumpkin." Given that Mal in many chronicles was called Niskinya, this may suggest that the p
2 CONC atronym Nikitich derived from the patronym Niskinich.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==From the Russian Wikipedia page for Malusha:==
2 CONT
2 CONT Father of Malusha "Malk Lyubchanin"
2 CONT
2 CONT In the Tale of Bygone Years, the following is said of the origin of Malusha:
2 CONT
2 CONT Volodymer was from Malusha, Olga's housekeeper, the same who was the sister of Dobrinja, and whose father of them both was Malak Lyubechanen.
2 CONT
2 CONT * The Chronicle, quoted by Karpov in "St Vladimir" 1997, p. 13.
2 CONT
2 CONT In this regard, historians have tried to identify Malk Lyubechanin. The following hypotheses have been made about his origin.
2 CONT
2 CONT Prozorovsky, in 1864, believed she was the daughter of the Prince of the Drevlian Mal, who led a revolt in which Prince Igor was killed. This severely angered Princess Olga. Traces of Mal were lost after 945, and it is probable th
2 CONC at he did not escape the revenge of the princess. But according to Prozorvsky, he was taken captive. The epithet for the father of Malushin Lyubchanin, he explained, was that Olga, after organizing a pogrom of the Drevlian, put hi
2 CONC m in Liubech, and his respective children were taken into slavery as prisoners.
2 CONT
2 CONT In addition, scientists believe that the "princess" (Malusha) was not a concubine, but rather a lawful wife of Svyatoslav. This alternative is based on the recognition of Vladimir by Olga as on par with the legitimate sons of Svya
2 CONC toslav, as well as the inviolability of Dobrinja (also son of Malk) in Novgorod, and his successful career and universal respect, which, according to scientists, are evidence of his aristocratic status and "legitimate" family conn
2 CONC ections through his sister. This alternative had been forgotten and once again revived in 1971 in the article "The Drevlian origin of Prince Vladimir," published in the Ukrainian Historical Journal, later in "Following Dobrynya".
2 CONT
2 CONT Shakhmatov believes that her father was Mstisha-Lyut Sveneldich, whose name he believes evolved into Malk. Sveneld was probably a prince and a distinguished man who was captured. His descendants included Dobrynya, Vyshata, and Jan
2 CONC  Vyshatich, as written in the Primary Chronicle about a century after Igor's death, based on oral family traditions about their ancestors (Sveneld, Mstisha-Lyut, Dobrynya, Kosnyatine, Ostromir). Shakhmatov also demonstrated, by an
2 CONC alyzing the compound name, what happened to the imposition of Lyuta Sveneldich on Mstislav Vladimirovich Lyuty, Prince of Tmutarakan, who lived a half-century later. Malk himself, in this viewpoint, was "mutated" by the epics to a
2 CONC  character named "Nikita Zaleshanin." In the dictionary under the editorship of Yoannina in the article where Dobrynya appaered the suggestion "son of Malk Lyubechanin, Msishi-Lyuta (or Mstislav) Sveneldich.
2 CONT
2 CONT Grushevsky reacted negatively to both findings.
2 CONT
2 CONT Currently, the Shakhmatova hypothesis has been refuted. It is based on the Chronicle, and Jan Dlugosh did not reach our time through the Russian Chronicles. His name as Prince of Drevlyane reads "Miskin," and Shakhmatov decided it
2 CONC  was a derivative of Mistinya, then brought it closer to Mistisha (Mstislav). However, in the original, the Dlugosh name read as Nishkina (Polish Niszkina), which makes the construction of Shakhmatov all wrong. It also makes ident
2 CONC ification of Mistishi and Lyuta Sveneldichey unlikely. The most thorough critique comes from historians Solovyov and Poppe.
2 CONT
2 CONT Many historians also reject Prozorovsky's hypothesis that Malak Lyubechanin was Prince of the Drevlian to Prince Mal (e.g., Rybakov, Poppe, Karpov). However, this hypothesis remains quite popular and prevalent in today's published
2 CONC  genealogical compilations.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==External Links & Sources:==
2 CONT
2 CONT * [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mal_(prince) Wikipedia: Mal (prince)]
2 CONT * [https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D0%9C%D0%B0%D0%BB%D1%83%D1%88%D0%B0 Russin Wikipedia: Малуша - Malusha]
