1 OCCU Grand Prince Of Magyars (947), Alto Príncipe de Magyars, prins-regent, Høvding, Prince, de Hongrie, 955, Prins i Ungern 955-c972, Taksony (Toxun) var den tredje hertug av Ungarn 944 - 972, Storfurste av Ungern., 931, książę węgier
2 CONC ski 0950 r.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} His mother is uncertain
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/51/%C3%81rp%C3%A1d-h%C3%A1z.jpg '''Az Árpád-ház családfája - Family tree of Árpád dynasty'''] 
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taksony_of_Hungary Taksony of Hungary] From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Taksony of Hungary Grand Prince of the Hungarians Taksony in Chronicon Pictum Reign 	c. 955 - before 972 Predecessor 	Fajsz Successor 	Géza Issue -1. Géza of Hungary -2. Michael, Duke between Morava and Esztergom Father 	Zoltán Mo
2 CONC ther 	Unknown daughter of Menmarót Died 	before 972
2 CONT
2 CONT Taksony (? – before 972), Grand Prince of the Hungarians (c. 955 - before 972).
2 CONT
2 CONT Taksony was the son of Zoltán (Zaltas), the fourth son of Árpád, the second Grand Prince of the Hungarians. The Gesta Hungarorum mentions that his mother was a daughter of Menmarót, the local military leader in the region of Bihar
2 CONC  (Romanian: Biharea) at the time of the Hungarian settlement occupation (Honfoglalás). Taksony married a Pecheneg or Bulgar woman[1].
2 CONT
2 CONT In 947, Taksony lead a raid to Italy till Apulia, and King Berengar II of Italy had to buy the peace by paying a large amount of money to him and his followers. He might have taken part in the Battle of Lechfeld (Hungarian: augsbu
2 CONC rgi csata) where King Otto I of Germany won a decisive victory over the Hungarians. After the defeat the Hungarians stopped their raids (kalandozások) in Western Europe, but they began to pillage the Byzantine Empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT Taksony became the Grand Prince of the Hungarians just or shortly after the Battle of Lechfeld, but his authority must have been only nominal over some regions of the Carpathian Basin inhabited by the Hungarians. During his rule a
2 CONC  large number of Pechenegs and Khalyzians immigrated to the territory of the future Hungary.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 963, Pope John XII ordained the first Roman Catholic missionary bishop, Zacheus for the Hungarians[2], but he probably never visited them.
2 CONT
2 CONT Taksony arranged the marriage of his son Géza of Hungary to Sarolt, the daughter of Gyula of Transylvania. [edit] Marriage and children
2 CONT
2 CONT # c. 945: Unnamed "of the territories of the Cumans"
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Géza of Hungary, Grand Prince of the Hungarians (c. 945 – 997)
2 CONT     * Michael (Mihály), Duke between Morava and Esztergom (– ca 978 or bef. 997). Married Adelajda of Poland (– aft. 997), daughter or sister of Mieszko I of Poland, and had:  -1. Vazul -2. László (Ladislas "the Bald"), Duke betwe
2 CONC en March and Gran (d. 1029), married c. 1000 Premislavna (d. c. 1015), daughter of Great Prince Vladimir I of Kiev, and had:  -2.1 Bonuslo, Duke between March and Gran (d. 1048)
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Kristó Gyula - Makk Ferenc: Az Árpád-ház uralkodói (IPC Könyvek, 1996)
2 CONT     * Korai Magyar Történeti Lexikon (9-14. század), főszerkesztő: Kristó Gyula, szerkesztők: Engel Pál és Makk Ferenc (Akadémiai Kiadó, Budapest, 1994)
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
2 CONT    1. ^ The Gesta Hungarorum mentions that he married a woman "of the territories of the Cumans", but the Cumans had not crossed the Volga River before the 11th century.
2 CONT    2. ^ Liutprand of Cremona: Liber de rebus gestis Ottonis magni imperatoris.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Another name for Taksony was Toxun. 
2 CONT
2 CONT General Notes: 
2 CONT
2 CONT I hans tid kom hunnerne i 909 inn i Thüringen, i 910 helt til Augsburg, 932 til Merseburg og 955 atter til Augsburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT Taksony førte krig mot tyskerne og de bysantiske keisere.
2 CONT
2 CONT Han var farfar til Stefan den Hellige (979 - 1038). Selv ble han ikke døpt, men ga sin sønn og etterfølger, Geza, en kristen hustru, Sarolte.
2 CONT
2 CONT Noted events in his life were:
2 CONT
2 CONT • Acceded: 3rd Duke of Hungary, 944. 
2 CONT
2 CONT • Acceded: Prince of Magyars, 947. 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Taksony (? – before 972), Grand Prince of the Magyars (c. 955 - before 972).
2 CONT
2 CONT Taksony was the son of Zoltán (Zaltas), the fourth son of Árpád, the second Grand Prince of the Magyars. The Gesta Hungarorum mentions that his mother was a daughter of Menmarót, the local military leader in the region of Bihar (R
2 CONC omanian: Biharea) at the time of the Hungarian settlement occupation (Honfoglalás). Taksony married a Pecheneg or Bulgar woman.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 947, Taksony lead a raid to Italy till Apulia, and King Berengar II of Italy had to buy the peace by paying a large amount of money to him and his followers. He might have taken part in the Battle of Lechfeld (Hungarian: augsbu
2 CONC rgi csata) where King Otto I of Germany won a decisive victory over the Magyars. After the defeat the Magyars stopped their raids (kalandozások) in Western Europe, but they began to pillage the Byzantine Empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT Taksony became the Grand Prince of the Magyars just or shortly after the Battle of Lechfeld, but his authority must have been only nominal over some regions of the Carpathian Basin inhabited by the Magyars. During his rule a large
2 CONC  number of Pechenegs and Khalyzians immigrated to the territory of the future Hungary.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 963, Pope John XII ordained the first Roman Catholic missionary bishop, Zacheus for the Magyars, but he probably never visited them.
2 CONT
2 CONT Taksony arranged the marriage of his son Géza of Hungary to Sarolt, the daughter of Gyula of Transylvania.
2 CONT --------------------
