2 CONT ACED 
2 CONT DATE 0911When Charles III the Simple King of France gave Ragnvaldsson Rollo, aka Rolf
2 CONT the Pirate Normandy, and made him Robert I Duke of Normandy to prevent him
2 CONT from attacking nearer to Paris, he threw his daughter Giesela into the deal.
2 CONT TYPE E-Mail Message
2 CONT AUTH McCreight, William <william.mccreight@de.unisys.com>
2 CONT TITL Children of Ragnvaldsson Rollo (Robert I Duke of Normandy) and Giesela
2 CONT DATE 2 Jun 1999
2 CONT LOCA GEN-MEDIEVAL-L@rootsweb.com/PowerMac 6500>Applications>Reunion>Documents-source
2 CONT DATE 24 APR 2000
2 CONT
2 CONT EVEN
2 CONT TYPE Title (Facts Pg)
2 CONT PLAC Duc de Normandie - 'Gonge Rolf' (The Ganger)Norwegian Viking settled at mouth of Seine. After fighting many battles, made an agreement with King Charles the Simple - received land in Normandie
2 CONT  and 911 became 1st Duc de Normandie and vassel of King.
2 CONT
2 CONT OCCU 1st Duke of Normandy
2 CONT
2 CONT GIVN Rollo Duke of
2 CONT SURN NORMANDY
2 CONT DATE 15 Dec 2000
2 CONT HIST: @N642@
2 CONT
2 CONT GIVN Rollo, the Granger, of
2 CONT SURN NORMANDY
2 CONT NSFX Count of Rouen & 1st Duke Normandy
2 CONT ABBR Compuserve
2 CONT TITL Any information taken from Compuserve.
2 CONT AUTH Various authors
2 CONT PAGE From a message sent by T.E. Graves
2 CONT EVEN Recieved title of 1st Duke of Normandy
2 CONT TYPE Misc
2 CONT DATE 912
2 CONT ABBR History Of England
2 CONT TITL History textbook
2 CONT AUTH Katharine Coman & Elizabeth Kimball Kendall
2 CONT PUBL The Macmillian Co, 1905
2 CONT PAGE pp 59
2 CONT QUAY 2
2 CONT
2 CONT EVEN
2 CONT TYPE Title (Facts Page)
2 CONT PLAC 1st Duke, leader Norman PiratesRollo devatated Holland and appeared upon the Seine while Gottfried ravaged the valleys of to Meuse and Saheldt. They burned and sacked Cologne, Bonn, Treves, Metz
2 CONT  and other cities, sabling their horses at Aix-La- Chapelle, in the Cathedral Chich of Charlemagne. A furore Normaunorum Libre nos Domine, came to be part of the Catholic litany.
2 CONT Hastings, at the head of a band of Nothmen, sacked Bordeaux, Lisbon and Seville,; defeated the Moorish conquerors of Spain at Cordova; crossed the staits in Morocco; repassed them; overan Tuscany;
2 CONT  returned to France,where other chieftains had had various success against Chales the Bald, and embrasedChristianity.
2 CONT His name, the most dreaded of all the Vikings, was adopted by many successors. With safe winter quarters in Spain, they extended their ravages into Naples, Sicily and the coasts of the Greek Empire.
2 CONT  Anarchy, meanwhile, prevailing in France, in the autumn of 885 they laid siege to Paris. After a year of the siege was into [??] blockade, but at last Charles the Fat, bought off the Northmen with
2 CONT  700 pounds of silver and a free passage to the Upper Seine and Burgandy. The most redoubtable of the Northmen afterward was Hrolf, better known as Rollo, chiefain, of Norwegian parentage, first Duke
2 CONT  of Normandy, and direct ancestor, in the sixth generation, of WILLIAM THE CONQUEROR. In the words of Snorro Sturleson: "He was so mighty of a stature that there was no horse of strength and size to
2 CONT  bear him. He was therefore always on foot, and was called the Marcher." He ravaged Friesland and the countries watered by Schieldt, and took Rouen, St Le Bayeux and Evreux. From Charles the Simple
2 CONT  he accepted the hand of a daughter, together with a tract of Nuestrian territory north of the Seine, from  Andalye to the sea [modern Normandy]  , in exchange for Christian baptism and an oath of
2 CONT  fealty [912]. Thus arrested the Scandavain flood which had devestaded France for more than a century. Rollo distributed among his followers the lands of Nuestria, to be held as the Duke of Normandy.
2 CONT  Thus were laid the foundations for the feudal system which William the Conqueror transplanted into England [1066-1087]. Few external traces of the Scandinavians are to be found in modern Normandy.
2 CONT  Yet for a time the Scandinavian gods divided with the Saviour the religious reverence of the people of that country. Monasteries and cathedrals were built, however, with what magnificence their
2 CONT  splendor remains attest. The Normans adopted the language of the vanquished province, but greatly modified it. It was the langue d'oni [the language d'oc being south of the Loire], which became
2 CONT  under Norman inspiration the peculiar medium of romantic poetry.
2 CONT From : The Pedigree and History of the Washington Family, by Albert Welles
2 CONT
2 CONT EVEN
