1 OCCU Saint, Queen of Germany, Countess of Ringelheim, Reine, de Saxe, Princesse, de Westphalie, de Germanie, German Queen, Queen of East Franconia (919-936), Helgon i katolska kyrkan, Kejsarinna av Rom, of Ringelheim, Hennes helgondag 
2 CONC är 14.3, Empress, Queen
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} -https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Matilda_of_Ringelheim
2 CONT
2 CONT http://genealogics.org/getperson.php?personID=I00020486&tree=LEO
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Relationships'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Parents:
2 CONT *Father: Theoderic (d. 8 November 917, possible second great grandson of Duke Widukind)
2 CONT *Mother: 2 possibilities
2 CONT **1. Reinhild, daughter of Gotfrid the Dane and Gisela [Carolingian] (from the Europäische Stammtafeln, regarded to be a guess based on the Vita Mathildis)
2 CONT **2. Reginlind, possible sister of Bovo Bishop of Chalons-sur-Marne (from a passage in the Gesta Episcoporum Cameracensium, and transmission of the name Frederuna, known sister, to one of the daughters)
2 CONT
2 CONT Siblings:
2 CONT *1. Widukind
2 CONT *2. Immed I
2 CONT *3. Reginbern
2 CONT *5. Amelrada (wife of Eberhard, Graf in der Drenthe und im Salland, b. 7 September)
2 CONT *6. Frederuna (wife of Wichmann II Billung, d. 18 January 971)
2 CONT *7. Bia or Pia (mother of Friedrich, d. 25 May after 938)
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse:
2 CONT * Heinrich I "der Vogelsteller/the Fowler" King of Germany (c 876 - 2 Jul 936)
2 CONT
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT *Note: Thankmar (907/909 - 28 Jul 938) was not her child, but rather the child of Heinrich's first wife, Hatheburg (which ended because of an unspecified "outrage")
2 CONT *1.  Otto I "der Große" King of Germany (23 Nov 912 - 7 May 973)
2 CONT *2. Gerberga (913/914 - 5 May 984, wife of Giselbert, Duke of Lotharingia, and Louis IV "d'Outremer" King of the West Franks)
2 CONT *3. Heinrich (Dec 919/22 Apr 922 - 1 Nov 955), temporary Duke of Lotharingia, Duke of Bavaria
2 CONT *4. Hedwig (c 922 - 9 Jan 958 or after 965) wife of Hugues "le Grand" ducs des Francs
2 CONT *5. Bruno (May 925 - 11 Oct 965) Archbishop of Koln, Duke of Lotharingia
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Basic information'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth: c 896 (FMG), other estimates include 895 (English Wikipedia) and 892 (Dutch Wikipedia). German Wikipedia places the birth in Enger, a region of Sachsen (Present Mordrhein-Westfalen)
2 CONT
2 CONT Baptism: Unknown
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage: 909, in Wallhausen, Mulachgau (Present Bad Kreuznach), Herzogtum Franken (Present Rhineland-Palatinate), Ostenfrankenreich (Present Germany) with Heinrich I "der Vogelsteller" (FMG) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Death: 14 March 968 in Quedlinburg, Hartingau, Ostfalia (Present Landkreis Harz), Herzogtum Sachsen (Present Saxony-Anhalt), Heiliges Römisches Reich (Present Germany)
2 CONT
2 CONT Burial: Quedlinburg Stiftskirche, Quedlinburg, Landkreis Harz, Saxony-Anhalt, Germany
2 CONT
2 CONT Occupation: 
2 CONT *Queen of Germany (6 May 919 - 2 July 936)
2 CONT *Duchess consort of Saxony (30 November 912 – 2 July 936)
2 CONT *Lay Abbess of Nivelles
2 CONT
2 CONT Timeline:
2 CONT *892-896 born in Engern region of Herzogtum Sachsen, descended from Herzog Widukind of Sachsen.
2 CONT *While a young girl, was visited by Otto "der Erlauchte" Graf im Südthüringau, who was taken by her beauty, and had her betrothed to his son, Heinrich. Heinrich was apparently married to a widow named Hatheburg first, however, by 
2 CONC which he had a son named Thankmar, but the marriage ended because of an unspecified "outrage" (the "outrage" could have been the marriage itself).
2 CONT *909, married Heinrich at Wallhausen (age 13-17)
2 CONT *February-November 912, estimated pregnancy and birth of Otto I "der Große" (age 16-20)
2 CONT *30 November 912, becomes Duchess Consort of Sachsen (age 16-20)
2 CONT *913/914, birth of Gerberga in Nordhausen (age 17-22)
2 CONT *8 November 917, death of her father Theoderic (age 21-25)
2 CONT *6 May 919, becomes Queen of Germany upon election of her husband as King (age 23-29)
2 CONT *Between December 919 and 22 April 922, birth of Heinrich I, future Herzog von Bayern (age 23-30)
2 CONT *922, birth of Hedwig, future wife of Hugues "le Grand" ducs des Francs (age 26-30)
2 CONT *May 925, birth of Bruno, youngest son (age 29-33)
2 CONT *928/929, marriage of Gerberga to Giselbert, Graf von Maasgau, possibly by arrangement with King Heinrich I as part of the terms of his submission. Gerberga soon after becomes pregnant with Mathilda's first granddaughter, Alberade
2 CONC  (age 32-37)
2 CONT *930, eldest son, Otto I, is elevated to Associate King to rule alongside his father, King Heinrich I (age 34-38)
2 CONT *2 July 936, death of King Heinrich I "der Vogelsteller" (age 40-44)
2 CONT *7 August 936, ascension of eldest son King Otto I "der Große" as King of Germany (age 40-44)
2 CONT *9 May or 14 September 937, Hedwig is married off to Hugues "le Grand", who was appointed Duc des Francs the previous year. (age 41-45)
2 CONT *28 Jul 938, death of Thankmar ends his rebellion, murdered in front of a church altar at Eresburg - murderer is punished by King Otto. Heinrich, Mathilda's second son, is captured by Eberhard, Herzog of Franconia. (age 42-46)
2 CONT *2 October 939, Battle of Andernach, Duke Giselbert II of Lotharingia drowns, widowing eldest daughter Gerberga. Almost immediately, she marries Louis IV "d´Outremer" King of the Franks, who sought to use the marriage to assert co
2 CONC ntrol of Lotharingia. Mother and daughter likely never see each other again (age 43-47)
2 CONT *940, Heinrich is named as Herzog von Lothringen, in succession to the deposed Giselbert, but fails to gain any ground against Louis "d'Outremer". He soon returns to the Herzogtum Sachsen. In the same year, Hedwig gives birth to h
2 CONC er son Hugues, later known as Hugues Capet, first King of France. Youngest son Bruno is appointed by his elder brother King Otto as Chancellor of Germany (age 44-48)
2 CONT *947, Heinrich is installed by King Otto as Herzog von Bayern (age 51-55)
2 CONT *948, youngest son Bruno, while serving as Chancellor of Germany, is appointed as Abbot of Lorsch (age 52-56)
2 CONT *951, eldest son King Otto invades Italy and in Padova late in the year, proclaims himself King of Italy. In the following year, he appoints Berengario di Ivrea as his viceroy before returning to Germany (age 55-59)
2 CONT *953, youngest son Bruno steps down as Chancellor of Germany to become the Archbishop of Koln and Duke of Lotharingia by his elder brother, King Otto. However, he begins to plot against his brother on behalf of his sister Hedwig a
2 CONC nd her husband, Hugues "le Grand" to deliver Lotharingia to Western Francia. Eventually, he repents of the scheme, particularly after Liudolf's rebellion against his father, King Otto, is quelled, and is forgiven (alongside Liudol
2 CONC f). (age 57-61)
2 CONT *1 November 955, shortly after being restored as Herzog von Bayern, Heinrich I, Mathilda's second son, dies, and is succeeded by her 4-year-old grandson Heinrich II (soon nicknamed "der Zänker" for his quarrelsome nature). In the 
2 CONC same year, King Otto defeats the Magyars, ending their invasion of central Europe (age 59-63)
2 CONT *June 956, Hugues "le Grand" dies, leaving Hedwig as the widowed mother of Hugues Capet, future first King of France (age 60-64)
2 CONT *9 January 958, Hedwig, widowed mother of Mathilda's grandchild Hugues Capet, might have died by this time (age 62-66)
2 CONT *959, Upper and Lower Lotharingia are divided in two. Upper Lotharingia is given over to Hedwig's son-in-law Frederic, and Lower Lotharingia is retained by Mathilda's youngest son, Bruno, Archbishop of Koln (age 63-67)
2 CONT *August 961, after several years of abuse of power, Berengario di Ivrea faces punishment as King Otto I invades Italy a second time, against him. This was at the behest of Pope John XII, who sought an end to Berengano's tyranny (a
2 CONC ge 65-69)
2 CONT *2 February 962, after driving Berengario di Ivrea from much of Italy (eventually he is captured after surrendering his fortress of San Leo near Montefeltro), eldest son King Otto I is proclaimed by Pope John XII as Emperor. This 
2 CONC marks the start of the political entity later known as the Holy Roman Empire (age 66-70)
2 CONT *11 October 965, Mathilda's youngest son Bruno, Archbishop of Koln and Herzog von Niederlothringen, dies. Hedwig, widowed mother of Mathilda's grandchild Hugues Capet, might have survived to this time (her death date is uncertain)
2 CONC . (Mathilda is age 69-73)
2 CONT *14 March 968, Mathilda, former Queen of Germany and Lay Abbess of Nivelles, dies at Quenlinburg, and shortly after is buried at the Stiftskirche there. (age 72-76).
2 CONT
2 CONT Alternate Names: Mechthild, Matilda, Maud, Mathildis ____________________________________________________________________________________________St. Mathilda was the daughter of Theodoric, a Saxon Count. At an early age she was pl
2 CONC aced in the monastery of Erfurt under the care of Maud, her grandmother, who was Abbess of the monastery which she had entered after the death of her husband. Here St. Mathilda learned needlework and acquired the love of labor, pr
2 CONC ayer and spiritual reading. She remained in the convent until her parents gave her in marriage, in 913, to Henry "the Fowler," so called from his fondness for hawking. He became Duke in 916 on the death of his father, and in 919 h
2 CONC e was chosen to succeed Conrad as King of Germany. The pious Queen adorned the throne by her many virtues. She visited and comforted the sick and the afflicted, instructed the ignorant, succored prisoners, and endeavored to conver
2 CONC t sinners, and her husband concurred with her in her pious undertakings. After twenty-three years of married life King Henry died, in 936. No sooner had he expired than she had a Mass offered up for the repose of his soul, and fro
2 CONC m that moment she renounced all worldly pomp. Of her three sons, Otho afterward became Emperor, Henry was Duke of Bavaria, and St. Bruno edified the Church as Archbishop of Cologne. Otho became King of Germany in 937, and in 962 h
2 CONC e was crowned Emperor at Rome. In the contest between her two sons, Otho and Henry, for the crown which was elective, the Queen favored the former, a fault she expiated by great suffering, for both these sons subjected her to a lo
2 CONC ng and cruel persecution. She died in 968. Her feast day is March 14th.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: Author and Publisher - Catholic Online
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Foundation for Medieval Genealogy Medlands page for Saxony, Dukes and Electors (covering her birth family):
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/SAXONY.htm#Mathildedied968
2 CONT
2 CONT THEODERIC (-8 Nov 917[31]).  
2 CONT *Widukind names "Thiadrici" as father of Queen Mathilde, specifying that the family was "stirpis magni ducis Widukindi"[32].  
2 CONT *The father of Queen Mathilde is named "Thietricus" in the Vita Mathildis Reginæ[33].  
2 CONT
2 CONT m REGINLIND [Reinhild], daughter of --- (-11 May <nowiki>----</nowiki>).  
2 CONT *The wife of Theoderich is named "Reinhildam, Danorum Fresonumque germine procreatam" in the Vita Mathildis Reginæ[34].  According to Europäische Stammtafeln[35], she was Reinhild, daughter of Gotfrid the Dane & his wife Gisela [C
2 CONC arolingian], which is presumably a guess based on this description in the Vita Mathildis.  However, the chronology is not ideal.  
2 CONT *Regino records the marriage in 882 of "Gisla filia Hlotharii" and "rex Godofridus Nordmannorum"[36].  If this couple's daughter was the mother of Queen Mathilde, the latter's estimated birth date (see below) would need to be push
2 CONC ed forward by several years, which makes the chronology for her known descendants tight.  
2 CONT *A better fit may be Reginlind, [sister of Bovo Bishop of Chalons-sur-Marne, daughter of ---].  The known sister of Bishop Bovo was Frederuna, wife of Charles III "le Simple" King of the Franks.  The hypothesis that there was anot
2 CONC her sister married to Theoderic would explain (1) the name Frederuna being transmitted to Regenhild's daughter, and (2) Berenger Bishop of Cambrai, recorded elsewhere as nepos of Queen Frederuna, being described as "…Ottonis imper
2 CONC atoris proxime consanguineus" in the Gesta Episcoporum Cameracensium[37].  If this is correct, the reference to Reginlind being "Danorum Fresonumque germine procreatam" has not been explained.  
2 CONT *A list of names in the Libri Confraternitatum Sancti Galli sets out (in order) "Thieterich, Reginhilt, *, Oto, Amalrat, Perectheid, Friderun, Pia", immediately after a list of family members of Heinrich I King of Germany and his 
2 CONC wife, which no doubt refers to the latter's parents and sisters, "Oto" being the only person not so far identified[38].  The list is undated but was presumably written during the period [929/36] as King Heinrich's son-in-law Duke 
2 CONC Giselbert is included (married in [928/29]) but not his son-in-law Hugues Duc des Francs (married in 937).  
2 CONT *The necrology of Merseburg records the death "11 May" of "Reinhild mater regine Mahtildis"[39].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Theoderic & his wife had seven children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT i)          WIDUKIND .  
2 CONT *Widukind names (in order) "Widukind, Immed et Reginbern" as brothers of Queen Mathilde[40]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ii)         IMMED [I] .  
2 CONT *Widukind names (in order) "Widukind, Immed et Reginbern" as brothers of Queen Mathilde[41]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT iii)        REGINBERN .  
2 CONT *Widukind names (in order) "Widukind, Immed et Reginbern" as brothers of Queen Mathilde, specifying that Reginbern fought against the Danes[42]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT iv)       MATHILDE ([896]-Quedlinburg 14 Mar 968, bur Quedlinburg Stiftskirche).  
2 CONT *Widukind names "Mahthilda" as wife of King Heinrich, also naming her father and three brothers[43].  Thietmar names Mathilde as daughter of "Dietrich and Reinhild" when recording her marriage to Heinrich, and specifies that she w
2 CONC as "a descendant of the lineage of King Widukind"[44].  Her alleged descent from Widukind is also referred to in the Vita Mahthildis[45].  
2 CONT *Thietmar records that Quedlinburg was bestowed on Mathilde as part of her dower 16 Sep 929[46], and that she established the convent there thirty days after the death of her husband[47].  
2 CONT *Lay Abbess of Nivelles.  
2 CONT *The necrology of Fulda records the death "968 II Id Mar" of "Mahthild regina"[48].  
2 CONT
2 CONT m (Wallhausen 909) as his second wife, HEINRICH Graf, son of OTTO "der Erlauchte" Graf im Südthüringau & his wife Hedwig [Babenberg] ([876]-Memleben 2 Jul 936, bur Quedlinburg Stiftskirche).  
2 CONT *He was elected as HEINRICH I "der Vogelsteller/the Fowler" King of Germany at Fritzlar 6 May 919. 
2 CONT
2 CONT v)        AMELRADA ([7 Sep] <nowiki>----</nowiki>).  
2 CONT *A list of names in the Libri Confraternitatum Sancti Galli sets out (in order) "Thieterich, Reginhilt, *, Oto, Amalrat, Perectheid, Friderun, Pia", immediately after a list of family members of Heinrich I King of Germany and his 
2 CONC wife, which no doubt refers to the latter's parents and sisters[49].  The Vita Domni Deoderici Episcopi Maioris names "Amelrada" as wife of "comite Everardo", sister of "Mathildis reginæ…filiæ Thiadrici ducis", and she and her hus
2 CONC band as parents of "Deodericum ex pago Saxoniæ Hamalant"[50].  
2 CONT *The necrology of Gorze records the death "VII Id Sep" of "Amarrada comitissa"[51].  
2 CONT *m EBERHARD, son of [EBERHARD Graf im Keldachgau und im Bonngau [Ezzonen] & his wife --- (-[3 Sep] before 964).  Graf in der Drenthe und im Salland. 
2 CONT
2 CONT vi)       FREDERUNA (-18 Jan 971).  
2 CONT *Thietmar refers to "Counts Wichmann and Ekbert…brothers" as sons of Emperor Otto I's maternal aunt[52], but does not name their mother.  A list of names in the Libri Confraternitatum Sancti Galli sets out (in order) "Thieterich, 
2 CONC Reginhilt, *, Oto, Amalrat, Perectheid, Friderun, Pia", immediately after a list of family members of Heinrich I King of Germany and his wife, which no doubt refers to the latter's parents and sisters[53].  It is also possible tha
2 CONC t the wife of Wichmann [II] was the sister Bia, unless she can be identified as the mother of Friedrich (see below).  
2 CONT *The necrology of Fulda records the death "970 Id Jan" of "Fridarun comitissa [anc Christi]"[54], presumably showing that she became a nun before she died.  
2 CONT *m WICHMANN [II], son of BILLUNG & his wife --- (-23 Apr 944). 
2 CONT
2 CONT vii)      BIA [Pia] (-25 May <nowiki>----</nowiki>)  
2 CONT *A list of names in the Libri Confraternitatum Sancti Galli sets out (in order) "Thieterich, Reginhilt, *, Oto, Amalrat, Perectheid, Friderun, Pia", immediately after a list of family members of Heinrich I King of Germany and his 
2 CONC wife, which no doubt refers to the latter's parents and sisters[55].  "Otto…rex" granted property "in Gereslevo…in pago Svevia in comitatu Crhistiani" on the request of "Friderici fidelis nostri" to "nobili matronæ…Biæ ipsius…matr
2 CONC i" by charter dated 21 Oct 937, in consultation with "Burchardi, Ebarhardi, Chuonradi, Heinrici atque Utonis…comitum"[56], although it is not known whether this was the same Bia.  
2 CONT *The necrology of Merseburg records the death "25 May" of "Bia soror regine Mahtildis"[57].  
2 CONT *same person as…?  BIA (-after 21 Oct 937).  "Otto…rex" granted property "in Gereslevo…in pago Svevia in comitatu Crhistani" to "nobili matronæ Bia ipsius…matri" at the request of "Friderici fidelis nostri" by charter dated 21 Oct
2 CONC  937[58].  It is not certain that this refers to the same person as Bia, daughter of Theoderic, but this is likely to be the case.  No other noble lady of this name has been identified around the date of this charter, and "matrona
2 CONC " is the term usually applied to members of the high nobility.  The wording of the charter suggests that Bia's husband had died before the date of the grant.  
2 CONT *m --- (-before 21 Oct 937).  The name of Bia's husband is not known.  Bia & her husband had one child: Friedrich (d. after 21 October 937)
2 CONT
2 CONT References:
2 CONT
