2 CONT In English:
2 CONT
2 CONT Mathilde the Holy (b. c.895 in Enger, d. 14 March 968 in Quedlinburg) was the wife of King Heinrich I, and from the Immedinger family.
2 CONT
2 CONT Life:
2 CONT
2 CONT (English Wikipedia notes that the details of St. Mathilde's life come largely from brief mentions in the Res gestae saxonicae sive annalium libri tres of the monastic historian Widukind of Corvey, and from two sacred biographies -
2 CONC  the Vita antiquior and the Vita posterior - written, respectively circa 974 and 1003.
2 CONT
2 CONT Matilda, daughter of the Graf von Westphalia Dietrich (a descendant of Saxon hero Widukind - c.730-807) and his wife Reginlind (or Reinhild). She was brought up in a convent with her grandmother in Herford (where her reputation fo
2 CONC r beauty and virtue attracted the attention of Duke Otto of Saxony, who betrothed her to his son). 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 909, she was married at the Sangerhausen royal palace in Wallhausen to Herzog Heinrich "der Vogelsteller" ("The Fowler") von Sachsen, who later became King of Germany. She bore him three sons and two daughters: 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Konig and Kaiser Otto the Great, 
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Herzog (Duke) Heinrich von Bayern, 
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Bruno, Archbishop of Cologne, 
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Gerberga, who married firstly Duke Giselbert of Lotharingia, and secondly Carolingian King Louis IV of West Francia, and 
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. Hadwig, who married Louis IV's rival and most powerful vassal, Duke Hugh The Great. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 929, she received from her husband as dowry: Quedlinburg, Pohlde, Nordhausen, Grone, and Duderstadt.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of her husband in 936 (he was buried on castle hill in Quedlinburg), she founded a convent. She remained at the court of her son Otto, and was given the tasks of the late King, as well as to educate her daughters t
2 CONC o being higher nobility. These tasks kept Mathilde occupied, and she personally handed over to her granddaughter Mathilde, daughter of Otto the Great, the convent Reichsregentin (997-999) under Otto III. She was the first abbess o
2 CONC f Quedlinburg convent. She also founded other abbeys and monasteries in different locations. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In matters of succession, she apparently preferred her son Heinrich over Otto, which led to such a strong disagreement that for some time she was confined to Enger/Herford. (English Wikipedia says that a cabal of royal advisors ac
2 CONC cused her of weakening the royal treasury in order to pay for her charitable activities.) 
2 CONT
2 CONT In Enger (in Westphalia), she founded the Kanonikerstift in 947. (English Wikipedia notes that she was returned to Otto's court at the urging of his first wife, Anglo-Saxon princess Queen Edith.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Mathilde distinguished herself as the benefactor of the poor, and as a founder of religious orders. (English Wikipedia says that she is celebrated for her devotion to prayer and almsgiving; her first biographer depicted her in a p
2 CONC assage taken from the 6th century Vita of the Frankish Queen Radegund by Venantius Fortunatus as leaving her husband's side in the middle of the night to sneak off to church and pray.)
2 CONT
2 CONT She bore the title of lay abbess of Nivelles and died in her abbey in Quedlinburg. Mathilde was buried next to her husband in the local church. (English Wikipedia says that St. Mathilde founded many religious institutions, includi
2 CONC ng the canonry of Quedlinburg, Saxony-Anhalt, a center of Ottonian ecclesiastical and secular life, and the burial place for St. Mathilde and her husband, and the convent of Nordhausen, Thuringia, which is the likely source of at 
2 CONC least one of her Vitae.) A commemorative plaque was dedicated to her in the Walhalla in Regensburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT Feast Day
2 CONT
2 CONT (English Wikipedia notes that she was later canonized, with her cult largely confined to Saxony and Bavaria.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Catholic: 14 March (this is not a universal feast day, but only for the German speaking regions)
2 CONT
2 CONT Evangelical: 14 March
2 CONT
