1 NOTE 887190584. Kong Henrik I Fuglefangeren OTTOSON av Tyskland(21603) was born about 876.(21604) He was a Hertug in 912 in Sachsen.(21605) He was a Konge between 919 and 936 in Tyskland.(21606) He died 936 av slag.(21607) Stifter av d
2 CONC et sachsiske hus, hertug i Sachsen egter farens død 912. He was married to Mathilde THEODIDRIKSDTR av Ringelheim ?.2.909.
1 NOTE BIOGRAPHY
2 CONT Heinrich was born in 876, the son of Otto, duke of Saxony, and Haduwig von Babenberg. Emperor-Elect Konrad I had designated Heinrich as his successor, which was supported by the Saxon and Franconian nobility. Although he was the f
2 CONC irst non-Frank ruler of the Holy Roman Empire, he adopted Frankish customs, dress and manners.
2 CONT
2 CONT He regarded the support of the nobility as sufficient for his position, and refused to be crowned by the bishops. As duke of Saxony he had shown his independence from Emperor and Church. However when he became emperor he grew clos
2 CONC er to the Church.
2 CONT
2 CONT The most serious problem of his reign was the independence of the higher nobility. This had been caused by the disintegration of monarchical powers in the preceding fifty years. He re-established imperial control over Suabia, Loth
2 CONC aringia and Bavaria. However, in general he allowed the dukes a free hand within their own territories.
2 CONT
2 CONT Several times after 925 he raided the territories of his uncivilized neighbours, the Slavic Wends and the Hungarian Magyars, taking Brandenburg from the Wends, and defeating the Magyars in battle in 933. In the conquered lands he 
2 CONC built fortified cities as military strongholds.
2 CONT
2 CONT His rule restored much of the power and prestige of the monarchy so that, before he died (on 2 July 936), he obtained the recognition of his son Otto as his successor.
1 NOTE He was elected King of Germany in 918. He was Duke of Saxony and is considered one of the Holy Roman Emperors, although he was never officially named as such. His unofficial term was 919 to 936.
1 NOTE He has sometimes been considered as Holy Roman Emperor, Henry I.
1 NOTE Line 5172 from GEDCOM File not recognizable or too long:
2 CONT   NAME Heinrich I "The Fowler" King Of The /GERMANS/
1 NOTE He was elected King of Germany in 918. He was Duke of Saxony and is considered one of the Holy Roman Emperors, although he was never officially named as such. His unofficial term was 919 to 936.
1 NOTE Henrik I "Fågelfängaren" Saxon DYNASTY,       also called LIUDOLFING DYNASTY, ruling house of German kings (Holy Roman emperors) from 919 to 1024. It came to power when the Liudolfing duke of Saxony was elected German king as Henr
2 CONC y I (later called the Fowler), in 919.        Henry I's son and successor, Otto I the Great (king 936-973, western emperor from 962), won a decisive victory over the Magyars at the Battle of Lechfeld near Augsburg in 955 and conti
2 CONC nued the policy, initiated by Henry I, of German expansion into Slavic territory to the east; he also intervened in Italy and, like several of his successors, established control over the papacy. By investing churchmen in Germany 
2 CONC with lands, he laid down a long-lasting framework of support for the crown against the lay nobility. He was crowned emperor by Pope John XII at Rome in 962. He concluded the Privilegium Ottonianum, a treaty that regulated relation
2 CONC s between emperor and pope, and initiated a Holy Roman Empire of the German nation. His son Otto II (973-83) continued his policy, but his grandson Otto III (983-1002) was interested in Italian affairs to the detriment of Germany.
2 CONC         The last member of the line, Henry II (1002-24), turned his attention back to Germany. When Henry II died childless in 1024, another descendant of Henry I, Conrad II, the Salian, was elected king, thus initiating the Salia
2 CONC n dynasty.        
