2 CONT Ben M. Angel notes: Robert I was never a Capet (this was a family name created by Hugh Capet, based on a place name). The name of his family or dynasty was "Robertian" (from Robert Le Fort, his father). He was to the best of my un
2 CONC derstanding never nicknamed "Le Grand". Please indicate the source you are using here if this is contrary to your information.
2 CONT
2 CONT From the English Wikipedia page on Robert I of France: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_I_of_France with excerpts from the French Wikipedia page not included in the English article: http://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_Ier_de_Fr
2 CONC ance
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert I (15 August 866 – 15 June 923), King of Western Francia (922 – 923), was the younger son of Robert the Strong, count of Anjou, and the brother of Odo, who became king of the Western Franks in 888. 
2 CONT
2 CONT West Francia evolved over time into France; under King Odo, the capital was fixed on Paris, a large step in that direction. His family is known as the Robertians.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert was present at the Siege of Paris in 885. Appointed by King Odo ruler of several counties, including the county of Paris, the March of Neustria, and abbot in commendam of many abbeys, Robert also secured the office of Dux F
2 CONC rancorum, a military dignity of high importance. He did not claim the crown of West Francia when his brother died in 898; but recognising the supremacy of the Carolingian king, Charles III the Simple, he was confirmed in his offic
2 CONC es and possessions, after which he continued to defend northern Francia from the attacks of the Norsemen.
2 CONT
2 CONT The peace between the king and his powerful vassal was not seriously disturbed until about 921. The rule of King Charles, and especially his partiality for a certain Comte Hagano had aroused some irritation (he was made into Charl
2 CONC es' principle advisor and favorite - the French Wikipedia page suggests Hagano served merely as a pretext for a premeditated bid for power); and, supported by many of the clergy and by some of the most powerful of the Frankish nob
2 CONC les, Robert took up arms, drove Charles III into Lorraine, and was himself crowned king of the Franks (rex Francorum) at Rheims (crowned by Gautier Archbishop of Sens) on Sunday 30 June 922. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Collecting an army, Charles III marched against the usurper and, on 15 June 923, in the stubborn and sanguinary Battle of Soissons (near the city of the same name), Robert I was killed, according to one tradition in single combat 
2 CONC with his rival (the French Wikipedia page says this was Fulbert, Faubert, or Foubert).
2 CONT
2 CONT (The French Wikipedia continues the story, saying that Robert's son, Hugh the Great, denied Charles III the Simple his victory, preventing him from recovering the crown of Western Francia. The nobility instead elect Raoul Duke of 
2 CONC Burgundy, stepson of Robert I; he was consecrated on 13 July 923.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert was married twice. Through his first wife, Aelis/Adele du Maine, he had two daughters. Each married powerful lay vassals of their father: Emma of France (894–935) to Rudolph, Duke of Burgundy, and Hildebranda (895–931) to H
2 CONC erbert II of Vermandois. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Through his second wife, Béatrice of Vermandois, daughter of Herbert I of Vermandois, he had his only son, Hugh the Great, who was later dux Francorum and father of King Hugh Capet, and a daughter Richilda. He may have had other d
2 CONC aughters.
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
2 CONT This article incorporates text from the Encyclopædia Britannica Eleventh Edition, a publication now in the public domain.
2 CONT
2 CONT King of Western Francia (922–923) 	 Preceded by Charles the Simple 	 Succeeded by Rudolph
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT From the English Wikipedia page on the Battle of Soissons (923): http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Soissons_(923)
2 CONT
2 CONT The Battle of Soissons in 923 was a battle during which King Robert I of France was killed, possibly by King Charles III (by legend in single combat), and the latter was defeated and imprisoned by Rudolph, Duke of Burgundy who suc
2 CONC ceeded Robert I as French monarch.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT French Wikipedia page on Robert I de France: http://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_Ier_de_France
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Ier (né vers 860 - mort le 15 juin 923[1]), fils cadet du comte d'Anjou Robert le Fort, il était le frère d'Eudes et fut élu roi de Francie occidentale en 922.
2 CONT
2 CONT Investi du titre de duc des Francs en 892 par son frère Eudes, ce dernier le nomme à la tête de plusieurs comtés, y compris le comté de Paris et la marche de Neustrie. Robert était également abbé in commendam de plusieurs abbayes.
2 CONC  Il ne revendiqua pas la couronne de France quand son frère mourut en 898, mais il reconnut la prétention du roi carolingien, Charles III le Simple, qui, selon Richer de Reims, le confirma duc des Francs[2],[3]. (Cette précoce tit
2 CONC ulature de "duc des Francs" reste cependant sujette à caution[4]). Robert continua à défendre le nord de la France contre les attaques des Normands.
2 CONT
2 CONT La paix entre Charles III et Robert dura jusqu'en 921. Le clergé et les nobles s'irritèrent contre le roi Charles III qui favorisait particulièrement le comte Haganon (bien qu'il puisse s'agir d'un prétexte politique[5]).
2 CONT
2 CONT Avec l'appui des nobles les plus puissants, Robert attaqua le roi Charles qui s'enfuit en Lorraine. Robert fut couronné roi des Francs à Reims par l'archevêque de Sens, Gautier, le dimanche 30 juin 922. Charles rassembla une armée
2 CONC  et marcha contre Robert, et le 15 juin 923, Robert fut tué par Fulbert, Faubert, Foubert[6] au cours de la bataille de Soissons. Selon certains comme Adalbert, archevêque de Magdebourg, continuateur de la chronique de Réginon de 
2 CONC prüm, Robert périt de la main même de Charles[7].
2 CONT
2 CONT Grâce au courage et au sang-froid du fils de Robert, Hugues le Grand, la victoire revint au clan des Robertiens et le roi Charles III ne put récupérer sa couronne. Les grands du royaume élurent alors Raoul, duc de Bourgogne, beau-
2 CONC fils du roi Robert Ier, comme roi des Francs. Il fut sacré le 13 juillet 923.
2 CONT
2 CONT D'une première épouse nommée Aélis/Adèle du Maine, Robert avait eu :
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Adèle, mariée avant 907 à Herbert II, comte de Vermandois
2 CONT
2 CONT Il épousa ensuite Béatrice de Vermandois, fille d'Herbert Ier, comte de Vermandois et eut de celle-ci :
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Emma, mariée vers 918 avec Raoul, duc de Bourgogne, puis roi de France. 2. Hugues le Grand, duc des Francs, qui fut le père d'Hugues Capet. 3. Richilde.
2 CONT
