2 CONT Robert I, Roi de France (1) M, #105187, d. circa 15 June 923 Last Edited=13 Jun 2008
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert I, Roi de France was the son of Robert of Neustria, Duke of Neustria. (1) He died circa 15 June 923, killed in action. (1), (2)
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert I, Roi de France gained the title of Roi Robert I de France circa 29 June 922. (1), (2)
2 CONT
2 CONT Child of Robert I, Roi de France and Beatrice de Vermandois -1. Adela de France b. c 895 Children of Robert I, Roi de France -1. Emma de France (1) -2. Liégarde de France+ (3) -3. Hugues of Neustria, Comte de Paris+ (2) b. c 895, 
2 CONC d. 9 Jun 956
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT     * BIRTH: BEF 0923, Bourgogne, France
2 CONT     * DEATH: 15 Jun 0923, Soissons, France 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT From the Foundation for Medieval Genealogy page on France Capetian Kings: http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/CAPET.htm#RobertIdied923B
2 CONT
2 CONT ROBERT, son of ROBERT "le Fort" Comte [de Tours], Marquis en Neustrie & his wife --- ([860]-killed in battle near Soissons 15 Jun 923).  
2 CONT
2 CONT "Rodbertum fratrem Odonis regis" is named in the Cartulaire de Saint-Bertin[53].  
2 CONT
2 CONT He was installed as lay abbot of Marmoutier on the death of Hugues l’Abbé in 886[54].  
2 CONT
2 CONT He succeeded his brother in 888 as Marquis en Neustrie, and probably also as Comte de Paris, d'Orléans et de Tours.  After his brother King Eudes captured Poitou in 893, he installed Robert as Comte de Poitou but the latter was ex
2 CONC pelled by Adémar, son of Comte Emenon[55].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Abbot of Saint-Martin de Tours:  "Robert abbé de Saint-Martin" confirmed possessions of Saint-Martin de Tours by charter dated 30 Aug 894[56].  Eudes King of France granted the abbey of Saint-Hilaire de Poitiers to the bishop of P
2 CONC oitiers, at the request of "marchiones…Hrobertus…atque Ademarus", by charter dated to [894][57].  
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of his brother in 898, Robert supported Charles III King of France who seems to have confirmed Robert's position in Neustrie.  "Robert abbé de Saint-Martin" restored "l´hospice de Saint-Clément" to Saint-Martin de 
2 CONC Tours by charter dated 900[58].  He rebelled against the king in 922, triggered by the confiscation of the monastery of Chelles by King Charles from Rothilde (who was the mother-in-law of Robert's son Hugues) in favour of his favo
2 CONC urite Haganon.  
2 CONT
2 CONT He was elected ROBERT I King of France 22 Jun 922, consecrated at Reims by Gauthier Archbishop of Sens.  King Robert was killed fighting ex-King Charles, although his forces won the battle[59].  
2 CONT
2 CONT The necrology of Chartres cathedral records the death "XVII Kal Jul" of "Rotbertus rex"[60].  The necrology of the abbey of Saint-Magloire records the death "XVII Kal Jul" of "Robertus rex"[61].  The necrology of Auxerre cathedral
2 CONC  records that "Rotbertus rex" was killed in battle 15 Jun[62]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT m firstly ---.  
2 CONT
2 CONT The name of Robert's first wife is not known.  However, as King Robert's known wife Béatrix de Vermandois could not have been the mother of his daughter Adela (married to the brother of Béatrix) it is assumed that this earlier mar
2 CONC riage is correct.   
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Europäische Stammtafeln[63], Robert's first wife was named AELIS.  This may be based on the 21 May 907 donation of Rebais abbey to the church of Paris which refers to "comitis Rotberti et Adele comitisse"[64].  Howeve
2 CONC r, as shown below, this is more likely to refer to Robert and his daughter than his wife. 
2 CONT
2 CONT m secondly ([897]) BEATRIX de Vermandois, daughter of HERIBERT I Comte de Vermandois & his wife --- ([880/83]-after 26 Mar 931).  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Historia Francorum Senonensis records that "sororem Herberti" was the wife of "Robertus princeps" who rebelled against Charles III "le Simple" King of France[65].  Guillaume de Jumièges records that Duke Robert's wife was the 
2 CONC sister of Héribert, by whom he had a son Hugues[66].  "Hugo rector Abbatiæ sancti Martini" names "genitoris nostri Rotberti quondam regis ac genitricis nostræ domnæ Beatricis" in his charter dated 26 Mar 931[67].  
2 CONT
2 CONT The marriage date is estimated by Werner on the assumption that the marriage accompanied the political reconciliation between Béatrix's father and Robert's uncle[68].  If the 907 donation mentioned above in fact refers to Robert's
2 CONC  first wife, the marriage would of course have taken place after that date. 
2 CONT
2 CONT King Robert I & his first wife had one child:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.  [ADELA] (before 898-).  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Chronicle of Alberic de Trois-Fontaines refers to "comitis Heriberti gener…Roberti" and in a later passage to the sister of "dux Hugo Cappatus" as the wife of "comitis Heriberti de Peroni, Campanie et Veromandie" but does not 
2 CONC name her[69].  Her origin is confirmed by Flodoard naming "Hugo dux cum nepotibus suis, Heriberti filiis" in 943[70].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Her birth date is estimated from the birth of her first child in [915].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Her name is deduced as follows.  The 21 May 907 donation of Rebais abbey to the church of Paris refers to "comitis Rotberti et Adele comitisse"[71].  Although this phrasing usually indicates husband and wife, Settipani suggests th
2 CONC at the chronology of the life of King Robert's son Hugues (attested as Robert's son by his second wife Béatrix) favours his birth, and therefore his father's second marriage, well before 907, which would mean "Adele" could not hav
2 CONC e been Robert's wife.  This reasoning appears based firstly on Hugues already being married in [914], and secondly on the probability of his having reached the age of majority when he was recognised as duke in 922.  The 907 docume
2 CONC nt is explained as referring to Robert and his elder daughter.  The issue, however, is not beyond doubt, especially if the document in question was misdated.  
2 CONT
2 CONT m (before 21 May 907) HERIBERT II Comte de Vermandois, son of HERIBERT I Comte de Vermandois[-Carolingian] & his wife [Liedgardis] --- ([880]-23 Feb 943, bur Saint Quentin).
2 CONT
2 CONT King Robert I & his [first/second] wife had one child:
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. EMMA (-2 Nov 934).  
2 CONT
2 CONT Flodoard names "Emma regis Rotberti filia" when recording that she obliged Seulf Archbishop of Reims to consecrate her as queen at Reims in 923 in the absence of her husband fighting[72].  Rodulfus Glaber names "Emmam…sororem…magn
2 CONC i Hugonis" as wife of "Rodulfus, Richardi ducis Burgundiæ filius", suggesting that she was instrumental in persuading her brother to support her husband's accession as king[73].  "Emme nostri imperii consortis" and "Emma coniux me
2 CONC a" is named in the charters of "Rodolfus Francorum rex" dated 21 Jun 931 and 1 Jul 931[74].  
2 CONT
2 CONT There is no indication whether Emma was born from her father's first or second marriage.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Flodoard records the death of "Emma regina" at the end of his passage dated 934[75].  The necrology of the abbey of Saint-Germain-des-Prés records the death "IV Non Nov" of "domna Emma regina"[76].  
2 CONT
2 CONT m ([911/19]) RODOLPHE [Raoul] Comte de Bourgogne, son of RICHARD “le Justicier” Duke of Burgundy & his wife Adélais d’Auxerre [Welf] (-Auxerre, Yonne 15/16 Jan 936, bur Abbaye de Sainte-Colombe de Sens).  
2 CONT
2 CONT He succeeded his father in 921 as Duke of Burgundy.  
2 CONT
2 CONT He was elected as RAOUL King of France in 13 Jul 923 at Soissons to succeed his father-in-law, consecrated by Gautier Archbishop of Sens at the Abbey of Saint-Médard de Soissons. 
2 CONT
2 CONT King Robert I & his second wife had one child: 
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. HUGUES ([898]-Dourdan, Essonne 16 Jun 956, bur église de l'Abbaye royale de Saint-Denis).  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Historia Francorum Senonensis names "Hugo Magnus" as son of "Robertus princeps [et] sororem Herberti"[77].  He was installed as HUGUES "le Grand" Duc des Francs in 936. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Hertig Robert av huset Capet och bror till salig kung Odo var av den starkaste Kung Karls vasaller, men han lydde bara kungen När han själv hade nytta av det. Om kung Karl förstod sig på kartan, kund han se Att Roberts valde var L
2 CONC ika stort som hans eget Återstående arvland: Robert var herre över Vermandois, Champagne och Anjou, därtill Greve av Paris och Chartres, Tours och Blois, Orléans och Dreux. Kung Roberts här segrade I ett slagg mot normanderna vid 
2 CONC Soissons, Frankrike, i juni år 923, men kung Robert stupade själv i slaget.
2 CONT
2 CONT Källa: "Normandernas Hövding" av Rune Pär Olofsson.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Robert I (född cirka 865, död 15 juni 923) var en frankisk robertingisk kung över västfrankiska riket . Son till Robert den starke, hertig av Anjou; bror till Odo, hertig av Paris.
2 CONT
2 CONT Genom sin bror Odo erhöll Robert fler grevskap inklusive grevskapet Paris och blev också utsedd till abbot in commendam vid många kloster och Robert själv säkrade posten som hertig av frankerna, en viktig militär titel. När hans b
2 CONC ror dog 898 gjorde inte Robert anspråk på broderns titel som kung, utan accepterade den karolingiske tronpretendenten Karl den enfaldiges överhöghet. Detta gjorde han kunde behålla sina poster och egendomar och fortsätta att bekäm
2 CONC pa attackerna från vikingar.
2 CONT
2 CONT Friden mellan kungen och hans mäktiga rival varade ända till 921 då Karls styre, och särkskilt hans svaghet för en viss Hagano, väckte sådan irritation hos prästerskapet och många av de mäktigaste adelsmännen att Robert tog till v
2 CONC apen och fördrev Karl till Lothringen. Robert kröntes till kung i Reims 28 juni 922. Karl ställde dock upp en armé och gick till motangrepp och i närheten av Soissons 15 juni 923 drabbade rivalerna samman. Robert blev dödad i stri
2 CONC den, enligt traditionen i en kamp man mot man med sin rival.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert efterlämnade en son Hugo den store vars son Hugo Capet kom att överta tronen från den karolingiska ätten.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Foi representante de Odo (seu irmão) em de diversos condados, incluindo o condado de Paris, abade superior de muitas abadias, e Duque dos Francos, uma dignidade militar de importância elevada.
2 CONT
2 CONT Não reivindicou a Coroa de França quando seu irmão morreu em 898 mas, reconhecendo a supremacia do rei carolíngio, Carlos III, o Simples, teve confirmadas as suas posses territoriais, tendo, depois, continuado a defender o norte d
2 CONC e França dos ataques dos Normandos.
2 CONT
2 CONT A paz entre o Rei e seu poderoso vassalo não foi perturbada seriamente até aproximadamente 921. As ações de Carlos e, em especial a sua parcialidade a favor de um tal de Hagano, despertaram-lhe alguma impopularidade e, apoiado por
2 CONC  muitos do clérigos e por diversos dos mais poderosos nobres franceses, Roberto se fez coroar Rei francês em Reims, no dia 29 de junho de 922.
2 CONT
2 CONT Comandando um poderoso exército, Carlos marchou de encontro ao usurpador, e no dia 15 de junho de 923, em uma batalha sanguinária perto de Soissons, Roberto foi morto, de acordo com a tradição, em combate com seu rival.
2 CONT
2 CONT Roberto I deixou um filho, Hugo, o Grande, Duque dos francos, e seu neto, Hugo Capeto, futuro Rei da França e fundador da Dinastia dos Capetos.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT From the Celtic Casimir online family tree: http://www.celtic-casimir.com/webtree/2/2516.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert I DE BOURGOGNE King of the West Franks 574,616 Born: Abt 866, Bourgogne, Marne, France  Married (2): Before 898, Paris, Seine, France 605  Married (3): Not Married  Died: 15 Jun 923, Soissons, Aisne, Picardy, France  Ancest
2 CONC ral File Number: 9G81-BX. 
2 CONT
2 CONT General Notes: Marquess of Neustria 898-923 Western Frankish King 922-923 In 922, Robert succeeded Charles III, the Simple, to the kingdom of France, but died the next year. On his death, his son-in-law Rudolph took the government
2 CONC . France France (Duke Of France Of House Of Capet Ii) November 29, 995 
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage Information: Robert married Alpaidia CAROLING Princess of France, daughter of Ludwig (Louis I "Le Pieux") CAROLING King of the Frank, Holy Roman Emperor and Princess Ermengarde DE HESBAYE. (Alpaidia CAROLING Princess of F
2 CONC rance was born in 818.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage Information: Robert also married Bйatrice DE VERMANDOIS Queen of France, daughter of Cte Herbert I DE VERMANDOIS and Countess Bertha (Bйatrice) DE MORVOIS of France, before 898 in Paris, Seine, France 605. (Bйatrice DE VE
2 CONC RMANDOIS Queen of France was born about 880 in Vermandois, Neustria and died in Mar 931 in Europe.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage Information: Robert also married Adele DE FRANCE Queen of the Franks. (Adele DE FRANCE Queen of the Franks died 758.)
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Robert I of France From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert I (August 15, 866 – June 15, 923), king of West Francia (922 – 923), was the younger son of Robert the Strong, count of Anjou, and the brother of Odo, who became king of the Western Franks in 888. West Francia evolved over 
2 CONC time into France; under Odo, the capital was fixed on Paris, a large step in that direction. His family is known as the Robertians. He was present at the Siege of Paris in 885. Appointed by Odo ruler of several counties, including
2 CONC  the county of Paris, and abbot in commendam of many abbeys, Robert also secured the office of Dux Francorum, a military dignity of high importance. He did not claim the crown of West Francia when his brother died in 898; but reco
2 CONC gnising the supremacy of the Carolingian king, Charles the Simple, he was confirmed in his offices and possessions, after which he continued to defend northern Francia from the attacks of the Norsemen. The peace between the king a
2 CONC nd his powerful vassal was not seriously disturbed until about 921. The rule of Charles, and especially his partiality for a certain Hagano, had aroused some irritation; and, supported by many of the clergy and by some of the most
2 CONC  powerful of the Frankish nobles, Robert took up arms, drove Charles into Lorraine, and was himself crowned king of the Franks (rex Francorum) at Rheims on June 29, 922. Collecting an army, Charles marched against the usurper and,
2 CONC  on June 15 923, in a stubborn and sanguinary battle near Soissons, Robert was killed, according to one tradition in single combat with his rival. Robert was married twice. Through his first wife, Aelis, a descendant of Charlemagn
2 CONC e, he had two daughters. Each married powerful lay vassals of their father: Emma of France (894–935) to Rudolph, Duke of Burgundy, and Hildebranda (895–931) to Herbert II of Vermandois. Through his second wife, Béatrice of Vermand
2 CONC ois, daughter of Herbert I of Vermandois, he had his only son, Hugh the Great, who was later dux Francorum and father of King Hugh Capet, and a daughter Richilda. He may have had other daughters.
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
2 CONT This article incorporates text from the Encyclopædia Britannica Eleventh Edition, a publication now in the public domain.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Robert I (August 15, 866 – June 15, 923), king of West Francia (922 – 923), was the younger son of Robert the Strong, count of Anjou, and the brother of Odo, who became king of the Western Franks in 888. West Francia evolved over 
2 CONC time into France; under Odo, the capital was fixed on Paris, a large step in that direction. His family is known as the Robertians.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was present at the Siege of Paris in 885. Appointed by Odo ruler of several counties, including the county of Paris, and abbot in commendam of many abbeys, Robert also secured the office of Dux Francorum, a military dignity of 
2 CONC high importance. He did not claim the crown of West Francia when his brother died in 898; but recognising the supremacy of the Carolingian king, Charles the Simple, he was confirmed in his offices and possessions, after which he c
2 CONC ontinued to defend northern Francia from the attacks of the Norsemen.
2 CONT
2 CONT The peace between the king and his powerful vassal was not seriously disturbed until about 921. The rule of Charles, and especially his partiality for a certain Hagano, had aroused some irritation; and, supported by many of the cl
2 CONC ergy and by some of the most powerful of the Frankish nobles, Robert took up arms, drove Charles into Lorraine, and was himself crowned king of the Franks (rex Francorum) at Rheims on June 29, 922. Collecting an army, Charles marc
2 CONC hed against the usurper and, on June 15, 923, in a stubborn and sanguinary battle near Soissons, Robert was killed, according to one tradition in single combat with his rival.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert was married twice. Through his first wife, Aelis, a descendant of Charlemagne, he had two daughters. Each married powerful lay vassals of their father: Emma of France (894–935) to Rudolph, Duke of Burgundy, and Hildebranda 
2 CONC (895–931) to Herbert II of Vermandois. Through his second wife, Béatrice of Vermandois, daughter of Herbert I of Vermandois, he had his only son, Hugh the Great, who was later dux Francorum and father of King Hugh Capet, and a dau
2 CONC ghter Richilda. He may have had other daughters.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://pt.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roberto_I_de_França
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT From http://www.rpi.edu/~holmes/Hobbies/Genealogy/ps03/ps03_442.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert did not claim the crown on his brother's death in 898, but recognized the Carolingian king, Charles III; Robert continued to defend northern France from Norman attacks as "duke of the Franks"; about 921 he gathered support 
2 CONC for his claim, and drove Charles into Lorraine; 06-29-922 he was crowned at Rheims and the next year faced Charles' attempt to oust him, during which "in a stubborn and sanguinary battle near Soissons, Robert was killed, according
2 CONC  to one tradition, in single combat with his rival." {-Encyclopaedia Britannica, 1956 Ed., 19:346} 
2 CONT
2 CONT References: [AR7],[Weis1]
2 CONT --------------------
