2 CONT Robert 1 of France was born on August 15, 866 in Angers, Maine-et-Loire, Pays de la Loire, France. He was the son of Robert the Strong, count of Anjou (820-866) and Adelaide of Tours (818-866).
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert's first wife was Aelis. By her he had two daughters: Adele of France (c. 887–aft. March 931) to Herbert II of Vermandois.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert married secondly, c. 890, Béatrice of Vermandois, daughter of Herbert I of Vermandois. Together they had : Emma of France (894–935), married to Rudolph, Duke of Burgundy, Hugh the Great,(898-) who was later dux Francorum. H
2 CONC ugh was the father of Hugh Capet, King of the Franks.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert I of France (866–923) was the king of West Francia from 922 to 923. Before his succession to the kingdom he was Count of Poitiers, Count of Paris and Marquis of Neustria and Orléans. He succeeded the Carolingian king Charle
2 CONC s the Simple, who in 898 had succeeded Robert's brother Odo. Robert and Odo's family is known as the Robertians.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert was present at the Siege of Paris in 885. He was appointed by Odo as the ruler of several counties, including the county of Paris, and abbot in commendam of many abbeys. Robert also secured the office of Dux Francorum, a mi
2 CONC litary dignity of high importance. He did not claim the crown of West Francia when his brother died in 898; instead recognized the supremacy of the Carolingian king, Charles the Simple. Charles then confirmed Robert in his offices
2 CONC  and possessions, after which he continued to defend northern Francia from the attacks of the Norsemen. Robert defeated a large band of Norse in the Loire Valley in 921, and the defeated invaders converted to Christianity and sett
2 CONC led near Nantes. Robert was succeeded as king by his son-in-law Rudolph, Count of Burgundy, also known as Raoul.
2 CONT
