1 NOTE BIOGRAPHY
2 CONT Robert was born after September 866, the son of Rutpert IV, Graf in Wormsgau, duke of Francia, and Aelis de Tours. Her was the brother of Eudes, who became king of the Western Franks in 888. West Francia evolved over time into Fra
2 CONC nce; under Eudes, the capital was fixed on Paris. His family is known as the Robertians.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert was present at the Siege of Paris in 885. Appointed by Eudes the ruler of several counties, including the county of Paris, and abbot _in commendam_ of many abbeys, Robert also secured the office of _Dux Francorum,_ a milita
2 CONC ry dignity of high importance. He did not claim the crown of West Francia when his brother died in 898; but he recognised the supremacy of the Carolingian king, Charles 'the Simple', and was confirmed in his offices and possession
2 CONC s, after which he continued to defend northern Francia from the attacks of the Norsemen.
2 CONT
2 CONT The peace between the king and his powerful vassal was not seriously disturbed until about 921. The rule of Charles, and especially his partiality for a certain Hagano, had aroused some irritation. Supported by many of the clergy 
2 CONC and by some of the most powerful of the Frankish nobles, Robert took up arms, drove Charles into Lorraine, and was himself crowned king of the Franks (_Rex Francorum_) at Reims on 29 June 922. Collecting an army, Charles marched a
2 CONC gainst the usurper, and on 15 June 923, in a bloody battle near Soissons, Robert was killed, according to one tradition in single combat with his rival.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert was married twice. Through his first wife Aelis he had a daughter Adela who married his vassal Heribert II, comte de Vermandois. Through his second wife Beatrice he had his only son Hugues 'the Great', who was later _Dux Fr
2 CONC ancorum_ and father of King Hugues Capet. Robert's daughter Emma married Raoul, duke of Burgundy, who succeeded as king of France after Robert's death.
1 NOTE Comte Robert I of Paris (892-893), 
2 CONT Elu Roi de France (922), tué à Soisson contre le roi légitime Charles III le simple.
1 NOTE From THE RUFUS PARKS PEDIGREE by Brian J.L. Berry, chart pg 56.
2 CONT
2 CONT Page 57:
2 CONT
2 CONT 9.     ROBERT I, d.923; younger son who inherited from his older brother Eudes(Odo) 898 all the Robertian lands between the Seine and the Loire; swore fealty to the Carolingian Charles III the Simple; the grandson of CHARLES THE B
2 CONC ALD.  From 911 on, ROBERT'S role became more decisive; his defeat of the Normans at Chartres in that year paved the way for the Treaty of Saint- Clair-sur-epte, by which Charles III assigned to the Normans a specific territory (No
2 CONC rmandy), under Duke ROLLO.  But dessension arose between ROBERT and Charles and in 922 the former, with his son-in-law, Duke Rudolph of Burgandy, headed a revolt.  The insurgents elected ROBERT king of Reims 29 June 922 and he was
2 CONC  crowned by Walter, Archbishop of Sens.  Charles however gathered an army in Loraine and in a battle near Soissons 15 June 923, ROBERT was killed.  He mar. (2) Beatrix by whom he had HUGH MAGNUS.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: "Ancestral Roots of Sixty Colonists" by Frederick Lewis Weis.
