1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT https://www.geni.com/people/R%C3%A9gnier-Reginar-III-comte-de-Hainaut/6000000001744825392?through=6000000002043195252 was not a documented husband of https://www.geni.com/people/Mathilde-d-Arleux-d-Equisheim/6000000000536956012?th
2 CONC rough=6000000001744825392 or father of https://www.geni.com/people/Ermengarde-de-Hainaut-dame-de-Valenciennes/6000000000536954974?through=6000000001744825392.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT *https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reginar_III,_Count_of_Hainaut
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Reginar III ''' (c. 920 – 973) was Count of Hainaut from approximately 940 until his exile in 958. <br> 
2 CONT He was the son of Reginar II, Count of Hainaut.
2 CONT
2 CONT He took part in the rebellion of his uncle Gilbert, Duke of Lorraine. When Gilbert was killed in 939, Reginar had to pledge fealty to King Otto the Great.''[citation needed]'' <br> He then allied himself with King Louis IV of Fran
2 CONC ce, but King Otto sent duke Hermann of Swabia to quell the rebels in 944.
2 CONT
2 CONT Otto appointed Conrad the Red as duke of Lotharingia, who tried to diminish the power of Reginar. However, when Conrad rose against Otto, Reginar supported him. In an anarchic situation, Reginar appropriated the dowry of Gerberga 
2 CONC of Saxony, Otto's sister and mother of the French king, and also church property.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 957, Bruno, Archbishop of Cologne, who had also been appointed duke of Lotharingia, restored order and defeated Reginar. <br> As Reginar refused to submit, he was exiled to Bohemia, where he died before 973.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT He fathered two sons:
2 CONT
2 CONT *Reginar IV, Count of Mons
2 CONT *Lambert I of Leuven
2 CONT
2 CONT --------
2 CONT
2 CONT -http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/HAINAUT.htm#ReginarIIIdied973
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.         '''REGINAR''' [III] ([920]-973).  The Annales Hanoniæ name "Raginerus" as son of "Raginerus [filius Ragineri dicti Longi-colli]"[33].  "Otto…rex" confirmed the possession of Kloster Süsteren by Prüm abbey by charter dat
2 CONC ed 1 Jun 949, signed by "Cuonradus dux, Herimannus dux, Hezzo comes, Godefridus comes, Rudolfus comes, Reginherus comes"[34].  He succeeded his father as Comte de Hainaut, although the date he took control of the county is not kno
2 CONC wn.  "Ragineri comitis…" signed the charter dated to [936/57] under which "Adhelardus et conjux mea Fulcuera" donated a serf to the abbey of Saint-Ghislain[35].  In 956, Reginar seized Brussels and the surrounding areas which had 
2 CONC been the dower of Gerberge Queen of France on her first marriage to his uncle Giselbert Duke of Lotharingia.  King Lothaire, together with Bruno Archbishop of Köln, invaded Count Reginar's territory, captured his fort on the Chier
2 CONC  and took his family prisoner, releasing them only after Reginar returned the land which he had taken[36].  The Gesta Episcoporum Cameracensium records that Reginar was banished to Bohemia in [958] by Archbishop Bruno[37], but was
2 CONC  later reinstated in Hainaut by Otto II King of Germany[38].  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' ADELA, daughter of --- (-961).  The Annales Hanoniæ record the death in 961 of "Adela comitissa Montensis…uxor Ragineri comitis"[39], although as this passage follows closely on those dealing with the exploits of "Ragineru
2 CONC s Longi-colli" it is unclear to which Count Reginar the Annales are referring at this point.  She is named in secondary sources as [https://www.geni.com/people/Adelaide-von-Dagsbourg/6000000009437912210 Adela von Dachsburg], daugh
2 CONC ter of [https://www.geni.com/people/Hugo-V-count-in-Nordgau/6000000003827280792 Hugo] [V] Graf von Dachsburg und Egisheim [Alsace] & his wife [https://www.geni.com/people/Hildegarde-de-Ferrette/6000000002187888470 Hildegard] ---, 
2 CONC but the primary source which confirms that this is correct has not yet been identified.  
2 CONT
2 CONT   Comte Reginar [III] & his wife had two children:
2 CONT *a)         REGINAR [IV] (after 947-1013). .. m ([996]) HEDWIGE [Avoie] de France
2 CONT *b)         LAMBERT [I] de Hainaut ([950]-killed in battle Florennes 12 Sep 1015).  The Annales Hanoniæ name "Lambertus…comes Lovaniensis" as son of "Rignerii comitis Montensis"[45].  " He established himself as LAMBERT [I] "le Ba
2 CONC rbu" Comte de Louvain.  See  COMTES de LOUVAIN. 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Ben M. Angel note:
2 CONT
2 CONT Original death location was mentioned as Pilsen within the present Czech Republic. However, Pilsen as a city was not founded for more than another 250 years. As such, I've removed Pilsen as the death location, and left it within t
2 CONC he Herzogtum Bohmen (technically part of the entity that would become known as the Holy Roman Empire in the 12th century, but very much an autonomous state within the Empire).
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Régnier III fut un comte de Hainaut et de 940 à 958. Il était fils de Régnier II, comte de Hainaut.
2 CONT
