2 CONT
2 CONT The Origins of the Warenne Family < [https://historytheinterestingbits.com/2021/05/22/the-origins-of-the-warenne-family/ link] > 
2 CONT
2 CONT > In 1782 Rev John Watson wrote a two-volume biography of the Warenne earls of Surrey for Sir George Warren, to demonstrate the knight’s descent from the Warenne earls. Watson tried to establish the origins of the Warennes, but hi
2 CONC s family trees are confusing, and his sources are not cited. He claimed that the Warennes were '''descended from Herfastus through a daughter who married Walter de Saint Martin'''. This daughter supposedly gave birth to William de
2 CONC  Warenne, Earl of Varenne in Normandy, who in turn married a daughter of Rafe de Torta, a Danish nobleman who was protector of Normandy in the time of Duke Richard I. This William de Warenne was, supposedly, the father of William 
2 CONC I de Warenne. Although there are no sources mentioned, it seems likely that Rev Watson got his information from the chronicler Robert de Torigny. There was no mention of Rodulf, who is clearly identified in the cartulary of the Ho
2 CONC ly Trinity of Rouen as being the father of William de Warenne and his older brother: ‘ filii eorum Rodulfus et Willelmus’.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Robert de Torigny and the family of Gunnor, Duchess of Normandy'' by Todd A. Farmerie [Modified from an article which appeared in Dec 1996 on soc.genealogy.medieval] < [https://www.kent-opc.org/opcfamilydata/getperson.php?person
2 CONC ID=I15159&tree=tree1 link] >
2 CONT
2 CONT > The parentage of Gunnor and her siblings is unknown. While some sources call her father Herfastus, this was in fact the name of her brother. She has also been claimed as daughter of the Danish royal family, but there is no evide
2 CONC nce for this, and the context of her coming to the attention of Richard I and the family's subsequent rise to power militates against her being a royal daughter. Douglas argued (in a 1944 English Historical Review article on the f
2 CONC amily of William Fitz Osbern), based on the donations of brother Arfast to the monastery of St. Pere, that the root of the family was in the Cotetin region of Normandy, but van Houts has suggested that the Cotetin land was granted
2 CONC  to Arfast, rather than inherited by him. Thus we are left with the more ambiguous statements of Torigny and others that she was a member of a Norman family of Danish origins. <BR>
2 CONT
2 CONT > Wevia, the only other sister of Gunnor named by Torigny, married Osbern de Bolbec (who is otherwise unknown to history). They had at least two sons: Walter Giffard, ancestor of the English Giffard/Gifford families, and also, thr
2 CONC ough a daughter, of the Clare family; and Godfrey, whose son William de Arques had two daughters and co-heiresses.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT NOTES AND QUERIES: < [https://www.gutenberg.org/files/29664/29664-h/29664-h.htm link] > No. 218. Saturday, December 31. 1853 Price Fourpence Stamped Edition 5d.
2 CONT
2 CONT >Osbernus filius Herfasti (Vol. viii., p. 515.).—In reply to the Query of Mr. Sansom, "Whether Osborn de Crespon, the brother of the Duchess of Normandy, had a brother of the same name?" I beg to reply that there appears to be dis
2 CONC tinct evidence that he had; for in a grant of lands by Richard II., Duke of Normandy, who died in 1026, to the monks of St. Michael, there are, along with the signatures of his son Richard and several other witnesses, those of Osb
2 CONC ernus frater Comitissæ, and Osbernus filius Arfast (Lobineau, tom. ii. p. 97.). One of those may probably have become Abbot of S. Evroult. No doubt Mr. Sansom is well aware that one of the same family was Osborn, Bishop of Exeter.
2 CONC  He was a son of Osborn de Crespon, and brother of the Earl of Hereford, premier peer of England. In 1066 he forbad the monks to be buried in the cloisters of their monasteries; but they resisted his injunction, and, on an appeal 
2 CONC to the Pope, obtained a decision against him (Mabillon). For an eulogium on him see Godwin, De presul. Angl. He died in 1104, and was buried in the cathedral at Exeter. … <BR>
2 CONT
2 CONT >I think there can be little doubt that Herfastus "the Dane" was the father of Gunnora, wife of Rich. I., Duke of Normandy; of Aveline, wife of Osbernus de Bolebec, Lord of Bolbec and Count of Longueville; and of Weira, wife of Tu
2 CONC rolf de Pont Audomere. The brother of these three sisters was another Herfastus, Abbot of St. Evrau; who was the father of Osbernus de Crepon, Steward of the Household, and Sewer to the Conqueror.
2 CONT
