2 CONT Foulque I "le Roux" Comte d´Anjou names "Ingelgerio genitore meo…" in a charter dated to [929/30][34].
2 CONT
2 CONT There is doubt whether the other references to Ingelger which follow accurately reflect the historical reality of his life. The Gesta Consulum Andegavorum names "Ingelgerius" as son of "Tertullus nobilem dux" & his wife[35]. The G
2 CONC esta Consulum Andegavorum names "Ingelgerius…iuvenis filius Tortulfi"[36]. The Chronico Turonensi names "Ingelgerius comes Andergavensis" as "nepos Hugonis Ducis Burgundiæ"[37].
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles II "le Chauve" King of the West Franks gave him "a piece of a fief in the castle of Landonense".
2 CONT
2 CONT He was installed as viscount of the city of Orléans by Louis II "le Bègue" King of the West Franks, and appointed royal representative at Tours. The Historia Comitum Andegavorum records that Louis II "le Bègue" King of the West Fr
2 CONC anks, therefore dated to [877/79], granted "dimidium Andegavis comitatum" to "Ingelgerius"[38].
2 CONT
2 CONT The Gesta Consulum Andegavorum records that Ingelger died from "focositatem, phthisim et hydropisim"[39].
2 CONT
2 CONT The Gesta Consulum Andegavorum records the burial of "Ingelgerius" at "ecclesia beati Martini Castrinovi"[40].
2 CONT
2 CONT m ADELAIS, niece of ADALHARD Bishop of Tours and of RAINO Bishop of Angers, daughter of ---.
2 CONT
2 CONT Comte Ingelger & his wife had one son: 1. Foulques I Le Roux (b. c.888 d. August 941/942, buried Chateauneuf, eglise St-Martin, became first Comte d'Anjou 929-941/42)
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Wikipedia page on Ingelger: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ingelger
2 CONT
2 CONT Ingelger or Ingelgarius (died 888) was a Frankish nobleman, who stands at the head of the Plantagenet dynasty. Later generations of his family believed he was the son of Tertullus (Tertulle) and Petronilla.[1] He was born in Renne
2 CONC s.
2 CONT
2 CONT Around 877 he inherited his father Tertullus's lands in accordance with the Capitulary of Quierzy which Charles the Bald had issued. His father's holdings from the king included Château-Landon in beneficium, and he was a casatus i
2 CONC n the Gâtinais and Francia. Contemporary records refer to Ingelger as a miles optimus, a great military man.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Later family tradition makes his mother a relative of Hugh the Abbot,[3] an influential counselor of both Louis II and Louis III of France, from whom he received preferment. By Louis II Ingelger was appointed viscount of Orléans, 
2 CONC which city was under the rule of its bishops at the time.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT At Orléans Ingelger made a matrimonial alliance with one of the leading families of Neustria, the lords of Amboise. He married Adelais, whose maternal uncles were Adalard, Archbishop of Tours, and Raino, Bishop of Angers. Later In
2 CONC gelger was appointed prefect (military commander) at Tours, then ruled by Adalard.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT At some point Ingelger was appointed Count of Anjou, at a time when the county stretched only as far west as the Mayenne River. Later sources credit his appointment to his defence of the region from Vikings,[4] but modern scholars
2 CONC  have been more likely to see it as a result of his wife's influential relatives.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT He was buried in the church of Saint-Martin at Châteauneuf-sur-Sarthe. He was succeeded by his son Fulk the Red.[4]
2 CONT
