2 FILE https://media.geni.com/p13/14/a6/8d/5a/53444860fc2f814e/marchiennes_original.jpg?hash=e3ba094ab0c970260efc41d0fc6a618142d3af9e67f4a87ecac2254bb5170129.1798099199
2 FORM jpg
2 TITL House of Ostrevent (conjectured)
2 _PRIM Y
2 NOTE A note about this blazon. First, there is absolutely no evidence that this coat of arms was used by anyone in the family of Hucbald comte de Ostrevent. In order to better follow this short lineage based on onomastics, I’ve determi
3 CONC ned through a bit of logic that this blazon “could have been used”, based on the following:
3 CONT 1. I’ve found no evidence of a blazon used for Gouy or Ostrevent in this time period, remember that the age of heraldry did not begin until the late 12th century so there is no evidence of any coat of arms used prior to that date.
3 CONT 2. Based on the following entomology the name Gouy was not used until 1429: 877: Villam Gaugiacum in pago Ostrebanno (Gouy en pays d'Ostrevent) - (Marchiennes cartulary), 1102: Goyacum, 13th century: Goy d'Alès Belonne, 1429: Gouy
3 CONC , 1613: Gouy-en-Ostrevant, 1789: Gouy-sous-Bellonne (unchanged since).
3 CONT 3. The Abbey of Marchiennes referred to was founded in 630 by Adelbard of Douai. The geographic location of Douai is north of Canbrai midway between it and Lille in the area later referred to as Ostrevent.
3 CONT 4. A photo from the Cartulaire Abbaye de Marchiennes, Fig 1, shows this blazon at the top of the image (it is noted that this cartulary may not have been created until 1604 so s not contemporary but historical).
3 CONT 5. Referring to Cawley at fmgMedlands “Vanderkindere suggests that "Gaugeium" was Gouy-sous-Bellone, in the area of Ostrevent, not Gouy-en-Arrouaise which is near Cambrai.
3 CONT 6. Fig 2 shows the current civic blazon per Wikipedia used by the commune of Gouy-sous-Bellone. It is noted that there is a resemblance in that the same sable carbuncle on a gold (or) background is used, overlain by a chief azure 
3 CONC with three garbs of wheat, or. It is not uncommon for a current commune to use the ancient arms of the “first lord”, and it is even less uncommon for the arms to have become a variant of those arms retaining elements of the origin
3 CONC al.
3 CONT 7. So I’ve come to the conclusion that Fig 3 could have been born by the ancient lords of Ostrevent. I note that there is such an image in the archive at Geneanet.
