1 OCCU A Varangian chieftain of the Rus'
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ORIGINS: There is no clear evidence supporting Rurik's origins beyond the statement that he was a "varagian" ([https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Varangians '''Varangian]), but even the meaning of this word is unclear, 
2 CONC he may have come from any of the Scandinavian or Baltic countries. This area of northern Europe was very fluid at the time with no clear definitions or national boundaries. Rurik's genetic heritage, N1c1, is typical of Finnish peo
2 CONC ple however this does not mean that he himself was from the area now known as Finland (for a detailed discussion [https://www.geni.com/discussions/184111?msg=1232070 '''read along here]).
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------
2 CONT
2 CONT The earliest generations of the so-called [https://www.geni.com/projects/Rurik-Dynasty-862-1598/83 '''Rurikid] family are reconstructed solely on the basis of the sparse information in the "Povest' vremennykh let" or 'Tale of the 
2 CONC Years of Time', better known as the [http://www.mgh-bibliothek.de/dokumente/a/a011458.pdf ''' Russian Primary Chronicle] and also sometimes known as Nestor´s Chronicle.  As pointed out by Franklin & Shepard, the extant manuscripts
2 CONC  of the Primary Chronicle which date from the 12th century should not be taken at face value as they must have been compiled from patchy sources of information.  It is likely that the compilers exaggerated the role of Rurik's fami
2 CONC ly in the 9th and 10th centuries, in order to establish a lengthy, credible history for the Russian principalities which were flourishing by the 12th century.  Any reconstructed genealogy of the Rurikid dynasty during the early ye
2 CONC ars, as well as all dates and even names, must be viewed with caution.
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------
2 CONT
2 CONT The connection of Umila to Rurik as his mother and Efanda as his wife is controversial and based only upon the writings of [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vasily_Tatishchev '''Vasily Tatishchev'''].
2 CONT
2 CONT Tatishchev's work, published after his death in 1750, is purported to be based on the [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ioachim_Chronicle '''Ioachim Chronicle'''] an ancient manuscript which he discovered but was then lost. Modern thi
2 CONC nking is that the nature of the Ioachim Chronicle is highly dubious.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------------
