2 CONC rince of Magyars
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zoltán_of_Hungary  '''Zoltán'''][1] (Hungarian pronunciation: [ˈzoltaːn]; c. 880 or 903 – c. 950), also Zolta,[1][2][3][4] Zsolt,[1][2] Solt[1][2] or Zaltas[2] is mentioned in the Gesta Hungarorum as
2 CONC  the third Grand Prince of the Hungarians who succeeded his father Árpád around 907. Although modern historians tend to deny this report on his reign, because other chronicles do not list him among the Hungarian rulers, there is c
2 CONC onsensus that even if Zoltán never ascended the throne, all monarchs ruling in Hungary from the House of Árpád after around 955 were descended from him.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Zoltán of Hungary From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT Zoltán of Hungary Grand Prince of the Magyars 
2 CONT Predecessor 	Árpád 
2 CONT Successor 	Fajsz 
2 CONT Spouse 	Unnamed daughter of Menmarót
2 CONT Issue Taksony 
2 CONT Full name Zoltán or Zaltas or Zolta 
2 CONT Father 	Árpád 
2 CONT Mother 	Unknown 
2 CONT Born 	 ? 
2 CONT Died 	c. 947
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Zoltán (? – 947), also known as Zaltas, according to the mediaeval chronicles, was the third Grand Prince of the Magyars.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the youngest (fifth) son of Árpád[1]. The Gesta Hungarorum names Zolta as the successor of his father, and also mentions that he married a daughter of Menmarót, the local military leader in the region of Bihar (Romanian: Bi
2 CONC harea) at the time of the Hungarian conquest (Hungarian: Honfoglalás).
2 CONT
2 CONT Latest researches questioned his position as a ruler and prefer other sons of Árpád.
2 CONT
2 CONT In any case, his leadership meant much less than with previous leaders, as during his time, tribal leaders had most of the powers. In this era, the raids of Hungarians (kalandozások) were common in much of Europe.
2 CONT
2 CONT Child
2 CONT *Taksony, Grand Prince of the Magyars (? – before 973)
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT *     * Korai Magyar Történeti Lexikon (9-14. század), főszerkesztő: Kristó, Gyula, szerkesztők: Engel, Pál és Makk, Ferenc (Akadémiai Kiadó, Budapest, 1994)
2 CONT *     * Kristó, Gyula: A Kárpát-medence és a magyarság régmúltja (1301-ig) (Szegedi Középkortörténeti Könyvtár, Szeged, 1993)
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT *    1. ^ Kōnstantinos Porhyrogennētos: De Administrando Imperio.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------------
2 CONT Zolta [szerkesztés]
2 CONT
2 CONT A Wikipédiából, a szabad enciklopédiából. Zolta (másnéven: Solt, Zsolt vagy Zoltán) Árpád legkisebb fia volt azok közül, akiknek nevét a krónikák megőriztek számunkra. A későbbi krónikák szerint ő volt Árpád utóda, mint nagyfejede
2 CONC lem, azonban ez nagy valószínűséggel nem igaz, azért gondolták ezt a későbbi krónikások, mert fia, Taksony nagyfejedelem lett, és a későbbi primogenitúra elv alapján akkor „nyilvánvalóan” Zoltának is annak kellett lennie. [1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Forrás: http://hu.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zolta
2 CONT -----------------------
2 CONT English: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zolt%C3%A1n_of_Hungary
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Árpád was the son of Grand Prince Álmos (Grand Prince of the Magyars), leader of the Hungarian tribal federation; his mother's name and descent is unknown[1].
2 CONT
2 CONT The emergence of the Magyar tribes and their leaders is a specific period in the history of the Hungarian people that refers to the time starting from when the Magyars were considered a people separate and identifiable from other 
2 CONC Ugric speakers (1000-500 BC) up until their occupation and settlement of the Carpathian Basin around 896 AD (Hungarian: Honfoglalás)[2].
2 CONT
2 CONT In 894, Árpád and Kurszán negotiated together with the representatives of the Byzantine emperor, Leo VI the Wise the terms under which the confederation of the Magyar tribes was willing to assist the Byzantine Empire against Emper
2 CONC or Simeon I of Bulgaria.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Zoltán (? – 947), also known as Zaltas, according to the mediaeval chronicles, was the third Grand Prince of the Magyars.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the youngest (fifth) son of Árpád[1]. The Gesta Hungarorum names Zolta as the successor of his father, and also mentions that he married a daughter of Menmarót, the local military leader in the region of Bihar (Romanian: Bi
2 CONC harea) at the time of the Hungarian conquest (Hungarian: Honfoglalás).
2 CONT
2 CONT Latest researches questioned his position as a ruler and prefer other sons of Árpád.
2 CONT
2 CONT In any case, his leadership meant much less than with previous leaders, as during his time, tribal leaders had most of the powers. In this era, the raids of Hungarians (kalandozások) were common in much of Europe.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Zoltán (? – 947), also known as Zaltas, according to the mediaeval chronicles, was the third Grand Prince of the Magyars.
2 CONT
