1 NOTE Lived between 845 and 886.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hild (Ragnhild) went to king Harald Hårfagre to beg a favour for her son, Gange-Rolv, when ca. 886 the king declared him an outlaw all over Norway because of his shore raid in Viken, but Gange-Rolv had to leave the country.
2 CONT
2 CONT From Snorre Sturlasson: Harald Hårfagre's Saga:
2 CONT
2 CONT "24. ROLF GANGER DRIVEN INTO BANISHMENT.
2 CONT
2 CONT Earl Ragnvald was King Harald's dearest friend, and the king had the greatest regard for him. He was married to Hild, a daughter of Rolf Nefia, and their sons were Rolf and Thorer.
2 CONT ...
2 CONT Rolf became a great viking, and was of so stout a growth that no horse could carry him, and wheresoever he went he must go on foot; and therefore he was called Rolf Ganger ["Walker"]. He plundered much in the East sea. One summer,
2 CONC  as he was coming from the eastward on a viking's expedition to the coast of Viken, he landed there and made a cattle foray. As King Harald happened, just at that time, to be in Viken, he heard of it, and was in a great rage; for 
2 CONC he had forbid, by the greatest punishment, the plundering within the bounds of the country. The king assembled a Thing, and had Rolf declared an outlaw over all Norway. When Rolf's mother, Hild heard of it she hastened to the king
2 CONC , and entreated peace for Rolf; but the king was so enraged that her entreaty was of no avail. Then Hild spake these lines: --
2 CONT "Think'st thou, King Harald, in thy anger,
2 CONT To drive away my brave Rolf Ganger
2 CONT Like a mad wolf, from out the land?
2 CONT Why, Harald, raise thy mighty hand?
2 CONT Why banish Nefia's gallant name-son,
2 CONT The brother of brave udal-men?
2 CONT Why is thy cruelty so fell?
2 CONT Bethink thee, monarch, it is ill
2 CONT With such a wolf at wolf to play,
2 CONT Who, driven to the wild woods away
2 CONT May make the king's best deer his prey." "
