2 CONT m REGINLIND [Reinhild], daughter of --- (-11 May <nowiki>----</nowiki>). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Theoderic & his wife had seven children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. WIDUKIND .  Widukind names (in order) "Widukind, Immed et Reginbern" as brothers of Queen Mathilde[39].  
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. IMMED [I] .  Widukind names (in order) "Widukind, Immed et Reginbern" as brothers of Queen Mathilde[40].  
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.  REGINBERN .  Widukind names (in order) "Widukind, Immed et Reginbern" as brothers of Queen Mathilde, specifying that Reginbern fought against the Danes[41].  
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. MATHILDE ([896]-Quedlinburg 14 Mar 968, bur Quedlinburg Stiftskirche, married Heinrich, Graf Heinrich, future King of Germany, became Queen of Germany and saint).  
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. AMELRADA ([7 Sep] <nowiki>----</nowiki>, married Eberhard, Graf in der Drenthe und im Salland)
2 CONT
2 CONT 6. FREDERUNA (-18 Jan 971, married Wichmann II, son of Billung, later became a nun before death).
2 CONT
2 CONT 7. BIA [Pia] (-25 May <nowiki>----</nowiki>, married and had a son named Friedrich)
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the German Wikipedia page on Ringelheim:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ringelheim
2 CONT
2 CONT Ringelheim existed before Frankish times at a crossroad of two military and trade routes (between Brunswick, Goslar, and Hildesheim). It had jurisdiction over the salt district of Saltzgitteraner. Probably in the reign of the King
2 CONC  of East Franconia Heinrich I and Mathilde from the House of Immedinger (919-936) was a royal convent, possibly formed by Graf Immat (Immed I?) of the House of Immedinger in 940.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Immedinger
2 CONT
2 CONT Die Immedinger waren ein sächsisches Adelsgeschlecht zur Zeit der Liudolfinger, zu deren Vorfahren – wenn auch nicht im Mannesstamm – der Sachsenherzog Widukind gehörte.
2 CONT
