2 CONC  brother Bruno as duke after the latter's death in battle in 880. His dynasty, named after his father, is called the Liudolfing.
2 CONT
2 CONT By a charter of Louis the Younger to the Abbey of Gandersheim dated 26 January 877, the pago Suththuringa (country of South Thuringia) is described as in comitatu Ottonis (in Otto's county). In a charter of 28 January 897, Otto is
2 CONC  described as marchio and the pago Eichesfelden (Eichsfeld) is now found to be within his county (march). He was also the lay abbot of Hersfeld Abbey in 908. He was described as magni ducis Oddonis (great duke Otto) by Widukind of
2 CONC  Corvey when describing the marriage of his sister, Liutgard, to King Louis.
2 CONT
2 CONT Otto rarely left Saxony. He was a regional prince and his overlords, Louis the Younger and Arnulf, with both of whom he was on good terms, rarely interfered in Saxony. In Saxony, Otto was king in practice and he established himsel
2 CONC f as tributary ruler over the neighbouring Slav tribes, such as the Daleminzi.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Widukind of Corvey, Otto was offered the kingship of East Francia after the death of Louis the Child in 911, but did not accept it on account of his advanced age, instead suggesting Conrad of Franconia. The truthfulne
2 CONC ss of this report is considered doubtful.
2 CONT
2 CONT Otto's wife was Hathui (Hedwiga), daughter of Henry of Franconia. Otto is buried in the monastery of Gandersheim. He had two sons, Thankmar and Liudolf, who predeceased him, but his third son Henry succeeded him as duke of Saxony 
2 CONC and was later elected king. His daughter Oda married Zwentibold, King of Lotharingia.
