2 CONT Unattributed English-language biography:
2 CONT
2 CONT ROBERT-THE STRONG:
2 CONT
2 CONT Marquess of Neustria, was the father of Eudes, (King Odo) of the West Franks (c857-898) and of ROBERT I, King of France (865-923). ROBERT I's daughter married Raoul of Burgundy, who later became King Rudolph of France. ROBERT I's 
2 CONC son, HUGH-THE-GREAT, Duke of Franks, Count of Paris, was the brother-in-law of King Rudolph. HUGH-the-GREAT's son, HUGH CAPET, lived from approximately 938 to 996, and was crowned King of France in 987
2 CONT
2 CONT French warrior, marquess of Neustria; father of the French kings Eudes and Robert I and ancestor of the Capetians. He joined the rebellious nobles against Charles II, Emperor of the West. They invited Louis the German to invade Fr
2 CONC ance (858). Becoming reconciled to Charles in 861, Robert was charged with the defense of the country between the Seine and the Loire, from which he repelled the Bretons and the Normans. He was killed fighting against the Normans.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Occupation: Marquis of Neustria 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT From the English Wikipedia page on the Battle of Brissarthe: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Brissarthe
2 CONT
2 CONT The Battle of Brissarthe was fought on 2 July 866[1]), between the Franks and a joint Breton-Viking army near Brissarthe, Neustria. It was marked by the death of Robert the Strong, the Neustrian margrave, and Ranulf I, the duke of
2 CONC  Aquitaine.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 866, Salomon, Duke of Brittany, allied with Hastein (Hasting), a Danish chieftain, for an expedition into Anjou, Maine, and Touraine. In the course of the campaign, Le Mans was sacked. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert, commander of the afflicted regions, assembled a large army to expel them. He was joined by Ranulf of the region of Poitou and Gauzfrid and (his son) Hervé of Maine.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Frankish army succeeded in intercepting the Danes before they reached their boats on the Loire River. 
2 CONT
2 CONT (French Wikipedia says that Robert knew he couldn't stop the Danes from sacking Le Mans, but he knew that they had to return to their boats by way of the river. He waited with his army near his villa of Chateauneuf-sur-Sarthe, a p
2 CONC lace where the river splits into several branches, creating an obstruction for the Viking river boats.) 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Danes (forced to come ashore) took refuge in a church, but the Franks besieged them (after failing in an assault on its stone walls). During the night, the Danes attempted to escape. During the ensuing battle, Robert was kille
2 CONC d, Ranulf mortally wounded by an arrow, and Hervé injured. With the loss of their leaders, the Franks had to retreat.
2 CONT
2 CONT (The French Wikipedia page says: Robert Le Fort posted sentinels, and while some of his men went to plunder the Viking boats still in the river, he removed his armor (including the broigne chain cloak he had been wearing). At nigh
2 CONC tfall, Hastein tried to escape. Robert Le Fort, without armor, went to the forefront of the Franks, who easily push the Vikings back into the church. In the fighting, Robert is cut down, and the Comte de Poitiers is seriously woun
2 CONC ded by an arrow; he dies during the following October. Without a leader, the Franks withdraw and the Vikings escape.)
2 CONT
2 CONT In 867, Charles the Bald entered negotiations (at Compiegne) with Salomon (through Pascweten, his step-son) and Charles the Bald recognised him as King of Brittany (as well as the succession to Riwallon, Salomon's son). He concede
2 CONC d the Cotentin and possible the Avranchin to the Breton. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Hastein continued to ravage the Loire Valley for many more years. He hit Bourges in 867, Orléans in 868, and Angers in 872. Charles appealed for assistance to Salomon.
2 CONT
2 CONT (According to the French Wikipedia page: For the Robertian dynasty, the consequences were almost as important, as his sons Odo and Robert - our ancestor - were placed under the guardianship of Abbot Hugh, to which his honors are g
2 CONC iven over, honors which nearly went to the Welf lineage.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Primary sources
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Regino of Prüm (Chronicles) 2. Annales Bertiniani (Annals of St. Bertin)
2 CONT
