1 NOTE !    (1) Count of Orleans
1 NOTE Hij wordt vermeld in 836 als getrouwe van Lodewiik de Vrome.
2 CONT Hij schenkt twee hoeven in de Wormsgouw aan Lorsch; vermeld als graaf ca. 837 te Pfungstadt (als Rutpert IV); kiest in 840 partij voor Lotharius en begeeft zich dientengevolge eerst naar Bourgondië en vervolgens naar Neustrië; her
2 CONC tog van Francie, de eerste die de naam Capet draagt, vanwege zijn vermelding als leken-abt te Tours. Bijgenaamd Le Fort. Met hem vangt men meestal de stamboom aan van de Capetingers, maar enkele van zijn voorouders zijn ook bekend
2 CONC  (zie Gens Nostra 1964, blz. 279 en ook Isenburg, AF.II.13 en verder Isenburg NF.II.10).
2 CONT Hij is identiek met 'Robert le Fort' (Robert 'de Sterke').
2 CONT Hij wordt vermeld als lekenabt van St. Martin de Marmoutier bij Tours op 3 apr. 852 en is ('vir illuster') missus dominicus in Maine, Touraine, Anjou, Séois, Corbonnais, mogelijk (vanwege de nabijheid der Bretons) reeds markgraaf 
2 CONC van Anjou; graaf van Parijs en Orleans.
2 CONT Hij komt in opstand 856-861; daarna hertog van Francië ('Dux Caroli Calvis regis) tegen de Bretons (861); graaf van Autun, Auxerre, Nevers en markgraaf 865/6.
2 CONT Hij is gesneuveld tegen de Noormannen bij Brissarthe aan de oever van de Loire op 15 sept. 866. Hij is de stamvader der Robertijnen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bron: Schwennicke Volume II page 10: Rutpert IV (Robert I) Graf im Wormsgau, Comte de Tours, nobilis Franciae and Comte de Paris, killed in battle 15 September or 25 July 866; survived by his second wife Adelaide/Aelis de Tours. H
2 CONC e is the father of Robert I, King of France (King Robert was born after his father was killed).
1 NOTE Grecve av Paris
1 NOTE Robert IV the Strong (also known as Rutpert) (820-September 15, 866), was Margrave in Neustria. His family is named after him and called Robertians. He was first nominated by Charles the Bald missus dominicus in 853. Robert was th
2 CONC e father of the kings Odo and Robert I of France. Robert was the great-grandfather of Hugh Capet and thus the ancestor of all the Capetians. His father was Robert of Worms.
2 CONT
2 CONT Origins and rise to power
2 CONT While very little is known about the beginnings of the Robertian family, historians have been able to adduce that the family of nobles had its origins in Hesbaye. During the reign of Louis the German, the Robertian family moved fr
2 CONC om East Francia to West Francia. After his arrival in West Francia, Charles the Bald showed his favour of the family defecting from his enemy Louis by assigning Robert to the lay abbacy of Marmoutier in 852. In 853 the position of
2 CONC  missus dominicus in the provinces of Maine, Anjou, and Touraine was given him and he had de facto control of the ancient ducatus Cenomannicus, a vast duchy centred on Le Mans and corresponding to the regnum Neustriae. Robert's ri
2 CONC se came at the expense of the established family of the Rorigonids and was designed to curb their regional power and to defend Neustria from Viking and Breton raids.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Revolt
2 CONT Despite the fact that he was a favoured noble of Charles, Robert joined a rebellion against the king in 858. He led the Frankish nobles of Neustria with the Bretons under Salomon in inviting Louis the German to invade West Francia
2 CONC  and receive their homage. The revolt had been sparked by the marriage alliance between Charles and Erispoe, Duke of Brittany, and by the investment of Louis the Stammerer with the regnum Neustriae (856). These actions significant
2 CONC ly curtailed the influence both of Salomon and Robert. Charles compensated Robert for the losses suffered in this civil war by giving him the counties of Autun and Nevers in Burgundy, which greatly enlarged his landholdings. In 85
2 CONC 6 he had to defend Autun from Louis the German following the death of Lothair I. But following Erispoe's assassination in November 857, both he and Salomon rebelled.
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis the German reached Orléans in September 858 and received delegations from the Breton and Neustrian leaders, as well as from Pepin II. The Neustrian rebels had chased Louis the Stammerer from Le Mans, his capital, earlier th
2 CONC at year. In 861, Charles made peace with Robert and appointed him Count of Anjou, even though he had been involved in the revolt.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT War with Bretons and Vikings
2 CONT While count of Anjou, Robert was able to successfully defend the northern coast against the threat of a Viking invasion. In 862 Charles granted Louis the Stammerer, his son, the lay abbacy of Saint Martin of Tours, a small benefic
2 CONC e in comparison with the kingdom he had received in 856 (and lost in 858). The young Louis rebelled and was quickly joined by Salomon, who supplied him with troops for a war against Robert.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 862 two groups of Vikings-one the larger of two fleets recently forced out of the Seine by Charles the Bald, the other a fleet returning from a Mediterranean expedition-converged on Brittany, where one (the Mediterranean) was h
2 CONC ired by the Breton duke Salomon to ravage the Loire valley.[1] Robert captured twelve of their ships, killing all on board save a few who fled. He then opened negotiations with the former Seine Vikings, and hired them against Salo
2 CONC mon for 6,000 pounds of silver. The purpose of this was doubtless to prevent them from entering the service of Salomon.[2] Probably Robert had to collect a large amount in taxes to finance what was effectively a non-tributary Dane
2 CONC geld designed to keep the Vikings out of Neustria.[3] The treaty between the Franks and the Vikings did not last more than a year: in 863 Salomon made peace and the Vikings, deprived of an enemy, ravaged Neustria.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert made war on Pepin II in his later years. In 863 he had to defend Autun again from Louis the German, this time after the death of Charles of Provence. Robert was in Neustria during 865 and 866, with Bretons and Vikings ravag
2 CONC ing the environs of Le Mans.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Death and legacy
2 CONT In 866, Robert was killed at the Battle of Brissarthe while, unsurprisingly, defending Francia against a joint Breton-Viking raiding party, led by Salomon, Duke of Brittany, and the Viking chieftain Hastein. During the battle, Rob
2 CONC ert had entrapped the Viking commander in a nearby church. Thinking he was not endangered, Robert took off his armour and began to besiege the church. Once Robert was unarmoured, the trapped Vikings launched a surprise attack and 
2 CONC killed him before he had time to re-arm. His success against the Vikings led to his heroic characterisation as "a second Maccabaeus" in the Annales Fuldenses.
2 CONT
2 CONT The name of Robert's wife is not attested in primary sources. According to some modern scholars she was Adelaide or Adalais, a daughter of Hugh of Tours (and thus an Etichonid) and the widow of Conrad I of Auxerre (died 862), a We
2 CONC lf. Since Robert already had children by 862, Adelaide would have to have been his second wife. French genealogist Christian Settipani has identified the source of this identification as the unreliable twelfth-century Chronicle of
2 CONC  Saint-Bénigne de Dijon, which was interpolated into the chronicle of Alberic of Trois-Fontaines.[4] The Europäische Stammtafeln has identified Robert's first wife as a certain Agane. Whatever the facts, two of Robert's sons bec
2 CONC ame kings of France: Odo and Robert.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT Smith, Julia M. H. Province and Empire: Brittany and the Carolingians. Cambridge University Press: 1992. ISBN 0-521-38285-8 
2 CONT Hummer, Hans J. Politics and Power in Early Medieval Europe: Alsace and the Frankish Realm 600 - 1000. Cambridge University Press: 2005. ISBN 0-521-85441-2 
2 CONT Bradbury, Jim. The Capetians, Kings of France 987-1328. Hambledon Continuum: 2007. ISBN 978-1-85285-528-4 
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT ^ Einar Joranson (1923), The Danegeld in France (Rock Island: Augustana), 59-61. 
2 CONT ^ Robert probably expected Salomon to hire them to replace the defeated Mediterranean Vikings, then to attack Neustria from two sides: with the Viking ships ascending the Loire and Breton troops invading by land. 
2 CONT ^ In 860-1 Charles the Bald had collected a general tax to pay a Danegeld of 5,000 pounds. The king had probably authorised Robert's payment. 
2 CONT ^ The Chronicle of Saint-Bénigne names Regine, que cum esset iuvencula fuit concubina Karoli Magni iam senioris as the wife of Roberti Fortis marchionis, but this Regina, concubine of Charlemagne, must have been born by 785 at th
2 CONC e latest, since she had borne a son by 801. A marriage to Robert is chronologically implausible. 
