2 CONT from &quot;Our Folk&quot; by Albert D Hart, Jr.
1 NOTE BIOGRAPHY
2 CONT Rutpert IV was born about 820, son of Rutpert III, Graf in Wormsgau, and Waldrada/Vaudree of Orléans.
2 CONT
2 CONT His first marriage was with Agane, about whom nothing is known. About 864 he married as his second wife Aelis de Tours, daughter of Hugues, comte de Tours, and widow of Konrad I, Graf in Argengau und Linzgau.
2 CONT
2 CONT From 836 to 840 Rutpert was count in the Worms region. In 852 he became lay abbot of Saint-Martin-de-Marmoutier near Tours in Anjou. At the end of the 9th century, Charles 'the Bald', king of _Francia occidentalis_ and later Holy 
2 CONC Roman Emperor, allied himself to the duke of Brittany and expelled the Vikings from Anjou, the region originally known as the _pasus andecavis_ named after the Gaul tribe of Andecaves. In 853 he appointed Rutpert _missus dominicus
2 CONC _ for the regions of Tours and Angers in Anjou. Later Rutpert became commander in the Autunois, which in 856 was threatened by would-be successors after the death of Emperor Lothar I, and in 863 in connection with the division of 
2 CONC the kingdom of Charles de Provence.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 858 Rutpert and Archbishop Ganelon were the leaders of the nobility in opposition to Charles 'the Bald'; they wanted to offer the crown of _Francia occidentalis_ to his half-brother Ludwig II 'the German', king of the East-Fran
2 CONC ks.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rutpert became duke of Francia, the lands between the Seine and the Loire, and as such he was responsible for the struggle against the Vikings and Bretons. He fell against the Vikings at Brissarthe on 15 September 866.
1 NOTE Guarri, Count of Morvois is listed as the father of Bertha deMorvois, according to Paul Carter.
1 NOTE _P_CCINFO 1-2782
1 NOTE  Robert the Strong (died 866), count of Anjou and Blois; at first rebelled against Charles the Bald but later won king's confidence by defense of the Seine and Loire valleys against the Normans and Bretons; his two sons, Odo and R
2 CONC obert I, became kings of France.
1 NOTE Father of Robert The Strong of Neustria. Robert was King o
2 CONT
2 CONT --Other Fields
2 CONT
2 CONT Ref Number: 347
1 NOTE Source: The Rufus Parks Pedigree by Brian J.L. Berry.  Chart: page  56.
2 CONT
2 CONT Page 57:
2 CONT
2 CONT 8.	Rutpert IV (Robert the Strong, le Fort, der Starke), d. 866; Count in Western Nesustria and various other territories in the vicinity of Loire; by 852 was serving the Carolingian Charles II The Bald as count and missus dominicu
2 CONC s in the west of the kingdom; created Count of Anjoi and Blois; lay Abbot of St. Martin de Marmoitier, Tours, 852-66; joined the magnates' revolt in 856 but was reconciled with Charles 861; fought several engagements and secured t
2 CONC he neutrality of the Normans on the Seine.  Charles  sent him to Burgundy 864 to keep on eye on Girard, Count of Vienne, but had to recall him in July 865, when he won a great victory over the Normans.  He was granted full control
2 CONC  in Western Neustria early in 866.  He was sometimes called duke, at that time a military, not necessarily a hereditary title.  He d. in battle against the Normans at Brissarthe, possibly 15 Sept 866.  He mar. (2) Adèlaide (Aelis,
2 CONC  Adelheid) of Alsace. c.810-c.866, widow of Conrad I, Count of Aargau and Auxerre, and dau. of Hugo (Hugo) III, Count of Alsace, by his wife Bava...a dynasty which had held that principality from 662 at the least.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: "Ancestral Roots of Sixty Colonists" by Frederick Lewis Weis.
2 CONT
