2 CONT His daughter Emma brought him the county of Provins, nucleus of the later county of Champagne.
2 CONT
2 CONT His wife, Luitgarde of Vermandois, was the widow of William I of Normandy. She married Theobald between 942 and 945 and bore him four children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Theobald (d. 962) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh, Archbishop of Bourges (d. 985) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Odo (d. 996), count of Blois 
2 CONT
2 CONT Emma (d. 1003), married William IV of Aquitaine -------------------- http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theobald_I_of_Blois -------------------- (Vi)comte de Tours
2 CONT
2 CONT Comte de Blois, de Chateaudun et de Chartres
2 CONT
2 CONT Seigneur de Montau en Laonois, de Vierzon et de Sancerre en Berry -------------------- Known as Theobald I de Blois on this site -http://cybergata.com/roots/1040.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------- http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theobald_I,_Count_of_Blois
2 CONT
2 CONT Theobald I (913–978), called the Cheat(er) or the Trickster (le Tricheur), was the first count of Blois, Chartres, and Châteaudun from 960, and Tours from 945.
2 CONT
2 CONT Theobald was initially a vassal of Hugh the Great, Duke of France. Around 945, he captured King Louis IV to the benefit of Hugh. In return for freedom, the king granted him the city of Laon. He took the title of "count" in Tours. 
2 CONC He seized Chartres and Châteaudun and remarried his sister to Fulk II of Anjou. In 958, he met Fulk in Verron and the two described themselves as "governor and administrator [of the] kingdom [of Neustria]" and comites Dei gratia (
2 CONC "counts by the grace of God").
2 CONT
2 CONT Theobald's sister married Alan II of Brittany and Theobald governed the duchy during the minority of her son Drogo. Thus, Theobald extended his influence all the way to Rennes.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 960, he began opposing Richard I of Normandy and entered into a long war with the Normans. In 961, he attacked Évreux. The Normans responded by attacking Dunois. In 962, he launched an assault on Rouen which failed. The Normans
2 CONC  burned Chartres in response. He took control of the fortresses of Saint-Aignan in the Loir-et-Cher, Vierzon, and Anguillon in Berry.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the minority of Hugh Capet, he reinforced Chartres and Châteaudun. Around 960, he built Saumur. By his death, he had built a vast power on the Loire, dominating central France.
2 CONT
2 CONT His daughter Emma brought him the county of Provins, nucleus of the later county of Champagne.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT His wife, Luitgarde of Vermandois, was the widow of William I of Normandy. She married Theobald between 942 and 945 and bore him four children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Theobald (d. 962)
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh, Archbishop of Bourges (d. 985)
2 CONT
2 CONT Odo (d. 996), count of Blois
2 CONT
2 CONT Emma (d. 1003), married William IV of Aquitaine
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------- http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theobald_I_of_Blois
2 CONT
2 CONT And in French: http://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thibaud_Ier_de_Blois
2 CONT
2 CONT Theobald I (died 16 January between 975 and 978), called the Cheat(er) or the Trickster (le Tricheur), was the first count of Blois, Chartres, and Châteaudun from 960, and Tours from 945.
2 CONT
2 CONT Theobald was initially a vassal of Hugh the Great, Duke of France. Around 945, he captured King Louis IV to the benefit of Hugh. In return for freedom, the king granted him the city of Laon. He took the title of "count" in Tours. 
2 CONC He seized Chartres and Châteaudun and remarried his sister to Fulk II of Anjou. In 958, he met Fulk in Verron and the two described themselves as "governor and administrator [of the] kingdom [of Neustria]" and comites Dei gratia (
2 CONC "counts by the grace of God").
2 CONT
2 CONT Theobald's sister married Alan II of Brittany and Theobald governed the duchy during the minority of her son Drogo. Thus, Theobald extended his influence all the way to Rennes.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 960, he began opposing Richard I of Normandy and entered into a long war with the Normans. In 961, he attacked Évreux. The Normans responded by attacking Dunois. In 962, he launched an assault on Rouen which failed. The Normans
2 CONC  burned Chartres in response. He took control of the fortresses of Saint-Aignan in the Loir-et-Cher, Vierzon, and Anguillon in Berry.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the minority of Hugh Capet, he reinforced Chartres and Châteaudun. Around 960, he built Saumur. By his death, he had built a vast power on the Loire, dominating central France.
2 CONT
2 CONT His daughter Emma brought him the county of Provins, nucleus of the later county of Champagne.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Family
2 CONT
2 CONT His wife, Luitgarde of Vermandois, was the widow of William I of Normandy. She married Theobald between 942 and 945 and bore him four children:
2 CONT
2 CONT * Theobald (d. 962)
2 CONT
2 CONT * Hugh, Archbishop of Bourges (d. 985)
2 CONT
2 CONT * Odo (d. 996), count of Blois
2 CONT
2 CONT * Emma (d. 1003), married William IV of Aquitaine
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.britannica.com/EBchecked/topic/590538/Theobald-I
2 CONT
2 CONT Theobald earned his nickname “the Cheat” fighting with his neighbours, among them the kings of France, the dukes of Normandy, and the church of Reims. He seized the area around Blois about 940 and later augmented his holdings with
2 CONC  the counties of Chartres and Châteaudun. In 945 Hugh the Great made Theobald responsible for holding the French king Louis IV prisoner.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/CENTRAL%20FRANCE.htm#ThibautIdied975 -------------------- Theobald I (913–975), called the Trickster (le Tricheur meaning cheater), was the first count of Blois, Chartres, and Châteaudun as well as 
2 CONC count of Tours.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents
2 CONT
2 CONT [hide] 1 Life
2 CONT
2 CONT 2 Family 3 Notes 4 References
2 CONT
2 CONT Life[edit]
2 CONT
2 CONT Theobald I was the son of Theobald le Vieux of Blois,[a][1] who from 908 on was viscomte of Tours.[2] His wife, and the mother of Theobald was Richildis, a great-granddaughter of Rorgon I, Count of Maine.[1] Theobald I was initial
2 CONC ly a vassal of Hugh the Great, Duke of France.[3] Around 945, Louis IV was captured by Northmen and given over to Hugh the Great, who placed the king in Theobald's custody.[3] After about a year in his vassal's custody king Louis 
2 CONC negotiated his freedom by offering Hugh the city of Laon which Hugh then gave to Theobald.[4] Theobald was the count of Tours from 942, was also count of Blois and in 960 count of Châteaudun and Chartres.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Theobald's sister had married Alan II of Nantes, the Duke of Brittany, giving Theobald influence all the way to Rennes.[6] However the death of Alan II left a void in Brittany and left it vulnerable to encroachment by either the N
2 CONC ormans or the Angevins.[7] Theobald made an alliance with Fulk II of Anjou which gave him control of Saumur a strategic citadel located between the Loire and Thouet rivers guarding the Angevin march.[7] This included control of th
2 CONC e monastery of Saint-Florent inside the walls of Saumur.[7] In turn the recently widowed Fulk married Theobald's sister, the widow of Alan II of Nantes.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 960, he began opposing Richard I of Normandy and entered into a long war with the Normans. In 961, he attacked Évreux. The Normans responded by attacking Dunois. In 962, he launched an assault on Rouen which failed. The Normans
2 CONC  burned Chartres in response. He took control of the fortresses of Saint-Aignan in the Loir-et-Cher, Vierzon, and Anguillon in Berry. During the minority of Hugh Capet, he reinforced Chartres and Châteaudun. By his death, he had b
2 CONC uilt a vast power on the Loire, dominating central France.
2 CONT
2 CONT About 943-44,[8] he married Luitgarde of Vermandois, widow of William I of Normandy.[9] She was the daughter of Herbert II, Count of Vermandois and Hildebrand of France, daughter of king Robert I of France.[10] Her half-brother wa
2 CONC s Hugh the Great Duke of France.[11]
2 CONT
2 CONT Family[edit]
2 CONT
2 CONT He and his wife Luitgarde of Vermandois had four children: Theobald (d. 962).[8] Hugh, Archbishop of Bourges (d. 985).[8] Odo (d. 995), succeeded his father as Count of Blois.[8] Emma (d. aft. 1003), married William IV of Aquitain
2 CONC e.[b][8]
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes[edit]
2 CONT
2 CONT a.Jump up ^ Theobald was also called Theobald 'the Elder' who in 878 replaced Warnegald as viscount in Maine, quite probably on the basis of his marriage to a Rorgonid cousin Richildis. See: Pierre Riché, The Carolingians (1993), 
2 CONC p. 237. b.Jump up ^ His daughter Emma brought him the county of Provins, nucleus of the later county of Champagne.
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------- . Theobald (Theobald ) I (le Tricheur) de Blois Count of Champagne-14641 was born circa 913 in Blois, Loir-Et-Cher, Orleanais Centre, France. He died 1008 in Montmorency, Seine-et-Oise, France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Theobald married (MRIN:9146) Luitgarde (Liegarde) de Vermandois Duchess of Normandy-14642, daughter of Herbert II, comte de Vermandois-11905 and Adèle [Adela ( de France)] Hildebrand ( Hildebranda) de France Hildebrante Liegarde (
2 CONC Capet, of Neustria)-14643 (MRIN:8465). Luitgarde was born before 925 in Vermandois, Aisne, Picardy, France. She died after 985 in Matemoutier Abbey, Vermandois, Normandie, France and was buried in Abbaye de Saint-Père, Chartres, E
2 CONC ure-et-Loir, Centre, France.
2 CONT
2 CONT They had the following children:
2 CONT
2 CONT 2 M i. Eudes I (Odo) de Blois comte de Blois-16651 was born 950 in Marmoutier, Alsace, France. He died 12 Mar 996 in Marmoutier, Alsace, France and was buried in Tours, , France. Eudes married (MRIN:9145) Berthe (bertha) de Bourgo
2 CONC gne Reine consort de France-16652, daughter of Conrad "the Peaceful" 'le Pacifique' de Bourgogne King of Burgundy ( Roi de Bourgogne)-16654 and Mathilde de France-16653 (MRIN:8614). Berthe was born 964. She died circa January 16, 
2 CONC 1010.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3 F ii. Hildegarde de Blois, de Normandie-14639 was born circa 940 in Blois, Loir-Et-Cher, Orleanais Centre, France. She died 1008 in Montmorency, Seine-et-Oise, France. Hildegarde married (1-MRIN:7312) Thibault de Montlhéry, (de 
2 CONC Bray) , Seigneur de Montlhéry & de Bray-14640. Thibault was born 983 in Montlhery, Hurepoix, Seine Et Oise, France. He died 1031 in Montlhery, Hurepoix, Seine Et Oise, France and was buried in Montlhéry, France. Hildegarde also ma
2 CONC rried (2-MRIN:8971) Hugh de Chateaudun, I-17248, son of Rampo de Châteaudun-17249 and Mathilde de Vendôme-17247 (MRIN:8972). Hugh was born 965 in Eure Et Loir, Beauce, Centre, France. He died 10 Jun 1026 in Chateaudun, Loir-et-Che
2 CONC r, Centre, France.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Had the nickname of "Le Tricheur", (The Cheat).
