1 NOTE {geni:about_me} "Niskinin" (often spelled Niskinia, Niskinei, or Niskyne) is not typically listed as a distinct historic figure, but rather as an alternate name or ancestral patronymic associated directly with Prince Mal of the Dr
2 CONC evlians.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Possible kinship==
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Vasily Tatischev, in the Ioachimskoy Chronicle, Mal is said to be "Prince Drevlian, son of Niskinin".
2 CONT
2 CONT Some historians believe Mal's children were Malusha (the housekeeper of Princess Olga and mother of Prince Vladimir) and Dobrynya. They occupied positions in the court household of Olga and Svyatoslav, but failed to reach any cons
2 CONC iderable height in public administration.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dobrynya became governor of Novgorod, where he was soon sent with Vladimir and Malusha. The Chronicle calls Malusha's father "Malk Lyubchanin." In epics, Dobrynya has the patronym Nikitich and is reputed to be a "country bumpkin."
2 CONC  Because Mal is called "Niskinya" in some chronicles, as suggested by the patronym Nikitich, some later sources have suggested the patronym Niskinich.
2 CONT
