2 CONT =Árpád=
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Grand Prince of the Magyars
2 CONT
2 CONT Statue of Árpád
2 CONT
2 CONT Reign 	c. 895 – c. 907
2 CONT
2 CONT Predecessor 	Álmos
2 CONT
2 CONT Successor 	Zoltán
2 CONT
2 CONT Father 	Álmos
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother 	Unknown
2 CONT
2 CONT Born 	c. 845
2 CONT
2 CONT Died 	c. 907
2 CONT
2 CONT Árpád (c. 845 – c. 907), the second Grand Prince of the Magyars (Hungarians) (c. 895 – c. 907). Under his rule the Magyar people settled in the Carpathian basin. The dynasty descending from him ruled the Magyar tribes and later th
2 CONC e Kingdom of Hungary until 1301.
2 CONT
2 CONT His life
2 CONT
2 CONT Árpád and the six other chieftains of the Magyars. From the Chronicon Pictum, 1360.
2 CONT
2 CONT Árpád was the son of Grand Prince Álmos (Grand Prince of the Magyars), leader of the Hungarian tribal federation; his mother's name and descent is unknown[1].
2 CONT
2 CONT The emergence of the Magyar tribes and their leaders is a specific period in the history of the Hungarian people that refers to the time starting from when the Magyars were considered a people separate and identifiable from other 
2 CONC Ugric speakers (1000-500 BC) up until their occupation and settlement of the Carpathian Basin around 896 AD (Hungarian: Honfoglalás)[2].
2 CONT
2 CONT In 894, Árpád and Kurszán negotiated together with the representatives of the Byzantine emperor, Leo VI the Wise the terms under which the confederation of the Magyar tribes was willing to assist the Byzantine Empire against Emper
2 CONC or Simeon I of Bulgaria.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the spring of next year, the Magyar tribes attacked the Bulgarian Empire and defeated Emperor Simeon I, obliging him to conclude peace with the Byzantine Empire. Emperor Simeon, however, entered into an alliance with the Pechen
2 CONC egs, who were the eastern neighbours of the Hungarian tribal federation, and he made an attack against the Magyar troops. In the Battle of Southern Buh, Emperor Simeon I defeated their army; shortly afterwards, the Pechenegs attac
2 CONC ked and pillaged their territories. The Magyar tribes were obliged to leave Etelköz and move to the Carpathian Basin where they settled down (Honfoglalás).
2 CONT
2 CONT The Magyars led by Árpád crossing the Carpathians - a detail of the Arrival of the Hungarians by Árpád Feszty et al. oil on canvas cyclorama (Ópusztaszer National Memorial Site, Hungary)
2 CONT
2 CONT The circumstances of Álmos' death are unclear. The leaders of the seven Hungarian tribes proclaimed Árpád to Grand Prince of the Magyars[3]; therefore Árpád is considered traditionally to lead the Honfoglalás ("the occupation of t
2 CONC he country")[4].
2 CONT
2 CONT In 896 the Hungarian tribes occupied the Upper Tisza river, from there they undertook numerous looting raids in central and western Europe, and in 900/901 they moved to Pannonia[5]. The Magyars entering the Pannonian fields in 896
2 CONC  may have represented about 200,000–250,000 people.
2 CONT
2 CONT Based on Arabic sources, Árpád's title seems to have been kende[6] or gyula. In that time kende was the spiritual leader of the Magyar tribes, while the gyula led their military campaigns. According to legends, Árpád hold the firs
2 CONC t "parliamentary" session with 40 other "nobles" on horseback before 900 AD.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Levente
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Tarhos (Tarkacsu) (? – ?)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Üllő (Jeleg) (? – ?)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Jutocsa (Jutas) (? – ?)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Zoltán of Hungary (? – c. 947)
2 CONT
2 CONT Legacy
2 CONT
2 CONT Monument of Árpád in Ópusztaszer, Hungary
2 CONT
2 CONT Although the founder of the Kingdom of Hungary was not Árpád (as he lived a century earlier) - but his descendant Saint Stephen I –, he is generally thought of as the forefather of Hungarians and is often affectionally mentioned a
2 CONC s our father Árpád (Hungarian: Árpád apánk). Árpád was the founder of the dynasty named after him, which would rule over the kingdom of Hungary till 1301.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Kristó, Gyula - Makk, Ferenc: Az Árpád-ház uralkodói (IPC Könyvek, 1996)
2 CONT
