1 OCCU Dux Orientalium Saxonum, Graf von Sachsen (844-866), Duke of Saxony, Milite, duc de Saxe, Margrave de Saxe-Orientale (840-850), Graaf van Wormsgau, książę saski, Duke, Margrave en Saxe Orientale, hertog van Saksen
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Ben M. Angel's summary:
2 CONT
2 CONT Relationships:
2 CONT
2 CONT Known Parents: Graf Brunhart von Engern/Angaria (Mittelalter Genealogie), all other information unknown or unconfirmed (spouse unknown)
2 CONT
2 CONT Siblings: Unknown
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse: Oda von Sachsen (daughter of a Billung prince and Aeda), c.805-913
2 CONT
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT *1. Bruno von Sachsen (d. 880) died crossing a flooded river or in battle during an expedition against the Danes.
2 CONT *2. Otto "der Erlauchte" von Sachsen (d. 912), Graf im Südthüringau, Graf im Eichsfeld (888), Lay Abbot of Hersfield (908), husband of Hedwig/Hathui.
2 CONT *3. Thankmar, Abbot of Corvey (877/879)
2 CONT *4. Liutgard, wife of Ludwig III der Jungere, King of the East Franks (876-911), King of Bavaria (879-911), King of Lotharingia/Lorraine (880-911).
2 CONT *5 Enda, wife of Unknown
2 CONT *6. Hathmod (840-874), first Abbess of Gandersheim (852/856-874)
2 CONT *7. Gerberga (d. 896/897), second Abbess of Gandersheim (874-896/897)
2 CONT *8. Christina (d. 919/920), third Abbess of Gandersheim (897)
2 CONT *One unnamed daughter and three unnamed sons, died young.
2 CONT
2 CONT Basic information and justifications:
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth: circa 805 (English and German Wikipedia, Genealogie Mittelalter says 805/820) - location unknown, but most likely in the historic province of Engern/Angaria (present Niedersachsen), Stammesherzogtum Sachsen (Duchy of Saxony
2 CONC ), Eastern Franconia
2 CONT
2 CONT Wedding: c820/835 - location unknown
2 CONT
2 CONT Death: 11 March 866 (FMG) - location unknown
2 CONT
2 CONT Burial: First Kloster Gandersheim in Brunshausen (Bad Gandersheim), Landkreis Northeim, Niedersachsen
2 CONT
2 CONT Alternate names: Ludolf
2 CONT
2 CONT Occupation: Dux Orientalium Saxonum, Graf von Sachsen (844-866)
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Foundation for Medieval Genealogy page on Saxony (covering his birth family):
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/SAXONY.htm#Liudolfdied866A
2 CONT
2 CONT Three brothers, parents not known, although the primary source which confirms this relationship has not so far been identified.  
2 CONT
2 CONT It is assumed that the brothers were related to Bruno (d. after 775) and his son Ekbert (d. after 834, husband of Ida).  If this is correct, from a chronological point of view they may have been brothers of Ekbert.  It is also pos
2 CONC sible that the relationship was through Ekbert's (unknown) mother. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. BRUNO .  
2 CONT *Brun was named as father of Liudolf in the early 13th century Gandersheimer Reimchronik[103], but no earlier source has so far been identified which confirms the relationship.  
2 CONT *m ---.  The name of Bruno's wife is not known.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Bruno & his wife had one child:
2 CONT
2 CONT a)         LIUDOLF (-11 Mar 866, bur Brunshausen).  Graf. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Reference:
2 CONT
2 CONT *[103] Wolff, L. (ed.) (1969) Die Gandersheimer Reimchronik des Priesters Eberhard 2nd Ed. (Altdeutsche Textbibliothek, Tübingen), 9, lines 139-44, cited in Jackman, D. C. (1997) Criticism and Critique, sidelights on the Konradine
2 CONC r (Oxford Unit for Prosopographical Research), p. 146 footnote 40. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Foundation for Medieval Genealogy page on Saxony (covering his married life):
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/SAXONY.htm#Liudolfdied866B
2 CONT
2 CONT LIUDOLF, son of BRUNO & his wife --- (-11 Mar 866, bur Brunshausen).  
2 CONT
2 CONT Brun was named as father of Liudolf in the early 13th century Gandersheimer Reimchronik[105], but no earlier source has so far been found which confirms the relationship.  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Chronicon Hildesheimense records that Liudolf founded the abbey of Gandersheim in 852, first at Brunshausen[106].  Widukind records that "Liudulfus" transferred relics of Pope Innocent to Rome[107].  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Annales Alamannicorum record "Ludolfus dux Saxoniæ avus Heinrici" among those who swore allegiance in 864[108].  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Annales Xantenses record the death in 866 of "Liudolfus comes a septentrione"[109]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT m ODA, daughter of BILLUNG princeps & his wife Aeda (-17 May 913).  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Carmen de Primordiis Cœnobii Gandersheimensis names the wife of "Liudulfus" as "Oda…Francorum…de stirpe potentum, filia Billungi…atque Aedæ"[110].  
2 CONT
2 CONT "Oda comitissa, Pipini regis Italiæ ex filia neptis, Hliudolfi Ducis vidua" founded Kloster Calbe an der Milde, by charter dated 885[111].  
2 CONT
2 CONT "Arnolfus…rex" confirmed donations of his predecessor of land "in pago Nordthuringa dicto in comitatu Liudulfi in loco Uuanzleua" to Kloster Gandersheim naming "fideli costræ in sanctimoniali habitu constitutæ…Odæ" by an undated c
2 CONC harter, placed in the compilation among charters dated [891/92], which names "filia eius Gerberga abbatissa"[112].  "Otto…rex" confirmed privileges to Kloster Gandersheim "avo illius Sigihardo comiti in pago Chiemihgovue in comita
2 CONC tu Sigihardi" to "comiti nostro Eberhart" by charter dated 4 May 947 in which he names "proavo nostro Liutulfo…et eius coniuge Oda…et avo nostro Ottone" recalling their involvement in the foundation of the monastery[113]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Liudolf & his wife had [twelve] children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. BRUNO (-killed in battle in Saxony 2 Feb 880).  
2 CONT *The Annalista Saxo records "Brunonis ducis" as brother of "Otto filius Liudolfi ducis"[114].  "Hludowicus…rex" granted immunities to Kloster Gandersheim, naming "Brun et Otto nostri fideles comites…[et] Liutolf genitor eorum…[et]
2 CONC …Gerbirg soror eorundem comitum" by charter dated 26 Jan 877[115].  
2 CONT *The Annales Fuldenses name "Brun ducem et fratrem reinæ, Wicmannum, Bardonem, alterum Bardonem et tertium Bardonem, Thiotherium, Gerrichum, Liutolfum, Folcwartum, Avan, Thiotricum, Liutharium" as those killed in battle in 880 in 
2 CONC Saxony against "Nordmannis"[116].  The Gesta Francorum lists "Brun ducem et fratrem reginæ" as one of the twelve counts who were killed fighting the Danes in 880[117].  Thietmar records that "Duke Bruno…great uncle" of Bruno Archb
2 CONC ishop of Köln, was drowned in a flooded river on 2 Feb while on an expedition against the Danes[118].  The Erchanberti Breviarum records that "Ludovicus rex Franciæ" had one son "Hug…de concubina" who [in 880] fought the Vikings "
2 CONC cum Theoderico et Marcwardo…episcopis et Bardone fratre Liutkardæ reginæ"[119], "Bardone" presumably being an error for "Brunone", although this version appears to conflate two battles (one at the river Scheldt and one in Saxony) 
2 CONC which are reported separately in the Annales Fuldenses.  The Gesta Francorum lists "Bardonum…alterum Bardonum [et] tertium Bardonum" as three of the twelve counts who were killed fighting the Danes in 880[120].  The other two coun
2 CONC ts named "Bardo" or "Bruno" have not been identified. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. OTTO "der Erlauchte" (-30 Nov 912[121], bur Gandersheim Stiftskirche).  
2 CONT *The Annalista Saxo records "Otto" as "filius Liudolfi ducis"[122].  
2 CONT *Graf im Südthüringau.  
2 CONT *Graf im Eichsfeld 888. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. THANKMAR .  
2 CONT *Europäische Stammtafeln[123] names Thankmar as a son of Liudolf & his wife but the primary source which confirms this has not so far been identified.  
2 CONT *[Abbot of Corvey 877/79].  “Ludolphus comes” donated property “in Daelhem et in Adonhusen” to Corvey monastery “pro filio suo Tancmaro”[124]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. LIUTGARD (-17 or 30 Nov 885, bur Aschaffenburg).  
2 CONT *Widukind names "Liudgardam sororem Brunonis ac magni ducis Oddonis" as wife of "orientales Francos imperantium Hluthowicus"[125].  "Hludowicus…rex" made a donation of property in "villa…Winenheim" to Kloster Lorsch in the name of
2 CONC  "comiti…Werinhario" by charter dated 4 Jan 877, naming "coniuge nostra Liutgarda"[126].  
2 CONT *The necrology of Fulda records the death in 885 of "Liutgart regina"[127].  The death and burial place of "Liudgardis regina" are recorded in the Annalista Saxo[128].  
2 CONT *m (before 29 Nov 874) LUDWIG, son of LUDWIG II "der Deutsche" King of the East Franks & his wife Emma [Welf] ([835]-Frankfurt-am-Main 20 Jan 882, bur Kloster Lorsch).  
2 CONT *He succeeded his father in 876 as LUDWIG III "der Jüngere" King of the East Franks, Saxony and ½ Lotharingia.  
2 CONT *King of Bavaria 879.  
2 CONT *King of Lotharingia 880. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. ENDA .  
2 CONT *Europäische Stammtafeln[129] names Enda as a daughter of Liudolf & his wife, and her marriage, but the primary source which confirms this has not so far been identified.  
2 CONT *m ---.
2 CONT
2 CONT 6.  HATHUMOD (840-29 Sep 874, bur Brunnshausen).  
2 CONT *The Chronicon Hildesheimense records that "Hathamodam eius ducis [Liudolfi] filiam" was was installed as first abbess of Gandersheim in 852, and that she died 18 years later[130].  Her life and death are recounted in the Vita et 
2 CONC Obitus Hathamodæ[131].  Her death is recorded in the Annalista Saxo[132]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 7.  GERBERGA (-5 Sep [896/97]).  
2 CONT *The Chronicon Hildesheimense records that "Gerbergam sororem [Hathamodæ]" succeeded her sister as second abbess of Gandersheim[133].  "Gerburgis" is named sister of "Hathumod"[134], whom she succeeded as Abbess of Gandersheim in 
2 CONC 874[135].  "Hludowicus…rex" granted immunities to Kloster Gandersheim, naming "Brun et Otto nostril fideles comites…[et] Liutolf genitor eorum…[et]…Gerbirg soror eorundem comitum" by charter dated 26 Jan 877[136]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 8. CHRISTINA (-1 Apr [919/20], bur Gandersheim Stiftskirche).  
2 CONT *Thankmar records that "Sororem autem eius [=Gerburgis [et] Hathumod] Cristinam" entered Gandersheim, specifying that they were all daughters of "Oda"[137].  
2 CONT *Abbess of Gandersheim 897-897.
2 CONT
2 CONT 9. daughter (-young).  
2 CONT *Europäische Stammtafeln[138] refers to an unnamed daughter of Liudolf & his wife who died young, but the primary source which confirms this has not so far been identified. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 10. son (-young).  
2 CONT *Europäische Stammtafeln[139] refers to two or three unnamed sons of Liudolf & his wife who died young, but the primary source which confirms this has not so far been identified.  11. son (-young).  
2 CONT *Europäische Stammtafeln[140] refers to two or three unnamed sons of Liudolf & his wife who died young, but the primary source which confirms this has not so far been identified. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 12. [son (-young).  
2 CONT *Europäische Stammtafeln[141] refers to two or three unnamed sons of Liudolf & his wife who died young, but the primary source which confirms this has not so far been identified.] 
