2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT ==Duke Liudolf of von Sachsen==
2 CONT
2 CONT * Liudolf (c. 805 - 12 March 864 or 866) was a Saxon count, and Count of Eastphalia. Liudolf had extended possessions in eastern Saxony, and was a leader in the wars of King Louis the German against Normans and Slavs. The ruling L
2 CONC iudolfing House, also known as the Ottonian dynasty, is named after him; he is its oldest verified member. Unknown whether the coffin in the church actually does contain his remains.
2 CONT In 845/846, Liudolf and his wife founded a house of holy canonesses, duly established at their proprietary church in Brunshausen around 852, and moved in 881 to form Gandersheim Abbey. Liudolf's minor daughter Hathumoda became the
2 CONC  first abbess. Other daughters followed in that role. - https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/132067250/liudolf-duke_of-von_sachsen
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Spouse==
2 CONT
2 CONT * Married: About 830 Liudolf married Oda, daughter of a Frankish princeps named Billung and his wife Aeda. By marrying a Frankish nobleman's daughter, Liudolf followed suggestions set forth by Charlemagne about ensuring the integr
2 CONC ity of the Carolingian Empire in the aftermath of the Saxon Wars through marriage. Oda died on 17 May 913, supposedly at the age of 107.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==They had at least seven children:==
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Bruno''' (c. 830/840 – 880), succeeded his father as a Saxon leader,[4] supposed progenitor of the Brunonids
2 CONT * '''Oda of Saxony''' (c. 845-874), married to Lothar I, Count of Stade
2 CONT * '''Otto the Illustrious''' (c. 851 – 912), succeeded his brother in 880,[4] father of King Henry the Fowler
2 CONT * '''Liutgard''' (c. 840 – 885), married the East Frankish ruler Louis the Younger, second son of King Louis the German, in 874.[5]
2 CONT * '''Hathumoda''' (c. 840 – 874), first Abbess of Brunshausen from 852[6]
2 CONT * '''Gerberga''' (d. 896/97), Abbess of Brunshausen from 874[6]
2 CONT * '''Christina''' (d. 919/20), Abbess of Gandersheim from 896/97
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Short History==
2 CONT
2 CONT In 845/846, Liudolf and his wife went on a pilgrimage to Rome, and upon approval by Pope Sergius II they founded a house of holy canonesses dedicated to Pope Saints Anastasius and Innocent around 852. The monastery, duly establish
2 CONC ed at their proprietary church in Brunshausen,[7] was consecrated by the Hildesheim bishop Altfrid and Liudolf's minor daughter Hathumoda became its first abbess. The convent was relocated in 881 to form Gandersheim Abbey, elevate
2 CONC d to an Imperial monastery by Liudolf's grandson Henry the Fowler in 919.
2 CONT
2 CONT While King Louis the German was preoccupied with Imperial politics, Liudolf, relying on the rank as well as the allodial lands he had inherited from his ancestors, rose to a leading position among the Saxon nobles – made evident b
2 CONC y the marriage of his daughter Liutgard with King Louis the Younger. He is buried in his proprietary monastery of Brunshausen. His successions by his sons Bruno and Otto met with no resistance.
2 CONT
