1 NOTE BIOGRAPHY
2 CONT Liudolf was a 'comes' or count. This office, which resulted from appointment by an emperor or king, originally was to enforce royal authority and required administrative functions that could include military service. The office wa
2 CONC s not necessarily held for life, unless someone happened to die in office, and under Charlemagne it was not intended to be inherited.
2 CONT
2 CONT A war leader was commonly called _dux_, the origin of the modern title 'duke'. He would only be referred to as _dux_ during a particular campaign for which he was appointed to command an army. Once he ceased to exercise that comma
2 CONC nd, he effectively ceased to be _dux_, although a chronicler might use the term again when writing the man's obituary or simply stating a fact about his past activities.
2 CONT
2 CONT Liudolf married Oda about 836 and they had eleven or twelve children. Liudolf died on 11 March 866 and was named 'Liudolfus comes' in _Annales Xantenes_ relating to his death. Earlier he had been described as 'dux Saconiae'.
1 NOTE 887309128. Storhertug Liudolf BRUNOSON Sachsen (22098) was born about 806. (22099) He was a Storhertug in 844 in Sachsen. (22100) He died in 866 in Kloster i Brunhausen. (22101) Berømt som stamfar til det sachsiske hertughus. Var 
2 CONC på pilgrimsferd til Rom sammen med sin gemalinne Oda under Pave Sergius II. i årene 844-47. Grunnet 852 et kloster i Brunshausen. Stifter av Gandersheim 856. He was married to Oda BILLUNGSDTR av Sachsen about 836. (22102)
