0 @N6000000001783257242@ NOTE Seigneur de Beaumont and Pont-Audemer,
1 CONT
1 CONT He md Adelise of Meulan abt 1045, France, daughter of Waleran of Meulan, Count of Meulan. She was b abt 1032, Meulan, Normandy, France, d 1081.
1 CONT
1 CONT Eldest son of his father, he appears to have remained faithful to Duke William during the disturbances of his early reign, during which he defeated and slew Roger de Tosny. Said to have furnished 60 ships for the invasion of Engla
1 CONC nd, but did not participate in such, as he remained in Normandy as the principle adviser of the Duchess Maud, to whom the government of the duchy was entrusted. Up until 1082, he constantly appears as a witness to the charters of 
1 CONC William the Conqueror. At the time of the Domesday survey in 1086, he held land in Dorset and Gloucestershire. Around 1088, he founded the collegiate church of the Holy Trinity at Beaumont-le-Roger, and was a benefactor of St. Pie
1 CONC rre-de Preux and St. Wandrille. Shortly after 1090, Roger became a monk at the Abbey of Preux. He is known to have had two sons (Robert, Earl of Leicester, and Henry, Earl of Warwick) and one daughter, Aubreye. The day of his deat
1 CONC h was 3 December, but the year is not known.
0 @N6000000001503810170@ NOTE Life
1 CONT
1 CONT Roger was nicknamed Barbatus or La Barbe because he wore a moustache and beard while the Normans usually were clean shaven. This peculiarity is recognized in the forty-first panel of the Bayeux Tapestry where he is depicted sittin
1 CONC g at a feast with Duke William on his left hand, Odo brother of William and Bishop of Bayeux in the centre.
1 CONT
1 CONT Planché tells us that "he was the noblest, the wealthiest, and the most valiant seigneur of Normandy, and the greatest and most trusted friend of the Danish family." There is an explanation for this - as an older cousin who had ne
1 CONC ver rebelled against the young Duke, he was part of the kinship group of noblemen that William relied upon in governing Normandy and fighting off frequent rebellion and invasions. The historian Frank McLynn notes that William reli
1 CONC ed on relatives descended via his mother (namely his half-brothers and brothers-in-law) and on relatives descended from the Duchess Gunnora's sisters, since his own paternal kin had proved unreliable.
1 CONT
1 CONT Wace, the 12th century historian, says that "at the time of the invasion of England, Roger was summoned to the great council at Lillebonne, on account of his wisdom; but that he did not join in the expedition as he was too far adv
1 CONC anced in years." Although Roger could not fight, he did not hesitate in contributing his share of the cost, for he provided at his own expense sixty vessels for the conveyance of the troops across the channel. Furthermore, his eld
1 CONC est son and heir fought bravely at Hastings as noted in several contemporary records. As a result, Roger's elder sons were awarded rich lands in England, and both eventually were made English earls by the sons of the Conqueror.
0 @N6000000002187742250@ NOTE Roger de Beaumont
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Roger de Beaumont-le-Roger (c. 1015 – 29 November 1094). He was son of Humphrey de Vielles and Albreda de la Haye Auberie.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was nicknamed Barbatus or La Barbe from the fact of his wearing a moustache and beard whilst the Normans usually were clean shaven. This peculiarity is recognized in the forty first panel of the Bayeux Tapestry where he is depi
1 CONC cted sitting at a feast with Duke William on his left hand, Odo brother of William and Bishop of Bayeux in the centre. The tapestry was probably made to the order of the Bishop and is now preserved in the museum attached to the Pu
1 CONC blic Library at Bayeux.
1 CONT
1 CONT Planché tells us that "he was the noblest, the wealthiest, and the most valiant seigneur of Normandy, and the greatest and most trusted friend of the Danish family." Wace, the 12th century historian, says that "at the timeof the i
1 CONC nvasion of England, Roger was summoned to the great council at Lillebonne, on account of his wisdom; but that he did not join in the expedition as he was too far advanced in years." Although Roger could not fight, he did not hesit
1 CONC ate in contributing his share of the cost, for he provided at his own expense sixty vessels for the conveyance of the troops auross the channel.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Family and children
1 CONT He was married Adeline of Meulan, daughter of Waleran III, Count de Meulan and Oda de Conteville. Their children were:
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert de Beaumont, 1st Earl of Leicester. 
1 CONT Henry de Beaumont, 1st Earl of Warwick. 
1 CONT William de Beaumont. 
1 CONT Alberee de Beaumont, Abbess of Eton. 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT External links
1 CONT The Conqueror and His Companions: Robert de Beaumont
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Sources
1 CONT Edward T. Beaumont, J.P. The Beaumonts in History. A.D. 850-1850. Oxfor
1 CONT J.R. Planché. The Conqueror and His Companions. London: Tinsley Brothers, 1874. 
0 @N6000000002248121718@ NOTE Of Roger, Count de Beaumont, it is unanimously recorded that he was the noblest, the wealthiest, and the most valiant seigneur of Normandy.  Son of Humphrey de Vielles, and grandson of Thorold de Pontaudemer, a descendant of the K
1 CONC ings of Denmark, through Bernard the Dane, a companion of the first Norman Conqueror, Duke Rollo, illustrious as was such as origin in the eyes of his countrymen, he considered his alliance with Adelina, Countess of Meulent, suffi
1 CONC ciently honorable and important to induce him to adopt the title of her family in preference to that of his own.
0 @N6000000003853681628@ NOTE Roger de Beaumont
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT Roger de Beaumont-le-Roger (c. 1015 – 29 November 1094) was son of Humphrey de Vielles (himself a great-nephew of the Duchess Gunnora of Normandy) and his wife Albreda de la Haye Auberie. Roger de Beaumont, Lord of Beaumont-le-Rog
1 CONC er and Pont-Audemer, Viscount of Hiesmes, was thus a second cousin once removed of the Conqueror.
1 CONT
1 CONT Roger was nicknamed Barbatus or La Barbe because he wore a moustache and beard while the Normans usually were clean shaven. This peculiarity is recognized in the thirty-second panel of the Bayeux Tapestry where he is depicted sitt
1 CONC ing at a feast with Duke William on his left hand, Odo, brother of William and Bishop of Bayeux, in the centre.
1 CONT
1 CONT Planché tells us that "he was the noblest, the wealthiest, and the most valiant seigneur of Normandy, and the greatest and most trusted friend of the Danish family." There is an explanation for this - as an older cousin who had ne
1 CONC ver rebelled against the young Duke, he was part of the kinship group of noblemen that William relied upon in governing Normandy and fighting off frequent rebellion and invasions. The historian Frank McLynn notes that William reli
1 CONC ed on relatives descended via his mother (namely his half-brothers and brothers-in-law) and on relatives descended from the Duchess Gunnora's sisters, since his own paternal kin had proved unreliable.
1 CONT
1 CONT Wace, the 12th century historian, says that "at the time of the invasion of England, Roger was summoned to the great council at Lillebonne, on account of his wisdom; but that he did not join in the expedition as he was too far adv
1 CONC anced in years." Although Roger could not fight, he did not hesitate in contributing his share of the cost, for he provided at his own expense sixty vessels for the conveyance of the troops across the channel. Furthermore, his eld
1 CONC est son and heir fought bravely at Hastings as noted in several contemporary records. As a result, Roger's elder sons were awarded rich lands in England, and both eventually were made English earls by the sons of the Conqueror.
1 CONT
1 CONT He married circa 1048 or earlier Adeline of Meulan (ca. 1014-1020 - 1081), daughter of Waleran III, Count de Meulan and Oda de Conteville, and sister and heiress of a childless Count of Meulan. Meulan eventually passed to their el
1 CONC der son who became Count of Meulan in 1081. 
1 CONT
1 CONT  Sources
1 CONT Edward T. Beaumont, J.P. The Beaumonts in History. A.D. 850-1850. Oxfor
1 CONT J.R. Planché. The Conqueror and His Companions. London: Tinsley Brothers, 1874
1 CONT
0 @N6000000003051159552@ NOTE [Weis 133] Seigneur de Beaumont Pont-Audemar
0 @N6000000003635725658@ NOTE [1291] WSHNGT.ASC file (Geo Washington Ahnentafel) # 2180208
0 @N6000000003949529732@ NOTE Seigneur (feudal Lord) of Beaumont, Pontaudemer, Brionne and Vatteville, Normandy; married Adeline, sister of Hugh Count of Meulan and daughter by his 1st wife of Waleran Count of Meulan. [Burke's Peerage]  Earl of Mellent
1 CONT The well-known Roger de Beaumont held Sturminster Marshal, Dorset, in 1086; it descended to the counts of Meulan through Roger's eldest son, Robert count of Meulan. That Roger took his name from Beaumont is a part of the general h
1 CONC istory of Normandy. It follows that Roger's descendants, the counts of Meulan, the Earls of Leicester, and the Earls of Warwick, all derive from Beaumont-le-Roger. [The Origins of Some Anglo-Norman Families]
1 CONT SEIGNEUR of BEAUMONT AND PONT-AUDEMER, was eldest son of Humphrey. He appears to have remained faithful to the young Duke William during the disturbances of the earlier part of his reign, in the course of which he defeated and sle
1 CONC w Roger de Tosny. He is said to have furnished 60 ships for the invasion of England. He did not accompany the expedition, but remained in Normandy as the principal adviser of the Duchess Maud, to whom the government of the duchy w
1 CONC as entrusted. In 1071 the King committed Morcar as a prisoner to his custody. Up to the year 1082 he constantly appears as a witness to the charters of William the Conqueror. At the -time of the Domesday Survey, 1086, he held land
1 CONC  in Dorset and co. Gloucester. About 1088 he founded the collegiate church of the Holy Trinity at Beaumont-le-Roger for canons of St. Frideswide of Oxford. He was a benefactor of St. Pierre-de-Préaux and of St. Wandrille. 
0 @N6000000004533071828@ NOTE Roger de Beaumont; Seigneur (feudal Lord) of Beaumont, Pontaudemer,Brionne and Vatteville, Normandy; married Adeline, sister of HughCount of Meulan and daughter by his 1st wife of Waleran Count ofMeulan. [Burke's Peerage]
1 CONT
1 CONT ---------------------------------------
1 CONT
1 CONT The well-known Roger de Beaumont held Sturminster Marshal, Dorset, in1086; it descended to the counts of Meulan through Roger's eldest son,Robert count of Meulan.  That Roger took his name from Beaumont is apart of the general his
1 CONC tory of Normandy.  It follows that Roger'sdescendants, the counts of Meulan, the Earls of Leicester, and theEarls of Warwick, all derive from Beaumont-le-Roger.  [The Origins ofSome Anglo-Norman Families]
0 @N6000000005076818673@ NOTE Seigneur de Portaudemer
1 CONT
1 CONT Roger de Beaumont, Seigneur de Portaudemer was the son of Humphrey de Vieilles, Seigneur de Vielles et Pont-Audemer and Aubreye (?).1 He died on 29 November 1094.
1 CONT      Roger de Beaumont, Seigneur de Portaudemer was also known as Roger de Bellomont, Earl of Mellent.2 He gained the title of Seigneur de Portaudemer.
1 CONT Child of Roger de Beaumont, Seigneur de Portaudemer
1 CONT Anice (?) 3
1 CONT Children of Roger de Beaumont, Seigneur de Portaudemer and Adeline de Meulan
1 CONT Henry de Newburgh, 1st Earl of Warwick+ d. 11232
1 CONT Robert de Meulan, 1st Earl of Leicester+ b. c 1046, d. 5 Jun 1118
1 CONT Citations
1 CONT [S6] G.E. Cokayne; with Vicary Gibbs, H.A. Doubleday, Geoffrey H. White, Duncan Warrand and Lord Howard de Walden, editors, The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain and the United Kingdom, Extant, Extinct 
1 CONC or Dormant, new ed., 13 volumes in 14 (1910-1959; reprint in 6 volumes, Gloucester, U.K.: Alan Sutton Publishing, 2000), volume VII, page 521. Hereinafter cited as The Complete Peerage.
1 CONT [S22] Sir Bernard Burke, C.B. LL.D., A Genealogical History of the Dormant, Abeyant, Forfeited, and Extinct Peerages of the British Empire, new edition (1883; reprint, Baltimore, Maryland: Genealogical Publishing Company, 1978), p
1 CONC age 399. Hereinafter cited as Burkes Extinct Peerage.
1 CONT [S6] Cokayne, and others, The Complete Peerage, volume III, page 167.
