1 CONT Sometime around 1093 he tried to marry Matilda (or Edith), daughter of king Malcolm III of Scotland. She instead married Henry I of England, and this may be the cause of William's great dislike of Henry I, which was to be his appa
1 CONC rent motivator in the following years.
1 CONT
1 CONT He accompanied Robert Curthose in his 1101 invasion of England, and afterwards lost his English lands and titles and was exiled to Normandy. There he complained to Curthose that he expended great effort in the duke's behalf and ha
1 CONC d in return lost nearly everything. Curthose's return to England in 1103 was apparently made to convince his brother to restore William's earldom. This was successful, though Curthose had to give up he had received after the 1101 
1 CONC invasion, and subsequently William was loyal to king Henry.
1 CONT
1 CONT To further insure William's loyalty Henry considered marrying him to one of his many illegitimate daughters. He was however disuaded by Archbishop Anselm of Canterbury, for any of the daughters would have been within the prohibite
1 CONC d degrees of consanguinity. The precise nature of the consanguinous relationship Anselm had in mind has been much debated, but it is most likely he was referring to common descent from the father of duchess Gunnor.
1 CONT
1 CONT William was one of the commanders on Henry's side (against Robert Curthose) at the Battle of Tinchebray in 1108. Afterwards, with his loyalty thus proven, he became more prominent in Henry's court.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1110 Curthose's son William Clito escaped along with Helias of Saint-Saens, and afterwards Warenne received the forfeited Saint-Saens lands, which were very near his own in upper Normandy. By this maneuver king Henry further as
1 CONC sured his loyalty, for the successful return of Clito would mean at the very least Warenne's loss of this new territory.
1 CONT
1 CONT He fought at the Battle of Bremule in 1119, and was at Henry's deathbed 1135.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1118 William acquired the royal-blooded bride he desired when married Elizabeth de Vermandois. She was a daughter of count Hugh of Vermandois, a son of Henry I of France, and was the widow of Robert de Beaumont, 1st Earl of Lei
1 CONC cester.
1 CONT
1 CONT By Elizabeth he had 3 sons and 2 daughters:
1 CONT
1 CONT William de Warenne, 3rd Earl of Surrey; 
1 CONT Reginald, who inherited his father's property in upper Normandy. He married Adeline, daughter of William, lord of Wormgay in Norfolk, by whom he had a son William, whose daughter and sole heir Beatrice married first Dodo, lord Bar
1 CONC dolf, and secondly Hubert de Burgh; 
1 CONT Ralph 
1 CONT Gundrada, who married first Roger de Beaumont, 2nd Earl of Warwick, and second William, lord of Kendal, and is most remembered for expelling king Stephen's garrison from Warwick Castle; 
1 CONT Ada, who married Henry of Scotland, 3rd Earl of Huntingdon 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT References
1 CONT C. Warren Hollister, "The Taming of a Turbulent Earl: Henry I and William of Warenne", Historical Reflections 3 (1976) 83-91 
1 CONT C. Warren Hollister, Henry I (2001) 
