0 @N6000000002247892029@ NOTE Henry I, was also Duke of Burgundy, Count of Paris
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry I, c. 1008–1060, king of France (1031–60), son and successor of King Robert II. To defend his throne against his mother, his brothers Robert and Eudes, and subsequently against the count of Blois, he secured, at the cost of 
1 CONC territorial concessions, the aid of Robert I, duke of Normandy, and of Geoffrey Martel, count of Anjou. After the submission of his brother Robert, Henry unwisely invested him with the duchy of Burgundy, setting up a powerful riva
1 CONC l to the French kingdom. He found the chief enemy of his later reign in Robert of Normandy's son William, later William I of England, who successfully resisted two invasions by Henry. Henry was succeeded by his son Philip I.
0 @N6000000003853681684@ NOTE Henry I of France
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia 
1 CONT
1 CONT Reign As co-King: 14 May 1027 – 20 July 1031;
1 CONT As senior King: 20 July 1031 – 4 August 1060 
1 CONT Coronation 14 May 1027, Cathedral of Reims 
1 CONT Titles Duke of Burgundy (1016 – 1032) 
1 CONT Born 4 May 1008(1008-05-04) 
1 CONT  Reims, France 
1 CONT Died 4 August 1060 (aged 52) 
1 CONT  Vitry-en-Brie, France 
1 CONT Buried Saint Denis Basilica, Paris, France 
1 CONT Predecessor Robert II 
1 CONT Successor Philip I 
1 CONT Consort Matilda of Frisia (d.1044)
1 CONT Anne of Kiev (between 1024 and 1032 – 1075) 
1 CONT Issue Philip I (1052 – 1108)
1 CONT Hugh the Great, Count of Vermandois (1053 – 1101) 
1 CONT Royal House House of Capet 
1 CONT Father Robert II (March 27, 972 – July 20, 1031) 
1 CONT Mother Constance of Arles (973 - July 25, 1034) 
1 CONT French Monarchy 
1 CONT Direct Capetians 
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry I 
1 CONT    Philip I 
1 CONT    Hugh, Count of Vermandois 
1 CONT Henry I (4 May 1008 – 4 August 1060) was King of France from 1031 to his death. The royal demesne of France reached its lowest point in terms of size during his reign and for this reason he is often seen as emblematic of the weakn
1 CONC ess of the early Capetians.
1 CONT
1 CONT A member of the House of Capet, Henry was born in Reims, the son of King Robert II (972–1031) and Constance of Arles (973–1032). He was crowned King of France at the Cathedral in Reims on May 14, 1027, in the Capetian tradition, w
1 CONC hile his father still lived. He had little influence and power until he became sole ruler on his father's death.
1 CONT
1 CONT The reign of Henry I, like those of his predecessors, was marked by territorial struggles. Initially, he joined his brother Robert, with the support of their mother, in a revolt against his father (1025). His mother, however, supp
1 CONC orted Robert as heir to the old king, on whose death Henry was left to deal with his rebel sibling. In 1032, he placated his brother by giving him the duchy of Burgundy which his father had given him in 1016.
1 CONT
1 CONT In an early strategic move, Henry came to the rescue of his very young nephew-in-law, the newly appointed Duke William of Normandy (who would go on to become William the Conqueror), to suppress a revolt by William's vassals. In 10
1 CONC 47, Henry secured the dukedom for William in their decisive victory over the vassals at the Battle of Val-ès-Dunes near Caen.
1 CONT
1 CONT A few years later, when William, who was cousin to King Edward the Confessor of England (1042–66), married Matilda, the daughter of the count of Flanders, Henry feared William's potential power. In 1054, and again in 1057, Henry w
1 CONC ent to war to try to conquer Normandy from William, but on both occasions he was defeated. Despite his efforts, Henry I's twenty-nine-year reign saw feudal power in France reach its pinnacle.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry had three meetings with Henry III, Holy Roman Emperor—all at Ivois. In early 1043, he met him to discuss the marriage of the emperor with Agnes of Poitou, the daughter of Henry's vassal. In October 1048, the two Henries met 
1 CONC again, but the subject of this meeting eludes us. The final meeting took place in May 1056. It concerned disputes over Lorraine. The debate over the duchy became so heated that the king of France challenged his German counterpart 
1 CONC to single combat. The emperor, however, was not so much a warrior and he fled in the night. But Henry did not get Lorraine.
1 CONT
1 CONT King Henry I died on August 4, 1060 in Vitry-en-Brie, France, and was interred in Saint Denis Basilica. He was succeeded by his son, Philip I of France, who was 7 at the time of his death; for six years Henry I's Queen, Anne of Ki
1 CONC ev, ruled as regent.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was also Duke of Burgundy from 1016 to 1032, when he abdicated the duchy to his brother Robert Capet.
0 @N6000000002909010018@ NOTE Henry I (4 May 1008 – 4 August 1060) was King of France from 1031 to his death. The royal demesne of France reached its lowest point in terms of size during his reign and for this reason he is often seen as emblematic of the weakn
1 CONC ess of the early Capetians. This is not entirely agreed upon, however, as other historians regard him as a strong but realistic king, who was forced to conduct a policy mindful of the limitations of the French monarchy.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Reign
