0 @N6000000002187734757@ NOTE Gundred
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Gundred was the wife of William de Warenne. With him, she built Lewes Castle and founded Lewes Priory.
0 @N6000000001744810718@ NOTE [s2.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT Source: Church of JC of the LDS "Ancestral File" CD-Rom database, ver 4.17. This source names Gundred as the daughter of William the Conqueror and his wife Matilda.
1 CONT
1 CONT However, David C. Douglas contends: "The view once held that Matilda was already married [to Gerbod] when William sought her hand , and was then the mother of a daughter, Gundrada, later the wife of William of Warenne, has now bee
1 CONC n conclusively disproved by the researches of Chester Waters and Sir Charles Clay. There is no reason to suppose that Gundrada was the daughter of either William or Matilda."
1 CONT
1 CONT He continues in a different section: "But of all this group of Flemish connections, the most interesting was a certain Gerbod, who was probably advocatus of the abbey of Saint-Bertin. Described as Flandrensis, he was apparently th
1 CONC e son of another advocatus of the same name, and in 1070 he was entrusted with the earldom of Chester. At about the same time his brother, named Fredric, received lands in East Anglia while his sister, Gundrada, married William of
1 CONC  Warenne. It was an important connection, and if it had been established permanently in England, the Flemish element...might have been larger..." But Gerbod returned to Flanders in less than a year, while Fredric was probably kill
1 CONC ed by the followers of Hereward in 1079.
1 CONT
1 CONT David C. Douglas, "William the Conqueror: The Norman Impact Upon England" (Berkeley: Univ of Calif Press, 1964) 76 and 392.
1 CONT
1 CONT See also Charles T. Clay, "Early Yorkshire Charters", V. 8, 40-46 and Chester Waters, "Gundrada de Warenne" (Exeter, 1884) and C. Brunel, "Actes--Comtes de Pontieu", #4.Source: Church of JC of the LDS "Ancestral File" CD-Rom datab
1 CONC ase, ver 4.17. This source names Gundred as the daughter of William the Conqueror and his wife Matilda.
1 CONT
1 CONT However, David C. Douglas contends: "The view once held that Matilda was already married [to Gerbod] when William sought her hand , and was then the mother of a daughter, Gundrada, later the wife of William of Warenne, has now bee
1 CONC n conclusively disproved by the researches of Chester Waters and Sir Charles Clay. There is no reason to suppose that Gundrada was the daughter of either William or Matilda."
1 CONT
1 CONT He continues in a different section: "But of all this group of Flemish connections, the most interesting was a certain Gerbod, who was probably advocatus of the abbey of Saint-Bertin. Described as Flandrensis, he was apparently th
1 CONC e son of another advocatus of the same name, and in 1070 he was entrusted with the earldom of Chester. At about the same time his brother, named Fredric, received lands in East Anglia while his sister, Gundrada, married William of
1 CONC  Warenne. It was an important connection, and if it had been established permanently in England, the Flemish element...might have been larger..." But Gerbod returned to Flanders in less than a year, while Fredric was probably kill
1 CONC ed by the followers of Hereward in 1079.
1 CONT
1 CONT David C. Douglas, "William the Conqueror: The Norman Impact Upon England" (Berkeley: Univ of Calif Press, 1964) 76 and 392.
1 CONT
1 CONT See also Charles T. Clay, "Early Yorkshire Charters", V. 8, 40-46 and Chester Waters, "Gundrada de Warenne" (Exeter, 1884) and C. Brunel, "Actes--Comtes de Pontieu", #4.
