0 @N6000000002187734746@ NOTE William de Warenne, 1st Earl of Surrey
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT William de Warenne, 1st Earl of Surrey, (died 1088) was one of the Norman aristocrats who fought at the Battle of Hastings and became great landowners in England.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was a son of Ralph de Warenne and Emma and a grandnephew of duchess Gunnor, wife of duke Richard I of Normandy. As a young man he helped secure duke William's hold on Normandy, most notably in the campaigns of 1052 through 1054
1 CONC  which culminated in the Battle of Mortemer. After this battle Roger de Mortemer forfeited most of his lands, and the duke gave them to William. The de Warenne surname derives from the castle of that name on the River Varenne, whi
1 CONC ch flows through the territory William acquired in Upper Normandy.
1 CONT
1 CONT William was one of the nobles who advised duke William when the decision to invade England was being considered. He fought at Hastings, and afterwards received the Rape of Lewes in Sussex, and subsequently lands in twelve other sh
1 CONC ires. In addition to the cluster around Lewes, there were clusters around the castles he built at Castle Acre in Norfolk and Conisbrough in Yorkshire. By the time of the Domesday survey he was one of the wealthiest landholders in 
1 CONC England.
1 CONT
1 CONT William was loyal to William II, and it was probably after the rebellion of 1088 that he was created Earl of Surrey. He died shortly afterwards of wounds he received while helping suppress the rebellion.
1 CONT
1 CONT He married twice, first to Gundred (Latin: Gundrada), sister of Gerbod, Earl of Chester, and secondly to a sister of Richard Gouet. William and Gundred had three children: William de Warenne, 2nd Earl of Surrey (d. 1138); Edith de
1 CONC  Warenne who married Gerard de Gournay; and Reynold de Warenne, who inherited lands from his mother in Flanders and died before 1118.
1 CONT
1 CONT It was at one time thought that Gundred was a daughter of William the Conqueror and his wife Matilda of Flanders. This was disproved in the 19th Century but nevertheless remains in many faulty genealogies. William and Gundred were
1 CONC  married around 1070, when her brother Frederick was killed by Hereward the Wake. [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT References
1 CONT Victoria Chandler, "Gundrada de Warenne and the Victorian Gentleman-Scholars", Southern History 12 (1990) 68 
1 CONT Edward A. Freeman, "The Parentage of Gundrada, Wife of William of Warren", English Historical Review 3 (1888) 680-701 
1 CONT C. P. Lewis, "The Earldom of Surrey and the Date of Domesday Book", Historical Research 63 (1990) 
1 CONT Notes on the family of Gerbod, Earl of Chester, and of Gundred, wife of William de Warenne, Earl of Surrey 
1 CONT William Warenne, reprinted from the 1917 Dictionary of National Biograp
0 @N6000000000437356742@ NOTE Name Suffix:<NSFX> Earl of Surrey Still Living.
0 @N6000000001744806502@ NOTE [s2.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT William de Warren was a noble from East Normandy who is listed as one of the commanders who accompanied William the Conqueror at the Battle of Hastings, and helped to finance the venture. He is also named as a baron in the Magna C
1 CONC arta. He seems to have been closely associated with Hugh de Gournay.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sources on this line: United Ancestries Linked Pedigrees CD-Rom (1992): Cites as source "English Baronies" by I.J. Sanders. See <Http://www.canlink.com/hon/norman.htm> for list of those at the Battle of Hastings, and <http://www.c
1 CONC anlink.com/hon/baron1,htm> for the major feudal barons of the Magna Carta.
1 CONT
1 CONT Historian David Douglas recounts that after the Battle of Arques, in February 1054, "William [of Normandy's] force was at any rate large enough for it to be divided into two contingents opertating to the west and east of the Seine
1 CONC . The Duke himself with men from middle Normandy faced the invaders who were advancing under the French king through the Evrecin. On the other side of the river, Robert (count of Eu) with Hugh of Gournay, Walter Giffard, Roger of 
1 CONC Mortemer, and the young William of Warenne, came out from their own lands to withstand the eastern incursion [of the French force] under Count Odo and Count Rainald... [which] seems to have been unprepared for this levy from easte
1 CONC rn Normandy. Having entered the duchy by way of Neufchatel-en-Bray, [the French force] advanced to the neighborhood of Mortemer, and there gave itself up to unrestrained rape and pillage. Widely scattered and demoralized, it thus 
1 CONC offered itself as an easy target....The slaughter was considerable." The battle was of decisive importance...the Norman victory was complete, and the King of France withdrew. "The battle of Mortemer reflected a major crisis in Nor
1 CONC man history, and never again was Duke William to be faced by so formidable a threat to ...his power."  David C. Douglas, "William the Conqueror" (Berkeley: Univ of Calif Press, 1964,  p. 68)
1 CONT
1 CONT The Warennes were named by Christopher Brooke as one of the families in English history who formed the backbone of the baronial class after the Norman Conquest, who had made their fortune in Normandy either in the service of WIlli
1 CONC am or one of his immediate predecessors. See Brooke's "From Alfred to Henry III, 871-1272" (NY: Norton, 1961) 108.
1 CONT
1 CONT Brian Tompsett of the University of Hull <B.C.Tompsett@dcs.hull.ac.uk> follows ancestry  in "Aspects of Robert of Torigny's Genealogies Revisited" in Nottigham Medieval Studies xxxvii, 1993, pp 21-27, as cited by AB Wilson and S B
1 CONC aldwin <abwilson@uclink2.berkeley.edu>.William de Warren was a noble from East Normandy who is listed as one of the commanders who accompanied William the Conqueror at the Battle of Hastings, and helped to finance the venture. He 
1 CONC is also named as a baron in the Magna Carta. He seems to have been closely associated with Hugh de Gournay.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sources on this line: United Ancestries Linked Pedigrees CD-Rom (1992): Cites as source "English Baronies" by I.J. Sanders. See <Http://www.canlink.com/hon/norman.htm> for list of those at the Battle of Hastings, and <http://www.c
1 CONC anlink.com/hon/baron1,htm> for the major feudal barons of the Magna Carta.
1 CONT
1 CONT Historian David Douglas recounts that after the Battle of Arques, in February 1054, "William [of Normandy's] force was at any rate large enough for it to be divided into two contingents opertating to the west and east of the Seine
1 CONC . The Duke himself with men from middle Normandy faced the invaders who were advancing under the French king through the Evrecin. On the other side of the river, Robert (count of Eu) with Hugh of Gournay, Walter Giffard, Roger of 
1 CONC Mortemer, and the young William of Warenne, came out from their own lands to withstand the eastern incursion [of the French force] under Count Odo and Count Rainald... [which] seems to have been unprepared for this levy from easte
1 CONC rn Normandy. Having entered the duchy by way of Neufchatel-en-Bray, [the French force] advanced to the neighborhood of Mortemer, and there gave itself up to unrestrained rape and pillage. Widely scattered and demoralized, it thus 
1 CONC offered itself as an easy target....The slaughter was considerable." The battle was of decisive importance...the Norman victory was complete, and the King of France withdrew. "The battle of Mortemer reflected a major crisis in Nor
1 CONC man history, and never again was Duke William to be faced by so formidable a threat to ...his power."  David C. Douglas, "William the Conqueror" (Berkeley: Univ of Calif Press, 1964,  p. 68)
1 CONT
1 CONT The Warennes were named by Christopher Brooke as one of the families in English history who formed the backbone of the baronial class after the Norman Conquest, who had made their fortune in Normandy either in the service of WIlli
1 CONC am or one of his immediate predecessors. See Brooke's "From Alfred to Henry III, 871-1272" (NY: Norton, 1961) 108.
1 CONT
1 CONT Brian Tompsett of the University of Hull <B.C.Tompsett@dcs.hull.ac.uk> follows ancestry  in "Aspects of Robert of Torigny's Genealogies Revisited" in Nottigham Medieval Studies xxxvii, 1993, pp 21-27, as cited by AB Wilson and S B
1 CONC aldwin <abwilson@uclink2.berkeley.edu>.
0 @N6000000001783338146@ NOTE Basic Life Information
1 CONT
1 CONT William was a son of Rodulf II de Warenne and Emma and a grandnephew of duchess Gunnor, wife of duke Richard I of Normandy. The de Warenne surname derives from the castle of that name on the River Varenne, which flows through the 
1 CONC territory William acquired in Upper Normandy in the region today called Bellencombre.
1 CONT
1 CONT As a young man, William played a prominent role in protecting the Norman realm of the future William the Conqueror's from a major invasion by the King of France in February 1054 at the Battle of Mortemer. After this battle Roger d
1 CONC e Mortemer forfeited most of his lands, and the duke gave them to William.
1 CONT
1 CONT Battle of Hastings
1 CONT
1 CONT William was one of the nobles who advised Duke William when the decision to invade England was being considered. He fought at Hastings, and afterwards received the Rape of Lewes in Sussex, and subsequently lands in twelve other sh
1 CONC ires. He built castles at Lewes (Sussex), Reigate (Surrey), Castle Acre (Norfolk) and Conisbrough in Yorkshire. By the time of the Domesday survey he was one of the wealthiest landholders in England with holdings in 12 counties.
1 CONT
1 CONT Defending the Realm
1 CONT
1 CONT He fought against rebels at the Isle of Ely in 1071 where he showed a special desire to hunt down Hereward the Wake who had murdered his brother the year before.
1 CONT
1 CONT William was loyal to William II William Rufus), and it was probably in early 1088 that he was created Earl of Surrey.
1 CONT
1 CONT Marriages and Children
1 CONT
1 CONT He was married twice.
1 CONT
1 CONT First, William married Gundred (Latin: Gundrada), sister of Gerbod the Fleming, Earl of Chester. The children of William and Gundred were:
1 CONT William de Warenne, 2nd Earl of Surrey (d. 1138)
1 CONT Edith de Warenne who married Gerard de Gournay
1 CONT Reynold de Warenne, who inherited lands from his mother in Flanders and died before 1118
1 CONT
1 CONT Second, William married  a sister of Richard Gouet.
1 CONT
1 CONT Death
1 CONT
1 CONT William died shortly after becoming Earl of Surrey from wounds he received while helping suppress the rebellion of 1088. At the time of his death, it has been estimated that his wealth was equivalent to £73.9 billion in today's mo
1 CONC ney.
1 CONT
1 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_de_Warenne,_1st_Earl_of_Surrey
0 @N6000000003051205571@ NOTE MISC: Earl of Warren and Surrey, a near kinsman of William the Conqueror William de WARREN E arl of Surrey and Warenne (1055-1088) [Pedigree] Son of Ralph de WARENNE (998-1074) and Emm a de ST.MARTINEarl of Surrey. Seigneur de Ver
1 CONC ennes, near Dieppe, France.REF Baronage1. Lord o f Reigate, Lews, Coningsburgh; Lord of Bellencombre, in Normandy.Commander in the Norman Army , Sep 1066.
1 CONT
1 CONT The following is from pboren@pnut.rand.org (Pat Boren) and may contain errors.
1 CONT
1 CONT WILLIAM de WARENNE was considered a near kinsman of William the Conqueror. He received larg e grants of land in England in recognition of his distinguished part in the Battle of Hasting s. In 1075, he was appointed joint chief jus
1 CONC ticiar and helped suppress there bellion of the e arls of Hereford and Norfolk. In 1077 he founded the St. Pancras Prioryof Lewes (where he i s buried), the first house of the Cluniacorder in England. The position of his castle at
1 CONC  Lewe s rendered his loyalty especially useful to King William Rufus during the rebellion of1088, f or which he was granted the earldom of Surrey. In addition to Lewes,William held over 40 mano rs in Sussex. He married Gundred and
1 CONC  had Reginald, Edith, and William.E-Mailb. ABT 1055, of B ellencombe, S-Infr, Franced. 24 Jun 1088, Lewes, Surrey, Eng.b. BEF 1036 d. 24 Jun 1089
1 CONT
1 CONT [From "The Origins of Some Anglo-Norman Families"]For this identification see Mr. Loyd's pape r 'The Origin of the Family of Warenne' in Yorkshire Arch. Journal, vol. xxxi, pp. 97-113. T he hamlet of Varenne lies on the river Vare
1 CONC nne c. 2 miles S of Arques and c. 13 miles N of Be llencombre. The latter place, arr. Dieppe, cant. Bellencombre, where there was a castle, bec ame the caput of the Warenne honour in Normandy.
1 CONT
1 CONT [Wikipedia, "William de Warenne, 1st Earl of Surrey]
1 CONT William de Warenne, 1st Earl of Surrey, (died 1088) was one of the Norman aristocrats who fought at the Battle of Hastings and became great landowners in England.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was a son of Ralph de Warenne and Emma and a grandnephew of duchess Gunnor, wife of duke Richard I of Normandy. As a young man he helped secure duke William's hold on Normandy, most notably in the campaigns of 1052 through 1054
1 CONC  which culminated in the Battle of Mortemer. After this battle Roger de Mortemer forfeited most of his lands, and the duke gave them to William. The de Warenne surname derives from the castle of that name on the River Varenne, whi
1 CONC ch flows through the territory William acquired in Upper Normandy.
1 CONT
1 CONT William was one of the nobles who advised duke William when the decision to invade England was being considered. He fought at Hastings, and afterwards received the Rape of Lewes in Sussex, and subsequently lands in twelve other sh
1 CONC ires. In addition to the cluster around Lewes, there were clusters around the castles he built at Castle Acre in Norfolk and Conisbrough in Yorkshire. By the time of the Domesday survey he was one of the wealthiest landholders in 
1 CONC England.
1 CONT
1 CONT William was loyal to William II, and it was probably in early 1088 that he was created Earl of Surrey. He died shortly afterwards of wounds he received while helping suppress the rebellion of 1088.
1 CONT
1 CONT Family
1 CONT He married twice:
1 CONT
1 CONT First, Gundred (Latin: Gundrada), sister of Gerbod the Fleming, Earl of Chester.
1 CONT Second, to a sister of Richard Gouet
1 CONT
1 CONT Children of William and Gundred
1 CONT - William de Warenne, 2nd Earl of Surrey (d. 1138)
1 CONT - Edith de Warenne who married Gerard de Gournay
1 CONT - Reynold de Warenne, who inherited lands from his mother in Flanders and died before 1118
