0 @N6000000009304989116@ NOTE #Générale#Comte de Varennes (en Normandie) et de Surrey (en Angleterre). Participe à la conquête de l'Angleterre aux côtés de Guillaume Le Conquérant. Personnage cependant un peu énigmatique, à la fois très puissant à l'époque et 
1 CONC très mal connu.
0 @N6000000003403249610@ NOTE [alfred_descendants10gen_fromrootsweb_bartont.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT (CP IV 670 chart III, VII 520, 523-526, 737, X 351). Weis 50-24-2h.f.
1 CONT Note:  Above Weis 50-24-h. refers to 162-25.  There is no such no. in 5th Ed.
1 CONT Earl of Warren, cr. Earl of Surrey, a companion of The Conqueror. (YFT. p. 127).
1 CONT
1 CONT "Earl of Warrenne in France, cousin of William the Conqueror, was created Earl of Surrey; Lord of Reigate, Conningsburg; Lord of Bellencombre, in Normandy; born before 1036; Kinght; Count de Warenne in Normandy; Councellor in Norm
1 CONC andy, 1066; Councellor of Regency March-December, 1067; Honory Brother of the Abby of Cluni about 1076; founder and patron of Lewes and Castleacre priories; d. June 14, 1089.
1 CONT He came to England with the Conqueror, and having distinguished himself at the battle of Hastings, obtained an immense portion of the public spoliation.  He had large grants of lands in several counties, amongst which were the Bar
1 CONC ony of Lewes in Sussex, and the maners of Carletune and Bennington in Lincolnshire.  So extensive, indeed, were those grants that his possessions resembled more the dominions of a sovereign prince than the estate of a subject.  Wh
1 CONC ile in the office of Justicar, some great disturbers of the public peace having refused to apper before him and his colleague, in obediance to citation, the Earl took up arms and defeated the rebels in a battle at Fagadune, when, 
1 CONC he is said, for the purpose of striking terror, to have cut off the right foot of each of his prisoners.  Of these rebels Ralph de Ware, Earl of Norfolk and Roger, Earl of Hereford, were the ringleaders.  His Lordship was also hig
1 CONC hly esteemed by King William Rufus.  He m. Gundred, daughter of King William I, before 1070; she d. May 27, 1085. Her Mother Matilda, Queen of England, was daughter of Baldwin V, Count of Flanders and his wife, Princess Adela, dau
1 CONC ghter of Robert II, the Pious,King of France."  (YFT p. 91)
