0 @N6000000003949643305@ NOTE He inherited from his father the Seigneur du Ponteautorf, de Torville, Torcy, Torny, and Torly, and became Sire du Ponteaudemer which became his principal residence and by which name he was commonly known. By his marriage he enhan
1 CONC ced his position among the Norman nobility, and he was a prominent figure during the reigns of Dukes Richard II., Richard III., and Robert "the Devil" (996-1035).
1 CONT Tourade, the eldest of the three sons of Torf, is said to be the ancestor of Beaumont or Bellomont, Counts of Mellent, in France, and Earls of Leicester. [Sir Bernard Burke, Dormant, Abeyant, Forfeited, and Extinct Peerages, Burke
1 CONC 's Peerage, Ltd., London, 1883, p. 261, Harcourt, Barons Harcourt, of Stanton Harcourt, co. Oxford] He was a prominent figure during the reigns of Dukes Richard II, Richard II, and Robert "the Devil" (996-1035).
0 @N6000000004533071031@ NOTE Turton has Tourade m. to Eva (Dulceline) de Crepon, but Todd A.Farmerie states that her name was Duvelina.
