1 CONT Yaroslav was a notable patron of book culture and learning. In 1051, he had a Russian monk Ilarion proclaimed the metropolitan of Kiev, thus challenging old Byzantine tradition of placing Greeks on the episcopal sees. Ilarion's di
1 CONC scourse on Yaroslav and his father Vladimir is frequently cited as the first work of Old Russian literature
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1019, Yaroslav married Ingegerd Olofsdotter, daughter of the king of Sweden, and gave Ladoga to her as a marriage gift. There are good reasons to believe that before that time he had been married to a woman named Anna, of dispu
1 CONC ted extraction.[citation needed]
1 CONT
1 CONT In the Saint Sophia Cathedral, one may see a fresco representing the whole family: Yaroslav, Irene (as Ingigerd was known in Rus), their five daughters and five sons. Yaroslav married three of his daughters to foreign princes who 
1 CONC lived in exile at his court: Elizabeth of Kiev to Harald III of Norway (who had attained her hand by his military exploits in the Byzantine Empire); Anastasia of Kiev to the future Andrew I of Hungary, and the youngest daughter An
1 CONC ne of Kiev married Henry I of France and was the regent of France during their son's minority. Another daughter may have been the Agatha who married Edward the Exile, heir to the throne of England and was the mother of Edgar Æthel
1 CONC ing and St. Margaret of Scotland.
1 CONT
1 CONT Yaroslav had one son from the first marriage (his Christian name being Ilya), and 6 sons from the second marriage. Apprehending the danger that could ensue from divisions between brothers, he exhorted them to live in peace with ea
1 CONC ch other. The eldest of these, Vladimir of Novgorod, best remembered for building the Saint Sophia Cathedral in Novgorod, predeceased his father. Three other sons-Iziaslav, Sviatoslav, and Vsevolod-reigned in Kiev one after anothe
1 CONC r. The youngest children of Yaroslav were Igor of Volynia and Vyacheslav of Smolensk.
1 CONT
1 CONT <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yaroslav_I_the_Wise>
1 CONT
1 CONT Marriages and Children
1 CONT
1 CONT Yaroslav had a son, but the name of the mother is unknown:
1 CONT Elias, Prince of Novgorod
1 CONT
1 CONT Yaroslav married 1019 to Ingeborg Olafsdottir, a daughter of Olaf Skötkonung, King of Sweden. Yaroslav and Ingeborg had the following children:
1 CONT Anne of Kiev
1 CONT Izyaslav I, Prince of Kiev, 1054 - 1078, deposed 1068 - 1069 and 1073 - 1076.
1 CONT Svyatoslav II, Prince of Kiev, 1073 - 1076
1 CONT Vsevolod I, Prince of Kiev, 1076 - 1093, deposed 1077 - 1078.
1 CONT Ellisif ( or Elizabeth) of Kiev, married first in 1045 to Harald III Hardrada, King of Norway, who was slain on September 25, 1066 at the Battle of Stamford Bridge.  Ellisif married second in 1067 to Svend II, King of Denmark.
1 CONT Valdimar ( or Holti) the Nimble, Prince of Novgorod, 1036 - 1052.
1 CONT Anastasia Agmunda of Kiev, married circa 1046 to Andrew I, King of Hungary.
1 CONT Viacheslav, Prince of Smolensk 1054 - 1056
1 CONT Igor of Vladimir, Prince of Vladimir 1054 - 1060
1 CONT Dobronega (or Maria) of Kiev, married 1038 to Casimir I, King of Poland. (? Vladimir too?)
1 CONT
