0 @N6000000006348351716@ NOTE Yaroslav I
1 CONT  born 980
1 CONT died Feb. 2, 1054
1 CONT
1 CONT byname  Yaroslav The Wise,  Russian  Yaroslav Mudry  grand prince of Kiev from 1019 to 1054.
1 CONT
1 CONT A son of the grand prince Vladimir, he was vice-regent of Novgorod at the time of his father's death in 1015. Then his eldest surviving brother, Svyatopolk the Accursed, killed three of his other brothers and seized power in Kiev.
1 CONC  Yaroslav, with the active support of the Novgorodians and the help of Varangian (Viking) mercenaries, defeated Svyatopolk and became the grand prince of Kiev in 1019.
1 CONT
1 CONT Yaroslav began consolidating the Kievan state through both cultural and administrative improvements and through military campaigns. He promoted the spread of Christianity in the Kievan state, gathered a large collection of books, 
1 CONC and employed many scribes to translate Greek religious texts into the Slavic language. He founded churches and monasteries and issued statutes regulating the legal position of the Christian Church and the rights of the clergy. Wit
1 CONC h the help of Byzantine architects and craftsmen, Yaroslav fortified and beautified Kiev along Byzantine lines. He built the majestic Cathedral of St. Sophia and the famous Golden Gate of the Kievan fortress. Under Yaroslav the co
1 CONC dification of legal customs and princely enactments was begun, and this work served as the basis for a law code called the Russkaya Pravda (“Russian Justice”).
1 CONT
1 CONT Yaroslav pursued an active foreign policy, and his forces won several notable military victories. He regained Galicia from the Poles, decisively defeated the nomadic Pechenegs on the Kievan state's southern frontier, and expanded 
1 CONC Kievan possessions in the Baltic region, suppressing the Lithuanians, Estonians, and Finnish tribes. His military campaign against Constantinople in 1043 was a failure, however.
1 CONT
1 CONT Trade with the East and West played an important role in Kievan Rus in the 11th century, and Yaroslav maintained diplomatic relations with the European states. His daughters Elizabeth, Anna, and Anastasia were married respectively
1 CONC  to Harald III of Norway, Henry I of France, and Andrew I of Hungary.
1 CONT
1 CONT In his testament, Yaroslav sought to prevent a power struggle among his five sons by dividing his empire among them and enjoining the younger four sons to obey the eldest, Izyaslav, who was to succeed his father as grand prince of
1 CONC  Kiev. This advice had no lasting effect, and civil war ensued after Yaroslav's death.
1 CONT
1 CONT "Yaroslav I." Encyclopædia Britannica from Encyclopædia Britannica Premium Service. <http://www.britannica.com/eb/article-9077846> [Accessed November 13, 2005].
0 @N6000000003086748075@ NOTE Yaroslav the Wise  Upon the death of Vladimir in 1015, his dominions were divided among his sons, and strife immediately developed. Vladimir's eldest son, Svyatopolk, called The Accursed (reigned 1015, 1018-1019), held the supreme
1 CONC  power and, to secure his position, murdered his brothers Boris and Gleb. Svyatopolk was, in turn, defeated and deposed by his brother Yaroslav the Wise, prince of Novgorod. Yaroslav attempted to recreate the empire of his grandfa
1 CONC ther, Svyatoslav, and by 1036 had succeeded in making himself ruler of all Russia. With him, the Kievan Rus state reached its greatest power. Yaroslav made Kiev an imperial capital with magnificent buildings, including the notable
1 CONC  Hagia Sophia of Kiev (Cathedral of the Holy Wisdom). Schools were opened, and the grand duke revised the first Russian law code, the Russkaya Pravda (Russian Truth). To consolidate the position of his heirs, Yaroslav devised a sy
1 CONC stem of precedence, grading the various principalities from the smallest to Kiev, the most powerful, so that, as a grand duke of Kiev died, each vassal below him was moved to a larger principality, ending with the throne of Kiev.
0 @N6000000001783604390@ NOTE Basic Life Information
1 CONT
1 CONT Kievan Rus achieved its greatest power and splendor under Yaroslav the Wise in the 11th century. Yaroslav made Kiev a great city and built magnificent buildings, including the notable Cathedral of Saint Sophia or Hagia Sophia of K
1 CONC iev. Yaroslav did much to develop Kievan Rus education and culture. He also revised the first Russian law code, the so-called Russkaya Pravda or Russian Justice.  After his death in 1054, Kievan Rus declined.  Yaroslav's grandson,
1 CONC  Vladimir II Monomachus, made the final attempt to unite Kievan Rus, but after his death in 1125 the fragmentation continued as other Kievan Rus principalities challenged Kiev's supremacy.
1 CONT
1 CONT By the 13th century, the East Slavic lands became a loose federation of city-states, held together by common language, religion, traditions, and customs. Although ruled by members of the house of Rurik, these city-states were ofte
1 CONC n at war with one another.  The area became an easy target for bands of invading Asiatic Mongols.
1 CONT
1 CONT http://www.robertsewell.ca/kiev.html
1 CONT
1 CONT Rise to the Throne
1 CONT
1 CONT Early years of Yaroslav's life are shrouded in mystery. He was one of the numerous sons of Vladimir the Great, presumably his second by Rogneda of Polotsk, although his actual age (as stated in the Primary Chronicle and corroborat
1 CONC ed by the examination of his skeleton in the 1930s) would place him among the youngest children of Vladimir. It has been suggested that he was a child begotten out of wedlock after Vladimir's divorce with Rogneda and his marriage 
1 CONC to Anna Porphyrogeneta, or even that he was a child of Anna Porphyrogeneta herself. Yaroslav figures prominently in the Norse Sagas under the name of Jarisleif the Lame; his legendary lameness (probably resulting from an arrow wou
1 CONC nd) was corroborated by the scientists who examined his relics.
1 CONT
1 CONT In his youth, Yaroslav was sent by his father to rule the northern lands around Rostov the Great but was transferred to Novgorod the Great, as befitted a senior heir to the throne, in 1010. While living there, he founded the town 
1 CONC of Yaroslavl (literally, Yaroslav's) on the Volga. His relations with father were apparently strained, and grew only worse on the news that Vladimir bequeathed the Kievan throne to his younger son, Boris. In 1014 Yaroslav refused 
1 CONC to pay tribute to Kiev and only Vladimir's death prevented a war.
1 CONT
1 CONT During the next four years Yaroslav waged a complicated and bloody war for Kiev against his half-brother Sviatopolk, who was supported by his father-in-law, Duke Boleslaus I of Poland. During the course of this struggle, several o
1 CONC ther brothers (Boris and Gleb, Svyatoslav) were brutally murdered. The Primary Chronicle accused Svyatopolk of planning those murders, while the Saga of Eymund is often interpreted as recounting the story of Boris's assassination 
1 CONC by the Varangians in the service of Yaroslav.
1 CONT
1 CONT Yaroslav defeated Svyatopolk in their first battle, in 1016, and Svyatopolk fled to Poland. But Svyatopolk returned with Polish troops furnished by his father-in-law Duke Boleslaus of Poland, seized Kiev and pushed Yaroslav back i
1 CONC nto Novgorod. In 1019, Yaroslav eventually prevailed over Svyatopolk and established his rule over Kiev. One of his first actions as a grand prince was to confer on the loyal Novgorodians (who had helped him to regain the throne),
1 CONC  numerous freedoms and privileges. Thus, the foundation for the Novgorod Republic was laid. The Novgorodians respected Yaroslav more than other Kievan princes and the princely residence in the city, next to the marketplace (and wh
1 CONC ere the veche often convened) was named the Yaroslavovo Dvorishche after him. It is thought that it was at that period that Yaroslav promulgated the first code of laws in the East Slavic lands, the Yaroslav's Justice, better known
1 CONC  as Russkaya Pravda.
1 CONT
1 CONT <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yaroslav_I_the_Wise>
1 CONT
1 CONT Reign
1 CONT
1 CONT Leaving aside the legitimacy of Yaroslav's claims to the Kievan throne and his postulated guilt in the murder of his brothers, Nestor and later Russian historians often represented him as a model of virtue and styled him the Wise.
1 CONC  A less appealing side of his personality may be revealed by the fact that he imprisoned his younger brother Sudislav for life. Yet another brother, Mstislav of Tmutarakan, whose distant realm bordered on the Northern Caucasus and
1 CONC  the Black Sea, hastened to Kiev and inflicted a heavy defeat on Yaroslav in 1024. Thereupon Yaroslav and Mstislav divided Kievan Rus: the area stretching left from the Dnieper, with the capital at Chernihiv, was ceded to Mstislav
1 CONC  until his death in 1036.
1 CONT
1 CONT In his foreign policy, Yaroslav relied on the Scandinavian alliance and attempted to weaken the Byzantine influence on Kiev. In 1030 he reconquered from the Poles Red Rus, and concluded an alliance with king Casimir I the Restorer
1 CONC , sealed by the latter's marriage to Yaroslav's sister Maria. In another successful military raid the same year, he conquered the Estonian fortress of Tarbatu, built his own fort in that place, which went by the name of Yuriev (af
1 CONC ter St George, or Yury, Yaroslav's patron saint) and forced the surrounding province of Ugaunia to pay annual tribute.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1043 Yaroslav staged a naval raid against Constantinople led by his son Vladimir and general Vyshata. Although the Rus' navy was defeated, Yaroslav managed to conclude the war with a favourable treaty and prestigious marriage o
1 CONC f his son Vsevolod to the emperor's daughter. It has been suggested that the peace was so advantageous because the Kievans had succeeded in taking a key Byzantine possession in Crimea, Chersones.
1 CONT
1 CONT To defend his state from the Pechenegs and other nomadic tribes threatening it from the south he constructed a line of forts, composed of Yuriev, Boguslav, Kaniv, Korsun, and Pereyaslav. To celebrate his decisive victory over the 
1 CONC Pechenegs in 1036 (who thereupon never were a threat to Kiev) he sponsored the construction of the Saint Sophia Cathedral in 1037. Other celebrated monuments of his reign, such as the Golden Gates of Kiev, have since perished.
1 CONT
1 CONT Yaroslav was a notable patron of book culture and learning. In 1051, he had a Russian monk Ilarion proclaimed the metropolitan of Kiev, thus challenging old Byzantine tradition of placing Greeks on the episcopal sees. Ilarion's di
1 CONC scourse on Yaroslav and his father Vladimir is frequently cited as the first work of Old Russian literature
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1019, Yaroslav married Ingegerd Olofsdotter, daughter of the king of Sweden, and gave Ladoga to her as a marriage gift. There are good reasons to believe that before that time he had been married to a woman named Anna, of dispu
1 CONC ted extraction.[citation needed]
1 CONT
1 CONT In the Saint Sophia Cathedral, one may see a fresco representing the whole family: Yaroslav, Irene (as Ingigerd was known in Rus), their five daughters and five sons. Yaroslav married three of his daughters to foreign princes who 
1 CONC lived in exile at his court: Elizabeth of Kiev to Harald III of Norway (who had attained her hand by his military exploits in the Byzantine Empire); Anastasia of Kiev to the future Andrew I of Hungary, and the youngest daughter An
1 CONC ne of Kiev married Henry I of France and was the regent of France during their son's minority. Another daughter may have been the Agatha who married Edward the Exile, heir to the throne of England and was the mother of Edgar Æthel
1 CONC ing and St. Margaret of Scotland.
1 CONT
1 CONT Yaroslav had one son from the first marriage (his Christian name being Ilya), and 6 sons from the second marriage. Apprehending the danger that could ensue from divisions between brothers, he exhorted them to live in peace with ea
1 CONC ch other. The eldest of these, Vladimir of Novgorod, best remembered for building the Saint Sophia Cathedral in Novgorod, predeceased his father. Three other sons-Iziaslav, Sviatoslav, and Vsevolod-reigned in Kiev one after anothe
1 CONC r. The youngest children of Yaroslav were Igor of Volynia and Vyacheslav of Smolensk.
1 CONT
1 CONT <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yaroslav_I_the_Wise>
1 CONT
1 CONT Marriages and Children
1 CONT
1 CONT Yaroslav had a son, but the name of the mother is unknown:
1 CONT Elias, Prince of Novgorod
1 CONT
1 CONT Yaroslav married 1019 to Ingeborg Olafsdottir, a daughter of Olaf Skötkonung, King of Sweden. Yaroslav and Ingeborg had the following children:
1 CONT Anne of Kiev
1 CONT Izyaslav I, Prince of Kiev, 1054 - 1078, deposed 1068 - 1069 and 1073 - 1076.
1 CONT Svyatoslav II, Prince of Kiev, 1073 - 1076
1 CONT Vsevolod I, Prince of Kiev, 1076 - 1093, deposed 1077 - 1078.
1 CONT Ellisif ( or Elizabeth) of Kiev, married first in 1045 to Harald III Hardrada, King of Norway, who was slain on September 25, 1066 at the Battle of Stamford Bridge.  Ellisif married second in 1067 to Svend II, King of Denmark.
1 CONT Valdimar ( or Holti) the Nimble, Prince of Novgorod, 1036 - 1052.
1 CONT Anastasia Agmunda of Kiev, married circa 1046 to Andrew I, King of Hungary.
1 CONT Viacheslav, Prince of Smolensk 1054 - 1056
1 CONT Igor of Vladimir, Prince of Vladimir 1054 - 1060
1 CONT Dobronega (or Maria) of Kiev, married 1038 to Casimir I, King of Poland. (? Vladimir too?)
1 CONT
1 CONT http://www.robertsewell.ca/kiev.html
0 @N6000000006422183351@ NOTE
1 CONT John Sloan and Micha Jelisavic write:  He was the sixth son of Vladimir I Svyatoslavich, prince of Kyiv. He married Ingigerd, daughter of Olaf, King of Sweden. His sons were Ilya (Elias), prince of Novgorod, (d 1020); Vladimir, pr
1 CONC ince of Novgorod (d 1043); Izyaslav I, prince of Kyiv (d 1107); Svyatoslav II, usurper prince of Kyiv and prince of Chernigiv (d 1076); Vsyevolod I, prince of Kyiv (d 1093); Vyacheslav, prince of Smolensk (d 1057); and Igor, princ
1 CONC e of Vladimir-in-Volynia (d 1060).
1 CONT
1 CONT On the death of their father, Vladimir, Svyatopolk I seized the throne and had the half-brothers, Boris, Gleb, and Vsyevelod, killed. Yaroslav was ruling in Novgorod. He marched south with a combined Novgorodian-Varangian army. Se
1 CONC e Svyatopolk I. In 1017 Svyatopolk set the Polish King, Boleslaw Khrabryi, against the Russians. On the 22nd of July, a Polish army under the command of the Polish kingreached the Bug river, then the border dividing Russian holdin
1 CONC gs from those Polish. Here they met Yaroslav and his cohorts. With a surprise attack the Poles routed the Russians and unopposed came upon Kiev. Here they captured the wife and sisters of Yaroslav, one of whom became Boleslaw's co
1 CONC ncubine. In the "Kronika Thietmara," it is recorded that the Polish bishop Rhinebern in the 1020s found himself in Rus' as a choir master for the daughter of Boleslaw who was marrying the son of Vladimir of Kiev--Svyatopolk. Page 
1 CONC 150, Priniatiye Khristianstva Narodami Tsentral'noi I Yugo-Vostochnoi YevropyI Kreshchenie Rusi, 1988. After several campaigns Yaroslav was again and firmly ensconced on the throne of Kiev and generously rewarded to Novgorodians b
1 CONC efore he allowed them to go home.
1 CONT
1 CONT The most important monument, (or better, a part of a monument) that dates from the reign of Yaroslav, is the Cathedral of Saint Sophia in Kiev, built in 1037 to commemorate the Russian victory over the Patzinaks (Pechenegs) in the
1 CONC  previous year. Originally the cathedral was conceived on the Greek cross plan and built in line with Yaroslav's fondness for monumental forms, solemn dispositions and very richly decorated frescoes and mosaics. At that time the C
1 CONC athedral had five aisles terminated in semicircular apses and six smaller cupolas, dominated by the seventh central large cupola over the central square. Four piers each of about 23 feet in diameter mark the square and support fou
1 CONC r arched vaults that carry the central cupola. The drum of this cupola has several (12) tall, large windows that bring light in from all sides and lavishly enlighten the frescoes and mosaics that are under the main cupola or the n
1 CONC earby apse, in contrast with other parts of the cathedral which are rather dark. When the Tatars invaded Kiev they spared the cathedral but when Lithuanians and Poles moved in after them, Saint Sophia was seriously damaged and an 
1 CONC entire wall collapsed. Since then the cathedral was restored and renovated several times. Its exterior received an entirely different appearance when in the seventeenth century Metropolitan Moghila and Hetman Mazepa decided to enl
1 CONC arge and restore the cathedral. Two more aisles were added, making the cathedral 176 feet wide, and six more cupolas were put on. At this time the ancient hemispheric Byzantine cupolas were replaced by pear-shaped cupolas that bec
1 CONC ame popular in Ukraine in the seventeenth and the eighteenth centuries. Later several more cupolas were added and the entire outline of the edifice, regretfully, turned into a sort of Ukrainian baroque. To make the exterior of the
1 CONC  cathedral even more exotic, in 1953-1954 Soviet authorities gilded the cupolas. On a few occasions excavations were made inside and around the cathedral by the Soviet archaeologists. According to some, the ancient walls and found
1 CONC ations show that the original building had many details in common with old churches built in Armenia during or before the eleventh century. These findings prompted some Soviet historians to conclude that first Russian monuments we
1 CONC re more influenced by Armenian and Georgian masters than by the Greeks.
1 CONT
1 CONT Chronology:
1 CONT 1016-1019 AD - Yaroslav organizes two campaigns with Varangian and Novgorodian troops to unseat Svyatopolk from the throne at Kyiv
1 CONT 1020 AD - Yaroslav's son Vladimir is born. Elias was born while Yaroslav was still at Novgorod and was placed on command at Novgorod when Yaroslav left, but died that same year.
1 CONT 1021 AD - Bryacheslav Izyaslavich, grandson of Vladimir I captures Novgorod and then returns to Polotsk. Yaroslav marches north and defeats Bryacheslav at the Sudomir River and frees the Novgorodian prisoners, forcing Bryacheslav 
1 CONC to flee to Polotsk.
1 CONT 1022 AD - Yaroslav marches on Brest. Mstislav, ruler of Tmutorokan, attacks the Kasogians. He defeats the Kasogian ruler, Rededya, in single combat.
1 CONT 1023 AD - Mstislav marches north with combined Khazar, Kasogian and personal druzhina to attack Yaroslav.
1 CONT 1024 AD - Yaroslav is still at Novgorod when Mstislav arrives at Kyiv. The Kyivans refuse him entry so he marches on to take Chernigiv on the other side of the Dniper. The chronicler reports that in the same year magicians appeare
1 CONC d at Suzdal and than Yaroslav had to march there to expel them before organizing a campaign to Kyiv. He follows the standard practice by sending for Varangian troops. The two armies meet at Listven. Mstislav deploys his Severians 
1 CONC in the center of his line opposite Yaroslav's Varangians. He keeps his personal druzhina out of the fray during a night battle in the midstof a tremendous thunder storm. The result is a victory for Mstislav after which he is doubl
1 CONC y pleased that the Severians and Varangians had taken all the casulaties while his personal troops remained unscathed. Meanwhile Yaroslav flees again to Novgorod. Mstislav proposes a peace treaty by which he will have the left ban
1 CONC k Dniper from Chernigiv to Murom and Yaroslav will have the right bank Dniper including Novgorod. Yaroslav is warry and stays at Novgorod.
1 CONT 1026 AD - Yaroslav has a new army of sufficient strength to venture south and enter Kyiv. He accepts Mstislav's proposal for split domain. They manage to live in peace until Mstislav dies.
1 CONT 1027 AD - Yaroslav's third son, Svyatoslav, is born.
1 CONT 1030 AD - Yaroslav captures Bel'z. His fourth son, Vsyevolod, is born. He founds Yur'yev. There is a revolt in Poland after the death of Boleslaw the Great. Many priests and bishops are killed.
1 CONT 1031 AD - Yaroslav and Mstislav lead a joint campaign into Poland. They retake the Cherven towns and resettle many Poles along the southern Russian frontier.
1 CONT 1032 AD - Yaroslav expands Russian towns and fortified line south along the Ros River.
1 CONT 1033 AD - Mstislav's son, Eustathius, dies, leaving his without heir.
1 CONT 1036 AD - Mstislav dies while hunting and his part of Rus reverts to Yaroslav. Yaroslav goes to Novgorod and installs his son, Vladimir, at prince. This year Yaroslav has another son, Vyacheslav. Yaroslav also imprisoned his broth
1 CONC er, Sudislav, at Pskov.
1 CONT 1036 AD - While Yaroslav is in Novgorod the Pecheneg again launch a major offensive to besiege Kyiv. Yaroslav sails to Kyiv. He enters the city and then sallies out toward the Pecheneg force in the field where the St Sophia cathed
1 CONC ral is now. Yaroslav deploys his forces with Kyivans in right, Varangians in center and Novgorodians on left. The struggle lasts most of the day before the Pecheneg are routed and flee in all directions.
1 CONT 1037 AD - Yaroslav expands the city fortress and also the outer city walls. He builds the great St Sophia cathedral and the Church of the Annunciation over the Golden Gate, the most impressive of thethree gates in the new city wal
1 CONC l.
1 CONT 1038 AD - Yaroslav attacks the Yatvingians (southern Lithuania).
1 CONT 1039 AD - The Church of the Blessed Virgin, (The tithe church) started by Vladimir in the inner part of the city, which was damaged in the city fire of 1017, is re-consecrated.
1 CONT 1040 AD - Yaroslav again attacks Lithuania. (Probably trying to open a more direct route to the Baltic via the Dvina river.
1 CONT 1041 AD - Yaroslav campaigns by river against the Masovians, who live along the Northern Bug and Vistula.
1 CONT 1042 AD - Yaroslav's son, Vladimir, attacks the Yam district (between Novgorod and the coast).
1 CONT 1043 AD - Yaroslav sends his son, Vladimir, to attack Byzantium with a very large naval expedition. The Byzantine emperor, Constantine IX, is surprised but eventually victorious by use of "Greek Fire". On the return voyage they at
1 CONC tempt an attack at Varna and are again defeated.
1 CONT 1043 AD - Yaroslav arranges the marriage of his sister, Dobronega-Maria, to Casimir, prince of Poland. Yaroslav's son, Izyaslav, marries Casimir's sister. Casimir returns 800 prisoners captured by Boleslav.
1 CONT 1044 AD - Bryachislav Izyaslavich, prince of Polotsk, dies and is succeeded by his son, Vseslav.
1 CONT 1045 AD - Vladimir Yaroslavich founds the church of St Sophia in Novgorod, which is largely intact to the present time.
1 CONT 1046-7 AD - Yaroslav conquers the Mazovians and gives the region to Casimir.
1 CONT 1048-50 Yaroslav's wife, Ingigerd, dies.
1 CONT 1051 AD - Yaroslav appoints Hilarion as Metropolitan of Rus with his chair at the new cathedral of St Sophia. The Cave Monastery (Pechersky Lavra) just south of Kyiv and the Church at Berestovo were also founded during Yaroslav's 
1 CONC reign.
1 CONT 1052 AD - Vladimir, Yaroslav's eldest son, dies at Novgorod.
1 CONT 1054 AD - Yaroslav dies at Vyshgorod and is succeeded by his eldest remaining son, Izyaslav I, who is at Novgorod at the time. The Chronicle contains Yaroslav's testament in which he urges his sons to live in peace with each other
1 CONC . He designates Izyaslav to be prince of Kyiv, Svyatoslav to be prince of Chernigiv, Vsyevolod to Pereyaslavl, Igor to Vladimir-in-Volynia, and Vyacheslav to Smolensk.
0 @N6000000007329696738@ NOTE [Descent from the Battle of Hastings, Kenneth J. Hart]:
0 @N6000000008630618055@ NOTE Burke calls him Great Duke of Russia. Snorri Sturlasson call himPrince of
1 CONT Holmgarth and shows his children as Holti-Nimble, Vissivald, Ellisif.
1 CONT
1 CONT         Donald Lines Jacobus (1883-1970), the "Founder of Scientific
1 CONT Genealogy in America" wrote an article in The American Genealogist(TAG)
1 CONT 9:13-15 entitled "The House of Rurik."  I quote:  "To correct the many
1 CONT errors that have appeared in print, and to aid those who follow thepastime
1 CONT of tracing "royal ancestry," the following condensed account of theearly
1 CONT Rurikides is here printed.  It is based in large part on "Genealogieset
1 CONT Mariages Occidentaux des Rurikides Russes du Xe au XIIIe Siecle,"published
1 CONT at Rome in 1927 as Vol. IX, No. 1, of *Orientalia Christiana.* Theauthor,
1 CONT N. de Baumgarten, is probably the best living authority on earlyRussian
1 CONT history, and every statement made on the fourteen genealogical tablesof
1 CONT his monograph is fully supported by the citation of contemporarydocuments
1 CONT and chronicles."
1 CONT
1 CONT         I am not attacking Jacobus, who is a giant among genealogistsand
1 CONT certainly needs no defenders.  Neither am I disagreeing with Alexander
1 CONT Agamov, in Moscow, who has pointed out that there is no credibleevidence
1 CONT that Rurik was ever "Prince of Kiev" and progenitor of the linebeginning
1 CONT with Igor, Grand Prince of Kiev, who married Olga. I hope AlexanderAgamov
1 CONT is reading this.  Some historians and genealogists, Russians inparticular,
1 CONT  have taken sharp issue with the theory that the Kievan Rus wasfounded by
1 CONT a "Dane" rather than a "Slav"----and the evidence for "The Varangian
1 CONT Theory" seems fragmentary and inconclusive, at best.
1 CONT
1 CONT         G. Andrews Moriarty and Walter L. Sheppard in TAG 28:91-95also
1 CONT quote the N. de Baumgarten material as authoritative [specifically
1 CONT "Orientalia Christiana, No. 119, N. de Baumgarten, "Aux Origines de la
1 CONT Russie," p. 79.  Both Jacobus and Moriarty/Sheppard headline theircharts
1 CONT with "Rurik (d. 879) Grand Prince of Kiev."  Jacobus probably did notread
1 CONT 10th to 13th century Russian. But--- some of us may.
1 CONT
1 CONT         For anyone who might conceivably have access to the original,1927,
1 CONT N. de Baumgarten source---is it provable that, "every statement madeon the
1 CONT fourteen genealogical tables of his monograph is fully supported bythe
1 CONT citation of contemporary documents and chronicles?"
1 CONT
1 CONT         Or, is it possible that Jacobus and the other experts simply
1 CONT trusted in N. de Baumgarten's scholarship and professionalism---anddid not
1 CONT really check out the facts themselves. The Editor of a journal, suchas
1 CONT TAG, certainly cannot check out every fact and document himself. But,in
1 CONT this particular case, Jacobus gives the N. de Baumgarten material his
1 CONT personal imprimatur, as cited above [TAG 9:13, Paragraph 2]
1 CONT
1 CONT         So----has N. de Baumgarten in his "OrientaliaChristiana"---dealing
1 CONT with "The House of Rurik"---been totally discredited by subsequentrigorous
1 CONT scholarship---or does his judgment still seem credible to some serious
1 CONT scholars----or are there alternate explanations?
1 CONT
1 CONT         This is an intriguing question of interest to many folks whoare
1 CONT descended from Anne of Kiev (c. 1024-c.1066) [Anna Yaroslavna] whomarried
1 CONT Henry I, King of France.
