0 @N6000000001783252110@ NOTE Basic Life Information
1 CONT
1 CONT Also known as Constance of Arles.  Weis calls her Constance of Provence, has her born abt 986. He and the others agree on her death 25 July 1032, however.
1 CONT
1 CONT Constance of Arles
1 CONT
1 CONT Constance of Arles (973 - July 25, 1034) was the third wife and queen of King Robert II of France. She was the daughter of William I, count of Provence and great-grandson of Charles-Constantine; and Adelais of Anjou, daughter of F
1 CONC ulk II of Anjou. She was the sister of Count William II of Provence.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1003, she was married to King Robert, after his divorce from his second wife. The marriage was stormy; the family of Robert's second queen, Bertha, opposed her, and Constance was despised for importing her Provençal kinfolk. Ro
1 CONC bert's friend, Hugh of Beauvais, tried to convince the king to repudiate her in 1007. Constance's response was to have Beauvais murdered by the knights of her kinsman, Fulk Nerra. In 1010 Robert even went to Rome, accompanied by h
1 CONC is former wife Bertha, to seek permission to divorce Constance and remarry Bertha. Constance encouraged her sons to revolt against their father, and then favored her younger son, Robert, over her elder son, Henri.
1 CONT
1 CONT During the famous trial of Herefast de Crepon (who was alleged to be involved with the Cathars) in 1022, the crowd outside the church in Orleans became so unruly that, according to Moore:
1 CONT
1 CONT At the king's command, Queen Constance stood before the doors of the Church, to prevent the common people from killing them inside the Church, and they were expelled from the bosom of the Church. As they were being driven out, the
1 CONC  queen struck out the eye of Stephen, who had once been her confessor, with the staff which she carried in her hand.
1 CONT The symbolism, or reality, of putting an eye out is used often in medieval accounts to show the ultimate sin of breaking of one's oath, whether it be heresy, or treason to ones lordship, or in this case both. Stephen's eye was put
1 CONC  out by the hand of a Queen wielding a staff (royal scepters were usually tipped with a cross) thus symbolically providing justice for the treasoned lord on earth and in heaven.
1 CONT
1 CONT Marriage and Children
1 CONT
1 CONT Constance and Robert had seven children:
1 CONT Advisa, Countess of Auxerre, (c.1003-after 1063), married Count Renaud I of Nevers
1 CONT Hugh Magnus, co-king (1007-September 17, 1025)
1 CONT Henri (May 4, 1008-August 4, 1060)
1 CONT Adela, Countess of Contenance (1009-June 5, 1063), married (1) Duke Richard III of Normandy (2) Count Baldwin V of Flanders
1 CONT Robert I, Duke of Burgundy (1011-March 21, 1076)
1 CONT Eudes (1013-1056)
1 CONT Constance (1014-unknown), married Manasses de Dammartin
1 CONT
1 CONT At Constance's urging, her eldest son Hugh Magnus was crowned co-king alongside his father in 1017. Hugh Magnus demanded his parents share power with him, and rebelled against his father in 1025. He died suddenly later that year, 
1 CONC an exile and a fugitive. Robert and Constance quarelled over which of their surviving sons should inherit the throne; Robert favored their second son Henri, while Constance favored their third son, Robert. Despite his mother's pro
1 CONC tests, Henry was crowned in 1027. Fulbert, Bishop of Chartres, wrote a letter claiming that he was "frightened away" from the consecration of Henry "by the savagery of his mother, who is quite trustworthy when she promises evil."
1 CONT
1 CONT Constance encouraged her sons to rebel, and Henri and Robert began attacking and pillaging the towns and castles belonging to their father. Robert attacked Burgundy, the duchy he had been promised but had never received, and Henry
1 CONC  seized Dreux. At last King Robert agreed to their demands and peace was made which lasted until the king's death.
1 CONT
1 CONT King Robert died in 1031, and soon Constance was at odds with both her elder son, Henri, and her younger son Robert. Constance seized her dower lands and refused to surrender them. Henri fled to Normandy, where he received aid, we
1 CONC apons, and soldiers from his brother Robert. He returned to besiege his mother at Poissy, but Constance escaped to Pontoise. She only surrendered when Henri began the siege of Le Puiset and swore to slaughter all the inhabitants.
1 CONT
1 CONT Death
1 CONT
1 CONT Constance died in 1034, and was buried beside her husband Robert at Saint-Denis Basilica.
1 CONT
1 CONT <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Constance_of_Arles>
0 @N6000000002187596327@ NOTE Constance of Arles
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Constance of Arles (973 - July 25, 1034) was the third wife and queen of King Robert II of France. She was the daughter of Count Guilhem II of Provence and Adelais of Anjou, and the sister of Count Guilhem III of Provence.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1003 she was married to King Robert, after his divorce from his second wife. The marriage was stormy; the family of Robert's second queen, Bertha, opposed her, and Constance was despised for importing her Provençal kinfolk. Rob
1 CONC ert's friend, Hugh of Beauvais, tried to convince the king to repudiate her in 1007. Constance's response was to have Beauvais murdered by the knights of her kinsman, Fulk Nerra. In 1010 Robert even went to Rome, accompanied by hi
1 CONC s former wife Bertha, to seek permission to divorce Constance and remarry Bertha. Constance encouraged her sons to revolt against their father, and then favored her younger son, Robert, over her elder son, Henri.
1 CONT
1 CONT During the famous trial of Herefast de Crepon (who was alleged to be involved with the Cathars) in 1022, the crowd outside the church in Orleans became so unruly that, according to Moore:
1 CONT
1 CONT "At the king's command, Queen Constance stood before the doors of the Church, to prevent the common people from killing them inside the Church, and they were expelled from the bosom of the Church. As they were being driven out, th
1 CONC e queen struck out the eye of Stephen, who had once been her confessor, with the staff which she carried in her hand." 
1 CONT The symbolism, or reality, of putting an eye out is used often in medieval accounts to show the ultimate sin of breaking of one's oath, whether it be heresy, or treason to ones lordship, or in this case both. Stephen's eye was put
1 CONC  out by the hand of a Queen wielding a staff (royal scepters were usually tipped with a cross) thus symbolically providing justice for the treasoned lord on earth and in heaven.
1 CONT
1 CONT Constance and Robert had seven children:
1 CONT
1 CONT Advisa, Countess of Auxerre, (c.1003-after 1063), married Count Renaud I of Nevers 
1 CONT Hugh Magnus, co-king (1007-September 17, 1025) 
1 CONT Henri (May 4, 1008-August 4, 1060) 
1 CONT Adela, Countess of Contenance (1009-June 5, 1063), married (1) Duke Richard III of Normandy (2) Count Baldwin V of Flanders 
1 CONT Robert I, Duke of Burgundy (1011-March 21, 1076) 
1 CONT Eudes (1013-1056) 
1 CONT Constance (1014-unknown), married Manasses de Dammartin 
1 CONT At Constance's urging, her eldest son Hugh Magnus was crowned co-king alongside his father in 1017. Hugh Magnus demanded his parents share power with him, and rebelled against his father in 1025. He died suddenly later that year, 
1 CONC an exile and a fugitive. Robert and Constance quarelled over which of their surviving sons should inherit the throne; Robert favored their second son Henri, while Constance favored their third son, Robert. Despite his mother's pro
1 CONC tests, Henry was crowned in 1027. Fulbert, Bishop of Chartres, wrote a letter claiming that he was "frightened away" from the consecration of Henry "by the savagery of his mother, who is quite trustworthy when she promises evil."
1 CONT
1 CONT Constance encouraged her sons to rebel, and Henri and Robert began attacking and pillaging the towns and castles belonging to their father. Robert attacked Burgundy, the duchy he had been promised but had never received, and Henry
1 CONC  seized Dreux. At last King Robert agreed to their demands and peace was made which lasted until the king's death.
1 CONT
1 CONT King Robert died in 1031, and soon Constance was at odds with both her elder son, Henri, and her younger son Robert. Constance seized her dower lands and refused to surrender them. Henri fled to Normandy, where he received aid, we
1 CONC apons, and soldiers from his brother Robert. He returned to besiege his mother at Poissy, but Constance escaped to Pontoise. She only surrendered when Henri began the siege of Le Puiset and swore to slaughter all the inhabitants.
1 CONT
1 CONT Constance died in 1034, and was buried beside her husband Robert at Saint-Denis Basilica.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Sources
1 CONT Jessee, W. Scott. A missing Capetian princess: Advisa, daughter of King Robert II of France (Medieval Prosopography), 1990 
1 CONT Nolan, Kathleen D. Capetian Women, 2003. 
1 CONT Moore, R.I. The Birth of Popular Heresy, 1975. 
0 @N6000000001744830661@ NOTE [s2.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT Source: Church of JC of the LDS "Ancestral File" CD-Rom database, ver 4.17.
1 CONT
1 CONT There is an identity confusion between Constance of Arles and Constance of Toulouse. One is daughter of William III Taillefer Count of Toulouse, the other the daughter of William Count of Provence and Arles. The wives of these two
1 CONC  men were sisters: Arsinde (Blanche) d'Anjou and Adelaide (Blanca) d'Anjou.Source: Church of JC of the LDS "Ancestral File" CD-Rom database, ver 4.17.
1 CONT
1 CONT There is an identity confusion between Constance of Arles and Constance of Toulouse. One is daughter of William III Taillefer Count of Toulouse, the other the daughter of William Count of Provence and Arles. The wives of these two
1 CONC  men were sisters: Arsinde (Blanche) d'Anjou and Adelaide (Blanca) d'Anjou.
