0 @N6000000002187596174@ NOTE Robert II of France
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert II the Pious (French: Robert II le Pieux) (March 27, 972 - July 20, 1031) was King of France from 996 to 1031. A member of the Capetian Dynasty, Robert II was born on March 27, 972 in Orléans, France, the son of King Hugh C
1 CONC apet (938-996) and Adelaide of Aquitaine (952-1004).
1 CONT
1 CONT In 987, Robert's father had the nobles crown him as successor at Orléans on December 30th, thereby confirming the house of Capet as rulers of France. After Robert became king he did as his father and crowned his eldest son Hugh Ma
1 CONC gnus as his successor. But, due to Prince Hugh Magnus's death, another son, Henri, became king.
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert, despite marital problems that saw him temporarily excommunicated by Pope Gregory V, was a very devout Roman Catholic, hence the name Robert the Pious. He was very musically inclined and was a composer, a chorister, and a p
1 CONC oet, making his palace a place of religious seclusion, where he conducted the matins and vespers in his royal robes. Part of Robert's piety at the time, was because he did not tolerate heretics and harshly punished them.
1 CONT
1 CONT The kingdom Robert inherited was not large, and in an effort to increase his power, he vigorously pursued his claim to any of the feudal lands as they became vacant which action usually resulted in war with a counter-claimant. In 
1 CONC 1003, his invasion of Burgundy was thwarted and it would not be until 1016 that he was finally able to get the support of the Church and be recognized as the Duke of Burgundy.
1 CONT
1 CONT The pious King Robert II made few friends and many enemies, including his own sons. His eldest son Hugh Magnus died suddenly while in rebellion against his father. Robert's surviving sons, Henri and Robert, also turned against the
1 CONC ir father, in a civil war for power and property. King Robert's army was beaten and he retreated to Beaugency outside Paris.
1 CONT
1 CONT He died in the middle of the war with his sons on July 20, 1031 at Melun, France. He was interred with his third wife, Constance of Arles (973-July 25, 1032) in Saint Denis Basilica. He was succeeded by his and Constance's second 
1 CONC son, Henri I.
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert II married:
1 CONT
1 CONT c. 989, 1) Susanne (Rosala), Princess of Italy (c.945 - January 26, 1003). Widow of Count Arnulf II of Flanders. She was much older than Robert, and the marriage was arranged by his father. Robert divorced her a year later. 
1 CONT c. 996, 2) Bertha, Princess of Burgundy (952-1035). Widow of Count Theobald II of Blois. Because she was his cousin, Pope Gregory V would not sanction the marriage and Robert was excommunicated. However, after long negotiations wi
1 CONC th the new Pope Silvester II the marriage was annulled. 
1 CONT In 1001, 3) Constance of Arles (973-July 25, 1034) Daughter of a certain Count William, an intriguing and ambitious woman, who made life miserable for her husband. She was the mother of his children: 
1 CONT Advisa, Countess of Auxerre, (c.1003-after 1063), married Count Renaud I of Nevers 
1 CONT Hugh Magnus, co-king (1007-September 17, 1025) 
1 CONT Henry I of France (May 4, 1008-August 4, 1060) 
1 CONT Adela, Countess of Contenance (1009-June 5, 1063), married (1) Duke Richard III of Normandy (2) Count Baldwin V of Flanders 
1 CONT Robert I, Duke of Burgundy (1011-March 21, 1076) 
1 CONT Eudes (1013-1056) 
1 CONT Constance (1014-unknown), married Manasses de Dammartin 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Hugh Capet King of France
1 CONT 996–1031 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Henry I 
1 CONT --------------------------------------------
1 CONT Robert II, "The Pious" King of France
1 CONT
1 CONT Born: 27 Mar 972, Orleans, France
1 CONT
1 CONT Married 1003 to Constance (de Arles) de Toulouse
1 CONT
1 CONT Died: 20 Jul 1031, Melun, France
1 CONT
1 CONT --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT  Robert II, "The Pious", King of France 
1 CONT
1 CONT buried St Denis, France
1 CONT --------------------------------
1 CONT Robert II, the Pious (972 - 1031) was King of France from 996 to 1031. A member of the Capetian Dynasty, Robert II was born on March 27, 972 in Orleans, France, the son of King Hugh Capet (938-996) and Adelaide of Aquitaine (952-1
1 CONC 004). 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT In 987, Robert’s father had the nobles crown him as successor at Orléans on December 30th, thereby confirming the house of Capet as rulers of France. After Robert became king he did as his father and crowned his eldest son Hugh as
1 CONC  his successor. But, due to Prince Hugh's death, another son, Henri, became king. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert, despite marital problems that saw him temporarily excommunicated by Pope Gregory V, was a very devout Roman Catholic, hence the name Robert the Pious. He was very musically inclined and was a composer, a chorister, and a p
1 CONC oet, making his palace a place of religious seclusion, where he conducted the matins and vespers in his royal robes. Part of Robert's piety at the time, was because he did not tolerate heretics and harshly punished them. 
1 CONT
1 CONT The kingdom Robert inherited was not large, and in an effort to increase his power, he vigorously pursued his claim to any of the feudal lands as they became vacant which action usually resulted in war with a counter-claimant. In 
1 CONC 1003, his invasion of Burgundy was thwarted and it would not be until 1016 that he was finally able to get the support of the Church and be recognized as the Duke of Burgundy. 
1 CONT
1 CONT The pious King Robert II made few friends and many enemies, including his own sons Henri and Robert. They turned against their father, in a civil war for power and property. King Robert’s army was beaten and he retreated to Beauge
1 CONC ncy outside Paris. 
1 CONT
1 CONT He died in the middle of the war with his sons on July 20, 1031 at Melun, France. He was interred with his third wife, Constance d'Arles (973-July 25, 1032) in Saint Denis Basilica. 
1 CONT
1 CONT He was succeeded by his and Constance's second son, Henri I. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert II married: 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT c. 988, 1) Susanne (Rosala), Princess of Italy (c.966 - January 26, 1003). The marriage was arranged by Robert’s father and ended in divorce. 
1 CONT
1 CONT c. 996, 2) Bertha, Princess of Burgundy (952-1035). Because she was his cousin, Pope Gregory V would not sanction the marriage and Robert was excommunicated. However, after long negotiations with the new Pope Silvester II the marr
1 CONC iage was annulled. 
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1001, 3) Constance Taillefer d'Arles (973-July 25, 1032) 
1 CONT Issue (3): 
1 CONT
1 CONT Adáele (Alix) - (1003 - January 8, 1079) 
1 CONT Hugh - (1007-September 17, 1025) 
1 CONT Henry I - (May 4, 1008 - August 4, 1060) 
1 CONT Adelaide Havoise - (1009-June 5, 1063) 
1 CONT Robert - (1011 - March 21, 1076) 
1 CONT Eudo (Odes) - (1013-1056) 
1 CONT Constance - (1014 - unknown) 
1 CONT http://www.deloriahurst.com/deloriahurst%20page/1654.html
0 @N6000000002322288370@ NOTE The King of France who took Burgundy into the French realm. His domain was
1 CONT not extensive, so when the duke of Burgundy died without heir in 1002, Robert
1 CONT went to war against a rival claimant. only in 1015, however, did he finally
1 CONT succeed in subduing the rich duchy. The gain, however, was only transitory,
1 CONT for in 1032 Henry I granted Burgundy to his brother, Robert, and it
1 CONT thereafter remained for centuries outside royal control.
0 @N6000000001783211349@ NOTE Basic Life Information
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert II (27 March 972 - 20 July 1031), called the Pious or the Wise, was King of France from 996 until his death. The second reigning member of the House of Capet, he was born in Orléans to Hugh Capet and Adelaide of Aquitaine.
1 CONT
1 CONT Co-rule with Father
1 CONT
1 CONT Immediately after his own coronation, Robert's father Hugh began to push for the coronation of Robert. "The essential means by which the early Capetians were seen to have kept the throne in their family was through the association
1 CONC  of the eldest surviving son in the royalty during the father's lifetime," Andrew W. Lewis has observed, in tracing the phenomenon in this line of kings who lacked dynastic legitimacy. Hugh's claimed reason was that he was plannin
1 CONC g an expedition against the Moorish armies harassing Borrel II of Barcelona, an invasion which never occurred, and that the stability of the country necessitated a co-king, should he die while on expedition. Ralph Glaber, however,
1 CONC  attributes Hugh's request to his old age and inability to control the nobility. Modern scholarship has largely imputed to Hugh the motive of establishing a dynasty against the claims of electoral power on the part of the aristocr
1 CONC acy, but this is not the typical view of contemporaries and even some modern scholars have been less sceptical of Hugh's "plan" to campaign in Spain. Robert was eventually crowned on 30 December 987. A measure of Hugh's success is
1 CONC  that when Hugh died in 996, Robert continued to reign without any succession dispute, but during his long reign actual royal power dissipated into the hands of the great territorial magnates.
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert had begun to take on active royal duties with his father in the early 990s. In 991, he helped his father prevent the French bishops from trekking to Mousson in the Kingdom of Germany for a synod called by Pope John XV, with
1 CONC  whom Hugh was then in disagreement.
1 CONT
1 CONT Marital Problems
1 CONT
1 CONT As early as 989, having been rebuffed in his search for a Byzantine princess,Hugh Capet arranged for Robert to marry the recently-widowed daughter of Berengar II of Italy, Rozala, who took the name of Susannah upon becoming Queen.
1 CONC  She was many years his senior. She was the widow of Arnulf II of Flanders, with whom she had children, the oldest of whom was of age to assume the offices of count of Flanders. Robert divorced her within a year of his father's de
1 CONC ath. He tried instead to marry Bertha, daughter of Conrad of Burgundy, around the time of his father's death. She was a widow of Odo I of Blois, but was also Robert's cousin. For reasons of consanguinity, Pope Gregory V refused to
1 CONC  sanction the marriage, and Robert was excommunicated. After long negotiations with Gregory's successor, Sylvester II, the marriage was annulled.
1 CONT
1 CONT Finally, in 1001, Robert entered into his final and longest-lasting marriage: to Constance of Arles, the daughter of William I of Provence. She was an ambitious and scheming woman, who made life miserable for her husband by encour
1 CONC aging her sons to revolt against their father.
1 CONT
1 CONT Piety
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert, however, despite his marital problems, was a very devout Catholic, hence his sobriquet "the Pious." He was musically inclined, being a composer, chorister, and poet, and making his palace a place of religious seclusion, wh
1 CONC ere he conducted the matins and vespers in his royal robes. However, to contemporaries, Robert's "piety" resulted from his lack of toleration for heretics: he harshly punished them.
1 CONT
1 CONT Military Career
1 CONT
1 CONT The kingdom Robert inherited was not large, and in an effort to increase his power, he vigorously pursued his claim to any feudal lands which became vacant, which action usually resulted in war with a counter-claimant. In 1003, hi
1 CONC s invasion of the Duchy of Burgundy was thwarted and it would not be until 1016 that he was finally able to get the support of the Church and be recognized as Duke of Burgundy.
1 CONT
1 CONT The pious Robert made few friends and many enemies, including his own sons: Hugh Magnus, Henry, and Robert. They turned against their father in a civil war over power and property. Hugh died in revolt in 1025. In a conflict with H
1 CONC enry and the younger Robert, King Robert's army was beaten and he retreated to Beaugency outside Paris, his capital. He died in the middle of the war with his sons on 20 July 1031 at Melun. He was interred with Constance in Saint 
1 CONC Denis Basilica. He was succeeded by his son Henry, in both France and Burgundy.
1 CONT
1 CONT Marriages and Children
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert had no children from his short-lived marriage to Susanna. His illegal marriage to Bertha gave him one stillborn son in 999, but only Constance gave him surviving children:
1 CONT Constance, married Manasses de Dammartin
1 CONT Adele of France, married Renauld I, Count of Nevers on 25 January 1016 and had issue.
1 CONT Hugh Magnus, co-king (1017-1025)
1 CONT Henry I, successor
1 CONT Robert, became Duke of Burgundy
1 CONT Odo (1013-c.1056), who may have been mentally retarded and died after his brother's failed invasion of Normandy
1 CONT Adela (d. 1079), married firstly Richard III of Normandy and secondly Baldwin V of Flanders.
1 CONT
1 CONT Relationship and Child
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert also left an illegitimate son: Rudolph, Bishop of Bourges.
1 CONT
1 CONT <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_II_of_France>
