1 CONT Robert II (of France), called Robert the Pious (970?-1031), king of France (996-1031), the son of King Hugh Capet, born in Orlans, and educated at Reims under the French scholar Gerbert, who later became Pope Sylvester II. In 996 
1 CONC Robert married, as his second wife, his cousin Bertha of Burgundy (born 962?). Two years later Pope Gregory V (died 999) excommunicated him and annulled this marriage, which was considered incestuous by the church; in 1003 Robert 
1 CONC submitted to the pope and married the daughter of the marquis of Provence, Constance of Arles (died 1032), by whom he had four sons. He recognized Hugh (1007-25), the eldest of these sons, as his successor. After Hugh's death in 1
1 CONC 025, the other sons, aided by their mother, revolted; Robert was still fighting them at the time of his own death. Robert was called The Pious because of his humility and charity; he was also esteemed as a soldier and ruler.
1 CONT
