0 @N6000000005598936549@ NOTE Robert was distinguished for his piety and learning, he also sought t o strengthen the weak royal power, conquered several towns, and secure d the duchy of Burgundy for the crown. His son and successor was Henr y I.  Robert's biog
1 CONC rapher was Helgaud.  Robert is styled as tall, rath er fat, long faced, mild eyed with a broad nose, and a bull beard.  B y nature, he is described as naturally gentle, humble, pious and virtu ous, an affable man who was kind.  Bo
1 CONC th Robert and his father supporte d the limited 'pax dei' or 'Truce / Peace of God' initially declared a t the Synod of Charroux in 989.  Among other things, it disallowed fig hting between Wednesday evening and following Monday m
1 CONC orning each wee k of the year as well as on any holidays or any of the numerous feast days.
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert was a pupil of Gerbert of Aurillac (who later became Pope Sylve ster II) and thus familiar with Latin and was fond of books.  He is re ferenced as a 'veritable monk' on the throne and in his last years sou ght consolation i
1 CONC n his faith.  Robert was a royal composer, chorister , and poet, who might have ruled Italy but preferred that his palace s hould be his cloister, where he lived in the enjoyment of melody and s ong. He conducted vespers in his ro
1 CONC yal robes.  One of his greatest ene mies was Eudes de Blois.  As such, he allied himself with Rollo, the G ranger, a viking, who had established himself in the area of Normandie .
1 CONT
1 CONT He married in 988 (1) Rosala (Rozela), daughter of King Berengar II. o f Italy, but this marriage was repudiated in 992, married in 997 (2) B ertha of Bourgogne, daughter of Conrad of Burgundy, who died after 101 7, also divorced 
1 CONC in 1003/5; and (3) Constance Berengar (Berenger) of A rtes, borncirca 987, died in Melun in 1032 buired at St. Denis, daught er of William Berengar II (Berenger), Count of Provence, by his 2nd ma rriage.  During his reign he added
1 CONC  to the royal bishoprics held by hi s father.  They included of Troyes and Auxerrre in Sens, Chalons-sur-M arne in the province of Rheims, Macon in the province of Lyon and the archbishopric of Bourges.  The reign of Robert, saw a
1 CONC  considerable exp ansion of the Royal domain and part of that was the annexation of Burg undy, and the extension of his authority over the counties of Dreux an d Melun.
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert II thePious (French: Robert II le Pieux) (March 27, 972 - July 20, 1031) was King of France from 996 to 1031. A member of the Capetia n Dynasty, Robert II was born on March 27, 972 in Orléans, France, th e son of King Hugh 
1 CONC Capet (938-996) and Adelaide of Aquitaine (952-1004 ). In 987, Robert's father had the nobles crown him as successor at Or léans on December 30th, thereby confirming the house of Capet as ruler s of France. After Robert became kin
1 CONC g he did as his father and crowne d his eldest son Hugh Magnus as his successor. But, due to Prince Hug h Magnus' death, another son, Henri, became king.  Robert, despite mar ital problems that saw him temporarily excommunicated i
1 CONC n 997 (he and B ertha were related in the 6th degree) by Pope Gregory V, was a very de vout Roman Catholic, hence the name Robert the Pious. He was very musi cally inclined and was a composer, a chorister, and a poet, making hi s 
1 CONC palace a place of religious seclusion, where he conducted the matin s and vespers in his royal robes. Part of Robert's piety at the time, was because he did not tolerate heretics and harshly punished them.
1 CONT
1 CONT The kingdom Robert inherited was not large, and in an effort to increa se his power, he vigorously pursued his claim to any of the feudal lan ds as they became vacant which action usually resulted in war with a c ounter-claimant. 
1 CONC  At the time of his uncle Henri  de Bourgogne's deat h in 1002, Robert put himself forward as the rightful heir of Burgundy , instead of Henri's step-son and legitimate heir Otto 'Guillaume' de Bourgogne.  In 1003, his invasion of
1 CONC  Burgundy was thwarted and it woul d not be until 1016 that he was finally able to get the support of th e Church and be recognized as the Duke of Burgundy.  During Robert's e fforts to gain Burgundy, he was assisted by Duke Richa
1 CONC rd II de Normand ie in his efforts.  The crown of France did not retain Burgundy for lo ng and while Robert held it for the rest of his reign, it was lost onc e his son Henri ascended to the throne.  ON 22 April 1015, Robert siez 
1 CONC ed the town of Sens and by agreement with Renaud - Count of Sens, hal f of the town would revert to the crown, as well as the entire county .  On 6 August 1023, Robert met Henry II of the Holy Roman Empire, alo ng the Meuse river.
1 CONC   The emper was encamped at Ivois and King Robert a t Mouzon. The pious King Robert II made few friends and many enemies, including his own sons. His eldest son Hugh Magnus died suddenly whil e in rebellion against his father. Rob
1 CONC ert's surviving sons, Henri I o f France and Robert I, Duke of Burgundy, also turned against their fat her, in a civil war forpower and property. King Robert's army was beat en and he retreated to Beaugency outside Paris.
1 CONT
1 CONT He died in the middle of the war with his sons on July 20, 1031 at Mel un, France. He was interred with his third wife, Constance of Arles (9 73-July 25, 1032) in Saint Denis Basilica. He was succeeded by his an d Constance's seco
1 CONC nd son, Henri I.  Robert married first ca. 989, Susa nne (Rosala), Princess of Italy (c.945 - January 26, 1003). Widow of C ount Arnulf II of Flanders, by whom she had issue. She was much oldert han Robert, and the marriage was ar
1 CONC ranged by his father. Robert divorc ed her a year later.
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert married second ca. 996, Bertha, Princess of Burgundy (952-1035) . Widow of Count Theobald II of Blois. Because she was his cousin, Pop e Gregory V would not sanction the marriage and Robert was excommunica ted. However, aft
1 CONC er long negotiations with the new Pope Silvester II t he marriage was annulled.  Robert married in 1001, Constance of Arles( 973-July 25, 1034) Daughter of a certain Count William, an intriguing and ambitious woman, who made life 
1 CONC miserable for her husband by encour aging her sons to revolt against their father. She was the mother of h is children:
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert's children include: Advisa, Countess of Auxerre, (c.1003-after1 063), married Count Renaud I of Nevers. Their children inherited theco unties of Nevers and Auxerre; Hugh Magnus, or Hugh "The Great" co-kin g (1007-September 
1 CONC 17, 1025); Hugh is said to have been married (or bet rothed) before his death to Halwisa (Hawisa?) or Elisabeth d'Avoye, da u of one Henri l'Oiseteur, who married 2ndly Hamon, Count of Corbeil. Elisabeth's second husband Hamon was
1 CONC  younger brother of William, Coun t of Corbeil (possibly also known as William, Count of Arques), and a s such he was a descendant of Richard I "The Fearless" or "Sans-Peur, " Duke of Normany, great-grandfather William II, Duke of
1 CONC  Normandy, lat er styled "The Conqueror". One of Hamon and Elisabeth's sons was appar ently Robert Fitzhamon (b ca. 1045 - 1055 d. 1107) who was an importan t figure in Anglo-Norman history from 1087 to 1106. However, these mar ri
1 CONC ages and the exact connections of Hamon to the Dukes of Normandy hav e not been proven.  Henry I of France (May 4, 1008-August 4, 1060) anc estor of later Kings of France. Adela, Countess of Contenance, later A dela, Countess of F
1 CONC landers (1009-June 5, 1063), married Duke Richard I II of Normandy; and (2) Count Baldwin V of Flanders. She is better kno wn as the mother of Matilda of Flanders, wife of William the Conqueror ; Robert I, Duke of Burgundy (1011-M
1 CONC arch 21, 1076) ancestor of the fir st Capetian house of Burgundy;  Eudes (1013-1056) died after the faile d invasion of Normandy and Constance (1014-unknown), married Manasses de Dammartin.  Robert's descendants would go on to rul
1 CONC e not only Franc e, but England (through his granddaughter Matilda of Flanders' marriag e to William the Conqueror), Portugal, Castille, and Leon.
