0 @N6000000008799929863@ NOTE [Jeremiah Brown.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT [from Ancestry.com 81120.GED, references "France in the Making, 843-1180, by Jean Dunbabin, p136]
1 CONT
1 CONT In 988, Hugh Capet sought a Byzantine bride for Robert. Had the Emperor been accommodating, Hugh's initiative would have been liberally rewarded. As it was, Robert had to content himself with the daughter of an Italian king, a mar
1 CONC riage which, though satisfactory from the point of blood and dowry, failed miserably in human terms. Robert's second attempt, the alliance with Berthe of Burgundy, came up against the insuperable obstacle of ecclesiastical opposit
1 CONC ion, led by the very section of the reforming church on whose good offices he relied so heavily. The third marriage, with Constance of Arles, was a stormy one; yet the lady, though personally unpopular, was clearly valued as a wor
1 CONC thy prize for a king.
1 CONT
1 CONT [from Ancestry.com 139798.GED]
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert reigned 996-1031. He was a royal composer, chorister and poet, who might have ruled Italy but preferred that his palace would be his cloister, where he lived in the enjoyment of melody and song. He conducted the matins and 
1 CONC vespers in his royal robes. He was very devout.
