1 CONT William DE WARREN b: Abt 1055in Of Bellencombe,Seine Inferieure,France
0 @N6000000001783338165@ NOTE Basic Life Information
1 CONT
1 CONT William was a son of Rodulf II de Warenne and Emma and a grandnephew of duchess Gunnor, wife of duke Richard I of Normandy. The de Warenne surname derives from the castle of that name on the River Varenne, which flows through the 
1 CONC territory William acquired in Upper Normandy in the region today called Bellencombre.
1 CONT
1 CONT As a young man, William played a prominent role in protecting the Norman realm of the future William the Conqueror's from a major invasion by the King of France in February 1054 at the Battle of Mortemer. After this battle Roger d
1 CONC e Mortemer forfeited most of his lands, and the duke gave them to William.
1 CONT
1 CONT Battle of Hastings
1 CONT
1 CONT William was one of the nobles who advised Duke William when the decision to invade England was being considered. He fought at Hastings, and afterwards received the Rape of Lewes in Sussex, and subsequently lands in twelve other sh
1 CONC ires. He built castles at Lewes (Sussex), Reigate (Surrey), Castle Acre (Norfolk) and Conisbrough in Yorkshire. By the time of the Domesday survey he was one of the wealthiest landholders in England with holdings in 12 counties.
1 CONT
1 CONT Defending the Realm
1 CONT
1 CONT He fought against rebels at the Isle of Ely in 1071 where he showed a special desire to hunt down Hereward the Wake who had murdered his brother the year before.
1 CONT
1 CONT William was loyal to William II William Rufus), and it was probably in early 1088 that he was created Earl of Surrey.
1 CONT
1 CONT Marriages and Children
1 CONT
1 CONT He was married twice.
1 CONT
1 CONT First, William married Gundred (Latin: Gundrada), sister of Gerbod the Fleming, Earl of Chester. The children of William and Gundred were:
1 CONT
1 CONT William de Warenne, 2nd Earl of Surrey (d. 1138)
1 CONT Edith de Warenne who married Gerard de Gournay
1 CONT Reynold de Warenne, who inherited lands from his mother in Flanders and died before 1118
1 CONT
1 CONT Second, William married  a sister of Richard Gouet.
1 CONT
1 CONT Death
1 CONT
1 CONT William died shortly after becoming Earl of Surrey from wounds he received while helping suppress the rebellion of 1088. At the time of his death, it has been estimated that his wealth was equivalent to £73.9 billion in today's mo
1 CONC ney.
1 CONT wikipedia
0 @N6000000003051205547@ NOTE [From "The Origins of Some Anglo-Norman Families"]For this identification see Mr. Loyd's paper 'The Origin of the Family of Warenne' in Yorkshire Arch. Journal, vol. xxxi, pp. 97-113. The hamlet of Varenne lies on the river Varenn
1 CONC e c. 2 miles S of Arques and c. 13 miles N of Bellencombre. The latter place, arr. Dieppe, cant. Bellencombre, where there was a castle, became the caput of the Warenne honour in Normandy.
0 @N6000000003635725178@ NOTE [1494] WSHNGT.ASC file (Geo Washington Ahnentafel) # 4360424 = 230244
0 @N6000000003949658023@ NOTE 1st son and heir, is known only from his subscriptions to two charters of his father for the Holy Trinity of Rouen. As his father's lands near Rouen and in the pays de Caux did not pass to his son William or William's descendants,
1 CONC  it is likely that Rodulf succeeded to them on his father's death; he married Emma, whose parentage is unknown. [Complete Peerage XII/1:493, XIV:603, (transcribed by Dave Utzinger)]
1 CONT [From "The Origins of Some Anglo-Norman Families"]  For this identification see Mr. Loyd's paper 'The Origin of the Family of Warenne' in Yorkshire Arch. Journal, vol. xxxi, pp. 97-113. The hamlet of Varenne lies on the river Vare
1 CONC nne c. 2 miles S of Arques and c. 13 miles N of Bellencombre. The latter place, arr. Dieppe, cant. Bellencombre, where there was a castle, became the caput of the Warenne honour in Normandy.
1 CONT Curt Hofemann, curt_hofemann@yahoo.com, provided the following additional information on Rodulf, in a post-em:
1 CONT K.S.B. Keats-Rohan "Poppa of Bayeux and her Family":
1 CONT 1027-35: first occurrence of Ralph de Warenne in a charter for Saint-Amand [p22]
1 CONT c1050: grant of land in Vascoeuil by Ralph de Warenne and wife Beatrice; charter mentions Ralph's brother Godfrey and was attested by Gotmund miles abbatis. Dateable to c1050 by a reference to Roger de Beaumont as Vicomte of Rouen
1 CONC  [p22] 
1 CONT 1050's: well known charters of early 1050s by which Ralph de Warenne and his wife Beatrice were associated with the lands of Roger fitz Bishop Hugh of Coutances and his sons. [p23]
1 CONT Research note: K-R p22 contradicts CP (& Holloway & Wagner) by stating Rodulph/Ralph died before Beatrice. Beatrice is listed as living 1053 (CP XII/1:492 & ES III:698) & dead before 1059 (CP XII/1:492, K-R p22, Moriarty p184, Wag
1 CONC ner p46]. K-R states a grant "made by widow Beatrice" to Preaux of land near Dozule, Eure was "dated during the time of William son of Count Robert, suggesting that William had not yet begun the series of military achievements tha
1 CONC t enabled him to be detached from his father in such references, i.e. before c1054-60". To do: check CP's source of the 1074 grant. Also is the term "widow" K-R's or stated in the grant of the land near Dozule?... Curt 
1 CONT RODULF DE WARENNE, 1st son and heir, is known only from his subscriptions to two charters of his father for the Holy Trinity of Rouen. As his father's lands near Rouen and in the pays de Caux did not pass to his son William or Wil
1 CONC liam's descendants, it is likely that Rodulf succeeded to them on his father's death; he married Emma, whose parentage is unknown. [Complete Peerage XII/1:493, XIV:603, (transcribed by Dave Utzinger)]
1 CONT [From "The Origins of Some Anglo-Norman Families"]
1 CONT For this identification see Mr. Loyd's paper 'The Origin of the Family of Warenne' in Yorkshire Arch. Journal, vol. xxxi, pp. 97-113. The hamlet of Varenne lies on the river Varenne c. 2 miles S of Arques and c. 13 miles N of Bell
1 CONC encombre. The latter place, arr. Dieppe, cant. Bellencombre, where there was a castle, became the caput of the Warenne honour in Normandy.
1 CONT Curt Hofemann, curt_hofemann@yahoo.com, provided the following additional information on Rodulf, in a post-em:
1 CONT .S.B. Keats-Rohan "Poppa of Bayeux and her Family":
1 CONT 1027-35: first occurrence of Ralph de Warenne in a charter for Saint-Amand [p22]
1 CONT c1050: grant of land in Vascoeuil by Ralph de Warenne and wife Beatrice; charter mentions Ralph's brother Godfrey and was attested by Gotmund miles abbatis. Dateable to c1050 by a reference to Roger de Beaumont as Vicomte of Rouen
1 CONC  [p22]
1 CONT 1050's: well known charters of early 1050s by which Ralph de Warenne and his wife Beatrice were associated with the lands of Roger fitz Bishop Hugh of Coutances and his sons. [p23]
1 CONT Research note: K-R p22 contradicts CP (& Holloway & Wagner) by stating Rodulph/Ralph died before Beatrice. Beatrice is listed as living 1053 (CP XII/1:492 & ES III:698) & dead before 1059 (CP XII/1:492, K-R p22, Moriarty p184, Wag
1 CONC ner p46]. K-R states a grant "made by widow Beatrice" to Preaux of land near Dozule, Eure was "dated during the time of William son of Count Robert, suggesting that William had not yet begun the series of military achievements tha
1 CONC t enabled him to be detached from his father in such references, i.e. before c1054-60". To do: check CP's source of the 1074 grant. Also is the term "widow" K-R's or stated in the grant of the land near Dozule?... Curt
