0 @N6000000001783262662@ NOTE Basic Life Information
1 CONT The daughter of Anslech and Gillette was Ertemberge de Briquebec, 935-985, who married her possible uncle, Torf The Rich de Harcourt, son of Bernard The Dane. [The incestuous nature of many early Crispin family marriages is well d
1 CONC ocumented - see Ivo Carnot, Epist. 45.] Touroude of Pont-Audemer, eldest son of Torf and Ertemberge, married Duvelina, sister of Gonnor de Crepon, spouse of Duke Richard of Normandy. [Robert of Torigni, G.N.D, Book VIII., ch. 36-3
1 CONC 7.]
1 CONT
1 CONT <http://stanhopefamilyorigins.com/>
0 @N6000000009304872954@ NOTE #Générale##Générale#Dame de Bricquebec.
0 @N6000000000437047030@ NOTE Name Prefix:<NPFX> Prince Name Suffix:<NSFX> of Denmark "the Rich [inglis family.and Keith .4FBK.ged.FBK.FBK.FBK.FTW] Torf wandered to the count ry of Greenland. "In 1007 a rich Greenlander,Torfin, determined to emigrate to Vinlan
1 CONC d,[now New England]. His followers numbered 60, and he was accompan ied by his wife, Gudrida, the widow of a previous explorer. Five other women were on board,and the ships were freighted with all kinds of domestic animal s, tools
1 CONC  and provisions for a permanent colony. Gudrida had been the first female to see thenew world, having accompanied her former husband the previous year. The Expedition prospered. The natives came in great numbers and trafficked in 
1 CONC furs and produce. Gudrida bore a son, Snorro, the first birth of Eu ropean parentage in America, who is said to have been an ancestor of the scul ptor Thorwaldson. Thefamily remained three years in the colony, but ultimate ly retu
1 CONC rned, and Thorfin settled and died in Iceland. The widow made a pilgra mage to Rome in her bereavement, and died in a cloister founded by her son in  Iceland. Other chiefs went to Vinland, but their history throws no further l igh
1 CONC t upon the colony.They, however, discovered land extending far away to th e south-west and inhabited by natives of different caste, of darker color and  more vigorous frame. The colony perished at last, destroyed probably like th 
1 CONC at of Greenland. Traces of it were found by Jesuit missionaries among the I ndians Gaspe at the mouth of the St. Lawrence, a tribe which revered the cros s before the arrival of the missionaries. Pysical constitution and peculiari
1 CONC t ies of manners and custom are also cited in confirmation of European descent.  Father Charlevoix adds that 'many marks distinguishing them from other Ameri can Indians go far tomake me believe that they are a colony of Europeans
1 CONC  deg enerated into savagesthrough destitution.'"
0 @N6000000000682018156@ NOTE Date: Tue, 07 Feb 1995 09:43:00 -0500 (EST)
1 CONT
1 CONT Line 17605 from GEDCOM File not recognizable or too long:
1 CONT   NOTE Date: Tue, 07 Feb 1995 09:43:00 -0500 (EST) CONT From: Gordon Fisher fac_fisher@vax1.acs.jmu.edu
1 CONT
1 CONT About Torf, this is what the book by J Gardner Bartlett, *Newberry Genealogy*, Boston, 1914, p 3 says:
1 CONT
1 CONT "TORF, SEIGNEUR DE TORVILLE, a great Norman feudal baron, born about A. D. 920, is the earliest historical progenitor of the Newburgh or Newberry family from whom a certain and unbroken male line has been traced.  Probably he was 
1 CONC a grandson of one of the viking chiefs of Scandinavia who accompanied Rollo about 900 A.D.
1 CONT in the Norse invasion of northern France where they permanently settled and gave to the country its name "Normandy".  Torf possessed numerous lordships in Normandy, being Seigneur de Torville, Torcy, Torny, Torly, du Ponteautorf, 
1 CONC etc.  He married about 950, ERTEMBERGE DE BRIQUEBEC. Children:  i. TOUROUDE, SIRE DU PONTEAUDEMER, b. about 950. ii. TURCHETIL, SEIGNEUR DE TURQUEVILLE, ancestor of the celebrated Harcourt family of Normandy and England.  iii. WIL
1 CONC LIAM DE TORVILLE."
1 CONT
1 CONT The Newburgh-Newborough-Newberry line is alleged to go through the first of these children, TOURUDE.  Given the waywardness of spelling up til comparatively recent times, I'm skeptical about the "Torville", "Torcy", etc., being di
1 CONC fferent (there's also the problem of reading medieval calligraphy).  My wife loves the name of TORF's wife, given its resemblance to "bric-a-brac".
1 CONT
1 CONT from Search for the Passengers of the Mary & John - 1630, Volume 17, West Country Ancestries, 1620-1643, Part 1, ISBN 0-941273-17-2.  The Mary & John Clearing House [Burton w. Spear; 5602-305th st.; Toledo, OH 43611].  sent by Rob
1 CONC ert W. Oliphant; 3 Robinson Road; Westford, MA  01886; Email:  oliphant pages 103-110:
1 CONT
1 CONT NEWBERRY, THOMAS (1594-1635) OF DORCHESTER. MA
1 CONT
1 CONT Thomas Newbury brought his family to Dorchester, MA on the "Recovery of London" in 1634.  His ancestry traces back to a Norman origin.  The following condensed ancestry was taken from the Search for the Passengers of the Mary & Jo
1 CONC hn series, Vol. 13, p. 3-8, which was taken from The Newberry Genealogy by J. Gardner Bartlett, 1914, and from Complete Peerage by G. E. Cokayne.
1 CONT
1 CONT   1. Torf
1 CONT
1 CONT   2. Thorold. Seigneur of Pont-Audemer - Born about 950 A.D.
1 CONT
1 CONT   3. Humphrey du Veulees, Seignaur o Pont-Audemer, Vielles,
1 CONT      Beaumont and Beaumontel.  Founder of the monasteries at
1 CONT      Preaux, St. Acue and at St. Leger.  Born about 980 A.D.  He
1 CONT      m. Aubrey de la Haie (d.a. 1045), heiress of the Forest of
1 CONT      Brotonne.
1 CONT
1 CONT   4. Roger de Beaumont - Died about 1094/7.  Seigneur of Pont-
1 CONT      Audemer, Beaumont, Brionne and Vieilles in Normandy.  He m.
1 CONT      Adeline, living in 1081, daughter and heiress of Waleran,
1 CONT      Count of Meulan.
1 CONT
1 CONT   5. Henry de Beaumont, 1st Earl of Warwick - Born about 1048, at
1 CONT      the Castle of Newborough in Normandy, from which the family
1 CONT      name is derived; died 1119.  He m. Margaret the daughter of
1 CONT      Geoffrey, (of Perche and Mortagne, by his wife, Beatrice of
1 CONT      Montdidiei).
1 CONT
1 CONT   6. Robert de Newburgh - Born about 1100.  Henry I gave him the
1 CONT      manor of Winfrith in Dorset.
1 CONT
1 CONT   7. Roger de Newburgh - Born about 1135.  He settled in the
1 CONT      manor of Winfrith, Dorset.  There is a Winfrith Newburgh and
1 CONT      a Bindon Abbey about 12 miles East of Weymouth, Dorset.  He
1 CONT      died a. 1192 and was buried at Bindon Abbey.
1 CONT
1 CONT NOTE: Bartlett took the next ten generations of the Newburgh family at Winfrith, Dorset from Hutchin's History of Dorset.
1 CONT
1 CONT   8. Robert de Newburgh - Born about 1175. Heir to Winfrith Manor
1 CONT      that remained in the Newburgh line until 1515.  He is buried
1 CONT      at Bindon Abbey, a. 1230.
1 CONT
1 CONT   9. Robert de Newburgh - Born about 1200.  Heir to Winfrith
1 CONT      Manor.  Died 1246.
1 CONT
1 CONT  10. Henry de Newburgh - Born about 1223.
1 CONT
1 CONT  11. John de Newburgh - Born about 1250.  Heir to Dorset estates.
1 CONT      Bu. in Bindon Abbey, a. 1309.
1 CONT
1 CONT  12. Sir Robert de Newburgh, knt. - Born about 1280.  Heir to
1 CONT      Dorset estates.  Died 1338.
1 CONT
1 CONT  13. Sir Thomas Newburgh, knt. - Born about 1315.  Heir.  Died a.
1 CONT      1365.
1 CONT
1 CONT  14. John Newburgh, Esq. - Born about 1340.  Died 04-June-1381
1 CONT      and bu. in Bindon Abbey.  He m. Margaret Poyntz, a. 1370,
1 CONT      dau. of Nicholas de Poyntz.  For her Poyntz ancestry, see
1 CONT      Weis, Ancestral Roots, 6th Edition, pp. 220-221   Her
1 CONT      ancestry traces to Charlemagne.
1 CONT
1 CONT 15.  John Newburgh, Esq. - Born about 1370.  Died a. 1440.  He m.
1 CONT      Joan de la Mere, by 1400, dau. of Sir John de 1a Mere, Knt.
1 CONT      Lived in Dorset.
1 CONT
1 CONT  16. John Newburgh, Esq. - Born about 1400.  He d. 01-Apr-1484.
1 CONT      Bu. in Bindon Abbey, in a tomb at the foot of his father's
1 CONT      tomb.  His will was probated 09-Apr-1484 (PCC 20 Legge).  He
1 CONT      m. (2) Alice, dau. of William Carnet of Toomer, Somerset,
1 CONT      who bought him the manor of Berkley, Somerset (1 mi. NE of
1 CONT      Frome).  She was the widow of John Westbury.
1 CONT
1 CONT  17. Thomas Newborough - Born about 1445.  Died 15-Mar-1512/13.
1 CONT      He inherited the manor of Berkley, Somerset (1 mile NE of
1 CONT      Frome).  Bu. there where there is a memorial inscription in
1 CONT      his honor.  He m. Alice ____, a. 1484, who m. (2) Thomas
1 CONT      Kyrton.  She d. 1525.
1 CONT
1 CONT  18. Walter Newborough - Born about 1487   He inherited 700 acres
1 CONT      in Dorset and he founded a new minor branch at the manor
1 CONT      house of Francis or Worth Francis, Netherbury.  Died 12-Aug-
1 CONT      1517, leaving a one month old heir.  He m. Elizabeth Birport
1 CONT      who m. (2) George Strangeways, of Muston, Dorset.
1 CONT
1 CONT  19. Richard Newburgh - Born 1517, heir.  His will, dated 03-Dec-
1 CONT      1568 (PCC 2 Lyon).  Bu. in Netherbury church.  He m.
1 CONT      Elizabeth Horsey, a. 1552, dau. of William Horsey, gent. of
1 CONT      Binghams manor of Netherbury.  She m. (2) _______ Woodshaw.
1 CONT
1 CONT      He succeeded to the manor of Othe Francis in Netherbury,
1 CONT      Kingston and Bryan's Puddle, Dorset and lands in Swange,
1 CONT      Corfe-Castle, Worth and Winterbourne-Zelstone (20 miles East
1 CONT      of Dorchester) (Hutchins' History of Dorset).  In 1914 the
1 CONT      old manor house of Othe Francis (or Worth Francis), in
1 CONT      Netherbury, was a farm house situated by the river Birt,
1 CONT      near Netherbury church.
1 CONT
1 CONT Children of Richard Newburgh & Elizabeth Horsey (Newberry-19)
1 CONT
1 CONT   a. Elizabeth Newburgh - B.a. 1553.
1 CONT
1 CONT   b. Walter Newburgh - B.a. 1555. d. Feb. 1598/9.  Heir to his
1 CONT      father's estates.  Will, proved 17-May-1599, London (PCC 34
1 CONT      Kidd).  He m. Elizabeth Culliford of Dorset.  Her will
1 CONT      proved 10-Jun-1619 (PCC 58 Parker).
1 CONT
1 CONT      Children of Walter Newburgh & Elizabeth Culliford
1 CONT
1 CONT      i.   Emme Newburgh - B.a. 1591, Netherbury.  She married
1 CONT           John Baker, about 1614.
1 CONT
1 CONT      ii.  John Newburgh - Bpt. 12-Dec-1592, Netherbury.  d.y.
1 CONT
1 CONT      iii. Alice Newburgh - Bpt. 06-Feb-1593/4, Netherbury.
1 CONT
1 CONT      iv.  Walter Newburgh - Bpt. 27-May-1595.  Heir of the manor
1 CONT           of Othe Francis in Netherbury.  Graduated from
1 CONT           Brasenose College, Oxford, B.A., 29-Jan-1611/2 and
1 CONT           M.A., 05-July-1614.  In 1618, his widowed mother
1 CONT           Elizabeth purchased the avowson (or right of nomination
1 CONT           of the clergyman) of the parish of Symondsbury, Dorset.
1 CONT
1 CONT           She died a few months later and the avowson passed to
1 CONT           Walter.  He studied theology at Oxford, received a B.D.
1 CONT           in 1623 and then nominated himself to the living of
1 CONT           Symondsbury, where he was rector from 1624 until his
1 CONT           death, 03-June-1631.  He was a staunch supporter of
1 CONT           Rev. John White's efforts in New England.  His will,
1 CONT           dated 26-May-1631, proved 07-Nov-1632 (PCC 113 Awdley).
1 CONT
1 CONT           He married (1) Catherine Strode, dau. of Sir Richard
1 CONT           Strode, Knt. of Newenham, Devon, a "Dorchester
1 CONT           Adventurer" -  She died 11-Nov-1625 Leaving no issue.
1 CONT           He married (2) Jane Browne, dau. of John Browne Esq. of
1 CONT           Frampton, Dorset, about 1626.  She married (2) about
1 CONT           1635 Rev. John Stoughton, D.D., brother of Thomas
1 CONT           Stoughton who came to Dorchester, MA, in 1630.   John
1 CONT           Stoughton was a Puritan clergyman rector of Aller,
1 CONT           Somerset (12 mi. East of Taunton) and later St. Mary
1 CONT           Aldermaybury, London.  Rev. Walter Newburgh and Jane
1 CONT           Browne had three children:  John, 1627, Elizabeth,
1 CONT           1629, and Walter, 1631.
1 CONT
1 CONT      v.   Elizabeth Newburgh - Bpt. 1596, East Stoke, Dorset.
1 CONT           She marrie James Millward, 1623, of Batcombe, Somerset.
1 CONT
1 CONT      vi.  Thomas Newburgh - Bpt. 14-Apr-1597, Netherbury.
1 CONT
1 CONT      vii. Robert Newburgh - Born 1599.  Died 1642.  His will, 18-
1 CONT           Oct-1642 proved, 01-Feb-1642/3.   (Arch. of Berks,
1 CONT           1642, Original will)  He married (1) Mary Davies, 27-
1 CONT           Mar-1627, St. Anne, Blackfriars, London.  He was
1 CONT           educated in law and was clerk to Sir George Croke,
1 CONT           knt., London judge.
1 CONT
1 CONT   c. Richard Newburgh - See below
1 CONT
1 CONT   d. Katherine Newburgh - Born about 1559.
1 CONT
1 CONT   e. William Newburgh - Born about 1561. Buried, 28-Oct-1623,
1 CONT      Frampton, Dorset.  Resided in Symondsbury, Dorset.  He
1 CONT      graduated from Queen's College, Oxford B.A. 03-Feb-1583/4,
1 CONT      studied in London and was a lawyer.  No children.  H will
1 CONT      proved, 31-May-1633 (PCC 47 Russell).
1 CONT
1 CONT  20. Richard Newburgh - Born about 1557.  Died about 1629.  He
1 CONT      married his kinswoman Grace Matthew, 15-Jan-1580, Yarcombe,
1 CONT      Devon, dau. of John Matthew, and grandau. of Stephen and
1 CONT      Isabel (Newburgh) Matthew.  She was buried, 18-Dec-1632,
1 CONT      Yarcombe Devon, (5 mi. West of Chard).  Richard, being a
1 CONT      younger son, inherited no lands and was not educated for a
1 CONT      profession and he did not secure much estate by his
1 CONT      marriage.  His wife was heiress to a small landed estate in
1 CONT      Yarcombe, Devon, 12 miles from Netherbury and he lived there
1 CONT      the rest of his life.  NOTE:  Grace Matthew may have been
1 CONT      related to Frances Matthew, who came from Ottery St. Mary,
1 CONT      Devon, in 1631.
1 CONT
1 CONT      Children of Richard Newburgh & Grace Matthew (Bpt. Yarcombe)
1 CONT
1 CONT      a.   John Newburgh - Bpt. 02-Mar-1581/2
1 CONT
1 CONT      b.   William Newburgh - Bpt. 18-Apr-1584.
1 CONT
1 CONT      c.   Alice Newburgh - Bpt. 31-Dec-1586.
1 CONT
1 CONT      d.   Fides Newburgh - Bpt. 01-Oct-1589.
1 CONT
1 CONT      e.   Robert Newburgh - Bpt. 01-Apr-1592.
1 CONT
1 CONT      f.   Thomas Newburgh - See below
1 CONT
1 CONT      g.   Dorothy Newburgh - Bpt. 16-Sep-1597.  Bu. 06-Aug-1600.
1 CONT
1 CONT      h.   Robert Newburgh - Bpt. 23-Apr-1600, Yarcombe, and
1 CONT           buried there, 25-Aug-1687.  His will, dated 12-Nov-1677
1 CONT           proved, 19-Sep-1687 (PCC 117 Foote).  He married, (1)
1 CONT           Anne ______, 1620 (mother of all his children), buried
1 CONT           11-Apr-1646, Yarcombe, Devon.  He married (2) Anstis
1 CONT           Hellyer, 11-Jul-1656, of Church Station.  One of his
1 CONT           descendants may have been Robert Newbary of Yarcombe
1 CONT           who m. Elizabeth Copelstone of Marshwood, 20-Feb-1720,
1 CONT           at Whitchurch Canonicorum, Dorset, to which parish
1 CONT           Joseph Newberry (b.a. 1620) son of Thomas Newberry of
1 CONT           Dorchester, MA returned a. 1648.
1 CONT
1 CONT           Children of Robert Newburgh & (1) Anne (Bpt. Yarcombe)
1 CONT
1 CONT           i.   Grace Newburgh - Bpt. 04-Feb-1620/1. d.y.
1 CONT
1 CONT           ii.  Henry Newburgh - Bpt. 26-Jan-1622/3   His will,
1 CONT                dated 07-Jun-1692 proved, 10-Oct-1696 (PCC 209
1 CONT                Bond).  He lived and was buried in Yarcombe.  He
1 CONT                married (1) widow Mrs. Anne Warring, a. 1650 and
1 CONT                (2) Lydia Ball, 14-June-1660 and (3) Grace
1 CONT                (Ward?).
1 CONT
1 CONT           iii. John Newburgh - Bpt. 31-Dec-1624.  Bu. 15-Jan-
1 CONT                1644/5.
1 CONT
1 CONT           iv.  Mary Newburgh - Bpt. 07-Jan-1626/7.
1 CONT
1 CONT           v.   Samuel Newburgh - Bpt. 11-Dec-1631.  Died, 1696.
1 CONT                He married, (1) Anne Davy alias Wheeler, a. 1653,
1 CONT                dau. of Richard and Thomasine Davy alias Wheeler.
1 CONT                He married (2) Elizabeth Turner, a. 1660.  His
1 CONT                will, dated 19-Dec-1696, proved 27-Apr-1697 (Arch.
1 CONT                of Exeter, Original will).  Eight children.
1 CONT
1 CONT           vi.  Joseph Newburgh - Bpt. 23-Mar-1633/4.  d.y.
1 CONT
1 CONT           vii. Sarah Newburgh - Bpt. 07-Oct-1635.  Married
1 CONT                Clement Bovett, a. 1656, who d. 1663.  Three
1 CONT                children.
1 CONT
1 CONT           viii Elizabeth Newburgh - Bpt. 19-Oct-1639.  She
1 CONT                married John Prince, 10-Jul-1656.
1 CONT
1 CONT           ix.  Hannah Newburgh - Bpt. 01-Sep-1642.  Bu. 30-Jun-
1 CONT                1647.
1 CONT
1 CONT           x.   John Newburgh - Bpt. 15-Jan-1645/7.  d.y.
1 CONT
1 CONT      i.   Henry Newburgh - Bpt. 08-Jul-1603.
1 CONT
1 CONT  21. Thomas Newberry - Bpt. 11-Nov-1594, Yarcombe, Devon.   He
1 CONT      died suddenly in December, 1635, in Dorchester, MA, as he
1 CONT      was preparing to move to Windsor, CT.  He married (1) Jane
1 CONT      Dabinot, a. 1619 (b.a. 1600), dau. of Christopher Dabinot
1 CONT      (See Search for the Passengers of the Mary & John series
1 CONT      Vol. 11, p. 98) of Yarcombe, Devon and Charstock, Marshwood,
1 CONT      and Upway, Dorset.  Will of Christopher Dabinot,  01-Jul-
1 CONT      1637 (PCC 112 Goare).  Christopher's estate was between
1 CONT
1 CONT Line 17829 from GEDCOM File not recognizable or too long:
1 CONT   NOTE  21. Thomas Newberry - Bpt. 11-Nov-1594, Yarcombe, Devon.   He CONT -2,000 and -6,000, so Thomas Newberry evidently enhanced his
1 CONT      -2,000 and -6,000, so Thomas Newberry evidently enhanced his
1 CONT      fortune by his marriage.  Joane died about 1629, leaving
1 CONT      five small children.
1 CONT
1 CONT      Thomas married (2) Jane (Dabinot?), about 1630, perhaps the
1 CONT      cousin of his first wife and dau. of John and Joan Dabinot
1 CONT      of Chardstock, Dorset.  John Dabinot wrote his will in 1624,
1 CONT
1 CONT Line 17835 from GEDCOM File not recognizable or too long:
1 CONT   NOTE      Thomas married (2) Jane (Dabinot?), about 1630, perhaps the CONT
1 CONT   bequeathing -150 to his dau., Jane, when she married with
1 CONT      bequeathing -150 to his dau., Jane, when she married with
1 CONT      the consent of her mother and his overseers, Christopher
1 CONT      Dabinot and Thomas Newberry.  Jane Dabinot was baptized 12-
1 CONT      Jun-1611, Chardstock, Dorset.  Perhaps Thomas "consented"
1 CONT      she should marry himself, soon after he became a widower
1 CONT      with five children, ages lO, 8, 6, 4 and 1. She married (2)
1 CONT      Rev. John Warham, 1637, as his second wife and died, 23-Apr-
1 CONT      1655, Norwalk, CT.
1 CONT
1 CONT      In 1626 Thomas was living in Marshwood, Dorset, 7 miles
1 CONT      North of Lyme Regis, on an estate called "Coweleyes".  In
1 CONT      1625 this property belonged to his father-in-law,
1 CONT      Christopher Dabinot, on a 99 year lease for lives, with
1 CONT      remainder of term to Joseph Newberry (b. 1620) and Benjamin
1 CONT      Newberry (b. 1624), sons of Thomas.
1 CONT
1 CONT      Thomas Newberry apparently continued at Marshwood (Marshwood
1 CONT      Vale) until he sailed for New England.  There are two
1 CONT      conflicting records on when he sailed.  William Whiteway of
1 CONT      Dorchester, Dorset, an associate of Rev. John White, kept a
1 CONT      diary (now in the British Museum, London), which states that
1 CONT      "1634 April 17, Mr. Newburgh of Marshwood Vale and many
1 CONT      others set sail from Weymouth towards New England".  This
1 CONT      agrees with the first appearance of Thomas Newberry in the
1 CONT      Dorchester, MA, records on 01-Sep-1634.
1 CONT
1 CONT      However, Thomas Newberry's name also appears on the
1 CONT      passenger list of the "Recovery" of London, which sailed
1 CONT      from Weymouth, Dorset, 31-Mar-1634.  This ship carried other
1 CONT      people associated with the "Mary & John" group of 1630,
1 CONT      including Jonathan Gillett and Stephen Terry, returning to
1 CONT      New England with their new wives.  (See Search for the
1 CONT      Passengers of the Mary & John series Vol. 2, p. 112).
1 CONT
1 CONT      By 1635 Thomas Newberry had acquired a large, 400 acre farm,
1 CONT      one mile in length two-thirds of a mile wide, an area now in
1 CONT      the city of Quincy, MA.  He soon became one of the most
1 CONT      prominent citizens of Dorchester and in 1634 he was one of
1 CONT      the ten men chosen to order the affairs of the Dorchester
1 CONT      plantation.
1 CONT
1 CONT      He wrote his will, 12-Oct-1635, and died soon after.  The
1 CONT      inventory of his estate was taken, 28-Jan-1636, and it
1 CONT
1 CONT Line 17873 from GEDCOM File not recognizable or too long:
1 CONT   NOTE      He wrote his will, 12-Oct-1635, and died soon after.  The CONT totaled -1520.  Only one out of one hundred early settlers
1 CONT      totaled -1520.  Only one out of one hundred early settlers
1 CONT      possessed such wealth at that time.  The most valuable items
1 CONT      in his inventory were his Dorchester farm and livestock
1 CONT
1 CONT Line 17876 from GEDCOM File not recognizable or too long:
1 CONT   NOTE      He wrote his will, 12-Oct-1635, and died soon after.  The CONT (-693), land in England (-300), meadows and livestock in
1 CONT      (-693), land in England (-300), meadows and livestock in
1 CONT
1 CONT Line 17877 from GEDCOM File not recognizable or too long:
1 CONT   NOTE      He wrote his will, 12-Oct-1635, and died soon after.  The CONT Dorchester (-270). He left -200 to his wife and all her
1 CONT      Dorchester (-270). He left -200 to his wife and all her
1 CONT      "household stuff she brought with her at her marriage".  The
1 CONT      remainder of his estate was to be divided equally among his
1 CONT      seven children.
1 CONT
1 CONT      Thomas Newberry's widow, Jane, married (2) Rev. John Warham,
1 CONT      in 1637, whose first wife died in late 1634, and they both
1 CONT      moved to Windsor with all of the Newberry children.
1 CONT
1 CONT      Children of Thomas Newberry & (1) Joan Dabinot
1 CONT
1 CONT      a.   Joseph Newberry - Born about 1620.  He was granted land
1 CONT           in Windsor, where he remained until 1648 (age 28), but
1 CONT           soon after he returned to England and settled on the
1 CONT           estate called, "Coweleyes", which his grandfather had
1 CONT           obtained on a 99-year lease for lives.  He was still
1 CONT           there in 1675, when he brought a suit against his
1 CONT           cousin, Gideon Hayne.  He married, ______ and had
1 CONT           children recorded at Whitchurch Canonicorum, Dorset:
1 CONT           Joseph (buried 1658), Sarah (Bpt. 1654, buried, 1655)
1 CONT           and Benjamin (Bpt. 1656).
1 CONT
1 CONT      b.   Sarah Newberry - Born about 1622.  She died 11-Jul-
1 CONT           1684, Windsor.  She married Henry Wolcott, Jr., 18-Nov-
1 CONT           1641, son of Henry Wolcott and Elizabeth Saunders.
1 CONT           Henry, Jr. died 12-Jul-1680.  He was an importing
1 CONT           merchant, who acquired considerable wealth.  He became
1 CONT           prominent in public life; Deputy to the General Court
1 CONT           (1660-1661) and magistrate from 1662 until his death.
1 CONT
1 CONT Line 17902 from GEDCOM File not recognizable or too long:
1 CONT   NOTE      b.   Sarah Newberry - Born about 1622.  She died 11-Jul- CONT
1 CONT     His estate: -3,977 (-2,784 in Connecticut and -1,193 in
1 CONT           His estate: -3,977 (-2,784 in Connecticut and -1,193 in
1 CONT           England).  (For their eight children (1643-1658) and
1 CONT           descendants see Search for the Passengers of the Mary &
1 CONT           John series Vol. 9, p. 79-83)
1 CONT
1 CONT      c.   Major Benjamin Newberry - Born about 1624.  Died 11-
1 CONT           Sep-1689, Windsor. He married Mary Allyn, 11-Jun-1646,
1 CONT           Windsor, dau. of Hon. Matthew Allyn and Margaret Wyatt
1 CONT           of Braunton, Devon, and Windsor, CT.  He was granted
1 CONT           land in Windsor in 1640 and he spent his life
1 CONT           accumulating land and in farming.  In 1656 he entered
1 CONT           public life and was prominent for 33 years in civil and
1 CONT           military affairs.  In the will of his mother's sister,
1 CONT           Anne (Dabinot) Gibbs May 1670, of Marshwood, Dorset,
1 CONT           she gave, "to my nephew, Joseph Newbury, the
1 CONT           hithermost, Coweleyes, next to the Barton and to his
1 CONT           brother (Thomas) in New England, the fathermost
1 CONT           Coweleyes" (PCC 19 Bence).  His own estate totaled
1 CONT
1 CONT Line 17919 from GEDCOM File not recognizable or too long:
1 CONT   NOTE      c.   Major Benjamin Newberry - Born about 1624.  Died 11- CONT
1 CONT      -563.  Nine children.
1 CONT           -563.  Nine children.
1 CONT
1 CONT      d.   Mary Newberry - Bpt. 22-Oct-1626, Whitchurch
1 CONT           Canonicorum, Dorset.  Died 29-Aug-1688.  She married
1 CONT           Capt. Daniel Clark, 13-Jun-1644, Windsor (1622- 1710)
1 CONT           who m. (2) Mrs. Martha (Pitkin) Wolcott.  He came to
1 CONT           Windsor with Rev. Huit.  He was Deputy (1657-1661),
1 CONT           Assistant (1662-1664), Secretary of the Connecticut
1 CONT           Colony (1658-1663), and captain (1664).  Ten children.
1 CONT
1 CONT      e.   John Newberry - Bpt. 19-Feb-1628/9, Whitchurch
