0 @N6000000002248243873@ NOTE Torf, which is a Danish name is probably a variant of Thorof which means Thor's Wolf, Thor being the God of Thunder.
1 CONT
1 CONT Places in Normandy named for hiTorfville, Tourville, Tourneville, Turville, Turvile and Turuoville.
0 @N6000000003635727624@ NOTE [1060] WSHNGT.ASC file (Geo Washington Ahnentafel) # 17441664
0 @N6000000003949643326@ NOTE see the  "Newberry Genealogy" by J. Gardiner Bartlett where the line will be found  continued back through 14 more generations to Torf, Seigneur de torville, a  great  Norman Feudal Baron, b. abt. 920, in a certain and unbroken li
1 CONC ne."     --- Ernest Flagg, *Genealogical Notes on the Founding of New England*,  1926 (rep  1990), p 337 
1 CONT "TORF, SEIGNEUR DE TORVILLE, a great Norman feudal baron, born about A. D.  920, is 
1 CONT the earliest historical progenitor of the Newburgh or Newberry family from whom a certain and unbroken male line has been traced. Probably he was a grandson of  one of the viking chiefs of Scandinavia who accompanied Rollo about 9
1 CONC 00 A.D. in  the Norse invasion of northern France where they permanently settled and gave  to the country its name "Normandy".  Torf possessed numerous lordships in  Normandy, being Seigneur de Torville, Torcy, Torny, Torly, du Po
1 CONC nteautord,  etc.  (P) 
1 CONT He married about 950, ERTEMBERGE DE BRIQUEBEC.  Children: i.  TOUROUDE, SIRE DU  PONTEAUDEMER, b. about 950.  ii. TURCHETIL, SEIGNEUR DE TURQUEVILLE, ancestor of  the celebrated Harcourt family of Normandy and England.   iii. WILL
1 CONC IAM DE TORVILLE." --- J. Gardner Bartlett, *Newberry Genealogy: The Ancestors and  Descendants of  Thomas Newberry of Dorchester, Mass., 1624, 920-1914*, Boston, 1914, p 3. 
1 CONT There is a footnote on p. 3 to this entry:  "It has been suggested that he was a son of  Bernard the Dane, the most powerful of the feudal nobles of Normandy during  the reign of Duke William I. (927-943) and Regent during the min
1 CONC ority of Duke  Richard I. (943-955); but this claim has not been proved."  He was married to  Ertemberge de BRIQUEBEC about 950.
