1 CONT Saint Vladimir Svyatoslavich the Great (c.958–1015) was the grand prince of Kiev who converted to Christianity in 988, and proceeded to baptise the whole Kievan Rus. His name is known in three traditions: in Old East Slavic and mo
1 CONC dern Ukrainian as Volodymyr (?????????), in Old Church Slavonic and modern Russian as Vladimir (????????), and in the Norse and modern Scandinavian languages as Valdemar. Contents [hide] 
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Bloody way to the throne
1 CONT     * 2 Years of pagan rule
1 CONT     * 3 Baptism of Rus
1 CONT     * 4 Christian reign
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Bloody way to the throne
1 CONT
1 CONT Vladimir was the youngest son of Svyatoslav I by his slave girl Malusha, described in the Norse sagas as a prophetess who lived to the age of 100 and was brought from her cave to the palace to predict the future. Hagiographic trad
1 CONC ition of dubious authenticity connects his childhood with the name of his grandmother, Olga Prekrasa, who was Christian and governed the capital during Svyatoslav's frequent military campaigns. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Transferring his capital to Pereyaslavets in 969, Sviatoslav designated Vladimir ruler of Novgorod the Great but gave Kiev to his legitimate son Yaropolk. After Sviatoslav's death (972), a fratricidal war erupted (976) between Yar
1 CONC opolk and his younger brother Oleg, ruler of the Drevlians. In 977 Vladimir fled to his kinsmen in Scandinavia, collecting as many of the Viking warriors as he could to assist him to recover Novgorod, and on his return the next ye
1 CONC ar marched against Yaropolk. Vladimir and Rogneda (1770). Enlarge Vladimir and Rogneda (1770). 
1 CONT
1 CONT On his way to Kiev he sent ambassadors to Rogvolod (Norse: Ragnvald), prince of Polotsk, to sue for the hand of his daughter Rogneda (Norse: Ragnhild). The well-born princess refused to affiance herself to the son of a bondswoman,
1 CONC  but Vladimir attacked Polotsk, slew Rogvolod, and took Ragnhild by force. Actually, Polotsk was a key fortress on the way to Kiev, and the capture of Polotsk and Smolensk facilitated the taking of Kiev (980), where he slew Yaropo
1 CONC lk by treachery, and was proclaimed konung, or kagan, of all Kievan Rus. [edit] 
1 CONT
1 CONT Years of pagan rule
1 CONT
1 CONT In addition to his father's extensive domain, Vladimir continued to expand his territories. In 981 he conquered the Cherven cities, the modern Halychyna; in 983 he subdued the Yatvyags, whose territories lay between Lithuania and 
1 CONC Poland; in 985 he led a fleet along the central rivers of Russia to conquer the Bulgarians of the Kama, planting numerous fortresses and colonies on his way. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Though Christianity had won many converts since Olga's rule, Vladimir had remained thorough going pagan, taking eight hundred concubines (besides numerous wives) and erecting pagan statues and shrines to gods. It is argued that he
1 CONC  attempted to reform Slavic paganism by establishing thunder-god Perun as a supreme deity. It is probable that he instituted the practice of human sacrifices as well. [edit] 
1 CONT
1 CONT Baptism of Rus Modern statue in London, UK Enlarge Modern statue in London, UK 
1 CONT
1 CONT In the year 987, as the result of a consultation with his boyars, Vladimir sent envoys to study the religions of the various neighboring nations whose representatives had been urging him to embrace their respective faiths. The res
1 CONC ult is amusingly described by the chronicler Nestor. Of the Muslim Bulgarians of the Volga the envoys reported there is no gladness among them; only sorrow and a great stench, and that their religion was undesirable due to its tab
1 CONC oo against alcoholic beverages and pork. Russian sources also describe Vladimir consulting with Jewish envoys (who may or may not have been Khazars), and questioning them about their religion but ultimately rejecting it, saying th
1 CONC at their loss of Jerusalem was evidence of their having been abandoned by God. Ultimately Vladimir settled on Christianity. In the churches of the Germans his emissaries saw no beauty; but at Constantinople, where the full festiva
1 CONC l ritual of the Orthodox Church was set in motion to impress them, they found their ideal: "We no longer knew whether we were in heaven or on earth," they reported, "nor such beauty, and we know not how to tell of it." If Vladimir
1 CONC  was impressed by this account of his envoys, he was yet more so by political gains of the Byzantine alliance. 
1 CONT
1 CONT In 988, having taken the town of Chersones in Crimea, he negotiated for the hand of the emperor Basil II's sister, Anna. Never had a Greek princess married a Barbarian before, as matrimonial offers of French kings and German emper
1 CONC ors had been peremptorily rejected. In short, to marry the 27-year-old princess to a pagan Slav seemed impossible. Vladimir, however, was baptized at Chersones, taking the Christian name of Basil out of compliment to his imperial 
1 CONC brother-in-law; the sacrament was followed by his marriage with the Roman princess. Returning to Kiev in triumph, he destroyed pagan monuments and established many churches, starting with the splendid Church of the Tithes (989) an
1 CONC d monasteries on Mt. Athos. [edit] 
1 CONT
1 CONT Christian reign Historic statue in Kiev, Ukraine Enlarge Historic statue in Kiev, Ukraine 
1 CONT
1 CONT He now formed a great council out of his boyars, and set his twelve sons over his subject principalities. With his neighbors he lived at peace, the incursions of the savage Pechenegs alone disturbing his tranquillity. After Anna's
1 CONC  death, he married again, most likely to a granddaughter of Otto the Great. 
1 CONT
1 CONT He died at Berestovo, near Kiev, while on his way to chastise the insolence of his son, Prince Yaroslav of Novgorod. The various parts of his dismembered body were distributed among his numerous sacred foundations and were venerat
1 CONC ed as relics. One of the largest Kievan cathedrals is dedicated to him. The University of Kiev was named after the man who both civilized and Christianized Kievan Rus. There is the Order of St. Vladimir in Russia. Eastern Orthodox
1 CONC  churches celebrate the feast day of St. Vladimir on 15 July. 
1 CONT
1 CONT His memory was also kept alive by innumerable folk ballads and legends, which refer to him as Krasno Solnyshko, that is, the Fair Sun. With him the Varangian period of Eastern Slavic history ceases and the Christian period begins.
1 CONC
0 @N6000000002176677956@ NOTE The most significant event of his reign was his conversion to Byzantine
1 CONT Christianity in 988 and the institution of that religion as the official
1 CONT religion of the Russian people (see Orthodox Church). After casting off
1 CONT several pagan wives, he married Anne, the sister of the Byzantine Emperor,
1 CONT Basil II. See Anne, message under his wife. He did, however, forcibly convert
1 CONT Kiev and Novgorod to Christianity.
