1 CONT
1 CONT Vladimir and Rogneda (1770).Rogneda of Polotsk (962-1002) is theRussian name for Ragnhild, whose father Ragnvald (Slavic: Rogvolod)came from overseas (i.e., from Scandinavia) and established himself atPolotsk in the mid-10th centu
1 CONC ry.
1 CONT
1 CONT It has been speculated that Ragnvald belonged to the Yngling royalfamily of Norway. In or about 980, Vladimir of Novgorod, on learningthat she was betrothed to his brother Yaropolk I of Kiev, took Polotskand forced Rogneda to marr
1 CONC y him. Having raped Rogneda in the presenceof her parents, he ordered them to be killed, along with two ofRogneda's brothers.
1 CONT
1 CONT Rogneda gave him several children. The four sons were Yaroslav theWise, Vsevolod, Mstislav, and Izyaslav. She also bore two daughters,one of whom is named by Nestor the Chronicler as Predslava (taken as aconcubine of Boleslaus I o
1 CONC f Poland, according to Gallus). A laterchronicle tells a story, most likely taken from a Norse saga, ofRogneda plotting against Vladimir and asking her elder son, Izyaslav,to kill him. As was the Norse royal custom, she was sent w
1 CONC ith herelder son to govern the land of her parents, i.e. Polotsk. Izyaslav'sline continued to rule Polotsk and the newly-found town of Izyaslavluntil the Mongol invasion.
1 CONT
1 CONT There is another legend, that she became a nun and took the nameAnastasia.
