1 CONT William I of Provence
1 CONT
1 CONT William I (c. 950 - 993, after 29 August), called the Liberator, was Count of Provence from 968 to his abdication. In 975 or 979, he took the title of marchio or margrave. He is often considered the founder of the county of Proven
1 CONC ce. He and his elder brother Rotbold II, sons of Boso II of Arles, both carried the title of comes or count concurrently, but it is unknown if they were joint-counts of the whole of Provence or if the region was divided. His broth
1 CONC er never bore any other title than count so long as William lived, so the latter seems to have attained a certain supremacy.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 980, he was installed as Count of Arles. His sobriquet comes from his victories against the Saracens by which he liberated Provence from their threat, which had been constant since the establishment of a base at Fraxinet. At th
1 CONC e Battle of Tourtour in 973, with the assistance of the counts of the High Alps and the viscounts of Marseille and Fos, he definitively routed the Saracens, chasing them forever from Provence. He reorganised the region east of the
1 CONC  Rhône, which he conquered from the Saracens and which had been given him as a gift from King Conrad of Burgundy. Also by royal consent, he and his descendants controlled the fisc in Provence. With Isarn, Bishop of Grenoble, he re
1 CONC populated Dauphiné and settled an Italian count named Ugo Blavia near Fréjus in 970 in order to bring that land back to cultivation. For all this, he figures prominently in Ralph Glaber's chronicle with the title of dux and he app
1 CONC ears in a charter of 992 as pater patriae.
1 CONT
1 CONT He donated land to Cluny and retired to become a monk, dying at Avignon, where he was buried in the church of Saint-Croix at Sarrians. He was succeeded as margrave by his brother. His great principality began to diminish soon afte
1 CONC r his death as the castles of his vassals, which he had kept carefully under ducal control, soon became allods of their possessors.
1 CONT
1 CONT Marriages and Children
1 CONT
1 CONT He married 1st Arsenda, daughter of Arnold of Comminges and their son was:
1 CONT William II of Provence
1 CONT
1 CONT He married 2nd (against papal advice) in 984, Adelaide of Anjou, daughter of Fulk II of Anjou and Gerberga of Maine, and their daughter was:
1 CONT Constance of Arles (973 - 1034), married Robert II of France
1 CONT
1 CONT <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_I_of_Provence>
0 @N6000000001783576117@ NOTE Basic Life Information
1 CONT
1 CONT William III of Toulouse
1 CONT
1 CONT William III Taillefer (also spelled Tallefer or Tallifer; 975 - September 1037) was the Count of Toulouse, Albi, and Quercy from 972 or 978 to his death. He was the first of the Toulousain branch of his family to bear the title ma
1 CONC rchio, which he inherited (c.975) from Raymond II of Rouergue.
1 CONT
1 CONT His parentage has been subject to reevaluation. He has traditionally been called son of Raymond III Pons and Garsinda. However, recent research has revealed that William was instead son of Adelais of Anjou, known to have married a
1 CONC  Raymond, "Prince of Gothia". This discovery has required a complete reevaluation of the succession to the County of Toulouse during this period, and no scholarly consensus has developed.
1 CONT
1 CONT He and his vassals were notorious usurpers of church property. He stole from the abbey of Lézat, but gave it back between 1015 and 1025. Pope John XIX ordered him to stop his vassals from taking the lands of Moissac, a problem lat
1 CONC er remedied by his successor, Pons, who gave Moissac to Cluny.
1 CONT
1 CONT William became the most powerful prince in western Languedoc and he saw the rise of the House of Capet in France and a corresponding decrease in royal authority recognised in the south. He bore the title of marchio prefatus in pag
1 CONC o Tholosano: "prefect margrave in the Toulousain country." His influence extended into the Narbonensis and even Provence, on behalf of his wife. His power did not remain undiminished in his own city of Toulouse, where he was force
1 CONC d by a council of local noblemen and clerics to give up dues imposed on the market there.
1 CONT
1 CONT Before 992, William married Emma, daughter of Rotbold III of Provence. From her he gained titles and lands to Provence. From a prior marriage, he had two sons, Raymond and Hugh, who died young. His eldest son by Emma, Pons, inheri
1 CONC ted Toulouse and the title of Margrave of Provence. His second son Bertrand became Count of Forcalquier, a Provençal fief. He had two daughters: Rangarda, wife of Peter Raymond of Carcassonne by Emma, and Ildegarda Elisa, wife of 
1 CONC Fulk Bertrand of Provence by Emma. He had an illegitimate daughter that married Otto Raymond of L'Isle-Jourdain.
1 CONT
1 CONT Notes
1 CONT
1 CONT e.g. Lewis, p 341.
1 CONT Some historians have suggested a single additional generation (referred to as Raymond III of Toulouse, his father Raymond Pons being stripped of an ordinal), while others follow the Codice de Roda in giving Raymond Pons a son Raym
1 CONC ond who in turn had sons Hugh and Raymond. Identifying the last with the husband of Adelais inserts two generations, making William the great-grandson of Raymond Pons. Yet another reconstruction suggests that Raymond and Garsinda 
1 CONC died childless, and that Toulouse passed to kinsman Raymond II of Rouergue, from whom the husband of Adelais would descend. See Martin de Framond, "La succession des comtes de Toulouse autour de l'an mil(940--1030): reconsidératio
1 CONC ns", Annales du Midi 204 (1993), pp 461--488; Thierry Stasser, "Adélaïde d'Anjou. Sa famille, ses mariages, sa descendance", Le Moyen Age 103,1 (1997): 9-52; FMG for different reconstructions.
1 CONT
1 CONT <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_III_of_Toulouse>
1 CONT
1 CONT Other Source
1 CONT
1 CONT Relevant Discussion on the Talk Page. (October 2008)
1 CONT
1 CONT This whole page is a disaster. Traditionally, Raymond III Pons was father of William III. We now know that this was not the case, but that throws all numbering into chaos, and there is no scholarly consensus. One reconstruction in
1 CONC serts only a single Raymond. They then niftily solve the numbering problem by removing the number from Raymond Pons and calling the new one Raymond III. This has the advantage of not requiring a renumbering of the later Raymonds, 
1 CONC but is not the most likely solution to the problem (in fact, it seems to be preferred primarily by genealogists, simply because they don't want to renumber the later Raymonds).
1 CONT
1 CONT The Codice de Roda gives a pedigree where (Raymond) Pons was father of a Raymond, and grandfather of a Hugh and a Raymond. The will of Garsenda, widow of Raymond, names separately her nepos (nephew or grandson) count Hugh, and nep
1 CONC os Raymond, son of Gunidildis. This leads to the reconstruction that Raymond Pons had Raymond who married Gunidildis, having in turn Hugh and Raymond who married Adelais, in turns having William, i.e. two new Raymonds. (An unpubli
1 CONC shed alternative that has been discussed is an attempt to merge the two, making William son of Adelais the younger half-brother of Hugh and Raymond, sons of Gunidildis, but this too has problems.) Then there is the most unusual, i
1 CONC nterpreting nepos as nephew, an alternative reconstruction identifies count Hugh with the Count of Rouergue, and makes Raymond, father of William a member of this same Rouergue branch of the family instead.
1 CONT
1 CONT In other words, there are three published reconstructions, and several more guesses, with no consensus. This Raymond III page does not accurately portray the situation, but rather mixes and matches different parts from different t
1 CONC heories, creating an nonsensical chimera (for example, stating in the text that "Raymond III" had Pons Raymond (completely unsupported) and William, then listing his children as Raymond and Hugh). I don't even know the best way of
1 CONC  fixing the problem, but when the historians themselves can't even decide if there is one or two generations here, and everyone is using different ordinals to refer to the missing counts, it is hardly NPOV to present it as has bee
1 CONC n done here. Perhaps the best solution is to create a single page for the Succession of Toulouse, 950-978 which can discuss the entire controversy without forcing these round pegs into the square holes of individual pages for coun
1 CONC ts that may never have existed, and about whom we really know nothing other than who they might or might not have married or had as children or parents, all of which will be different depending on which reconstruction is followed.
1 CONC  Agricolae (talk) 17:31, 4 October 2008 (UTC)
1 CONT
1 CONT Do what you think is best, but you would have to do it. You can see what sources I used; they are out of date. I even knew of the succession/genealogy issue when I created it, but I was unaware of exactly what the issue was, so I 
1 CONC had no idea what information was now known to be incorrect and what needed revising. Unfortunately, I do not have easy access the sources required to clean up the mess. If you create a lenthy bibliography of the sources needed, th
1 CONC ough, I can check to see if I can access some and help fix it, but otherwise you are almost certainly the only regular contributor qualified and capable of doing it. (Since I don't know about the controversy, I cannot say whether 
1 CONC "Succession of Toulouse, 950-978" is a good idea, but if you think so, I encourage it.) Srnec (talk) 06:26, 5 October 2008 (UTC)
1 CONT
1 CONT Well, that won't work either. Settipani places Raymond Pons' death in 940/944, so even the date range in the page name is problematic. I am at a loss as to how to square this circle. The real problem is that an encyclopedia is des
1 CONC igned to present consensus. When there is no consensus, having articles for people who may never have existed becomes problematic. As to recent references, I am unaware of anything other than Stasser, Fromond and Settipani that sp
1 CONC ecifically analyze the problem. I suspect the eventual consensus will be that there were two additional Raymonds as counts, descending from Raymond Pons - basically the Roda document's version (followed by Stasser and Settipani, a
1 CONC nd one alternative given by Fromond), but as I am sure you appreciate, scholars don't just get together and vote on a consensus, it can take a century for a scholarly paradigm to shift to a new one, particularly in a field where t
1 CONC here is only infrequent publication.
1 CONT
1 CONT I have read back through some of the sources, and now have a better idea what I think the solution is, but for Wikipedia purposes, my analysis is irrelevant unless I publish it. Solving this problem here requires a way to discuss 
1 CONC this issue on a page or pages that accurately reflect(s) the uncertainty, and neither numbers nor dates would seem up to the task. Can anyone suggest a page title? I hesitate to use something cute like "The Missing Counts of Toulo
1 CONC use" but that is basically the kind of thing we need. Maybe I should just create it and hope someone renames it with a better name. Agricolae (talk) 21:00, 6 October 2008 (UTC)
1 CONT
1 CONT I understand. On the matter of a title: Counts of Toulouse, 940-978? In discussing the succession the issue of the date of death of the previous count will come up, so the article will be discussion the issue of who was count duri
1 CONC ng the period 940-954. Also, this title would make a good link from the page Counts of Toulouse (to which County of Toulouse is a redirect). The succession could be treated as a section at that article, but it might be unwieldly a
1 CONC nd distracting there. Srnec (talk) 04:22, 7 October 2008 (UTC)
1 CONT
1 CONT <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talk:Raymond_III_of_Toulouse#Disputed>
0 @N6000000002187931020@ NOTE William III of Toulouse
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT William III Taillefer (also spelled Tallefer or Tallifer; 975 – September 1037) was the Count of Toulouse, Albi, and Quercy from 972 or 978 to his death. He was the first of the Toulousain branch of his family to bear the title ma
1 CONC rchio, which he inherited (c.975) from Raymond II of Rouergue.
1 CONT
1 CONT His parentage has been subject to reevaluation. He has traditionally been called son of Raymond Pons and Garsinda.[1] However, recent research has revealed that several historical Counts of Toulouse have been overlooked, and that 
1 CONC William was actually the son of Raymond III of Toulouse, whom he succeeded in 978, and Adelais of Anjou.[2]
1 CONT
1 CONT He and his vassals were notorious usurpers of church property. He stole from the abbey of Lézat, but gave it back between 1015 and 1025. Pope John XIX ordered him to stop his vassals from taking the lands of Moissac, a problem lat
1 CONC er remedied by his successor, Pons, who gave Moissac to Cluny.
1 CONT
1 CONT William became the most powerful prince in western Languedoc and he saw the rise of the House of Capet in France and a corresponding decrease in royal authority recognised in the south. He bore the title of marchio prefatus in pag
1 CONC o Tholosano: "prefect margrave in the Toulousain country." His influence extended into the Narbonensis and even Provence, on behalf of his wife. His power did not remain undiminished in his own city of Toulouse, where he was force
1 CONC d by a council of local noblemen and clerics to give up dues imposed on the market there.
1 CONT
1 CONT Before 992, William married Emma, daughter of Rotbold III of Provence. From her he gained titles and lands to Provence. From a prior marriage, he had two sons, Raymond and Hugh, who died young. His eldest son by Emma, Pons, , inhe
1 CONC rited Toulouse and the title of Margrave of Provence. His second son Bertrand became Count of Forcalquier, a Provençal fief. He had two daughters: Rangarda, wife of Peter Raymond of Carcassonne by Emma, and Ildegarda Elisa, wife o
1 CONC f Fulk Bertrand of Provence by Emma. He had an illegitimate daughter that married Otto Raymond of L'Isle-Jourdain.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Notes
1 CONT ^ e.g. Lewis, p 341. 
1 CONT ^ Martin de Framond, "La succession des comtes de Toulouse autour de l'an mil(940--1030): reconsidérations", Annales du Midi 204 (1993), pp 461--488; Thierry Stasser, "Adélaïde d'Anjou. Sa famille, ses mariages, sa descendance", L
1 CONC e Moyen Age 103,1 (1997): 9-52. A surviving charter explicitly naming Adelaide as William's mother has been found. However, see FMG for a different reconstruction. 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sources
1 CONT Lewis, Archibald R. The Development of Southern French and Catalan Society, 718–1050. University of Texas Press: Austin, 1965. 
1 CONT Foundation for Medieval Genealogy: Toulouse. 
1 CONT Thierry Stasser, "Adélaïde d'Anjou. Sa famille, ses mariages, sa descendance", Le Moyen Age 103,1 (1997): 9-52 
1 CONT Ancestral Roots of Certain American Colonists Who Came to America Before 1700 by Frederick Lewis Weis, Lines: 185-2, 185A-5 
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Raymond Counts of Toulouse
1 CONT 978-1037 Succeeded by
1 CONT Pons 
