1 CONC er never bore any other title than count so long as William lived, so the latter seems to have attained a certain supremacy.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 980, he was installed as Count of Arles. His sobriquet comes from his victories against the Saracens by which he liberated Provence from their threat, which had been constant since the establishment of a base at Fraxinet. At th
1 CONC e Battle of Tourtour in 973, with the assistance of the counts of the High Alps and the viscounts of Marseille and Fos, he definitively routed the Saracens, chasing them forever from Provence. He reorganised the region east of the
1 CONC  Rhône, which he conquered from the Saracens and which had been given him as a gift from King Conrad of Burgundy. Also by royal consent, he and his descendants controlled the fisc in Provence. With Isarn, Bishop of Grenoble, he re
1 CONC populated Dauphiné and settled an Italian count named Ugo Blavia near Fréjus in 970 in order to bring that land back to cultivation. For all this, he figures prominently in Ralph Glaber's chronicle with the title of dux and he app
1 CONC ears in a charter of 992 as pater patriae.
1 CONT
1 CONT He donated land to Cluny and retired to become a monk, dying at Avignon, where he was buried in the church of Saint-Croix at Sarrians. He was succeeded as margrave by his brother. His great principality began to diminish soon afte
1 CONC r his death as the castles of his vassals, which he had kept carefully under ducal control, soon became allods of their possessors.
1 CONT
1 CONT Marriages and Children
1 CONT
1 CONT He married 1st Arsenda, daughter of Arnold of Comminges and their son was:
1 CONT William II of Provence
1 CONT
1 CONT He married 2nd (against papal advice) in 984, Adelaide of Anjou, daughter of Fulk II of Anjou and Gerberga of Maine, and their daughter was:
1 CONT Constance of Arles (973 - 1034), married Robert II of France
1 CONT
1 CONT <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_I_of_Provence>
