0 @N6000000002187631261@ NOTE Adelaide of Aquitaine
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Adele or Adelaide of Aquitaine (or Adelaide of Poitiers) (c. 945 or 952–1004) was the daughter of William III of Aquitaine and Adele of Normandy.
1 CONT
1 CONT Her father used her as security for a truce with Hugh Capet, whom she married in 970. In 987, after the death of Louis V, the last Carolingian king of France, Hugh was elected the new king with Adelaide as queen. They were proclai
1 CONC med at Noyon and blessed at Reims. They were the founders of the Capetian dynasty of France.
1 CONT
1 CONT Their children were:
1 CONT
1 CONT Hedwige (or Hadevide, or Avoise) (c. 969–after 1013), wife of Rainier IV of Hainaut 
1 CONT the future king Robert II (972–1031), crowned co-king 987 to order to consolidate the new dynasty 
1 CONT Gisele (974–1002), wife of Hugh I of Ponthieu 
1 CONT Adelaide, (990–1063), wife of Renaud I of Nevers 
1 CONT This biography of a French peer or noble is a stub. You can help Wikipedia by expanding it.
