0 @N6000000000337408211@ NOTE Weis, p. 57:  King of France 987-996.  First of the Capetian Kings of France.  
0 @N6000000002319315641@ NOTE Capet, Hugh (939?-996), king of France 987-996; elected by nobles and
1 CONT prelates to succeed Louis V, last of the Carolingians; founded Capetian
1 CONT Dynasty
1 CONT
1 CONT CAPETIAN DYNASTY. In 987 the descendants of Charlemagne were displaced
1 CONT on the throne of France by Hugh Capet. The dynasty, or sequence of
1 CONT rulers, that he founded encompassed 15 kings, the last of whom was
1 CONT Charles IV, who died in 1328. These kings are known as Capetians "of
1 CONT the direct line." They were followed by the 13 kings of the Valois
1 CONT branch of the family. In 1589 the Capetians of the Bourbon line
1 CONT succeeded them. Descendants of the Bourbon line are still living: King
1 CONT Juan Carlos of Spain, who came to the throne in 1975, is a Bourbon (see
1 CONT Bourbon, House of). Three Capetians were also emperors at
1 CONT Constantinople between 1216 and 1261.
1 CONT    Hugh Capet ruled only a small domain around Paris, since the rest of
1 CONT France was in the hands of powerful local lords. His successors
1 CONT gradually increased the size of the kingdom, largely through
1 CONT inheritance and conquest.
1 CONT    One of the most outstanding Capetians was Philip II Augustus, who
1 CONT ruled from 1180 to 1223. He reclaimed for France much of the territory
1 CONT in the west held by England. Louis IX, also a Capetian, one of the
1 CONT greatest French kings, was canonized by the Roman Catholic church. (See
1 CONT also Louis, Kings of France; Philip, Kings of France.)
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT ---------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT Excerpted from Compton's Interactive Encyclopedia
1 CONT Copyright (c) 1994, 1995 Compton's NewMedia, Inc. All Rights Reserved
