0 @N6000000001956380824@ NOTE
1 CONT --French Hugues Capet king of France from 987 to 996, and the first of a direct line of 14 Capetian kings of that country. The Capetian dynasty derived its name from his nickname (Latin capa, "cape").
1 CONT Hugh was the eldest son of Hugh the Great, duke of the Franks. On his father's death in 956, Hugh Capet inherited vast estates in the regions of Paris and Orléans, extending insome places south of the Loire River. He thus became o
1 CONC ne of the most powerful vassalsin the kingdom and a serious danger to the Carolingian king, Lothair. Hugh married Adelaide, daughter of William III, Duke of Aquitaine, in 970, but his efforts to extend his influence into that sout
1 CONC hwestern kingdom were unsuccessful. From 978 to 986 Hugh was allied with the German emperors Otto II and Otto III and with Adalbero, archbishop of Reims, in political intrigues against the Carolingian king. By 985 Hugh was actuall
1 CONC y the ruler in all but title; and, after the brief reign of Lothair's son, Louis V (986-987), Hugh was elected king of France in May 987 by the assembly of Frankish magnates. Adalbero was able to convince the magnates that the cro
1 CONC wn was elective rather than hereditary and that Charles of Lorraine, the only legitimate Carolingian contender, was unfit to rule. Hugh was crowned at Noyon on July 5, 987. Scholars are generally agreedthat Hugh's election was 
1 CONT not a revolutionary action. His grandfather Robert I, his great-uncle Eudes, and his uncle Rudolf (Raoul) had all earlier been non-Carolingian kings.
1 CONT Hugh's reign was marked by the unavailing efforts of Charles of Lorraine (imprisoned 991) to assert himself and by continual conflict between Eudes I, count of Blois, and Fulk Nerra of Anjou, whom Hugh later supported. In 993 Eude
1 CONC s was aided by the bishopof Laon in an unsuccessful conspiracy to deliver Hugh and his son Robert over to Otto III. That no one was punished for the incident indicated the weakness of the new Capetian dynasty. Hugh's crown was pro
1 CONC bably 
1 CONT preserved by the inability of his enemies to coordinate their activities against him.
1 CONT The Capetian dynasty's subsequent rule for more than 300 years has invested Hugh Capet's reign with a greater significance than his actual achievements merit. Very soon after ascending the throne, Hugh Capet arranged the coronatio
1 CONC n (December 987) of his own son, Robert, who upon Hugh's death succeeded to the throne without difficulty. This practice of crowning the heir during the father's lifetime was continued by the Capetians until the time of Louis VII 
1 CONC and undoubtedly contributed to the dynasty's stability and longevity.
0 @N6000000002240761143@ NOTE Capet, Hugh (939?-996), king of France 987-996; elected by nobles and
1 CONT prelates to succeed Louis V, last of the Carolingians; founded Capetian
1 CONT Dynasty
1 CONT
1 CONT CAPETIAN DYNASTY. In 987 the descendants of Charlemagne were displaced
1 CONT on the throne of France by Hugh Capet. The dynasty, or sequence of
1 CONT rulers, that he founded encompassed 15 kings, the last of whom was
1 CONT Charles IV, who died in 1328. These kings are known as Capetians "of
1 CONT the direct line." They were followed by the 13 kings of the Valois
1 CONT branch of the family. In 1589 the Capetians of the Bourbon line
1 CONT succeeded them. Descendants of the Bourbon line are still living: King
1 CONT Juan Carlos of Spain, who came to the throne in 1975, is a Bourbon (see
1 CONT Bourbon, House of). Three Capetians were also emperors at
1 CONT Constantinople between 1216 and 1261.
1 CONT    Hugh Capet ruled only a small domain around Paris, since the rest of
1 CONT France was in the hands of powerful local lords. His successors
1 CONT gradually increased the size of the kingdom, largely through
1 CONT inheritance and conquest.
1 CONT    One of the most outstanding Capetians was Philip II Augustus, who
1 CONT ruled from 1180 to 1223. He reclaimed for France much of the territory
1 CONT in the west held by England. Louis IX, also a Capetian, one of the
1 CONT greatest French kings, was canonized by the Roman Catholic church. (See
1 CONT also Louis, Kings of France; Philip, Kings of France.)
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT ---------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT Excerpted from Compton's Interactive Encyclopedia
1 CONT Copyright (c) 1994, 1995 Compton's NewMedia, Inc. All Rights Reserved
0 @N6000000002240775398@ NOTE Duke of the Franks title from father, Hugh the Great, 956. Hugh Capet was the
