1 CONT From Ancestral File (TM), data as of 2 January 1996.
1 CONT
1 CONT Deadpg 6, "The Plantagenet Ancestry"  by W.H. Turton, published 1928
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Please tell me about ANY errors, as this data base is in constantly changingAlias:<ALIA> Gunnor of Denmark
1 CONT Danish Settlers
1 CONT MRSNY   Sources: RC 24, 89, 166, 168, 222; Kraentzler 1153, 1156, 1174, 1180, 1194,
1 CONT 1211, 1432, 1443; A. Roots 121E; Coe; Norr; Onslow; Pfafman; Davis.
1 CONT    Roots: (Danish wife) Gunnor, died 1027 or 1031, daughter of forester
1 CONT of Arques.
1 CONT    K. calls her Gonnor de Crepon. Norr: Gunnor (Gunora), (952)-1031.
1 CONT    Onslow in "The Dukes of Normandy and Their Orgin" says:
1 CONT    Richard I heard about a charming woman married to the superintendent of
1 CONT forests at Secheville near Arques. Richard visited Secheville, met the
1 CONT forester's beautiful wife, Sainfrida, and "had the impudence to suggest
1 CONT to her husband that he should be allowed to make her better acquaintance.
1 CONT    "The poor man was much perturbed and went in trouble to his wife. The lady
1 CONT had no mind to become the mistress of the Duke, but her morals were not so
1 CONT rigid as to prevent her from making capital our of the situation for her
1 CONT family. Sainfrida had three sisters, Gunnor, Adelina and Veva (Wevia), and a
1 CONT brother named Herfast. Gunnor was a very pretty girl and by no means
1 CONT particular. Sainfrida told her husband to pretend to fall in with the Duke's
1 CONT proposals and give Richard facilities to pay her a visit. But when Richard
1 CONT arrived, Gunnor took Sainfrida's place. The Duke did not discover the trick
1 CONT until the following day; but so delighted was he with Gunnor that not only did
1 CONT he forgive Sainfrida, but thanked her for having saved him from mortal sin! The
1 CONT result was that Gunnor became his wife by Danish custom and eventually the
1 CONT mother of a numerous family, the eldest of which was Richard II of Normandy."
1 CONT    Gunnor apparently had children by Richard before they were married and before
1 CONT his marriage to Emma. Evidently the Richard-Gunnor union was legitimized later.
1 CONT    RC 166 says Gunnor's father was Herbastus de Crepon, Forester of Arques (in
1 CONT Denmark). Were there two foresters, the father of Gunnor and Sainfrida and also
1 CONT the husband of Sainfrida? Or did the story become tangled over the years?
1 CONT    Coe spells name Gunore.
1 CONT    Davis: Gonnor, daughter of a forester of Sauqueville in Britanny. Second
1 CONT wife of Richard I. She married (2) Eperleng, "farmer" of the mills of Pitres.
1 CONT
1 CONT                               *****
1 CONT
1 CONT SOURCES:
1 CONT   1.  Wingate, John.  _William the Conqueror_, genealogical tables and notes.
1 CONT       Gunnor was the sister of Herfast of Denmark.  She was the mistress, or
1 CONT       wife "by the Danish custom" of Richard I, Duke of Normandy, and married
1 CONT       him after the death of his wife, Emma.  She had both legitimate and
1 CONT       illegitimate children by Richard.
1 CONT   2.  Douglas, David C.  _William the Conqueror_: The Norman Impact Upon
1 CONT       England_.  Berkeley and Los Angeles: University of California Press,
1 CONT       1964; page 145.  He calls Gunnor the sister of Herfast and the aunt of      Osbern, the steward of William the Conqueror's father, Duke Robert.
1 CONT   3.  Stuart, Roderick W.  _Royalty for Commoners_.  2nd Edition. Baltimore,
1 CONT       MD: Genealogical Publishing Company, Inc., 1992; lines 89-1 and 166-33.
1 CONT   4.  Norr, Vernon M.  _Some Early English Pedigrees_, page 60, generation 38,
1 CONT       and page 23a.  States that Richard I had issue by and later md (2) (after
1 CONT       Agnes died) Gunora, b. 952, d. 1031; sister of a forester's wife whom
1 CONT       Richard desired but who tricked him with her younger sister.
1 CONT   5.  Ancestral File (AFN:9HMD-WL).  One line gives her birth date as "abt 935"
1 CONT       and only refers to her as "Concubine 1".  She is also shown with the
1 CONT       following other parents in the Ancestral File (AFN:FLGR-TL):
1 CONT         Herbastus /DE CREPON/ (AFN:FLGR-SF) and Herbastus De C /MRS./
1 CONT
1 CONT 1  NAME Gunnora (Gunnor) /de Crepon/
1 CONT 2  SOUR S033320
1 CONT 3  DATA
1 CONT 4  TEXT Date of Import: Jan 17, 2001
1 CONT 1  BIRT
1 CONT 2  DATE ABT. 942
1 CONT 2  PLAC of, Normandy, France
1 CONT 2  SOUR S033320
1 CONT 3  DATA
1 CONT 4  TEXT Date of Import: Jan 17, 2001
1 CONT 1  DEAT
1 CONT 2DATE 1031
1 CONT 2  PLAC ,France
1 CONT 2  SOUR S033320
1 CONT 3  DATA
1 CONT 4  TEXT Date of Import: Jan 17, 2001
1 CONT
1 CONT Note:
1 CONT Gunnor was Danish, the daughter of the forester of Arques. Although Richard married her, he had been betrothed about 945 to Emma, daughter of Hugh Capet, Count of Paris. He married Emma in 960. After Emma's death about 968, he mar
1 CONC ried Gunnor in a Christian marriage in order to legitimatize their children. "Robert de Torigny and the family of Gunnor, Duchess of Normandy", by Todd A. Farmerie [Modified from an article which appeared in Dec 1996 on soc.geneal
1 CONC ogy.medieval]
1 CONT
1 CONT There have been many requests for information on the various Norman relationships compiled by Robert de Torigny. This is an attempt to summarize and harmonize several recent works on some of the lines: Elisabeth M C van Houts. Rob
1 CONC ert of Torigni as Genealogist. in Studies in Medieval History presented to R. Allen Brown, p.215-33; Kathleen Thompson. The Norman Aristocracy before 1066: the Example of the Montgomerys. in Historical Research 60:251-63; and K S 
1 CONC B Keats-Rohan. Aspects of Torigny's Genealogy Revisited. in Nottingham Medieval Studies 37:21-7.
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert de Torigny, writing after the Norman Conquest, recorded the genealogical traditions which tied many of the Norman nobility to the family of Gunnor, first mistress of Richard I, then Duchess of Normandy. He reported the trad
1 CONC ition that Richard had become infatuated with the wife of one of his foresters, but being the pious wife, she substituted her sister Gunnor, much to everyone's satisfaction. He proceded to name the siblings of Gunnor, and also ind
1 CONC icated the she had numerous nieces, who are left unnamed, but whose marriages and descendants are provided.
1 CONT
1 CONT The genealogical information contained in his account has at various times been praised and condemned, but recent opinion seems to favor the view that, while minor errors abound, the genealogies accurately represent a tradition of
1 CONC  shared descent that may account for the rapid rise of these nobles.
1 CONT
1 CONT The parentage of Gunnor and her siblings is unknown. While some sources call her father Herfastus, this was in fact the name of her brother. She has also been claimed as daughter of the Danish royal family, but there is no evidenc
1 CONC e for this, and the context of her coming to the attention of Richard I and the family's subsequent rise to power militates against her being a royal daughter. Douglas argued (in a 1944 English Historical Review article on the fam
1 CONC ily of William Fitz Osbern), based on the donations of brother Arfast to the monastery of St. Pere, that the root of the family was in the Cotetin region of Normandy, but van Houts has suggested that the Cotetin land was granted t
1 CONC o Arfast, rather than inherited by him. Thus we are left with the more ambiguous statements of Torigny and others that she was a member of a Norman family of Danish origins.
1 CONT
1 CONT The only known brother of Gunnor was Arfast/Herfast, of whom we gain what little insight we have from a trial of heretics conducted by King Robert II of France. Arfast testified that he had pretended to join the sect, all the bett
1 CONC er to denounce them when the time arose. He later donated lands to the monastery of St. Pere, to which he retired. He had at least two sons: Osbern, who was steward to the later Dukes, and was murdered by William de Montgomery whi
1 CONC le defending the young Duke William; and Ranulf, known from charters. Osbern maried a niece of Richard I (the daughter of his half-brother) and by her was the father of the Conquest baron William Fitz Osbern.
1 CONT
1 CONT Gunnor had at least three sisters, of which the oldest appears to have been Senfria (Seinfreda), who was wife of the (unnamed) forester from the area of St. Vaast d'Equiqueville, and it was her charms which are said first to have 
1 CONC attracted the attentions Duke Richard I. She appears to have had at least one daughter, Joscelina, wife of Hugh de Montgomery. (Torigny makes Joscelina daughter of another sister, Wevia, but a contemporary of Torigny, in demonstra
1 CONC ting the genealogical impediment to a marriage of a bastard of Henry I to a Montgomery descendant specifically calls Joscelina's mother Senfria, and the inheritance by the Montgomerys of large holdings suggests that Joscelina was 
1 CONC a significant coheiress to her parents, which does not match Wevia's family where the two sons would be expected to acquire most of the family land.) Hugh de Montgomery and Joscelina had a son Roger, but contrary to Torigny's stat
1 CONC ements, he was not the Conquest baron of that name, but instead his father. By a wife possibly named Emma, Roger had: Hugh; Roger (who married Mabel of Belleme and played a significant role in pre-Conquest Normandy); William (who 
1 CONC murdered cousin Osbern); Robert, and Gilbert.
1 CONT
1 CONT Duvelina, a second sister of Gunnor, married Turulf de Pont Audemer, son of a Norman founder Torf, and uncle of the first of the Harcourts. They had at least one son, Humphrey de Vielles, who in turn was father of Roger de Beaumon
1 CONC t, another Conquest-era baron.
1 CONT
1 CONT Wevia, the only other sister of Gunnor named by Torigny, married Osbern de Bolbec (who is otherwise unknown to history). They had at least two sons: Walter Giffard, ancestor of the English Giffard/Gifford families, and also, throu
1 CONC gh a daughter, of the Clare family; and Godfrey, whose son William de Arques had two daughters and co-heiresses.
1 CONT
1 CONT Torigny indicates that Gunnor had numerous nieces, naming the descendants of several of them, but usually not naming the nieces themselves or their parents. As has already been seen with niece Joscelina, the accounts of these fami
1 CONC lies are more difficult to harmonize with other available sources.
1 CONT
1 CONT One niece is said to have married Nicholas de Bracqueville, and to have had William Martel and Walter de St. Martin. As to Martel, there seems to have been a connection to Bracqueville, since Hawise, daughter of Nicholas married H
1 CONC ugh de Wareham, son of a Grippo. Hugh had a brother Geoffrey Martel, but beyond this no recent analysis provides any insight as to the descent of the later Martels. Walter de St. Martin is even more of a problem, since elsewhere T
1 CONC origny incorrectly makes him brother of William de Warenne, but the ancestry given there is clearly false. Thus it is not clear that Torigny knew the exact connection of Walter, and there is no evidence to help clarify his true or
1 CONC igins.
1 CONT
1 CONT A second niece is said to have married Richard, vicomte of Rouen (who was son of Tesselin). He had a son Lambert of St. Saens, whose son Helias married a bastard daughter of Robert II of Normandy. (If the connection here given is 
1 CONC correct, then these two were within the prohibited degree, which may throw doubt on the relationship, or simply suggest that the relationship did not come to light at the time.) Based on later interactions between Montgomery and W
1 CONC arenne (thought to be related to this branch) it has been speculated that this niece was sister of Joscelina, which is possible but unsupported. 
1 CONT
1 CONT It appears to be through this family that the relationship of two more Norman barons come into play, but not exactly as Torigny presents it. He shows yet another niece marrying Ranulph de Warenne, and by him having William de Ware
1 CONC nne and Roger de Mortimer. This is clearly untrue, because Roger appears to have been a generation older than William. The solution appears to be that Torigny (as he had done with the Montgomerys) compressed two people, a father a
1 CONC nd son of the same name, into one individual. Ranulph de Warenne (I) appears to have married Beatrice, sister of Richard, vicomte of Rouen, and thus sister-in-law of one of Gunnor's nieces (thus it would appear th
1 CONT
1 CONT *****
1 CONT
1 CONT SOURCES:
1 CONT 1.  Wingate, John.  _William the Conqueror_, genealogical tables and notes.
1 CONT Gunnor was the sister of Herfast of Denmark.  She was the mistress, or
1 CONT wife "by the Danish custom" of Richard I, Duke of Normandy, and married
1 CONT him after the death of his wife, Emma.  She had both legitimate and
1 CONT illegitimate children by Richard.
1 CONT 2.  Douglas, David C.  _William the Conqueror_: The Norman Impact Upon
1 CONT England_.  Berkeley and Los Angeles: University of California Press,
1 CONT 1964; page 145.  He calls Gunnor the sister of Herfast and the aunt of Osbern, the steward of William the Conqueror's father, Duke Robert.
1 CONT 3.  Stuart, Roderick W.  _Royaltyfor Commoners_.  2nd Edition. Baltimore,
1 CONT MD: Genealogical Publishing Company,Inc., 1992; lines 89-1 and 166-33.
1 CONT 4.  Norr, Vernon M.  _Some Early English Pedigrees_, page 60, generation 38,
1 CONT and page 23a.  States that Richard I had issue by and later md (2) (after
1 CONT Agnes died) Gunora, b. 952, d. 1031; sister of a forester's wife whom
1 CONT Richard desired but who tricked him with her younger sister.
1 CONT 5.  Ancestral File (AFN:9HMD-WL).  One line gives her birth date as "abt935"
1 CONT and only refers to her as "Concubine 1".  She is also shown with the
1 CONT following other parents in the Ancestral File (AFN:FLGR-TL):
1 CONT Herbastus /DE CREPON/ (AFN:FLGR-SF) and Herbastus De C /MRS./
