0 @N6000000001956312589@ NOTE
1 CONT --Louis IV of France took the boy-duke into his protective custody, apparently intent upon reuniting Normandy to the crown's domains; but in 945 Louis was captured by the Normans, and Richard was returned to his people. Richard wi
1 CONC thstood further Carolingian attempts to subdue his duchy and, in 987, was instrumental in securing the French crown for his brother-in-law, the Robertian Hugh Capet.
0 @N6000000002244417313@ NOTE In the history of the Dukes of Normandy, there is no information between 931
1 CONT and 942 as to the Dukes of Normandy. The 1st duke was Rollo, a direct
1 CONT ancestor of the rest, 860?-931?, the leader of a menacing band of Viking
1 CONT raiders. The Vikings set up their bases at the mouths of rivers. In 911 a
1 CONT large company of Vikings, under Rollo, accepted from King Charles the Simple
1 CONT  of France, the simple territory in the lower Seine Valley that became known
1 CONT as Normandy. Rollo, therefore, became the 1st Duke of Normandy. See Rollo.
0 @N6000000002248112927@ NOTE Note for: Richard Sans Peur, 28 AUG 933 - 20 NOV 996 
1 CONT 3 Duke of Normandy 
1 CONT Was only 10 years old when he succeeded, and the King of France, taking advantage of his age, thought it a favorable opportunity for reuniting Normandy to the dominion of the French crown. With this view, he went to Rouen soon aft
1 CONC er the assassination of Duke William, and found means to carry off the young duke under pretence of protecting him from his enemies, and superintending his education. However, by the dexterity of Osmond, his governor, Richard cont
1 CONC rived, in a short time, to make his escape. The French king pursued him into Normandy and was defeated and taken prisoner by Airgold, King of Denmark, who the Normans had invited to the assistance of their young duke; nor was he s
1 CONC et at liberty till he signed a solemn engagement not to molest Richard in the enjoyment of his duchy and was moreover obliged to deliver up his two sons, and some of his nobles, as hostages to his future conduct. This treaty was c
1 CONC heifly managed through the mediation of Hugh the Great, Duke of Paris, who was so much pleased with the young Duke of Nomandy, that he betrothed to him his daughter Emma, then only eigtht years of age, whom he afterwards married. 
1 CONC Some years after, Hugh the Great, being on his bed appointed Richard, guardian of his son Hugh Capte, a trust which he faithfully executed and moreover greatly contributed by his exertions to the elevation of Hugh Capet to the thr
1 CONC one of France. 
1 CONT Richard displayed great personal courage in battle and was called "Sans Peur" by his contemporaries. He had no children by the Princess Emma. After her death he married Gunnor, a Norman lady, by howme he had Richard, William, Coun
1 CONC t of Du, ancestor to the Clifford family and several other children. 
1 CONT He died in great sentiments of piety at the abbey of Fecamp, in Normandy in 996 and was buried in the abbey church.
1 CONT
1 CONT Source:  web site of John Kelly - http://www.spectrumdata.com/kelly/
0 @N6000000002909004856@ NOTE Richard I of Normandy (born 28 August 933, in Fécamp Normandy, France died November 20, 996, in Fécamp) was the Duke of Normandy from 942 to 996; he is considered the first to actually have held that title. He was called Richard t
1 CONC he Fearless (French, Sans Peur).
1 CONT
1 CONT Birth
1 CONT He was born to William I of Normandy, ruler of Normandy, and his wife, Sprota. He was still a boy when his father died in 942. His mother was a Breton concubine captured in war and bound to William by a Danish marriage. After Will
1 CONC iam died, Sprota became the wife of Esperleng, a wealthy miller; Rodulf of Ivry was their son and Richard's half-brother.[citation needed]
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Life
1 CONT Richard was still a boy when his father died, and so he was powerless to stop Louis IV of France when he seized Normandy. Louis kept him in confinement in his youth at Lâon, but he escaped with the assistance of Osmond de Centvill
1 CONC e, Bernard de Senlis (who had been a companion of Rollo of Normandy), Ivo de Bellèsme, and Bernard the Dane (ancestor of families of Harcourt and Beaumont). In 968, Richard agreed to "commend" himself to Hugh, Count of Paris. He t
1 CONC hen allied himself with the Norman and Viking leaders, drove Louis out of Rouen, and took back Normandy by 947. He later quarrelled with Ethelred II of England regarding Viking invasions of England because Normandy had been buying
1 CONC  up much of the stolen booty.
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard was bilingual, having been well educated at Bayeux. He was more partial to his Danish subjects than to the French. During his reign, Normandy became completely Gallicized and Christianized. He introduced the feudal system 
1 CONC and Normandy became one of the most thoroughly feudalized states on the continent. He carried out a major reorganization of the Norman military system, based on heavy cavalry. He also became guardian of the young Hugh, Count of Pa
1 CONC ris, on the elder Hugh's death in 956.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Marriages
1 CONT He married 1st (960) Emma of France, daughter of Hugh "The Great" of France, and Hedwiga de Sachsen. They were betrothed when both were very young. She died 19 Mar 968, with no issue.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to Robert of Torigni, not long after Emma's death, Duke Richard went out hunting and stopped at the house of a local forester. He became enamoured of the forester's wife, Seinfreda, but she being a virtuous woman, sugges
1 CONC ted he court her unmarried sister, Gunnor, instead. Gunnor became his mistress, and her family rose to prominence. Her brother, Herefast de Crepon, may have been involved in a controversial heresy trial. Gunnor was, like Richard, 
1 CONC of Norse descent, being a Dane by blood. Richard finally married her to legitimize their children:
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard II "the Good", Duke of Normandy (966) 
1 CONT Robert, Archbishop of Rouen, Count of Evreux, died 1037. 
1 CONT Geoffrey, Count of Eu, b. abt 962 died abt 1015. (Parentage [mother] not certain) 
1 CONT Mauger, Earl of Corbeil, died after 1033; his alleged grandson (or perhaps great-grandson) was Robert Fitzhamon, an important Anglo-Norman baro
1 CONT Robert Danus, died between 985 and 989 
1 CONT Emma of Normandy (c.985-1052) wife of two kings of England. 
1 CONT Maud of Normandy, wife of Odo II of Blois, Count of Blois, Champagne and Chartres 
1 CONT Hawise of Normandy (b. ca. 978), d. 21 February 1034. m. Geoffrey I, Duke of Brittany 
1 CONT Beatrice of Normandy, Abbess of Montvilliers d.1034 m. Ebles of Turenne (d.1030 (divorced) 
1 CONT Papia m. Gilbert de St Valery. 
1 CONT Fressenda (ca. 995-ca. 1057), m. Tancred of Hauteville. 
1 CONT Muriella m. Tancred of Hauteville. 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Mistresses
1 CONT Richard was known to have had several other mistresses and produced children with many of them. Known children are:
1 CONT
1 CONT Geoffrey, Count of Brionne, (b. ca. 970) 
1 CONT William, Count of Eu (ca. 972-26 January 1057/58) m. Leseline de Turqueville (d. 26 January 1057/58). 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Death
1 CONT He died in Fecamp, France on November 20, 996 of natural causes.
