0 @N6000000000232599184@ NOTE ==========
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard I, Duke of Normandy
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT   (Redirected from Richard I of Normandy)
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard I of Normandy (born 28 August 933, in Fecamp Normandy, France died November 20, 996, in Fecamp) was the Duke of Normandy from 942 to 996; he is considered the first to actually have held that title. He was called Richard t
1 CONC he Fearless (French, Sans Peur).
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Birth
1 CONT
1 CONT He was born to William I of Normandy, ruler of Normandy, and his wife, Sprota. He was still a boy when his father died in 942. His mother was a Breton concubine captured in war and bound to William by a Danish marriage. After Will
1 CONC iam died, Sprota became the wife of Esperleng, a wealthy miller; Rodulf of Ivry was their son and Richard's half-brother.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Life
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard was still a boy when his father died, and so he was powerless to stop Louis IV of France when he seized Normandy. Louis kept him in confinement in his youth at Lâon, but he escaped with the assistance of Osmund de Centevil
1 CONC le, Bernard de Senlis (who had been a companion of Rollo of Normandy), Ivo de Bellèsme, and Bernard the Dane (ancestor of families of Harcourt and Beaumont). In 968, Richard agreed to "commend" himself to Hugh, Count of Paris. He 
1 CONC then allied himself with the Norman and Viking leaders, drove Louis out of Rouen, and took back Normandy by 947. He later quarrelled with Ethelred II of England regarding Viking invasions of England because Normandy had been buyin
1 CONC g up much of the stolen booty.
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard was bilingual, having been well educated at Bayeux. He was more partial to his Danish subjects than to the French. During his reign, Normandy became completely Gallicized and Christianized. He introduced the feudal system 
1 CONC and Normandy became one of the most thoroughly feudalized states on the continent. He carried out a major reorganization of the Norman military system, based on heavy cavalry. He also became guardian of the young Hugh, Count of Pa
1 CONC ris, on the elder Hugh's death in 956.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Marriages
1 CONT
1 CONT He married 1st (960) Emma of Paris, daughter of Hugh the Great, Duke of the Franks and Count of Paris. They were betrothed when both were very young. She died after 966, with no issue.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to Robert of Torigni, not long after Emma's death, Duke Richard went out hunting and stopped at the house of a local forester. He became enamoured of the forester's wife, Seinfreda, but she being a virtuous woman, sugges
1 CONC ted he court her unmarried sister, Gunnor, instead. Gunnor became his mistress, and her family rose to prominence. Her brother, Herefast de Crepon, may have been involved in a controversial heresy trial. Gunnor was, like Richard, 
1 CONC of Norse descent, being a Dane by blood. Richard finally married her to legitimize their children:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Richard II "the Good", Duke of Normandy (966)
1 CONT     * Robert, Archbishop of Rouen, Count of Evreux, died 1037.
1 CONT     * Mauger, Earl of Corbeil, died after 1033; his alleged grandson (or perhaps great-grandson) was Robert Fitzhamon, an important Anglo-Norman baron.
1 CONT     * Robert Danus, died between 985 and 989
1 CONT     * Emma of Normandy (c.985-1052) wife of two kings of England.
1 CONT     * Maud of Normandy, wife of Odo II of Blois, Count of Blois, Champagne and Chartres
1 CONT     * Hawise of Normandy (b. ca. 978), d. 21 February 1034. m. Geoffrey I, Duke of Brittany
1 CONT     * Beatrice of Normandy, Abbess of Montvilliers d.1034 m. Ebles of Turenne (d.1030 (divorced)
1 CONT     * Papia m. Gilbert de St Valery
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Mistresses
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard was known to have had several other mistresses and produced children with many of them. Known children are:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Geoffrey, Count of Brionne, (b. ca. 970)
1 CONT
1 CONT     * William d'Eu, Count d'Eu, (b. ca. 985).
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Death
1 CONT
1 CONT He died in Fecamp, France on November 20, 996 of natural causes.
1 CONT
1 CONT ==========
0 @N6000000000945053998@ NOTE Richard ?den Fryktløse? var hertug av Normandie 942 - 996. 
1 CONT Han ble også herre over Bretagne i 945, men måtte kjempe for sitt land mot Ludvig IV
1 CONT av Frankrike.
1 CONT Richard var gift 1. gang med Emma, søster til Hugo Capet, men de hadde ingen barn.
