0 @N6000000001353956317@ NOTE . He played a significant part in the events of his time. Before 1035he was a witness to a charter of the youthful Duke William. In his early career he made a mark as a sea-going warrior, and in 1036 he was one of the commanders o
1 CONC f the Norman fleet that took the Anglo-Saxon Prince Edward (later the Confessor) to England, in that young man's hope that with the help of his mother, Queen Emma, he could gain the English throne upon the death of Canute. But he 
1 CONC was not welcome there, and returned to Normandy to bide his time.
1 CONT Hugh II was one of the three named leaders of the Norman knights in the battle of Mortemer in 1054. When he died is not certain, but it mayhave been during a battle against the French King Henri I. He lived to a considerable age, 
1 CONC being known in his later days as "old Hugh". Weknow nothing of his wifeBefore 1035 he was a witness to a charter of the youthful Duke William. In his early career he made a mark as a sea-going warrior, and in 1036 he was one of th
