0 @N6000000001744961129@ NOTE Hugh de Gournay was a noble from East Normandy who is listed as one of the commanders who accompanied William the Conqueror at the Battle of Hastings, and helped to finance the venture.  An Ascelin de Gournay is also listed. Hugh 
1 CONC is also named as a baron in the Magna Carta. He seems to have been closely associated with William de Warenne.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sources on this line: United Ancestries Linked Pedigrees CD-Rom (1992): Cites as source "English Baronies" by I.J. Sanders. See <Http://www.canlink.com/hon/norman.htm> for list of those at the Battle of Hastings, and <http://www.c
1 CONC anlink.com/hon/baron1,htm> for the major feudal barons of the Magna Carta.
1 CONT
1 CONT Historian David Douglas recounts that after the Battle of Arques, in February 1054, "William [of Normandy's] force was at any rate large enough for it to be divided into two contingents opertating to the west and east of the Seine
1 CONC . The Duke himself with men from middle Normandy faced the invaders who were advancing under the French king through the Evrecin. On the other side of the river, Robert (count of Eu) with Hugh of Gournay, Walter Giffard, Roger of 
1 CONC Mortemer, and the young William of Warenne, came out from their own lands to withstand the eastern incursion [of the French force] under Count Odo and Count Rainald... [which] seems to have been unprepared for this levy from easte
1 CONC rn Normandy. Having entered the duchy by way of Neufchatel-en-Bray, [the French force] advanced to the neighborhood of Mortemer, and there gave itself up to unrestrained rape and pillage. Widely scattered and demoralized, it thus 
1 CONC offered itself as an easy target....The slaughter was considerable." The battle was of decisive importance...the Norman victory was complete, and the King of France withdrew. "The battle of Mortemer reflected a major crisis in Nor
1 CONC man history, and never again was Duke William to be faced by so formidable a threat to ...his power."  David C. Douglas, "William the Conqueror" (Berkeley: Univ of Calif Press, 1964,  p. 68)
0 @N6000000001744991059@ NOTE Historian David Douglas recounts that after the Battle of Arques, in February 1054, "William [of Normandy's] force was at any rate large enough for it to be divided into two contingents opertating to the west and east of the Seine
1 CONC . The Duke himself with men from middle Normandy faced the invaders who were advancing under the French king through the Evrecin. On the other side of the river, Robert (count of Eu) with Hugh of Gournay, Walter Giffard [of Longue
1 CONC ville], Roger of Mortemer, and the young William of Warenne, came out from their own lands to withstand the eastern incursion [of the French force] under Count Odo and Count Rainald... [which] seems to have been unprepared for thi
1 CONC s levy from eastern Normandy. Having entered the duchy by way of Neufchatel-en-Bray, [the French force] advanced to the neighborhood of Mortemer, and there gave itself up to unrestrained rape and pillage. Widely scattered and demo
1 CONC ralized, it thus offered itself as an easy target....The slaughter was considerable." The battle was of decisive importance...the Norman victory was complete, and the King of France withdrew. "The battle of Mortemer reflected a ma
1 CONC jor crisis in Norman history, and never again was Duke William to be faced by so formidable a threat to ...his power." David C. Douglas, "William the Conqueror" (Berkeley: Univ of Calif Press, 1964,  p. 68)
0 @N6000000001956339276@ NOTE
1 CONT Hugh II, Seigneur de Gournay, most probably the son of the former Hugh, is the personage I have already mentioned as believed to be "the old Hue" of Wace's Chronicle, and the Hugo Miles who anthorised the gift of the land of Calve
1 CONC lville to the Abbey of Montvilliers by William while Count of the Hiemois.
1 CONT Mr. Daniel Gurney, in the first volume of his sumptuous work, "The Record of the House of Gournay," remarks in his notice of this charter that Calvelville, it seems likely, is the modern Conteville, so called from this donation by
1 CONC  William the Count. If there were any facts to be adduced in support of this otherwise mere fancy, they would be very important, inasmuch as they would enlighten us respecting the parentage and position of Herluin de Conteville, w
1 CONC hose name has been preserved to us from the accident of his being "le mari de sa femme." Beatrice, Abbess of Montvilliers, was aunt to Robert Duke of Normandy, William's father, and William Malet, as we have seen, had power to giv
1 CONC e Conteville to the Abbey of Bec.
1 CONT This second Hugh was one of the Norman leaders of the fleet of forty ships which accompanied Edward the Saxon Prince, son of King Ethelred, to England in 1035, when, on the death of Knute, he made an attempt to recover the kingdom
1 CONC . The expedition sailed from Barfleur, and landed at Southampton, but was ill received by the English, who had espoused the cause of Harold Harefoot. Edward, seeing the disposition of the country, returned with his fleet to Barfle
1 CONC ur, more fortunate than his brother Alfred, who, at the same time making a descent on Dover, was taken prisoner by Earl Godwin, confined in the Monastery of Ely, had his eyes put out, and died shortly afterwards.As forHugh de Gour
1 CONC nay, in whom at this moment we are more specially interested, the last we hear of him is that he became a monk in Normandy, where he died some time after 1085; but nothing is positively known how long after, or what was the cause 
1 CONC of his death, and the assertion that he "was hurt" at Cardiff, "and died after in Normandy," is quite reconcilable with the fact, if it be one, that he became a monk there, as it was a common practice in those days for a warrior t
1 CONC o assume the monastic habit even in articulo mortis; and the same observation applies to Roger de Montgomeri, who died a monk at Shrewsbury in 1094.Hugh DE GOURNAY II was born about 1010 in Normandy, France. Date is estimated. He 
1 CONC served in the military in 1035 in England. This second Hugh was one of the Norman leaders of the fleet of forty ships which accompanied Edward the Saxon Prince, son of King Ethelred, to England In 1035, when, on the death of Knute
1 CONC , he made an attempt to recover the kingdom. The expedition sailed from Barfleur, and landed at Southampton, but was ill received by the English, who had espoused the cause of Harold Harefoot. Edward, seeing the disposition of the
1 CONC  country, returned with his fleet to Barfleur, more fortunate than his brother Alfred, who, at the same time making a descent on Dover, was taken prisoner by Earl Godwin, confined in the Monastery of Ely, had his eyes put out, and
1 CONC  died shortly afterwards He served in the military in 1054 in Mortemer, France. "...The commanders east of the Seine, Count Robert of Eu, Hugh of Gournay, William Crispin, and Walter Giffard, found their opportunity when the Frenc
1 CONC h had entered the unfortified town of Mortemer and had given themselves up to revelry. Fire and 
1 CONT sword did the work. The whole French army was slain, scattered, or taken prisoners..." He served in the military in 1066 in Hastings, Sussex, England. Battle of Hastings He died about 1074 in Normandy, France. He is said by the No
1 CONC rman chroniclers to have been mortally wounded in a battle at Cardiff in 1074, and carried to Normandy, where he died. There is, however, considerable doubt about their account of this battle, as it is clear that several persons s
1 CONC aid to have been engaged or slain in it were either deceased long prior to it, or could not possibly have been present
