0 @N6000000002187596549@ NOTE Boso of Tuscany
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Boso (885–936) was the count of Arles (895–911 and 926–931, as Boso II) and Avignon (911–931), and margrave of Tuscany (931–936). He was the younger son of Theobald of Arles and Bertha, illegitimate daughter of Lothair II of Lotha
1 CONC ringia. His elder brother was Hugh, king of Italy.
1 CONT
1 CONT Boso supported his brother's Italian designs. Hugh responded by appointing him margrave of Tuscany when he deposed Lambert in 931. Boso was also very active in Lorraine, opposing Henry I of Germany. He was finally deposed and arre
1 CONC sted in 936 to be replaced by Hugh's son Humbert.
1 CONT
1 CONT He married Willa, daughter of Rudolph I of Burgundy, and had two daughters:
1 CONT
1 CONT Bertha or Gersenda, married firstly Boso of Provence and secondly Raymond II of Rouergue 
1 CONT Willa, married Berengar II of Italy 
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Lambert Margrave of Tuscany
1 CONT 931 – 936 Succeeded by
1 CONT Boso 
0 @N6000000002187570481@ NOTE Boso of Provence
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Boso was a Frankish nobleman from the Bosonid-family, who was related to the Carolingian dynasty,[1] who rose to become King of Provence (879 – 887).
1 CONT
1 CONT Boso was the son of Bivin of Gorze, a count in Lotharingia. His aunt Theutberga was the wife of king Lothair II of Lotharingia. Boso was also a nephew of the Italian count Boso, for whom he was named, and of Hucbert, lay abbot of 
1 CONC Saint Maurice d’Agaune, to which he succeeded as lay abbot in 869.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Service of Charles the Bald 
1 CONT 2 Out of favour 
1 CONT 3 Independent rule 
1 CONT 4 Notes 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Service of Charles the Bald
1 CONT In 870, Charles the Bald, King of West Francia, married Boso's sister Richilde. This marriage paved the way for Boso's career in the service of his royal brother-in-law. In the same year, Boso was appointed Count of Lyon and Vienn
1 CONC e, replacing Gerard of Roussillon.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 872, Charles appointed him chamberlain and magister ostiariorum (master of porters) to his heir Louis the Stammerer. Boso likewise received investiture as Count of Bourges. Louis was reigning as a subordinate king of Aquitaine,
1 CONC  but because of his youth, it was Boso who looked after the administration of that realm.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the autumn of 875, Boso accompanied Charles on his first Italian campaign and at the diet of Pavia in February 876 he was appointed arch-minister and missus dominicus for Italy and elevated to the rank of duke. He was probably 
1 CONC also charged with the administration of Provence. He acted as a viceroy and increased his prestige even more by marrying Ermengard, the only daughter of the Emperor Louis II.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Out of favour
1 CONT However, he disapproved of Charles' second Italian campaign in 877 and conspired with other like-minded nobles against his king. After Charles's death in October, these nobles forced Charles's son to confirm their rights and privi
1 CONC leges.
1 CONT
1 CONT Boso also formed close relations to the Papacy and accompanied Pope John VIII in September 878 to Troyes, where the Pope asked King Louis for his support in Italy. The Pope adopted Boso as his son and probably offered to crown Lou
1 CONC is emperor. It is said that he wanted to crown Boso emperor.[2]
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Independent rule
1 CONT In April 879, Louis died, leaving behind two adult sons, Louis and Carloman. Boso joined with other western Frankish nobles and advocated making Louis III of France the sole heir of the western kingdom, but eventually both brother
1 CONC s were elected kings. Boso, however, renounced allegiance to both brothers and in July claimed independence by using the style Dei gratia id quod sum: by the Grace of God, that is what I am. He also claimed that his imperial fathe
1 CONC r-in-law had named him as his heir. On 15 October 879, the bishops and nobles of the region around the rivers Rhône and Saône assembled in the Synod of Mantaille elected Boso king as successor to Louis the Stammerer, the first non
1 CONC -Carolingian king in Western Europe in more than a century.[3]. This event marks the first occurrence of a "free election" among the Franks, without regard to royal descent, inspired by a canonical principle (but not constant prac
1 CONC tice) of ecclesiastical elections.
1 CONT
1 CONT Boso's realm, usually called the Kingdom of Provence, comprised the ecclesiastical provinces of the archbishops of Arles, Aix, Vienne, Lyon (without Langres), and probably Besançon, as well as the dioceses of Tarentaise, Uzès, and
1 CONC  Viviers.
1 CONT
1 CONT After Louis and Carloman had divided their father's realm at Amiens in March 880, the two brothers joined to march against Boso. They took Mâcon and the northern parts of Boso's realm. Then uniting their forces with those of Charl
1 CONC es the Fat, they unsuccessfully besieged Vienne from August to November.
1 CONT
1 CONT In August 882, Boso was again besieged at Vienne by his relative, Richard, Count of Autun, who took the city in September. After this, Boso could not regain most of his realm and was restricted to the vicinity of Vienne.
1 CONT
1 CONT He died in 887 and was succeeded by his son Louis the Blind.
1 CONT
1 CONT Boso was the progenitor of three successful lineages, called the "Bosonids" by modern historians. Through his marriage to Ermengard he also had two daughters: Ermengard (c.877-917), who married Manasses, Count of Chalon, and Ethel
1 CONC berga, who married first Carloman and then William the Pious, Count of Auvergne.[4]
1 CONT
1 CONT It is possible (though not certain) that the famous Guilla of Provence, queen of Upper Burgundy, was his daughter, then presumably by an earlier wife than Ermengarde.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Notes
1 CONT ^ His mother's father, Boso, provided a daughter, Tetburgis/Teutberga, Boso's aunt, to be wife of Lothair II. (Constance B. Bouchard, "The Bosonids or Rising to Power in the Late Carolingian Age" French Historical Studies 15.3 (Sp
1 CONC ring 1988, pp. 407-431) genealogical table, p.409.. 
1 CONT ^ Caravale, Mario (ed). Dizionario Biografico degli Italiani: LXI Guglielmo Gonzaga – Jacobini. Rome, 2003. 
1 CONT ^ Constance B. Bouchard, "The Bosonids or Rising to Power in the Late Carolingian Age" French Historical Studies 15.3 (Spring 1988), pp. 407-431. 
1 CONT ^ Riché, Les Carolingiens: Une famille qui fit l'Europe, genealogical table XII (Bosonides). 
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Louis the Stammerer King of Lower Burgundy
1 CONT 879–887 Succeeded by
1 CONT Louis the Blind 
