0 @N6000000003949647391@ NOTE Boson II, King de Provenc Andre Roux: Scrolls, 215.) (Stuart, Royalty for Commoners, Page 241, Line 343-39.) (Rosamond, Frankish kingdom under Carolingians, Page 259). AKA: Boson II, Comte de Vienne; Boson II became Count of Vienn
1 CONC e in 870 Boson II became Count of Vienne in 870. AKA: Boson II, Count de Bourges. AKA: Boson II, King de Bourgogne; Boson II became King of Lower Burgundy in 879 Boson II became King of Lower Burgundy in 879. Born: before 854, son
1 CONC  of Beuve=Bouin, Comte de Bourgogne and Richilde d'Arles, Boson II is presumed to have been at least 10 years of age when he became Count.   Note -between 869 and 882: In 869, Boson II's sister Richilde married Charles the Bald. I
1 CONC n 871, he was made Count of Vienne, and became the Count of Bourges a year later. After arranging an exchange with Count Theudebert of Vermandois, Boson also acquired the Countship of Autun, so that his territory included most of 
1 CONC Burgundy and Provence including the regions of the Lyonnais and Viennois.
1 CONT When Charles the Bald was crowned emperor in 875, he made Boson his dux et missus Italiae sacrique palatii archiminister. In other words, Boson ruled Italy for Charles, and once there took the opportunity to
1 CONT marry Ermengarde, daughter of Emperor Louis II. He was the King of Provence from 879 to 887, being elected at Montaille (near Vienne) on 15-Oct-879 the King of Burgundy. As soon as he was crowned, he
1 CONT consolidated his position, appointed the powerful bishop of Autun, Adalgar as his archchancellor, and made Geilo, Abbot of Tournus, the bishop of Langres. There was also some collusion between Boson and his Lotharingian countrymen
1 CONC  who had rallied in support of Hugh, Lothar II's bastard, in his attempt to claim the right to rule Lotharingia. Boson's elevation and Hugh's pretentions had the effect of uniting all the Carolingians - Louis theYounger (Le Jeune)
1 CONC , Charles the Fat (Le Gros), Louis III andCarloman - and their supporters against him. Hugh was dealt with quickly, but Boson presented a more serious obstacle to Charles Le Gros as well as to Carloman and Hugh the Abbot. Charles 
1 CONC seized the Italian lands of Boso's mother-in-law Engelberge, kidnapped Engelberge herself, appropriated the temporal of the archbishopric of Besancon and occupied Lausanne. By 881, Boson had lost the Jura and transjurane
1 CONT region to Charles Le Gros. The county of Autun was conferred on Richard, Boson's brother, who supported Carloman from then on. In 882, Vienne was attacked by the Frankish army under Richard who captured Boson's wife and his daught
1 CONC er.  Abandoned by the Pope, his brother and his chancellor who defected to Charles Le Gros, and forsaken by the bishops and counts, Boson died in
1 CONT historical obscurity. 
