0 @N6000000000437040241@ NOTE Name Prefix:<NPFX> Queen Name Suffix:<NSFX> Of Franks Of Saxony, Abbess Of Notre Dame
0 @N6000000002187580118@ NOTE Gerberga of Saxony
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Gerberga of Saxony (c. 913–5 May 984) was a daughter of Henry the Fowler, king of Germany, and Matilda of Ringelheim.
1 CONT
1 CONT She married first Gilbert, Duke of Lorraine. They had four children:
1 CONT
1 CONT Gerberge of Lorraine b. about 925. Married Adalbert I of Vermandois. 
1 CONT Alberade of Lorraine b. about 929. Married Renaud (originally as Ragenold), a Viking chieftain who became the Count of Roucy [1]. 
1 CONT Henri, Duke of Lorraine b. about 932 
1 CONT Wiltrude, Duchess of Bavaria b. about 937. Married Berthold, Duke of Bavaria. 
1 CONT She married secondly Louis IV of France in 939. They were parents to eight children:
1 CONT
1 CONT Lothair of France (941-986) 
1 CONT Mathilde b. about 943; married Conrad of Burgundy 
1 CONT Hildegarde b. about 944 
1 CONT Carloman b. about 945 
1 CONT Louis b. about 948 
1 CONT Charles, Duke of Lower Lorraine (953-993) 
1 CONT Alberade b. before 953 
1 CONT Henri b. about 953 
1 CONT Contemporary sources describe her as a highly educated, intelligent and forceful player in the political game of the time.
1 CONT
1 CONT Louis IV died on 10 September 954. As a widow, Gerberga became a nun and served as the abbess of Notre Dame in Laon. She died in Reims, Champagne.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sources
1 CONT Bouchard, Constance Brittain. (2001) Those of My Blood: Constructing Noble Families in Medieval Francia. University of Pennsylvania Press. 
1 CONT " Women in power 750-1000" from Guide2womenleaders.com, last accessed January 13, 2007 
0 @N6000000000337406029@ NOTE Source #1:  Elizabeth Hallam, ed, "The Plantagenet Chronicles" (New York:  Weidenfeld & Nicolson, 1986), pp. 19-24.
1 CONT
1 CONT Count of Anjou (941-960)  Fulk the Good had three sons.  Geoffrey, the eldest, became Count of Anjou, while the second, Guy, became bishop of Le Puy.  Drogo, the youngest, and Fulk's favourite - he had been conceived when Fulk was
1 CONC  past middle age - was educated in literature and the liberal arts and through the kindness 0f King Hugh Capet of France, he succeeded his brother as bishop of Le Puy.
1 CONT Under the rule of Fulk the Good, the region enjoyed a time of tranquil prosperity.
1 CONT
1 CONT Source #2:  Frederick Lewis Weis, "Ancestral Roots of Certain American Colonists Who Came to America Before 1700" - Seventh Edition, with additions and corrections by Walter Lee Sheppard, Jr., assisted by David Faris (Baltimore, M
1 CONC D:  Genealogical Publishing Co, 1995), p. 106
0 @N6000000000437066435@ NOTE Name Prefix:<NPFX> Count Name Suffix:<NSFX> Ii, Of Anjou "The Good"
0 @N6000000001353883397@ NOTE Count of Anjou, Mâcon and Neve
0 @N6000000001354436392@ NOTE Count of Anjou, Mâcon and Neve
0 @N6000000000945065890@ NOTE Fulk ?den Gode? var den annen greve av Anjou 941 - 958. 
1 CONT Han var mild, religiøs og lite krigersk. Fulk arbeidet for å forbedre jordbruket i Anjou som
1 CONT hadde blitt ødelagt av krig.
1 CONT I et dokument av 942 bekrefter han en gave til klosteret i St. Julien de Tours.
1 CONT 26.12.943 var han i Paris sammen med sin bror, Gui, som var biskop av Soissons
1 CONT fra 937. I 958 var han tilstede på et møte i Verron angående grensene mellom Anjou og
1 CONT Tourenne.
