0 @N6000000003949647977@ NOTE The following was given in a post-em by Curt Hofemann, curt_hofemann AT yahoo.com:
1 CONT ID: I00387 Foulques II "Le Bon" Comte d' Anjou
1 CONT (son) Foulques Seigneur de Briole b: ABT 941 in Anjou/Pays-de-la-Loire, France
1 CONT Count of Anjou [Ref: Weis AR7 118:20, Weis MC5 53:21]
1 CONT 941-958: Count of Anjou [Ref: Tapsell p203]
1 CONT 940-962 (or 942-960): 3rd Count of Anjou, [Ref: McBride: Vol I File 9: The Paternal Ancestry of Homer Beers James]
1 CONT ..and under his successor, Fulk II the Good, the destruction caused by the preceding wars was repaired. [Ref: Ency Britannica]
1 CONT According to legend, he was learned and saintly, dressed as a cleric and said of Louis IV, King of France, who had mocked his piety, 'an illiterate king is a crowned ass'. [Ref: Leo van de Pas’ Genealogics citing: Paget] Note: "a 
1 CONC man of wit, but perhaps lacking in tact - Louis IV was not the fool his father, Charles III "the Simple", has been profiled to have been" Curt
1 CONT Alas, I no longer have access to a terrific source which may/may not have more to say about Foulques II: "Fragmentum historieae Andegavensis" (Fragments of a History of the Counts of Anjou) From 'Chroniques des comtes d'Anjou et d
1 CONC es seigneurs d'Amboise' by Count Fulk le Rechin 1096 (Louis Kalpen and Rene Poupardin), 1913, Paris. Should you find access to the UCLA library which probably has it, you will find this source that may have more to say about Foulq
1 CONC ues II. If memory serves me right Fulk "le Rechin" wrote many things modern scholars dispute, but he was at least contemporary, though obviously, he may have embellished a thing or two about his ancestry.
