0 @N6000000002187631272@ NOTE William III of Aquitaine
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT William III of Aquitaine (915 – April 3, 963), nicknamed Tête d'Étoupe (Towhead) was William II of Poitou Count of Poitiers and Duke of Aquitaine from 935 to his death.
1 CONT
1 CONT William was son of Ebalus of Aquitaine and Emiliene. He married Gerloc of Normandy (renamed Adele), daughter of Rollo of Normandy. They had at least two children: Adelaide, who married King Hugh I of France (Hugh Capet) and Willia
1 CONC m, the heir.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Sources
1 CONT Ademar of Chabannes 
1 CONT Dudo of Saint-Quentin 
1 CONT William of Jumièges 
1 CONT See also  
1 CONT Dukes of Aquitaine family tree 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Ebalus Duke of Aquitaine Succeeded by:
1 CONT William IV 
1 CONT Count of Poitiers 
0 @N6000000001783162039@ NOTE Basic Life Information
1 CONT
1 CONT William III of Aquitaine
1 CONT
1 CONT William III (915-3 April 963), called Towhead (French: Tête d'étoupe, Latin: Caput Stupe) from the colour of his hair, was the "Count of the Duchy of Aquitaine" from 959 and Duke of Aquitaine from 962 to his death. He was also the
1 CONC  Count of Poitou (as William I) from 935 and Count of Auvergne from 950. The primary sources for his reign are Ademar of Chabannes, Dudo of Saint-Quentin, and William of Jumièges.
1 CONT
1 CONT William was son of Ebalus Manzer and Emilienne. He was born in Poitiers. He claimed the Duchy of Aquitaine from his father's death, but the royal chancery did not recognise his ducal title until the year before his own death.
1 CONT
1 CONT Shortly aftered the death of King Rudolph in 936, he was constrained to forfeit some land to Hugh the Great by Louis IV. He did it with grace, but his relationship with Hugh thenceforward deteriorated. In 950, Hugh was reconciled 
1 CONC with Louis and granted the duchies of Burgundy and Aquitaine. He tried to conquer Aquitaine with Louis's assistance, but William defeated them. Lothair, Louis's successor, feared the power of William. In August 955 he joined Hugh 
1 CONC to besiege Poitiers, which resisted successfully. William, however, gave battle and was routed.
1 CONT
1 CONT After the death of Hugh, his son Hugh Capet was named duke of Aquitaine, but he never tried to take up his fief, as William reconciled with Lothair.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was given the abbey of Saint-Hilaire-le-Grand, which remained in his house after his death. He also built a library in the palace of Poitiers.
1 CONT
1 CONT Marriage and Children
1 CONT
1 CONT He married Gerloc (renamed Adele), daughter of Rollo of Normandy. They had at least two children:
1 CONT Adelaide, who married Hugh Capet
1 CONT William, his successor in Aquitaine. He abdicated to the abbey of Saint-Cyprien in Poitiers and left the government to his son.
1 CONT
1 CONT <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_III_of_Aquitaine>
0 @N6000000001783162028@ NOTE Basic Life Information
1 CONT
1 CONT Guillaume III, Duc d'Aquitaine was born circa 915. He is the son of Ebalus, Duc d'Aquitaine.
1 CONT
1 CONT Guillaume III, Duc d'Aquitaine also went by the nick-name of William 'Towhead'. He was a member of the House of Poitiers.1 He succeeded to the title of Duc d'Aquitaine in 934. He gained the title of Comte de Poitou.
1 CONT
1 CONT Marriage and Children
1 CONT
1 CONT He married Adele de Normandie, daughter of Rollo Ragnvaldsson, 1st Duc de Normandie and Poppa of Normandy de Valois, in 935.  Children of Guillaume III, Duc d'Aquitaine and Adele de Normandie
1 CONT Guillaume IV, Duc d'Aquitaine b. c 937, d. bt 995 - 996
1 CONT Adelaide de Poitou b. c 945, d. bt 1004 - 1005
1 CONT
1 CONT Death
1 CONT
1 CONT He died on 3 April 963.
1 CONT
1 CONT <http://thepeerage.com/p10662.htm>
