0 @N6000000007329573557@ NOTE In article <abwilson.hip.berkeley.edu>, <abwilson -at-
1 CONT uclink2.berkeley.edu> (Alan B. Wilson) wrote:
1 CONT snip  >  > The article by K. S. B. Keats-Rohan says that 'she [Beatrice
1 CONT de   Vascoeuil] >  was probably the daughter of Tesselin vicomte of
1 CONT Rouen, allegedly   husband > of  Gunnor's niece.  Further conjecture
1 CONT would allow us to suggest that >  Tesselin's wife was a sister of
1 CONT Joscelina, the Gunnoride niece who   married >  Roger II's grandfather
1 CONT Hugh de Montgomery.''  >  If Tesselin, vicomte of Rouen, was married to a
1 CONT sister of Josceline   who was  married to Hugh de Montgomery, then she
1 CONT must be a daughter of Osbern   de Bolbec  and Wevie (tables 637 and 695,
1 CONT vol. iii, Europaische Stammtafeln). We   can  extend Keats-Rohan's table
1 CONT as follows.
1 CONT Osbern de Bolbec = Wevie,
1 CONT | sister of Gunnor                                                     |
1 CONT Tesselin = ? de Bolbec                             vicomte of Rouen |
1 CONT |                               Rodulf I = Beatrice
1 CONT de Warenne | de Vascoeuil                                               |
1 CONT |   |          Rodulf II = Emma
1 CONT Roger         de Warenne |                                       de
1 CONT Mortemer                           |
1 CONT |                                 |    Rodulf III
1 CONT William = Gundrada   de Warenne                   de Warenne
1 CONT One problem I have with this reconstruction is that Keats-Rohan makes
1 CONT Roger de  Mortemer the son of Rodulph I de Warenne.  While this avoids
1 CONT the   problem of  making him the brother of William de Warenne, it
1 CONT ignores the clear   evidence  that Roger de Mortemer was the son of Hugh,
1 CONT bishop of Coutances (as   presented,  e.g., in L. C. Loyd, 'The origin of
1 CONT the family of Warenne,' The   Yorkshire  Archaeological Journal, xxxi,
1 CONT 97-113, especially p. 99).
1 CONT Keats-Rohan does not provide any reason that I can see for his
1 CONT conjecture that  the wife of Tesselin was a sister of Josceline.
1 CONT Another problem is that it makes Anselm of Bec's objection to a
1 CONT projected  marriage between a natural daughter of Henry I and William de
1 CONT Warenne, the 2nd  earl, off by two generations.  (See Loyd, p. 108.)
1 CONT Keats-Rohan's reference to Hugh de Montgomery as grandfather (rather
1 CONT than  father) of Roger II, while not relevant to the argument, is not
1 CONT reassuring.
1 CONT Another possible configuration which seems to better fit the known
1 CONT facts is as  follows:
1 CONT Tesselin                  Bishop Hugh = sister of Gunnor vicomte of
1 CONT Rouen     of Coutances |     |                                  |     |
1 CONT |              |      |  Beatrice = (1) Rodulf I (2) = Emma
1 CONT Roger                de Warenne    |                                  de
1 CONT Mortemer                                     |                  |
1 CONT |         Rodulf II                     William = Gundrada        de
1 CONT Warenne                  de Warenne
1 CONT This is the solution posited in the Dictionary of National   Biography
1 CONT in  both the essays on Ralph de Mortimer and on William de Warenne. (Also
1 CONT C.  Waters, 'Gundrada de Warenne' is referred to.)  It leaves the problem
1 CONT of the  reference to Rodulf's  wife  as a niece of Gonnor.  Any
1 CONT suggestions?   Alan B.  Wilson <abwilson -at- uclink2.berkeley.edu>
1 CONT [Alan B. Wilson <abwilson -at- uclink2.berkeley.edu> 6-16-96
1 CONT soc.gen.med]  pboren -at- nut.rand.org (Pat Boren) wrote:
1 CONT >Alan B. Wilson <abwilson -at- uclink2.berkeley.edu> wrote: > >> ~
1 CONT Bishop Hugh  >> of Coutances  >>           |  >>        Rodulf II = Emma
1 CONT >>       de Warenne |  >>                  |  >>          >>       |
1 CONT |  >>  Rodulf III                     William = Gundrada >> de Warenne
1 CONT de Warenne
1 CONT Appendix A of Vol ix of the Complete Peerage to which Stewart Baldwin
1 CONT referred  us is an article by L. C. Loyd on the Mortemer-Warenne
1 CONT relationship.   He makes  it clear that Bishop Hugh of Coutances is not
1 CONT the father of either   Roger de  Mortemer or of any Warenne.  >  snip  >
1 CONT >WILLIAM, Earl of Warenne in Normandy, may have been the son of   Walter
1 CONT >de St.   Martin.  He married a daughter of Ralph de Torta, a noble Dane
1 CONT and   >protector  of Normandy, and had a son, Ralph (Rodulf II).  >
1 CONT L. C. Loyd also indicates that there is no record of a Walter de
1 CONT Saint-Martin  in the eleventh century.  His position in the Warenne
1 CONT ancestry is   another bit  of misinformation which we can credit Torigni
1 CONT for.
1 CONT >RALPH (Rodolphus) was known as Sire de Garenne.  He first married
1 CONT Beatrice  >and second Emma, by whom he had Ralph (no issue) and WILLIAM
1 CONT (m.   >Gundrada).  >
1 CONT My reconstruction of Keats-Rohan's proposed relationships was:
1 CONT Osbern de Bolbec = Wevie,
1 CONT | sister of Gunnor                                                     |
1 CONT Tesselin = ? de Bolbec                             vicomte of Rouen |
1 CONT |                               Rodulf I = Beatrice
1 CONT de Warenne | de Vascoeuil                                        |
1 CONT |                                               |
1 CONT Rodulf II = Emma                                  Roger         de
1 CONT Warenne |                                       de Mortemer
1 CONT |
1 CONT |                            |    Rodulf III
1 CONT William = Gundrada   de Warenne                   de Warenne
1 CONT The wife of Tesselin, and hence Bolbec & Wevie, was quite speculative,
1 CONT although there is supposed to be a kin of Gunnor in the Warenne
1 CONT ancestry.  I  now think the case for two Rodulfs - the first who married
1 CONT Beatrice   and the  second who married Emma - to be quite string.  My
1 CONT thanks to Stuart   Baldwin for  requiring me to read more closely.  //
