0 @N6000000001783470397@ NOTE Basic Life Information
1 CONT
1 CONT William Longsword was the son of Rollo, founder of the Rollonid dynasty that would become dukes of Normandy and after 1066, kings of England. In William’s day, however, the Rollonid principality was still a fragile entity centered
1 CONC  around Rouen and, in terms of Realpolitik, not extending far west of the Seine.  Most of William’s career was spent in relative obscurity, but in the late 930s he suddenly emerged onto the stage of Frankish royal politics, first 
1 CONC because of his war with Arnulf of Flanders, and then because of his support for King Louis IV (d’Outremer) at a time when Louis’ star seemed to be fading.
1 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_I,_Duke_of_Normandy
1 CONT
1 CONT Marriages and Children
1 CONT
1 CONT He was married twice, firstly to Sprota (Adela) of Senlis, a daughter of Hubert, Count of Senlis and Vermandois which produced issue:-
1 CONT Richard "the Fearless" of Normandy
1 CONT Raoul D'Ivry
1 CONT
1 CONT He was married for a second time in 935 to Luitgarda of Vermandois, a niece of his first wife Sprota.
1 CONT
1 CONT Death
1 CONT
1 CONT In December 942, at the peak of his good relations with Louis, Arnulf called for a peace conference with William; there, William was murdered (traditionally, Arnulf is said to have arranged the murder; some day I will argue that h
1 CONC e did not)..  A Planctus (mourning poem) was composed probably shortly after; Jules Lair suggests plausibly if not definitively that it was in 943.  (Catalog references:  Chevalier 10576; Walther 10205; Schaller & Ewald Könsgen 88
1 CONC 13; Yearley L81.) This poem, although it survives only in corrupt and incomplete versions and is largely hagiographic in content, nevertheless is a critical source for early Norman history.  It is by far the earliest work written 
1 CONC about the Normans from a Norman point of view, and some historical nuggets can be gleaned from it.
1 CONT <http://www.englishmonarchs.co.uk/dukes_of_normandy.htm>
1 CONT
1 CONT English Translation of William's Planctus
1 CONT
1 CONT Ringing out to the farthest reaches, tearful plectrum of the tongue
1 CONT echoing, blowing, with a sad heart,
1 CONT mourn the loss of the great peace, once ours,
1 CONT now taken away.
1 CONT All weep for innocent, slain William.
1 CONT This man, born in an overseas city to a father
1 CONT remaining in the error of the pagans,
1 CONT but to a mother dedicated to the nourishing faith,
1 CONT was washed by the sacred water.
1 CONT All weep for innocent, slain William.
1 CONT With his father dying the infidels,
1 CONT warlike, rose up against him:
1 CONT trusting completely in God, he
1 CONT subjugated them to himself with his strong right hand.
1 CONT All weep for innocent, slain William.
1 CONT One time he made King Louis
1 CONT a lord to him, one who would reign,
1 CONT in order that with him he would surpass his enemy
1 CONT and rule in the way of kings.
1 CONT All weep for innocent, slain William.
1 CONT He, taught the unity of the Trinity
1 CONT by Martin, the trinity of the Unity,
1 CONT three are one and one is three,
1 CONT he founded a monastery...
1 CONT All weep for innocent, slain William.
1 CONT ...which was named in honor of Saint Peter.
1 CONT After which, we should admit, he would have brought himself there,
1 CONT where he would have seemed to be devoted to the life of the monks in his fashion.
1 CONT All weep for innocent, slain William.
1 CONT With so many good things needing to be counted, we hesitate
1 CONT to announce such a crime, a loss to everyone,
1 CONT to be recited with sobbing and weeping,
1 CONT a lamb butchered by a wolf.
1 CONT All weep for innocent, slain William.
1 CONT There was a certain wealthy man, full of trickery,
1 CONT and he was called Arnulf the Fleming,
1 CONT with whom he associated himself by a sworn oath,
1 CONT that fortunate one to the miserable one.
1 CONT All weep for innocent, slain William.
1 CONT A meeting was set up for a Saturday,
1 CONT with no hostage given, at a certain river
1 CONT that brutal one hurried to the innocent one,
1 CONT the milder one to be sacrificed.
1 CONT All weep for innocent, slain William.
1 CONT For gathering together on the following day,
1 CONT as if friends (merely by behavior and not in heart)
1 CONT they indicated that they would speak,
1 CONT concealing their animosity.
1 CONT All weep for innocent, slain William.
1 CONT With the sun setting in the west,
1 CONT the innocent one rowing back across,
1 CONT [gibberish, but the sense seems to be “messengers called him back.”]
1 CONT All weep for innocent, slain William.
1 CONT “A secret of [our] master is concealed from you until now,
1 CONT which will be beneficial to himself and to you.”
1 CONT Considering it on this side [of the river], dreading the one he was going to meet,
1 CONT ordering it, he hurried [back across the river].
1 CONT All weep for innocent, slain William.
1 CONT They met him as he was disembarking from the alder ship,
1 CONT hiding [weapons?] in their cowls;
1 CONT one of them [hit?] his head with a sword
1 CONT [gibberish]
1 CONT All weep for innocent, slain William.
1 CONT Seeing this, two avengers*
1 CONT murdered and plundered the unarmed one;
1 CONT in such a manner they sent his body to the earth, his spirit to heaven,
1 CONT him to Christ.
1 CONT All weep for innocent, slain William.
1 CONT *A tradition holds that after William defeated a Norman rival, Riulf, he treacherously had Riulf murdered, and that his own murder was an act of vengeance by Riulf’s relatives.
1 CONT There were two noblemen of the world
1 CONT O William, called by the same name,
1 CONT You were one of them, called “of Rouen”;
1 CONT and the other still shines at Poitou.
1 CONT Let us pray for them.
1 CONT All weep for innocent, slain William.
1 CONT O William, bringer and lover of peace,
1 CONT consoler and defender of the poor,
1 CONT supporter of widows and orphans,
1 CONT now joyfully joined to heaven,
1 CONT all weep for innocent, slain William.
1 CONT Hail Richard, Rouennais count,
1 CONT prince and father of the county, hail;
1 CONT may Christ concede the days of life to you,
1 CONT so that you may be with him without end.  Amen.
1 CONT
1 CONT (http://vlib.iue.it/carrie/documents/planctus/text/index.html)
