0 @N6000000002187721507@ NOTE He was Seigneur of Bellême and Alençon.
0 @N6000000003232515112@ NOTE Comte de Bellême & d'Alencon, Seigneur de Saosnois and Domfront. He fought many battle with the Count of Maine to maintain his control of Saosnais. He married Mathilde [Maud] de Ganelon [Gonelon] (972-1033), of Conde-Sur-Noireau, 
1 CONC in Calvados. His second wife was Adelais [?]. It is said he was named Talvas for the shield called a Talvas which he carried.He favored Duke Richard III of Normandy over his brother, Robert "the Devil." On the death of Richard Gui
1 CONC llaume refused to accept Robert as his successor and rebelled. He was defeated and besieged in his caslte of Alencon. He was forced to beg for mercy, approaching the Duke with bare feet and a saddle on his shoulders. He later rebe
1 CONC lled once more and his forces were destroyed at the Battle of Blavon. William died on 6 October 1027. He had five sons, Fulk [Foulques], who died at the battle of Blavon, Robert, Guillaume II, Yves, Bishops of Seez, and Guerin.
1 CONT
1 CONT I do not have verification on all information that you have downloaded.  Please feel free to contact me @ promise_me_tomorrow@yahoo.com for errors/corrections/ or any additional information, especially if you are willing to share 
1 CONC information
0 @N6000000003232515114@ NOTE (Research):Or William Talvas I. Also as William Talevas, Willelmus Talvacius. He profited from being the vassal of several competing lords. Comte de Bellême, as a fief of the King of France, Comte d'Alencon, as a fief of the Duke 
1 CONC of Normandy, and Lord of the castle of Domfront, as a fief of the Count of Maine. Lord of Seez and Seigneur de Saosnois. Comte de Perche.
1 CONT     "High among these great houses, the third in rank among the original Norman nobility, stood the house of Belesme, whose present head was William, surnamed Talvas. The domains held by his family, partly of the Crown of France [
1 CONC including Belesme], partly of the Duchy of Normandy [valleys of the Orne and Sarthe], might almost put him on a level with princes rather than the ordinary nobles." - from "The History of the Norman Conquest of England: Its Causes
1 CONC  and Its Results" by Edward Augustus Freeman
1 CONT "He fought many battle with the Count of Maine to maintain his control of Saosnais. He married Mathilde [Maud] de Ganelon [Gonelon] (972-1033), of Conde-Sur-Noireau, in Calvados. His second wife was Adelais [?]. It is said he was 
1 CONC named Talvas for the shield called a Talvas which he carried.
1 CONT He favored Duke Richard III of Normandy over his brother, Robert "the Devil." On the death of Richard Guillaume refused to accept Robert as his successor and rebelled. He was defeated and besieged in his castle of Alencon. He was 
1 CONC forced to beg for mercy, approaching the Duke with bare feet and a saddle on his shoulders. He later rebelled once more and his forces were destroyed at the Battle of Blavon. William died on 6 October 1027. He had six sons,
1 CONT (-2) Fulk
1 CONT (-2) Robert
1 CONT (-2) Yves [Ivo], Bishop of Seez
1 CONT (-2) Guillaume II, father of Mabel
1 CONT (-2) Guerin [Warin/Warinus]
1 CONT (-2) Benedict, a monk
