0 @N6000000006422148655@ NOTE !Name is;  Foulques I, "le Roux", Count Of /ANJOU/
0 @N6000000000337443706@ NOTE Source #1:  Elizabeth Hallam, ed, "The Plantagenet Chronicles" (New York:  Weidenfeld & Nicolson, 1986), pp. 19-24.
1 CONT
1 CONT The son of Ingelgar, Fulk the Red, built effectively on his foundations and became count of Anjou by 941.
0 @N6000000009304860530@ NOTE #Générale#Comte de Nantes 908-919, puis devient Vicomte d'Anjou (909-929) puis s'autoproclame Comte d'Anjou en 929. Armes: d'azur au chef de gueules à l'escarboucle d'or percée du champ, pommetée et fleurdelysée de même.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT inhumation : Tours Saint-Martin 37
1 CONT
1 CONT #Générale#Profession : Comte d'Anjou.
1 CONT Il occupe le comté de Nantes de 907 à 919.
1 CONT Décès : vers 923 ou en 941
0 @N6000000001783169109@ NOTE Fulk I of Anjou, called the Red, was son of viscount Ingelger of Angers, and was the first count of Anjou from 898 to 941. He increased the territory of the viscounty of Angers and it became a county around 930. During his reign h
1 CONC e was permanently at war with the Normans and the Bretons. He occupied the county of Nantes in 907, but abandoned it to the Bretons in 919. He married Rosalie de Loches. He died around 941 and was succeeded by his son Fulk II. (Wi
1 CONC kipedia)
1 CONT
1 CONT Under one of the sons of Robert 'the Strong' [le Fort], Anjou was entrusted to a certain Ingelger [Enjuger], who became the founder of the first Angevin dynasty. Ingelger's son Fulk [Foulque] I the Red [le Roux] rid the country of
1 CONC  the Normans and enlarged his domains by taking part of Touraine. He died in 942, and under his successor, Fulk II 'the Good' [le Bon], the destruction caused by the preceding wars was repaired. Geoffrey [Geoffroi] I Grisegonelle 
1 CONC [d.987], who succeeded Fulk II in about 960, began the policy of expansion that was to characterize this first feudal dynasty. He helped Hugh Capet to seize the French crown but died some months after the new king's accession (987
1 CONC ). (http://www.xenophongroup.com/montjoie/anjou.htm)
