1 CONT Royal & Noble Genealogical Data on the Web: Rolf became known as Robert or Rollo in Normandy.  I have shown them as father and son, but this is really a way of showing how his attribution changed.  AKA Rolf Wend-a-Foot.  Does this
1 CONC  mean that Rolf and Rollo are the same person or are father and son as shown? dl  refer to both sources. Royal & Noble, and Ancesters of Maud Flanders. dl
0 @N6000000000640022848@ NOTE Gange-Rolv, Scandinavian rover who founded the duchy of Normandy. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Making himself independent of King Harald I of Norway, Rollo sailed off to raid Scotland, England, Flanders, and France on pirating expeditions and, about 911, established himself in an area along the Seine River. Charles III the 
1 CONC Simple of France held off his siege of Paris, battled him near Chartres, and negotiated the treaty of Saint-Clair-sur-Epte, giving him the part of Neustria that came to be called Normandy; Rollo in return agreed to end his brigand
1 CONC age. He gave his son, William I Longsword, governance of the dukedom (927) before his death. Rollo was baptized in 912 but is said to have died a pagan.
0 @N6000000003300509211@ NOTE
1 CONT Rollo was the Norse conqueror of Normandy.
1 CONT A Viking, and the founder of the Duchy of Normandy. During the reign of A lfred the Great, Rollo could have been a member of the 'Great Heaten Host ' who attacked England around 892. His first known whereabouts are in Fra nce in 9
1 CONC 11 where he laid siege to Chartres. The Vikings were beaten on th is occasion and the king of France Charles III used his advantage of th e situation to persuade the Vikings to stay by offering them land and s o help protect Franc
1 CONC e from further Viking raids.
1 CONT Rollo renounced his pagan past and became a Christian. Rollo laid the fou ndation for the Norman dynasty that resulted in William the Conqueror 22 0 invading England in 1066.
1 CONT In the year 911  Rollo founds the Norman Dynasty
1 CONT In return for land in Northen France, Rollo 647 , a Viking, swears alle giance to the Frence king Charles III. Charles' motives were to use a set tled band of Vikings in his country to help prvent more Viking attacks i n the futur
1 CONC e. The hope that Charles may have had of removing the Viking s at a later date proved impossible to implement.
1 CONT 1st Count of Rouen (0911-0928)
1 CONT Rollo acquired the name Göngu-Hrolf meaning Hrolf "the Walker" because h e was so big that no horse could carry him.
1 CONT Rollo and his fellow Vikings seized Rouen on the river Seine at the end o f the 9th century. Rollo forced Charles III "the Simple", King of Franc e into recognising the Northmen (Normanni) at the Treaty of St. Clair-sur -Epte in 0
1 CONC 911. Rollo converted to Christianity and became Count of Rouen , technically owing fealty to Charles III.
1 CONT Between 0911 and 0928 Rollo and his son William "Longsword" expand thei r lands with military power, capitalising on the infighting in the Franki sh aristocracy. In 0924 they captured Bayeux and Maine.
1 CONT (Hrólf) Duchy of Normandy, by King Charles "the Simple" of France, by a T reaty signed at Castle St. Clair-sur-Epte in 911 AD and from which plac e the Sinclairs (St. Clairs) take their name.
1 CONT Some say that Rollo was the same as Ganger-Hrolf, or Rollo the Ganger, wh o was called that because he was so gangly, to be specific, his legs wer e so long that when he rode a horse it looked like he was walking; Gange r means Wa
1 CONC lker. However, according to Peter Kurrild-Klitgaard, Ph.D., th e early sources all distinguish the two men
1 CONT Sometimes referred to as Rolf.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to some sources, Rollo was born as early as 846.
1 CONT
1 CONT A Norwegian Viking chieftain, Rollo became the "first Norman" when he an d his followers settled in the lower Seine area in about 911. At that tim e, he received the Duchy of Normandy from Charles III of France.
1 CONT
1 CONT Rollo was known as the Walker to his fellow Vikings because he was so sto ut he couldn't find a horse to carry him, although according to an altern ate theory it was because his legs were so long that they touched the gro und whil
1 CONC e Rollo was astride.
1 CONT
1 CONT Rollo
1 CONT In 911, a group of Scandinavian raiders under the leadership of Roll o sailed up the Seine and forced the French king to cede French territory . The price the king asked was that Rollo become a subject of the king an d swear loyal
1 CONC ty. This he did, and the Norsemen settled a very small are a in the north of France. Rollo, however, considered himself to be an ind ependent ruler and aggressively set about increasing the territory unde r his control. This const
1 CONC ant expansion of territory would become the hall mark of the Norman experience in history.
1 CONT Normandy was in name a duchy of France, but the Norman dukes ruled th e area as if it were an independent kingdom with little interference fro m the French king. By the eleventh century, the duchy of Normandy had bec ome one of th
1 CONC e most powerful regions in western Europe. There were, howev er, even more promising times ahead-in 1066, the Norman duke, William th e Bastard, conquered the English forces of Harold Godwinson and became ki ng of England. Norman 
1 CONC culture and political structure would cross the cha nnel and dramatically change English culture and history.
1 CONT The Scandinavians who settled Normandy very quickly adopted the religi on, customs, and language of the surrounding French populations. Rollo co nverted to Catholicism, but the adoption of French culture and language d id not imme
1 CONC diately alter the social structure of the Norman lords. From 9 11 until 980, the history of the Normans is one of constant blood-feuds a nd territorial battles, a history similar to that played out in early Sca ndinavia, the Danel
1 CONC aw in England, and Iceland.
1 CONT Around 980, however, the Normans began to develop a unique set of inst itutions that would catapult them into the front-rank of European power a nd cultural influence. The most significant event in early Norman histor y was the pl
1 CONC acing of Hugh Capet on the throne of France-the Capetians onl y gained the throne through the help of the Normans and in gratitude, the y allowed the Normans to operate independently.
1 CONT Once free from monarchical intrusion, the Norman dukes began to solidi fy an administrative system over their territories. This system became th e model for subsequent medieval government: the feudal system. The Norman s faced spo
1 CONC radic resistance from nobility within their domains. To counte r this nobility, the Norman lords made clergy, who were largely drawn fro m the nobility, as their vassals since the monastic and church lands wer e on lands owned by 
1 CONC the duke. All the knights resident on church and mona stic lands the dukes forced into military loyalty. They used this core o f vassals and knights to overcome the nobility which were forced to ente r into feudal obligations to t
1 CONC he duke.
1 CONT The word, "feudal," comes from the word, "feud." A feudal obligation , then, was essentially built off of clan or tribal protection. For the e arly tribal Scandinavians, the only way to enforce law was through clan p rotection and
1 CONC  blood-feuds. Should a crime be committed against a member o f the clan, it was the job of the entire clan to either seek retributio n or enforce a penalty. It was on this ground that the dukes of Noramnd y built their feudal syst
1 CONC em. Under this system, lay nobility were allowe d to control a certain amount of territory. They were required, however , to enter into oaths to the duke; these oaths required their military se rvice should the duke require it.
1 CONT The feudal system allowed the Norman dukes to control a vast amount o f territory independently of the Capetian kings. It gave the dukes larg e military resources guaranteed through a network of loyalties. From Norm andy, the feud
1 CONC al system spread rapidly first to Italy and then France-wit h Duke William II, the Bastard, this new and powerful form of governmen t would cross the channel to England.
1 CONT As with the Scandinavian settlers of Iceland, the Normans did not sta y put in Normandy. With a growing scarcity of land in the eleventh centur y, some Norman lords migrated to Italy where they carved out their own in dependent No
1 CONC rman duchies. Italy had remained a largely non-urbanized an d backward country after it had been devastated by Justinian's attempt t o retake the western empire at the beginning of the sixth century. The es tablishment of Norman d
1 CONC uchies and the feudal system in Italy was the prim ary reason for the recovery of Italy in the later middle ages.
1 CONT William
1 CONT The turning point in European history, however, was the Norman invasion o f England in 1066. England had seen Scandinavian invasions before; thes e invasions and the subsequent emigrations had carved out an entire Danis h kingdom 
1 CONC in the north of England, the Danelaw. When the Norman descendan ts of Scandinavian raiders returned in the eleventh century, they gaine d control over Anglo-Saxon England and would eventually be responsible fo r English supremacy 
1 CONC over most of Britain.
1 CONT The man responsible had been born to the Duke of Normandy before tha t Duke had married; he would be called William the Bastard and his clai m to the duchy was tenuous at best. For all that, however, he would effec tively rule the
1 CONC  duchy for fifty years (1035- 1087).
1 CONT It was under William that the feudal system attained its final form. H e completed the process of making the lay nobility vassals and perfecte d the form of military service they would provide. Each vassal would prov ide a certain
1 CONC  number of knights or horsemen-the number was determined b y the amount of land that had been granted to them. The tenants-in-chie f of the various lands could not build castles without permission; this a llowed William to prevent
1 CONC  untrustworthy nobility from having defenses aga inst the duke. Finally, while the tenants-in-chief had almost complete co ntrol over their lands and the revenues from those lands, the administrat ion of law and the collection of 
1 CONC taxes was in part controlled by an offic ial of the duke, the viscount. However, many of the nobility that were va ssals of the duke had made knights and others sub-vassals under themselve s. So while the nobility owed loyalty to 
1 CONC William, the sub-vassals owed th eir loyalty to the various nobility. Under William, however, all sub-vass als became vassals of the duke. The network of loyalties always snaked ba ck to the powerful figure of the duke-should thei
1 CONC r be a conflict betwee n a nobleman and the duke, the sub-vassals of the nobleman were require d to run to the side of the duke.
1 CONT This system of government, an elaborate and administrative extension o f the older logic of clan protection, allowed William to assemble a milit ary of over one thousand knights-quite possibly the largest military forc e in Europe
1 CONC  in the eleventh century. After securing the peace in Normand y through the elaboration of feudal government, William began to look acr oss the channel in the 1050's and hatched his schemes to seize English te rritory and the Angl
1 CONC o-Saxon monarchy.
1 CONT England, though larger, wealthier, and more progressed than Normandy , was administratively the contrary of the small duchy. While the Norman s had concentrated power and resources, the Anglo-Saxons had allowed thei r monarchy to 
1 CONC decline. The country was divided into a myriad of earldom s which were run as more or less independent kingdoms. Individual earls c ontrolled not only their lands, but royal revenues, law, and privileges.
1 CONT William claimed the throne of England based on a promise given him b y the Edward the Confessor, the Anglo-Saxon king. Having been captured b y the Normans and then ransomed, Edward, according to Norman propaganda , promised to Wi
1 CONC lliam the throne of England when he passed away. Needles s to say, the throne of England was not granted to William on Edward's de ath, but instead fell to Harold Godwinson.
1 CONT William used this insult as a pretext to invade England. However, Haro ld had also to deal with a Norwegian claim to the throne and a subsequen t invasion. He successfully held back that invasion, but as soon as he wa s finished, 
1 CONC William landed in England. William faced an Anglo-Saxon arm y that had just fought an invading Norwegian and had been marched south t o meet him. This, combined with the incredibly superior military concentr ation of the Norman fo
1 CONC rces, allowed William to easily defeat the Anglo-Sa xons and kill Harold. The Anglo-Saxon period in England effectively cam e to an end, but the importation of Norman culture and government would o pen up a radically new and dynam
1 CONC ic tangent in English history.
1 CONT When William took the crown, his first order of business was to brea k the power of the Anglo-Saxon earls who were the real powers in England , not the king. The history of William's reign in England, and that of hi s predecessors
1 CONC , would be to develop the feudal system into a full-out pol itical monarchy. That is the history of medieval England, a history tha t coincides with a gradual dissemination of a common European culture an d society. From The Chron
1 CONC icle of St. Denis Based on Dudo and William of Jumièges [Vo l. III, p. 105].
1 CONT The king had at first wished to give to Rollo the province of Flanders, b ut the Norman rejected it as being too marshy. Rollo refused to kiss th e foot of Charles when he received from him the duchy of Normandy. "He wh o receives
1 CONC  such a gift," said the bishops to him, "ought to kiss the foo t of the king." "Never," replied he, "will I bend the knee to anyone, o r kiss anybody's foot." Nevertheless, impelled by the entreaties of the F ranks, he ordered one
1 CONC  of his warriors to perform the act in his stead. Th is man seized the foot of the king and lifted it to his lips, kissing i t without bending and so causing the king to tumble over backwards. At th at there was a loud burst of la
1 CONC ughter and a great commotion in the crow d of onlookers. King Charles, Robert, Duke of the Franks, the counts an d magnates, and the bishops and abbots, bound themselves by the oath of t he Catholic faith to Rollo, swearing by the
1 CONC ir lives and their bodies an d by the honor of all the kingdom, that he might hold the land and transm it it to his heirs from generation to generation throughout all time to c ome. When these things had been satisfactorily perfor
1 CONC med, the king return ed in good spirits into his dominion, and Rollo with Duke Robert set ou t for Rouen.
1 CONT In the year of our Lord 912 Rollo was baptized in holy water in the nam e of the sacred Trinity by Franco, archbishop of Rouen. Duke Robert, wh o was his godfather, gave to him his name. Rollo devotedly honored God an d the Holy C
1 CONC hurch with his gifts. . . . The pagans, seeing that their chi eftain had become a Christian, abandoned their idols, received the name o f Christ, and with one accord desired to be baptized. Meanwhile, the Norm an duke made ready f
1 CONC or a splendid wedding and married the daughter of th e king [Gisela] according to Christian rites.
1 CONT Rollo gave assurance of security to all those who wished to dwell in hi s country. The land he divided among his followers, and, as it had bee n a long time unused, he improved it by the construction of new buildings . It was peop
1 CONC led by the Norman warriors and by immigrants from outside re gions. The duke established for his subjects certain inviolable rights an d laws, confirmed and published by the will of the leading men, and he co mpelled all his peopl
1 CONC e to live peaceably together. He rebuilt the churche s, which had been entirely ruined; he restored the temples, which had bee n destroyed by the ravages of the pagans; he repaired and added to the wa lls and fortifications of the
1 CONC  cities; he subdued the Britons who rebelle d against him; and with the provisions obtained from them he supplied al l the country that had been granted to him.
0 @N6000000006422192661@ NOTE !Name is;  Rollo (Robert I), Duke Of /NORMANDY/
0 @N6000000001424894434@ NOTE [hezboone.FTW]
1 CONT !Per "My Boone Family":  HOUSE OF BJORN.  Rollo the Ganger (860-932)
1 CONT and
1 CONT Normandy (Condensed from the account by H.H.Boyesen)  Purloined from
1 CONT "A Norse
1 CONT Family Saga" by Margaret Anderson and Gladys Garland.
1 CONT By 872 A.D. Harald the Fairhaired had united all the scattered
1 CONT earldoms of
1 CONT Norway under his own sway and he issued a stringent order forbidding
1 CONT pillaging
1 CONT within his own kingdom under penalty of outlawry.  In those days
1 CONT sailing out
1 CONT into the world as a viking and plundering foreign lands was held to
1 CONT be a most
1 CONT honorable custom--a "liberal education for chieftains' sons."  But
1 CONT at that time
1 CONT the Norwegians had as yet no national feeling and so regarded
1 CONT Harald's prohibit-
1 CONT ion against plundering their own shores as absurd and arbitrary.
1 CONT Rollo (or
1 CONT Hrolf), son of the King's best friend, Rognovald, Earl of More,
1 CONT undertook to
1 CONT disregard this order, landing in the south of Norway and making
1 CONT havoc among the
1 CONT coast dwellers.  He was outlawed by King Harald.  Rollo betook
1 CONT himself to the
1 CONT Hebrides.  There his great strength and sagacity, no less than his
1 CONT distinguished
1 CONT birth, secured him a favorable reception and much influence.  He
1 CONT gradually
1 CONT assumed command over the Norse exiles and it was probably at his
1 CONT advice that
1 CONT they abandoned the bleak and barren Hebrides for a sunnier clime.
1 CONT Early in the
1 CONT tenth century a large expedition was fitted out and sailed south,
1 CONT landing first
1 CONT in Holland.  Finding that all-too-accessible country already
1 CONT devastated by other
1 CONT vikings, they proceeded to the coast of France and entered the mouth
1 CONT of the
1 CONT river Seine.  The empire of Charlemagne (747-814) had been broken up
1 CONT and divided
1 CONT amoung his grandsons, and the fraction which was to be France, was
1 CONT then confined
1 CONT between the Loire and the Meuse.  King Charles the Simple, a feeble,
1 CONT foolish,
1 CONT and good-natured man, was utterly unequal to defending his territory
1 CONT against
1 CONT foreign invaders or domestic pretenders.  Rollo, meantime, pillaged
1 CONT the area
1 CONT around the city of Rouen.  In this dilemma, the Archbishop of Rouen
1 CONT offered
1 CONT himself as an ambassador to the pagans, hoping he might become and
1 CONT instrument in
1 CONT the hand of God to avert the impending doom.  Rollo, who could honor
1 CONT courage,
1 CONT even in an enemy, received him courteously, pledged himself, in case
1 CONT the city
1 CONT surrendered, to take peaceful possession of it and to molest no one.
1 CONT Keeping
1 CONT his promise, his ships sailed up the river, made his entry into
1 CONT Rouen, inspected
1 CONT the fortifications, the water supply and all points of strategic
1 CONT interest.
1 CONT Pleased, he resolved to remain.  With Rouen as his base of supplies,
1 CONT he made
1 CONT expeditions up the Seine and established a great camp near the
1 CONT confluence of the
1 CONT Seine and Eure.  Hither an army under the command of Reynault, Duke
1 CONT of France,
1 CONT came to drive Rollo out of the country, but instead, was killed in
1 CONT the battle
1 CONT that ensued.  The victorious Rollo sailed up the river and laid
1 CONT siege to Paris.
1 CONT Finding the capital too strong, he retired to Rouen and continued to
1 CONT ravage the
1 CONT surrounding territory.  Rollo conquered the city of Bayeux and slew
1 CONT its ruler,
1 CONT Count Berenger, whose beautiful daughter, Popa, he married.  Now he
1 CONT changed his
1 CONT tactics and took permanent possession of the towns that fell into
1 CONT his hands. By
1 CONT this time the peasants had discovered that Rollo was man of his
1 CONT word, a lord of
1 CONT mighty will, ruthless in enforcing obedience, but open-handed and
1 CONT generous to
1 CONT those who would serve his purposes.  He was now chosen King--a title
1 CONT he later
1 CONT exchanged for that of Duke of Normandy.  He continued to conquer
1 CONT smaller cities
1 CONT and ravage the land until the King of France in desparation made a
1 CONT treaty with
1 CONT Rollo, thus ridding France of a dangerous foe and at the same time
1 CONT making a
1 CONT powerful friend who might help him keep his unruly nobles in order.
1 CONT He there-
1 CONT fore made Rollo sovereign of his northern province with the title of
1 CONT Duke of
1 CONT Normandy (912 A.D.) and gave his daughter Gisela in marriage.  Rollo
1 CONT became a
1 CONT Christian and celebrated his marriage to Gisela with great pomp in
1 CONT Rouen.  His
1 CONT previous marriage to Popa did not seem to have caused him any
1 CONT scruple, though
1 CONT he seems to have regarded the latter as a wife and when Gisela died
1 CONT he resumed
1 CONT marital relations with Popa.  She was the mother of his beloved son,
1 CONT William
1 CONT Longsword.  The peasants were not slow to discover that they were
1 CONT better off
1 CONT under Rollo than they had ever been under their former ruler, and
1 CONT they rewarded
1 CONT him with sincere loyalty and devotion.  Rollo the Ganger (walker)
1 CONT must have been
1 CONT nearly eighty years old when he died.  His son, William Longsword,
1 CONT who succeeded
1 CONT him, was a man of milder disposition, and in vigor and sagacity,
1 CONT inferior to his
1 CONT father.  Rollo's descendant in the fifth generation was William the
1 CONT Conqueror,
1 CONT who inherited in a larger measure the qualities of his great ancestor.
