1 CONT
1 CONT Dro 13 år gammel på vikingferd til Østersjøen. Gjorde strandhogg i Viken og
1 CONT ble fredløs. Dro så til Orknøyene og Suderøyene. Senere dro han med andre
1 CONT vikinger til utløpet av Seinen og herjet der i årene 900 - 911. Stormet også
1 CONT mot Paris. Var gift med Popa. I 911 tilbød Karl den enfoldige ham hertugdømmet
1 CONT og sin datter Gisela, mot å holde landevern mot andre vikinger. Rolf slo til.
1 CONT Han lot seg døpe og fikk navnet Robert I. Han bodde i Rouen.
0 @N6000000007329706354@ NOTE [Brit. Enc.] Rollo (c. 860- c. 932), 1st duke of Normandy.  [In a
1 CONT previous article, camfield -at- olympus.net (Tom Camfield) says]:  :>
1 CONT Amazingly, after innumerable newsgroup and e-mail responsesdealing
1 CONT pretty much with the associated validity of Sigurd Ring and Ragnar
1 CONT Lodbrok,  the finer points of spelling in Icelandic and Norwegian, etc.,
1 CONT the central  point of my original query about the ancestry of Rollo has
1 CONT not  been  specifically addressed.
1 CONT So you have shamed me into answering, as best I can :)  :> No one has
1 CONT come forth to state: 'Rollo's parents were. . .' I have begun  to assume
1 CONT that no one actually knows.
1 CONT In order to draw any conclusions on the subject, one must decide whether
1 CONT one  is to beleive the Icelandic sagas that address periferally the
1 CONT foundation of  Normandy.  These include Orkneyinga Saga, and Heimskringla
1 CONT (or whatever they  are).  If you accept the historical nature of these
1 CONT accounts, then there can  be no doubt as tho the identity of his parents,
1 CONT as you have described them  below.  If you do not accept them as
1 CONT historical, then there is no known  resource that does more than suggest
1 CONT a  nation of origin (usually Norway).
1 CONT :> From my own charts, a brief ahnentafel would be in the following form:
1 CONT 1. Rollo (or however one wants to modify the name).
1 CONT
1 CONT 2. Ragnvald the Wise, (ca. 820-890), Morejarl.  3. Ragnhild Rolvsdatter.
1 CONT 4. Oystein Glumra ('the Noisy'), Morejarl, b. 788.  5. (Ascrida?)
1 CONT Ragnvaldsdatter.//  I do not think #5 and her parentage comes from the
1 CONT sagas mentioned above.  I  know it was in Stuart, but that and 50 cents
1 CONT will buy you a Coke (TM). I  have  not looked into his source for this
1 CONT statement, but in the discussions that I  have seen relating to the royal
1 CONT families of Norway and Denmark, no such  person as her father is even
1 CONT mentioned.
1 CONT :> 6. Rolv Nevja/Rolf Nevra.
1 CONT
1 CONT 8. Ivar Opllendingjarl Halvdansson, b. 756.//  following Orkneyinga saga.
1 CONT :> 10. Ragnvald Gudrodsson, b. Agder.  11. Tora  Sigurdsdatter.//  I know
1 CONT of no source for Ragn. Gudrodsson.  I have to call this entire  pedigree
1 CONT into question at this point.  This may be the result of one of  the
1 CONT infamous attempts to provide every hero with a line from Ragnar Lodbrok.
1 CONT
1 CONT :> 16. Halvdan the Old, b. 724.//  Moncreffe makes him the same as
1 CONT Halfdan hvitbien (below), while Orkneyinga  saga provides a more fabulous
1 CONT descent to an early colonizer of Norway.  However, I have seen a
1 CONT discussion that suggests that `Halfdan the Old' is  another pedigree
1 CONT decoration, appearing at the head of several unrelated  families at
1 CONT different times in history.
1 CONT :> 20. Gudrod II of Agder.  22. Sigurd Orm-i-Oye ('Snake/Worm in Eye').//
1 CONT Again, I don't know the source for this information, and question it.  :>
1 CONT 40. Gudrod Halvdansson (son of Halvdan Hvitbein/'Whiteleg' or
1 CONT 'Whitebone'.//
1 CONT Likely a historical king of Norway.  I personally follow some more recent
1 CONT scandinavian authors in rejecting Haworth's (and probably others'
1 CONT identification as the Danish king Godfried of Frankish annals, who died
1 CONT ca.  812.  (which identification is even followed by the latest Ancestral
1 CONT Roots,  Moriarty, Moncreffe, and others)
1 CONT :> 44. This would be the controversial Ragnar Lodbrok, whose wife was
1 CONT Aslaug  Sigurdsdatter kraka ('the Crow')taking us well into that area
1 CONT that many  look upon as a Mother- Gooseland of genealogy (but an area
1 CONT that  many of us  feel must have a bit more basis in fact).
1 CONT The branches of this continue on back through all manner of legendary
1 CONT individuals. . .//
1 CONT I would agree with that assessment.  :> One can take exception at many
1 CONT places along the line with just the  foregoingwhich is why I have not
1 CONT proceeded further and offered a greater  wealth of controversy.  But can
1 CONT it be generally accepted, at least, that  Ragnvald the Wise was the
1 CONT father of Rollo of Norman fame? I won't trash  any//
1 CONT If you accept the sagas, which most do at least at this point in the
1 CONT story.  :> of my material until someone offers me a superior alternative.
1 CONT And, any  refinements to this version would certainly be appreciated.
1 CONT :-))) (that's  sort of a Norwegian smile, I guess.)//
1 CONT As with most things, there is no `answer', just a long explanation. Todd.
1 CONT [TC] 'Origin and History of the Montgomerys...', B. G. de Montgomery,
1 CONT (William Blackwood and Sons Ltd, Edinburgh and London, 1948)...pp. 30-32.
1 CONT The author cites as proof of his contention that Rollo was the son of
1 CONT Invar  (d. 872), son of Ragnar Lothbroc (also father of Ivar Boneless,
1 CONT Halfdan  Vidserk and Ubbe), son of Halfdan, son of Sigurd Ring:
1 CONT 1. The statement of Dudo de St. Quentin ('De Moribus et Actis Primorum
1 CONT Normanniae Ducum') that Rollo was a Danish prince.
1 CONT 2. Dudo wrote his story some 60 years after Rollo's death (d.931) at the
1 CONT request of his grandson Richard I. Snorre wrote his saga two centuries
1 CONT later.
1 CONT 3. Snorre's inconsistency in describing Rollo's father as a dear friend
1 CONT of  King Harold Fairhair, yet relating Rollo's assault on Viken as if an
1 CONT enemy.
1 CONT 4. The existence of another Earl Rolf/Riulf in Normandy, whom Snorre may
1 CONT have  mistaken for Rollo.
1 CONT 5. Dudo's reference to Rollo's brother Gorm, who arrived in France in the
1 CONT company of his 'uncle Sigfrid, Ragnar's son, and of Godfrid, the son of
1 CONT Harold Klak,' and played a prominent role in the Battle of Saulcourt in
1 CONT 881.
1 CONT 6. Rollo was present at the siege of Paris in 885, thus was fighting in
1 CONT France with the sons of Lothbroc, as was his brother Gorm.
1 CONT etc.... bring in Bernard the Dane, more about Gorm, and such.  12.
1 CONT William the Conqueror was a descendant in the fifth generation from
1 CONT Rollo, Ingvar's son. 'After his arrival in England William opened
1 CONT Ingvar's  grave.' (citing 'Ragnar's Saga,' ch. 22).
1 CONT There is considerably more to the pursuit of this alleged descent by de
1 CONT Montgomery (the relationship of other chapters, etc.) but this gives a
1 CONT general idea. Is anyone familiar with Dudo de San Quentin? I'm intrigued
1 CONT by  the statement that he wrote only 60 years after Rollo's death.
1 CONT Tom Camfield - camfield -at- olympus.net 538 Calhoun St., Port Townsend
1 CONT WA  98368 Curmudgeon Emeritus, School of Hard Knocks//
1 CONT Tilleggskilde: Wurts, John S., Magna Charta: The Pedigrees  of the
1 CONT Barons, Philadelphia, PA: Brookfield Publishing Co, 1942.
1 CONT //  [Tom Bjornstad, <elcontob -at- telepost.no>] ..Rollo, or
1 CONT 'Goungu-Hrslfr'  (written Gange-Rolv in modern Norwegian) most probably
1 CONT was the father of  Richard I the Fearless of Normandy..and received the
1 CONT first ducy of Normandy  in 911. According to Norwegian history his father
1 CONT was Ragnvald Morejarl (or  Rannald or Reginald, Earl of More) from More
1 CONT in Norway, which British,  French, Islandic and Norwegian researchers has
1 CONT agreed on, despite that  Danish, Swedish and Germans means he is
1 CONT descended from Denmark (according to  Johannes Steenstrup and his
1 CONT research of the stories written  by Dudo of St.  Quentin (died bef.
1 CONT 1043). However, the Danish version is  much too fantastic  to be right
1 CONT and in many places also are inaccurate.
1 CONT Ragnvald (who lived in the last part of the 800's ) had also the son
1 CONT Torv-Einar who became Earl of Orkney.  //
