0 @N6000000000945071822@ NOTE Greve av Poitou 890 - 893 og 902 - 935. 
1 CONT Hertug av Aquitania 928 - 932.
1 CONT Ebles forsøkte å motstå Eudes og den av ham utnevnte greve Adhèmar som var nevø
1 CONT til den gamle greven Emeno. Da Karl den Enfoldige tiltråtte regjeringen, falt Adhèmar i unåde i
1 CONT 902 og ble jaget ut av Poitou av Ebles.
1 CONT Han slo normannerne foran Chartres i 911.
1 CONT Da han i 927-28 arvet Auvergne og Limousin, antok ha titelen hertug av Aquitania i
1 CONT konkurranse med Raymond III av Toulouse. Han ble imidlertid berøvet Aquitania i 932 av den
1 CONT robertinske kongen Raoul og beholdt kun Poitou.
1 CONT Ebles var gift
1 CONT 1. gang i 892 med Eremburge og
1 CONT 2. gang før 911 med Emiliane som skal ha vært mor til Vilhelm og som døde før 929.
0 @N6000000000571069369@ NOTE Ebalus or Ebles Manzer or Manser (ca 870 – 935) was Count of Poitou and Duke of Aquitaine on two occasions: from 890 to 892 and from 902 (Poitou) and 927 (Aquitaine) to his death.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ebles was son of Ranulf II of Aquitaine. The meaning of his surname is disputed. Manzer is a Germanic habitational name, but also a Germanic personal name formed from magin, meaning "strength" or "might" (cf magnus). It may also b
1 CONC e a corruption of the Hebrew mamzer, meaning bastard, hence the appellation sometimes seen, Ebles the Bastard, and his supposed Jewish mother. He married an Emilienne and by her had two sons: Ebalus, Bishop of Limoges, and William
1 CONC  III of Aquitaine.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ebles succeeded his father Ranulf in 890, but was driven out in 892 by Aymar, who was supported by Eudes of France. Ebles gained the backing of William the Pious, Count of Auvergne, who placed Aquitaine under his own authority in 
1 CONC 893.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 902, Ebles launched the reconquest of his county with an army lent by his distant relative William the Pious. He took Poitiers while Aymar was away and established control of the county. He was invested as count by Charles III,
1 CONC  with whom Ebles had been raised.
1 CONT
1 CONT The comital title was the only one to which he ever had legitimate investiture. Ebles alloted the abbey of Saint-Maixent to Savary, Viscount of Thouars, who had been his constant supporter. He restructured Poitou by creating new v
1 CONC iscounties in Aulnay and Melle and dissolved the title and position of Viscount of Poitou upon the death of its holder, Maingaud, in 925.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 904, he conquered the Limousin. In 911, Ebles was in Chartres with an army to opposed Rollo, the Viking leader.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 927, William the Younger, successor of William the Pious, and then his successor, his brother Acfred, died in the space of one year. Acfred had made Ebles his heir, Ebles thus found himself Duke of Aquitaine, Count of Berry, Au
1 CONC vergne, and Velay.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 929, King Rudolph started trying to reduce the power of Ebles. He withdrew from him access to Berry, then in 932 he transferred the titles of Duke of Aquitaine and Count of Auvergne to the Count of Toulouse, Raymond III. Moreov
1 CONC er, the territory of La Marche, which was under the control of the lord of Charroux, vassal of Ebles, was transformed into an independent county. 
