1 CONT SAXONY
1 CONT
1 CONT The Franks under Charlemagne slowly conquered the pagan Saxon tribes (782-804). The Saxons were initially subsumed by the Frankish Empire, but emerged as a separate kingdom during the Carolingian fragmentation that followed.
1 CONT
1 CONT 888 -918
1 CONT  Saxony emerges as one of the more powerful stem duchies in East Francia (kings of Germany), once the formal split is made between East and West Franks. The earliest dukes seize the throne of East Francia and under Otto I create w
1 CONC hat comes to be known the Holy Roman Empire. 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT DUCHY & KINGDOM OF SAXONY
1 CONT AD 888 - 962
1 CONT
1 CONT 880 - 912
1 CONT  Otto the Illustrious  
1 CONT 912 - 936
1 CONT  Henry I the Fowler King of the Saxons (918-936). 
1 CONT 936 - 973
1 CONT  Otto I the Great King. First Emperor of German Roman Empire (962-973). 
1 CONT 962 -1025
1 CONT  Otto's line continues as Holy Roman Emperors. 
1 CONT
0 @N6000000002187580321@ NOTE Henry the Fowler
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry I, the Fowler (German: Heinrich der Vogler) (876 - July 2, 936), was Duke of Saxony from 912 and king of the Germans from 919 until his death in 936. First of the Ottonian Dynasty of German kings and emperors, he is generall
1 CONC y considered to be the founder and first king of the medieval German Empire, known until then as the East Franconian Kingdom. An avid hunter, he obtained the epithet "the Fowler" because he was allegedly fixing his birding nets wh
1 CONC en messengers arrived to inform him that he was to be king.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry was the son of Otto the Illustrious, duke of Saxony, and his wife Hedwiga, a great-granddaughter of Charlemagne. In 906 he married Hatheburg, daughter of the Saxon count Erwin, but divorced her in 909 after she had given bir
1 CONC th to his son Thankmar. Later that year he married Matilda of Ringelheim, daughter of Dietrich, count in western Saxony (Westfalia). Matilda bore him three sons and two daughters and founded many religious institutions, including 
1 CONC the abbey of Quedlinburg where Henry is buried, and was later canonized.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry became duke of Saxony upon his father's death in 912 and, an able ruler, continued to strengthen Saxony, frequently in conflict with his neighbors to the South, the dukes of Franconia.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 918 king Conrad I of the East-Franconian Empire, and duke of Franconia, died and recommended Henry as his successor as king, despite the fact that they had been at odds with each other from 912 to 915 over the title to lands in
1 CONC  Thuringia. Conrad's choice was conveyed by duke Eberhard III of Franconia, Conrad's brother and heir, to the assembled Franconian and Saxon nobles at the Reichstag of Fritzlar in 919, which duly elected Henry to be king. Henry re
1 CONC fused to be anointed by a high church official, the only king of his time not to undergo that rite – allegedly because he did not wish to be king by the church's but by the people's acclaim. Duke Burkhard I of Swabia soon swore fe
1 CONC alty to the new king, but duke Arnulf of Bavaria did not submit until Henry invaded Bavaria in 921 and Arnulf swore fealty to him.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry regarded the kingdom as a confederation of tribal duchies rather than a feudal kingdom and himself as primus inter pares. Rather than seeking to administer the empire through counts, as Charlemagne had done and his successor
1 CONC s had attempted, Henry allowed the dukes of Franconia, Swabia and Bavaria to maintain complete internal control of their holdings. In 925, he defeated Giselbert, duke of Lotharingia (Lorraine), and brought that realm, which had be
1 CONC en lost in 910, back into the German kingdom as the fifth tribal duchy (the others being Saxony, Franconia, Swabia, and Bavaria), but allowing Giselbert to remain in power and marrying his daughter Gerberga to his new vassal in 92
1 CONC 8.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry was a very able military leader. Germany had been repeatedly raided by the Magyars (Hungarians), and in 924 Henry paid them a tribute to secure a ten-year truce so that he could fortify towns and train a new elite cavalry fo
1 CONC rce. With his new army, he conquered the Havelli and the Daleminzi in 928 and put down a rebellion in Bohemia in 929. When the Magyars began raiding again, he led an army of all German tribes to victory over them at the battle of 
1 CONC Riade in 933, stopping one of their advances into Germany. He also pacified territories to the north, where the Danes had harried the Frisians off to the sea. The monk and historian Widukind of Corvey in his Rex gestae Saxonicae r
1 CONC eports that the Danes were subjects of Henry the Fowler. Henry incorporated territories held by the Wends, who together with the Danes had attacked Germany, into his kingdom and also conquered Schleswig in 934.
1 CONT
1 CONT When Henry died on 2 July 936, all German tribes were united in a single kingdom. Henry I is therefore considered the first German king and the founder of the eventual Holy Roman Empire of the German Nation (Heiliges Römisches Rei
1 CONC ch Deutscher Nation).
1 CONT
1 CONT His son Otto succeeded him as Emperor Otto I ("the Great"). His second son, Henry, became duke of Bavaria. A third son, Brun (or Bruno), became archbishop of Cologne. His son from his first marriage, Thankmar, rebelled against his
1 CONC  half-brother Otto and was killed in battle in 936. His daughter Gerberga married Duke Giselbert of Lorraine and subsequently King Louis IV of France. His youngest daughter Hedwige of Saxony married Duke (Hugh the Great) of France
1 CONC  and was the mother of Hugh Capet, the first Capetian king of France.
1 CONT
1 CONT He appears in Return to Castle Wolfenstein when the Nazis are trying to wake him from the dead so they can win the war.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Conrad I King of Germany
1 CONT 919–936 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Otto I 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Otto I the Illustrious Duke of Saxony
1 CONT 912–936 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Otto the Great 
1 CONT -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT Henry I of Germany
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry I the Fowler 
1 CONT King of the Germans, Duke of the Saxons 
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry met by a delegation offering the crown while trapping birds. This depiction of the legend was painted by Hermann Vogel in 1900. 
1 CONT Reign 23 April 919 – 2 July 936 
1 CONT Coronation None 
1 CONT Born 876 
1 CONT  Memleben, Saxony 
1 CONT Died 2 July 936 
1 CONT  Memleben 
1 CONT Predecessor Conrad I 
1 CONT Successor Otto I 
1 CONT Consort Matilda of Ringelheim 
1 CONT Royal House Liudolfing 
1 CONT Father Otto the Illustrious 
1 CONT Mother Hedwiga of Franconia 
1 CONT Henry I the Fowler (German: Heinrich der Finkler or Heinrich der Vogler; Latin: Henricius Auceps) (876 – 2 July 936) was the duke of Saxony from 912 and king of the Germans from 919 until his death. First of the Ottonian Dynasty o
1 CONC f German kings and emperors, he is generally considered to be the founder and first king of the medieval German state, known until then as East Francia. An avid hunter, he obtained the epithet "the Fowler"[1] because he was allege
1 CONC dly fixing his birding nets when messengers arrived to inform him that he was to be king.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Family 
1 CONT 2 Succession 
1 CONT 3 Policy 
1 CONT 4 Death and aftermath 
1 CONT 5 Notes 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family
1 CONT Born in Memleben, in what is now Saxony-Anhalt, Henry was the son of Otto the Illustrious, duke of Saxony, and his wife Hedwiga, daughter of Henry of Franconia and Ingeltrude and a great-great-granddaughter of Charlemagne. In 906 
1 CONC he married Hatheburg, daughter of the Saxon count Erwin, but divorced her in 909, after she had given birth to his son Thankmar. Later that year he married St Matilda of Ringelheim, daughter of Dietrich, count in western Saxony (W
1 CONC estfalia).St Matilda bore him three sons and two daughters and founded many religious institutions, including the abbey of Quedlinburg where Henry is buried, and was later canonized.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Succession
1 CONT Henry became duke of Saxony upon his father's death in 912. An able ruler, he continued to strengthen Saxony, frequently in conflict with his neighbors to the South, the dukes of Franconia.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 918 Conrad I, king of the East-Francian Empire and duke of Franconia, died. Although they had been at odds with each other from 912 to 915 over the title to lands in Thuringia, before he died Conrad had recommended Henry as his
1 CONC  successor as king. Conrad's choice was conveyed by Duke Eberhard of Franconia, Conrad's brother and heir, at the Reichstag of Fritzlar in 919. The assembled Franconian and Saxon nobles duly elected Henry to be king. Henry refused
1 CONC  to be anointed by a high church official - the only king of his time not to undergo that rite – allegedly because he did not wish to be king by the church's but by the people's acclaim. Duke Burchard II of Swabia soon swore fealt
1 CONC y to the new king, but duke Arnulf of Bavaria did not submit until Henry invaded Bavaria in 921.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Policy
1 CONT Henry regarded the kingdom as a confederation of tribal duchies rather than as a feudal kingdom and saw himself as primus inter pares. Instead of seeking to administer the empire through counts, as Charlemagne had done and as his 
1 CONC successors had attempted, Henry allowed the dukes of Franconia, Swabia and Bavaria to maintain complete internal control of their holdings. In 925 he defeated Giselbert, duke of Lotharingia (Lorraine), and brought that realm, whic
1 CONC h had been lost in 910, back into the German kingdom as the fifth tribal duchy (the others being Saxony, Franconia, Swabia, and Bavaria). Allowing Giselbert to remain in power as duke of Lotharingia, Henry arranged the marriage of
1 CONC  his daughter Gerberga of Saxony to his new vassal in 928.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry was an able military leader. In 924 Henry paid a tribute to the Magyars (Hungarians), who had repeatedly raided Germany, and thereby secured a ten-year truce so that he could fortify towns and train a new elite cavalry force
1 CONC . During the truce with the Magyars, Henry conquered the Havelli and the Daleminzi in 928 and put down a rebellion in Bohemia in 929. When the Magyars began raiding again, he led an army of all German tribes to victory at the batt
1 CONC le of Riade in 933 near the river Unstrut, stopping their advance into Germany. He also pacified territories to the north, where the Danes had harried the Frisians by sea. The monk and historian Widukind of Corvey in his Res gesta
1 CONC e Saxonicae reports that the Danes were subjects of Henry the Fowler. Henry incorporated into his kingdom territories held by the Wends, who together with the Danes had attacked Germany, and also conquered Schleswig in 934.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Death and aftermath
1 CONT Henry died of a cerebral stroke on 2 July 936 in his palatium in Memleben, one of his favourite places. By then all German tribes were united in a single kingdom. Henry I is therefore considered the first German king and the found
1 CONC er of the eventual Holy Roman Empire of the German Nation (Heiliges Römisches Reich Deutscher Nation). He has sometimes been considered as Holy Roman Emperor, Henry I.
1 CONT
1 CONT His son Otto succeeded him as Emperor. His second son, Henry, became duke of Bavaria. A third son, Brun (or Bruno), became archbishop of Cologne. His son from his first marriage, Thankmar, rebelled against his half-brother Otto an
1 CONC d was killed in battle in 936. After the death of her husband Duke Giselbert of Lotharingia, Henry's daughter Gerberga of Saxony married King Louis IV of France. His youngest daughter Hedwige of Saxony married Duke (Hugh the Great
1 CONC ) of France and was the mother of Hugh Capet, the first Capetian king of France.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry returned to public attention as a character in Richard Wagner's opera, Lohengrin (1850). There are indications that Heinrich Himmler imagined himself the reincarnation of the first king of Germany.[2]
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Notes
1 CONT ^ A fowler is one who hunts wildfowl. 
1 CONT ^ Frischauer, Willi. Himmler, the Evil Genius of the Third Reich. London: Odhams, 1953, pages 85-88; Kersten, Felix. The Kersten Memoirs: 1940-1945. New York: Macmillan, 1957, page 238. 
