0 @N6000000001578856154@ NOTE In 922, Robert led a rebellion against King Charles III _king_of_France (Charles the Simple) and was crowned king by a party of nobles and clergy, but he was soon killed in battle. His son-in-law, Raoul of Burgundy, succeeded him.
0 @N6000000002187570727@ NOTE Robert I of France
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert I (c. 865 - June 15, 923), king of Western Francia, or king of the Franks, was the younger son of Robert the Strong, count of Anjou, and the brother of Odo, or Eudes, who became king of the western Franks in 888.
1 CONT
1 CONT Appointed by Odo ruler of several counties, including the county of Paris, and abbot in commendam of many abbeys, Robert also secured the office of duke of the Franks, a military dignity of high importance. He did not claim the cr
1 CONC own of Western Francia when his brother died in 898; but recognizing the supremacy of the Carolingian king, Charles III, the Simple, he was confirmed in his offices and possessions, after which he continued to defend northern Fran
1 CONC cia from the attacks of the Normans.
1 CONT
1 CONT The peace between the king and his powerful vassal was not seriously disturbed until about 921. The rule of Charles, and especially his partiality for a certain Hagano, had aroused some irritation; and, supported by many of the cl
1 CONC ergy and by some of the most powerful of the Frankish nobles, Robert took up arms, drove Charles into Lorraine, and was himself crowned king of the Franks at Reims on June 29, 922. Collecting an army, Charles marched against the u
1 CONC surper, and on June 15 923, in a stubborn and sanguinary battle near Soissons, Robert was killed, according to one tradition in single combat with his rival.
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert left a son, Hugh the Great, duke of the Franks, and his grandson was Hugh Capet, king of France.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT References
1 CONT This article incorporates text from the 1911 Encyclopædia Britannica, which is in the public domain. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Charles III King of Western Francia
1 CONT 922–923 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Raoul 
1 CONT ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT Robert I of France
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert I (after September 866 posthumously - June 15, 923), king of Western Francia, or king of the Franks, was the younger son of Robert the Strong, count of Anjou, and the brother of Odo, or Eudes, who became king of the western
1 CONC  Franks in 888.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was present at the Siege of Paris in 885. Appointed by Odo ruler of several counties, including the county of Paris, and abbot in commendam of many abbeys, Robert also secured the office of duke of the Franks, a military dignit
1 CONC y of high importance. He did not claim the crown of Western Francia when his brother died in 898; but recognizing the supremacy of the Carolingian king, Charles III, the Simple, he was confirmed in his offices and possessions, aft
1 CONC er which he continued to defend northern Francia from the attacks of the Normans.
1 CONT
1 CONT The peace between the king and his powerful vassal was not seriously disturbed until about 921. The rule of Charles, and especially his partiality for a certain Hagano, had aroused some irritation; and, supported by many of the cl
1 CONC ergy and by some of the most powerful of the Frankish nobles, Robert took up arms, drove Charles into Lorraine, and was himself crowned king of the Franks at Reims on June 29, 922. Collecting an army, Charles marched against the u
1 CONC surper, and on June 15 923, in a stubborn and sanguinary battle near Soissons, Robert was killed, according to one tradition in single combat with his rival.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Family and children
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert was married twice:
1 CONT
1 CONT He married first Aelis (a great-great-great-granddaughter of Charlemagne), by whom he had two daughters:
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. Emma of France (894 – 935), married Rudolph, Duke of Burgundy.
1 CONT    2. Hildebrante of France (895 – 931), married Herbert II of Vermandois.
1 CONT
1 CONT He married second Beatrix of Vermandois, daughter of Herbert I of Vermandois, by whom he had:
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. Hugh the Great, duke of the Franks (who was the father of Hugh Capet, king of France).
1 CONT    2. Richilde and possibly another daughter.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * This article incorporates text from the Encyclopædia Britannica Eleventh Edition, a publication now in the public domain.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Charles III King of Western Francia
1 CONT 922–923 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Raoul
