0 @N6000000001354290848@ NOTE younger son of Robert the Strong of Neustria, and briefly king of France (922-923), or West Francia. His decisive victory over the Northmenat Chartres (911) led to a treaty settling one group of these fierce warriors in Normandy.
1 CONT Robert faithfully served his older brother, King Eudes, during Eudes's reign (888-898). Though on Eudes's death he became one of the most powerful Frankish lords, inheriting all the family lands between the Seine and the Loire riv
1 CONC ers, he swore fealty with other magnates to the new king, the Carolingian Charles III the Simple. Nevertheless, he wasalready served in his domains by viscounts, officials usually regarded as instruments of regal power. From 911 o
1 CONC nward, his role became more decisive: his defeat of the Northmen at Chartres paved the way for the Treaty of Saint-Clair-sur-Epte, by which Charles assigned them territory in Normandy.
1 CONT Robert's military success greatly enhanced his prestige, and dissension between him and the King became undisguised. When Charles III imprudently offered preferment exclusively to lords from Lorraine, the Neustrian lords, led by R
1 CONC obert, broke into open revolt. About 921, Robert,supported by many of the clergy and by some of the most powerful Frankish nobles, took up arms, drove Charles into Lorraine, and was himself crowned king of the Franks at Reims on J
1 CONC une 29, 922. Collecting an army, Charles marched against the usurper, and on June 15, 923, in a stubborn and sanguinary battle near Soissons, Robert was killed, according to one tradition, in single combat with his rival. Robert l
1 CONC eft a son, Hugh the Great, duke of the Franks, and his grandson was Hugh Capet, king of France. [Encyclopaedia Britannica CD '97]
1 CONT ----------
1 CONT Count of Paris and Poitiers, anti-king of France, Duke of France, Marquis of Neustria and Orléans. [Roderick W. Stuart, Royalty for Commoners, 3rd ed., Genealogical Publishing Company, Baltimore MD, 1998]
1 CONT Robert I (b. c. 865--d. June 15, 923, Soissons, Fr.), younger son of Robert the Strong of Neustria, and briefly king of France (922-923), or West Francia. His decisive victory over the Northmen at Chartres (911) led to a treaty se
1 CONC ttling one group of these fierce warriors in Normandy.
1 CONT Robert faithfully served his older brother, King Eudes, during Eudes's reign (888-898). Though on Eudes's death he became one of the most powerful Frankish lords, inheriting all the family lands between the Seine and the Loire riv
1 CONC ers, he swore fealty with other magnates to the new king, the Carolingian Charles III the Simple. Nevertheless, he wasalready served in his domains by viscounts, officials usually regarded as instruments of regal power. From 911 o
1 CONC nward, his role became more decisive: his defeat of the Northmen at Chartres paved the way for the Treaty of Saint-Clair-sur-Epte, by which Charles assigned them territory in Normandy.
1 CONT Robert's military success greatly enhanced his prestige, and dissension between him and the King became undisguised. When Charles III imprudently offered preferment exclusively to lords from Lorraine, the Neustrian lords, led by R
1 CONC obert, broke into open revolt. About 921, Robert,supported by many of the clergy and by some of the most powerful Frankish nobles, took up arms, drove Charles into Lorraine, and was himself crowned king of the Franks at Reims on J
1 CONC une 29, 922. Collecting an army, Charles marched against the usurper, and on June 15, 923, in a stubborn and sanguinary battle near Soissons, Robert was killed, according to one tradition, in single combat with his rival. Robert l
1 CONC eft a son, Hugh the Great, duke of the Franks, and his grandson was Hugh Capet, king of France. [Encyclopaedia Britannica CD '97]
1 CONT ----------
1 CONT Count of Paris and Poitiers, anti-king of France, Duke of France, Marquis of Neustria and Orléans. [Roderick W. Stuart, Royalty for Commoners, 3rd ed., Genealogical Publishing Company, Baltimore MD, 1998]
0 @N6000000002322288429@ NOTE Robert served his older brother, King Eudes, during his reign, 888-898.
1 CONT Later, Robert's military success greatly enhanced his prestigue, and the
1 CONT dissention between him and the new king, Charles III the Simple, became
1 CONT undisquised. The Neustrian lords, led by Robert, broke into open revolt
1 CONT against Charles and Robert was elected king at Reims in June of 922. In a
1 CONT battle at Soissons a year later, Robert was killed, but his army routed that
1 CONT of Charles. His grandson was Hugh Capet, founder of the Capetian dynasty.
