0 @N6000000001503895572@ NOTE
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT
1 CONT Regnier III, Count of Hainaut from 940 to 958, was son of Regnier II, Count of Hainaut. His name is also given as Reginar, and other variants.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was born c. 920 in Brabant. He died in 973. At that time he was in Bohemia, having been captured by Otto the Great; this followed exile imposed by Bruno I, Archbishop of Cologne.
1 CONT
1 CONT Together with his brother Rodolphe, he took part in the rebellion of his uncle, Gislebert of Lotharingia. When Gislebert was killed, in 939, Regnier had to make submission to Otto.
1 CONT
1 CONT He then took up with Louis IV d'Outremer, but Otto sent duke Hermann of Swabia to quell the rebels in 944. Otto's nominee as duke of Lotharingia was Conrad the Red; who tried to diminish the power of Regnier.
1 CONT
1 CONT Conrad then rose against the Emperor, and Regnier had the latter's support. In an anarchic situation, Regnier grabbed territory from the Empire and France. He appropriated the dowry of Queen Gerberge, Otto's sister and mother of t
1 CONC he French king. He also took from church property. In the end Bruno of Cologne, who had been nominated duke of Lotharingia, restored some order. After fierce fighting he defeated Regnier, who refued to submit and was exiled.
1 CONT
1 CONT Reginar IV, Count of Mons was his son, as was Lambert I of Leuven.
0 @N6000000002187798384@ NOTE Reginar III, Count of Hainaut
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Reginar III (c.920-973) was Count of Hainaut from 940 to 958
1 CONT
1 CONT He was born in Brabant as the son of Reginar II, Count of Hainaut.
1 CONT
1 CONT Together with his brother Rodolphe, he took part in the rebellion of his uncle Gilbert, Duke of Lorraine. When Gilbert was killed in 939, Regnier had to pledge fealty to King Otto the Great.
1 CONT
1 CONT He then allied himself with King Louis IV of France, but King Otto sent duke Hermann of Swabia to quell the rebels in 944.
1 CONT
1 CONT Otto appointed Conrad the Red as duke of Lotharingia, who tried to diminish the power of Reginar. However, when Conrad rose against Otto, Reginar supported him. In an anarchic situation, Reginar appropriated the dowry of Queen Ger
1 CONC berge, Otto's sister and mother of the French king, and also church property.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 953, Bruno, Archbishop of Cologne, who had also been appointed duke of Lotharingia, restored order and defeated Reginar.
1 CONT
1 CONT As Reginar refused to submit, he was exiled to Bohemia, where he died.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family
1 CONT
1 CONT He fathered two sons:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Reginar IV, Count of Mons
1 CONT     * Lambert I of Leuven
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] External link
1 CONT
1 CONT     * American Pictures.com link
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Reginar II Count of Hainaut
1 CONT 940–958 Succeeded by
1 CONT Godfrey I
