0 @N6000000001744801955@ NOTE [s2.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT There is a definite connection between the Mortimer family and the de Warenne family, but the genealogies are too confusing to connect the trees with any specific certainty.
1 CONT
1 CONT Some sources add a generation between Walter de St. Martin and William de Warenne: Raoul de Warenne, benefactor to Trinite du Mont at Rouen, as father of both William de Warenne and Roger de Mortemer, and Raoul having a brother Ga
1 CONC utier de St. Martin. (Source: Crispin and Macary, Falaise Roll, London, 1938, per the Hist. Trinite du Mont, v. 17, pp. 52, 183).
1 CONT
1 CONT Alternately, as a lesser choice (saying the pedigrees of de Torigny are incorrect), using Robert de Torigny in his Interpolation of Jumieges (328-9), Crispin and Macary cite the Chron. de Torigny, II, p. 201, as source for Gautier
1 CONC  de St. Martin (Duchesne) as the father of both William de Warenne and Roger de Mortemer, and the Jumieges Interp. de Torigny which says that William de Warenne was the father of Roger de Mortemer... In all cases, it seems, the mo
1 CONC ther of William de Warenne was a niece of the Duchess GONNOR de CREPON.
1 CONT
1 CONT In their Addenda (p. 183), Crispin and Macary say: "The known facts are these: In 1054 after the battle of Mortemer, Roger de Mortemer was deprived of his castle of this name by Duke William, which was given to young William de Wa
1 CONC rren I. The solution is to be found in a charter of the Cartulary of St. Wandrille, where is recorded "Roger, son of Raoul de Warren," which establishes quite clearly that this Roger de Mortemer and William de Warren were the sons
1 CONC  of Raoul de Warren. It is, nevertheless, very strange that Orderic Vital styles Willliam the cousin or kinsman of Roger instead of his brother. He was in a position to get very direct information, because he tells us himself that
1 CONC  Roger de Warren, son of Erneis de Coutances and enphew of William de Warren I, became a monk at St. Evroult, a monastery where Orderic wrote his history. However this may be, there is no reason to doubt Robert de Torigny's statem
1 CONC ent of their having been descended from a niece of the duchess Gonnor, and the truth of the matter seems to be that Raoul de Warren's father really was the husband of one of these nieces, whether he was Gautier de St. Martin or so
1 CONC me other person."
1 CONT
1 CONT Also--somehow connected to Nicholaus de Baqueville.There is a definite connection between the Mortimer family and the de Warenne family, but the genealogies are too confusing to connect the trees with any specific certainty.
1 CONT
1 CONT Some sources add a generation between Walter de St. Martin and William de Warenne: Raoul de Warenne, benefactor to Trinite du Mont at Rouen, as father of both William de Warenne and Roger de Mortemer, and Raoul having a brother Ga
1 CONC utier de St. Martin. (Source: Crispin and Macary, Falaise Roll, London, 1938, per the Hist. Trinite du Mont, v. 17, pp. 52, 183).
1 CONT
1 CONT Alternately, as a lesser choice (saying the pedigrees of de Torigny are incorrect), using Robert de Torigny in his Interpolation of Jumieges (328-9), Crispin and Macary cite the Chron. de Torigny, II, p. 201, as source for Gautier
1 CONC  de St. Martin (Duchesne) as the father of both William de Warenne and Roger de Mortemer, and the Jumieges Interp. de Torigny which says that William de Warenne was the father of Roger de Mortemer... In all cases, it seems, the mo
1 CONC ther of William de Warenne was a niece of the Duchess GONNOR de CREPON.
1 CONT
1 CONT In their Addenda (p. 183), Crispin and Macary say: "The known facts are these: In 1054 after the battle of Mortemer, Roger de Mortemer was deprived of his castle of this name by Duke William, which was given to young William de Wa
1 CONC rren I. The solution is to be found in a charter of the Cartulary of St. Wandrille, where is recorded "Roger, son of Raoul de Warren," which establishes quite clearly that this Roger de Mortemer and William de Warren were the sons
1 CONC  of Raoul de Warren. It is, nevertheless, very strange that Orderic Vital styles Willliam the cousin or kinsman of Roger instead of his brother. He was in a position to get very direct information, because he tells us himself that
1 CONC  Roger de Warren, son of Erneis de Coutances and enphew of William de Warren I, became a monk at St. Evroult, a monastery where Orderic wrote his history. However this may be, there is no reason to doubt Robert de Torigny's statem
1 CONC ent of their having been descended from a niece of the duchess Gonnor, and the truth of the matter seems to be that Raoul de Warren's father really was the husband of one of these nieces, whether he was Gautier de St. Martin or so
1 CONC me other person."
1 CONT
1 CONT Also--somehow connected to Nicholaus de Baqueville.
