0 @N6000000001353685879@ NOTE The surname or title de Vermandois originated from Vermand, a county named from its capital in Picardy, now Department Aisne in northeastern France, seat of the Veromandui of Roman times. The house of Vermandois is one of the most
1 CONC  ancient and famous of the early French noble houses, and is descended in direct male line from the Emperor Charlemagne. The records of the counts go back to Herbert, grandson of Bernard of Italy. From 1045 to 1083, the counts pos
1 CONC sessed also the Valois. In 1102, Raoul de Vermandois was the reigning count. Hugh the Great, Earlof Vermandois, was one of the leaders of the First Crusade and died at Tarsus in Cilicia in 1102.
1 CONT Count of Vermandois and Soissons
1 CONT Seigneur de Senlis, Peronne and St. Quentin Event: Titled Count of Vermandois and Soissons 2
1 CONT Event: Titled Seigneur de Senlis, Peronne and St. Quentin 2
