0 @N6000000002187690298@ NOTE Zoltán of Hungary
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Zoltán (? – 947), also known as Zaltas, according to the mediaeval chronicles, was the third High Prince of the Magyars.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was the youngest (fourth) son of Árpád[1]. The Gesta Hungarorum names Zolta as the successor of his father, and also mentiones that he married a daughter of Menmarót, the local military leader in the region of Bihar (Romanian: 
1 CONC Biharea) at the time of the Hungarian occupation (Honfoglalás).
1 CONT
1 CONT Latest researches questioned his position as a ruler and prefer other sons of Árpád.
1 CONT
1 CONT In any case, his leadership meant much less than with previous leaders, as during his time, tribal leaders had most of the powers. In this era, the raids of Hungarians (kalandozások) were common in much of Europe.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Child
1 CONT Taksony, High Prince of the Magyars (? – before 973) 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sources
1 CONT Ancestral Roots of Certain American Colonists Who Came to America Before 1700 by Frederick Lewis Weis, Line 243-2. 
1 CONT Korai Magyar Történeti Lexikon (9-14. század), foszerkeszto: Kristó, Gyula, szerkesztok: Engel, Pál és Makk, Ferenc (Akadémiai Kiadó, Budapest, 1994) 
1 CONT Kristó, Gyula: A Kárpát-medence és a magyarság régmúltja (1301-ig) (Szegedi Középkortörténeti Könyvtár, Szeged, 1993) 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT ^ Konstantinos Porhyrogennetos: De Administrando Imperio. 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Árpád High Prince of the Magyars
1 CONT ? - ? Succeeded by
1 CONT Fajsz 
1 CONT
