0 @N6000000003949647747@ NOTE He was called "Rognvald, the Powerful and Resourceful." According to medieval historian, Snorri Sturluson, Rognvald governed North Moer and Raums Dale in Norway. Rognvald was a close friend of King Harald I (Haarfagre/Fairhair). 
1 CONT c. 868 Ragnvald fought on the side of his kinsman against these pirate-jarls, and was rewarded with the territories of More and Romsdal. This was after the Battle of Solskel, in which Jarl Hunthiof of More and Jarl Novke of Romsda
1 CONC l were defeated. More and Romsdal are in western Norway, bordering on the Atlantic Ocean.  Occupation: Jarl of Maera, More and Romsdahl.  The old vikings had long beards and hair. There came a time when ways began to change and th
1 CONC at fashion changed as well. King Harold Fairhair, first king of Norway was given his name by our ancestor, Earl Rognvald. It happened in this way: King Harold was at a feast at Rognvald's holding at More and prepared for it by hav
1 CONC ing a bath and having his long hair washed. Earl Rognvald cut his hair which had been uncut and uncombed for 10 years and had caused his name "Lufa" or "Rough Matted Hair". Such beautiful, abundant, fair hair was revealed after th
1 CONC e washing that Rognvald gave him the name "Harold Harfager" (Harold Fairhair). All present agreed. This was described in the Hiemskringla by Snorri Sturleson.  Ragnvald was surprised in his hall and burned alive circa 894 by Halfd
1 CONC an Haaleg and Gudred Liomi, King Harald's sons by Snaefrid, dau. of Swasi. 
