0 @N6000000000945071836@ NOTE Hertug av Aquitania og greve av Poitou 867 - 890. 
1 CONT Rainulf ble med vanskelighet sin fars etterfølger og regjerte til 890. Han slo i 868 tilbake
1 CONT normannernes angrep på Poitiers.
1 CONT Rainulf ville ikke annerkjenne Eudes og lot seg etter sigende proklamere som konge av
1 CONT Aquitanien av de andre grevene. Alliert med normannerne, han ektet datteren til en av deres
1 CONT anførere, dannet han et parti til gunst for Karl ?den Enfoldige?. Eudes angrep Poitou, men
1 CONT sluttet fred og anerkjente Rainulf som greve. Senere ble Rainulf forgiftet av Eudes.
1 CONT Hans hustru het muligens Irmgard.
0 @N6000000000571069402@ NOTE Ranulf II (also spelled Rannoux, Rannulf, Ramnulf, and Ranulph; 850 – 5 August 890) was Count of Poitou from 866 and Duke of Aquitaine from 887. On the death of Charles the Fat in 888, he styled himself King of Aquitaine and did s
1 CONC o until 889 or his death, after which the title fell into abeyance.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ranulf may have been selected as a temporary king by the Aquitainian nobles, for they accepted Odo of France after his death. Only the Annales Fuldenses definitively give him this title. He is recorded to have taken custody of Cha
1 CONC rles, the young son of Louis the Stammerer and he certainly did not recognise Odo as king. He appeared in the Annales Vedastes in 889 with the title dux maximae partis Aquitaniae: "duke of the major part of Aquitaine." He founded 
1 CONC the viscountcy of Thouars at about that time, part of larger movement to creat viscounts with powers over regional fortresses to man them against the Vikings.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ranulf was a son of Ranulf I and Bilichild of Maine. He married an Ermengard (died 935) and by her had a son, Ranulf III, who succeeded him in Poitiers. His illegitimate son Ebalus succeeded him in Aquitaine and, upon the death of
1 CONC  Ranulf III, in Poitiers too. 
