0 @N6000000000337406338@ NOTE Source #1: NEHGR 117: 268-271 - Count in Wormgau, active 836;  killed;  called "Robert the Strong" - had two sons by second wife:  Odo, King of the Franks and Robert I.
0 @N6000000003051310815@ NOTE [Weis, 50] seen 836; of Anjou, Blois, Tours, Auxerre, Nevers; called Robert the Strong.
0 @N6000000001353916504@ NOTE Robert THE STRONG (LE FORT) (d. Sept. 15, 866, Brissarthe, Fr.), count of Anjou and of Blois, appears as rector of the abbey of Marmoutierin 852, and as one of Charles the Bald's missi dominici in 853; but soon afterward he was am
1 CONC ong those who rebelled against Charles and invited the king's half-brother, Louis the German, to invade West Francia.
1 CONT In 860 Robert came to terms with Charles, who made him count of Anjouand of Blois and entrusted him with the defense of that part of his kingdom which lay between the Seine and the Loire, a district which hadsuffered greatly from 
1 CONC the ravages of the Normans and the Bretons. A great victory over the Northmen in 865 was followed by the King's grantto Robert of full control over Neustria early the next year. He was killed in battle at Brissarthe in Oct. 866, l
1 CONC eaving two sons, Odo or Eudes, and Robert, both of whom became kings of the Franks. The memory of Robert's exploits brought great prestige to his family. His sons, Eudes and Robert I, both became kings of West Francia (or France);
1 CONC  and the Capetian kings (from 987) were his direct descendants. [Encyclopaedia Britannica CD '97; Encyclopaedia Britannica, 1961 ed., Vol. 19, p.348: ROBERT THE STRONG]
0 @N6000000000953533275@ NOTE
1 CONT Rutpert IV "The Strong" Count of Wormgau was the son of Rutpert III Count of Wormgau and (?) Wialdruth. He was a Count. 
1 CONT Seen 836, of Anjou, Blois, Tours, Auxerre, Nevers. Was killed 866.
