0 @N6000000002187570747@ NOTE Robert the Strong
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert the Strong (Old Frank: Rutpert) (died September 15, 866), also known as Robert IV, was a count of Tours. He was nominated by Charles the Bald missus dominicus for the Tours and Angers regions in 853. After a rebellion again
1 CONC st Charles II in 855, he became duke for the region between Seine and Loire. From this time he was responsible for fighting against Normans and Britons, and he eventually met his demise in 866 fighting the Normans in the Battle of
1 CONC  Brissarthe.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was possibly married to Adelaide, daughter of Louis the Pious and Ermengarde of Hesbaye. Robert was the father of Odo, Count of Paris and Robert I of France, who both became King of Western Francia. Robert was the great-grandfa
1 CONC ther of Hugh Capet and thus the ancestor of all the Capetians.
1 CONT ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT Schwennicke Volume II page 10: Rutpert IV (Robert IV) Graf im Wormsgau, Comte de Tours, nobilis Franciae and Comte de Paris, killed in battle 15 September or 25 July 866; survived by his second wife Adelaide/Aelis de Tours. He is 
1 CONC the father of Robert I, King of France (King Robert was born *after* his father was killed). Source: Dan Pomerleau, Brian Tompsett, Leo van de Pas 
0 @N6000000002322288449@ NOTE He served Charles the Bald, and, by his bold and inspiring military
1 CONT leadership, succeeded in checking the depredations of the Northman who were
1 CONT laying waste the settlements near the Loire. A great victory in 865 was
1 CONT followed by the king's grant to Robert of full control over Neustria early
1 CONT the next year, but he was killed in a skirmish against those same Northmen in
1 CONT September. The memory of Robert's exploits brought great prestigue to his
1 CONT family. All Capetian kings from 987 were his direct descendants.
