1 CONT note couple : #Générale#s:Auréjac
1 CONT
1 CONT #Générale#Profession : Comte d'Anjou et de Tours en 852, de Blois(2ème du titre)et d'Orléans un peu plus tard.
1 CONT Abbé laïc de Marmoutier près de Tours, puis de Saint-Martinde Tours.
1 CONT
1 CONT #Générale#Il fut abbé laïque de Marmoutier. Il fut couronné comte d'Anjou en0854. Il fut  couronné  comte d'Autun en 0860. Il décède en 0866 à Brissarthe,Angers,  Maine-et-Loire, France.
1 CONT  Duc de France.
1 CONT  Marquis de Neustrie
0 @N6000000000945078301@ NOTE Robert ?den Sterke? var greve av Tours, Auxerre og Blois og marki av Neustria. 
1 CONT Han var vistnok sønn til Theodoret eller Wittekind III. Hans mor skal være Adelheid av
1 CONT Tours som var datter til greve Hugo av Tours.
1 CONT Robert skal ha vært gift med en Adelheid som muligens var datter til Ludvig ?den
1 CONT Fromme?. Det antydes imidlertid også at hun kan ha vært datter til Karl ?den Skallede?. Hun
1 CONT var hans annen hustru og skal ha vært søster til keiserinne Irmengard. Robert kunne ikke ha
1 CONT ektet henne før mot slutten av sitt liv. Hun kan derfor ikke ha vært mor til Odo og Robert, og
1 CONT da heller ikke til Richilde. Hennes ekteskap med Robert kan neppe ha funnet sted før 866.
0 @N6000000000232618624@ NOTE =========
1 CONT
1 CONT Baldwin I, Count of Flanders
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT   (Redirected from Baldwin I of Flanders)
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Baldwin I (probably born 830s, died 879), also known as Baldwin Iron Arm (the epithet is first recorded in the 12th century), was the first count of Flanders.
1 CONT
1 CONT Baldwin was the son of a certain Audacer, about whom nothing definite is known; his legendary origins are rejected by modern scholarship. At the time Baldwin first appears in the records he was already a count, presumably in the a
1 CONC rea of Flanders, but this is not known. Count Baldwin rose to prominence when he eloped with princess Judith, daughter of Charles the Bald, king of West Francia. Judith had previously been married to Ethelwulf and his son (from an
1 CONC  earlier marriage) Ethelbald, kings of Wessex, but after the latter's death in 860 she had returned to France.
1 CONT
1 CONT Around Christmas 861, at the instigation of Baldwin and with her brother Louis' consent Judith escaped the custody she had been put under in the city of Senlis after her return from England. She fled north with Count Baldwin. Char
1 CONC les had given no permission for a marriage and tried to capture Baldwin, sending letters to Rorik of Dorestad and Bishop Hungar, forbidding them to shelter the fugitive.
1 CONT
1 CONT After Baldwin and Judith had evaded his attempts to capture them, Charles had his bishops excommunicate the couple. Judith and Baldwin responded by traveling to Rome to plead their case with Pope Nicholas I. Their plea was success
1 CONC ful and Charles was forced to accept. The marriage took place on 13 December 863 in Auxerre. By 870 Baldwin had acquired the lay-abbacy of St. Pieter in Ghent and is assumed to have also acquired the counties of Flanders and Waas,
1 CONC  or parts thereof by this time. Baldwin developed himself as a very faithful and stout supporter of Charles and played an important role in the continuing wars against the Vikings. He is named in 877 as one of those willing to sup
1 CONC port the emperor's son, Louis the Stammerer. During his life Baldwin expanded his territory into one of the major principalities of Western Francia, he died in 879 and was buried in the Abbey of Saint-Bertin, near Saint-Omer.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family
1 CONT
1 CONT Baldwin was succeeded by his son by Judith, Baldwin II (c. 866 - 918). The couple's first son was named Charles after his maternal grandfather, but he died young. His third son Raoul (Rodulf) (c. 869 - murdered 896) became Count o
1 CONC f Cambrai around 888, but he and his brother joined king Zwentibold of Lotharingia in 895. In 896 they attacked Vermandois and captured Arras, Saint-Quentin and Peronne, but later that year Raoul was captured by count Heribert and
1 CONC  killed.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Baldwin I of Flanders
1 CONT     * Counts of Flanders
1 CONT
1 CONT =========
0 @N6000000000269748586@ NOTE Baldwin I of Flanders (died 879), also known as Baldwin Iron Arm, was the first count of Flanders.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] History
1 CONT Baldwin was the son of the forester Inghelram, also called Audacer. Baldwin rose to prominence when he eloped with princess Judith, daughter of Charles the Bald, king of West Francia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Judith had previously been married to Ethelwulf and his son Ethelbald, kings of Wessex. Charles had given no admission to the marriage and tried to hunt Baldwin down. When this failed he had excommunicated him.
1 CONT
1 CONT Judith and Baldwin responded by traveling to Rome to plead their case to Pope Nicholas I. When this was granted Charles was forced to accept the marriage. To give his new son-in-law a proper status, Charles made him count of Ghent
1 CONC .
1 CONT
1 CONT In the following years Baldwin was also given the counties of Ternois and Flanders in 862. Baldwin developed himself as a very faithful and stout supporter of Charles and played an important role in the continuing wars against the
1 CONC  Vikings.
1 CONT
1 CONT Apart from distuingishing himself he also expanded his territory quickly into a main principality of Western Francia
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family
1 CONT Baldwin was succeeded by his son by Judith, Baldwin II of Flanders. His second son Rudolf (865-896) became Count of Cambrai.
