0 @N6000000003615386490@ NOTE Baldwin I (of Constantinople) (1172-1205), first Latin emperor of Constantinople (1204-5), born in Valenciennes, France. In 1200 he joined the Fourth Crusade and assisted in the recapture of Constantinople for the Byzantine empero
1 CONC r Isaac II Angelus. When Isaac failed to pay the Crusaders for their services, they turned against him and sacked the town. Both Isaac and his son were killed; Baldwin was chosen emperor and was crowned in 1204. The Byzantines, in
1 CONC voking the aid of the Bulgarians, rebelled and seized Adrianople (now Edirne). Baldwin laid siege to the town, but he was defeated and taken prisoner. He died in captivity.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT "Baldwin I (of Constantinople)," Microsoft Encarta 96 Encyclopedia. 1993-1995 Microsoft Corporation.  Funk & Wagnalls Corporation.
0 @N6000000003949658138@ NOTE The first ruler of Flanders of whom history has left any record is Baldwin, surnamed Bras-de-fer (Iron-arm). This man, a brave and daring warrior under Charles the Bald, fell in love with the king's daughter Judith, the youthful w
1 CONC idow of two English kings, married her, and fled with his bride to Lorraine. Charles, though at first very angry, was at last conciliated, and made his son-in-law margrave (Marchio Flandriae) of Flanders, which he held as an hered
1 CONC itary fief. The Northmen were at this time continually devastating the coast lands, and Baldwin was entrusted with the possession of this outlying borderland of the west Frankish dominion in order to defend it against the invaders
1 CONC . He was the first of a line of strong rulers, who at some date early in the 10th century exchanged the title of margrave for that of Count.    Ancestry by Joseph Foster p 178 1884 Edition: Count of Flanders and Artois (per Anna D
1 CONC ahlquist - Royal Families of Medieval Scandinavia, etc.1997, p54-55)
1 CONT Unless you wish to take the word of genealogies evidently compiled a couple of centuries after the fact, the parentage of Baldwin I of Flanders is apparently unknown.  Witger's genealogy of count Arnulf, an early genealogy of the 
1 CONC counts of Flanders written in the middle of the tenth century [MGH SS 5, 302-4], has much to say about the Carolingian ancestry of the counts of Flanders (starting with Ansbertus, alleged grandfather of Charlemagne's ancestor Arnu
1 CONC lf, bishop of Metz), but does not give the parentage of Baldwin I. 
0 @N6000000003051129345@ NOTE [Wikipedia, "Baldwin I, Count of Flanders", retrieved 22 Dec 07]
1 CONT Baldwin I (died 879), also known as Baldwin Iron Arm, was the first count of Flanders.
1 CONT
1 CONT Baldwin was the son of the forester Inghelram, also called Audacer. Baldwin rose to prominence when he eloped with princess Judith, daughter of Charles the Bald, king of West Francia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Judith had previously been married to Ethelwulf and his son Ethelbald, kings of Wessex. Charles had given no admission to the marriage and tried to hunt Baldwin down. When this failed he had excommunicated him.
1 CONT
1 CONT Judith and Baldwin responded by traveling to Rome to plead their case to Pope Nicholas I. When this was granted Charles was forced to accept the marriage. To give his new son-in-law a proper status, Charles made him count of Ghent
1 CONC .
1 CONT
1 CONT In the following years Baldwin was also given the counties of Ternois and Flanders in 862. Baldwin developed himself as a very faithful and stout supporter of Charles and played an important role in the continuing wars against the
1 CONC  Vikings.
1 CONT
1 CONT Apart from distuingishing himself he also expanded his territory quickly into a main principality of Western Francia
1 CONT
1 CONT Family
1 CONT Baldwin was succeeded by his son by Judith, Baldwin II of Flanders. His second son Rudolf (865-896) became Count of Cambrai.
