0 @N6000000000232618612@ NOTE ==========
1 CONT
1 CONT Judith of Flanders
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT • Ten things you may not know about Wikipedia •
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT This article does not cite any references or sources. (April 2007)
1 CONT Please help improve this article by adding citations to reliable sources. Unverifiable material may be challenged and removed.
1 CONT
1 CONT Carolingian Kings
1 CONT of West Francia
1 CONT
1 CONT Charles II the Bald
1 CONT Children
1 CONT    Louis the Stammerer
1 CONT    Charles the Child
1 CONT    Carloman
1 CONT Louis II the Stammerer
1 CONT Children
1 CONT    Louis III
1 CONT    Carloman
1 CONT    Charles the Simple
1 CONT Louis III
1 CONT Carloman
1 CONT Charles III the Simple
1 CONT Children
1 CONT    Louis d'Outremer
1 CONT Louis IV d'Outremer
1 CONT Children
1 CONT    Lothair
1 CONT    Charles
1 CONT Lothair
1 CONT Children
1 CONT    Louis the Indolent
1 CONT    Arnulf
1 CONT Louis V the Indolent
1 CONT Followed by
1 CONT House of Capet
1 CONT
1 CONT Judith of Flanders (844 – 870) was a daughter of the Frankish king Charles the Bald. Through her marriage to two kings of Wessex she was first a queen, then later through her third marriage to Baldwin, she became Countess of Fland
1 CONC ers.
1 CONT
1 CONT Judith was born in October of 844, the daughter of Charles the Bald, King of the Franks, and Ermentrude.
1 CONT
1 CONT Her father gave her in marriage to Ethelwulf, King of Wessex on October 1, 856 at Verberie sur Oise, France. Soon after, Ethelwulf's son Ethelbald forced his father to abdicate. Following Ethelwulf's death on January 13, 858, Ethe
1 CONC lbald married his widowed stepmother. However, the marriage was annulled in 860 on the grounds of consanguinity.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Elopement
1 CONT
1 CONT Judith eloped with Baldwin in January 862. They were likely married at the monastery of Senlis before they eloped. The couple was in hiding from Judith's father, King Charles the Bald, until October after which they went to her un
1 CONC cle Lothair II for protection. From there they fled to Pope Nicholas I. The pope took diplomatic action and asked Judith's father to accept the union as legally binding and welcome the young couple into his circle - which ultimate
1 CONC ly he did. The couple then returned to France and were officially married at Auxerre.
1 CONT
1 CONT Baldwin was accepted as son-in-law and was given the land directly south of the Scheldt to ward off Viking attacks. Although it is disputed among historians as to whether King Charles did this in the hope that Baldwin would be kil
1 CONC led in the ensuing battles with the Vikings, Baldwin managed the situation remarkably well. Baldwin succeeded in quelling the Viking threat, expanded both his army and his territory quickly, and became one of the most faithful sup
1 CONC porters of King Charles. The March of Baldwin came to be known as the County of Flanders and was for a long time the most powerful principality of France.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Succession
1 CONT
1 CONT Judith and Baldwin had a son, Baldwin II, Count of Flanders, born in 864. Judith died in 870.
1 CONT
1 CONT ==========
0 @N6000000000269742470@ NOTE Judith of Flanders (844 '96 870) was a daughter of the Frankish king Charles the Bald and therefore she was a princess. Through her marriage to two kings of Wessex she first became queen, and later through her third marriage to Ba
1 CONC ldwin she became countess of Flanders.
1 CONT
1 CONT Judith was probably born in October of 844, the daughter of Charles the Bald, King of the Franks, and Ermentrude.
1 CONT
1 CONT Her father gave her in marriage to Ethelwulf, King of Wessex on October 1, 856 at Verberie sur Oise, France.
1 CONT
1 CONT Soon after this, Ethelwulf's son Ethelbald forced his father to abdicate. After his father's death on January 13, 858, Ethelbald married his widowed stepmother, but the marriage was annulled in 860 on the grounds of consanguinity.
1 CONT
1 CONT She eloped with Baldwin I, Count of Flanders, in January 862. They were probably married at the monastery of Senlis before they eloped. The couple went into hiding until October from Judith's father King Charles the Bald, when the
1 CONC y went to her uncle Lothair II for protection. From there they fled to Pope Nicholas I for protection. The pope took diplomatic action and asked Judith's father to accept the legal binding of the sacred marriage and welcome the yo
1 CONC ung couple in his circles - which ultimately he did. The couple then went back to France and then officially married at Auxerre. Baldwin was accepted as son-in-law and was given the land directly south of the Scheldt to fight Viki
1 CONC ng attacks. Although among historians it is still disputed whether King Charles did this in the hope that Baldwin would be killed in the ensuing battles with Vikings, Baldwin managed the situation remarkably well, hunted the Vikin
1 CONC gs down, and expanded both his army and his territory quickly. He became one of the most strong, secure and faithful supporters of King Charles. The March of Baldwin became to be known as the County Flanders and was for a long tim
1 CONC e the most powerful principality of France.
1 CONT
1 CONT They had a son, Baldwin II, Count of Flanders, born in 864.
1 CONT
1 CONT Judith died in 870.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Judith in fiction
1 CONT Judith is a significant character in The Marsh King, a juvenile historical novel by C. Walter Hodges, which gives her a fictional son by her marriage to Ethelbald.
1 CONT
1 CONT Judith is also depicted in 'Judith of France' and the sequel, "Journey for a Princess" both by Margaret C. Leighton.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 't Brug (Wes-Vlaams)
1 CONT
1 CONT Judith van West-Francie (geboren ca. 844 en gestorven in 870) was d'ééste gravinne van Vloandern.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [bewerkn] Leevn
1 CONT Judith was een dochtere van Karel de Kale, de keunink van Vrankryk. Os ze twolf joar oed was, den eersten oktober van 't joar 856, wier ze getrouwd me den Iengelsen keunink, Aethelwulf van Wessex. Achter da tie in 858 z'n kèèse ût
1 CONC gebloazen aad, most ze trouwen met ur styfzeune, Aethelbald van Wessex, een zeune van Aethelwulf ût 't èèste bedde en die ook keunink van Iengeland wier. Moa je gienkt ie ook dood en ze kwaamp zie ip ur zeventienste ton moa were n
1 CONC oa Vrankryk. In ofwachtienge van een volgende trouwkandidoat platste ur voader ur oender toezicht van den bisschop van Senlis.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ols Boudewijn I van Vlaanderen, loater genoemd met den Ijzeren Arm, ur ût Senlis oentvoerdige tegen Kestdag 861, liet ur voader z'olle twee in den ban van de Kerke sloan. Ze vluchtigen èèst noa Lotharyngen en loater zelfs noa Rôme
1 CONC . Do leien ze under gevol ût an de paus Nikolaas I die een brief schreef noar ur voader Karel de Kale. Da kwam ton nog t'ope met een dreigement van Boudewijn dat ie wel e kêe zoe kunnen t'ope doen me de Vikings die de noordzeekust
1 CONC  bedreigdigden. Woar ip dat Koarel liet weetn dat ie nie kost weerstoan an de dryngende vrage van de paus dat ie zoed and over erte geleid èn. Den dertiensten december 863 trouwdige Judith mè Boudewijn en kreeg ze ols trouwcadeau 
1 CONC van ur voader de Pagus Flandrensis of anders gezeid 't Groafschap Vlaanderen. Boudewijn I met de Ijzeren Arm wordt doarom aanzien ols den éésten graaf van 't Graafschap Vloandern en dank zie Judith kostn de Vlaamsche graven toe in
1 CONC  de 12ste eeuwe stoefn ip under Karolingische ofkomst. Judith van West-Francie en Boudewijn met de Ijzeren Arm aan t'ope vier kienders: Karel, Boudewijn, Gunhilda en Rudolf. 'T èèste is joenk gestorv'n, mo de drie andere en 't ver
1 CONC re geschipt.
