0 @N6000000002187631344@ NOTE Theobald I of Blois
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Theobald I (died 16 January between 975 and 978), called the Cheater or the Trickster (le Tricheur), was the first count of Blois, Chartres, and Châteaudun from 960, and Tours from 945.
1 CONT
1 CONT Theobald was initially a vassal of Hugh the Great, Duke of France. Around 945, he captured King Louis IV to the benefit of Hugh. In return for freedom, the king granted him the city of Laon. He took the title of "count" in Tours. 
1 CONC He seized Chartres and Châteaudun and remarried his sister to Fulk II of Anjou. In 958, he met Fulk in Verron and the two described themselves as "governor and administrator [of the] kingdom [of Neustria]" and comites Dei gratia (
1 CONC "counts by the grace of God").
1 CONT
1 CONT Theobald's sister married Alan II of Brittany and Theobald governed the duchy during the minority of her son Drogo. Thus, Theobald extended his influence all the way to Rennes.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 960, he began opposing Richard I of Normandy and entered into a long war with the Normans. In 961, he attacked Évreux. The Normans responded by attacking Dunois. In 962, he launched an assault on Rouen which failed. The Normans
1 CONC  burned Chartres in response. He took control of the fortresses of Saint-Aignan in the Loir-et-Cher , Vierzon, and Anguillon in Berry.
1 CONT
1 CONT During the minority of Hugh Capet, he reinforced Chartres and Châteaudun. Around 960, he built Saumur. By his death, he had built a vast power on the Loire, dominating central France.
1 CONT
1 CONT His daughter Emma brought him the county of Provins, nucleus of the later county of Champagne.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family
1 CONT
1 CONT His wife, Luitgarde of Vermandois, was the widow of William I of Normandy. She married Theobald between 942 and 945 and bore him four children:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Theobald (d.962)
1 CONT     * Hugh, Archbishop of Bourges (d.985)
1 CONT     * Odo (d.996), count of Blois
1 CONT     * Emma (d.1003), married William IV of Aquitaine
