0 @N6000000001353897712@ NOTE ADELAIDE (ADELHEID) German ADELHEID DIE HEILIGE, French SAINTE ADÉLAÏDE, Italian SANTA ADELAIDE (b. 931--d. Dec. 16, 999, Seltz, Alsace [now in France]; feast day December 16), was the daughter of Rudolph II of Burgundy, and marri
1 CONC ed, in 947, Lothair, who succeeded his father Hugh as king of Italy. Lothair died in 950 and Adelaide was imprisoned at Como by his successor, Berengar II, marquis of Ivrea, who wished tocompel her to marry his son Adalbert. After
1 CONC  four months (Aug. 951) she escaped and took refuge at Canossa with Atto, count of Modena-Reggio. Meanwhile Otto I, the German king, whose English wife, Edgitha, haddied in 946, came to Italy. Adelaide met him at Pavia, asked him 
1 CONC to help her regain the throne. Otto marched into Lombardy (September 951),declared himself king, and married her (December 951). On Feb. 2, 962, she was crowned empress at Rome by Pope John XXII immediately afterher husband, and s
1 CONC he accompanied Otto in 966 on his third expedition to Italy, where she remained with him for six years. She devoted her time to promoting Cluniac monasticism and to strengthening the allegiance of the German church to the emperor.
1 CONT After Otto I's death (May 7, 973), Adelaide exercised for some yearsa controlling influence over her son, the new emperor, Otto II, untiltheir estrangement in 978. The causes of their estrangement are obscure, but it was possibly 
1 CONC due to the empress' lavish expenditure in charity and church building, which was a serious drain on the imperial finances. In 978 she left the court and lived partly in Italy, partly with her brother Conrad, king of Burgundy, by w
1 CONC hose mediation she was ultimately reconciled to her son. In 983, shortly before his death, Ottoappointed her his regent in Italy, and, in concert with the Empress Theophano, widow of Otto II, and Archbishop Wiligis of Mainz, defen
1 CONC ded the right of her infant grandson, Otto III, to the German crown against the pretensions of Henry the Quarrelsome, duke of Bavaria.
1 CONT In June 984 the infant king was handed over by Henry to the care of the two empresses; but the masterful will of Theophano the Greek empress soon obtained the upper hand. Adelaide lived in Lombardy from 985 to 991and had no voice 
1 CONC in German affairs. After the death of Theophanoon June 15, 991, Adelaide returned to Germany to serve as sole regent, in concert with Archbishop Willigis and a council of princes of theempire, and held it until Otto was declared o
1 CONC f age in 995. In 996 theyoung king went to Italy to receive the imperial crown, and from thisdate Adelaide retired from court life, devoting herself to pious exercises, to correspondence with the abbots Majolus and Odilo of Cluny,
1 CONC  and to the foundation of churches and religious houses. She died on Dec. 17, 999, and was buried in the convent of Saints Peter and Paul, her favorite foundation, at Salz in Alsace. By the emperor Otto I she had four children: Ot
1 CONC to II (d. 983); Mathilda, abbess of Quedlinburg (d. 999); Adelheid (Adelaide), abbess of Essen (d. 974); and Liutgard
0 @N6000000000945061584@ NOTE Grevinne av Burgund. 
1 CONT Adelheids far, Rudolf II av Burgund, døde i 937. Hennes mor, Bertha av Schwaben,
1 CONT giftet seg igjen med kong Hugo av Italien. Adelheid ble da, mens hun enda var barn, forlovet
1 CONT med Hugos sønn, Lothar og gift med ham i 947.
1 CONT Kong Lothar av Italia, som var av Karl ?den Store?'s ætt, døde i 950. En maktlysten og
1 CONT tyrannisk markgreve i Nord-Italia, Berengar, hadde deretter oppkastet seg til konge av Italia.
1 CONT Ettersom den avdøde kong Lothars enke, den vakre og folkekjære Adelheid, ble betraktet
1 CONT som arving til tronen, gjorde Berengar henne uskadelig ved å sperre henne inne, han utsatte
1 CONT henne til og med for den råeste mishandling, antagelig i det håp at hun skulle dø i fengslet. Det
1 CONT fortelles også at Berengar forsøkte å tvinge henne til ekteskap med sin sønn, Adelbert, for på
1 CONT den måten å skaffe sin ætt i hvert fall et skinn av rett til kongekronen. Men Adelheid holdt
1 CONT tappert stand.
1 CONT Ryktet om den unge dronningens lidelser trengte snart over Alpene og fylte den
1 CONT ridderlige kong Otto med rettferdig harme. Nå var anledningen der til å vinne den italienske
1 CONT kongekronen, og han dro med en hær over Alpene for å befri den skjønne og samtidig sette en
1 CONT av sine store politiske planer ut i livet. Sagnet har forøvrig pyntet litt på dette drama og har latt
1 CONT Adelheid skrive et brev til Otto, hvor hun anropte ham om hjelp og bød ham sin hånd og Italias
1 CONT kongekrone som takk. Otto var nemlig blitt enkemann noen år i forveien.
1 CONT Mens Otto gjorde forberedelser til krigstoget, greide Adelheid å flykte fra sine
1 CONT plageånder. En prest og en trofast terne som hadde fått lov til å bli hos henne, hadde med
1 CONT forenede krefter greidd å grave en underjordisk gang ut fra fengslet. En natt flyktet de alle tre,
1 CONT og etter å ha utstått mange farer og strabaser fant de beskyttelse i et befestet slott, som
1 CONT tilhørte en av Berengars motstandere.
1 CONT Så kom da Otto til Italia, og ble hilst med jubel overalt. Han holdt sitt høytidelige inntog i
1 CONT Berengars hovedstad Pavia og kalte seg fra da av konge av Italia. Fra alle kanter strømmet
1 CONT landets mektigste menn til og hyllet ham, mens Berengar trakk seg tilbake uten sverdslag og
1 CONT søkte tilflukt i en borg oppe i fjelltraktene. Deretter sendte Otto bud til Adelheid og anholdt om
1 CONT hennes hånd. Hun svarte ja og fulgte Ottos utsendinger tilbake til Pavia, hvor deres bryllup ble
1 CONT feiret 25.12.951.
1 CONT Adelheid øvet stor politisk innflytelse, spesielt da hun sammen med sin sønns hustru,
1 CONT den kloke og viljesterke Theophano, førte riksregimentet for den lille Otto III. I de første åtte
1 CONT årene ble regjeringen ledet av Theophano, og etter hennes død av Adelheid. Adelheid hadde
1 CONT lenge ligget i strid med sin svigerdatter, men hadde forsonet seg med henne da det gjaldt å
1 CONT bevare kronen for slekten. Adelheid fikk imidlertid aldri så stor innflytelse som Theofano hadde
1 CONT hatt, fordi den unge keiseren nektet å adlyde henne. Alftfor meget smiger fra smiskende
1 CONT hoffmenn hadde gjort den ualminnelig oppvakte gutten så trassig og innbilsk at farmoren til sist
1 CONT ikke orket alle nykkene hans lenger og dro tilbake til Italia, hvor hun alltid hadde likt seg best.
1 CONT Adelheid døde i kloster i 999.
0 @N6000000003051286314@ NOTE The Calendar of Saints says her first husband was poisoned by his successor. When she refused to marry the murderer's son, she was imprisoned. King Otto the Great freed her and married her. He died and her daughter-in-law forced A
1 CONC delaide to quit the royal presence. Throughout these troubles she remained gracious and loving and was canonised a hundred years after her death. 16 Dec is her feast day.
0 @N6000000002176646248@ NOTE When she married Otto, she was the widow of the king of Italy. Otto extended
1 CONT influence into Burgundy  when the Burgundian Princess Adelaide was taken
1 CONT prisoner by the margrave Berengar of Ivrea in 951. When she appealed to him
1 CONT for help, Otto marched into Italy , assumed the title of King of the Lombards,
1 CONT and married Adelaide himself, his first wife having died in 946. She acted as
1 CONT sole regent for grandson Otto III 991-994, who died during a rebellion of the
1 CONT Romans, awaiting military support from his successor, Henry II, in 1002.
0 @N6000000002187645206@ NOTE ADELAIDE
1 CONT
1 CONT Also known as 
1 CONT Adelaide of Burgundy; Adelaide of Italy; Adelheid 
1 CONT Memorial 
1 CONT 16 December 
1 CONT Profile 
1 CONT Daughter of King Rudolf II (Rupert II) of Upper Burgundy. Promised at age two in an arranged marriage as part of a treaty between Rudolf and Hugh of Provence. Married at age 16 to Lothair of Italy, who eventually became king of It
1 CONC aly. Widowed in 950 while still a teenager; Lothair was probably poisoned by his successor to the throne, Berengarius. As part of his attempt to solidify his grip on power, Berengarius ordered Adelaide to marry his son; she refuse
1 CONC d, and was imprisoned. She was freed soon after when the German king Otto the Great defeated Berengarius. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Adelaide married Otto in Pavia, Italy in 951. He was crowned Emperor in Rome in 952, and Adelaide reigned with him for 20 years. Widowed in 973, she was ill-treated by her step-son, Emperor Otto II and his wife Theophano, but even
1 CONC tually reconciled with her royal in-laws. 
1 CONT
1 CONT When Otto II died in 983, he was succeeded by his infant son, Otto III. Theophano acted as regent, and since she still did not like Adelaide, used her power to exile her from the royal court. Theophano died in 991, and Adelaide re
1 CONC turned once again to the court to act as regent for the child emperor. She used her position and power to help the poor, evangelize, especially among the Slavs, and to build and restore monasteries and churches. When Otto III was 
1 CONC old enough, Adelaide retired to the convent of Selta near Cologne, a house she had built. Though she never became a nun, she spent the rest of her days there in prayer. 
1 CONT Born 
1 CONT c.931 at Burgundy, France 
1 CONT Died 
1 CONT 999 at the monastery of Selta (Seltz), Alsace of natural causes 
1 CONT Canonized 
1 CONT 1097 by Pope Urban II 
1 CONT Patronage 
1 CONT abuse victims; brides; empresses; exiles; in-law problems; parenthood; parents of large families; people in exile; princesses; prisoners; second marriages; step-parents; victims of abuse; widows 
1 CONT Representation 
1 CONT empress dispensing alms and food to the poor, often beside a ship 
1 CONT Additional Information 
1 CONT Google Directory
1 CONT Saints: A concise Biographical Dictionary, by John Coulson
1 CONT Catholic Encyclopedia, by T J Campbell
1 CONT Wikipedia
1 CONT Ecole Glossary, by Karen Rae Keck
1 CONT Patronaj Sanktaj Indekso esperanto
1 CONT
1 CONT References 
1 CONT Communion of Saints, by Slaves of the Immaculate Heart of Mary
1 CONT Dictionary of Saints, by John Delaney
1 CONT
1 CONT Hard Copy 
1 CONT printer friendly version 
1 CONT Translate 
1 CONT español | français | deutsch | italiano | português 
