1 CONC due to the empress' lavish expenditure in charity and church building, which was a serious drain on the imperial finances. In 978 she left the court and lived partly in Italy, partly with her brother Conrad, king of Burgundy, by w
1 CONC hose mediation she was ultimately reconciled to her son. In 983, shortly before his death, Ottoappointed her his regent in Italy, and, in concert with the Empress Theophano, widow of Otto II, and Archbishop Wiligis of Mainz, defen
1 CONC ded the right of her infant grandson, Otto III, to the German crown against the pretensions of Henry the Quarrelsome, duke of Bavaria.
1 CONT In June 984 the infant king was handed over by Henry to the care of the two empresses; but the masterful will of Theophano the Greek empress soon obtained the upper hand. Adelaide lived in Lombardy from 985 to 991and had no voice 
1 CONC in German affairs. After the death of Theophanoon June 15, 991, Adelaide returned to Germany to serve as sole regent, in concert with Archbishop Willigis and a council of princes of theempire, and held it until Otto was declared o
1 CONC f age in 995. In 996 theyoung king went to Italy to receive the imperial crown, and from thisdate Adelaide retired from court life, devoting herself to pious exercises, to correspondence with the abbots Majolus and Odilo of Cluny,
1 CONC  and to the foundation of churches and religious houses. She died on Dec. 17, 999, and was buried in the convent of Saints Peter and Paul, her favorite foundation, at Salz in Alsace. By the emperor Otto I she had four children: Ot
1 CONC to II (d. 983); Mathilda, abbess of Quedlinburg (d. 999); Adelheid (Adelaide), abbess of Essen (d. 974); and Liutgard
0 @N6000000002322288802@ NOTE When she married Otto, she was the widow of the king of Italy. Otto extended
1 CONT influence into Burgundy  when the Burgundian Princess Adelaide was taken
1 CONT prisoner by the margrave Berengar of Ivrea in 951. When she appealed to him
1 CONT for help, Otto marched into Italy , assumed the title of King of the Lombards,
1 CONT and married Adelaide himself, his first wife having died in 946. She acted as
1 CONT sole regent for grandson Otto III 991-994, who died during a rebellion of the
1 CONT Romans, awaiting military support from his successor, Henry II, in 1002.
