0 @N6000000000945071144@ NOTE Høvding over ungarerne 896 - 907. 
1 CONT Arpad ble prins av Ungarn omkring 894. Han var den første ungarske storfyrste og
1 CONT magjarernes anfører da de ved år 900 erobret Ungarn. Fra ham nedstammer de senere konger
1 CONT av Ungarn like ned til Andreas III (1000 - 1301) som derfor kalles ?Arpader?.
1 CONT Arpaderslekten var konger av Ungarn 997 - 1301.
1 CONT Arpad betegnes som en noe dunkel historisk person. Han skal ha fullført magjarernes
1 CONT utbredelse til platået ved midtre Donau som begynte under Almos (800 - 844). Den siste kalles
1 CONT en halvt legendarisk person. Arpad skal stamme fra Attila som ble konge over hunnerne i 401.
1 CONT Etter Ludvig ?den Tyske?'s død ble ungarerne eller madjarene stadig farligere og mer
1 CONT pågående, og til slutt sto østfrankerne fullstendig vergeløse overfor dem. Ungarerne var et folk
1 CONT som var beslektet med hunnerne og tilhørte den finsk-ugriske folkestammen. Fra sine
1 CONT opprinnelige boplasser ved Volga hadde de på 800-tallet dratt vestover og slått seg ned på
1 CONT slettene omkring Donau og Theiss. Der ble de etterhånden mer og mer plagsomme for alle
1 CONT nabolandene, det østromerske rike, Tyskland og Italia. Ungarerne var et halvvilt nomadefolk
1 CONT som bodde i telt om sommeren og i sivhytter om vinteren, og det gikk de forferdeligste rykter om
1 CONT at de pleide å rive hjertet ut av brystet på sine falne fiender for å spise det og siden drikke
1 CONT blodet deres. Ypperlige ryttere og sikre skyttere som de var, pleide de å bringe forvirring i
1 CONT fiendens rekker ved lynsnare, overraskende angrep, for så å sende en skur av piler mot dem.
1 CONT Ved å late som om de flyktet, fikk de lokket fienden ut av sine faste stillinger, men så kunne
1 CONT plutselig en sterk reserve av ungarere bryte fram fra et bakhold og kaste seg over forfølgerne.
1 CONT Det var forferdelig å se disse hardbarkede krigerne når ?synsranden med ett ble formørket av
1 CONT deres rytterskarer og tusenere av hjelmer dukket opp som av et avgrunnsdyp?. Herjende,
1 CONT plyndrende og brennende stormet disse hordene fram og spredte død og fordervelse hvor de
1 CONT kom. Fra begynnelsen av 900-tallet var Tyskland i flere decennier fullstendig prisgitt dette
1 CONT halvville folket som ikke hadde noen forståelse for kulturelle verdier. Helt opp til Bremen i nord
1 CONT og til Roma og Syd-Italia i syd våget de seg. Flere ganger satte de til og med over Rhinen, på
1 CONT flåter som de bygget av tømmer fra Schwarzwald, og hjemsøkte Frankrike.
0 @N6000000002187699193@ NOTE Árpád
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT ÁrpádÁrpád (c. 845 – c. 907), the second High Prince of the Magyars (c. 895 – c. 907).
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 His life 
1 CONT 2 Children 
1 CONT 3 Legacy 
1 CONT 4 Sources 
1 CONT 5 References 
1 CONT 6 See also 
1 CONT 7 External links 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] His life
1 CONT
1 CONT Árpád was the son of Álmos, leader of the Hungarian tribal federation; his mother's name and descent is unknown.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 894, Árpád and Kurszán negotiated together with the representatives of the Byzantine emperor, Leo VI the Wise the terms under which the confederation of the Magyar tribes was willing to assist the Byzantine Empire against Emper
1 CONC or Simeon I of Bulgaria.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the spring of next year, the Magyar tribes attacked the Bulgarian Empire and defeated Emperor Simeon I of Bulgaria obliging him to conclude peace with the Byzantine Empire. Emperor Simeon, however, entered into an alliance with
1 CONC  the Pechenegs, who were the eastern neighbours of the Hungarian federation, and he made an attack against the Magyar troops. In the Battle of Southern Buh Simeon I defeated their army; shortly afterwards, the Pechenegs attacked a
1 CONC nd pillaged their territories. The Magyar tribes were obliged to leave Etelköz and move to the Carpathian Basin where they settled down (Honfoglalás).
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Prince Árpád is crossing the Carpathians. A detail of Árpád Feszty and assistants' vast (over 8000 m²) canvas (Ópusztaszer National Memorial Site, Hungary).
1 CONT
1 CONT The catastrophic defeats during the wars with the Bulgarian Empire and the Pechenegs caused Álmos' death, who was probably either assassinated or sacrificed. The leaders of the seven Hungarian tribes proclaimed Árpád to High Princ
1 CONC e of the Magyars[1]; therefore Árpád is considered traditionally to lead the Honfoglalás ("the occupation of the country"). In 896 the Hungarian tribes occupied the Upper Tisza river, from there they undertook numerous looting rai
1 CONC ds in central and western Europe, and in 900/901 they moved to Pannonia[2]. The Magyars entering the Pannonian fields in 896 represented about 200,000–250,000 people.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Árpád and the six other chieftains of the Magyars. From the Chronicon Pictum, 1360.Based on Arabic sources, Árpád's title seems to have been kende[3] or gyula. In that time kende was the spiritual leader of the Magyar tribes, whil
1 CONC e the gyula led their military campaigns. According to legends, Árpád is also said to have been holding the first "parliamentary" session with 40 other "nobles" on horseback before 900 AD.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Children
1 CONT Tarhos (Tarkacsu) (? – ?) 
1 CONT Üllo (Jeleg or Jeleg) (? – ?) 
1 CONT Jutocsa (Jutas) (? – ?) 
1 CONT Zoltan of Hungary (? – ?) 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Legacy
1 CONT Although he is not considered the founder of the Kingdom of Hungary – that was his descendant Stephen I –, he is generally thought of as the forefather of Hungarians and is often affectionally mentioned as our father Árpád. Árpád 
1 CONC was the founder of the dynasty named after him, which would rule over the kingdom of Hungary till 1301.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sources
1 CONT Ancestral Roots of Certain American Colonists Who Came to America Before 1700 by Frederick Lewis Weis, Line 243-1. 
1 CONT Kristó, Gyula - Makk, Ferenc: Az Árpád-ház uralkodói (IPC Könyvek, 1996) 
1 CONT Korai Magyar Történeti Lexikon (9-14. század), foszerkeszto: Kristó, Gyula, szerkesztok: Engel, Pál és Makk, Ferenc (Akadémiai Kiadó, Budapest, 1994) 
1 CONT Kristó, Gyula: A Kárpát-medence és a magyarság régmúltja (1301-ig) (Szegedi Középkortörténeti Könyvtár, Szeged, 1993) 
1 CONT Magyarország Történeti Kronológiája I. – A kezdetektol 1526-ig, foszerkeszto: Benda Kálmán (Akadémiai Kiadó, Budapest, 1981) 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT ^ The Byzantine De administrando imperio says around 950: Prior to this Árpád, the Magyars did never have another ruling prince ('archont') and since then up to today the ruling prince of Hungary has been from that family. However
1 CONC , his father was probably proclaimed to High Prince around 85
1 CONT ^ It is remarkable that Árpád was never mentioned by contemporary Western sources, which strengthens the idea that he was the spiritual ruler of the Magyars. 
1 CONT ^ Some scholars consider Kende to be the name of a person. 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] See also
1 CONT Árpáds 
1 CONT History of Hungary 
1 CONT Attila the Hun 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] External links
1 CONT Árpád, painting from the 19th century 
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Álmos High Prince of the Magyars
1 CONT c. 895 - c. 907 Succeeded by
1 CONT Unknown / Zoltán 
0 @N6000000003051286051@ NOTE [Wikipedia, "Árpád", retrieved 19 Oct 07]
1 CONT Árpád (c. 850?907) was the first ruler of Hungary. He was the probable leader of the Magyar tribe, and the founder of the Árpád dynasty. Although he is not considered the founder of the Kingdom of Hungary ? that was his descendant
1 CONC  Stephen I ?, he is generally thought of as the forefather of Hungarians and is often affectionally mentioned as our father Árpád.
1 CONT
1 CONT Árpád was the son of Álmos leader of the Hungarian tribal federation. According to medieval chronicles, seven proto-Magyar tribes elected him ? as the leader of one of those tribes ? their common leader in Etelköz around 890. He i
1 CONC s said to have been the leader ("prince" ? fejedelem) of the proto-Magyars for 20 years and to have died in 907. The Byzantine De administrando imperio says around 950: Prior to this Árpád, the Magyars did never have another rulin
1 CONC g prince ('archont') and since then up to today the ruling prince of Hungary has been from that family. Other sources however imply that there was a second ruling prince called Kurszán, who was either at the same "level" with Árpá
1 CONC d, or a kind of "vice-prince". Based on Arabic sources, Árpád's title seems to have been kende ? although some scholars consider Kende to be the name of a person ? or gyula.
1 CONT
1 CONT After several looting raids in Europe (from the 860s onwards), the proto-Magyars in Etelköz under Árpád, pushed by the Pechenegs from the East, decided to definitively pass the Carpathian Mountains. In 896 they occupied the Upper 
1 CONC Tisza river, from there they undertook numerous looting raids in central and western Europe, and in 900/901 they moved to Pannonia. The proto-Magyars entering the Pannonian fields in 896 represented about 200,000?250,000 people.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to De administrando imperio, his sons included (maybe not exclusively) :
1 CONT
1 CONT 1. Tarhos (Tarkacsu)
1 CONT 2. Üllo (Jeleg or Jeles)
1 CONT 3. Jutas (Jutocsa)
1 CONT 5. Liüntika (erroneously Levente in older references)
1 CONT 6. Zolta (Zaltasz) - the youngest one.
1 CONT According to legends, Árpád is also said to have been holding the first "parliamentary" session with 40 other "nobles" on horseback before 900 AD.
0 @N6000000003635700178@ NOTE [2722] WSHNGT.ASC file (Geo Wash Ah'tafel) # 1115890624 = 279480512
0 @N6000000003949818786@ NOTE A Finno-Urgrian people, the Magyars migrated southward from central Russia to the steppes of the Urals where they were engaged as nomadic herders. By the beginning of the fifth century AD the Magyars were on the move again, settli
1 CONC ng in the lands between the lower Volga and Don Rivers to the Caucasus Mountains, as tributaries of the turkic Khazars. Their kin, the Huns-Avars, had already moved westward and settled beyond the Carpathian Mountains.
1 CONT The Muslim victory at Al Laks (739-740 AD) caused great unrest in the Khazar empire and dislocated several tribes from the region of the Caucasus Mountains. Population growth in the region also increasingly exascerbated pressures 
1 CONC among the nomadic tribes. Gradually, the Khazars hold on the steppes weakened. Surrounded by hostile horse tribes, some Magyars filtered northward to Baskiria at the foot of the Ural Mountains, where they were later overrun by the
1 CONC  Mongols. The Khagan or chieftains of the seven Magyar tribes (Magyari or Megere, Nyek or Neke, Kurt-Djarmat or Kourtougermatos, Taryan or Tarianos, Yeno or Genach, Kari or Kare, and Kasi or Keszi) held an assembly to decide their
1 CONC  collective future and elected Arpad, as their Gyula or warleader to lead them to new lands over the Carpathian Mountains. Arpad was the son of the Khagan Almos (who traced his lineage to Attila the Hun). The alliance was sealed b
1 CONC y solemn vows sworn over a cup of mingled blood. 
1 CONT Unhappy at the faithlessness of their subjects, the Khazars and their allies, the Pechenegs harried the Magyars' journey westward. By 884 AD, the Magyars had occupied the city of Kiev. From here they continued, joined by three dis
1 CONC affected Turki or Ugrian tribes (the "Kabers") in a new federation of ten tribes referred to as the "Onorgur" or "Ten Arrows" (this is purportedly the linquisitic root of "Hungary"). They moved into the northern lands of the Bulga
1 CONC rs between the Dneper and lower Danube rivers. Here they settled down for a time to scout the region and make alliances with the Greeks in Byzantium. They occupied the cities of Ladomir and Halich, built a fortified camp at Munkac
1 CONC  and began cutting roads into the Carpathian highlands. During this period, the elder Khagan Almos died during the Magyar seige of Ung (Ungvar). Another tradition holds that Almos was killed after the crossing of the Carpathians i
1 CONC n a ritual sacrifice so that his spirit would strengthen his son Arpad.
