0 @N6000000003949818825@ NOTE In Moncreiffe's later book, "Royal Highness" (London, 1982), p. 83, it  is stated that Zsoltán of Hungary married a "Khazar princess, dau. of  MAROT, Khagan of Jewish Khazars between rivers Theiss & Szamos".   Since the table in M
1 CONC oncreiffe's earlier book "Blood Royal" left out  generations in some places, it is not clear whether Moncreiffe  regarded Menu Morat to be the same person as Marot or an ancestor of  Marot.  No sources are given by Moncreiffe in e
1 CONC ither book for this  claim, and "Blood Royal" has a large enough number of bad errors that  the information should not be trusted without confirmation elsewhere.  Nevertheless, I seem to recall that the claim also appears in ES (a
1 CONC t least in one of the earlier versions, perhaps not in Schwennicke's). 
1 CONT The Gesta Hungarorum tells the story of Menumorut twice. In the first passage, Menumorut declined "with a Bulgarian heart" (Bulgarico corde superbe mandando; Gesta Hungarorum, cap. 51, Scriptores Rerum Hungaricarum 1, p. 104) the 
1 CONC request of the Magyar ruler Árpád (907) to cede his territory between the Somes river and the Meses Mountains, and in the negotiations with the ambassadors Usubuu and Veluc of Árpád he invoked the sovereignty of the Byzantine Empe
1 CONC ror Leo VI the Wise.
1 CONT The ambassadors of Árpád crossed the Tisza and came to the capital fortress of Biharia, demanding important territories on the left bank of the river for their duke. Menumorut replied:
1 CONT     "Tell Arpad, duke of Hungary, your lord: Indebted we are to him as a friend to a friend, with all requisite to him, since he is a stranger and lacks many. Yet the territory he asked from our good will never will we bestow as l
1 CONC ong as we will be alive. And we felt sorry that duke Salanus conceded him a very large territory out either of love, which it is said, or out of fear, which is denied. Ourself on the other hand, neither out of love nor out of fear
1 CONC , we will ever concede him land, not even if spanning only a finger, although he said he has a right on it. And his words do not trouble our heart that he stressed he descends from the strain of king Attila, which was called the s
1 CONC courge of God. And if that one raped this country from my ancestor, now thanks to my lord the emperor of Constantinople, nobody can snatch it from my hands." (See also: The original text in Latin)
1 CONT The Magyars first besieged the citadel of Zotmar (Romanian: Satu Mare, Hungarian: Szatmár) and then Menumorut's castle in Bihar, and were able to defeat him.  The Gesta Hungarorum then retells the story of Menumorut. In the second
1 CONC  telling, he married his daughter into the Árpád dynasty. Her son Taksony, the grandson of Menumorut, became ruler of the Magyars and father of Mihály and Géza, whose son Vajk became the first King of Hungary in 1001 under the Chr
1 CONC istian baptismal name Stephen.
