0 @N6000000001540866386@ NOTE Ermentrude of Orléans
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT
1 CONT Ermentrude buried in front of Carloman I (751-771), Basilique Saint-DenisErmentrude of Orléans (also Hirmentrude or Irmintrud) (September 27, 823 ? October 6, 869) was Queen of Franks by her marriage to Charles the Bald, Holy Roma
1 CONC n Emperor and King of West Francia. She was the daughter of Odo, Count of Orleans and his wife, Engeltrude.
1 CONT Ermentrude had a gift for embroidery and an interest in religious foundations. Her husband gave her the Abbey of Chelles. She separated from her husband after he executed her rebellious brother William in 866, and retreated to the
1 CONC  life of a nunnery. Ermentrude was buried in the Basilique Saint-Denis, Paris, France.
0 @N6000000002187582477@ NOTE Ermentrude
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ermentrude Of Orleans (also Hirmentrude, Irmintrud, 823-869 AD) was a Frankish noblewoman. Daughter of Odo, Count of Orleans and Engeltrude, she married Charles the Bald, King of France in 842.
1 CONT
1 CONT She was a gifted embroiderer and was interested in religious foundations. Charles gave her the nunnery of Chelles. She died in 869.
1 CONT
1 CONT Her known children included Judith, Louis the Stammerer, Charles of Aquitaine, and Carloman of France.
1 CONT
1 CONT Retrieved from "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ermentrude"
1 CONT Categories
