0 @N6000000001353849846@ NOTE [blended.FTW]
1 CONT [mergebase.FTW]
1 CONT [Fix.FTW]
1 CONT Western Frankish King 843-877
1 CONT Frankish Emperor 875-877
1 CONT Louis the Pious became Frankish Emperor in 814 with no rivals to the throne. He had three sons, Lothar, Pepin, and Louis. In 817, Lothar was made co-Emperor with his father and King of Italy to replace Bernard, Pepin made King of 
1 CONC Aquitaine, and Louis made King of Bavaria. In 823, Louis had another son, Charles, this one by a new wife (the mother of the 3 brothers had died). Louis tried desperately to work Charles in as a successor, but the three brothers f
1 CONC ought him everytime he tried to reform his will. After much conflict, Emperor Louis dropped Lothar's imperial title in 829 and sent him off to Italy. The next year the brothers attacked, reinstated Lothar with his imperial title, 
1 CONC and had Judith, the mother of Charles, sent off to a nunnery. By 831, Louishad regained his power, brought back his wife, and again dropped Lothar's titles, this time all of them, and refused him to return to courtever again witho
1 CONC ut permission. That year Pepin revolted. In 832, Louis of Bavaria joined Pepin, and the Emperor Louis declaired Pepin deposed of all royal titles but he had no power to enforce this declairation, so Pepin continued to rule. In 833
1 CONC , the three again attacked with support from Louis's own generals and from Pope Gregory IV himself. They imprisoned their father and brother, and exiled Judith to Italy under watch of Lothar, and Louis and Pepin gained territory. 
1 CONC The next year, however, Louis and Pepin released their father and brother, broughtback his wife, and peace was made. In 835, Louis was re-crowned Emperor with great pomp. Pepin died in 838, and while Louis tried to have Charles cr
1 CONC owned king in Aquitaine, the nobles crowned Pepin's son PepinII. Neither had the authority to rule in the country. In 840, Louis the Pious died, and the three surviving brothers began a civil war for the division of the Empire.
1 CONT In 841, Charles and Louis of Bavaria ganged up on their brother Lothar, who had the support of Pepin II, who were defeated at Fontenay, France. In 842, Charles and Louis made a formal alliegance, and together put down a Saxon revo
1 CONC lt that year and a revolt in Aquitaine under Pepin II. In 843, the Treaty of Verdun was made between the three brothers, by which Charles would rule the Western Frankish Kingdom (France), with Pepin's Aquitaine a subkindom under t
1 CONC he ultimate authority of Charles, Lothar would rule the Middle Frankish Kingdom (Italy, Provence, and Lorraine) with the imperial title, and Louis would rule the Eastern Frankish Kingdom (Germany).
1 CONT During his reign in France, Charles suffered the awesome attacks of the Danes, starting in 853. In 846, he ceded Brittany to its Breton inhabitants, and due to force he had to give to them the Breton March in 851 and Maine in 857.
1 CONC  In 853 and 855, he was forced to allow Danish immigration into his kingdom. Another Danish army invaded in 856-9, destroying many French cities. In 858, Charles met with King Lothar II, who controlled the area near Denmark, to di
1 CONC scuss a formal defense. Two years later, Louis the German invaded France on the invite of Pepin IIand the Burgundian nobles, and Charles had so little authority that he couldn't even raise an army. The clergy finally pushed him ou
1 CONC t. In 868, Lothar died, and Louis the German and Charles the Bald divided upLotharingia between them, just as they had done on the death of Charles of Provence in 863. 865-6 saw more Danish invasions into France. In866, Charles fi
1 CONC nally bribed them to leave, and the East Frankish noble Hugh was made Duke to fight off the Norse. In 875, Emperor Louis II, died and on Christmas Day Pope John VIII crowned Charles Emperor inRome. Two years later, Charles died an
1 CONC d the French throne went to hisson Louis II.
1 CONT [Attempt.FTW]
1 CONT Western Frankish King 843-877
1 CONT Frankish Emperor 875-877
1 CONT Louis the Pious became Frankish Emperor in 814 with no rivals to the throne. He had three sons, Lothar, Pepin, and Louis. In 817, Lothar was made co-Emperor with his father and King of Italy to replace Bernard, Pepin made King of 
1 CONC Aquitaine, and Louis made King of Bavaria. In 823, Louis had another son, Charles, this one by a new wife (the mother of the 3 brothers had died). Louis tried desperately to work Charles in as a successor, but the three brothers f
1 CONC ought him everytime he tried to reform his will. After much conflict, Emperor Louis dropped Lothar's imperial title in 829 and sent him off to Italy. The next year the brothers attacked, reinstated Lothar with his imperial title, 
1 CONC and had Judith, the mother of Charles, sent off to a nunnery. By 831, Louishad regained his power, brought back his wife, and again dropped Lothar's titles, this time all of them, and refused him to return to courtever again witho
1 CONC ut permission. That year Pepin revolted. In 832, Louis of Bavaria joined Pepin, and the Emperor Louis declaired Pepin deposed of all royal titles but he had no power to enforce this declairation, so Pepin continued to rule. In 833
1 CONC , the three again attacked with support from Louis's own generals and from Pope Gregory IV himself. They imprisoned their father and brother, and exiled Judith to Italy under watch of Lothar, and Louis and Pepin gained territory. 
1 CONC The next year, however, Louis and Pepin released their father and brother, broughtback his wife, and peace was made. In 835, Louis was re-crowned Emperor with great pomp. Pepin died in 838, and while Louis tried to have Charles cr
1 CONC owned king in Aquitaine, the nobles crowned Pepin's son PepinII. Neither had the authority to rule in the country. In 840, Louis the Pious died, and the three surviving brothers began a civil war for the division of the Empire.
1 CONT In 841, Charles and Louis of Bavaria ganged up on their brother Lothar, who had the support of Pepin II, who were defeated at Fontenay, France. In 842, Charles and Louis made a formal alliegance, and together put down a Saxon revo
1 CONC lt that year and a revolt in Aquitaine under Pepin II. In 843, the Treaty of Verdun was made between the three brothers, by which Charles would rule the Western Frankish Kingdom (France), with Pepin's Aquitaine a subkindom under t
1 CONC he ultimate authority of Charles, Lothar would rule the Middle Frankish Kingdom (Italy, Provence, and Lorraine) with the imperial title, and Louis would rule the Eastern Frankish Kingdom (Germany).
1 CONT During his reign in France, Charles suffered the awesome attacks of the Danes, starting in 853. In 846, he ceded Brittany to its Breton inhabitants, and due to force he had to give to them the Breton March in 851 and Maine in 857.
1 CONC  In 853 and 855, he was forced to allow Danish immigration into his kingdom. Another Danish army invaded in 856-9, destroying many French cities. In 858, Charles met with King Lothar II, who controlled the area near Denmark, to di
1 CONC scuss a formal defense. Two years later, Louis the German invaded France on the invite of Pepin IIand the Burgundian nobles, and Charles had so little authority that he couldn't even raise an army. The clergy finally pushed him ou
1 CONC t. In 868, Lothar died, and Louis the German and Charles the Bald divided upLotharingia between them, just as they had done on the death of Charles of Provence in 863. 865-6 saw more Danish invasions into France. In866, Charles fi
1 CONC nally bribed them to leave, and the East Frankish noble Hugh was made Duke to fight off the Norse. In 875, Emperor Louis II, died and on Christmas Day Pope John VIII crowned Charles Emperor inRome. Two years later, Charles died an
1 CONC d the French throne went to hisson Louis II.
